window.WC_DOCS = [{"id": "00-foundation__constitutional-principles", "path": "00-foundation/constitutional-principles.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.2 — Constitutional Principles", "md": "# Section 0.2 — Constitutional Principles\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.2.1 – 0.2.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section declares the founding principles of the Republic and gives them binding force for all public power.*\n\n## Article 0.2.1 — Constitutionalism\n\n1. The Republic is founded on constitutionalism. Every public power is created by, bound by, and answerable to this Constitution.\n2. No organ of the State, and no person exercising public power, may act without a legal basis or beyond the limits this Constitution imposes.\n3. These principles bind all organs of the State and inform the interpretation of every other provision.\n\n## Article 0.2.2 — Democratic Self-Government\n\n1. The people govern themselves through free elections, through their representatives in the Parliament, through local government, and through the forms of participation this Constitution provides.\n2. Elections shall be free, periodic, and fair, and administered without interference.\n3. Every person has the right to take part in the public affairs of the Republic, directly or through freely chosen representatives.\n\n## Article 0.2.3 — Pluralism\n\n1. The Republic recognizes pluralism in opinion, belief, culture, and language as a source of democratic vitality and protects it as such.\n2. Public institutions shall serve all persons equally and shall remain independent of every party and interest group.\n3. The diversity of the people shall be reflected in public life.\n\n## Article 0.2.4 — Protection of Minorities\n\n1. Persons belonging to minorities shall be protected in their identity, language, culture, and religion, and shall not be forced to assimilate.\n2. Measures may secure the effective participation of minorities in public life, consistent with Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n3. The presence or absence of a numerical majority shall not determine the scope of any right.\n\n## Article 0.2.5 — Intergenerational Responsibility\n\n1. The natural environment, the public finances, and the cultural heritage of the Republic are held in trust for present and future generations.\n2. Public decisions shall take account of their long-term consequences; no generation shall foreclose the ability of the next to meet its own needs.\n3. Implementation belongs to the Parts governing the environment, public finance, and social policy.\n\n## Article 0.2.6 — Application of the Principles\n\n1. The principles in this Section are binding norms, not aspirations.\n2. Courts and every interpreter shall apply them in resolving uncertainty in the meaning of any provision of this Constitution or of any law.\n3. Nothing in this Section confers a power to override an express provision of this Constitution.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section gathers the Republic's founding commitments in one place so that later Parts can invoke a stable interpretive anchor, a technique favored by modern constitutions that state founding values before their rights catalogues. The principles carry interpretive and binding force, yet cannot contradict express text, which preserves clarity about what is command and what is guidance. Minority protection is paired with pluralism, treating diversity as an affirmative constitutional asset rather than a problem to be contained.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.3.1 (Inalienability of Popular Sovereignty); Article 0.6.1 (Representative Democracy); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__constitutional-supremacy", "path": "00-foundation/constitutional-supremacy.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.8 — Constitutional Supremacy", "md": "# Section 0.8 — Constitutional Supremacy\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.8.1 – 0.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Constitution as supreme law, the invalidation of conflicting law, the duty to uphold it, conforming interpretation, and the hierarchy of norms.*\n\n## Article 0.8.1 — Supreme Law\n\n1. This Constitution is the supreme law of the Republic.\n2. Any law, act, or practice inconsistent with it is without force or effect to the extent of the inconsistency.\n3. Obligations arising outside this Constitution bind within the legal order only through and subject to this Constitution, as provided by law.\n\n## Article 0.8.2 — Invalidation of Conflicting Law\n\n1. Every court, in a case properly before it, shall refuse to apply a provision of law inconsistent with this Constitution.\n2. The general invalidation of a provision, and other abstract questions of validity, belong to the Constitutional Court in the manner provided by Part 05 (The Judiciary).\n3. A declaration of invalidity takes effect from the moment and in the manner the deciding court specifies, with due regard to legality and to reliance interests.\n\n## Article 0.8.3 — Duty to Uphold the Constitution\n\n1. Every organ of the State, every person exercising public power, and every member of an institution established by this Constitution shall uphold and defend this Constitution.\n2. Obedience to a superior order shall not excuse a violation of this Constitution.\n3. The persons designated by this Constitution or by law shall take an oath or solemn undertaking to respect the constitutional order.\n\n## Article 0.8.4 — Conforming Interpretation\n\n1. Every law shall, where its language permits, be read in the way that best conforms to this Constitution.\n2. Where no conforming reading is reasonably possible, the deciding court shall decline to apply the provision to the extent of the inconsistency.\n3. Conforming interpretation shall not be used to create obligations or prohibitions that the enacted text does not bear.\n\n## Article 0.8.5 — Hierarchy of Norms\n\n1. The Constitution prevails over statute; statute prevails over delegated legislation; delegated legislation prevails over by-laws and other subordinate norms.\n2. A subordinate norm shall remain within the delegation from which it derives and consistent with the superior norm.\n3. A conflict between norms of equal rank is resolved by the later or the more specific norm, subject to judicial control.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section combines two techniques of constitutional supremacy often found separately: refusal of application by any court in a concrete case, in the diffuse-review tradition, and centralized abstract control by a Constitutional Court, in the continental tradition. Conforming interpretation is imposed as a duty before invalidation is reached, which reduces the disruptive consequences of striking down legislation. The hierarchy of norms makes explicit what many constitutions leave implied, helping students locate every norm within one ordered structure.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.4.1 (Legality); Article 1.1.3 (Duties of Organs of State); Article 0.5.1 (Allocation of Powers).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__democracy", "path": "00-foundation/democracy.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.6 — Democracy", "md": "# Section 0.6 — Democracy\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.6.1 – 0.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes representative, participatory, and direct democracy as the method of public life in the Republic.*\n\n## Article 0.6.1 — Representative Democracy\n\n1. The Republic is a representative democracy; the Parliament, elected by universal suffrage, represents the whole people.\n2. Members of the Parliament represent the whole people and are bound only by their conscience.\n3. Representatives remain accountable to the voters through periodic elections and through the scrutiny mechanisms of the Parliament.\n\n## Article 0.6.2 — Free and Periodic Elections\n\n1. Every citizen who has reached the age of eighteen years has the right to vote in elections and referendums.\n2. Elections shall be free, secret, periodic, and fair, and shall be administered by the Electoral Commission established under Part 20 (Independent Institutions).\n3. Voting is a civic duty; its discharge shall remain voluntary.\n\n## Article 0.6.3 — Political Pluralism\n\n1. Political parties may be formed freely, shall compete on equal terms, and shall conform to democratic principles in their internal organization.\n2. A party may be prohibited only where its aims or conduct incite violence or seek to destroy the democratic order, in the manner and by the organ this Constitution provides.\n3. Membership of a political party shall be voluntary and open, without discrimination.\n\n## Article 0.6.4 — Civil Society and Participation\n\n1. Associations, trade unions, and other organizations of civil society may be formed freely and may express views on public affairs.\n2. The State shall provide opportunities for public participation in the preparation of legislation and of major policy, and shall consider the results in good faith.\n\n## Article 0.6.5 — Direct Democracy\n\n1. The people may act directly through national referendums in the cases and manner provided by Part 06 (Elections and Referendums).\n2. A referendum result within its constitutional competence is binding and shall be implemented by the competent organs.\n3. A referendum shall not be used to evade the amendment thresholds this Constitution establishes, including those protecting entrenched provisions under Part 30 (Schedules).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section describes voting as both a civic right and a civic duty while leaving its discharge voluntary, a compromise that borrows the language of civic obligation without adopting compulsory voting. Representative, participatory, and direct democracy are given equal constitutional dignity, so that referendums supplement rather than replace parliamentary deliberation. The final safeguard, barring referendums from bypassing the amendment thresholds of Part 30 (Schedules), protects the entrenchment scheme that keeps core commitments beyond the reach of a single campaign.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.2.2 (Democratic Self-Government); Article 1.3.5 (Peaceful Assembly and Association); Article 2.1.5 (Civic Rights and Duties).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__human-dignity", "path": "00-foundation/human-dignity.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.7 — Human Dignity", "md": "# Section 0.7 — Human Dignity\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.7.1 – 0.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section declares human dignity inviolable, makes it the source of all rights, and extends its protection to science and new technologies.*\n\n## Article 0.7.1 — Inviolability of Human Dignity\n\n1. Human dignity is inviolable. It is the supreme value of the Republic and the source of every right and duty this Constitution recognizes.\n2. The dignity of every person shall be respected by all organs of the State and, where capable of application, by all persons and institutions.\n3. No circumstance, including a state of emergency, may justify treating a person as a means to an end or as an object of disposal.\n\n## Article 0.7.2 — Source of Rights and Duties\n\n1. The rights of Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms) apply human dignity to particular fields; they reinforce one another and shall be read together.\n2. Because dignity is universal, rights are universal and belong to every person without distinction.\n3. The recognition of a right entails the recognition of the correlative duties of others and of the State.\n\n## Article 0.7.3 — Prohibition of Degrading Treatment\n\n1. No person shall be subjected to torture or to cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment.\n2. Public institutions shall be organized and administered so as to prevent degradation, above all in places of detention.\n\n## Article 0.7.4 — Dignity in Science and Technology\n\n1. Scientific research and the application of new technologies shall respect human dignity; the human person shall be treated as an end and never merely as a means.\n2. The Parliament shall regulate fields in which science or technology may impair dignity, including research involving human beings, the processing of biological and genetic data, and automated decision-making.\n3. Such regulation shall not obstruct inquiry or innovation beyond what Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) justifies.\n\n## Article 0.7.5 — Respect for Dignity by Private Persons\n\n1. Every natural and juristic person shall respect the dignity of others in their relations, where such application is capable of realization.\n2. The State shall protect persons against degradation by private actors, including through the remedies provided in Part 16 (Civil Law).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Placing dignity first, before liberty and equality, follows the ordering made famous by the Basic Law of Germany, drafted as a response to systematic degradation, and now shared by many constitutions written after 1990. The express treatment of science and technology is forward-looking: it anticipates disputes over biotechnology and automated decision-making that older texts left to interpretation. The trade-off is an open-textured standard that courts must concretize, accepted in exchange for a guarantee that will not become obsolete as circumstances change.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.1 (Nature and Purpose of Rights); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.5.1 (Right to Privacy).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__preamble", "path": "00-foundation/preamble.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.1 — Preamble", "md": "# Section 0.1 — Preamble\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Preamble · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*The Preamble proclaims the identity, values, and founding resolve of the Republic and records the adoption of this Constitution.*\n\nWe the people of the Republic, remembering the struggles by which our freedom was won, grateful for the inheritance we hold in trust, and resolved to govern ourselves under law rather than force, do ordain and adopt this Constitution as the supreme law of our common life.\n\nWe affirm that human dignity is inviolable; that all public authority belongs to the people; and that the State exists to serve the person, never the person the State. We establish a unitary, democratic republic in which the law binds governor and governed alike, in which power is divided and checked so that no organ may rule alone, and in which the independence of courts and the integrity of public institutions are secure.\n\nWe commit ourselves to the rule of law; to equality before the law without discrimination of any kind; to pluralism in opinion, belief, and culture, with special care for minorities; to representative, participatory, and direct democracy through free and periodic elections; to freedom of expression and of the media; to solidarity among all who live here, including those who seek refuge among us; and to the protection of the natural and cultural inheritance of the Republic for those who come after us.\n\nWe acknowledge that rights are inseparable from duties. Every person shall respect the dignity and the rights of others; every organ of the State shall exercise its powers honestly, transparently, and accountably; and prosperity today shall not be purchased at the expense of tomorrow.\n\nThis Constitution is adopted by the people in a national referendum, having been deliberated upon and enacted by a freely elected constituent assembly, and it enters into force upon the proclamation of its ratification. From that moment, all public power flows from this Constitution alone, and any law or act inconsistent with it is without effect to the extent of the inconsistency.\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.2.1 (Constitutionalism); Article 0.3.1 (Inalienability of Popular Sovereignty); Article 0.8.1 (Supreme Law).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__rule-of-law", "path": "00-foundation/rule-of-law.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.4 — Rule of Law", "md": "# Section 0.4 — Rule of Law\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.4.1 – 0.4.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the rule of law: legality, legal certainty, non-retroactivity, the prohibition of arbitrariness, equality before the law, and judicial protection.*\n\n## Article 0.4.1 — Legality\n\n1. Public power may be exercised only on the basis of a law adopted by a competent organ in the manner this Constitution prescribes.\n2. Every restriction of a right, every imposition of a duty, and every withdrawal of a benefit requires such a legal basis.\n3. Norms of general application shall be adopted by the Parliament, save where this Constitution confers a normative power on another organ.\n\n## Article 0.4.2 — Legal Certainty and Accessibility\n\n1. All laws shall be published, accessible, clear, and stable.\n2. No norm shall take effect before its official publication.\n3. A provision whose meaning a person of ordinary understanding cannot discern shall be construed to its narrower consistent effect.\n\n## Article 0.4.3 — Non-Retroactivity\n\n1. No person shall be convicted or punished for an act that did not constitute an offense when committed; a later law may reduce a penalty already imposed.\n2. Laws shall operate prospectively, save where expressly provided otherwise for compelling reasons of general interest.\n3. Retroactive civil or administrative effects are permissible only where necessary and consistent with legitimate expectations and Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 0.4.4 — Prohibition of Arbitrariness\n\n1. Every exercise of public power shall rest on an adequate legal and factual basis and shall bear a reasonable relationship to its purpose.\n2. A decision is arbitrary where it is taken without reasons, on irrelevant considerations, in bad faith, or in disregard of relevant considerations.\n\n## Article 0.4.5 — Equality Before the Law\n\n1. All persons are equal before the law and are entitled to its equal protection and benefit.\n2. Public power shall be exercised without favor or disfavor toward any person or group, save for distinctions permitted by this Constitution.\n\n## Article 0.4.6 — Judicial Protection and Legitimate Expectations\n\n1. Every person whose rights or interests are affected by an exercise of public power has the right to an effective judicial remedy.\n2. Legitimate expectations arising from definitive administrative decisions shall be protected; their override requires a legal basis and justification under Article 1.1.4.\n3. Access to a remedy shall not be withdrawn, suspended, or rendered impracticable in fact or in law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section consolidates the rule of law in one place so that later Parts may invoke it without restating it, treating legality, certainty, and judicial protection as a single doctrine rather than several. The narrow retroactivity exceptions — the humane reduction of penalties and the rare civil case justified by compelling general interest — track settled comparative practice while keeping the general rule strict. The protection of legitimate expectations is included because administrative reliability is a dimension of the rule of law that judicial guarantees alone do not capture.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.6.1 (Fair Administrative Action); Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__separation-of-powers", "path": "00-foundation/separation-of-powers.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.5 — Separation of Powers", "md": "# Section 0.5 — Separation of Powers\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.5.1 – 0.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section allocates the legislative, executive, and judicial functions to distinct organs and completes the allocation with checks, balances, and independent institutions.*\n\n## Article 0.5.1 — Allocation of Powers\n\n1. Legislative power vests in the Parliament; executive power in the President, the Prime Minister, and the Cabinet; judicial power in the courts.\n2. Each organ shall exercise the functions this Constitution assigns to it and no others.\n3. Functions may be shared or delegated only as this Constitution or a law adopted under it expressly provides.\n\n## Article 0.5.2 — Checks and Balances\n\n1. The allocation of powers is completed by the checks this Constitution establishes, including the confidence of the Lower House in the Prime Minister, the President's single power to refer a Bill back to Parliament, the Upper House's power to delay legislation, the review of laws by the courts, and the mutual accountability of the organs of the State.\n2. No check shall be exercised so as to nullify the essential constitutional function of another organ.\n3. Each organ shall cooperate in good faith so that the constitutional order remains functional.\n\n## Article 0.5.3 — Independence of the Judiciary\n\n1. The courts are subject only to this Constitution and to the law.\n2. No organ of the State and no person may interfere with a pending judicial decision or give binding instructions to a judge in a case.\n3. The tenure, appointment, and financial guarantees that protect judicial independence are stated in Part 05 (The Judiciary).\n\n## Article 0.5.4 — Independent Institutions\n\n1. The Ombudsman, the Human Rights Commission, the Anti-Corruption Commission, the Electoral Commission, the Office of the Auditor-General, the Statistics Authority, and the Information Commission shall be independent in the exercise of their mandates and are not subordinate to any other organ.\n2. Their composition, tenure, and accountability are governed by Part 20 (Independent Institutions).\n3. Their budgets shall secure their operational autonomy, and their reports shall be published.\n\n## Article 0.5.5 — Limits of Delegation\n\n1. The essential elements of the legislative function shall not be delegated to the executive; the executive shall not delegate to private persons decisions that bind the public; and adjudication shall not be withdrawn from the courts except as this Constitution expressly provides.\n2. Every delegation shall be accompanied by parliamentary oversight and by clear standards limiting the discretion conferred.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section assigns powers by function but immediately supplies concrete checks, reflecting the view, shared by most modern constitutions, that a catalogue of separations without checks is ornamental. Independent institutions receive constitutional rank as a fourth branch in function rather than in name, comparable to the independent administrative and electoral authorities that twentieth-century constitutions developed. The limits of delegation preserve the heart of each function while allowing the practical flexibility a modern state requires.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.8.2 (Invalidation of Conflicting Law); Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial).\n"}, {"id": "00-foundation__sovereignty", "path": "00-foundation/sovereignty.md", "part": "00-foundation", "partTitle": "Foundation", "title": "Section 0.3 — Sovereignty", "md": "# Section 0.3 — Sovereignty\n\n> **Part 00 — Foundation** · Articles 0.3.1 – 0.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section vests sovereignty in the people of the Republic and regulates how it may be exercised and by whom.*\n\n## Article 0.3.1 — Inalienability of Popular Sovereignty\n\n1. Sovereignty belongs to the people of the Republic, who exercise it in the manner this Constitution provides.\n2. Popular sovereignty is inalienable and indivisible; no person, party, or organ may claim it for itself alone.\n3. The people act as one body politic under this Constitution, without distinction among citizens of any kind.\n\n## Article 0.3.2 — Exercise of Sovereignty\n\n1. The people exercise their sovereignty through elections and referendums, through the Parliament, the President, the Prime Minister and the Cabinet, the courts, and the institutions of local government.\n2. Each of these institutions holds its authority from this Constitution and shall exercise it within the limits this Constitution sets.\n3. An exercise of public power without constitutional or statutory authority is invalid from the moment it occurs.\n\n## Article 0.3.3 — Derivation of Public Authority\n\n1. Public authority derives solely from this Constitution and may be created, transferred, or terminated only as this Constitution or a law adopted under it provides.\n2. No organ of the State may confer public authority on any person or body except as this Constitution permits.\n3. A person acting under color of public power bears the burden of showing the legal basis of that power.\n\n## Article 0.3.4 — Prohibition of Usurpation\n\n1. The seizure or usurpation of public power is unlawful and shall have no legal effect.\n2. No lapse of time, election, or acquiescence shall validate an act of usurpation.\n3. Obedience to an order of a usurping authority shall not excuse the violation of rights or of this Constitution.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section adapts the classic declaration that sovereignty resides in the nation, stated most influentially in Article 3 of the French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, to a constitutional democracy in which the people bind themselves through the very constitution by which they act. The trade-off is that sovereignty is described as supreme yet self-limited, so no institution, including a transient parliamentary majority, can claim an unmediated mandate. The anti-usurpation article follows the practice of constitutions that refuse legal validation to coups regardless of their duration or apparent success.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.2.2 (Democratic Self-Government); Article 0.6.5 (Direct Democracy); Article 0.8.1 (Supreme Law).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__bill-of-rights", "path": "01-rights/bill-of-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.1 — Bill of Rights", "md": "# Section 1.1 — Bill of Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.1.1 – 1.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section defines the nature, application, limitation, and non-derogable core of the rights in this Part.*\n\n## Article 1.1.1 — Nature and Purpose of Rights\n\n1. The rights and freedoms of Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms) are inherent in human dignity and belong to everyone within the jurisdiction of the Republic.\n2. The enumeration of particular rights does not deny other rights inherent in human dignity.\n\n## Article 1.1.2 — Vertical and Horizontal Application\n\n1. This Part binds all organs of the State and all persons and institutions exercising public power.\n2. A provision of this Part also binds natural and juristic persons in their relations with one another to the extent that the right is capable of such application.\n\n## Article 1.1.3 — Duties of Organs of State\n\n1. All organs of the State shall respect, protect, promote, and fulfill the rights of this Part.\n2. Respect requires non-interference; protection requires preventing interference by others; promotion and fulfillment require positive measures, within available resources where qualified.\n\n## Article 1.1.4 — Limitation of Rights\n\n1. The rights of this Part may be limited only by a law of general application, and only to the extent that the limitation is reasonable and justifiable in an open and democratic society founded on human dignity, equality, and freedom.\n2. A limitation is reasonable and justifiable only if it satisfies every element of this test:\n   (a) legality — it is prescribed by a published law, precise enough to regulate conduct, and adopted by a competent organ;\n   (b) legitimate aim — it pursues an objective recognized by this Constitution or that law;\n   (c) suitability — it is capable of achieving that objective;\n   (d) necessity — there is no less restrictive means; and\n   (e) proportionality stricto sensu — the benefit to the general interest outweighs the harm to the right.\n3. The organ relying on a limitation bears the burden of justifying it.\n4. Nothing in this Article authorizes derogation from the rights listed in Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n\n## Article 1.1.5 — Non-Derogable Rights\n\n1. The following rights may not be derogated from in any circumstance, including an emergency declared under Part 26 (Emergency and Crisis):\n   (a) the right to life;\n   (b) freedom from torture and from cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment;\n   (c) freedom from slavery and from forced labor;\n   (d) freedom of conscience and religion;\n   (e) the core of fair-trial rights, including habeas corpus; and\n   (f) the principle of legality in criminal law.\n2. A provision that purports to suspend any of these rights is void from its adoption.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A single master limitation clause replaces per-right lists, so every restriction must pass one transparent test in a predictable sequence. The non-derogable list condenses the protections that international human-rights law treats as beyond suspension, as in Article 4 of the ICCPR and the regional emergency clauses. The burden of justification lies on the State, correcting the asymmetry of information between citizen and administration.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.7.1 (Inviolability of Human Dignity); Article 1.7.5 (Non-Derogable Core); Article 0.8.2 (Invalidation of Conflicting Law).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__consumer-rights", "path": "01-rights/consumer-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.14 — Consumer Rights", "md": "# Section 1.14 — Consumer Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.14.1 – 1.14.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the safety of goods and services, truthful information, fair contract terms, and accessible redress for consumers.*\n\n## Article 1.14.1 — Safe Goods and Services\n\n1. Every person has the right to goods and services that are safe and that conform to the standards provided by law.\n2. Producers, importers, and suppliers are liable, under the conditions provided by law, for damage caused by defective goods or unsafe services.\n3. Where a product or service proves dangerous after placement on the market, the supplier shall inform the public and, where required by law, withdraw it.\n\n## Article 1.14.2 — Truthful Information\n\n1. Consumers have the right to accurate and complete information on the essential characteristics, price, and terms of goods and services.\n2. Advertising shall not mislead; claims shall be verifiable; and practices that materially distort a consumer's decision-making are prohibited as provided by law.\n3. Information shall be provided in plain language, and labeling requirements shall be set by law.\n\n## Article 1.14.3 — Fair Contract Terms\n\n1. Contract terms that have not been individually negotiated shall be fair; a term is unfair where it causes a significant imbalance in the parties' rights and obligations to the detriment of the consumer.\n2. An unfair term shall not bind the consumer, without prejudice to the validity of the remainder of the contract.\n\n## Article 1.14.4 — Accessible Redress\n\n1. Consumers have access to complaints procedures, to small-claims and simplified procedures, and to collective actions provided by law.\n2. Redress shall be effective, proportionate, and dissuasive, and procedural costs shall not exceed what the value of the claim reasonably permits.\n3. The general law of contracts, within which these guarantees operate, is stated in Part 16 (Civil Law).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Consumer rights are constitutionalized to anchor a field otherwise scattered across commercial codes, with the anchor deliberately modest: safety, truthful information, fair standard terms, and an accessible remedy. The unfair-terms rule borrows the significant-imbalance standard developed in comparative consumer law, which targets adhesion contracts without displacing ordinary contract doctrine. Collective redress is included because individual losses are typically small while aggregate harm is large, which makes litigation economics a matter of access to justice.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 0.4.6 (Judicial Protection and Legitimate Expectations).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__digital-rights", "path": "01-rights/digital-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.13 — Digital Rights", "md": "# Section 1.13 — Digital Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.13.1 – 1.13.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section extends constitutional guarantees to the digital sphere: access to the internet, online expression and privacy, and protection from unlawful surveillance.*\n\n## Article 1.13.1 — Access to the Internet\n\n1. Every person has the right of access to the internet on non-discriminatory terms.\n2. The State shall promote universal coverage, affordability, and digital literacy.\n3. A disconnection or degradation of access requires a legal basis and is subject to Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); its statutory implementation is stated in Part 17 (Digital Law).\n\n## Article 1.13.2 — Online Expression\n\n1. Freedom of expression applies in full to communication through digital networks; a restriction applicable offline applies online only where its purpose and effects are comparable.\n2. No general obligation to monitor content may be imposed on intermediaries; specific removals or blockings require a legal basis, are subject to review, and respect the safeguards of Article 1.3.4 (Prohibition of Prior Restraint).\n\n## Article 1.13.3 — Digital Privacy\n\n1. The privacy guarantees of Article 1.5.1 (Right to Privacy) apply in full to digital environments, including devices, accounts, and the data they contain.\n2. Access to a device or account requires the same safeguards as access to a home.\n\n## Article 1.13.4 — Protection from Unlawful Surveillance\n\n1. Surveillance, interception, and the collection of communications data are permissible only under a judicial warrant, for a defined purpose and period, and only where necessary under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n2. Indiscriminate or bulk surveillance is prohibited unless authorized by law, necessary to address a grave threat to the life of the nation, and subject to independent review.\n3. A person subjected to unlawful surveillance has the right to be informed and to an effective remedy once notification no longer endangers a lawful purpose.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Digital rights are drafted as extensions of offline guarantees with equal force, which closes the loophole by which conduct restricted offline migrates online to escape control. Internet access is treated as a gateway right: without it, expression, education, and participation lose much of their value in a digital society. The surveillance clause channels even exceptional bulk powers through legality, necessity, and independent review, drawing on the comparative practice of oversight bodies for intelligence activities.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.1 (Right to Privacy); Article 1.3.4 (Prohibition of Prior Restraint); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__due-process", "path": "01-rights/due-process.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.6 — Due Process", "md": "# Section 1.6 — Due Process\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.6.1 – 1.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees fair administrative action — notice, reasons, hearing, impartiality, and review — for every public decision affecting rights or interests.*\n\n## Article 1.6.1 — Fair Administrative Action\n\n1. Every person has the right to fair administrative action in every decision of a public authority that affects the person's rights, interests, or legitimate expectations.\n2. Fairness applies to decisions taken under statute, under delegated authority, or in the exercise of any other public function.\n\n## Article 1.6.2 — Notice and Reasons\n\n1. Before a decision that may adversely affect a person is taken, the authority shall give notice of the matter and of the applicable criteria.\n2. A written decision shall state its reasons in sufficient detail to enable the person to understand it and to consider a remedy.\n\n## Article 1.6.3 — Hearing and Representation\n\n1. A person affected shall have a reasonable opportunity to present the person's case, to comment on adverse evidence, and, where the decision is serious, to be assisted or represented.\n2. The complexity of the matter and the stakes for the person shall determine the degree of formality required.\n\n## Article 1.6.4 — Review and Remedies\n\n1. A person affected by an administrative decision has the right to seek its review, first where an internal appeal is provided and ultimately before a court.\n2. Review shall be available without regard to the value or importance of the matter, and remedies shall be effective, proportionate, and timely.\n\n## Article 1.6.5 — Impartiality and Automated Decisions\n\n1. No person shall take a decision in which the person has a personal interest; decision-makers shall act without bias.\n2. A decision that significantly affects a person's rights may be prepared with the support of automated systems but shall not be taken solely by automated means; a human decision-maker shall review the case at the person's request.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Making fair administrative action a free-standing constitutional right, rather than a by-product of judicial review, owes much to the South African model and gives citizens a positive claim to notice, reasons, and hearing before decisions are taken. The graduated approach to formality keeps the right practical: more procedure where the stakes are high, less where they are modest. The automated-decision clause responds to a risk older texts could not foresee, requiring human review rather than prohibiting assistance by systems.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial); Article 0.4.6 (Judicial Protection and Legitimate Expectations); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__education-rights", "path": "01-rights/education-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.10 — Education Rights", "md": "# Section 1.10 — Education Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.10.1 – 1.10.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the right to education, free and compulsory basic education, equal access, and parental choice within public standards.*\n\n## Article 1.10.1 — Right to Education\n\n1. Every person has the right to education directed to the full development of the human personality and to the strengthening of respect for human rights and freedoms.\n2. The State shall progressively make higher education and vocational training accessible to all on the basis of capacity.\n3. The structure of the education system and its financing are stated in Part 11 (Education).\n\n## Article 1.10.2 — Free and Compulsory Basic Education\n\n1. Basic education shall be free of charge and compulsory for the period provided by law.\n2. No child shall be excluded from basic education for lack of means, and the State shall remove economic barriers, including through material support.\n3. Public basic education shall be available in every Province and shall reach children in rural and remote areas on equal terms.\n\n## Article 1.10.3 — Equal Access\n\n1. Access to educational institutions shall be without discrimination on any ground.\n2. The State shall take measures to enable learners with disabilities and learners belonging to minorities to participate in education on an equal basis with others, including instruction in minority languages.\n\n## Article 1.10.4 — Parental Choice\n\n1. Parents may choose non-public education for their children, provided that the institutions chosen conform to the standards the State sets for the equivalent public stage and do not infringe the rights of the child.\n2. The State may subsidize non-public institutions that meet public standards; a subsidy shall not condition admission on grounds prohibited by Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The right is layered: basic education is guaranteed immediately and free of charge, while higher education is governed by progressive realization, matching the capacity-based phrasing of international educational-rights law. Parental choice is preserved but subordinated to public standards and to the rights of the child, which prevents education from becoming a vehicle for the denial of other constitutional rights. This allocation anchors the non-negotiable core while leaving the design of the school system to Part 11 (Education).*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.1 (Equality Before and Under the Law); Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__environmental-rights", "path": "01-rights/environmental-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.12 — Environmental Rights", "md": "# Section 1.12 — Environmental Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.12.1 – 1.12.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the right to a healthy environment, the duty of the State to protect it, and standing and remedies for its enforcement.*\n\n## Article 1.12.1 — Right to a Healthy Environment\n\n1. Every person has the right to an environment that is not harmful to health or well-being, including clean air, safe and sufficient water, healthy ecosystems, and a stable climate.\n2. The right belongs to present and future generations alike and may be asserted before a serious threat is fully realized.\n\n## Article 1.12.2 — Duty of the State\n\n1. The State shall protect the environment, prevent pollution and ecological degradation, promote conservation, and secure ecologically sustainable development while promoting justifiable economic and social development.\n2. The State shall apply a precautionary approach where there is a risk of serious or irreversible harm.\n3. Environmental policy implementing this Section is stated in Part 13 (Environment and Natural Resources).\n\n## Article 1.12.3 — Standing and Access to Justice\n\n1. Any person, and any association acting in the interest of environmental protection, may seek review or other relief where this right or its implementation is threatened or breached, without needing to show injury beyond the interest relied upon.\n2. Proceedings shall be expeditious, and the law may provide that costs do not deter meritorious environmental claims.\n\n## Article 1.12.4 — Remedies and Enforcement\n\n1. Courts may grant preventive orders, restoration orders, compensation, and any other effective remedy in environmental matters.\n2. The standards adopted under Part 13 (Environment and Natural Resources) shall be interpreted in light of this Section.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The right is framed to be enforceable before catastrophe: standing is granted at the threshold of serious threat and extends to associations, which corrects the structural mismatch between diffuse environmental harm and the individualized conception of injury. The substantive content — air, water, ecosystems, climate — is listed so that courts are not left to invent the baseline. Implementation is deliberately routed to Part 13 (Environment and Natural Resources), keeping this Section judicial rather than administrative in character.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.2.5 (Intergenerational Responsibility); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__equality", "path": "01-rights/equality.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.2 — Equality", "md": "# Section 1.2 — Equality\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.2.1 – 1.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees equality before and under the law, prohibits discrimination on listed and open grounds, and disciplines affirmative measures.*\n\n## Article 1.2.1 — Equality Before and Under the Law\n\n1. All persons are equal before the law and entitled to its equal protection and equal benefit.\n2. Equality under the law requires that like cases be treated alike and that different cases be treated differently only on reasonable and objective grounds.\n\n## Article 1.2.2 — Prohibition of Discrimination\n\n1. The State shall not discriminate, directly or indirectly, against any person on any ground, including race, color, ethnic or social origin, nationality, sex, gender, pregnancy, marital or family status, language, religion or belief, political or other opinion, disability, age, property, birth, sexual orientation, gender identity, or other status.\n2. Discrimination is direct where a measure treats a person less favorably on a listed ground; it is indirect where an apparently neutral measure places persons sharing a listed ground at a particular disadvantage without justification under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 1.2.3 — Intersectional Discrimination\n\n1. Discrimination arising from the combination of two or more grounds shall be recognized and remedied as such.\n2. The State shall collect, consistent with the privacy of persons, the information needed to detect and remedy intersectional disadvantage.\n\n## Article 1.2.4 — Equality of Women and Men\n\n1. Women and men are equal in dignity and in rights; the State shall remove obstacles to the full enjoyment of this equality, including in public employment and public appointments.\n2. Measures protecting maternity or the health of mothers shall not constitute discrimination.\n\n## Article 1.2.5 — Affirmative Measures\n\n1. Measures may be adopted to advance persons or groups disadvantaged by past or continuing discrimination.\n2. Such measures shall pursue the removal of disadvantage, shall be reasonable, and shall lapse when their purpose is substantially achieved; they remain subject to Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n3. Affirmative measures shall not result in the permanent exclusion of any person from the exercise of a right.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The open formula \"other status\" keeps the catalogue current, while the express recognition of intersectional discrimination reflects a development that most constitutional texts still omit. Affirmative measures are permitted but disciplined: they must target demonstrated disadvantage and remain subject to the master limitation clause, which prevents temporary correction from hardening into permanent preference. The distinction between equality before the law and equality under the law preserves both the formal and the substantive dimension.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 2.1.1 (Citizenship of the Republic); Article 2.3.4 (Rights of Lawfully Resident Non-Citizens).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__fair-trial", "path": "01-rights/fair-trial.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.7 — Fair Trial", "md": "# Section 1.7 — Fair Trial\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.7.1 – 1.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the fair-trial rights of every person: an independent tribunal, the presumption of innocence, defense rights, habeas corpus, and a non-derogable core.*\n\n## Article 1.7.1 — Right to a Fair Trial\n\n1. In the determination of civil rights and obligations or of any criminal charge, every person is entitled to a fair and public hearing within a reasonable time by an independent and impartial tribunal established by law.\n2. The public may be excluded only by order of the tribunal, only to protect minors, privacy, or the integrity of justice, and only to the extent necessary.\n\n## Article 1.7.2 — Presumption of Innocence\n\n1. Every person charged with an offense shall be presumed innocent until proven guilty according to law in a final judgment.\n2. The burden of proof lies on the prosecution; the benefit of the doubt belongs to the accused; and public authorities shall not present a person as guilty before judgment.\n\n## Article 1.7.3 — Rights of the Accused\n\n1. In the determination of any criminal charge, the accused shall have, at minimum:\n   (a) to be informed promptly, in a language the accused understands, of the nature and cause of the charge;\n   (b) to adequate time and facilities for the preparation of the defense;\n   (c) to defend in person or through counsel of choice, with state-funded legal aid where the interests of justice so require;\n   (d) to examine witnesses against the accused and to obtain the attendance of witnesses for the defense; and\n   (e) to the free assistance of an interpreter where needed.\n2. The accused may not be compelled to testify or to confess guilt.\n\n## Article 1.7.4 — Habeas Corpus and Liberty\n\n1. Every person deprived of liberty shall be informed promptly of the reasons and shall be brought before a judge without undue delay.\n2. Every detained person has the right to challenge the lawfulness of the detention before a court, which shall decide promptly and order release where the detention is unlawful.\n\n## Article 1.7.5 — Non-Derogable Core\n\n1. The guarantees that constitute the core of fair-trial rights, including habeas corpus and the right to an independent and impartial tribunal, may not be derogated from, in accordance with Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n2. In a state of emergency, the remaining fair-trial guarantees may be adjusted only under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) and never below the non-derogable core.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section follows the structure of Article 6 of the European Convention on Human Rights but states the guarantees as direct constitutional commands and names habeas corpus expressly as non-derogable, closing the gap through which emergency regimes have historically eroded personal liberty. The presumption of innocence is extended to pre-judgment statements by public authorities, reflecting its function as a shield against the court of public opinion as well as the court of law. The prohibition of compelled testimony completes the accusatorial design.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 2.4.3 (Fair Status Determination).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__freedom-of-religion", "path": "01-rights/freedom-of-religion.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.4 — Freedom of Conscience and Religion", "md": "# Section 1.4 — Freedom of Conscience and Religion\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.4.1 – 1.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees freedom of conscience and religion, mandates State neutrality, protects free exercise and the autonomy of religious communities, and declares the freedom non-derogable.*\n\n## Article 1.4.1 — Freedom of Conscience and Religion\n\n1. Every person has the right to freedom of conscience, thought, and religion, including the freedom to change religion or belief and to hold no belief.\n2. This freedom may be manifested individually or in community, in public or in private, through worship, teaching, practice, and observance.\n3. No person shall be coerced in matters of conscience or religion.\n\n## Article 1.4.2 — Non-Establishment and Neutrality\n\n1. The Republic has no state religion and shall maintain neutrality toward all religions and beliefs.\n2. Public institutions shall neither privilege nor burden any religion or belief, and public office shall be open without religious test.\n3. Cooperation with religious communities is permissible only where it serves a public purpose open to all on equal terms and does not confer religious authority on the State.\n\n## Article 1.4.3 — Free Exercise\n\n1. Religious communities may establish and maintain institutions, schools, charities, and places of worship, and may regulate their own religious affairs.\n2. Restrictions on the manifestation of religion or belief are permissible only under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); the inner freedom of belief admits no limitation.\n\n## Article 1.4.4 — Institutional Autonomy of Religious Communities\n\n1. Religious communities shall determine their membership, doctrine, ministry, and worship without State interference, subject to generally applicable laws protecting rights and public order.\n2. Religious instruction in public schools shall be voluntary, and alternatives of equal standing shall be available.\n\n## Article 1.4.5 — Non-Derogation\n\n1. Freedom of conscience and religion may not be derogated from, even during a state of emergency, in accordance with Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n2. Emergency measures shall not single out religious communities for restriction except under the ordinary test of Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section pairs non-establishment with institutional autonomy so that neutrality toward religion is protected in both directions: the State must not command religion, and religious communities must not be absorbed by the State. The absolute protection of inner belief, with manifestation alone subject to limitation, tracks the distinction drawn in comparative doctrine between the internal and external forums of conscience. The express non-derogation clause removes any doubt that this freedom survives emergencies intact.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.10.4 (Parental Choice).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__freedom-of-speech", "path": "01-rights/freedom-of-speech.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.3 — Freedom of Expression", "md": "# Section 1.3 — Freedom of Expression\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.3.1 – 1.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees freedom of expression, information, and the media, protects political speech at the core, prohibits prior restraint, and secures assembly and association.*\n\n## Article 1.3.1 — Freedom of Expression and Opinion\n\n1. Every person has the right to hold opinions without interference and to seek, receive, and impart information and ideas of every kind, orally, in writing, in print, in art, or through any media, regardless of frontiers.\n2. No one shall be compelled to express or to declare an opinion.\n\n## Article 1.3.2 — Freedom of Information and the Media\n\n1. The media shall be free from censorship, from suspension, and from administrative control.\n2. Editorial independence shall be protected, and the law may promote media pluralism and transparency of ownership.\n3. Public bodies shall publish information proactively as provided by law, in addition to the right of access under Part 21 (Media and Information).\n\n## Article 1.3.3 — Political Speech\n\n1. Expression on political and public affairs occupies the core of this freedom and enjoys the widest protection, including criticism of public authorities, satire, and advocacy of lawful change.\n2. Public officials shall tolerate greater scrutiny than private persons and may invoke their reputation only within the limits of Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 1.3.4 — Prohibition of Prior Restraint\n\n1. No licensing, registration, or prior approval shall be required for the publication of print or online media.\n2. A court may restrain publication in advance only where the harm is grave and imminent, the restraint is no broader than necessary, and the test of Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) is satisfied.\n\n## Article 1.3.5 — Peaceful Assembly and Association\n\n1. Every person has the right to assemble peacefully and to form and join associations, including political parties and trade unions.\n2. Notification schemes for assemblies shall serve public-safety purposes only and shall not operate as a requirement of permission.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The prohibition of prior restraint is constitutionalized rather than left to media law because censorship by delay is the form of restriction hardest to remedy after the fact; the narrow judicial injunction exception keeps orders available for grave and imminent harm. Placing political speech at the core instructs courts to calibrate scrutiny by subject matter, an approach familiar from comparative free-expression doctrine. Assembly is paired with expression because, in practice, the public square is where speech acquires civic force.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.13.2 (Online Expression); Article 0.6.3 (Political Pluralism).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__healthcare-rights", "path": "01-rights/healthcare-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.11 — Healthcare Rights", "md": "# Section 1.11 — Healthcare Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.11.1 – 1.11.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees access to healthcare services, unconditional emergency care, progressive realization, and immediate minimum core obligations.*\n\n## Article 1.11.1 — Right of Access to Healthcare Services\n\n1. Every person has the right of access to healthcare services, including preventive, curative, and rehabilitative care.\n2. Access shall be on a non-discriminatory basis and shall be organized so that distance, cost, and administrative complexity do not defeat the right.\n3. The organization, financing, and quality standards of the health system are stated in Part 12 (Health).\n\n## Article 1.11.2 — Emergency Care\n\n1. A person in need of urgent care shall receive it without discrimination of any kind and without prior inquiry into ability to pay or administrative status.\n2. Facilities receiving public funds shall maintain the emergency capacity that law requires of them, and private facilities shall not refuse urgent care on grounds of payment.\n\n## Article 1.11.3 — Progressive Realization\n\n1. The State shall take deliberate, concrete, and targeted steps, to the maximum of its available resources, toward the full realization of the right of access to healthcare.\n2. A retrogressive measure, meaning a reduction of protection below its existing level, is permissible only where it is temporary, necessary, non-discriminatory, and justified under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 1.11.4 — Minimum Core Obligations\n\n1. At a minimum, the State shall ensure access to primary healthcare, to essential medicines, and to maternal and child health services for everyone within its territory, and shall prioritize underserved regions.\n2. The minimum core is immediate and is not subject to progressive realization; any shortfall shall be identified and remedied with priority.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section distinguishes the immediate minimum core from the progressively realizable remainder, a structure taken from international economic and social rights doctrine and tested in litigation over health rights. Emergency care is made immediate and unconditional because its denial is irreversible in a way that waiting-list delay is not. The bar on retrogression, subject to strict justification, prevents the slow erosion of achieved protection that would otherwise hollow out the right without ever formally repealing it.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.2.1 (Equality Before and Under the Law).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__labor-rights", "path": "01-rights/labor-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.9 — Labor Rights", "md": "# Section 1.9 — Labor Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.9.1 – 1.9.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees freedom of association in labor matters, fair remuneration, the prohibition of forced and child labor, and the right to strike.*\n\n## Article 1.9.1 — Freedom of Association in Labor Matters\n\n1. Every worker and every employer has the right to form and join organizations of the person's own choosing to promote and defend occupational interests.\n2. Organizations of workers and of employers may federate and may affiliate internationally; no one may be compelled to join or to remain outside.\n\n## Article 1.9.2 — Fair Remuneration\n\n1. Every worker has the right to remuneration sufficient for a decent standard of living for the worker and the worker's family.\n2. Women and men shall receive equal pay for work of equal value; the standards implementing this Section are stated in Part 10 (Labor).\n\n## Article 1.9.3 — Prohibition of Forced Labor\n\n1. Slavery, servitude, and forced labor are prohibited and may not be derogated from, in accordance with Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n2. \"Forced labor\" does not include work required in lawful detention under conditions consistent with this Constitution, service of a military character where lawfully imposed with a civilian alternative, or civic obligations of a general nature.\n\n## Article 1.9.4 — Prohibition of Child Labor\n\n1. Work by children is prohibited below the minimum age provided by law and, at any age, in conditions harmful to health, safety, or development.\n2. The worst forms of child labor are prohibited absolutely, and the protection of working minors is a duty of the State and of employers.\n\n## Article 1.9.5 — Right to Strike\n\n1. Workers have the right to strike in defense of their occupational interests, exercised peacefully and as provided by law.\n2. The law may restrict the strike right in essential services only to the extent necessary to protect the life, personal safety, or health of the population, and only with a substitute mechanism for the settlement of disputes; restrictions remain subject to Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Labor rights are stated as fundamental rights rather than as directives of social policy, which entitles them to the full protection of the master limitation clause and to horizontal application against private employers. The definition of forced labor incorporates the internationally recognized exceptions — penal work, military service with a civilian alternative, and ordinary civic duties — so that the prohibition targets compulsion rather than obligation as such. The strike right is paired with a mandatory substitute mechanism in essential services, preserving bargaining power without holding the population hostage.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 1.15.4 (Protection from Violence and Exploitation); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__privacy", "path": "01-rights/privacy.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.5 — Privacy", "md": "# Section 1.5 — Privacy\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.5.1 – 1.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects the privacy of the person, home, communications, and personal data, and subjects intrusions to judicial authorization.*\n\n## Article 1.5.1 — Right to Privacy\n\n1. Every person has the right to privacy, which includes the person's body, home, papers, communications, image, and personal data.\n2. Privacy is protected against interference by the State and, where capable of application, by private persons.\n\n## Article 1.5.2 — Inviolability of the Home\n\n1. The home is inviolable; no entry or search shall take place without the consent of the occupant or a judicial warrant.\n2. A warrant shall issue only on reasonable grounds, shall identify the place and the purpose of the search, and shall be executed as provided by law; the criminal-procedure regime is stated in Part 15 (Criminal Procedure).\n\n## Article 1.5.3 — Confidentiality of Communications\n\n1. The confidentiality of letters, telephone, electronic, and all other communications shall be respected.\n2. Interception or recording is permissible only under a judicial warrant that satisfies Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights), for a defined purpose, and for a defined period.\n\n## Article 1.5.4 — Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination\n\n1. Every person has the right to informational self-determination: to know what personal data concerning the person is processed, by whom, and for what purpose; to obtain rectification; and to a remedy against unlawful processing.\n2. Personal data shall be processed fairly and lawfully, only with consent or another basis provided by law; oversight belongs to the Information Commission, and the statutory regime is stated in Part 17 (Digital Law).\n\n## Article 1.5.5 — Judicial Authorization of Intrusions\n\n1. Searches, surveillance, and other intrusive measures shall be authorized or reviewed by a court, shall be proportionate to a legitimate aim, and shall be documented.\n2. A person subjected to an unlawful intrusion has the right to be informed of it once notification no longer endangers a lawful purpose of investigation, and to an effective remedy.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section constitutionalizes the warrant requirement for the home and for communications so that the cornerstone safeguards of criminal procedure cannot be diluted by ordinary legislation. Informational self-determination is recognized as a distinct dimension of privacy, an approach inspired by the German census decision of 1983 and now reflected in comprehensive data-protection regimes. The duty to inform the subject of an intrusion once secrecy is no longer needed balances investigative effectiveness against the right to know.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.13.3 (Digital Privacy); Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__property-rights", "path": "01-rights/property-rights.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.8 — Property Rights", "md": "# Section 1.8 — Property Rights\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.8.1 – 1.8.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects ownership and inheritance and governs expropriation, restrictions on use, compensation, and judicial review.*\n\n## Article 1.8.1 — Right to Property\n\n1. Every person has the right to own, use, enjoy, and dispose of property lawfully acquired, alone or with others, and to inherit property.\n2. No person shall be arbitrarily deprived of property. Intangible and intellectual interests enjoy protection to the extent provided by law.\n\n## Article 1.8.2 — Expropriation\n\n1. Expropriation is permissible only for a public purpose, only by or under a law, and only against just and prompt compensation.\n2. The public purpose shall be determined by law and shall not serve primarily the benefit of another private person.\n3. Compensation shall reflect the market value of the property at the time of the taking, shall be determined through a transparent procedure, and shall be paid promptly and, where staged, with interest from the date of the taking.\n\n## Article 1.8.3 — Restrictions on Use\n\n1. The use of property may be regulated by law for reasons of public health, safety, the environment, planning, and heritage, subject to Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n2. Regulation that imposes on a particular owner a disproportionate individualized burden equivalent in effect to a taking shall be treated as expropriation and compensated accordingly.\n\n## Article 1.8.4 — Compensation and Review\n\n1. A person whose property is taken or restricted enjoys judicial review of the taking, of the restriction, and of the amount of compensation.\n2. Review shall be accessible and expeditious, and compensation procedures shall not be so slow or uncertain as to nullify the guarantee.\n3. The detailed rules of ownership and succession between private persons are stated in Part 16 (Civil Law).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The classical triad — public purpose, legal basis, compensation — is completed by judicial review and by the rule on disguised expropriation, which prevents the State from achieving through regulation what it could not achieve through formal taking. The market-value benchmark and the requirement of prompt compensation reflect the standards found in international investment law and in modern constitutional texts. Property is protected as an institution of personal freedom rather than as an absolute barrier to land policy, which the affirmative-measures clause of Article 1.2.5 (Affirmative Measures) and planning law mediate.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 0.4.4 (Prohibition of Arbitrariness); Article 1.2.5 (Affirmative Measures).\n"}, {"id": "01-rights__rights-of-children", "path": "01-rights/rights-of-children.md", "part": "01-rights", "partTitle": "Rights", "title": "Section 1.15 — Rights of Children", "md": "# Section 1.15 — Rights of Children\n\n> **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms** · Articles 1.15.1 – 1.15.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the rights of the child: best interests as the paramount consideration, participation, protection from violence and exploitation, and non-derogable essentials.*\n\n## Article 1.15.1 — Rights of the Child\n\n1. Every child has the rights of this Section without discrimination of any kind, irrespective of the child's or the parents' status, including birth, nationality, or residence status.\n2. A child is a person below the age of eighteen years.\n\n## Article 1.15.2 — Best Interests of the Child\n\n1. In all actions concerning a child, whether by public or private institutions, the best interests of the child shall be a paramount consideration.\n2. Proceedings affecting a child shall be adapted to the child's age and situation and shall be decided without undue delay.\n\n## Article 1.15.3 — Participation of Children\n\n1. A child capable of forming views has the right to express those views freely in matters affecting the child, and due weight shall be given to them according to age and maturity.\n2. The child's views shall be heard, directly or through a representative, in judicial and administrative proceedings affecting the child.\n\n## Article 1.15.4 — Protection from Violence and Exploitation\n\n1. Every child has the right to protection from all forms of violence, neglect, exploitation, and trafficking, and from work that jeopardizes health, education, or development, consistent with Article 1.9.4 (Prohibition of Child Labor).\n2. The State shall protect the child from corporal punishment and from other cruel or degrading forms of discipline.\n3. Protection services implementing this Section are stated in Part 22 (Social Policy), and the duties of all institutions toward children in Part 28 (Special Populations).\n\n## Article 1.15.5 — Non-Derogable Essentials\n\n1. Every child has the right, from birth, to a name, to registration, and to acquire a nationality, consistent with Part 02 (Citizenship and Migration).\n2. Every child has the right to family care or, where the family is unavailable, to alternative care consistent with the child's dignity and development.\n3. The protections essential to the child's life, survival, and development may not be derogated from.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The best-interests standard is made paramount, not merely primary, following Article 3 of the Convention on the Rights of the Child, so that competing public interests cannot outweigh the child where the child's interests are central. Participation rights are paired with protection rights, reflecting the modern understanding of children as subjects of rights rather than objects of care. The non-derogable essentials — a name, registration, a nationality, and care — secure the conditions of legal personhood that a stateless or undocumented child cannot assert alone.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.4 (Prohibition of Child Labor); Article 2.1.4 (Prevention of Statelessness); Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__asylum", "path": "02-citizenship/asylum.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.4 — Asylum", "md": "# Section 2.4 — Asylum\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.4.1 – 2.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the right to seek and enjoy asylum, the absolute prohibition of refoulement, a fair status-determination procedure with legal aid, and dignified reception.*\n\n## Article 2.4.1 — Right to Seek and Enjoy Asylum\n\n1. Every person has the right to seek and to enjoy asylum in the Republic from persecution.\n2. No person shall be penalized for entering or remaining in the territory irregularly where the person claims protection without delay or shows good cause for the delay.\n\n## Article 2.4.2 — Non-Refoulement\n\n1. No person shall be removed, returned, extradited, or expelled to a territory where the person faces a real risk of persecution, of torture, or of cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment.\n2. This prohibition is absolute; it admits no exception, no balancing, and no limitation.\n\n## Article 2.4.3 — Fair Status Determination\n\n1. Every protection claim shall be determined individually, in a procedure that is fair and prompt, by a competent authority with decisions subject to independent appeal.\n2. The applicant shall be heard in person, with an interpreter where needed, and shall have access to the evidence relied upon.\n3. An adverse decision may be appealed with suspensive effect where the appeal invokes Article 2.4.2 (Non-Refoulement).\n\n## Article 2.4.4 — Legal Aid and Information\n\n1. An applicant who lacks sufficient means is entitled to state-funded legal aid in the procedure, consistent with the legal-aid regime of Part 05 (The Judiciary).\n2. Applicants shall receive information, in a language they understand, on the procedure, on their rights, and on the assistance available.\n\n## Article 2.4.5 — Dignified Reception\n\n1. Applicants shall receive reception conditions ensuring a dignified standard of living, including shelter, food, basic healthcare, and schooling for children.\n2. Detention of an applicant is permissible only exceptionally, under judicial authorization, and never as a deterrent.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Non-refoulement is drafted as absolute, without balancing or exception, because returning a person to persecution or torture is a harm the Constitution cannot remedy after the fact; the formulation follows the international prohibition of refoulement and the absolute ban on torture. Suspensive appeal rights operationalize the prohibition, since an unappealable removal would render the guarantee illusory. Reception standards and the exceptional status of detention keep the process humane without converting it into an incentive structure.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 1.6.1 (Fair Administrative Action); Article 2.5.1 (Status of Refugees).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__citizenship", "path": "02-citizenship/citizenship.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.1 — Citizenship", "md": "# Section 2.1 — Citizenship\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.1.1 – 2.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes citizenship of the Republic, its acquisition at birth and by descent, its equal standing, and the civic rights and duties it carries.*\n\n## Article 2.1.1 — Citizenship of the Republic\n\n1. There is a single citizenship of the Republic, held on equal terms by all citizens.\n2. Citizenship is the gateway to the political rights of this Constitution; it is acquired, held, and lost only as this Section and Sections 2.6 and 2.7 provide.\n\n## Article 2.1.2 — Citizenship by Birth\n\n1. Every child born in the territory of the Republic is a citizen by birth.\n2. A child born in the territory to parents serving a foreign state in an official capacity is not a citizen by birth, unless the child would otherwise be stateless.\n3. A newborn found in the territory whose parents are unknown is presumed, until the contrary is shown, to have been born in the territory.\n\n## Article 2.1.3 — Citizenship by Descent\n\n1. A child born outside the territory to a citizen parent is a citizen by descent, subject to registration as provided by law.\n2. The law shall keep registration accessible and shall not create a risk of statelessness.\n\n## Article 2.1.4 — Prevention of Statelessness\n\n1. No measure of law or administration shall render a person stateless.\n2. Where a person would otherwise be stateless, the State shall grant citizenship in the manner provided by law, subject only to safeguards against fraud consistent with Article 2.7.2 (Revocation for Fraud).\n\n## Article 2.1.5 — Civic Rights and Duties\n\n1. Citizens have the right to vote and to stand for public office as provided by this Constitution and by law.\n2. Citizens shall uphold this Constitution, comply with the law, and pay the taxes lawfully imposed.\n3. The duties of citizenship shall not diminish any right this Constitution guarantees.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The birth rule is deliberately generous — every child born in the territory is a citizen — with a narrow diplomat exception and a foundling presumption, a combination that makes statelessness by birth practically impossible within the Republic. Equality of citizenship forecloses graduated or tiered civic status, which history shows to be corrosive of both equality and loyalty. The pairing of civic rights with civic duties in one Article signals that membership is reciprocal without converting duties into conditions of rights.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.1 (Equality Before and Under the Law); Article 0.6.2 (Free and Periodic Elections); Article 2.7.4 (Prohibition of Arbitrary Deprivation).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__immigration", "path": "02-citizenship/immigration.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.3 — Immigration", "md": "# Section 2.3 — Immigration\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.3.1 – 2.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section allocates competence over entry and residence, guarantees lawful migration channels and family unity, and defines the rights of lawfully resident non-citizens and the due process of removal.*\n\n## Article 2.3.1 — Legislative Competence\n\n1. The entry, residence, and removal of non-citizens are governed by law adopted by the Parliament.\n2. The executive shall apply that law; administrative discretion shall be structured by published criteria and shall remain subject to review.\n\n## Article 2.3.2 — Lawful Migration Channels\n\n1. The law shall provide clear, accessible, and non-discriminatory channels for lawful entry and residence, including for work, study, family, and protection.\n2. A non-citizen admitted under a lawful channel is entitled to the rights attached to that status and to every right of this Constitution that is not reserved to citizens.\n\n## Article 2.3.3 — Family Unity\n\n1. The family is entitled to protection, and measures affecting entry or residence shall be interpreted and applied so as to preserve family unity.\n2. A resident may apply for the admission or continued residence of close family members; a refusal is subject to the test of Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) in light of the family's rights.\n\n## Article 2.3.4 — Rights of Lawfully Resident Non-Citizens\n\n1. Non-citizens lawfully resident enjoy treatment equal to that of citizens in work, social security, housing, education, and healthcare, except where this Constitution reserves a matter to citizens.\n2. A distinction between citizens and lawfully resident non-citizens is permissible only for political rights and such public functions as this Constitution or a law reserves to citizens; every other distinction requires justification under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 2.3.5 — Due Process in Removal\n\n1. No non-citizen shall be removed except by a reasoned decision under law, communicated in a language the person understands, and subject to review.\n2. Removal shall not take place while review is pending where the person invokes Article 2.4.2 (Non-Refoulement).\n3. Detention pending removal shall be judicially supervised, shall last no longer than necessary, and shall not be punitive.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section allocates migration control to ordinary legislation while embedding due-process and equality constraints in the Constitution, so that a politically volatile field remains governed by law rather than by ad hoc executive practice. The equality clause draws a principled line — political rights for citizens, civil and social rights for everyone lawfully present — which mirrors the comparative consensus on resident non-citizens. The suspensive effect of a refoulement claim protects the absolute character of Article 2.4.2 without freezing the migration system.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 2.4.2 (Non-Refoulement).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__loss-of-citizenship", "path": "02-citizenship/loss-of-citizenship.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.7 — Loss of Citizenship", "md": "# Section 2.7 — Loss of Citizenship\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.7.1 – 2.7.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section regulates the loss of citizenship: voluntary renunciation, revocation only for fraud in acquisition, judicial review, and the prohibition of arbitrary deprivation.*\n\n## Article 2.7.1 — Voluntary Renunciation\n\n1. A citizen may renounce citizenship in the manner provided by law.\n2. Renunciation shall not be accepted where it would render the person stateless.\n\n## Article 2.7.2 — Revocation for Fraud\n\n1. Citizenship may be revoked only where it was acquired by fraud, false representation, or concealment of a material fact, and only within a reasonable period after its acquisition.\n2. Revocation shall be decided by a competent authority on notice to the person concerned and shall state its reasons.\n3. Conduct occurring after acquisition shall not ground revocation; changes of belief, residence, or political loyalty shall never ground revocation.\n\n## Article 2.7.3 — Judicial Review of Deprivation\n\n1. A person affected by a decision to revoke citizenship has the right to full judicial review, including of the facts, the law, and the proportionality of the measure, before an independent court.\n2. A decision to revoke shall not take final effect until review has been available; where immediate effect is indispensable, the person retains the right to review without delay.\n\n## Article 2.7.4 — Prohibition of Arbitrary Deprivation\n\n1. Citizenship shall not be taken away as punishment for an offense, on political grounds, or on any collective basis.\n2. No measure shall render a person stateless, in accordance with Article 2.1.4 (Prevention of Statelessness).\n3. A measure affecting a parent shall not of itself affect the citizenship of the parent's child.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section separates voluntary renunciation from revocation and confines revocation to fraud in acquisition within a reasonable period, avoiding the open-ended deprivation grounds that recent comparative practice has shown to be both divisive and error-prone. The absolute bar on statelessness reflects the international treaties on statelessness and treats citizenship as the precondition for exercising every other right. Punitive and collective deprivation are excluded outright because citizenship is a legal status, not an instrument of discipline.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 2.1.4 (Prevention of Statelessness); Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial); Article 0.4.4 (Prohibition of Arbitrariness).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__nationality", "path": "02-citizenship/nationality.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.2 — Nationality", "md": "# Section 2.2 — Nationality\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.2.1 – 2.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the single nationality of the Republic, the registration of births, proof of status, and the independence of nationality from marriage.*\n\n## Article 2.2.1 — Single Nationality\n\n1. The nationality of the Republic is single and uniform throughout the territory.\n2. No Province, Region, or Municipality may confer, condition, or deny nationality or any element of civic status.\n3. The State shall maintain a reliable register of nationality and citizenship, accessible for correction as provided by law.\n\n## Article 2.2.2 — Registration of Births\n\n1. Every birth shall be registered promptly, without charge, and in the manner provided by law; the duty to register rests on the State as much as on the parents.\n2. The register may record only the data required to establish status and identity, and its treatment of data is subject to Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination).\n3. Where a birth is registered late, a procedure shall confirm the facts without penalizing the child.\n\n## Article 2.2.3 — Proof of Status\n\n1. Every citizen shall be issued, on request and without charge, a document evidencing citizenship, and an application shall be decided within a reasonable time.\n2. Doubt about a person's status shall be resolved by the interpretation most favorable to citizenship and least likely to cause statelessness.\n\n## Article 2.2.4 — Nationality and Marriage\n\n1. Neither marriage nor its dissolution shall affect the nationality of a spouse.\n2. The law may shorten the residence period required for the naturalization of a spouse on fair and published conditions, without distinction between women and men and without regard to the spouse's origin.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Uniform nationality prevents the fragmentation of civic status along regional lines and reinforces the unitary design of Part 00 (Foundation). Free and prompt birth registration is treated as a constitutional duty because unregistered births are the principal gateway to de facto statelessness, and the late-registration rule shifts its burdens away from the child. The marriage-neutrality rule reflects the international principle that women and men hold nationality independently of marital status. Proof of status complements registration by ensuring that citizenship can always be exercised, not merely held.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination); Article 2.1.4 (Prevention of Statelessness); Article 0.8.5 (Hierarchy of Norms).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__naturalization", "path": "02-citizenship/naturalization.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.6 — Naturalization", "md": "# Section 2.6 — Naturalization\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.6.1 – 2.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs residence-based naturalization: reasonable integration requirements, non-discrimination, and reasoned decisions subject to review.*\n\n## Article 2.6.1 — Right to Apply for Naturalization\n\n1. A non-citizen who has lawfully and habitually resided in the Republic for the period provided by law may apply for citizenship by naturalization.\n2. The residence period shall be reasonable, shall be the same for all applicants, and may be reduced only on fair and published conditions.\n3. No application shall be refused on a ground not provided by law.\n\n## Article 2.6.2 — Integration Requirements\n\n1. The law may require knowledge of a national language and of the basic constitutional order, tested without discrimination.\n2. Requirements shall be reasonable and proportionate: the standard shall be attainable by a person of ordinary diligence, shall accommodate age and disability, and shall not operate to exclude applicants on grounds prohibited by Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n\n## Article 2.6.3 — Non-Discrimination in Naturalization\n\n1. Naturalization shall be decided on uniform criteria for all applicants, without regard to national or ethnic origin, religion, or any ground listed in Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n2. Fees shall be moderate and shall not operate as a barrier to applicants of modest means.\n\n## Article 2.6.4 — Decision and Review\n\n1. An application shall be decided within a reasonable time by a reasoned decision.\n2. An unsuccessful applicant may seek review of the decision before a court.\n3. A successful applicant shall take a solemn undertaking to respect the constitutional order; the undertaking imposes no duty beyond those this Constitution already establishes.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section constitutionalizes the process rather than the threshold, fixing uniform, proportionate, and reviewable standards while leaving the residence period to legislation, a division that has allowed comparative systems to adjust integration policy without reopening the constitution. The attainability standard for civic-knowledge tests responds to the documented risk that such tests become covert filters. The solemn undertaking is drafted as a reaffirmation of existing duties, not as a source of new obligations.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 2.2.4 (Nationality and Marriage).\n"}, {"id": "02-citizenship__refugees", "path": "02-citizenship/refugees.md", "part": "02-citizenship", "partTitle": "Citizenship", "title": "Section 2.5 — Refugees", "md": "# Section 2.5 — Refugees\n\n> **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration** · Articles 2.5.1 – 2.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the protection regime for recognized refugees: equal status, rights to work and education, durable solutions, and integration support.*\n\n## Article 2.5.1 — Status of Refugees\n\n1. A person recognized as a refugee shall be granted protection in the Republic and shall enjoy every right of this Constitution and the additional rights of this Section.\n2. Refugees shall not be discriminated against on any ground, and their status shall be documented without charge.\n3. Identity and travel documents shall be issued to refugees as provided by law.\n\n## Article 2.5.2 — Rights to Work and Education\n\n1. Recognized refugees have the right to work and to engage in self-employment on the same terms as lawfully resident non-citizens under Article 2.3.4 (Rights of Lawfully Resident Non-Citizens).\n2. The children of refugees have the right to education on the same terms as the children of citizens, including basic education free of charge under Article 1.10.2 (Free and Compulsory Basic Education).\n3. Refugees have access to healthcare on the terms applicable to lawfully resident non-citizens.\n\n## Article 2.5.3 — Durable Solutions\n\n1. The State shall pursue durable solutions for refugees, including voluntary return in safety and dignity, local integration, and, in cooperation with international organizations, resettlement of refugees from abroad.\n2. Return shall occur only where it is genuinely voluntary; no refugee shall be pressured to return.\n\n## Article 2.5.4 — Integration Support\n\n1. The State shall provide language instruction, employment services, and civic orientation to enable refugees to participate in society.\n2. Support shall be structured toward self-reliance and shall not be administered so as to produce prolonged dependence or marginalization.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section treats refugee protection as a regime with an end: durable solutions — voluntary return, local integration, or resettlement — are constitutionalized so that camps and temporary status cannot become a warehouse without exit. Rights to work and education are the operational core, since exclusion from both is the chief cause of prolonged dependency. The framing follows the international refugee regime while leaving quotas, procedures, and program design to ordinary law.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 2.3.4 (Rights of Lawfully Resident Non-Citizens); Article 1.10.2 (Free and Compulsory Basic Education).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__anti-corruption", "path": "03-legislature/anti-corruption.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.10 — Member Integrity and Anti-Corruption", "md": "# Section 3.10 — Member Integrity and Anti-Corruption\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.10.1 – 3.10.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section imposes duties of integrity on members of the Parliament — disclosure, recusal, and accountability — and links the Houses to the Anti-Corruption Commission.*\n\n## Article 3.10.1 — Duty of Integrity\n\n1. Members shall exercise their functions in the public interest.\n2. A member shall not solicit or accept any gift, benefit, or promise offered in connection with the member's functions.\n3. Each House shall adopt a code of conduct and shall enforce it.\n\n## Article 3.10.2 — Asset Disclosure\n\n1. A member shall declare the member's assets and liabilities upon taking office, annually, and upon leaving office.\n2. Declarations shall be filed in a public registry maintained under the code of conduct.\n3. A member shall also declare assets of the member's household where they may be affected by the member's functions.\n\n## Article 3.10.3 — Recusal\n\n1. A member who has a personal interest in a matter before the House shall disclose that interest to the House.\n2. The member shall withdraw from the deliberation and the vote on that matter.\n3. The disclosure and the withdrawal shall be recorded in the minutes.\n\n## Article 3.10.4 — Sanctions and Expulsion\n\n1. A House may impose graduated sanctions on a member for a serious breach of the code of conduct.\n2. Expulsion requires a two-thirds majority of the members of the House.\n3. Expulsion shall terminate the mandate and shall entail loss of eligibility to sit in that legislature.\n\n## Article 3.10.5 — Cooperation with the Anti-Corruption Commission\n\n1. The Houses shall refer suspected offences of corruption to the Anti-Corruption Commission and to the Public Prosecution Service.\n2. Parliamentary privilege shall not bar the investigation or prosecution of corruption offences.\n3. The Anti-Corruption Commission may report to the House concerned on integrity matters, within the limits of individual privacy and the presumption of innocence.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Public asset disclosure converts abstract integrity into checkable fact, and recusal rules protect legislative decisions from private capture. The express statement that privilege never bars corruption prosecutions closes the most abused loophole in parliamentary immunity systems, a loophole likewise closed in the asset-declaration regimes of several Latin American constitutions. Expulsion is deliberately demanding — a two-thirds majority — so that it cannot be misused to purge political opponents for mere dissent.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.9.3 (Purpose and Limits); Article 3.7.5 (Oversight and Sanctions); Article 5.8.5 (Relationship to Police and the Anti-Corruption Commission).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__campaign-finance", "path": "03-legislature/campaign-finance.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.7 — Campaign Finance", "md": "# Section 3.7 — Campaign Finance\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.7.1 – 3.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section secures the transparency and integrity of the financing of political parties and election campaigns, from private donations to audited public funding.*\n\n## Article 3.7.1 — Disclosure of Donations\n\n1. Parties and candidates shall keep records of every donation received.\n2. They shall report the sources and amounts of donations to the Electoral Commission at the intervals and in the form fixed by law.\n3. The Commission shall publish the reports, protecting the identity of small donors as provided by law.\n\n## Article 3.7.2 — Limits on Contributions\n\n1. The law shall fix the maximum amount that any person may donate to a party or candidate within a calendar year.\n2. The law may fix limits on campaign expenditure.\n3. Limits shall be proportionate to the protection of equal opportunity in elections and shall not effectively prevent political participation.\n\n## Article 3.7.3 — Prohibited Donations\n\n1. Anonymous donations above a de minimis amount fixed by law shall be prohibited and shall be surrendered to the State.\n2. Donations from foreign States or from entities controlled by them shall be prohibited.\n3. Donations from public bodies and from entities holding contracts with the State shall be regulated by law to prevent the conversion of public resources into party advantage.\n\n## Article 3.7.4 — Public Funding\n\n1. Public funding of parties may be provided by law.\n2. Public funding shall be audited, shall follow a transparent formula, and shall not be allocated to reward the incumbent Government.\n3. Eligibility for public funding shall be conditional on compliance with the disclosure duties of this Section.\n\n## Article 3.7.5 — Oversight and Sanctions\n\n1. The Electoral Commission shall oversee compliance with this Section.\n2. Sanctions shall be graduated and proportionate, from warnings to the forfeiture of public funding and, for grave violations, referral to the Public Prosecution Service.\n3. Sanctions shall be subject to judicial review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Disclosure is treated as the primary tool: sunlight on donations deters capture at a lower cost to political speech than sweeping spending bans. The Canadian model of disclosure combined with contribution limits illustrates this balance in settled practice. Public funding is made conditional on transparency so that State money cannot subsidize opacity, and enforcement is left to the same independent body that administers the elections themselves.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.6.3 (Transparency of Financing); Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration); Article 3.10.2 (Asset Disclosure).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__elections", "path": "03-legislature/elections.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.4 — Elections to Parliament", "md": "# Section 3.4 — Elections to Parliament\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.4.1 – 3.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section fixes the timing of parliamentary elections, the mandate and vacancy of members, and the administration and validity of elections to both Houses.*\n\n## Article 3.4.1 — Timing of Elections\n\n1. Ordinary elections to the Lower House shall be held every fourth year.\n2. Elections to the Upper House shall be held every second year in accordance with Article 3.3.3 (Staggered Terms and Vacancies).\n3. Election dates shall be fixed by law. Early elections shall be held only under Article 3.2.5 (Dissolution and Early Elections).\n\n## Article 3.4.2 — Term and Mandate\n\n1. The mandate of a member shall begin upon validation of the member's election and shall end upon the first sitting of a newly elected House, or upon earlier cessation under this Section.\n2. The mandate shall be exercised freely. No member may be recalled by any body otherwise than as provided by this Constitution.\n3. Members shall declare their assets in accordance with Article 3.10.2 (Asset Disclosure).\n\n## Article 3.4.3 — Vacancies and By-Elections\n\n1. A vacancy in the Lower House shall be filled by replacement from the party list under Article 3.2.4 (Vacancies).\n2. A vacancy in the Upper House shall be filled by election by the Provincial Council concerned, for the remainder of the term.\n3. Where a by-election is required, the Electoral Commission shall conduct it without delay.\n\n## Article 3.4.4 — Administration and Validity\n\n1. The Electoral Commission shall have exclusive competence to organize and conduct elections to both Houses.\n2. The Commission shall certify the results and shall publish them together with the counting records.\n3. The validity of parliamentary elections shall be determined, on appeal, by the Constitutional Court under Part 06.\n\n## Article 3.4.5 — Loss of Seat\n\n1. A member shall lose the seat upon loss of eligibility, upon resignation accepted by the House, upon expulsion under Article 3.10.4 (Sanctions and Expulsion), or upon a final conviction of an offence that entails such loss by law.\n2. Decisions on the loss of a seat shall be subject to judicial review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section deals only with mandate mechanics — timing, vacancy, and validity — while the electoral system itself is governed by Part 06; the division keeps chamber rules and electoral rules separately amendable. Replacing members from the same list, rather than by new votes, respects the choice the electorate already made and keeps representation continuous. Final judicial determination of validity, instead of self-judgment by the Houses, follows the principle that electoral disputes demand an independent arbiter.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration); Article 3.2.4 (Vacancies); Article 3.3.3 (Staggered Terms and Vacancies).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__electoral-commission", "path": "03-legislature/electoral-commission.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.5 — The Electoral Commission", "md": "# Section 3.5 — The Electoral Commission\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.5.1 – 3.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states the constitutional status, independence, and exclusive electoral role of the Electoral Commission within the parliamentary setting.*\n\n## Article 3.5.1 — Constitutional Status\n\n1. The Electoral Commission shall be an independent constitutional institution.\n2. The Commission shall not form part of the Government, the Parliament, or any other organ of the State.\n3. The Commission shall perform its functions in the public interest and with impartiality.\n4. The Commission shall be self-governing in its administrative affairs under this Constitution and the law.\n\n## Article 3.5.2 — Independence\n\n1. The Commission shall exercise its functions independently and shall not be subject to direction in individual matters.\n2. Its members shall serve fixed terms and may be removed only for cause in the manner provided for the independent institutions under Part 20.\n3. The Parliament shall appropriate resources sufficient for the independent performance of the Commission's functions.\n\n## Article 3.5.3 — Exclusive Administration\n\n1. The Commission shall have exclusive competence to organize, conduct, and supervise elections to the Lower House and the Upper House.\n2. The Commission shall maintain the integrity of the electoral process for parliamentary elections, including the registration of voters and of candidates.\n3. No other organ may interfere with the conduct of an election while it is underway.\n\n## Article 3.5.4 — Accountability and Cooperation\n\n1. The Commission shall report annually to the Parliament on the administration of parliamentary elections.\n2. All organs of the State shall provide the Commission with the information and assistance it requires for the discharge of its functions.\n3. Decisions of the Commission shall state reasons and shall be subject to appeal under Part 06.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Placing the Commission outside the three classic branches prevents the incumbent Government from refereeing the very contest that could replace it. This Section states only the Commission's status and its role in parliamentary elections; composition, tenure, and general accountability are fixed uniformly with the other independent institutions in Part 20, so that one coherent constitutional design governs all of them.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.4.4 (Administration and Validity); Article 3.7.5 (Oversight and Sanctions); Article 3.4.1 (Timing of Elections).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__legislative-process", "path": "03-legislature/legislative-process.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.8 — The Legislative Process", "md": "# Section 3.8 — The Legislative Process\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.8.1 – 3.8.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section orders the path of a bill from introduction through committee scrutiny and public consultation to passage, presidential assent, and publication.*\n\n## Article 3.8.1 — Introduction of Bills\n\n1. Bills may be introduced by the Government, by a member of either House, or by a committee of either House.\n2. A citizens' initiative may lead to the introduction of a bill under the conditions of Part 06.\n\n## Article 3.8.2 — Committee Scrutiny\n\n1. Every bill shall be referred to a committee of the House of introduction before its second reading.\n2. Committees may hold public hearings, invite evidence, and assess the financial, social, and environmental impact of a bill.\n3. A committee shall report to the House with a recommendation or with amendments.\n\n## Article 3.8.3 — Readings and Public Consultation\n\n1. Each bill shall pass through three readings in each House.\n2. Drafts of Government bills shall be published for public consultation for a period fixed by law before the second reading.\n3. A House may shorten a stage only for urgency, recording the reasons in the minutes.\n\n## Article 3.8.4 — Joint Passage\n\n1. A bill shall be passed by both Houses in the same text.\n2. Where the Upper House delays an ordinary bill or a money bill, the procedures of Articles 3.3.4 (Powers over Ordinary Bills) and 3.3.5 (Powers over Money Bills) shall apply.\n\n## Article 3.8.5 — Assent and Referral\n\n1. A passed bill shall be presented to the President for assent.\n2. The President shall assent within thirty days or shall refer the bill back to the Parliament once, with written reasons.\n3. If the Parliament re-adopts the bill in the same text, the President shall assent within fifteen days.\n4. A referral under this Article may be exercised once in respect of each bill.\n\n## Article 3.8.6 — Publication and Entry into Force\n\n1. An Act shall be promulgated and published in the official gazette without delay.\n2. No Act shall enter into force before its publication.\n3. An Act may postpone its entry into force to a later day fixed in the Act itself.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Committee-centered scrutiny with published consultation inserts expertise and public voice before votes are disciplined, while the urgency exception is deliberately narrow and must be minuted. The single presidential referral is a pause, not a veto: the Parliament overcomes it by simple re-adoption, a design close to the deferential reference powers found in some parliamentary constitutions, such as that of Ireland. Publication as a condition of entry into force completes the principle of legality on the procedural side.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.3.4 (Powers over Ordinary Bills); Article 4.1.4 (Reserve Powers); Article 3.1.4 (Quorum and Decisions).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__lower-house", "path": "03-legislature/lower-house.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.2 — The Lower House", "md": "# Section 3.2 — The Lower House\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.2.1 – 3.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section fixes the composition, electoral principle, eligibility, vacancies, and dissolution of the Lower House, the chamber upon which the confidence of the Government depends.*\n\n## Article 3.2.1 — Composition and Term\n\n1. The Lower House shall consist of 400 members.\n2. Members shall be elected for a term of four years.\n3. The term shall begin with the first sitting of the House after an ordinary election and shall end when the newly elected House first meets.\n\n## Article 3.2.2 — Electoral Principle\n\n1. Members shall be elected by proportional representation with open candidate lists in Province-wide constituencies.\n2. A political party shall obtain at least 3% of the valid national votes cast to be allocated seats.\n3. The details of the electoral system shall be regulated by law in accordance with Part 06.\n\n## Article 3.2.3 — Eligibility\n\n1. Every citizen entitled to vote in elections to the Lower House shall be eligible for election.\n2. The law may prescribe limited grounds of incompatibility between the mandate and other public functions.\n3. Incompatibilities shall be construed narrowly and shall serve only the integrity of the mandate.\n\n## Article 3.2.4 — Vacancies\n\n1. A seat shall become vacant on death, resignation, loss of eligibility, or loss of the mandate under this Constitution.\n2. A vacant seat shall be filled by the next eligible candidate of the same party list, in the order determined by the voters' preferences at the last election.\n3. Where no eligible candidate remains, the seat shall be filled by a by-election under Article 3.4.3 (Vacancies and By-Elections).\n\n## Article 3.2.5 — Dissolution and Early Elections\n\n1. The Lower House may dissolve itself by a resolution adopted by a majority of its members.\n2. Where the Lower House fails to elect a Prime Minister within the period fixed in Article 4.2.1 (Election and Confidence), the President shall dissolve the House.\n3. Early elections shall be held within sixty days of dissolution.\n4. A dissolution shall not be repeated on the same grounds during the following legislature.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Open-list proportional representation with a modest 3% threshold balances proportionality against governability; comparative practice, such as the five percent threshold of Germany, shows how sharply the chosen level shapes party fragmentation. List-order replacement keeps seats filled without fresh elections while preserving the preferences the voters actually expressed. The rules on self-dissolution and failed government formation leave control over the chamber's end with the chamber itself, not with the executive.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.4.1 (Timing of Elections); Article 4.2.1 (Election and Confidence); Article 3.1.2 (Composition).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__parliament", "path": "03-legislature/parliament.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.1 — The Parliament", "md": "# Section 3.1 — The Parliament\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.1.1 – 3.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Parliament of the Republic as the supreme legislative authority and orders its composition, sessions, decisions, and presiding officers.*\n\n## Article 3.1.1 — Legislative Power\n\n1. The legislative power of the Republic shall be vested in the Parliament.\n2. The Parliament shall consist of two Houses, the Lower House and the Upper House.\n3. The Parliament shall be the supreme legislative authority of the Republic, subject only to this Constitution.\n4. No organ of the State may exercise legislative power except as conferred by this Constitution or delegated by an Act of Parliament.\n\n## Article 3.1.2 — Composition\n\n1. The Lower House shall comprise 400 members elected directly by the people.\n2. The Upper House shall comprise three members for each Province, elected by the Provincial Councils.\n3. Members of each House shall represent the whole people of the Republic.\n4. Members shall not be bound by instructions and shall be guided by their conscience and by this Constitution.\n\n## Article 3.1.3 — Sessions and Calendars\n\n1. Each House shall assemble without convocation on the day fixed by law and shall sit for such periods as its business requires.\n2. Each House shall adopt an annual legislative calendar and shall make it public.\n3. Sittings shall be public. A House may sit in closed session only for reasons of public security or the protection of private life, under its rules and with the reasons recorded in the minutes.\n\n## Article 3.1.4 — Quorum and Decisions\n\n1. Each House may act only if a majority of its members is present.\n2. Decisions shall be taken by a majority of the votes cast, unless this Constitution requires a different majority.\n3. If the votes are equally divided, the proposal shall be rejected.\n4. Voting shall be personal. The rules of each House shall provide for recorded votes at the request of a fraction of the members present.\n\n## Article 3.1.5 — Presiding Officers\n\n1. Each House shall elect a presiding officer and deputy presiding officers from among its members.\n2. The presiding officer shall maintain order, apply the rules of the House, and represent the House in its external relations.\n3. Joint sittings of both Houses shall be presided over by the presiding officer of the Lower House.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Bicameralism is deliberately asymmetric: the Lower House is the confidence chamber from which the Government flows, while the Upper House gives the Provinces a delaying voice without an absolute veto. The equal-division rule withholds a casting vote from any presiding officer, preserving neutrality in the chamber where government is made and unmade. Public sittings with narrow, recorded exceptions follow the presumption of transparency that runs through the whole Constitution.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.2.1 (Composition and Term); Article 3.3.1 (Composition); Article 3.8.4 (Joint Passage).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__parliamentary-privileges", "path": "03-legislature/parliamentary-privileges.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.9 — Parliamentary Privileges", "md": "# Section 3.9 — Parliamentary Privileges\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.9.1 – 3.9.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects freedom of speech in parliamentary proceedings and grants limited immunity, while confining both privileges to the protection of the institution.*\n\n## Article 3.9.1 — Freedom of Speech in Proceedings\n\n1. A member shall not be held liable in any proceeding, court, or tribunal outside the House for a vote given, a statement made, or a report presented in the proceedings of the House.\n2. The protection shall extend to the publication of the proceedings by order of the House.\n3. The protection shall not extend to statements repeated or published outside the proceedings.\n\n## Article 3.9.2 — Limited Immunity\n\n1. A member may not be detained or prosecuted for an act performed in the exercise of the mandate, except when apprehended in the commission of a serious offence.\n2. Immunity shall lapse when the mandate ends. It shall not bar investigation, indictment, or trial after the mandate has ended.\n\n## Article 3.9.3 — Purpose and Limits\n\n1. Privileges exist to protect the function of parliamentary representation, not the personal interests of members.\n2. No privilege may be invoked to shield corruption, fraud, or other serious wrongdoing.\n3. Privileges shall be construed narrowly.\n\n## Article 3.9.4 — Waiver and Review\n\n1. The House concerned may lift immunity at the reasoned request of the Public Prosecution Service, by a majority of its members.\n2. A member may waive the member's own privilege.\n3. A decision refusing to lift immunity may be reviewed by the Constitutional Court for abuse of power.\n\n## Article 3.9.5 — Internal Order and Contempt\n\n1. Each House shall control its own proceedings and may discipline conduct that obstructs them.\n2. Sanctions for contempt shall be proportionate, reasoned, and recorded.\n3. The rules of each House shall provide for review of disciplinary decisions by an internal body before recourse to the courts.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The guarantee protects institutional boldness: a member must be able to speak the truth about the powerful without fear of suit, and the House must be able to publish what is said. Modern drafting, following the narrower immunities of recent parliamentary constitutions such as the German Basic Law, confines protection to the proceedings themselves and expressly excludes its use as a corruption shield. The final review of refusals to lift immunity keeps the privilege from becoming a partisan veto over prosecution.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.10.4 (Sanctions and Expulsion); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts); Article 3.1.5 (Presiding Officers).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__political-parties", "path": "03-legislature/political-parties.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.6 — Political Parties", "md": "# Section 3.6 — Political Parties\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.6.1 – 3.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the free formation and democratic organization of political parties and permits their prohibition only on narrow grounds.*\n\n## Article 3.6.1 — Freedom of Formation\n\n1. Citizens may form political parties freely.\n2. Parties shall not require prior authorization. Registration with the Electoral Commission shall serve only the administration of elections.\n3. Parties shall participate in the formation of the political will of the people.\n\n## Article 3.6.2 — Internal Democracy\n\n1. The statutes of a party shall provide for the periodic election of its governing bodies and for the participation of its members in its decisions.\n2. Candidate selection shall follow transparent and democratic procedures, consistent with open-list elections under Article 3.2.2 (Electoral Principle).\n3. The statutes of a party shall be public.\n\n## Article 3.6.3 — Transparency of Financing\n\n1. Parties shall keep proper accounts of their income and expenditure.\n2. Parties shall publish audited accounts annually and shall disclose donations in accordance with Article 3.7.1 (Disclosure of Donations).\n\n## Article 3.6.4 — Prohibition\n\n1. A party may be prohibited only where its statutes or its conduct aim to abolish the democratic order by violence, or where it systematically incites violence against persons or groups.\n2. Prohibition shall be pronounced only by the Constitutional Court.\n3. Measures short of prohibition shall be preferred where they can avert the threat effectively.\n\n## Article 3.6.5 — Parliamentary Groups\n\n1. Members may associate in parliamentary groups under the rules of each House.\n2. The rules shall secure the rights of small groups and of members who belong to no group.\n3. Public funding of parliamentary groups shall follow the rules of Article 3.7.4 (Public Funding).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The narrow exception for violent anti-democratic parties, known from the constitutional practice of Germany, is confined to violence, requires a judicial pronouncement, and is softened by a preference for lesser measures, so that it cannot become a tool against lawful dissent. Regulating parties' internal democracy is justified by their constitutional role in forming the political will of the people, a role no ordinary private association shares.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.7.1 (Disclosure of Donations); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction); Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration).\n"}, {"id": "03-legislature__upper-house", "path": "03-legislature/upper-house.md", "part": "03-legislature", "partTitle": "Legislature", "title": "Section 3.3 — The Upper House", "md": "# Section 3.3 — The Upper House\n\n> **Part 03 — The Legislature** · Articles 3.3.1 – 3.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Upper House as the chamber of the Provinces, orders its staggered election, and confines its powers to the delay of legislation.*\n\n## Article 3.3.1 — Composition\n\n1. The Upper House shall consist of three members for each Province.\n2. The number of Provinces shall be fixed by law within the principles of Part 07.\n3. Members shall represent the interests of their Province and the people of the Republic.\n\n## Article 3.3.2 — Election by Provincial Councils\n\n1. The members for each Province shall be elected by its Provincial Council by secret ballot.\n2. Provincial law shall govern the method of election, subject to the principles of free and equal election.\n3. The Upper House shall not be dissolved.\n\n## Article 3.3.3 — Staggered Terms and Vacancies\n\n1. Members shall serve a term of six years.\n2. One member of each Province's delegation shall be elected every second year, so that the terms are staggered.\n3. A vacancy shall be filled for the remainder of the term by election under clause 2 of Article 3.3.2.\n\n## Article 3.3.4 — Powers over Ordinary Bills\n\n1. The Upper House may postpone its assent to an ordinary bill for up to ninety days by a resolution stating its objections.\n2. If the Lower House re-adopts the bill after the expiry of the delay, the bill shall be deemed passed.\n3. The Upper House shall have no power to defeat an ordinary bill.\n\n## Article 3.3.5 — Powers over Money Bills\n\n1. The Upper House may delay a money bill for up to twenty-one days.\n2. A bill whose sole purpose is the imposition, alteration, or repeal of a tax, an appropriation, a loan, or the management of public debt shall be a money bill.\n3. The presiding officer of the Lower House shall certify money bills, subject to a final determination by the Constitutional Court.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Three members per Province give each territorial unit an equal voice inside a unitary state, and the two-year staggering of six-year terms provides continuity while preventing wholesale turnover. The ninety-day delay for ordinary bills, similar to the delaying power of the Irish second chamber, allows reconsideration without enabling obstruction. The shorter twenty-one-day window for money bills protects the financial primacy of the confidence chamber, where the budget's political authors sit.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.8.4 (Joint Passage); Article 3.4.1 (Timing of Elections); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__cabinet", "path": "04-executive/cabinet.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.3 — The Cabinet", "md": "# Section 4.3 — The Cabinet\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.3.1 – 4.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes the Cabinet: the appointment of ministers from among members of the Parliament, collective responsibility, and the end of the Cabinet's mandate.*\n\n## Article 4.3.1 — Appointment of Ministers\n\n1. The President shall appoint and dismiss ministers on the proposal of the Prime Minister.\n2. Ministers shall be members of the Parliament at the time of appointment.\n3. The law may permit the appointment of a limited number of ministers who are not members of the Parliament; such ministers shall obtain a seat within six months or leave office.\n\n## Article 4.3.2 — Collective Responsibility\n\n1. The Cabinet shall decide the general policy of the Government.\n2. Ministers shall bear collective responsibility for Cabinet decisions and shall defend them before the Parliament.\n3. A minister who cannot support a Cabinet decision may resign; the resignation shall not suspend the decision.\n\n## Article 4.3.3 — Deliberation and Records\n\n1. The Cabinet shall be convened and chaired by the Prime Minister.\n2. Its deliberations shall be confidential; its decisions shall be recorded in minutes.\n3. The law may provide for Cabinet committees and for written procedure on routine matters.\n\n## Article 4.3.4 — Portfolio Assignment\n\n1. The Prime Minister shall allocate portfolios among the ministers and may reallocate them.\n2. Every reorganization of portfolios shall be notified to the Lower House without delay.\n\n## Article 4.3.5 — End of Mandate\n\n1. The mandate of the Cabinet shall end on the first sitting of a newly elected Lower House, on the resignation or death of the Prime Minister, or on the loss of confidence.\n2. A departing Cabinet shall manage only current business until a new Prime Minister is appointed.\n3. During that period it shall not make major appointments or adopt measures of long-term effect.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Collective responsibility gives the Parliament a single accountable government while leaving ministers real autonomy within their portfolios; the express right to resign disciplines the solidarity from within rather than abolishing it. The six-month rule for non-member ministers preserves parliamentary government against the gradual import of technocrats. The caretaker limits codify the widely shared convention that a departing government must not bind its successor.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.2.1 (Election and Confidence); Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny); Article 4.4.1 (Creation and Abolition by Law).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__civil-service", "path": "04-executive/civil-service.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.5 — The Public Service", "md": "# Section 4.5 — The Public Service\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.5.1 – 4.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes the Public Service of the Republic around merit, political neutrality, integrity, and protection from arbitrary dismissal.*\n\n## Article 4.5.1 — Merit Recruitment\n\n1. Admission to the Public Service shall be by open competition on the basis of merit, ability, and integrity.\n2. Public employment shall be open to all citizens on equal terms.\n3. The law shall regulate recruitment, advancement, and remuneration in the Public Service.\n\n## Article 4.5.2 — Political Neutrality\n\n1. Public servants shall act impartially and shall serve each successive Government with equal loyalty.\n2. Public servants shall not use their office for partisan purposes.\n3. The law may restrict the political activities of public servants in proportion to the sensitivity of their duties.\n\n## Article 4.5.3 — Integrity Codes\n\n1. The law shall provide codes of conduct for the Public Service.\n2. Senior public servants shall declare their assets on terms comparable to those of Article 3.10.2 (Asset Disclosure).\n3. The law shall protect persons who report wrongdoing within the Public Service in good faith.\n\n## Article 4.5.4 — Protection from Arbitrary Dismissal\n\n1. A public servant shall not be dismissed except for just cause established by a fair procedure.\n2. A public servant shall be heard before dismissal and may appeal to a court.\n3. Disciplinary measures shall be proportionate and reasoned.\n\n## Article 4.5.5 — Accountable Officers\n\n1. Each ministry and each public body shall have an accounting officer responsible for the legality and regularity of its expenditure.\n2. Accounting officers shall answer to the committees of the Parliament and shall cooperate with the Office of the Auditor-General.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A merit-based, neutral Public Service is the difference between a state that serves its laws and one that serves its incumbents; modern constitutions such as that of South Africa elevate public administration to constitutional rank for exactly this reason. Protection from arbitrary dismissal secures the neutrality the Section demands, since a servile service cannot tell the Government an unwelcome truth. The accounting officer figure welds administrative careers to parliamentary control of public money.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.4.4 (Accountability of Ministries); Article 4.6.1 (Scope of Executive Power).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__emergency-powers", "path": "04-executive/emergency-powers.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.8 — Emergency Powers", "md": "# Section 4.8 — Emergency Powers\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.8.1 – 4.8.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section fixes the ordinary role of the executive within the emergency framework; the regime of the state of emergency itself is governed by Part 26.*\n\n## Article 4.8.1 — Declaration and Countersignature\n\n1. A state of emergency shall be declared by the President on the proposal of the Government.\n2. The declaration shall require the countersignature of the Prime Minister.\n3. It shall state the grounds, the territorial scope, and the measures intended, and shall be published without delay.\n4. The police and the armed forces shall act under civilian authority during a state of emergency.\n\n## Article 4.8.2 — Notification and Information of Parliament\n\n1. The Government shall notify the Parliament of the declaration immediately and shall convene it without delay.\n2. The Government shall report periodically to the Parliament on the measures taken, at intervals fixed by the emergency legislation.\n3. The Parliament may at any time require information under Article 4.7.4 (Documents to Parliament).\n\n## Article 4.8.3 — Compliance with Part 26\n\n1. Measures taken in a state of emergency shall remain within the framework of Part 26.\n2. The duration, approval, and renewal of the state of emergency shall follow the rules of Part 26.\n3. The declaration shall lapse as provided by Part 26 unless approved by the Parliament.\n4. Nothing in this Article extends the powers of the Government beyond those conferred by law.\n\n## Article 4.8.4 — Accountability and Review\n\n1. Acts of the executive under a state of emergency shall be subject to judicial review without delay.\n2. The non-derogable core of rights under Section 1.1 shall remain inviolable.\n3. Officials remain personally responsible for unlawful acts committed under a state of emergency.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section is deliberately thin: Part 26 owns the emergency regime, so Part 04 states only who may declare, what must be told to the Parliament, and what must obey that regime. Countersignature prevents a ceremonial President from triggering emergency powers alone, and immediate judicial review reflects the settled lesson, embodied in the emergency constitution of the German Basic Law, that emergencies are precisely when courts must stay open.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.1.5 (Countersignature); Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__executive-accountability", "path": "04-executive/executive-accountability.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.7 — Executive Accountability", "md": "# Section 4.7 — Executive Accountability\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.7.1 – 4.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section makes the Government answerable to the Parliament through questions, committees, confidence, documents, and the personal responsibility of ministers.*\n\n## Article 4.7.1 — Question Time\n\n1. Ministers shall answer questions put by members of the Parliament.\n2. The Lower House shall hold an oral question time at regular intervals fixed by its rules.\n3. A minister shall not refuse to answer for reasons of convenience; refusal is permitted only where the law protects the information sought.\n\n## Article 4.7.2 — Committee Scrutiny\n\n1. Committees of the Parliament may summon ministers and public officials to appear before them.\n2. Committees may require the production of documents, subject to Section 3.9 and to statutory protection of privacy and security.\n3. A refusal shall be reasoned and may be reviewed by the House concerned.\n\n## Article 4.7.3 — Confidence Responsibility\n\n1. The Government shall hold the confidence of the Lower House.\n2. The Lower House may call upon a minister to account for the minister's conduct.\n3. The Prime Minister may put a question of confidence to the Lower House; its defeat shall entitle the Prime Minister to advise dissolution under Article 3.2.5 (Dissolution and Early Elections).\n\n## Article 4.7.4 — Documents to Parliament\n\n1. Ministers shall provide the Parliament with the information and documents necessary for its scrutiny.\n2. Exceptions shall be fixed by law, shall be construed narrowly, and shall cover only Cabinet deliberations, individual privacy, and the security of the Republic.\n3. The law shall provide an independent review of a refusal to disclose.\n\n## Article 4.7.5 — Personal Responsibility\n\n1. Each minister is personally responsible for the acts within the minister's portfolio and for the minister's own conduct.\n2. Responsibility under clause 1 does not exclude the collective responsibility of the Cabinet.\n3. Criminal liability of a minister shall not be barred by office; Sections 3.9 and 3.10 apply to ministers as members of the Parliament.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Accountability is layered: oral questions for immediacy, committees for depth, confidence for survival, and personal responsibility so that collective cover cannot hide individual fault. The exceptions to document production are deliberately narrow and contestable, translating the transparency principles of Part 27 into enforceable parliamentary rights. The question of confidence survives alongside the constructive vote because a government that cannot legislate should be able to return its mandate to the voters.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.2.4 (Constructive Vote of No Confidence); Article 4.3.2 (Collective Responsibility); Article 4.6.4 (Parliamentary Oversight).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__executive-powers", "path": "04-executive/executive-powers.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.6 — Executive Powers", "md": "# Section 4.6 — Executive Powers\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.6.1 – 4.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section defines the scope of executive power and its constitutional limits, including the regime for delegated legislation and judicial review of executive acts.*\n\n## Article 4.6.1 — Scope of Executive Power\n\n1. The executive power of the Republic shall be exercised by the Government, headed by the Prime Minister, in accordance with this Constitution and the law.\n2. The powers of the President are governed by Section 4.1.\n3. The executive shall faithfully implement this Constitution and the Acts of the Parliament.\n\n## Article 4.6.2 — General Limits\n\n1. The executive shall not impose or collect any tax except under an Act of the Parliament.\n2. The executive shall not raise a loan except under statutory authorization.\n3. The executive shall not order the detention of any person except under law and under judicial control.\n\n## Article 4.6.3 — Delegated Legislation\n\n1. The Government may make regulations and other instruments only under and within the limits of an Act of the Parliament.\n2. Every instrument shall state its legal basis and the reasons for its issue.\n3. An instrument shall not amend an Act or create an offence punishable by imprisonment.\n\n## Article 4.6.4 — Parliamentary Oversight\n\n1. Instruments of delegated legislation shall be laid before the Parliament without delay.\n2. The Parliament may annul an instrument by resolution under its rules.\n3. The law may provide for committees to review instruments before they take effect.\n\n## Article 4.6.5 — Judicial Review of Executive Acts\n\n1. Every act of the executive shall be subject to review by the courts for conformity with this Constitution and the law.\n2. No Act may exclude such review in respect of constitutional questions.\n3. Review under this Article is without prejudice to the jurisdiction of the Constitutional Court.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Delegated legislation is inevitable in a modern state, but this Section hedges it with a legal basis, reasons, publication, and parliamentary annulment, so that delegation never becomes abdication. The express bans on executive taxation, borrowing, and detention state the classic rule-of-law boundary between government by law and government by order. The unwaivable reach of judicial review closes the last door through which a majority might otherwise walk past the courts.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts); Article 4.7.4 (Documents to Parliament).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__head-of-government", "path": "04-executive/head-of-government.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.2 — The Prime Minister", "md": "# Section 4.2 — The Prime Minister\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.2.1 – 4.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Prime Minister as head of government, holding the confidence of the Lower House and removable only through a constructive vote of no confidence.*\n\n## Article 4.2.1 — Election and Confidence\n\n1. The President shall propose to the Lower House a candidate for Prime Minister, after consulting the parliamentary groups.\n2. The Lower House shall elect the Prime Minister by a majority of its members.\n3. The President shall appoint the person elected.\n4. If the Lower House does not elect a Prime Minister within twenty-one days after its first meeting following an election, or after the office falls vacant, the President shall dissolve the Lower House under Article 3.2.5 (Dissolution and Early Elections).\n5. The Prime Minister shall be a member of the Lower House.\n\n## Article 4.2.2 — Direction of Government\n\n1. The Prime Minister shall head the Government and shall direct its policy.\n2. The Prime Minister shall represent the Government in the Parliament.\n3. The Prime Minister shall coordinate the work of the ministers, who shall retain responsibility for their portfolios.\n\n## Article 4.2.3 — Countersignature\n\n1. The Prime Minister shall countersign acts of the President, except where this Constitution provides otherwise.\n2. An act countersigned under clause 1 shall be the responsibility of the Government.\n\n## Article 4.2.4 — Constructive Vote of No Confidence\n\n1. The Lower House may withdraw confidence in the Prime Minister only by electing a successor by the majority required in clause 2 of Article 4.2.1.\n2. The election of the successor and the withdrawal of confidence shall occur in a single vote.\n3. The President shall remove the Prime Minister and appoint the successor so elected.\n4. A defeated motion shall not bar a later motion.\n\n## Article 4.2.5 — Resignation and Succession\n\n1. The Prime Minister may resign by notice to the President.\n2. A Prime Minister whose resignation is accepted shall remain in office to manage current business until a successor is appointed.\n3. During the temporary absence of the Prime Minister, a minister designated by the Prime Minister shall act in the office.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The constructive vote obliges a hostile majority to agree on a replacement before it can remove the head of government — the mechanism the German Basic Law adopted to end the cabinet instability of earlier parliamentary systems. Formation begins with the President's proposal but is decided by the Lower House, which keeps the ceremonial head of state out of partisan bargaining. The twenty-one-day limit turns a prolonged formation crisis into a constitutional trigger for early elections rather than a hostage situation.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.3.1 (Appointment of Ministers); Article 3.2.5 (Dissolution and Early Elections); Article 4.7.3 (Confidence Responsibility).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__head-of-state", "path": "04-executive/head-of-state.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.1 — The President", "md": "# Section 4.1 — The President\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.1.1 – 4.1.7 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the President as head of state, elected by a joint sitting of the Parliament for a single term, with ceremonial duties, narrow reserve powers, and impeachment for grave violations.*\n\n## Article 4.1.1 — Election\n\n1. The President shall be elected by a joint sitting of both Houses.\n2. A candidate shall be elected by a majority of all the members of both Houses.\n3. If no candidate obtains that majority, a second round shall be held between the two leading candidates.\n\n## Article 4.1.2 — Term of Office\n\n1. The President shall serve a single term of six years.\n2. The President shall not be eligible for re-election.\n3. Before taking office, the President shall take an oath before the Constitutional Court to uphold this Constitution.\n\n## Article 4.1.3 — Ceremonial Duties\n\n1. The President shall represent the Republic at home and abroad.\n2. The President shall confer the honours of the Republic as provided by law.\n3. The President may address the Parliament and send messages to the nation on matters of common concern.\n\n## Article 4.1.4 — Reserve Powers\n\n1. The President may refer a bill back to the Parliament once, with written reasons, under Article 3.8.5 (Assent and Referral).\n2. The President shall make appointments required to be made on the nomination of a body defined by this Constitution, and shall appoint judges on the binding recommendation of the Judicial Service Commission.\n3. The President shall exercise no other power except as expressly conferred by this Constitution or by an Act.\n\n## Article 4.1.5 — Countersignature\n\n1. An act of the President shall require the countersignature of the Prime Minister in order to be valid.\n2. Countersignature shall not be required where this Constitution provides otherwise.\n\n## Article 4.1.6 — Impeachment\n\n1. The President may be impeached for a grave violation of this Constitution, for treason, or for another grave offence against the Republic.\n2. Indictment shall require a two-thirds majority of the members of the Lower House.\n3. The Constitutional Court shall decide the charge; removal shall require the concurrence of two-thirds of its judges.\n4. The President shall stand suspended while the charge is pending.\n\n## Article 4.1.7 — Vacancy and Succession\n\n1. The office shall become vacant on death, resignation, removal, or permanent incapacity.\n2. The presiding officer of the Lower House shall act as President until a successor is elected.\n3. A successor shall be elected under Article 4.1.1 (Election) within ninety days.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The design places the President above daily politics: elected by the whole Parliament, limited to a single six-year term, and acting mainly through countersigned acts, like the neutral presidents of German and Italian practice. The single referral and the appointment powers are reserve powers that check but can never displace the parliamentary majority. Assigning the impeachment trial to the Constitutional Court keeps removal judicial rather than political, unlike bicameral trial models.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.8.5 (Assent and Referral); Article 4.2.3 (Countersignature); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n"}, {"id": "04-executive__ministries", "path": "04-executive/ministries.md", "part": "04-executive", "partTitle": "Executive", "title": "Section 4.4 — Ministries and Portfolios", "md": "# Section 4.4 — Ministries and Portfolios\n\n> **Part 04 — The Executive** · Articles 4.4.1 – 4.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section keeps the map of the administration under law: the creation and abolition of ministries, the stability of portfolios, the coordination of government work, and ministerial accountability. It ties the machinery of government to the responsibility of ministers.*\n\n## Article 4.4.1 — Creation and Abolition by Law\n\n1. Ministries shall be created and abolished by Act of Parliament.\n2. An Act establishing a ministry shall define its mandate and scope.\n3. The Government may organize the internal structure of a ministry by decree under that Act.\n4. Each ministry shall be headed by a minister, who shall be accountable for it under Article 4.4.4 (Accountability of Ministries).\n\n## Article 4.4.2 — Stability of Portfolios\n\n1. Portfolios shall be defined with sufficient stability to permit accountability and institutional memory.\n2. A substantial reorganization of the ministries shall require an Act of Parliament.\n3. The reasons for any reorganization shall be stated to the Lower House.\n\n## Article 4.4.3 — Coordination Machinery\n\n1. The Prime Minister shall coordinate the work of the ministries through the Cabinet and a Government Office.\n2. The Government Office shall support the Cabinet, monitor the implementation of Cabinet decisions, and prepare their coordination.\n3. The Cabinet may establish committees of ministers for the coordination of defined fields of policy.\n4. The Government Office shall be headed by an official appointed by the Prime Minister.\n\n## Article 4.4.4 — Accountability of Ministries\n\n1. Each minister shall be accountable to the Parliament for the affairs of the minister's portfolio.\n2. Each ministry shall have an accounting officer responsible for the legality and regularity of its expenditure, under Article 4.5.5 (Accountable Officers).\n3. Ministries shall publish annual reports on their performance and shall present them to the Parliament.\n4. The annual reports shall state the action taken on the findings of the Office of the Auditor-General.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Requiring an Act to create or reshape ministries prevents a government from redrawing the administration to suit a coalition's convenience, while leaving internal organization to decree preserves day-to-day flexibility. Stability of portfolios is treated as a constitutional value because accountability to the Parliament presupposes a stable addressee: a minister cannot answer for a mandate that changes shape every season. Annual reporting and the accounting officer link each ministry into the audit architecture of Part 08, closing the circle from creation by statute to public explanation of results.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.3.1 (Appointment of Ministers); Article 4.7.1 (Question Time); Article 4.5.5 (Accountable Officers).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__access-to-justice", "path": "05-judiciary/access-to-justice.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.7 — Access to Justice", "md": "# Section 5.7 — Access to Justice\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.7.1 – 5.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees that justice is not only independent but reachable: timely proceedings, simplified procedures, proportionate fees, digital access, and interpretation.*\n\n## Article 5.7.1 — Timely Justice\n\n1. Proceedings shall be conducted within a reasonable time.\n2. The courts shall manage cases actively and shall fix time limits for the completion of the steps in proceedings.\n3. The law shall provide compensation where proceedings exceed a duration fixed by law and the delay is not attributable to the person affected.\n\n## Article 5.7.2 — Simplified and Small-Claims Procedures\n\n1. The law shall provide simplified procedures for small claims and for other suitable categories of cases.\n2. Simplified procedures shall be decided promptly and with limited formality.\n3. Self-represented parties shall receive support as provided by law.\n\n## Article 5.7.3 — Fees and Waivers\n\n1. Court fees shall be proportionate to the cost of the proceedings and to the means of the parties.\n2. A party lacking sufficient means shall not be prevented from bringing a case by fees; the law shall provide for full or partial waiver.\n\n## Article 5.7.4 — Electronic Filing and Remote Hearings\n\n1. The courts shall provide for the electronic filing of documents.\n2. Hearings may be held remotely where this serves the parties and the fairness of the proceedings.\n3. Remote hearings shall respect the public character of proceedings under Article 5.4.3 (Open Courts and Lay Assessors).\n\n## Article 5.7.5 — Interpretation and Accessibility\n\n1. A party who cannot understand or follow the proceedings shall receive the assistance of an interpreter free of charge.\n2. Court buildings and proceedings shall be accessible to persons with disabilities.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The duties of access bind the courts themselves, not only the litigant's pocket: delay, formalism, and fees are all barriers that the Constitution orders removed. The reasonable-time guarantee draws on the settled doctrine of the European Convention on Human Rights, while electronic filing and remote hearings are subordinated to open justice so that efficiency never quietly privatizes the courtroom. Interpretation free of charge makes the promise of a fair trial real for those who cannot follow the language of the court.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.9.1 (Right to Legal Aid); Article 5.4.3 (Open Courts and Lay Assessors); Article 5.2.5 (Practice Directions).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__constitutional-court", "path": "05-judiciary/constitutional-court.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.3 — The Constitutional Court", "md": "# Section 5.3 — The Constitutional Court\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.3.1 – 5.3.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Constitutional Court with eleven judges on single non-renewable twelve-year terms and vests in it the exclusive jurisdiction over constitutional questions.*\n\n## Article 5.3.1 — Status and Composition\n\n1. The Constitutional Court shall be the highest organ for the protection of this Constitution.\n2. It shall consist of eleven judges.\n3. It shall be distinct from the Supreme Court.\n\n## Article 5.3.2 — Terms of Judges\n\n1. Judges shall serve a single term of twelve years.\n2. A term shall not be renewable.\n3. Terms shall be staggered so that approximately one-third expire every four years; the initial terms shall be assigned by lot.\n4. A judge shall cease to hold office at the end of the term, at the retirement age fixed by law, or upon removal under Article 5.1.5 (Removal for Cause).\n\n## Article 5.3.3 — Exclusive Jurisdiction\n\n1. The Constitutional Court shall have exclusive jurisdiction over constitutional questions.\n2. It shall decide, in particular:\n   (a) the conformity of laws and other acts of public authority with this Constitution;\n   (b) disputes between organs of the State concerning their constitutional powers;\n   (c) disputes between Provinces, and between a Province and the organs of the State;\n   (d) appeals concerning the validity of elections and referendums;\n   (e) the prohibition of political parties under Article 3.6.4 (Prohibition);\n   (f) charges of impeachment against the President under Article 4.1.6 (Impeachment).\n\n## Article 5.3.4 — Individual Complaints\n\n1. A person who alleges the violation of a right of this Constitution by an act of public authority may complain to the Constitutional Court after exhausting the available remedies.\n2. A complaint shall be admissible only where it raises a question of constitutional significance.\n\n## Article 5.3.5 — Inter-Organ and Province Disputes\n\n1. A dispute under clause 2 of Article 5.3.3 may be brought by an organ of the State or by a Provincial Council affected by it.\n2. The Constitutional Court shall decide such disputes with binding effect.\n\n## Article 5.3.6 — Effect of Decisions\n\n1. Decisions of the Constitutional Court shall be reasoned and published.\n2. They shall be binding on all organs of the State, all courts, and all persons.\n3. Where the Court invalidates an act, the consequences shall follow Article 5.6.4 (Effects of Invalidation).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Eleven judges on single, non-renewable twelve-year terms, staggered by lot, were chosen to outlast legislative cycles while removing any hope of reappointment as a political reward — the logic of Hans Kelsen's original Austrian design. Centralizing constitutional questions in one court prevents parallel verdicts from the general courts and keeps the individual complaint meaningful by tying it to the exhaustion of ordinary remedies. Its jurisdiction doubles as the peace treaty between the branches: organ disputes end here rather than in constitutional crisis.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.6.3 (Abstract Review); Article 3.6.4 (Prohibition); Article 4.1.6 (Impeachment).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__judicial-appointments", "path": "05-judiciary/judicial-appointments.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.5 — The Judicial Service Commission", "md": "# Section 5.5 — The Judicial Service Commission\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.5.1 – 5.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Judicial Service Commission, sets merit criteria and transparent selection, and makes its recommendations binding on the President.*\n\n## Article 5.5.1 — Composition\n\n1. The Judicial Service Commission shall be composed of a majority of serving judges, together with members of the legal profession, teachers of law, and public members appointed with cross-party support.\n2. The composition and the terms of its members shall be fixed by law.\n3. The Commission shall be chaired by the President of the Supreme Court.\n\n## Article 5.5.2 — Merit Criteria\n\n1. Appointment shall be by merit, measured by professional excellence, integrity, independence, and suitability for judicial office.\n2. Vacancies shall be publicly advertised, with the criteria fixed in advance.\n3. The Commission shall seek a judiciary that reflects the diversity of the people of the Republic.\n\n## Article 5.5.3 — Transparent Hearings\n\n1. Candidates shall be interviewed by the Commission.\n2. Interviews shall be public, save where the private life of a candidate must be protected.\n3. Decisions shall be reasoned and shall be communicated to the candidates.\n\n## Article 5.5.4 — Binding Recommendations\n\n1. For every judicial vacancy, the Commission shall recommend to the President the candidate it considers best qualified.\n2. The President shall appoint the person recommended.\n3. The President may decline an appointment only where the candidate lacks an eligibility requirement fixed by law, and shall state the reasons in writing.\n\n## Article 5.5.5 — Tenure and Promotion\n\n1. The Commission shall govern the promotion, assignment, and evaluation of judges by published criteria.\n2. The law may provide for a probationary first appointment of limited duration.\n3. Nothing in this Article limits the removal procedure of Article 5.1.5 (Removal for Cause).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Making the recommendations binding strips the President of patronage while leaving the formal appointment in the hands of the head of state — stricter in effect than the advisory commissions of the United Kingdom and closer to the professional councils of continental Europe. Public, criteria-based hearings are the cheapest known prophylactic against quiet favouritism. Judicial majorities on the Commission protect independence, while the non-judge members prevent corporate self-selection.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.1.5 (Removal for Cause); Article 5.2.2 (Composition); Article 5.3.2 (Terms of Judges).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__judicial-independence", "path": "05-judiciary/judicial-independence.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.1 — Judicial Independence", "md": "# Section 5.1 — Judicial Independence\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.1.1 – 5.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the independence of the courts and judges, their security of tenure and funding, and the strict conditions of removal from office.*\n\n## Article 5.1.1 — Independence of Courts\n\n1. The courts shall be independent and subject only to this Constitution and the law.\n2. No organ of the State and no private person may give a binding instruction to a judge in a pending case.\n3. The administration of the courts shall be organized so as to protect their independence.\n\n## Article 5.1.2 — Independence of Judges\n\n1. Judges shall be independent and impartial in the exercise of their office.\n2. Judges shall decide cases according to law and conscience, without fear or favour.\n3. Judges shall not hold an executive office or an office in a political party.\n\n## Article 5.1.3 — Security of Tenure\n\n1. Judges shall hold office until the end of the term for which they were appointed or until the retirement age fixed by law.\n2. A judge shall not be transferred, suspended, or removed otherwise than as provided by this Section and by law.\n\n## Article 5.1.4 — Financial Autonomy\n\n1. The remuneration of judges shall be fixed by law and shall not be reduced during their term of office.\n2. The budget of the judiciary shall be presented to the Parliament as a distinct item and shall be adequate for the proper administration of justice.\n\n## Article 5.1.5 — Removal for Cause\n\n1. A judge may be removed only for gross misconduct or permanent incapacity.\n2. Removal shall be decided by the Judicial Service Commission by a two-thirds majority, after a proceeding in which the judge enjoys full rights of defence.\n3. The decision shall be subject to review by the Constitutional Court.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The salary guarantee is the oldest protection of judicial independence, familiar from Article III of the United States Constitution, and it matters as much as formal tenure: a judge who fears a pay cut is a dependent judge. Routing removal through the Judicial Service Commission, with constitutional-court review behind it, separates judges from the appetites of the government of the day. The distinct budget line prevents the same pressure from arriving through the appropriation route.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.5.4 (Binding Recommendations); Article 5.3.2 (Terms of Judges); Article 5.8.1 (Status and Independence).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__judicial-review", "path": "05-judiciary/judicial-review.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.6 — Judicial Review", "md": "# Section 5.6 — Judicial Review\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.6.1 – 5.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section distributes the power to refuse unconstitutional acts between all courts and the Constitutional Court, and fixes the effects of invalidation.*\n\n## Article 5.6.1 — Power of All Courts\n\n1. Every court shall refuse to apply an act of public authority that conflicts with this Constitution.\n2. Where the constitutionality of an applicable act is doubtful and decisive for the case, the court shall stay the proceedings and refer the question to the Constitutional Court.\n\n## Article 5.6.2 — Concrete Review\n\n1. A party to a pending case may raise the unconstitutionality of an applicable act.\n2. The court shall decide the objection, or shall refer the question as provided in clause 2 of Article 5.6.1.\n\n## Article 5.6.3 — Abstract Review\n\n1. The Constitutional Court shall review the conformity of laws with this Constitution also without a concrete case.\n2. Access shall be open to the Government, to one-third of the members of either House, and to the Provincial Councils, as provided by law.\n\n## Article 5.6.4 — Effects of Invalidation\n\n1. A judgment of invalidation shall be reasoned and published.\n2. The Constitutional Court shall determine whether the invalidation operates for the future only, and shall order the transitional arrangements necessary to protect legal certainty.\n\n## Article 5.6.5 — No Ouster of Review\n\n1. No Act of the Parliament and no other act may exclude the review of constitutional questions by the courts.\n2. An interpretation of a provision that avoids its unconstitutionality shall be preferred.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The design blends diffuse review — every court may refuse an unconstitutional act, as American practice since Marbury v. Madison teaches — with concentrated referral to the Constitutional Court, so that the decisive questions receive one authoritative answer. The ban on ouster clauses is the lock on the door: without it, a hostile majority could simply legislate itself out of judicial control. Prospective invalidation is kept as an exception, reserved for cases where immediate retroactivity would punish reliance on the old law.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 4.6.5 (Judicial Review of Executive Acts).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__legal-aid", "path": "05-judiciary/legal-aid.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.9 — Legal Aid", "md": "# Section 5.9 — Legal Aid\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.9.1 – 5.9.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section secures state-funded legal aid as the companion of effective access to justice: mandatory in serious criminal cases and means-tested in civil matters.*\n\n## Article 5.9.1 — Right to Legal Aid\n\n1. A person who lacks sufficient means shall have the right to legal aid where this is necessary for effective access to justice.\n2. Legal aid shall cover advice, representation, and the costs of proceedings as provided by law.\n\n## Article 5.9.2 — The Legal Aid Office\n\n1. The State shall maintain a Legal Aid Office to administer legal aid.\n2. The Office shall be independent in the assessment of individual applications.\n3. The law shall fix its organization and funding, which shall be sufficient to make the right under Article 5.9.1 effective.\n\n## Article 5.9.3 — Mandatory Criminal Legal Aid\n\n1. The State shall provide representation at its own expense where the interests of justice so require, and in particular where the person is detained or is charged with an offence punishable by imprisonment.\n2. Representation under clause 1 shall be available from the first interrogation by the authorities.\n3. No person shall be tried without counsel where the law makes representation mandatory.\n\n## Article 5.9.4 — Civil Legal Aid\n\n1. In civil and administrative matters, legal aid shall be means-tested.\n2. Priority shall be given to matters affecting the basic conditions of life, such as family, housing, and social protection.\n3. Legal aid shall be available in asylum procedures as provided by law.\n\n## Article 5.9.5 — Quality and Independence of Providers\n\n1. Providers of legal aid shall be qualified to the standard required by the matters concerned.\n2. Providers shall act independently in the interest of the person aided.\n3. The law shall provide for quality assurance of legal aid services.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Legal aid converts the fair-trial guarantee from paper into practice; without counsel, the rights protected by Section 1.7 are themselves unenforceable, a lesson taught in the United States by Gideon v. Wainwright. Mandatory state-funded defence in serious criminal cases reflects a near-universal constitutional consensus, while means-tested civil aid with priority categories concentrates scarce resources where the basic conditions of life are at stake. An independent office shields individual eligibility decisions from the Government whose conduct may itself be at issue.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.7.3 (Fees and Waivers); Article 5.7.1 (Timely Justice).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__lower-courts", "path": "05-judiciary/lower-courts.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.4 — High Courts and District Courts", "md": "# Section 5.4 — High Courts and District Courts\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.4.1 – 5.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section structures the general courts below the Supreme Court — the District Courts and the High Courts — and secures open justice, lay participation, and objective case allocation.*\n\n## Article 5.4.1 — Court Structure\n\n1. The general court system shall comprise the District Courts, the High Courts, and the Supreme Court.\n2. The District Courts shall be the courts of first instance.\n3. The High Courts shall hear appeals from the District Courts and shall try at first instance the categories of serious cases assigned to them by law.\n\n## Article 5.4.2 — Jurisdiction of the High Courts\n\n1. The High Courts shall exercise general jurisdiction within their districts.\n2. Specialized administrative jurisdiction may be assigned by law to chambers of the High Courts.\n3. The law shall fix the districts of the High Courts and of the District Courts.\n\n## Article 5.4.3 — Open Courts and Lay Assessors\n\n1. Hearings shall be public, and judgment shall always be pronounced in public.\n2. A court may exclude the public only for reasons of public security, public order, or the protection of private life or of minors, by reasoned order.\n3. There shall be no jury system. Lay assessors may participate in defined categories of cases as provided by law.\n\n## Article 5.4.4 — Case Allocation\n\n1. Cases shall be allocated to judges by objective, predetermined rules.\n2. The allocation rules shall be published and shall not be altered with effect for a pending case.\n\n## Article 5.4.5 — Specialized Chambers\n\n1. The law may establish specialized chambers, such as for family, commercial, or labor matters, within the courts.\n2. Specialization shall not create separate court systems outside the structure of this Section.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A three-level general system keeps the paths of appeal short enough to be usable while concentrating serious first-instance work in the High Courts. Lay assessors offer civic participation without the rigidity of a jury system, on the pattern of the lay judges of German courts, and they are confined to categories fixed by law. Fixed allocation rules remove the judge-shopping temptation that corrodes both fairness and the appearance of fairness.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.2.1 (Status and Jurisdiction); Article 5.7.2 (Simplified and Small-Claims Procedures); Article 5.1.1 (Independence of Courts).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__prosecutors", "path": "05-judiciary/prosecutors.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.8 — The Public Prosecution Service", "md": "# Section 5.8 — The Public Prosecution Service\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.8.1 – 5.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Public Prosecution Service as an independent organ, headed by the Public Prosecutor, and orders the transparency of its instructions and guidelines.*\n\n## Article 5.8.1 — Status and Independence\n\n1. The Public Prosecution Service shall be independent of the Government and of every other organ of the State.\n2. It shall prosecute offences and shall perform the other functions conferred on it by law.\n3. In the exercise of its functions it shall be objective and impartial and shall respect the rights of the persons concerned.\n\n## Article 5.8.2 — The Public Prosecutor\n\n1. The Public Prosecution Service shall be headed by the Public Prosecutor.\n2. The Public Prosecutor shall serve a single term of seven years and shall not be reappointed.\n3. The Public Prosecutor shall be appointed from among persons of recognized integrity and professional standing, in the manner provided by law.\n4. The law shall provide for a deputy and for the acting head in case of vacancy.\n\n## Article 5.8.3 — Instructions in Individual Cases\n\n1. The Service is hierarchical, but an instruction relating to an individual case shall be given in writing and entered in the case file.\n2. A prosecutor who considers an instruction unlawful shall record the objection and may request its withdrawal.\n\n## Article 5.8.4 — Prosecution Guidelines\n\n1. The Public Prosecutor shall issue general prosecution guidelines to ensure consistency and transparency.\n2. The guidelines shall be published and shall state the criteria for prosecution, for diversion, and for the discontinuance of charges.\n3. A deviation from the guidelines shall be justified in the case file.\n\n## Article 5.8.5 — Relationship to Police and the Anti-Corruption Commission\n\n1. The police shall investigate offences under the direction of the Public Prosecution Service.\n2. The Anti-Corruption Commission shall refer prosecutable cases to the Service, which shall decide on charges independently.\n3. The Service shall report annually to the Parliament on the exercise of its functions.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Prosecutorial independence is calibrated rather than absolute: the hierarchy is kept, but written, filed instructions expose any pressure to abuse, the technique of the Dutch public prosecution service. Requiring the Service, not the Anti-Corruption Commission, to decide charges keeps the investigative and accusatorial functions distinct and reviewable. The single seven-year term of the Public Prosecutor spans longer than one electoral cycle, detaching prosecution policy from the rhythm of elections.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.9.3 (Mandatory Criminal Legal Aid); Article 3.10.5 (Cooperation with the Anti-Corruption Commission); Article 5.1.1 (Independence of Courts).\n"}, {"id": "05-judiciary__supreme-court", "path": "05-judiciary/supreme-court.md", "part": "05-judiciary", "partTitle": "Judiciary", "title": "Section 5.2 — The Supreme Court", "md": "# Section 5.2 — The Supreme Court\n\n> **Part 05 — The Judiciary** · Articles 5.2.1 – 5.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Supreme Court as the apex of the general court system, orders its composition and panels, and empowers it to unify the case law.*\n\n## Article 5.2.1 — Status and Jurisdiction\n\n1. The Supreme Court shall be the highest court of the general court system.\n2. It shall hear final appeals in civil, criminal, and administrative matters that do not fall within the exclusive jurisdiction of the Constitutional Court.\n3. The law may require leave to appeal where a case raises no question of law of general importance.\n4. Its decisions shall bind the parties and shall be treated as authoritative statements of the law by all courts.\n\n## Article 5.2.2 — Composition\n\n1. The number of judges of the Supreme Court shall be fixed by law and shall be an odd number.\n2. Judges shall be appointed by the President on the binding recommendation of the Judicial Service Commission.\n3. The judges shall elect a President of the Court from among themselves.\n\n## Article 5.2.3 — Panels\n\n1. The Court shall sit in panels of three or five judges.\n2. A larger panel, and the Court sitting en banc, shall be constituted as provided by law for matters of principle.\n3. Judgments shall state reasons and shall be published.\n\n## Article 5.2.4 — Unification of Case Law\n\n1. Where panels of the Court have decided the same question of law differently, the Court sitting en banc shall resolve the divergence.\n2. A departure from a settled interpretation of the law shall require an en banc decision and published reasons.\n\n## Article 5.2.5 — Practice Directions\n\n1. The Court may issue practice directions on the conduct and management of proceedings.\n2. Practice directions shall be published and shall not conflict with this Constitution or the law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Separating the Supreme Court from the Constitutional Court, in the manner of the Austrian and German models, keeps ordinary appeal justice and constitutional adjudication professionally distinct and prevents the constitutionalization of every dispute. The en banc mechanism lets the Court unify its own case law instead of allowing silent contradictions to accumulate across panels. Leave requirements concentrate the Court's authority where it matters: questions of law capable of guiding the whole system.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.4.1 (Court Structure); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction); Article 5.5.4 (Binding Recommendations).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__election-disputes", "path": "06-elections/election-disputes.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.6 — Election Disputes", "md": "# Section 6.6 — Election Disputes\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.6.1 – 6.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes the resolution of electoral disputes: complaints to the Electoral Commission, appeal to the Constitutional Court, expedited timelines, and remedies.*\n\n## Article 6.6.1 — Complaints to the Electoral Commission\n\n1. Any voter, candidate, political party, or accredited observer may complain to the Electoral Commission of an irregularity affecting an election or referendum.\n2. The Commission shall decide complaints within short statutory periods, shall state reasons, and shall correct within the process what can be corrected.\n3. Decisions of the Commission under this Article may be appealed as provided in Article 3.5.4 (Accountability and Cooperation).\n\n## Article 6.6.2 — Appeal to the Constitutional Court\n\n1. The validity of an election or referendum, and the decisions of the Electoral Commission on complaints, may be brought before the Constitutional Court, which decides finally under Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n2. The persons and bodies entitled to complain under Article 6.6.1 shall have standing to appeal.\n\n## Article 6.6.3 — Expedited Timelines\n\n1. The law shall fix strict deadlines for filing, decision, and appeal, so that disputes are resolved before the certification of results or, exceptionally, before the newly elected body first meets.\n2. Timelines shall balance thorough adjudication with the certainty of the result that democratic continuity requires.\n\n## Article 6.6.4 — Remedies\n\n1. The Electoral Commission and the Constitutional Court may order a recount, the correction of records, the repetition of counting, or a new election in the area affected.\n2. A result may be annulled only where an irregularity occurred that was capable of affecting its outcome.\n3. Remedies shall be proportionate to the violation and shall restore, so far as possible, the will of the voters.\n\n## Article 6.6.5 — Finality and Publication\n\n1. Decisions on electoral disputes shall be reasoned, published, and binding under Article 5.3.6 (Effect of Decisions).\n2. Where an election is repeated, the organs of the State shall take the measures necessary to hold it without delay.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Two tiers serve two functions: the Commission corrects operational defects quickly, while the Constitutional Court provides final and independent judgment on validity, matching the allocation in Article 5.3.3. Expedited timelines manage the tension between thorough adjudication and the prompt, certain result that a democracy needs to keep functioning. Annulment is reserved for outcome-capable irregularities so that technical errors do not disenfranchise an entire electorate.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction); Article 3.4.4 (Administration and Validity); Article 5.3.6 (Effect of Decisions).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__election-observers", "path": "06-elections/election-observers.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.5 — Election Observers", "md": "# Section 6.5 — Election Observers\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.5.1 – 6.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the observation of elections and referendums by accredited domestic and international observers, their access to all stages, and their rights and duties.*\n\n## Article 6.5.1 — Right of Observation\n\n1. Domestic and international observers may observe all elections and referendums of the Republic.\n2. Accreditation shall be granted by the Electoral Commission on uniform, published criteria and shall not be refused on arbitrary or discriminatory grounds; refusal shall state reasons.\n3. Observation serves the public interest in the integrity of the electoral process and shall not be made dependent on the approval of any other organ of the State.\n\n## Article 6.5.2 — Accreditation and Access\n\n1. Accredited observers shall have access to all stages of the electoral process, including voter registration, the campaign period, polling, counting, tabulation, and the resolution of complaints.\n2. Observers shall have access to the information and documents necessary for observation, subject to the secrecy of the ballot and to legitimate security requirements fixed by law.\n\n## Article 6.5.3 — Rights and Duties of Observers\n\n1. Observers may attend all proceedings, take notes, record procedures as permitted by law, and publish their findings freely.\n2. Observers shall remain impartial, shall not interfere with the process, shall not campaign or display party symbols, and shall respect the secrecy of the ballot and the law.\n3. A report of serious irregularity submitted by an observer shall be recorded by the Electoral Commission and addressed under Article 6.6.1 (Complaints to the Electoral Commission).\n\n## Article 6.5.4 — Observer Codes of Conduct\n\n1. The Electoral Commission shall adopt codes of conduct for observers, covering impartiality, non-interference, and transparency of methods.\n2. Accreditation may be withdrawn only for a serious violation of the code, by a reasoned decision subject to review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Observation supplies independent evidence where neither the electorate nor the courts can be present at once, and international accreditation brings a comparative benchmark of good practice. Uniform criteria for accreditation prevent selective exclusion, while binding duties keep observers from becoming participants in the contest they watch. Withdrawal of accreditation is confined to proven misconduct so that unfavorable reporting can never be punished as such.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration); Article 6.4.5 (Public Counting and Publication); Article 6.6.1 (Complaints to the Electoral Commission).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__election-security", "path": "06-elections/election-security.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.4 — Election Security", "md": "# Section 6.4 — Election Security\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.4.1 – 6.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section secures the technical integrity of elections: the paper record, chain of custody, cybersecurity of electoral technology, post-election audits, and publication of counting records.*\n\n## Article 6.4.1 — Paper Record Requirement\n\n1. Every vote cast shall be recorded on a paper ballot or shall produce a verified paper trail that the voter can confirm and that is protected from alteration.\n2. The paper record shall be the authoritative basis for determining and recounting results.\n3. Voting technologies may be used only where they support, and never where they displace, the paper record.\n\n## Article 6.4.2 — Chain of Custody and Tamper-Evidence\n\n1. Ballots, ballot boxes, memory media, and related election materials shall be secured by tamper-evident procedures from issuance through counting to retention.\n2. Custody shall be documented at every transfer with the signatures of the transferring and receiving custodians and, where practicable, of witnesses.\n3. Election materials shall be retained for the period fixed by law and shall be destroyed only after dispute procedures are exhausted.\n\n## Article 6.4.3 — Cybersecurity Standards\n\n1. Electoral information systems shall meet cybersecurity standards fixed by law, including certification before use, separation from public networks, and continuous monitoring.\n2. The Electoral Commission shall test systems before each election and shall publish a summary of the results.\n3. An incident affecting the integrity of electoral data shall be investigated, documented, and disclosed without undue delay.\n\n## Article 6.4.4 — Post-Election Audits\n\n1. Before certification of results, the Electoral Commission shall audit a sample of counting units by hand comparison against the paper record.\n2. The audit shall be capable of detecting a wrong outcome and shall be expanded where discrepancies appear.\n3. Audit results and their reconciliation with machine counts shall be published.\n\n## Article 6.4.5 — Public Counting and Publication\n\n1. Counting shall be public, and party and candidate witnesses and accredited observers shall be entitled to attend under Article 6.5.2 (Accreditation and Access).\n2. Counting records shall be published together with the certified results in the manner provided by Article 3.4.4 (Administration and Validity).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Paper ballots or verified paper trails keep the decisive evidence in a form that citizens, parties, and courts can examine without expert intermediaries. A risk-limiting audit before certification converts trust in technology into statistical verification of the outcome itself. Chain-of-custody documentation makes tampering not only difficult but detectable after the fact, which matters more than perfect prevention.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.4.4 (Administration and Validity); Article 6.5.2 (Accreditation and Access); Article 6.6.4 (Remedies).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__electoral-system", "path": "06-elections/electoral-system.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.1 — The Electoral System", "md": "# Section 6.1 — The Electoral System\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.1.1 – 6.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section defines the electoral system of the Republic: equality of the vote, the proportional method for the Lower House, the allocation of seats, and boundary principles.*\n\n## Article 6.1.1 — Formal Equality of the Vote\n\n1. Every voter possesses one vote of equal formal weight in every election and referendum, and every valid vote shall be counted equally.\n2. The vote shall be general, equal, free, secret, and direct.\n3. Public authority shall protect the free formation of the voter's will and the free expression of that will against every improper influence.\n\n## Article 6.1.2 — The Electoral System for the Lower House\n\n1. Members of the Lower House shall be elected by proportional representation with open candidate lists in Province-wide constituencies, consistent with Article 3.2.2 (Electoral Principle).\n2. Each voter may cast a party vote and express a preference for one candidate on that list.\n3. Seats shall be allocated among parties in proportion to the valid votes cast nationwide.\n4. A party shall participate in the allocation of seats only if it obtains at least three percent of the valid national votes cast.\n\n## Article 6.1.3 — Allocation of Seats\n\n1. Seats shall be allocated on the basis of the Droop quota: valid votes in a constituency divided by its seats plus one.\n2. A party shall receive as many seats as its votes cover whole quotas; remaining seats shall be distributed by the method fixed by law.\n3. Candidates shall be declared elected in the order of the preferences they have received; where preferences do not distinguish, the party's list order shall govern.\n4. A vacant seat shall be filled under Article 3.2.4 (Vacancies).\n\n## Article 6.1.4 — Constituency Boundaries\n\n1. The Lower House shall be elected in constituencies corresponding to the Provinces.\n2. Constituencies for other elections shall be delimited so that each vote carries comparable weight; boundaries shall respect population equality, geographic contiguity, and the coherence of communities.\n3. Boundaries shall be determined by an independent boundary commission, published with reasons, and reviewed at regular intervals under Section 30.2 (Electoral Districts).\n\n## Article 6.1.5 — Stability of Electoral Law\n\n1. The details of the electoral system shall be regulated by law.\n2. Electoral law shall serve equal suffrage and shall not advantage particular parties or candidates.\n3. A material change to electoral law shall not enter into force within one year before an ordinary election, unless the Constitutional Court finds that the change is necessary to remedy a violation of equal suffrage.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Open-list proportional representation allocates seats strictly in proportion to votes while leaving the choice among candidates to the electorate, not to party headquarters. The three percent threshold screens out micro-parties without excluding substantial minorities, and the one-year stability rule prevents an incumbent majority from rewriting the rules mid-game. Boundary principles are stated in the Constitution so that districting cannot become a partisan instrument.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.2.2 (Electoral Principle); Article 3.4.1 (Timing of Elections); Article 6.2.1 (Automatic Registration).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__referendums", "path": "06-elections/referendums.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.7 — Referendums", "md": "# Section 6.7 — Referendums\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.7.1 – 6.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs national referendums: the routes by which they are triggered, their subject-matter limits, the mandatory referendum on entrenched amendments, campaign rules, and binding effect.*\n\n## Article 6.7.1 — Routes to a National Referendum\n\n1. The Parliament may provide for a national referendum by law adopted by a majority of the members of each House.\n2. A national referendum shall also be held on a citizens' initiative supported by the signatures of at least five percent of the persons registered to vote, verified by the Electoral Commission.\n3. The law shall fix the procedure for initiatives, including the period for signature collection and verification.\n\n## Article 6.7.2 — Subject-Matter Limits\n\n1. No referendum shall be held on the annual budget, on taxation, or on the declaration or termination of a state of emergency.\n2. No referendum may propose a measure that derogates from the rights listed in Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n3. A referendum shall not be used to evade the amendment thresholds of this Constitution, including the protection of entrenched provisions under Section 30.5 (Protected Provisions).\n\n## Article 6.7.3 — Mandatory Referendum on Entrenched Amendments\n\n1. A constitutional amendment affecting entrenched provisions shall not enter into force unless approved by a majority of the voters voting in a national referendum held for that purpose.\n2. The referendum shall be combined with a general election where possible, unless the urgency of the matter justifies a separate vote.\n\n## Article 6.7.4 — Campaign Rules\n\n1. Public authorities shall not campaign for a particular outcome and shall ensure that supporters and opponents enjoy equal treatment in the use of public facilities.\n2. The rules on campaign finance applicable to elections, including disclosure and oversight, apply to referendum campaigns under Article 3.7.5 (Oversight and Sanctions).\n3. The Electoral Commission shall publish neutral explanatory material on each question and shall ensure fair access to public service media for both sides.\n\n## Article 6.7.5 — Binding Effect and Review\n\n1. A referendum result within its constitutional competence is binding and shall be implemented by the competent organs without delay.\n2. The validity of a referendum may be challenged and finally decided by the Constitutional Court under Article 6.6.2 (Appeal to the Constitutional Court).\n3. A referendum result unconstitutional in its content shall not be implemented; its conformity is reviewed under Article 5.6.3 (Abstract Review).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Three trigger routes balance parliamentary control with citizen initiative, while the mandatory referendum on entrenched amendments gives the double lock of the entrenchment scheme its popular legitimacy. Subject-matter exclusions protect the budget and rights from majoritarian moments in which concentrated costs and diffuse benefits are hard for a single vote to adjudicate. Binding effect makes the referendum a genuine instrument of popular sovereignty rather than an advisory ornament.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.6.5 (Direct Democracy); Article 3.7.5 (Oversight and Sanctions); Article 6.6.2 (Appeal to the Constitutional Court).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__voter-registration", "path": "06-elections/voter-registration.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.2 — Voter Registration", "md": "# Section 6.2 — Voter Registration\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.2.1 – 6.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the registration of voters: automatic enrollment at the age of eighteen, continuous maintenance of accurate rolls, correction of errors, and the protection of registration data.*\n\n## Article 6.2.1 — Automatic Registration\n\n1. Every citizen shall be registered as a voter automatically upon reaching the age of eighteen years.\n2. Registration shall be based on the civil register and other public records; no application, fee, or test shall be required.\n3. The Electoral Commission shall notify newly registered persons of their registration and of the means of exercising the vote.\n\n## Article 6.2.2 — Maintenance of the Rolls\n\n1. The Electoral Commission shall maintain voter rolls that are continuous, accurate, and current.\n2. The rolls shall be updated continuously from public records and through verification exercises conducted without interrupting the right to vote.\n3. The rolls shall be open to public inspection for at least the period fixed by law before each election, so that errors may be discovered and corrected in time.\n\n## Article 6.2.3 — Inspection and Correction\n\n1. Every person may inspect the data recorded about that person in the rolls.\n2. A person may request correction of erroneous data; the Electoral Commission shall decide within the period fixed by law and shall state reasons.\n3. A decision refusing correction may be appealed under Article 6.6.1 (Complaints to the Electoral Commission).\n4. No eligible person may be removed from the rolls except for cause stated in a reasoned decision subject to appeal.\n\n## Article 6.2.4 — Protection of Registration Data\n\n1. Registration data may be processed only for electoral purposes and for other purposes expressly provided by law.\n2. Registration data shall not be sold, transferred for commercial use, or disclosed to political parties in a manner incompatible with the integrity and secrecy of the vote.\n3. The safeguards of the law on data protection apply, and the Information Commission may exercise its supervisory powers over the rolls.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Automatic registration shifts the burden of accuracy from the individual citizen to the State, eliminating the classic losses that registration deadlines and purge exercises produce. Continuous maintenance through public records avoids periodic re-enrollment drives, which historically open the door to selective manipulation of the rolls. Pairing openness with data protection ensures that the accuracy of the register and the privacy of the registered reinforce rather than undermine each other.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration); Article 0.6.2 (Free and Periodic Elections); Article 6.6.1 (Complaints to the Electoral Commission).\n"}, {"id": "06-elections__voting", "path": "06-elections/voting.md", "part": "06-elections", "partTitle": "Elections", "title": "Section 6.3 — Voting", "md": "# Section 6.3 — Voting\n\n> **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums** · Articles 6.3.1 – 6.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the conduct of voting: the secret ballot, accessibility, special voting arrangements, time to vote, and assistance to voters.*\n\n## Article 6.3.1 — The Secret Ballot\n\n1. Voting shall be by secret ballot in a booth or other place ensuring that no one can observe how a voter votes.\n2. No person may obtain information about how a voter has voted, and a voter shall not be obliged to disclose it.\n3. Exceptions necessary to render assistance shall be recorded without revealing the content of the vote.\n\n## Article 6.3.2 — Accessibility of Voting\n\n1. Polling stations shall be accessible to voters with disabilities, and the Electoral Commission shall provide reasonable accommodations suited to different forms of disability.\n2. The law shall ensure that distance, terrain, and similar obstacles do not deprive remote communities of effective access to the poll.\n\n## Article 6.3.3 — Special Voting Arrangements\n\n1. The law shall provide for absentee, postal, and early voting, and for voting in hospitals, care facilities, and places of detention.\n2. Detained persons retain the right to vote on the same terms as other voters.\n3. Special voting arrangements shall observe the same standards of secrecy, security, and counting as ordinary voting.\n\n## Article 6.3.4 — Time to Vote\n\n1. Election day shall be fixed by law so that every voter has a reasonable opportunity to vote.\n2. An employer shall grant an employee leave sufficient to vote without loss of pay and shall not penalize the exercise of the vote in any manner.\n\n## Article 6.3.5 — Assistance to Voters\n\n1. A voter who by reason of disability or illiteracy cannot vote without help may be assisted by a person of the voter's own choice or by an official of the polling station.\n2. Assistance shall be rendered only in the presence of the voter, shall preserve secrecy, and shall express the voter's will alone.\n3. The Electoral Commission shall prescribe uniform rules for assistance and shall train polling-station officials accordingly.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The secret ballot is treated as an active guarantee rather than a mere absence of observers, so the State must provide booths, enforce non-disclosure, and record assistance without recording votes. Extending identical integrity standards to postal, early, and in-facility voting prevents convenience channels from becoming weaker gates into the count. Paid time to vote converts a formal right into a practical one for shift and hourly workers, for whom polling hours are otherwise a barrier.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.6.2 (Free and Periodic Elections); Article 6.1.1 (Formal Equality of the Vote); Article 6.4.1 (Paper Record Requirement).\n"}, {"id": "07-local-government__decentralization", "path": "07-local-government/decentralization.md", "part": "07-local-government", "partTitle": "Local Government", "title": "Section 7.6 — Decentralization", "md": "# Section 7.6 — Decentralization\n\n> **Part 07 — Local Government** · Articles 7.6.1 – 7.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section declares subsidiarity the governing rule of devolution, provides for periodic devolution reviews, and protects local self-government against abolition.*\n\n## Article 7.6.1 — The Subsidiarity Principle\n\n1. Public functions shall be performed at the lowest level of government that can perform them effectively and efficiently.\n2. A higher level may perform a function itself only where the objectives of the function cannot be sufficiently achieved at the lower level, or where scale or external effects justify central action.\n3. The level relying on an exception under clause 2 bears the burden of justifying it.\n\n## Article 7.6.2 — Allocation and Review of Functions\n\n1. Functions among Provinces, Municipalities, and the organs of the Republic shall be allocated by law in accordance with Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle).\n2. Transfers of functions shall be accompanied by the corresponding resources and shall be adopted after consultation with the tier concerned.\n3. Assignments shall be drafted so that responsibilities can be attributed without ambiguity.\n\n## Article 7.6.3 — Periodic Devolution Reviews\n\n1. The Parliament shall review the distribution of functions and resources at intervals of no more than five years, with the participation of the Provinces and Municipalities.\n2. The review shall assess the exercise of devolved functions, the adequacy of financing, and the effects of supervision, and its conclusions shall be published.\n3. Independent evidence for the review may be commissioned from the Statistics Authority.\n\n## Article 7.6.4 — Protection of Local Self-Government\n\n1. The existence of the Provinces as self-governing units and the right of Municipalities to local self-government shall not be abolished except by constitutional amendment.\n2. A law shall not dissolve, suspend, or merge a Province or Municipality as a sanction for the exercise of its lawful competences.\n3. The Constitutional Court protects these guarantees under Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Subsidiarity is written as an operational rule with a burden of justification, not as a preface, so centralizing statutes must argue rather than assert. Periodic review turns devolution into a living settlement that can follow demography and administrative experience, with evidence supplied by the Statistics Authority. The constitutional-amendment lock answers the historical record of local government abolished or hollowed out by ordinary statute in many real states.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 7.1.4 (Supervision Limited to Legality); Article 7.5.3 (No Unfunded Mandates); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n"}, {"id": "07-local-government__local-elections", "path": "07-local-government/local-elections.md", "part": "07-local-government", "partTitle": "Local Government", "title": "Section 7.4 — Local Elections", "md": "# Section 7.4 — Local Elections\n\n> **Part 07 — Local Government** · Articles 7.4.1 – 7.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs elections to the Provincial Councils and the municipal councils: administration by the Electoral Commission, common integrity standards, timing, and common rolls.*\n\n## Article 7.4.1 — Administration by the Electoral Commission\n\n1. The Electoral Commission shall organize, conduct, and supervise elections to the Provincial Councils and to the municipal councils.\n2. The Commission shall have the same exclusive competence for local elections as for national elections under Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration).\n3. No organ of a Province or Municipality may interfere with the conduct of a local election.\n\n## Article 7.4.2 — Common Integrity Standards\n\n1. Local elections shall observe the same constitutional standards as national elections: universal and equal suffrage, the secret ballot under Article 6.3.1 (The Secret Ballot), paper records, observation, and the remedies of Part 06.\n2. Voting rights and rolls shall be identical to those used in national elections.\n3. Candidates and parties shall compete on equal terms, and public facilities shall be used neutrally.\n\n## Article 7.4.3 — Terms and Timing\n\n1. Provincial Councils shall be elected for five-year terms consistent with Article 7.1.2 (Provincial Councils); municipal councils shall be elected for five-year terms.\n2. Provincial and municipal elections shall ordinarily be held on the same day, at a date fixed by law.\n3. Early elections of a council shall be held only where the council cannot function, in the manner fixed by law.\n4. A vacancy in a council shall be filled for the remainder of the term in the manner fixed by law.\n\n## Article 7.4.4 — Common Electoral Rolls\n\n1. A single voter roll maintained under Article 6.2.2 (Maintenance of the Rolls) shall serve national, provincial, and municipal elections.\n2. No separate registration shall be demanded of any voter for a local election.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Routing local elections through the national Electoral Commission removes the most common source of local manipulation, the incumbent council running its own election. A single roll and a shared election day lower the administrative cost of devolution and raise turnout, while identical integrity standards prevent the local tier from becoming a testing ground for practices intolerable at national level.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration); Article 6.3.1 (The Secret Ballot); Article 6.2.2 (Maintenance of the Rolls).\n"}, {"id": "07-local-government__local-finance", "path": "07-local-government/local-finance.md", "part": "07-local-government", "partTitle": "Local Government", "title": "Section 7.5 — Local Finance", "md": "# Section 7.5 — Local Finance\n\n> **Part 07 — Local Government** · Articles 7.5.1 – 7.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes local finance: own revenues, shared taxes and equalization transfers, the prohibition of unfunded mandates, transparent local budgets, and borrowing limits.*\n\n## Article 7.5.1 — Own Revenues\n\n1. Provinces and Municipalities shall have revenues of their own adequate to discharge their functions.\n2. The law may authorize provincial and municipal taxes, fees, and charges; their bases and ceilings shall be fixed by law, and overlapping levies on the same base shall be coordinated.\n3. Revenue-raising powers shall be exercised without discrimination against residents of other Provinces or Municipalities, consistently with Article 8.1.1 (Legality of Taxation).\n\n## Article 7.5.2 — Shared Taxes and Equalization\n\n1. The law may assign to Provinces and Municipalities defined shares in national taxes.\n2. An equalization system shall reduce disparities in fiscal capacity among Provinces and Municipalities; its formula shall be objective, transparent, and fixed by law.\n3. Transfers shall not be conditional on the political orientation of the recipient authority.\n\n## Article 7.5.3 — No Unfunded Mandates\n\n1. A law that imposes new obligations on Provinces or Municipalities shall provide the funding required to perform them.\n2. Where the Parliament later reduces that funding, it shall correspondingly reduce the obligation.\n3. A dispute over the adequacy of funding may be brought before the Constitutional Court under Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n\n## Article 7.5.4 — Local Budgets and Transparency\n\n1. Each Province and Municipality shall adopt an annual budget before its fiscal year begins.\n2. Budgets and final accounts shall be published in a form that allows citizens to understand the sources and uses of funds.\n3. Execution data shall be published at least quarterly.\n\n## Article 7.5.5 — Local Borrowing\n\n1. A Province or Municipality may borrow only within limits and procedures fixed by law, which shall distinguish investment borrowing from current financing.\n2. Long-term borrowing shall serve investment purposes, and repayment obligations shall not exceed the ceilings fixed by law.\n3. Borrowing by the Republic itself is governed by Part 08 (Public Finance).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Adequate own revenues anchor local accountability: where services are paid from local sources, voters can price them politically. Equalization then corrects the geography of fiscal capacity so that equal citizenship does not depend on the wealth of a postal code, and the unfunded-mandate rule closes the oldest loophole in devolution, the center legislating costs downward. Borrowing limits protect both local solvency and the credit of the Republic.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.1.1 (Legality of Taxation); Article 8.3.1 (Statutory Authorization); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n"}, {"id": "07-local-government__municipalities", "path": "07-local-government/municipalities.md", "part": "07-local-government", "partTitle": "Local Government", "title": "Section 7.3 — Municipalities", "md": "# Section 7.3 — Municipalities\n\n> **Part 07 — Local Government** · Articles 7.3.1 – 7.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes Municipalities as the local tier of government: their councils and mayors, their general competence for local services, their by-laws, and community participation.*\n\n## Article 7.3.1 — Right to Local Self-Government\n\n1. Municipalities shall have the right to regulate and manage local affairs within the limits of this Constitution and the law.\n2. Municipalities are established and structured by law; their boundaries shall respect the coherence of settlements and communities.\n3. A Municipality may not be abolished or merged without a hearing of the populations concerned.\n\n## Article 7.3.2 — General Competence and Services\n\n1. Municipalities shall provide the services of the local tier, including water supply, sanitation, local roads, local public transport, and the maintenance of public spaces.\n2. A Municipality possesses general competence for local affairs not reserved by this Constitution or assigned by law to another level.\n3. Municipal services shall be provided without discrimination among residents and at standards fixed by law.\n\n## Article 7.3.3 — Municipal Councils and Mayors\n\n1. Each Municipality shall have a municipal council elected by its residents.\n2. The council shall elect a mayor from among its members; the mayor conducts the administration of the Municipality and represents it.\n3. The council shall adopt the municipal budget and may establish committees and local offices.\n\n## Article 7.3.4 — Local By-Laws\n\n1. Municipal councils may adopt by-laws within their competence, subject to publication and to supervision limited to legality under Article 7.1.4 (Supervision Limited to Legality).\n2. A by-law shall not take effect before its publication in the manner fixed by law.\n\n## Article 7.3.5 — Community Participation\n\n1. Municipalities shall inform their residents regularly about plans and decisions and shall provide for consultation before major local decisions.\n2. The law shall provide for petitions, public hearings, and other instruments of participation, which may include participatory budgeting for part of the investment budget.\n3. Decisions of local administration affecting individual rights or interests shall observe the guarantees of fair administrative action.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Municipalities receive the services closest to daily life — water, sanitation, local roads — because the tier that observes demand is best placed to supply it. A council-elected mayor keeps the local executive accountable to the representative body, a choice real constitutions resolve differently without affecting democratic quality. Participatory instruments are constitutionalized as duties of process rather than transfers of final decision, preserving elected accountability while opening it to residents.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 7.1.4 (Supervision Limited to Legality); Article 7.5.4 (Local Budgets and Transparency); Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle).\n"}, {"id": "07-local-government__provinces", "path": "07-local-government/provinces.md", "part": "07-local-government", "partTitle": "Local Government", "title": "Section 7.1 — The Provinces", "md": "# Section 7.1 — The Provinces\n\n> **Part 07 — Local Government** · Articles 7.1.1 – 7.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Provinces as the primary subnational units of the Republic, their elected councils, their devolved competences, and supervision limited to legality.*\n\n## Article 7.1.1 — The Provinces\n\n1. The Provinces are the primary subnational units of the Republic and enjoy self-government within the limits of this Constitution.\n2. The number, boundaries, and names of the Provinces shall be fixed by law within constitutional principles of population equality and community coherence.\n3. The Republic remains a unitary State; devolution allocates the exercise of public functions, not sovereignty.\n\n## Article 7.1.2 — Provincial Councils\n\n1. Each Province shall have a Provincial Council elected by the voters of the Province for a term of five years.\n2. The Council shall exercise the devolved competences of the Province, adopt its budget, and supervise its administration.\n3. The Council shall elect a presiding officer from among its members and shall establish its own rules of procedure.\n\n## Article 7.1.3 — Devolved Competences\n\n1. The law shall assign to the Provinces the functions that can be effectively and efficiently discharged at that level, applying the subsidiarity principle under Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle).\n2. Devolved competences include, in particular, provincial infrastructure, spatial planning, implementation of environmental policy, and the coordination of schools and health services within the framework of national law.\n3. Competences not assigned remain with the organs of the Republic; assignments shall be construed in favor of self-government where the text is open.\n\n## Article 7.1.4 — Supervision Limited to Legality\n\n1. Supervision of provincial action shall extend only to conformity with this Constitution and with law.\n2. Supervision shall not review the opportunity or merit of provincial decisions and shall use the least intrusive adequate means.\n3. Supervisory measures shall state reasons and shall be subject to judicial review; disputes between a Province and the organs of the State are decided by the Constitutional Court under Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n\n## Article 7.1.5 — Representation of Provincial Interests\n\n1. The Provinces participate in national decision-making through the election of members of the Upper House under Article 3.3.2 (Election by Provincial Councils).\n2. The Parliament shall hear the Provinces before adopting legislation affecting their core competences.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Provinces carry the main devolution because a unitary State can decentralize functions without fragmenting sovereignty, and the Upper House of three members per Province loops provincial interests back into national law-making. Confining supervision to legality, as distinct from review of expediency, preserves genuine political responsibility at the provincial level. Judicial protection before the Constitutional Court turns supervision from a political privilege of the center into a reviewable legal act.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.3.2 (Election by Provincial Councils); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction); Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle).\n"}, {"id": "07-local-government__regions", "path": "07-local-government/regions.md", "part": "07-local-government", "partTitle": "Local Government", "title": "Section 7.2 — Regions", "md": "# Section 7.2 — Regions\n\n> **Part 07 — Local Government** · Articles 7.2.1 – 7.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs Regions as cooperative groupings of Provinces for shared services, with voluntary composition and no separate sovereignty.*\n\n## Article 7.2.1 — Nature of Regions\n\n1. Regions are cooperative associations of Provinces established for the joint performance of shared services.\n2. A Region possesses no sovereignty and no independent legislative power; it exercises only the functions conferred on it.\n3. Membership of a Region shall be voluntary; a Province may join or leave under the conditions fixed by law and by the founding agreement.\n\n## Article 7.2.2 — Competences\n\n1. Regions may perform functions delegated to them by their member Provinces or assigned by law where a Province alone cannot perform them effectively.\n2. Fields of cooperation include transport networks, health planning, environmental and water management, and disaster-response coordination.\n3. A Region shall not be assigned functions that interfere with the core self-government of its member Provinces.\n\n## Article 7.2.3 — Regional Organs and Finance\n\n1. Each Region shall have a regional council composed of delegates appointed by the Provincial Councils of its members, and such offices as the council establishes.\n2. The founding agreement shall fix the manner in which the regional council decides so that it fairly reflects the member Provinces.\n3. A Region shall be financed by contributions of its member Provinces and by revenue of its own undertakings; it shall publish its budget and accounts.\n\n## Article 7.2.4 — Supervision and Disputes\n\n1. Acts of a Region are subject to supervision limited to legality in the same manner as provincial action under Article 7.1.4 (Supervision Limited to Legality).\n2. Disputes among member Provinces, or between a Region and a Province or the organs of the State, shall be decided by the Constitutional Court under Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Regions are given the weakest institutional form on purpose: they solve genuine scale problems — river basins, transport corridors, tertiary health planning — without creating a new sovereign tier or a chamber of regional veto. Voluntary membership keeps cooperation from hardening into imposed bureaucracy, while delegate councils preserve the democratic chain back to the Provincial Councils. The design borrows the logic of functional cooperation without importing the rigidity of a fixed federal layer.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 7.1.4 (Supervision Limited to Legality); Article 7.6.2 (Allocation and Review of Functions); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__audit", "path": "08-public-finance/audit.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.8 — Audit of Public Funds", "md": "# Section 8.8 — Audit of Public Funds\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.8.1 – 8.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes the audit of public funds by the Office of the Auditor-General: scope, mandate, access, reports to Parliament, and follow-up obligations.*\n\n## Article 8.8.1 — Scope of Audit\n\n1. All public funds shall be subject to audit, including the revenues, expenditures, assets, and liabilities of every organ of the State.\n2. Funds administered outside the budget and entities in which the State holds a controlling interest shall be included.\n3. Audit extends to recipients of public money with respect to the use of that money.\n\n## Article 8.8.2 — Mandate\n\n1. The Office of the Auditor-General shall audit:\n   (a) the financial statements of audited entities for truth and fairness;\n   (b) compliance of revenues and expenditures with law; and\n   (c) the economy, efficiency, and effectiveness of public programs.\n2. The Office performs these functions independently; its status and appointment are governed by Part 20 (Independent Institutions).\n3. The Office shall apply recognized international auditing standards.\n\n## Article 8.8.3 — Access and Cooperation\n\n1. The Office of the Auditor-General shall have unrestricted access to the records, premises, and information of audited entities.\n2. Officials shall furnish complete and truthful information; no organ may withhold records or instruct any person to withhold them.\n3. Confidentiality duties owed to third parties shall not defeat audit access, subject to the lawful protection of specially protected data.\n\n## Article 8.8.4 — Reports to Parliament\n\n1. The Office shall report annually on the accounts of the State and on the results of audits, and may issue special reports on matters of importance.\n2. Reports shall be laid before the Parliament, examined by a committee responsible for public accounts, and published without delay.\n3. The Government shall not alter the findings of the Office.\n\n## Article 8.8.5 — Follow-Up Obligations\n\n1. An audited entity shall respond to findings with a corrective plan within the period fixed by law.\n2. The Government shall report to the Parliament, at fixed intervals, on the implementation of corrective measures.\n3. The Office shall verify implementation and shall report persistent non-compliance to the Parliament.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Audit is the closing loop of the authorization chain: the Parliament appropriates, the administration spends, and the Office of the Auditor-General reports back on the Parliament's behalf. Financial, compliance, and performance auditing together answer not only whether money was lawful but whether it was worth spending. The follow-up clauses respond to the known weakness of audit institutions, which is not finding problems but getting them corrected.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.6.1 (No Expenditure Without Authorization); Article 8.6.4 (Publication of Execution Data); Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__budget", "path": "08-public-finance/budget.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.2 — The Budget", "md": "# Section 8.2 — The Budget\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.2.1 – 8.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the annual budget: preparation and unity, parliamentary adoption before the fiscal year, transparency, and the contingency reserve.*\n\n## Article 8.2.1 — The Annual Budget Law\n\n1. All revenues and expenditures of the Republic for each fiscal year shall be fixed by the annual budget law, which the Parliament shall adopt before the fiscal year begins.\n2. The Government shall submit the draft budget to the Lower House in sufficient time for its adoption before the fiscal year begins.\n3. Multi-year expenditure shall be stated separately in the budget law.\n\n## Article 8.2.2 — Unity and Completeness\n\n1. The budget shall comprise all revenues and all expenditures of the State, without offsetting and without funds outside the budget.\n2. Revenues shall not be earmarked except as provided by law for specific funds subject to the audit of Article 8.8.2 (Mandate).\n3. The budget shall be presented in a structure that permits informed parliamentary decision.\n\n## Article 8.2.3 — Parliamentary Adoption\n\n1. The Parliament holds primacy over appropriations; it may amend the draft budget, reducing expenditure freely and increasing it only where offset by corresponding revenue or savings.\n2. A money bill follows the procedure of Article 3.3.5 (Powers over Money Bills), including the limited delay applicable to money bills.\n3. Where the budget is not adopted before the fiscal year begins, the Lower House may by law authorize continuation of prior appropriations for a strictly limited period; no new programs may be started under such authority.\n\n## Article 8.2.4 — Budget Transparency\n\n1. The draft budget, its supporting documents, and its macroeconomic assumptions shall be published upon submission.\n2. The Parliament shall hold public hearings on the draft budget.\n3. The adopted budget, its execution, and the final accounts shall be published in open data form.\n\n## Article 8.2.5 — Contingency Reserve\n\n1. The budget law may establish a contingency reserve for unforeseeable and unavoidable needs.\n2. Drawings on the reserve shall be reported to the Parliament without delay and shall be approved by the Parliament after the fact within the period fixed by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The deadline — adopted before the fiscal year — is treated as a constitutional rule rather than an aspiration, because the interim-continuation clause is deliberately narrow: no new programs and a strict time limit. Parliamentary primacy over appropriations includes the power to amend, with offsetting requirements preserving overall balance. A contingency reserve recognizes that no parliament can foresee everything, while ex-post approval keeps even surprises inside the constitutional circuit of authorization.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.3.5 (Powers over Money Bills); Article 8.6.1 (No Expenditure Without Authorization); Article 8.8.2 (Mandate).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__central-bank", "path": "08-public-finance/central-bank.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.4 — The Central Bank", "md": "# Section 8.4 — The Central Bank\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.4.1 – 8.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Central Bank: independence, the primary mandate of price stability, exclusive note issue, instruments, and accountability.*\n\n## Article 8.4.1 — Status and Mandate\n\n1. The Central Bank shall be an independent constitutional institution and shall neither seek nor take instructions from the Government or any other organ of the State.\n2. The primary objective of the Central Bank shall be to maintain price stability; without prejudice to that objective, it shall support the general economic policy of the Republic.\n3. The Central Bank shall act as fiscal agent of the State, banker to credit institutions, and manager of the official foreign reserves.\n\n## Article 8.4.2 — Governance\n\n1. The Central Bank shall be directed by a governor and a governing board appointed for fixed terms longer than the parliamentary term and removable only for cause under a procedure fixed by law.\n2. Members shall declare their interests and shall take no instructions from political bodies during their term.\n3. The law shall ensure resources adequate for its mandate.\n\n## Article 8.4.3 — Exclusive Note Issue\n\n1. The Central Bank shall have the exclusive right to issue banknotes and coins of the national currency.\n2. The banknotes and coins it issues shall be the only legal tender in the territory of the Republic, as provided by law.\n3. The law shall protect the integrity of the currency and the security of banknotes and coins.\n\n## Article 8.4.4 — Instruments and Financial Stability\n\n1. The Central Bank shall determine its monetary policy and shall conduct open-market operations, set policy rates, and require minimum reserves.\n2. Operations with the State, including temporary purchases and sales of securities, shall be conducted on market terms for monetary policy purposes and shall not constitute monetary financing under Article 8.3.4 (Prohibition of Monetary Financing).\n3. The Central Bank shall contribute to financial stability and may act as lender of last resort to solvent institutions facing a liquidity shortage, under conditions fixed by law.\n4. Emergency liquidity support shall be reported to the Parliament after the fact; where a bank must be resolved, Article 9.6.5 (Resolution of Failing Banks) applies.\n\n## Article 8.4.5 — Accountability and Reporting\n\n1. The Central Bank shall publish its decisions, a monetary policy report, and audited annual financial statements.\n2. The governor shall report to the Parliament at least annually and shall answer its questions.\n3. The accounts of the Central Bank shall be audited annually by an independent external auditor appointed by the Parliament.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Independence with a single primary mandate follows the consensus that political time horizons systematically bias monetary policy toward inflation; accountability through publication and parliamentary reporting supplies the democratic legitimacy that independence withdraws from daily politics. The narrow market-terms exception reconciles the monetary-financing prohibition with routine open-market operations. Exclusive note issue makes the currency a public monopoly with a single accountable operator.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.3.4 (Prohibition of Monetary Financing); Article 9.6.3 (Independence of Supervision); Article 9.6.5 (Resolution of Failing Banks).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__fiscal-responsibility", "path": "08-public-finance/fiscal-responsibility.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.7 — Fiscal Responsibility", "md": "# Section 8.7 — Fiscal Responsibility\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.7.1 – 8.7.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section imposes the duties of fiscal responsibility: intergenerational sustainability, countercyclical management, reference targets fixed by law, an independent fiscal council, and risk reporting.*\n\n## Article 8.7.1 — Principle of Sustainability\n\n1. Fiscal policy shall be conducted so that present financing needs do not shift unreasonable burdens to future generations.\n2. In periods of exceptional revenue, the Republic shall build reserves or reduce debt, and countercyclical buffers shall be maintained so that downturns need not be met by procyclical retrenchment.\n3. Windfall revenues from non-renewable natural resources shall be treated under the fund regime of Part 13 (Environment and Natural Resources).\n\n## Article 8.7.2 — Reference Targets\n\n1. The law shall fix reference values for the deficit and for the debt of the Republic, consistent with Article 8.3.3 (Sustainability Limits).\n2. Budgets shall be based on prudent and realistic macroeconomic and revenue assumptions.\n3. Deviation from a reference value requires a published justification and a defined return path.\n\n## Article 8.7.3 — Independent Fiscal Council\n\n1. There shall be an independent fiscal council which assesses macroeconomic forecasts, compliance with the reference values, and long-term fiscal sustainability.\n2. The council shall have unrestricted access to the data of the public administration and shall publish its assessments.\n3. The assessment of the council on the draft budget shall be laid before the Parliament before adoption.\n\n## Article 8.7.4 — Fiscal Risk Reporting\n\n1. The budget documents shall include a statement of fiscal risks, including contingent liabilities, guarantees, and the finances of public entities.\n2. The Government shall update the statement during the year where a material risk crystallizes.\n3. The statement shall distinguish risks that can be absorbed within existing appropriations from those requiring a parliamentary decision.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Intergenerational equity is given operational content through buffers and reference targets rather than through a single numerical golden rule, because the right number varies with the economy and the era. An independent fiscal council corrects the documented optimism bias of government forecasts at the moment it matters most, before the budget is adopted. Risk reporting extends visibility to the guarantees and contingent liabilities where hidden debt historically accumulates.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.3.3 (Sustainability Limits); Article 8.2.1 (The Annual Budget Law); Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__government-spending", "path": "08-public-finance/government-spending.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.6 — Government Spending", "md": "# Section 8.6 — Government Spending\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.6.1 – 8.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the execution of public spending: the authorization requirement, accounting officers, in-year reallocation, and publication of execution data.*\n\n## Article 8.6.1 — No Expenditure Without Authorization\n\n1. Expenditure of public funds shall be made only under an appropriation of the budget law or under another statutory authorization.\n2. No organ may commit the Republic to expenditure without the authorization required by this Part.\n3. Expenditure shall be economical and shall pursue the purpose for which it was appropriated.\n\n## Article 8.6.2 — Accounting Officers\n\n1. Each appropriation shall have an accounting officer, designated as provided by law, personally responsible for its lawful and economical execution.\n2. An accounting officer shall refuse a payment ordered in breach of this Article and shall report the refusal to the Office of the Auditor-General and to the Parliament.\n3. The responsibility of the accounting officer does not displace the political responsibility of the minister concerned.\n\n## Article 8.6.3 — In-Year Reallocation\n\n1. The budget law shall fix the rules under which funds may be moved between lines within an appropriation during the year.\n2. Transfers between appropriations of different purposes, and any increase in total expenditure, require a supplementary budget law adopted under Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption).\n3. Reallocation shall be reported to the Parliament in the periodic execution reports.\n\n## Article 8.6.4 — Publication of Execution Data\n\n1. The Government shall publish monthly data on the execution of the budget, including revenues collected and expenditure by appropriation line.\n2. Data shall be published in open data form, in a timely manner, and in a form capable of comparison with the adopted budget.\n3. A final account of each fiscal year shall be laid before the Parliament and audited under Article 8.8.2 (Mandate).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The authorization rule is made operational by naming a person — the accounting officer — who must obey the appropriation or refuse and report; diffuse responsibility is how unlawful spending survives audits. Strict limits on in-year reallocation protect the parliamentary bargain from administrative erosion, because every transfer beyond a line is a small second budget. Monthly publication converts execution from an annual surprise into a continuously observable process.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption); Article 8.8.2 (Mandate); Article 8.2.5 (Contingency Reserve).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__public-debt", "path": "08-public-finance/public-debt.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.3 — Public Debt", "md": "# Section 8.3 — Public Debt\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.3.1 – 8.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs public borrowing and debt: statutory authorization, a published debt strategy, sustainability limits fixed by law, and the prohibition of monetary financing.*\n\n## Article 8.3.1 — Statutory Authorization\n\n1. The Republic may borrow, guarantee, or assume liabilities only by or under a statute that fixes the purpose and the ceiling.\n2. An authorization shall state the maximum principal and the purposes for which proceeds may be used.\n3. Guarantees and assumed liabilities shall be recorded and shall count toward the sustainability limits fixed by law.\n\n## Article 8.3.2 — Debt Strategy and Reporting\n\n1. The Government shall publish a medium-term debt strategy before each fiscal year and shall report annually on its execution.\n2. A consolidated register of the debt, guarantees, and contingent liabilities of the State shall be maintained and published.\n3. The Parliament shall be informed without delay of borrowing under standing authorizations.\n\n## Article 8.3.3 — Sustainability Limits\n\n1. The law shall fix reference values for the annual deficit and for total public debt, consistent with Article 8.7.1 (Principle of Sustainability).\n2. Where a reference value is exceeded, the Government shall present a corrective plan, and the Parliament shall take the decisions it requires.\n3. Escape clauses for exceptional events shall be defined by law, narrow in scope, and subject to reporting and a defined return path within the reference values.\n\n## Article 8.3.4 — Prohibition of Monetary Financing\n\n1. The State shall not obtain credit from the Central Bank, and the Central Bank shall not purchase debt instruments of the State at primary issuance.\n2. Overdraft facilities of the Treasury with the Central Bank are prohibited.\n3. Narrow operations of the Central Bank for monetary policy purposes are governed by Article 8.4.4 (Instruments and Financial Stability) and do not constitute monetary financing.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Borrowing is taxation postponed, so it is subjected to the same parliamentary authorship as taxation itself. A published strategy and a consolidated register of liabilities, including guarantees, make the true debt visible before elections rather than after. The monetary-financing prohibition protects the currency from fiscal dominance, while the narrow exception in Section 8.4 keeps ordinary monetary operations lawful without giving the State a hidden lender.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.4.4 (Instruments and Financial Stability); Article 8.7.1 (Principle of Sustainability); Article 8.6.1 (No Expenditure Without Authorization).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__public-procurement", "path": "08-public-finance/public-procurement.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.5 — Public Procurement", "md": "# Section 8.5 — Public Procurement\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.5.1 – 8.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs public procurement: open competitive procedures, value for money, integrity, an independent review body, and transparency through electronic publication.*\n\n## Article 8.5.1 — Principles\n\n1. Public procurement shall be open, competitive, non-discriminatory, and transparent, and shall achieve value for money.\n2. Award shall follow equal treatment of bidders, proportionality, and the protection of competition under Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition).\n3. Procurement shall not be used to grant favors to political associates or to distort competition.\n\n## Article 8.5.2 — Procedures and Award Criteria\n\n1. Contracts above the thresholds fixed by law shall be awarded after public advertisement of the opportunity, the criteria, and the procedure.\n2. Award criteria shall be fixed in advance and applied as published; the decision shall be reasoned and notified to the bidders.\n3. Direct award shall be permitted only in the narrow cases fixed by law, shall be documented, and shall be reported.\n\n## Article 8.5.3 — Integrity Rules\n\n1. Persons involved in procurement shall declare conflicts of interest and shall withdraw where their impartiality may be compromised.\n2. Bidders who have engaged in corruption, fraud, or collusion may be excluded from procedures for the period fixed by law, after a decision subject to review.\n3. The splitting of contracts to evade thresholds is prohibited.\n\n## Article 8.5.4 — Independent Review Body\n\n1. An independent review body shall hear the complaints of bidders concerning the conduct and the outcome of procedures.\n2. It may order the correction of the procedure, the suspension of an award, or the annulment of a decision, and may impose the sanctions fixed by law.\n3. Its decisions shall be reasoned, published in anonymized form, and subject to appeal to the courts.\n\n## Article 8.5.5 — E-Procurement and Publication\n\n1. The law shall provide for an electronic procurement system as the ordinary channel for advertising, submission, and record-keeping.\n2. Notices, award decisions, contract values, and, for significant contracts, performance reports shall be published in open data form.\n3. The records of procedures shall be retained for audit under Article 8.8.2 (Mandate).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Competition for public money is the cheapest anti-corruption technology available to the State, which is why openness and advertised criteria come before any efficiency exception. An independent review body gives losing bidders a fast, expert remedy that ordinary courts alone cannot match in speed, while judicial appeal remains intact. Publishing contract values in open data lets journalists and competitors do the oversight that inspectors cannot scale.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition); Article 8.8.2 (Mandate); Article 8.6.4 (Publication of Execution Data).\n"}, {"id": "08-public-finance__taxation", "path": "08-public-finance/taxation.md", "part": "08-public-finance", "partTitle": "Public Finance", "title": "Section 8.1 — Taxation", "md": "# Section 8.1 — Taxation\n\n> **Part 08 — Public Finance** · Articles 8.1.1 – 8.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section lays the constitutional foundations of taxation: the legality requirement, equity according to ability to pay, legal certainty, anti-abuse rules, and judicial review of assessments.*\n\n## Article 8.1.1 — Legality of Taxation\n\n1. Taxes, duties, and compulsory levies shall be imposed, altered, or abolished only by or under a law.\n2. No executive organ may introduce a tax or levy of any kind; charges for specific services rendered require statutory authorization.\n3. The elements of every tax — the persons liable, the base, the rate, and the exemptions — shall be defined in the law itself or by delegation clearly bounded by it.\n\n## Article 8.1.2 — Equity and Ability to Pay\n\n1. Taxation shall be apportioned according to economic capacity and shall be general; it shall not discriminate arbitrarily between persons in like circumstances.\n2. The law shall take account of a minimum subsistence level that remains untaxed.\n3. Tax benefits shall not be granted on grounds unrelated to the purposes of the tax system.\n\n## Article 8.1.3 — Non-Retroactivity and Certainty\n\n1. A tax law shall not apply to facts occurring before its entry into force.\n2. Retroactive taxation is permitted only where it benefits the taxpayer or where a serious defect of legality is being remedied, and never beyond a short period fixed by law.\n3. Tax law shall be drafted clearly, published before entry into force, and interpreted according to its purpose and the good faith of the taxpayer.\n\n## Article 8.1.4 — Anti-Abuse Rules\n\n1. The law may counter arrangements adopted for the principal purpose of avoiding tax, looking to their substance rather than their form.\n2. Anti-abuse powers shall be exercised under published rules and shall not override legitimate planning based on the express terms of the law.\n3. Tax information may be exchanged with foreign authorities under ratified agreements for the prevention of evasion.\n\n## Article 8.1.5 — Review of Tax Assessments\n\n1. A taxpayer may object to an assessment before the tax administration and may appeal to a court or an independent tribunal.\n2. Unlawfully collected amounts shall be refunded without undue delay.\n3. The law may provide for provisional collection pending review, subject to repayment of amounts found unlawful.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*No taxation without law is the oldest fiscal guarantee in constitutional history, and it is restated here absolutely, including against disguised levies. Legality keeps taxation the work of the elected Parliament; equity supplies the substantive standard by which those choices are measured. Non-retroactivity protects the reliability that long-term economic decisions require, while the anti-abuse clause acknowledges that formal legality alone cannot defeat engineered avoidance.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption); Article 7.5.1 (Own Revenues); Article 5.6.2 (Concrete Review).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__banking", "path": "09-economy/banking.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.6 — Banking", "md": "# Section 9.6 — Banking\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.6.1 – 9.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section regulates the business of banking through licensing, prudential supervision, deposit protection, and resolution, with supervision independent of the Government and the Central Bank's own mandate remaining governed by Section 8.4.*\n\n## Article 9.6.1 — Licensing of Banks\n\n1. No person may conduct banking business, meaning the taking of deposits or similar repayable funds from the public, without a license issued under law.\n2. Licenses shall be granted to applicants satisfying solvency, fitness, and governance requirements, and may be withdrawn only for cause after a fair procedure.\n\n## Article 9.6.2 — Prudential Supervision\n\n1. Banks shall maintain capital, liquidity, and large-exposure limits fixed by law, calibrated to internationally recognized prudential standards.\n2. An independent supervisory authority shall supervise banks continuously, examine them on site, and require the information necessary to assess their soundness.\n3. Banking groups shall be supervised on a consolidated basis, and information exchanged between supervisors shall remain confidential.\n\n## Article 9.6.3 — Independence of Supervision\n\n1. Neither the Government nor any other organ of the State may instruct the supervisory authority in the exercise of its powers over individual institutions.\n2. The supervisory authority shall cooperate with the Central Bank in matters of financial stability without prejudice to the institutional mandate of the Central Bank under Article 8.4.1 (Status and Mandate).\n\n## Article 9.6.4 — Deposit Protection\n\n1. A deposit insurance scheme shall protect depositors up to a statutory limit and shall be financed by contributions of credit institutions rather than by the general budget.\n2. The scheme shall pay out promptly upon the unavailability of deposits, and the statutory limit shall be reviewed periodically.\n\n## Article 9.6.5 — Resolution of Failing Banks\n\n1. A failing bank shall be resolved under a special statutory regime that preserves critical functions, allocates losses to shareholders and creditors in the order of priority fixed by law, and protects insured depositors.\n2. Resolution costs shall not be borne by public funds except as a last resort, after the resources of the bank are exhausted, and only subject to repayment obligations.\n3. Emergency liquidity support shall remain subject to Article 8.4.4 (Instruments and Financial Stability).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The capital, liquidity, and consolidated-supervision clauses track the Basel framework, principally Basel III, while the resolution and bail-in rules draw on the European Banking Union's Single Resolution Mechanism. Deposit insurance follows the post-crisis consensus on meaningful coverage and rapid payout, financed by the industry rather than the taxpayer. Supervision is deliberately separated from governmental instruction to resist forbearance toward politically connected banks. The accepted trade-off is that higher prudential standards constrain credit supply, accepted here in exchange for reduced systemic risk.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.4.1 (Status and Mandate); Article 8.4.4 (Instruments and Financial Stability); Article 9.7.4 (The Securities Regulator).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__bankruptcy", "path": "09-economy/bankruptcy.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.8 — Bankruptcy", "md": "# Section 9.8 — Bankruptcy\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.8.1 – 9.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section guarantees honest debtors an orderly, collective path through insolvency and creditors equal treatment, preferring reorganization where a viable enterprise can be preserved.*\n\n## Article 9.8.1 — Access to Insolvency Proceedings\n\n1. A debtor unable to pay debts as they fall due shall have access to insolvency proceedings under a uniform law enacted by Parliament.\n2. Proceedings shall be opened without undue delay upon the application of the debtor or of a creditor, and the estate of the debtor shall be administered for the collective benefit of creditors.\n3. The opening of proceedings shall stay individual enforcement actions against the estate.\n\n## Article 9.8.2 — Equal Treatment of Creditors\n\n1. Creditors of the same rank shall be treated equally and shall share distributions in proportion to their claims; priorities shall exist only as fixed by law.\n2. Claims of employees for remuneration earned before the opening of proceedings shall enjoy the priority assigned to them under Article 10.3.4 (Timely Payment).\n3. Creditors shall be notified of the opening of proceedings and of the periods fixed for filing their claims.\n\n## Article 9.8.3 — Reorganization\n\n1. Before liquidation, the law shall offer viable debtors a reorganization procedure enabling continuation of the enterprise where it promises better satisfaction of creditors than immediate liquidation.\n2. A reorganization plan may bind dissenting creditors only where they receive at least what liquidation would yield to them and the plan is confirmed by a court.\n\n## Article 9.8.4 — Discharge of Honest Debtors\n\n1. An honest natural-person debtor shall receive discharge of residual debts after a reasonable period fixed by law, not longer than necessary to protect creditors and deter abuse.\n2. Discharge shall not extend to debts arising from fraud, willful injury, or claims excluded by law.\n\n## Article 9.8.5 — Administration and Review\n\n1. Insolvency proceedings shall be conducted by a qualified administrator under judicial supervision, with duties to report to the creditors and to the court.\n2. The costs of administration shall be transparent and proportionate to the estate.\n3. The law shall provide for recognition of, and cooperation with, foreign insolvency proceedings consistent with the international obligations of the Republic.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The reorganization-first model reflects Chapter 11 of the United States Bankruptcy Code; the equal-treatment and stay rules follow the German Insolvenzordnung tradition; and the cross-border clause adapts the UNCITRAL Model Law on Cross-Border Insolvency. The fresh-start discharge accepts creditor losses in exchange for entrepreneurship and the rehabilitation of honest debtors. Court confirmation of plans and the liquidation-floor test protect minorities against coerced restructuring. The accepted trade-off is moral hazard, contained here by the fraud and willful-injury exclusions from discharge.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.4.5 (Dissolution and Insolvency); Article 10.3.4 (Timely Payment); Article 5.6.4 (Standards of Review).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__competition", "path": "09-economy/competition.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.2 — Competition", "md": "# Section 9.2 — Competition\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.2.1 – 9.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section establishes the protection of competition as a constitutional economic principle and entrusts its enforcement to an independent competition authority.*\n\n## Article 9.2.1 — Protection of Competition\n\n1. The Republic shall maintain an economic order founded on free and undistorted competition, which shall serve the welfare of consumers and the efficient allocation of resources.\n2. Parliament shall enact rules preserving competition as the primary coordinator of markets, in conjunction with the sectoral provisions of this Part.\n3. No organ of the State may adopt measures that eliminate competition in a market except as expressly authorized by a law serving a public interest and no broader than that interest requires.\n\n## Article 9.2.2 — The Competition Authority\n\n1. There shall be an independent competition authority, separate from the ministries, which shall investigate infringements, order remedies, and impose fines proportionate to the gravity and duration of an infringement.\n2. The authority shall act upon its own initiative or upon complaint, shall decide with published reasons, and shall be subject to full judicial review of both findings and remedies.\n3. Its members shall be appointed for fixed terms on grounds of competence and integrity and may be removed only for cause by a procedure prescribed by law.\n\n## Article 9.2.3 — Prohibited Agreements\n\n1. Agreements between undertakings, decisions of associations of undertakings, and concerted practices that prevent, restrict, or distort competition shall be prohibited by law.\n2. The law may exempt an agreement that improves production or distribution and affords consumers a fair share of the resulting benefit, provided that the essentials of competition are not eliminated.\n\n## Article 9.2.4 — Abuse of Dominance\n\n1. The abuse by one or more undertakings of a dominant position in a market shall be prohibited.\n2. Abuse shall include, in particular, exploitative pricing, exclusionary conduct toward competitors and suppliers, and discrimination between trading parties without objective justification.\n\n## Article 9.2.5 — Concentrations and Public Undertakings\n\n1. A concentration between undertakings that would significantly impede effective competition may be prohibited or approved subject to conditions by the competition authority, under a transparent and evidence-based procedure.\n2. Public undertakings and enterprises granted special or exclusive rights shall be subject to the same competition rules as private undertakings, without prejudice to Article 9.3.2 (Legal Monopolies).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The architecture follows the two-pillar prohibition of anticompetitive agreements and abuse of dominance consolidated in European Union competition law, with merger review added as a third safeguard against structural foreclosure. An independent authority, with fixed-term members and published decisions, guards enforcement against capture by incumbent firms and political interference. Objective-justification and exemption clauses preserve space for efficiency defenses. The accepted trade-off is that bright-line prohibitions may occasionally condemn efficiency-enhancing conduct.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.1.4 (The Social-Market Economy); Article 9.3.2 (Legal Monopolies); Article 5.6.3 (Review of Executive Action).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__corporations", "path": "09-economy/corporations.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.4 — Corporations", "md": "# Section 9.4 — Corporations\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.4.1 – 9.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section provides the constitutional basis for incorporation, limited liability, the duties of those who direct corporations, and the transparency that the corporate form owes to the public.*\n\n## Article 9.4.1 — Incorporation by Law\n\n1. Every person shall be free to establish, join, and manage corporations, cooperatives, and other forms of enterprise under general laws of equal application.\n2. Incorporation shall not require case-by-case permission of the State, and no corporate form shall be reserved to any class of persons.\n3. Cooperatives and mutual societies shall be recognized by law and may not be dissolved or deprived of their assets except by law and for cause.\n\n## Article 9.4.2 — Limited Liability\n\n1. A corporation validly formed shall possess legal personality distinct from its members, and the liability of members shall be limited to their contribution.\n2. The corporate form may not be used to defeat obligations imposed by law, to commit fraud, or to evade responsibility for wrongs; the law may disregard it in such cases.\n\n## Article 9.4.3 — Directors' Duties\n\n1. Directors shall manage the corporation with due care and loyalty to its lawful purpose and shall account for their management to the members.\n2. Directors shall not accept benefits from third parties in connection with their office and shall disclose conflicts of interest to the supervisory organ.\n3. The law may require undertakings above a size fixed by law to provide employees with information, consultation, and participation rights consistent with Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work).\n\n## Article 9.4.4 — Transparency of Ownership\n\n1. Corporations shall keep accounts according to uniform standards, shall have them audited where prescribed by law, and shall file them in a public register.\n2. The natural persons who ultimately own or control a corporation shall be recorded in a register of beneficial owners accessible to the competent authorities, and to the public to the extent provided by law.\n\n## Article 9.4.5 — Dissolution and Insolvency\n\n1. Corporations shall be supervised by the competent registries and the courts, which may order dissolution for persistent abuse of the corporate form.\n2. Dissolution shall not extinguish obligations owed to creditors, employees, or the public treasury.\n3. The insolvency of a corporation shall be governed by Article 9.8.1 (Access to Insolvency Proceedings) and the articles that follow it.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The incorporation model chosen here is the registration system, which treats company formation as a right subject to general law, in preference to concession systems that leave formation to administrative grace. Beneficial-ownership registers follow the transparency standards promoted by the Financial Action Task Force against money laundering. Directors' duties are stated as care and loyalty, with employee participation left to statutory calibration. The accepted trade-off is that limited liability externalizes part of business risk onto creditors and society, a cost offset here by transparency, audit, and abuse doctrines.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.1 (Right to property); Article 9.8.1 (Access to Insolvency Proceedings); Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__economic-freedom", "path": "09-economy/economic-freedom.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.1 — Economic Freedom", "md": "# Section 9.1 — Economic Freedom\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.1.1 – 9.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section guarantees the economic liberty of every person — freedom of contract, occupation, and enterprise — as the foundation of the social-market economy of the Republic, subject only to limits of general application imposed by law.*\n\n## Article 9.1.1 — Economic Liberty\n\n1. Every person shall be free to engage in economic activity, to choose and practice an occupation, and to found, acquire, and operate an enterprise alone or with others.\n2. These freedoms bind every organ of the State and every person exercising public authority.\n3. They may be limited only by a law of general application, never by individual discretionary measures.\n\n## Article 9.1.2 — Occupation and Enterprise\n\n1. Access to an occupation may be made subject to licensing or qualification requirements only where the law pursues a legitimate aim and the requirements are proportionate and applied without discrimination.\n2. Qualifications obtained in other jurisdictions shall be recognized to the extent prescribed by law.\n3. No enterprise may be transferred from its owner against the will of the owner except under the expropriation guarantees of Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation).\n\n## Article 9.1.3 — Freedom of Contract\n\n1. Parties shall be free to determine the content and terms of their contracts.\n2. The law may restrict freedom of contract to protect consumers, employees, tenants, and other structurally weaker parties, and to safeguard overriding public interests.\n3. Standard terms offered for general use shall be subject to statutory content control, and terms creating excessive imbalance to the detriment of the weaker party may be declared void.\n\n## Article 9.1.4 — The Social-Market Economy\n\n1. The economic order of the Republic is a social market economy, in which free initiative and competition are reconciled with solidarity and the protection of workers and the environment.\n2. Private ownership of the means of production is the general rule; the State may own or operate an enterprise only under a law serving a public purpose.\n3. Public and private enterprises shall compete on equal terms, and no enterprise shall enjoy preferential treatment or exemption from generally applicable law by reason of public ownership.\n\n## Article 9.1.5 — Limits and Judicial Review\n\n1. Economic activity may be regulated by law for the protection of public health and safety, consumers, employees, the environment, competition, and the stability of the financial system.\n2. Every restriction of economic freedom shall be general, proportionate, and non-retroactive, and shall remain open to effective judicial review.\n3. Where a restriction amounts to expropriation, just and prompt compensation is owed under Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*These articles adopt the ordoliberal insight that the State should shape a competitive order rather than direct production from above. The German Basic Law guarantees of occupational freedom and property, and the business-freedom clause of the European Charter of Fundamental Rights, supply the comparative models. The trade-off is proportionality review of economic regulation, protecting liberty at some cost in regulatory uncertainty.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.1 (Right to property); Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation); Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__financial-markets", "path": "09-economy/financial-markets.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.7 — Financial Markets", "md": "# Section 9.7 — Financial Markets\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.7.1 – 9.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section secures the freedom of capital markets and imposes the disclosure, integrity, and investor-protection rules on which their functioning depends.*\n\n## Article 9.7.1 — Freedom of Capital Markets\n\n1. The issuance, trading, and transfer of securities and other financial instruments shall be free, subject to the authorization and conduct requirements of this section.\n2. No restriction may be imposed on capital markets except by law for investor protection, market integrity, monetary policy, or public security.\n\n## Article 9.7.2 — Disclosure\n\n1. An offer of securities to the public shall be preceded by a prospectus containing the information necessary for investors to assess the issuer and the securities.\n2. Issuers of traded instruments shall publish periodic financial reports audited to statutory standards, and material information shall be disclosed to the market in a timely and equal manner.\n3. Persons responsible for a prospectus containing false or misleading statements shall be liable to compensate investors who acquired securities in reliance on it.\n\n## Article 9.7.3 — Market Integrity\n\n1. Insider dealing, unlawful disclosure of inside information, and market manipulation shall be prohibited by law.\n2. Market abuse shall be sanctioned by administrative fines or criminal penalties proportionate to the gravity of the conduct, without prejudice to the civil liability of wrongdoers toward injured parties.\n\n## Article 9.7.4 — The Securities Regulator\n\n1. An independent authority shall authorize, supervise, and sanction participants in financial markets, publish its rules and reasoned decisions, and report annually on the state of the markets.\n2. Its members shall be appointed for fixed terms on grounds of competence and integrity and may be removed only for cause by a procedure prescribed by law.\n3. The regulator shall cooperate with the supervisory authority for banks under Article 9.6.2 (Prudential Supervision) in the oversight of financial conglomerates.\n\n## Article 9.7.5 — Investor Protection\n\n1. Providers of investment services shall act honestly, fairly, and professionally in the best interest of their clients, shall assess the suitability of the products they offer, and shall communicate with clients in a clear and non-misleading manner.\n2. Investors shall have access to effective collective remedies for losses caused by violations of this section, without prejudice to Article 5.6.5 (Effective Remedies).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The disclosure philosophy follows the United States securities tradition of sunlight as the best disinfectant, operationalized through prospectus and periodic-reporting duties, while the integrity rules mirror modern market-abuse statutes. Suitability duties and collective redress answer the information asymmetry that disclosure alone leaves open between professionals and households. An independent regulator with fixed-term members completes the design, mirroring the institutional guarantees of the competition and banking authorities. The accepted trade-off is compliance cost, borne disproportionately by smaller issuers.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.6.2 (Prudential Supervision); Article 5.6.5 (Effective Remedies); Article 9.1.5 (Limits and Judicial Review).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__intellectual-property", "path": "09-economy/intellectual-property.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.9 — Intellectual Property", "md": "# Section 9.9 — Intellectual Property\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.9.1 – 9.9.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section protects creations of the mind as limited statutory rights, balanced against public access, education, research, and the public domain.*\n\n## Article 9.9.1 — Protection of Creations\n\n1. The law shall protect literary, scientific, and artistic works, inventions, distinctive signs, and other creations of the mind through rights of intellectual property.\n2. Such rights are guarantees of property within the meaning of Article 1.8.1 (Right to property) and may not be revoked except for cause and after a fair procedure.\n3. Protection shall not extend to abstract ideas, discoveries, methods of scientific thought, or mere information as such.\n\n## Article 9.9.2 — Limited Duration and Scope\n\n1. Exclusive rights shall be granted for limited periods and shall extend no further than the contribution of the protected creation requires.\n2. Rights shall not be renewable indefinitely, and any extension of protection shall apply only prospectively.\n\n## Article 9.9.3 — Exceptions for Education and Research\n\n1. The law shall permit quotation, criticism, commentary, teaching, research, private use, and archival reproduction to an extent consistent with the normal exploitation of the work.\n2. Exceptions shall secure access to educational materials for institutions of learning, consistent with Article 1.10.1 (Right to education), and accessible formats for persons with disabilities.\n\n## Article 9.9.4 — Compulsory Licenses\n\n1. In a public-health emergency, the law shall permit the grant of compulsory licenses enabling the production or importation of patented medicines, with adequate remuneration of the rightsholder.\n2. Compulsory licenses may otherwise be granted only for refusal to deal on reasonable terms, for anticompetitive conduct established under Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition), or for other public interests defined by law.\n3. The availability of compulsory licenses shall not prejudice the affordability safeguards for medicines under Article 12.5.4 (Access and Pricing Abuse).\n\n## Article 9.9.5 — The Public Domain\n\n1. Material not protected, or no longer protected, by intellectual property rights belongs to the public domain and may not be enclosed against uses permitted by law by technical or contractual means.\n2. The law shall provide for open access to publications and data resulting from research financed substantially by public funds.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The minimum-standards frame follows the TRIPS Agreement and the Berne Convention, while the limitations clause adopts the Berne three-step test as its discipline. Compulsory licensing in public-health emergencies implements the flexibilities confirmed by the Doha Declaration on the TRIPS Agreement and Public Health, giving the constitutional right to health priority over exclusive rights in defined circumstances. Open-access duties respond to the argument that publicly financed research should be publicly readable. The accepted trade-off is between innovation incentives and affordable access, managed here through mandatory exceptions rather than permanent weakening of rights.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 12.5.4 (Access and Pricing Abuse); Article 1.10.1 (Right to education); Article 1.8.1 (Right to property).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__monopolies", "path": "09-economy/monopolies.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.3 — Monopolies", "md": "# Section 9.3 — Monopolies\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.3.1 – 9.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section governs the exceptional cases in which a single supplier may be tolerated, imposing authorization, conduct, and sunset duties on legal and natural monopolies alike.*\n\n## Article 9.3.1 — Monopolies Exceptional\n\n1. No person may acquire or maintain a monopoly position otherwise than through superior performance in competition.\n2. A law may confer a monopoly only where a public interest cannot adequately be secured through competition, and only for a scope and duration no broader than that interest requires.\n3. Every monopoly conferred by law shall be subject to the duties of this section without further enactment.\n\n## Article 9.3.2 — Legal Monopolies\n\n1. A monopoly conferred by law shall carry obligations of universal service, equal treatment, price transparency, and open accounting, fixed by the authorizing law and supervised by the competent regulator.\n2. The continuation of every legal monopoly shall be reviewed at least every five years against its stated justification.\n3. A monopoly shall not be extended, by practice or by interpretation, to adjacent markets beyond its statutory scope.\n\n## Article 9.3.3 — Universal Service\n\n1. Where a service of general economic interest would not be supplied on commercially viable terms throughout the territory of the Republic, Parliament shall secure its provision through a regulated universal-service obligation.\n2. Such an obligation shall be imposed by law, shall be precisely defined, and shall be compensated transparently where it exceeds what a normal commercial operator would bear.\n\n## Article 9.3.4 — Essential Facilities\n\n1. An undertaking controlling infrastructure that cannot feasibly be duplicated and that is indispensable to competing suppliers shall grant access on fair, reasonable, and non-discriminatory terms.\n2. Terms of access shall be published or filed with the competent regulator, and disputes over access shall be decided within a time limit fixed by law.\n3. Where the technical structure of a network makes duplication uneconomic, Parliament shall separate the operation of the network from competitive supply, as provided for energy networks under Article 23.2.2 (Independent Regulation).\n\n## Article 9.3.5 — Remedies and Review\n\n1. Where the justification for a monopoly ceases to exist, the monopoly shall be terminated and competitive conditions restored without avoidable delay.\n2. The competition authority may order structural remedies, including divestiture, where conduct or regulation cannot restore effective competition by lesser means.\n3. Every decision under this section shall be reasoned, published, and open to full judicial review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This section codifies the essential-facilities doctrine developed in American and European Union case law, together with the practice of time-limited concessions for public utilities found in continental administrative law. The five-year review and the duty to restore competition convert a monopoly from a permanent privilege into a conditional trust. Universal service is honored through transparent, precisely defined obligations rather than informal cross-subsidies. The accepted trade-off is that regulated monopolies invite capture and underinvestment, risks checked here by sunset review, structural remedies, and judicial oversight.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.2.2 (The Competition Authority); Article 23.2.2 (Independent Regulation); Article 5.6.3 (Review of Executive Action).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__small-business", "path": "09-economy/small-business.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.5 — Small Business", "md": "# Section 9.5 — Small Business\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.5.1 – 9.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section commits the Republic to a regulatory environment in which small and medium-sized enterprises can form, operate, and grow, without weakening the protection owed to workers, consumers, and the environment.*\n\n## Article 9.5.1 — Proportionate Regulation\n\n1. Parliament shall define the categories of small and medium-sized enterprises by objective criteria and shall review the definition periodically.\n2. Legislative and regulatory proposals shall be assessed for their impact on small enterprises before adoption, and administrative burdens shall be scaled to the size and risk of the enterprise.\n3. Simplified procedures may be provided for the smallest enterprises, provided that the level of protection owed to workers, consumers, and the environment is not reduced.\n\n## Article 9.5.2 — One-Stop Administration\n\n1. The State shall maintain a single point of contact through which enterprises may complete registrations, declarations, and reporting obligations owed to public authorities.\n2. Information required once by one public authority shall be made available to other public authorities without fresh demands, in accordance with data protection law.\n\n## Article 9.5.3 — Prompt Payment\n\n1. Payment terms in commercial transactions shall be fair, and late payment shall carry interest and recovery costs fixed by law.\n2. The State and its organs shall pay their commercial invoices within the period fixed by law, and failure to do so shall attract the same interest and recovery costs as private late payment.\n\n## Article 9.5.4 — Promotion Programs\n\n1. The State may support access by small enterprises to credit, guarantees, training, advice, and markets through transparent, temporary, and non-discriminatory programs.\n2. Every support program shall state its objectives and shall be evaluated against them before its renewal.\n3. Support shall not amount to state aid that distorts competition within the meaning of Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition).\n\n## Article 9.5.5 — Equal Treatment in Markets\n\n1. Public procurement shall not impose requirements exceeding the subject matter of the contract and shall not be structured to favor large incumbent suppliers, consistent with Article 8.5.1 (Principles).\n2. Self-employed persons and economically dependent contractors shall enjoy the protections extended to them by law on equal terms with comparable enterprises.\n3. Nothing in this section shall confer on any enterprise an exemption from generally applicable law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section translates the think-small-first principle into constitutional form: impact assessment before adoption, burdens scaled to risk, and a single administrative entry point. Prompt-payment rules borrow from late-payment statutes that treat the State as the most consequential debtor of small suppliers. Support programs are framed as temporary and evaluable so that promotion does not harden into protectionism. The accepted trade-off is differentiated regulatory burdens, which add administrative complexity and invite boundary disputes over enterprise size.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.5.1 (Principles); Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition); Article 9.1.1 (Economic Liberty).\n"}, {"id": "09-economy__trade", "path": "09-economy/trade.md", "part": "09-economy", "partTitle": "Economy", "title": "Section 9.10 — Trade", "md": "# Section 9.10 — Trade\n\n> **Part 09 — The Economy** · Articles 9.10.1 – 9.10.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section guarantees freedom of external trade, reserves tariffs and trade measures to the law, and disciplines trade agreements and export controls.*\n\n## Article 9.10.1 — Freedom of External Trade\n\n1. Trade with foreign countries shall be free; no restriction may be imposed except by law.\n2. The territory of the Republic forms a single customs territory, in which no internal customs, duties, or obstacles to the movement of goods may be established.\n\n## Article 9.10.2 — Tariffs by Law\n\n1. Customs duties and other charges on imports and exports may be imposed only by or under a law, and their proceeds shall form part of the public revenue governed by Section 8.1.\n2. Tariffs and charges shall be applied equally to like goods from all trading partners, except preferences accorded under a customs union or free-trade agreement approved by Parliament, consistent with Article 19.5.2 (Tariffs and trade measures).\n3. Measures affecting imported goods shall not discriminate between imports and domestic goods of the same class.\n\n## Article 9.10.3 — Trade Agreements\n\n1. Trade agreements shall be negotiated and approved in accordance with Article 19.2.2 (Parliamentary approval) before ratification and shall be published as required for all treaties.\n2. Commitments negotiated with foreign partners shall be implemented only through measures adopted with domestic democratic authority.\n\n## Article 9.10.4 — Export Controls\n\n1. Export restrictions may be imposed by law only for security, non-proliferation, the prevention of grave humanitarian harm, or the fulfillment of obligations under international agreements, and shall be proportionate and time-limited.\n2. Export restrictions shall be applied without discrimination among destinations except as required by the international obligations of the Republic.\n3. The Government shall report annually to Parliament on the exercise of these powers, and restrictions shall lapse when the grounds for them cease to exist.\n\n## Article 9.10.5 — Trade Defense\n\n1. Anti-dumping, countervailing, and safeguard measures may be adopted only upon an investigation establishing the conditions fixed by law, for a limited period, and no broader than necessary.\n2. Interested parties shall have the right to be heard in the investigation and to seek judicial review of any measure adopted.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The non-discrimination clauses codify the most-favored-nation and national-treatment principles of the GATT and World Trade Organization system, and the unity of the customs territory follows the common-commercial-policy model of the European Union. Requiring parliamentary approval of trade agreements answers the democratic-deficit critique of negotiated commitments by making domestic adoption the gate through which every concession must pass. Narrow, non-discriminatory export controls keep security exceptions from becoming disguised protectionism. The accepted trade-off is a slower and more politically exposed trade policy, accepted as the price of accountability.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 19.5.2 (Tariffs and trade measures); Article 19.2.2 (Parliamentary approval); Article 8.1.1 (Legality of Taxation).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__collective-bargaining", "path": "10-labor/collective-bargaining.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.7 — Collective Bargaining", "md": "# Section 10.7 — Collective Bargaining\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.7.1 – 10.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section builds the machinery of negotiated industrial relations: a duty to bargain in good faith, the binding force of collective agreements, extension mechanisms, and the peaceful strike right with essential-services limits.*\n\n## Article 10.7.1 — Duty to Bargain in Good Faith\n\n1. Employers and the organizations of workers shall bargain in good faith over terms and conditions of employment, shall exchange the information necessary for meaningful negotiation, and shall not refuse to bargain because another representative organization also exists.\n2. The State shall maintain, with the participation of the social partners, machinery for conciliation and mediation of collective disputes, available promptly and free of charge.\n\n## Article 10.7.2 — Binding Force of Agreements\n\n1. Collective agreements bind the parties, who shall ensure their observance, and apply to the workers they cover for the period and scope they define.\n2. During the term of an agreement, industrial action concerning its subject matter is barred between the parties.\n3. Compulsory arbitration is prohibited except in essential services narrowly defined by law or where the parties consent.\n\n## Article 10.7.3 — Extension of Agreements\n\n1. The law may extend a collective agreement to an entire sector where the parties to it are demonstrably representative.\n2. Extension shall require published reasons and shall not impair the entitlement of unorganized workers to standards no less favorable than the statutory floors.\n\n## Article 10.7.4 — The Right to Strike\n\n1. Workers and their organizations have the right to strike peacefully in defense of their occupational and social interests, in fulfillment of Article 1.9.3 (Trade unions and collective bargaining).\n2. The strike shall be exercised in accordance with the procedures of the organizations concerned, and lockouts are subject to equivalent limitations.\n3. No person shall suffer criminal penalty for peaceful participation in a lawful strike.\n\n## Article 10.7.5 — Essential Services\n\n1. In essential services, whose scope shall be narrowly defined by law, the right to strike shall be exercised through minimum service arrangements agreed between the parties or, failing agreement, fixed by an independent body.\n2. Persons deprived of the right to strike in essential services shall have access to impartial arbitration.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Good-faith bargaining and the extension power follow ILO Convention No. 154 and Recommendation No. 163, which treat extension as legitimate where representativeness is shown and reasons are published. The strike right is drafted peacefully and precisely because it is the only sanction workers hold against unilateral employer power, while the essential-services exception, narrowly defined and paired with arbitration, follows the settled approach of the ILO supervisory bodies. The accepted trade-off is the extension mechanism itself: it spreads agreed terms across a sector but can entrench incumbent bargaining agents.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.3 (Trade unions and collective bargaining); Article 10.6.1 (Formation of Unions); Article 10.3.2 (The National Minimum Wage).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__employment", "path": "10-labor/employment.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.1 — Employment", "md": "# Section 10.1 — Employment\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.1.1 – 10.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section governs the individual employment relationship — free choice of work, the prohibition of forced labor, security against arbitrary dismissal, and the extension of protection to every form of work.*\n\n## Article 10.1.1 — Free Choice of Work\n\n1. Every person has the right to work and to a free choice of employment, and no person shall be barred from lawful work except as provided by law for the protection of others.\n2. Access to employment, vocational training, and advancement shall be open to all on the basis of qualification, and no person shall be discriminated against in any aspect of employment on a ground prohibited by Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of discrimination).\n3. The State shall pursue full, productive, and freely chosen employment through measures that sustain economic activity and develop skills.\n\n## Article 10.1.2 — Prohibition of Forced Labor\n\n1. Forced or compulsory labor is prohibited in every form, in fulfillment of Article 1.9.1 (Prohibition of forced labor), and no person shall be required to work under threat of penalty or without offering freely.\n2. The State shall take effective measures to prevent, detect, and punish forced labor, trafficking for labor exploitation, and debt bondage, including within supply chains.\n\n## Article 10.1.3 — Security Against Dismissal\n\n1. No employee shall be dismissed except for a reason recognized as valid by law, established by a fair procedure, and subject to review by an impartial body, consistent with Article 1.9.4 (Equality and security in employment).\n2. Termination shall carry notice or compensation as prescribed by law; collective dismissals shall require prior consultation with worker representatives and notification of the competent authority.\n3. Employment shall not be terminated, downgraded, or penalized because the employee exercised a constitutional or statutory right, reported a violation, or refused an unlawful instruction.\n\n## Article 10.1.4 — Atypical and Platform Workers\n\n1. Protection under this Part shall not depend on the form of the contract; every person performing work for another under an employment relationship, or in circumstances resembling one, shall enjoy the minimum standards of this Part.\n2. The law shall presume an employment relationship where control, economic dependence, or subordination is established, and shall prevent misclassification.\n3. Platform, domestic, seasonal, and home-based work shall be expressly covered by implementing legislation.\n\n## Article 10.1.5 — Public Employment Services\n\n1. The State shall maintain a public employment service, free of charge to workers, providing placement, labor-market information, vocational guidance, and reintegration support.\n2. Private placement agencies may operate only under license and shall not charge fees to workers.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Dismissal rules follow ILO Convention No. 158, whose sparse ratification shows that job security remains contested ground. Presuming employment from control and dependence shifts the burden to the party best placed to disclose the true arrangement. The trade-off is between dismissal protection and flexibility, resolved by requiring valid reasons while leaving notice and compensation levels to Parliament.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.1 (Prohibition of forced labor); Article 1.9.4 (Equality and security in employment); Article 4.5.2 (Merit recruitment).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__minimum-standards", "path": "10-labor/minimum-standards.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.2 — Minimum Standards", "md": "# Section 10.2 — Minimum Standards\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.2.1 – 10.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section fixes the non-waivable floor of labor protection and guarantees its reach to the workers most often excluded: minors, domestic and informal workers, and migrants.*\n\n## Article 10.2.1 — Non-Waivable Floors\n\n1. The standards established in this Part are minimum standards and shall not be diminished by contract, agreement, or waiver, except in favor of the worker.\n2. Collective agreements may improve upon, but may not reduce, the standards of this Part; any term to the contrary is void and replaced by the statutory minimum.\n3. The level of protection in force shall not be reduced by amendment.\n\n## Article 10.2.2 — Working Minors\n\n1. Parliament shall prescribe a minimum age for admission to employment, not below the end of compulsory schooling under Section 11.2 and in no case below fifteen years.\n2. The worst forms of child labor, such as slavery, trafficking, debt bondage, and commercial sexual exploitation, are prohibited absolutely, consistent with Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work) and Article 1.15.3 (Protection from harm).\n3. Persons above the minimum age but below eighteen years may work only under conditions protecting their safety, health, and schooling, and hazardous work by young persons is prohibited.\n\n## Article 10.2.3 — Domestic and Informal Workers\n\n1. Domestic workers, home workers, and workers in the informal economy shall enjoy the standards of this Part, including limits on hours, rest periods, and a written statement of terms.\n2. The State shall pursue the progressive formalization of informal work, extending registration, social security coverage, and inspection without penalizing the workers concerned.\n3. Migrant workers shall enjoy treatment no less favorable than national workers in respect of remuneration and conditions of work, and no employer shall retain the identity documents of a worker.\n\n## Article 10.2.4 — Labor Inspection\n\n1. The State shall maintain a system of labor inspection, adequately resourced and staffed by trained officials independent of improper influence, with free access to workplaces at any time without prior notice.\n2. Inspectors may require remediation, impose administrative penalties, and refer violations for prosecution; inspection results shall be published in aggregate.\n\n## Article 10.2.5 — Review of Standards\n\n1. Parliament shall review the adequacy of the statutory floors at intervals not exceeding five years, with the participation of the organizations of employers and workers.\n2. The review shall consider productivity, prices, and the composition of the labor force, and shall publish its findings and reasons.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The floor follows the ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, with the child-labor prohibitions tracking Conventions No. 138 and No. 182 and the coverage clauses adapting Convention No. 189 on domestic workers, historically the least inspected workforce. The inspectorate follows Convention No. 81, whose insistence on unannounced access recognizes that violations thrive on notice. The accepted trade-off is gradual formalization rather than immediate prohibition of informal work, which would drive vulnerable workers into hiding.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work); Article 1.15.3 (Protection from harm); Article 10.1.4 (Atypical and Platform Workers).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__unemployment", "path": "10-labor/unemployment.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.8 — Unemployment Protection", "md": "# Section 10.8 — Unemployment Protection\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.8.1 – 10.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section protects workers when work ends: insurance against job loss, activation services and training rights, and a floor of assistance for those the labor market leaves out.*\n\n## Article 10.8.1 — Right to Unemployment Protection\n\n1. Every worker who loses employment through no fault of that worker has the right to unemployment protection sufficient to maintain an adequate standard of living while seeking new work with dignity.\n2. Protection under this Article forms part of the social security system of the Republic and shall be coordinated with Article 22.1.1 (Right to Social Security).\n\n## Article 10.8.2 — Unemployment Insurance\n\n1. Parliament shall maintain a compulsory unemployment insurance scheme, funded by contributions of employers and workers and, where necessary, by the State, providing benefits proportionate to prior earnings and limited in duration by law.\n2. Benefits shall be payable promptly, shall be adjusted to preserve their value, and shall not be reduced as a penalty except for defined misconduct established by fair procedure.\n3. Contributions shall be segregated from general revenue, and accrued entitlements shall be portable across sectors and Provinces as prescribed by law.\n\n## Article 10.8.3 — Activation Services\n\n1. Every beneficiary has the right to placement services, vocational guidance, and support for relocation or self-employment, and the corresponding duty to remain available for and to seek suitable work.\n2. Work is suitable only if it corresponds to the capacity, prior occupation, and reasonable prospects of the beneficiary and offers conditions not less favorable than those prevailing locally.\n\n## Article 10.8.4 — Training Rights\n\n1. Every beneficiary has the right to vocational training and retraining under Article 11.5.5 (Retraining for Displaced Workers), with income support where the training requires it.\n2. The State shall maintain active labor-market programs, evaluated for effectiveness, with priority for regions and groups facing structural displacement.\n\n## Article 10.8.5 — Assistance of Last Resort\n\n1. A person whose entitlement is exhausted and who remains without work shall have access to assistance of last resort, without work requirements that would compel acceptance of degrading conditions.\n2. The State shall monitor long-term unemployment and act to prevent its intergenerational transmission.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The scheme tracks ILO Conventions No. 102 and No. 168 on minimum social security and protection against unemployment, while the balance of rights and duties reflects the flexicurity model associated with the Nordic systems: generous benefits, strong activation, and manageable dismissal barriers. Segregating contributions from general revenue protects the fund from raiding during fiscal stress. The suitability test limits what the State may demand before sanctions. The accepted trade-off is conditionality — work-search duties respect contributors but risk coercing the desperate, so the last-resort clause caps what may be demanded.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 22.1.1 (Right to Social Security); Article 11.5.5 (Retraining for Displaced Workers); Article 10.1.5 (Public Employment Services).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__unions", "path": "10-labor/unions.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.6 — Unions", "md": "# Section 10.6 — Unions\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.6.1 – 10.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section protects freedom of association at work: the formation and autonomy of unions, freedom from employer domination, and protection from anti-union discrimination.*\n\n## Article 10.6.1 — Formation of Unions\n\n1. Workers and employers, without distinction whatsoever, have the right to establish and, subject only to the rules of the organization concerned, to join organizations of their own choosing without previous authorization, in fulfillment of Article 1.9.3 (Trade unions and collective bargaining).\n2. No person shall be required to join or to refrain from joining an organization of workers or employers, and any arrangement compelling either is void.\n\n## Article 10.6.2 — Union Autonomy\n\n1. Workers' and employers' organizations shall draw up their constitutions and rules, elect their representatives freely by secret ballot, organize their administration, and formulate their programs without interference by public authorities or by each other.\n2. An organization may be dissolved or suspended only in accordance with law and by final judgment of a court, and its assets shall pass to a like organization.\n\n## Article 10.6.3 — Employer Non-Interference\n\n1. Employers shall not dominate or finance unions, shall not interfere in their administration, and shall not discriminate among workers by reason of the organization to which they belong.\n2. Yellow-dog arrangements, under which a worker renounces union membership as a condition of employment, are void.\n\n## Article 10.6.4 — Protection from Anti-Union Discrimination\n\n1. No worker shall be refused employment, dismissed, or subjected to any detriment by reason of union membership or lawful union activity, consistent with Article 1.9.4 (Equality and security in employment).\n2. Remedies shall include reinstatement and compensation sufficient to deter violation.\n\n## Article 10.6.5 — Federations and Pluralism\n\n1. Organizations shall have the right to federate, confederate, and affiliate with international organizations of workers and employers.\n2. The pluralism of organizations is guaranteed, and the law shall not reserve representation to a single confederation.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section follows ILO Conventions No. 87 and No. 98, the standards that define freedom of association at work for supervisory bodies worldwide. Requiring judicial dissolution and successorship of assets prevents the historical device of dissolving troublesome unions under administrative discretion. The ban on employer domination and the voiding of yellow-dog clauses answer the two oldest instruments of sham unionism. The accepted trade-off is unqualified pluralism, which preserves worker choice but can fragment bargaining strength across competing organizations.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.3 (Trade unions and collective bargaining); Article 1.9.4 (Equality and security in employment); Article 10.7.4 (The Right to Strike).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__wages", "path": "10-labor/wages.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.3 — Wages", "md": "# Section 10.3 — Wages\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.3.1 – 10.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section secures remuneration as the core of just conditions of work: a living-wage orientation, a national minimum wage set through tripartite negotiation, equal pay, and the protection of payment.*\n\n## Article 10.3.1 — Living-Wage Orientation\n\n1. Every worker has the right to remuneration sufficient to provide a decent living for the worker and the family of the worker, in fulfillment of Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work).\n2. The State shall pursue this objective progressively through wage-setting machinery, social transfers, and fiscal policy, and shall review adequacy against the cost of living at regular intervals.\n\n## Article 10.3.2 — The National Minimum Wage\n\n1. Parliament shall maintain a national minimum wage, fixed by law after negotiation in which the representative organizations of employers and workers participate on terms of equality with the Government.\n2. The minimum wage shall take full account of the needs of workers and of economic factors, shall be adjusted at regular intervals, and shall not be reduced by private agreement.\n3. Its coverage shall extend to every worker, including those paid by piece, task, or output, and to workers in every sector without exception.\n\n## Article 10.3.3 — Equal Pay\n\n1. Workers shall receive equal remuneration for work of equal value, including benefits and occupational pension rights, without discrimination on any ground, consistent with Article 1.2.3 (Equality of women and men).\n2. Pay secrecy clauses are void, and workers and their representatives shall have access to the information necessary to verify compliance with this Article.\n\n## Article 10.3.4 — Timely Payment\n\n1. Wages shall be paid regularly, in the national currency or by transfer of equivalent value, and in full; payment in kind may be permitted only to the extent authorized by law and shall not replace the monetary wage.\n2. Deductions shall be lawful only as prescribed by law and shall never reduce pay below the statutory minimum.\n3. Claims for wages earned before the opening of insolvency proceedings shall enjoy priority in those proceedings as provided by law.\n\n## Article 10.3.5 — Enforcement of Wage Claims\n\n1. Claims for unpaid wages shall be enforceable through a speedy and inexpensive procedure, with limitation periods that do not extinguish remuneration earned by work.\n2. Unlawful nonpayment shall attract surcharges payable to the worker, and repeated nonpayment shall constitute an offense.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section tracks ILO Conventions No. 95 on wage protection, No. 100 on equal remuneration, and No. 131 on minimum-wage fixing, and absorbs the living-wage aspiration adopted by the ILO in 2024. Because a constitution cannot fix an amount responsibly, the design constitutionalizes the process — tripartite negotiation, regular adjustment, insolvency priority — while leaving the figures to machinery responsive to economic conditions. The accepted trade-off is that the minimum wage floors the market but cannot by itself guarantee a living wage everywhere.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work); Article 1.2.3 (Equality of women and men); Article 10.7.1 (Duty to Bargain in Good Faith).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__working-hours", "path": "10-labor/working-hours.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.4 — Working Hours", "md": "# Section 10.4 — Working Hours\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.4.1 – 10.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section limits the time a person may be required to work and guarantees rest: daily and weekly limits, paid annual leave, controlled overtime, and protection in night work.*\n\n## Article 10.4.1 — Limits on Working Time\n\n1. Ordinary working hours shall not exceed eight hours a day or forty hours a week, and total hours including overtime shall not exceed the maximum fixed by law.\n2. These limits apply to every person in an employment relationship, including managerial, agricultural, and domestic workers, subject only to exceptions proportionate to genuine operational need.\n\n## Article 10.4.2 — Rest Periods\n\n1. Every worker is entitled to daily rest of not less than eleven consecutive hours and to an uninterrupted weekly rest period of not less than twenty-four hours.\n2. Work exceeding six consecutive hours shall be interrupted by a rest break of a duration fixed by law.\n\n## Article 10.4.3 — Paid Annual Leave\n\n1. Every worker is entitled to paid annual leave of not less than four weeks, in fulfillment of Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work), in addition to public holidays.\n2. Leave shall be genuine rest and may not be replaced by payment in lieu, except upon termination of employment.\n3. Entitlement to leave shall not depend on a minimum period of service beyond that reasonably required to accrue leave.\n\n## Article 10.4.4 — Overtime\n\n1. Overtime shall be voluntary except where a lawful and narrowly defined necessity, such as an accident or force majeure, compels it, and shall attract compensation at a premium rate fixed by law.\n2. Overtime shall be counted, recorded, and compensated whether performed at the workplace or remotely.\n\n## Article 10.4.5 — Night Work and Flexibility\n\n1. Night workers shall be entitled to reduced hours, periodic health assessment, and additional protection as prescribed by law.\n2. The law may authorize flexible arrangements, including averaging of hours over a reference period, only where collective agreements provide safeguards and health monitoring remains in force.\n3. Every worker shall have the right to disconnect from digital means of contact outside working hours, and derogations under this Article shall be reviewed periodically and shall not become the general rule.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The baseline follows ILO Convention No. 1 of 1919, which fixed the eight-hour day, and the European Union Working Time Directive of 2003, whose rest-period logic the right to disconnect extends to digital work. The four-week leave floor constitutionalizes what international labor standards treat as the minimum for genuine rest, immune from waiver under Article 10.2.1 (Non-Waivable Floors). Counting remote overtime closes the loophole that home working once opened. The accepted trade-off is flexibility: averaging periods are tolerated in exchange for collective safeguards and periodic review.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work); Article 10.2.1 (Non-Waivable Floors); Article 10.5.1 (The Safe-Workplace Duty).\n"}, {"id": "10-labor__workplace-safety", "path": "10-labor/workplace-safety.md", "part": "10-labor", "partTitle": "Labor", "title": "Section 10.5 — Workplace Safety", "md": "# Section 10.5 — Workplace Safety\n\n> **Part 10 — Labor** · Articles 10.5.1 – 10.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section guarantees safe and healthy working conditions: the duty of employers, the machinery of inspection, the right of workers to remove themselves from danger, and occupational health services.*\n\n## Article 10.5.1 — The Safe-Workplace Duty\n\n1. Every worker has the right to safe and healthy working conditions, including protection from physical, chemical, biological, and psychosocial hazards, in fulfillment of Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work).\n2. Every employer shall, so far as reasonably practicable, provide workplaces, equipment, and systems of work that are safe and without risk to health, together with information, training, and personal protective equipment at the expense of the employer.\n3. The State shall formulate and periodically re-examine a national policy on occupational safety and health in consultation with the organizations of employers and workers.\n\n## Article 10.5.2 — Risk Assessment and Information\n\n1. Employers shall assess risks systematically, record and report occupational injuries and diseases, and maintain emergency procedures.\n2. Workers and their representatives shall be informed of hazards, consulted on protective measures, and entitled to appoint safety representatives and committees without loss of pay.\n\n## Article 10.5.3 — The Inspection Authority\n\n1. Parliament shall establish an inspection authority for occupational safety and health, empowered to enter workplaces, order remediation, halt dangerous activity, and impose penalties.\n2. The authority shall coordinate with the general labor inspectorate under Article 10.2.4 (Labor Inspection) and shall publish the aggregate results of its inspections.\n\n## Article 10.5.4 — Right to Refuse Dangerous Work\n\n1. A worker who has reasonable justification to believe that continuing work presents an imminent and serious danger to life or health may remove himself or herself from that danger and shall suffer no adverse consequence for doing so.\n2. No worker may be required to return while the danger persists, except to take measures necessary to protect others.\n\n## Article 10.5.5 — Occupational Health Services\n\n1. The law shall provide for occupational health services entrusted with preventive advice, workplace surveillance, and the monitoring of the health of workers.\n2. A system of compensation for employment injuries and occupational diseases, covering medical care, income support, and rehabilitation, shall be maintained by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The architecture follows ILO Convention No. 155 and the European framework directive on safety and health at work, with the removal right recognized by both as the ultimate personal safeguard when prevention fails. The reasonably-practicable standard avoids an absolute liability that would be unworkable across industries, but the design pairs it with a dedicated inspection authority so that the standard cannot become a shield for negligence. Consultation rights convert workers from objects of protection into participants in it. The accepted trade-off is judgment-laden reasonableness review instead of strict liability.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.2 (Just conditions of work); Article 10.2.4 (Labor Inspection); Article 10.4.5 (Night Work and Flexibility).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__academic-freedom", "path": "11-education/academic-freedom.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.6 — Academic Freedom", "md": "# Section 11.6 — Academic Freedom\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.6.1 – 11.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section protects the freedom to inquire, to teach, and to publish; the security of the individuals who exercise that freedom; and the limits that scientific integrity and research ethics impose.*\n\n## Article 11.6.1 — Freedom of Research\n\n1. Research shall be free. Every researcher shall be free to choose methods and fields of inquiry, to pursue knowledge within and across disciplines, and to publish results without prior approval.\n2. No public or private interest shall require research to follow predetermined conclusions, and the independence of research results shall be protected as provided in Article 1.10.4 (Academic freedom).\n\n## Article 11.6.2 — Freedom of Teaching\n\n1. Teaching shall be free. Every teacher shall be free to present and discuss scholarly positions within the framework of the curriculum and to express academic judgments without fear of sanction.\n2. No test of political, religious, or ideological conformity shall be applied to admission, appointment, or promotion in any institution of learning.\n\n## Article 11.6.3 — Protection of Individuals\n\n1. No researcher, teacher, or student shall be dismissed, disciplined, or denied advancement for lawful research or expression, and the protection of expression under Article 1.3.1 (Freedom of expression) applies in full within institutions of learning.\n2. Institutions shall establish fair procedures, before independent bodies, for decisions affecting the status of academic staff and students.\n\n## Article 11.6.4 — Limits\n\n1. Academic freedom may be limited only by law, for a legitimate aim, and to the extent strictly necessary in a democratic society.\n2. Research involving human subjects, personal data, or dangerous biological material may be regulated for the protection of those interests, and nothing in this section shall authorize conduct prohibited by criminal law.\n\n## Article 11.6.5 — Research Integrity and Ethics\n\n1. Research shall be conducted with honesty; plagiarism, fabrication, and falsification constitute misconduct carrying consequences fixed by law or by the statutes of the institution.\n2. Research involving human subjects or personal data requires prior independent ethical review, consistent with the protections of personal data under the law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The guarantee synthesizes the UNESCO Recommendation of 1997 concerning the Status of Higher-Education Teaching Personnel and the research-and-teaching clause of the German Basic Law, extending protection to students as well as teachers. The clause forbidding predetermined conclusions answers both political steering and donor pressure, the twin masters to which research is most often sold. Ethics review appears as a constitutional limit so that protection of subjects and integrity of method discipline inquiry from within rather than by administrative fiat. The accepted trade-off is that strong protection makes research slower to redirect, even when public priorities change quickly.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.10.4 (Academic freedom); Article 1.3.1 (Freedom of expression); Article 11.4.1 (Institutional Autonomy).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__compulsory-education", "path": "11-education/compulsory-education.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.2 — Compulsory Education", "md": "# Section 11.2 — Compulsory Education\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.2.1 – 11.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section defines the compulsory period of basic education — free of charge, between the ages of six and fifteen — and the mutual duties of parents and the State in securing it.*\n\n## Article 11.2.1 — Free Compulsory Basic Education\n\n1. Every child between the ages of six and fifteen shall receive basic education, which shall be compulsory and free, in fulfillment of Article 1.10.1 (Right to education).\n2. The compulsory period shall cover at least the whole of basic education and its continuation into the first stage of a secondary pathway.\n3. The best interests of the child shall govern all decisions made under this section.\n\n## Article 11.2.2 — No Fees\n\n1. Instruction in public schools during the compulsory period shall be free of tuition and of charges for essential learning materials.\n2. No child shall be excluded from, or treated differently within, a public school for inability to pay any charge.\n\n## Article 11.2.3 — Duties of Parents\n\n1. Parents and guardians shall ensure that children of compulsory school age attend, or receive, the education required by this section.\n2. Parents may provide education at home or in non-school settings only where they register annually with the competent authority and the education meets the standards applicable to schools, and a refusal of registration may be appealed to an independent body.\n\n## Article 11.2.4 — Duties of the State\n\n1. The State shall provide a suitable school place, free of charge, within reasonable distance of the residence of every child of compulsory school age.\n2. The State shall ensure that attendance duties are enforced in a manner consistent with Article 1.10.3 (Parental liberty and private education), beginning with outreach, remedial educational assistance, and access to social services.\n3. Sanctions may be imposed only after support has failed or been refused, only by law, and never in a form that punishes a child for the conduct of others.\n\n## Article 11.2.5 — Support for Disadvantaged Pupils\n\n1. The State shall provide pupils whose families cannot meet related costs, and pupils with disabilities or other disadvantages, with the support necessary to participate on an equal basis, funded in accordance with Article 11.7.2 (Equitable Per-Pupil Funding).\n2. Schools shall admit pupils without discriminatory barriers, including children who are pregnant or parenting, children of migrant families, and children returning after interrupted schooling.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Free compulsory basic education is drafted as an immediately realizable obligation, following the reading of ICESCR Article 13 adopted in its General Comment 13, and the child-centered emphasis follows CRC Article 28. Home education is permitted but registered and inspected, tracking the European human-rights case law that conditions parental choice on compliance with State standards. Support-first enforcement borrows from welfare-based truancy models rather than penal ones. The accepted trade-off is that annual registration and verification of home education protect the child while burdening family autonomy.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.10.1 (Right to education); Article 1.10.3 (Parental liberty and private education); Article 11.7.2 (Equitable Per-Pupil Funding).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__education-finance", "path": "11-education/education-finance.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.7 — Education Finance", "md": "# Section 11.7 — Education Finance\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.7.1 – 11.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section fixes the duty of the State to fund education adequately and equitably — a guaranteed minimum share of public expenditure, per-pupil funding weighted for need, transparent reporting, and the maintenance of schools.*\n\n## Article 11.7.1 — Guaranteed Minimum Share\n\n1. Public expenditure on education shall not fall below the minimum share of total public expenditure prescribed by law, and the law shall set that share by reference to international benchmarks.\n2. The guaranteed share shall be appropriated through the annual budget under Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption) and shall give priority to basic and compulsory education.\n3. Reductions below the guaranteed share are impermissible; reductions within it shall be gradual, reasoned, and assessed for their effect on learners before they take effect.\n\n## Article 11.7.2 — Equitable Per-Pupil Funding\n\n1. Funds shall be distributed among institutions by a published formula weighted for need, including regional cost, learner disadvantage, and disability, so that the quality of education does not depend on the wealth of the community.\n2. The weights shall be reviewed when regional costs or pupil needs change, and targeted funds shall be used only for their stated purpose.\n\n## Article 11.7.3 — Transparent Reporting\n\n1. Public expenditure on education shall be reported annually, with its share of public expenditure and of gross domestic product published and its distribution among levels of education shown separately.\n2. Per-learner funding shall be published so that residents can compare institutions, and the terms of any public-private partnership in education shall be published.\n3. All public education funding shall be traceable in published budgets and audited accounts.\n\n## Article 11.7.4 — Maintenance of Schools\n\n1. The State shall keep public school buildings safe, accessible, and fit for instruction, and shall provide for their maintenance on a multi-year basis.\n2. Public basic and secondary education shall remain free of tuition, in fulfillment of Article 11.2.1 (Free Compulsory Basic Education), and private contributions shall never condition admission.\n\n## Article 11.7.5 — Review of Adequacy\n\n1. Parliament shall review the adequacy of education funding at intervals not exceeding five years, taking account of demography, prices, and the cost of achieving the aims of education.\n2. Educators in public service shall receive remuneration sufficient to attract and retain qualified professionals, paid reliably and on time.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The guaranteed-share mechanism follows the Education 2030 benchmark tradition, which counsels roughly four to six percent of gross domestic product for education, while anchoring the floor to public expenditure so that it survives economic downturns. Needs-weighted per-pupil formulas draw on the school-finance litigation of several jurisdictions, where weighted allocation has become the standard instrument for equalizing inputs across rich and poor communities. Publishing per-learner funding converts equity from aspiration into a figure anyone can verify. The accepted trade-off is that constitutional funding floors and formulas constrain budgetary flexibility.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption); Article 11.2.1 (Free Compulsory Basic Education); Article 1.10.1 (Right to education).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__education-system", "path": "11-education/education-system.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.1 — The Education System", "md": "# Section 11.1 — The Education System\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.1.1 – 11.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section organizes the education system as a whole — its structure, the public duty to provide it, equality of access, the place of minority languages, and the standing of private institutions.*\n\n## Article 11.1.1 — Structure of the System\n\n1. The education system of the Republic shall comprise early childhood education, basic education, secondary education, vocational training, and higher education, arranged so that completion of each level opens access to the next.\n2. Parliament shall establish by law the organization of public education, the allocation of responsibilities among national and local authorities, and the qualifications required of educators.\n3. Movement between general and vocational pathways shall remain possible throughout the life of a learner.\n\n## Article 11.1.2 — The Public Duty to Provide\n\n1. The State shall maintain a comprehensive education system open to all, extending from early childhood through adult learning, in fulfillment of Article 1.10.1 (Right to education).\n2. All education shall be directed to the aims stated in Article 1.10.2 (Aims of education), and Parliament shall establish national curriculum standards and a national qualifications framework applicable to public and private institutions alike.\n\n## Article 11.1.3 — Equality of Access\n\n1. No person shall be excluded from any public educational institution on a ground of discrimination prohibited by law, and the State shall remove barriers facing learners with disabilities, learners in rural or remote areas, and other disadvantaged learners.\n2. An independent authority shall evaluate the quality of schools and institutions, publish its findings, and advise on improvement; inspection shall concern outcomes, safety, and the rights of learners, not the content of lawful instruction.\n\n## Article 11.1.4 — Instruction in Minority Languages\n\n1. The language or languages of instruction shall be prescribed by law, having regard to the linguistic composition of the Republic and the rights of persons belonging to minorities.\n2. Persons belonging to minorities shall have adequate opportunities to learn their minority language and to receive instruction in it to the extent provided by law.\n\n## Article 11.1.5 — Private Institutions\n\n1. Persons and bodies may establish private educational institutions under conditions of transparency, non-discrimination, and standards no lower than those applicable to public institutions.\n2. Public authorities may support private institutions that charge no tuition and admit pupils without discrimination.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The structure follows the four-A scheme of availability, accessibility, acceptability, and adaptability developed under ICESCR General Comment 13, and the equal-standards rule for private institutions follows the Convention against Discrimination in Education of 1960. Instruction in minority languages implements the linguistic-identity guarantees of international minority-rights law within a unitary school system. A single independent quality authority keeps inspection focused on outcomes rather than on policing lawful viewpoints. The accepted trade-off is centralization: national standards secure comparability and equity but constrain local curricular experimentation.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.10.1 (Right to education); Article 1.10.2 (Aims of education); Article 11.7.1 (Guaranteed Minimum Share).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__higher-education", "path": "11-education/higher-education.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.3 — Higher Education", "md": "# Section 11.3 — Higher Education\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.3.1 – 11.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section governs access to higher education on the basis of merit, the financial support that keeps study within reach, the recognition of qualifications, and lifelong learning.*\n\n## Article 11.3.1 — Access by Merit\n\n1. Higher education shall be equally accessible to all on the basis of capacity, and no person shall be excluded on a ground of discrimination prohibited by law.\n2. Admission procedures shall be published in advance, based on transparent criteria, and subject to appeal.\n3. The State shall take measures, including preparatory and bridging programs, so that persons from disadvantaged backgrounds can compete for places on equal terms.\n\n## Article 11.3.2 — Financial Support\n\n1. The State shall maintain scholarships and need-based grants or loans on terms that allow qualified students to complete their studies without discrimination.\n2. Fees, where charged by public institutions, shall be published, capped by law, and accompanied by exemptions for students without means.\n3. Financial support shall be portable across institutions and regions within the Republic.\n\n## Article 11.3.3 — Recognition of Qualifications\n\n1. Degrees and periods of study completed at an accredited institution shall be recognized throughout the Republic without further examination.\n2. Qualifications obtained abroad shall be assessed and recognized under transparent procedures, consistent with the international obligations of the Republic, and within reasonable time limits.\n3. Accreditation of institutions and programs shall be exercised by the independent quality authority provided in Article 11.4.4 (Quality Assurance).\n\n## Article 11.3.4 — Lifelong Learning\n\n1. Institutions shall recognize prior learning and professional experience toward admission or credit.\n2. Public institutions shall provide part-time, continuing, and distance education for persons in employment and at every age, and public support shall not be limited to full-time study.\n\n## Article 11.3.5 — Student Rights\n\n1. Students shall have access to syllabi and assessment criteria, fair examination procedures, and a right of appeal against academic decisions.\n2. Students shall be represented in the governing bodies of institutions that receive public funds, consistent with Article 11.4.1 (Institutional Autonomy).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Equal access on the basis of capacity quotes ICESCR Article 13(2)(c), which makes higher education a progressively realized but non-discriminatory entitlement. Transparent admission and appeal rights answer the arbitrariness that historically accompanied selective entry, while bridging programs address unequal preparation rather than lowering standards. Recognition of foreign qualifications follows the recognition conventions of the European region, which convert credential evaluation from discretion into procedure. The accepted trade-off is statutory fee caps, which limit institutional pricing freedom in exchange for predictable access.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.10.1 (Right to education); Article 11.4.4 (Quality Assurance); Article 11.4.1 (Institutional Autonomy).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__universities", "path": "11-education/universities.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.4 — Universities", "md": "# Section 11.4 — Universities\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.4.1 – 11.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section secures the institutional autonomy and self-governance of universities, the security of academic appointments, independent quality assurance, and the accountability that autonomy must answer.*\n\n## Article 11.4.1 — Institutional Autonomy\n\n1. Universities shall govern themselves, within the law, over their statutes, admissions, curricula, research agendas, appointment of academic staff, and management of resources, in fulfillment of Article 1.10.4 (Academic freedom).\n2. Public authorities shall not issue instructions to universities concerning the content of research or teaching, nor intervene in their internal affairs except to enforce generally applicable law.\n\n## Article 11.4.2 — Self-Governance\n\n1. Each university shall adopt statutes establishing an academic senate responsible for academic affairs and a governing board responsible for finance, property, and long-term strategy.\n2. Statutes shall provide for the election of the rector and of members of the university organs by the academic staff and the students.\n\n## Article 11.4.3 — Academic Appointments and Tenure\n\n1. Academic posts shall be filled by open competition on the basis of merit, conducted by committees in which scholars of the relevant discipline hold a majority, with published criteria and reasoned decisions.\n2. The dismissal or other severe disadvantage of an academic staff member before the end of a term of office requires cause established through a fair procedure before an independent body, consistent with Article 11.6.3 (Protection of Individuals).\n\n## Article 11.4.4 — Quality Assurance\n\n1. An independent authority shall accredit universities and their programs against the national qualifications framework, shall publish its results, and shall periodically evaluate the quality of teaching and research.\n2. No institution may offer a degree-bearing program without accreditation.\n\n## Article 11.4.5 — Accountability for Public Funds\n\n1. Universities shall publish audited accounts, admission and graduation statistics, quality reports, and the outcomes of external evaluations.\n2. Public funding shall be allocated by transparent criteria, predictable over multi-year periods, in accordance with Article 11.7.3 (Transparent Reporting).\n3. Public authorities may verify the lawful use of funds but may not use evaluation as a pretext for interference in scholarship or in the appointment of academic staff.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Institutional autonomy follows the German Basic Law guarantee of research and teaching and the Magna Charta Universitatum of 1988, both of which treat self-governance as the precondition of free inquiry rather than as a privilege of the institution. Committee-based appointments with a scholarly majority adapt the continental university tradition that guards recruitment against political patronage. Pairing autonomy with mandatory audits and external quality evaluation follows the UNESCO Recommendation of 1997, which couples autonomy with responsibility. The accepted trade-off is that constitutional autonomy makes universities harder to steer, even when public priorities change quickly.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.10.4 (Academic freedom); Article 11.6.3 (Protection of Individuals); Article 11.7.3 (Transparent Reporting).\n"}, {"id": "11-education__vocational-training", "path": "11-education/vocational-training.md", "part": "11-education", "partTitle": "Education", "title": "Section 11.5 — Vocational Training", "md": "# Section 11.5 — Vocational Training\n\n> **Part 11 — Education** · Articles 11.5.1 – 11.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section guarantees vocational education and training as a pathway of equal standing, organizes apprenticeships under tripartite governance, and ensures that credentials gained are portable and renewable throughout working life.*\n\n## Article 11.5.1 — Vocational Pathways\n\n1. The State shall promote vocational education and training as a pathway of equal standing with general education, open to all without discrimination.\n2. Vocational programs shall confer qualifications respected by employers and by institutions of general and higher education, and movement between pathways shall remain open, consistent with Section 11.1.\n\n## Article 11.5.2 — Apprenticeships\n\n1. Parliament shall establish by law the conditions of apprenticeship and other work-based learning, including written agreements, remuneration, working-time limits, safety, mentoring, and the protection of health.\n2. Persons below the age of eighteen may be engaged in work-based learning only within the protections applicable to working minors under Article 10.2.2 (Working Minors).\n\n## Article 11.5.3 — Tripartite Governance\n\n1. The Government, the representative organizations of employers, and the representative organizations of workers shall participate together in designing curricula, examining candidates, and certifying work-based training.\n2. Parliament may establish levies or shared schemes so that the cost of training is not borne only by employers who train apprentices whom others later employ.\n\n## Article 11.5.4 — Portability of Credentials\n\n1. All vocational qualifications shall be defined within the national qualifications framework, shall certify demonstrated competencies rather than time spent in instruction, and shall be portable across the Republic, consistent with Article 11.3.3 (Recognition of Qualifications).\n2. Competencies acquired informally or in prior employment shall be assessable and creditable toward a qualification.\n\n## Article 11.5.5 — Retraining for Displaced Workers\n\n1. The State shall provide retraining opportunities for workers whose employment is displaced by technological or economic change, with income support where necessary, in coordination with the training rights under Article 10.8.4 (Training Rights).\n2. The State shall ensure impartial information and career guidance about vocational and educational pathways, available to learners and workers at every stage of life and free of charge to the individual learner.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The dual apprenticeship models of Germany and Switzerland show how employer participation secures labor-market relevance, and their insight is constitutionalized here as tripartite governance rather than left to custom. The competency-based, portable credential follows ILO Recommendation No. 195 of 2004, which treats skills as assets that must survive job changes. The levy clause addresses poaching: collective schemes prevent firms that train from subsidizing firms that do not. The accepted trade-off is that social-partner co-design embeds training in current demand, which can slow adaptation to new technologies.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 10.8.4 (Training Rights); Article 11.3.3 (Recognition of Qualifications); Article 10.2.2 (Working Minors).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__health-data", "path": "12-health/health-data.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.6 — Health Data", "md": "# Section 12.6 — Health Data\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.6.1 – 12.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section treats health data as a specially protected category, permitting secondary use only by law with strict safeguards and supervision by the Information Commission.*\n\n## Article 12.6.1 — Specially Protected Data\n\n1. Data concerning health, including genetic data, constitute a specially protected category of personal data.\n2. Processing of health data shall be permitted only on a lawful basis that is specifically provided for by law and accompanied by appropriate safeguards, consistent with Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination).\n\n## Article 12.6.2 — Primary Use in Care\n\n1. Health data may be processed for the direct care of the patient on the basis of consent or another lawful basis defined by law.\n2. Every patient shall have access to the health data concerning the patient and may request their rectification.\n\n## Article 12.6.3 — Secondary Use\n\n1. Secondary use of health data for research, planning, or public health shall be permitted only where:\n   (a) the purpose is defined by law;\n   (b) anonymized or pseudonymized data are used wherever the purpose allows;\n   (c) an ethics review has approved the processing; and\n   (d) safeguards prevent results from being used to the detriment of the persons concerned.\n\n## Article 12.6.4 — Supervision\n\n1. The Information Commission shall supervise the processing of health data within its general mandate under Article 20.7.2 (Mandate).\n2. Processing systems shall meet security, integrity, and access-control standards set by law, and significant breaches shall be reported and published.\n\n## Article 12.6.5 — Interoperability and Portability\n\n1. Health information systems shall be interoperable to ensure continuity of care, and records shall be portable when a patient changes provider.\n2. Technical design shall implement data minimization and purpose limitation as required by Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Health data receive a stricter regime than ordinary personal data because their misuse carries irreparable dignitary harm and because patients cannot realistically refuse care to refuse processing. The constitutional choice is a dual structure: free flow within direct care, and tightly conditioned secondary use reserved to statute with ethics review. Tying supervision to the Information Commission avoids creating a parallel regulator while signaling that health data are not merely another database.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination); Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing); Article 20.7.2 (Mandate).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__healthcare-system", "path": "12-health/healthcare-system.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.1 — Healthcare System", "md": "# Section 12.1 — Healthcare System\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.1.1 – 12.1.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section organizes the health system so that the right of access to healthcare under Section 1.11 is realized through universal coverage, strong primary care, and enforceable guarantees of timely treatment.*\n\n## Article 12.1.1 — Universal Access\n\n1. The State shall ensure that every person within the Republic can obtain necessary healthcare services without financial hardship, in fulfillment of the right stated in Article 1.11.1 (Right of Access to Healthcare Services).\n2. Parliament shall establish by law a coverage scheme that:\n   (a) defines the health services to which access is guaranteed;\n   (b) is financed from public revenues and, as the law provides, compulsory contributions;\n   (c) is portable across the whole territory of the Republic.\n\n## Article 12.1.2 — Public Financing Floor\n\n1. Public financing of the coverage scheme shall be sufficient to secure its minimum core obligations and shall not fall below the level needed to maintain existing services.\n2. Cost-sharing may be required only as the law provides and shall not operate as a barrier for persons without means.\n3. No person shall be denied emergency care for inability to pay, consistent with Article 1.11.2 (Emergency Care).\n\n## Article 12.1.3 — Primary and Community Care\n\n1. The health system shall be organized around accessible primary and community care, supported by referral to secondary and tertiary services.\n2. The State shall secure rural and remote coverage through fixed facilities, outreach, and mobile and telemedicine services.\n3. Regions may coordinate health planning across Provinces under Article 7.2.2 (Competences).\n\n## Article 12.1.4 — Waiting-Time Guarantees\n\n1. Parliament shall set by law maximum waiting times for defined categories of care.\n2. An independent authority shall monitor compliance with those guarantees and publish its findings.\n3. Where a guaranteed waiting time is exceeded, the patient shall have the right to obtain the treatment elsewhere at the cost of the coverage scheme.\n\n## Article 12.1.5 — Choice of Provider\n\n1. Patients may choose among providers participating in the coverage scheme.\n2. Private provision of healthcare is lawful, subject to licensing and to the standards applicable to public providers.\n3. The terms on which private providers participate in the coverage scheme shall be public and non-discriminatory.\n\n## Article 12.1.6 — Workforce and Equal Access\n\n1. The State shall plan for a sufficient health workforce and shall promote an equitable distribution of professionals across regions and specialties, with incentives for service in underserved areas.\n2. Health facilities and services shall be physically and informationally accessible to persons with disabilities, consistent with Article 28.3.3 (Accessibility).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This section turns the right of access under Section 1.11 into an administrable system: a statutory benefit package, a financing floor, and a waiting-time guarantee with a concrete remedy. The trade-off is that a benefit package defined by law forces prioritization decisions into Parliament rather than into clinical discretion or the courts. The waiting-time remedy borrows from targeted-treatment guarantees in European coverage systems.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.11.1 (Right of Access to Healthcare Services); Article 1.11.2 (Emergency Care); Article 7.2.2 (Competences).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__hospitals", "path": "12-health/hospitals.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.3 — Hospitals", "md": "# Section 12.3 — Hospitals\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.3.1 – 12.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section sets licensing and governance standards for hospitals and guarantees the patient rights of dignity, information, consent, and a second opinion.*\n\n## Article 12.3.1 — Licensing and Standards\n\n1. Hospitals may operate only under a license granted by law and shall comply with binding standards on staffing, hygiene, equipment, and quality of care.\n2. An inspection authority shall verify compliance, may order corrective measures, and may suspend or withdraw licenses for persistent or grave breaches.\n\n## Article 12.3.2 — Governance of Public Hospitals\n\n1. Public hospitals shall have governing boards that operate transparently and account for the use of public funds.\n2. Boards shall include members appointed on merit, and the law shall provide for representation of the communities the hospital serves.\n\n## Article 12.3.3 — Patient Rights\n\n1. Every patient has the right to be treated with dignity and respect for privacy.\n2. Every patient has the right to receive comprehensible information about diagnosis, treatment options, risks, and prognosis.\n3. Medical intervention requires the free and informed consent of the patient, consistent with the inviolability of human dignity under Article 0.7.1 (Inviolability of Human Dignity), save where the law provides for treatment without consent in narrowly defined circumstances.\n4. Patients shall have access to their medical records and to effective complaint procedures.\n\n## Article 12.3.4 — Second Opinion\n\n1. Before a major intervention with significant risks, the patient shall be entitled to request a second opinion, and reasonable time shall be allowed for obtaining it without endangering health.\n\n## Article 12.3.5 — Continuity of Care\n\n1. Discharge from hospital shall be planned with the patient and with providers of follow-up care.\n2. No patient shall be discharged earlier than medically appropriate for reasons of cost or administrative convenience.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Licensing with an inspection authority gives the constitutional health guarantee an operational grip on institutional quality, while the patient-rights clause codifies dignity, information, and consent at the point of care. The second-opinion right is deliberately limited to major interventions to protect it from trivialization and to respect urgent clinical timelines. The anti-premature-discharge rule addresses a well-documented failure mode of cost-pressured hospital systems without dictating clinical judgment.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.7.1 (Inviolability of Human Dignity); Article 12.4.2 (Ethics and Standards); Article 1.6.1 (Fair Administrative Action).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__medical-profession", "path": "12-health/medical-profession.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.4 — Medical Profession", "md": "# Section 12.4 — Medical Profession\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.4.1 – 12.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section entrusts licensing and professional discipline to independent professional chambers, protects ethics and continuing competence, and reconciles conscientious objection with the duty of referral.*\n\n## Article 12.4.1 — Professional Chambers\n\n1. Parliament shall establish by law independent professional chambers for the health professions.\n2. Chambers shall license practitioners, maintain registers, and set professional standards within the framework of the law.\n3. Chambers shall be independent of government direction in the exercise of their licensing and standard-setting functions.\n\n## Article 12.4.2 — Ethics and Standards\n\n1. Chambers shall adopt codes of ethics binding on their members, including duties of care, confidentiality, and honest dealing with patients.\n2. Practitioners shall maintain their competence through continuing education as the law requires.\n\n## Article 12.4.3 — Conscientious Objection\n\n1. A practitioner may refuse to perform a lawful intervention on grounds of conscience, provided that:\n   (a) the refusal does not create a real risk to the patient's life or health in an emergency; and\n   (b) the practitioner refers the patient promptly, in writing where practicable, to an available willing provider.\n2. The State shall ensure that access to services within the coverage scheme does not depend on the conscientious convictions of individual practitioners.\n\n## Article 12.4.4 — Discipline\n\n1. Chambers shall adjudicate professional misconduct through procedures that guarantee notice, a hearing, and reasons, and their decisions shall be subject to concrete review under Article 5.6.2 (Concrete Review).\n2. Sanctions shall be proportionate and shall include, where appropriate, conditions on practice, suspension, or removal from the register.\n\n## Article 12.4.5 — Qualifications and Mobility\n\n1. Chambers shall recognize qualifications on the basis of transparent criteria and may require assessment of competence.\n2. The scope of practice of each health profession shall be defined by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Independent professional chambers reconcile two risks: government control of medicine and unaccountable guild power. They receive licensing and standard-setting authority because clinical judgment is best regulated by peers, but their discipline procedures are constitutionally tethered to due process and judicial review. The conscientious-objection clause follows the referral-duty model: conscience is respected, yet the patient's lawful access to care does not collapse with the convictions of a single practitioner, and emergencies admit no refusal.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.11.2 (Emergency Care); Article 12.3.3 (Patient Rights); Article 5.6.2 (Concrete Review).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__medicines", "path": "12-health/medicines.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.5 — Medicines", "md": "# Section 12.5 — Medicines\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.5.1 – 12.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section governs the quality, safety, and availability of medicines, protecting generic competition and securing access to essential medicines against pricing abuse.*\n\n## Article 12.5.1 — Regulation of Medicines\n\n1. An independent medicines authority shall evaluate the quality, efficacy, and safety of medicines and grant marketing authorizations on the basis of scientific evidence.\n2. Decisions shall be reasoned, published, and subject to judicial review.\n3. Members of the medicines authority shall declare conflicts of interest and shall not hold interests in the industry that they regulate.\n\n## Article 12.5.2 — Essential Medicines\n\n1. Parliament shall provide by law for a national list of essential medicines.\n2. Public health facilities shall ensure the availability of essential medicines within the coverage scheme established under Article 12.1.2 (Public Financing Floor).\n\n## Article 12.5.3 — Generic Competition\n\n1. The law shall provide expeditious and reliable procedures for the authorization of generic and biosimilar medicines after patent or regulatory protection ends.\n2. Marketing authorizations shall not be used to prolong exclusive rights beyond their legal term.\n\n## Article 12.5.4 — Access and Pricing Abuse\n\n1. Practices that abuse a dominant position to deny or restrict access to essential medicines are unlawful and may be sanctioned under competition law.\n2. Where a patent is abused to obstruct the supply of an essential medicine, the State may apply the exceptions and compulsory-license measures permitted under Article 9.9.4 (Compulsory Licenses).\n\n## Article 12.5.5 — Pharmacovigilance\n\n1. The medicines authority shall operate a post-market surveillance system, including adverse-event reporting, recalls, and withdrawal of unsafe products.\n2. Serious safety information shall be communicated to professionals and the public without delay.\n3. The medicines authority shall publish an annual report on the safety and availability of medicines on the market.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section treats medicines as a chokepoint of the health guarantee: a formally free system fails if the pharmacy shelf is empty or unaffordable. The essential-medicines list, generic-authorization procedures, and competition safeguards work together, while the public-health licensing lever remains a last resort consistent with the constitutional balance of intellectual property. Post-market surveillance closes the loop, since safety evidence evolves after authorization.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.9.4 (Compulsory Licenses); Article 12.1.2 (Public Financing Floor); Article 1.14.1 (Safe Goods and Services).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__mental-health", "path": "12-health/mental-health.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.7 — Mental Health", "md": "# Section 12.7 — Mental Health\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.7.1 – 12.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section guarantees parity between mental and physical health and strictly conditions involuntary treatment on consent, least-restrictive means, and independent review.*\n\n## Article 12.7.1 — Parity of Esteem\n\n1. Mental healthcare shall be given parity with physical healthcare in financing, in the coverage scheme, and in the planning of services.\n2. The State shall develop community-based mental health services so that treatment is available close to where people live and work.\n\n## Article 12.7.2 — Consent and Support\n\n1. Treatment for mental illness requires the free and informed consent of the person, consistent with the right to personal integrity under Article 28.3.5 (Liberty and Integrity).\n2. The law shall provide for supported decision-making so that diminished capacity does not automatically remove a person's voice in treatment decisions.\n\n## Article 12.7.3 — Least-Restrictive Care\n\n1. Involuntary treatment or confinement may be ordered only where:\n   (a) a qualified professional has found a serious risk to the health or safety of the person or of others arising from a mental illness; and\n   (b) no less restrictive alternative is adequate.\n2. Involuntary measures shall be proportionate, time-limited, and reviewable, and shall be accompanied by care aimed at restoring autonomy.\n\n## Article 12.7.4 — Independent Review\n\n1. Every involuntary measure shall be subject to prompt review by a court or independent tribunal, and to periodic re-examination.\n2. The person concerned shall have the right to be heard, to be represented, and to receive legal aid under Article 5.9.1 (Right to Legal Aid).\n\n## Article 12.7.5 — Dignity in Facilities\n\n1. Facilities providing mental healthcare shall respect the dignity, privacy, and family contact of patients.\n2. Seclusion and restraint may be used only to prevent imminent harm, for the shortest time possible, and shall be recorded and reviewed.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Mental health is separated from general hospital law because its distinctive risk is the exercise of State and clinical power over persons rather than institutions. The parity clause targets the chronic underfunding of psychiatric services; the least-restrictive standard and independent review translate the general right to bodily integrity into psychiatry-specific safeguards. Supported decision-making reflects the shift from substitute decision-making toward preserving the legal agency of persons with impaired capacity.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 28.3.5 (Liberty and Integrity); Article 28.3.2 (Legal Capacity); Article 5.9.1 (Right to Legal Aid).\n"}, {"id": "12-health__public-health", "path": "12-health/public-health.md", "part": "12-health", "partTitle": "Health", "title": "Section 12.2 — Public Health", "md": "# Section 12.2 — Public Health\n\n> **Part 12 — Health** · Articles 12.2.1 – 12.2.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section governs preventive care, health promotion, and communicable-disease powers, subjecting every coercive measure to legality, proportionality, and independent scientific advice.*\n\n## Article 12.2.1 — Preventive Care\n\n1. The State shall provide preventive services, including immunization programs, screening for treatable conditions, and health education, as part of the coverage scheme under Article 12.1.1 (Universal Access).\n2. Participation in immunization programs may be encouraged by proportionate incentives and, for defined diseases, required by law subject to Article 12.2.4 (Scientific Advice and Transparency).\n\n## Article 12.2.2 — Health in All Policies\n\n1. Public authorities shall consider the effects of their decisions on population health and health equity.\n2. Environmental, food-safety, and occupational-health authorities shall coordinate their standard-setting with the health authority.\n\n## Article 12.2.3 — Communicable-Disease Surveillance\n\n1. The health authority shall operate systems for the surveillance and notification of communicable diseases.\n2. Personal data collected for surveillance shall be handled under the safeguards of Article 12.6.1 (Specially Protected Data) and used only for public-health purposes.\n\n## Article 12.2.4 — Scientific Advice and Transparency\n\n1. An independent scientific body shall advise the health authority on measures with major public-health impact.\n2. The evidence and reasoning supporting such measures shall be published, and dissenting scientific opinions shall be recorded.\n\n## Article 12.2.5 — Limits on Coercive Measures\n\n1. Isolation, quarantine, mandatory examination, and treatment orders may be imposed only by law, on the basis of the best available evidence, and only where necessary and proportionate to the risk.\n2. Such measures shall be the least restrictive available, shall be time-limited, and shall be subject to prompt review by a court or independent body.\n3. Restrictive measures shall not discriminate and shall not be used for any purpose other than the protection of health.\n\n## Article 12.2.6 — Emergencies and Compensation\n\n1. The ordinary powers of this section apply unless a public-health emergency is declared under Article 26.3.1 (Graduated Response).\n2. Persons bearing an extraordinary and disproportionate burden imposed for the protection of public health shall be entitled to compensation as the law provides.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Communicable-disease powers are placed on a strict legality-and-proportionality footing because history shows they can be stretched far beyond epidemiological need. The independent scientific-advice requirement insulates decision-making from short-term politics while keeping the evidence public. The compensation clause borrows from quarantine compensation traditions and disciplines the State against casual recourse to coercion. Ordinary surveillance and emergency regimes are kept distinct so that temporary powers do not silently become permanent.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 26.3.2 (Limits on Coercive Measures); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 12.6.3 (Secondary Use).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__air-quality", "path": "13-environment/air-quality.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.3 — Air Quality", "md": "# Section 13.3 — Air Quality\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.3.1 – 13.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section protects ambient air through binding standards, emission limits, a public monitoring network, and proportionate alert and restriction powers.*\n\n## Article 13.3.1 — Ambient Standards\n\n1. Parliament shall set by law binding ambient air quality standards protective of human health and the environment, implementing the general standards of Article 13.1.1 (Implementation Through Standards).\n2. Standards shall specify limit values, averaging periods, and margins of tolerance.\n3. Where a standard is persistently exceeded, the responsible authority shall report annually to Parliament on the causes and the corrective measures taken.\n\n## Article 13.3.2 — Emission Limits\n\n1. Installations, vehicles, and products may emit air pollutants only within limits set by law and, where required, under a permit.\n2. Permits shall be granted through transparent procedures and shall be reviewed at defined intervals.\n3. Emission limits for existing installations shall become stricter as the best available techniques evolve.\n\n## Article 13.3.3 — Monitoring and Public Information\n\n1. The State shall maintain a monitoring network measuring ambient air quality at representative locations.\n2. Monitoring data shall be published continuously and in an open, machine-readable format.\n3. Exceedance of an alert threshold shall trigger an automatic public warning without awaiting a separate decision.\n\n## Article 13.3.4 — Air Quality Plans and Alerts\n\n1. Where standards are exceeded, the responsible authority shall adopt an air quality plan with measures to achieve compliance within defined time limits.\n2. During pollution episodes, the authority may order temporary, proportionate, and non-discriminatory restrictions on activities contributing to the exceedance, after public warning.\n\n## Article 13.3.5 — Accountability\n\n1. Authorities that persistently fail to adopt or execute compliant air quality plans may be compelled to act through the remedies of Article 13.1.4 (Access to Justice).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Air quality law succeeds or fails on two design points: standards precise enough to be breached, and consequences that follow automatically from a breach. This section supplies both, adding continuous open monitoring so that exceedances are publicly known without litigation. Temporary episode restrictions are expressly non-discriminatory and time-limited, reflecting the lesson that emergency air measures have sometimes targeted the politically weakest activities first.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 13.1.1 (Implementation Through Standards); Article 13.1.4 (Access to Justice); Article 13.7.1 (Prevention First).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__biodiversity", "path": "13-environment/biodiversity.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.6 — Biodiversity", "md": "# Section 13.6 — Biodiversity\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.6.1 – 13.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section protects species and habitats, establishes protected areas, forbids State action that causes extinction, and imposes restoration obligations.*\n\n## Article 13.6.1 — Species and Habitat Protection\n\n1. The State shall protect native wild species and their habitats, maintaining science-based lists of threatened species and habitats requiring strict protection.\n2. Deliberate killing, capture, or disturbance of strictly protected species shall be prohibited save for narrowly defined exceptions in the law.\n\n## Article 13.6.2 — Protected Areas\n\n1. The State shall establish a coherent network of protected areas representing the diversity of ecosystems within the Republic.\n2. Protected areas shall have management plans, designated authorities, and financing sufficient for their purposes.\n3. The network shall be reviewed periodically for coherence and representativeness, and enlarged where science so requires.\n\n## Article 13.6.3 — Prohibition of Extinction\n\n1. No organ of the State shall adopt or authorize measures that it knows will cause the extinction of any species within the Republic.\n2. Where significant harm to protected species or habitats is unavoidable, the authority shall apply the mitigation hierarchy: avoidance, reduction, and compensation of the residual harm.\n\n## Article 13.6.4 — Restoration\n\n1. The State shall adopt targets and programs for the restoration of degraded ecosystems, prioritizing habitats essential to threatened species.\n2. Restoration obligations shall be imposed on those responsible for degradation in accordance with Article 13.7.2 (Polluter Pays).\n\n## Article 13.6.5 — Invasive Species and Wildlife Trade\n\n1. The State shall prevent, control, and where possible eradicate invasive alien species that threaten biodiversity.\n2. Trade in protected species shall be regulated by law, including stricter national measures where necessary for conservation.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The anti-extinction rule converts a moral intuition into an auditable constitutional command: a State organ cannot knowingly extinguish a species. Because extinction is irreversible, the clause admits no balancing against economic benefit, unlike ordinary environmental trade-offs, which remain governed by the mitigation hierarchy. Restoration duties complete the design, since protection alone cannot recover ecosystems already lost, and polluter-pays funding keeps the burden off the general taxpayer where a degrader can be identified. Restoration targets are set by law so that ambition is revisited by each Parliament rather than fixed for all time.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 13.7.2 (Polluter Pays); Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State); Article 13.5.3 (Reforestation Duty).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__climate-policy", "path": "13-environment/climate-policy.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.2 — Climate Policy", "md": "# Section 13.2 — Climate Policy\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.2.1 – 13.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section commits the Republic to national climate goals set by law, carbon budgeting, adaptation planning, and a just transition for affected workers and regions.*\n\n## Article 13.2.1 — Climate Goals\n\n1. Parliament shall set by law national climate goals, including long-term reduction targets for greenhouse-gas emissions.\n2. Goals shall be scientifically founded, publicly reasoned, and reviewed at fixed intervals.\n3. Goals shall include interim milestones that permit progress to be measured and enforced.\n\n## Article 13.2.2 — Carbon Budgeting\n\n1. The law shall establish successive carbon budgets that quantify the total emissions permitted over defined periods.\n2. The executive shall allocate sectoral pathways consistent with the budgets, report progress annually to Parliament, and explain any deviations.\n3. An independent body shall assess whether policies are compatible with the budgets and shall publish its assessment.\n4. Where a budget is projected to be exceeded, the executive shall present a corrective plan to Parliament within a defined period.\n\n## Article 13.2.3 — Adaptation\n\n1. The State shall adopt and periodically revise a national adaptation plan addressing climate risks to water, agriculture, infrastructure, settlements, and health.\n2. Public infrastructure decisions shall take account of projected climate risks, consistent with Article 23.2.5 (Sustainability and Resilience).\n\n## Article 13.2.4 — Just Transition\n\n1. Measures taken to reduce emissions shall be accompanied by support for workers and regions whose livelihoods are affected, including retraining for displaced workers under Article 11.5.5 (Retraining for Displaced Workers), income support, and diversification programs.\n2. The burdens of the transition shall be distributed in accordance with the principles of solidarity and equality.\n\n## Article 13.2.5 — International Cooperation\n\n1. The Republic shall pursue international cooperation on climate protection consistent with its obligations under international agreements.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Climate duties are framed as procedural discipline rather than judicially commanded outcomes: Parliament sets binding goals, the executive must report and explain, and an independent assessor closes the credibility gap between promise and policy. The just-transition clause is constitutionalized because decarbonization concentrates costs on identifiable workers and regions, and transition programs that ignore them have historically failed politically. The design draws on framework climate-law models pioneered in several national statutes during the late 2010s.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 23.2.5 (Sustainability and Resilience); Article 11.5.5 (Retraining for Displaced Workers); Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__environmental-rights", "path": "13-environment/environmental-rights.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.1 — Environmental Rights", "md": "# Section 13.1 — Environmental Rights\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.1.1 – 13.1.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section implements the constitutional right to a healthy environment of Section 1.12 through standards, impact assessment, participation, and access to justice.*\n\n## Article 13.1.1 — Implementation Through Standards\n\n1. The State shall implement the right stated in Article 1.12.1 (Right to a Healthy Environment) by maintaining binding environmental quality standards for air, water, soil, and noise.\n2. Standards shall be set by law at levels protective of human health and ecosystems and shall be reviewed periodically in the light of scientific evidence.\n\n## Article 13.1.2 — Environmental Impact Assessment\n\n1. Projects and plans likely to have significant effects on the environment may be authorized only after an assessment of their environmental impact.\n2. The assessment shall consider reasonable alternatives and mitigation measures, and its findings shall condition the decision that follows.\n\n## Article 13.1.3 — Information and Participation\n\n1. Every person has the right of access to environmental information held by public bodies, in accordance with Article 21.3.5 (Open Data and Reuse).\n2. The public shall be given early and effective opportunities to participate in the preparation of environmental plans, programs, and significant permitting decisions, and due account shall be taken of the participation.\n\n## Article 13.1.4 — Access to Justice\n\n1. Any person whose rights under Section 1.12 are affected, and associations acting in the public interest under conditions defined by law, shall have standing to challenge acts or omissions contrary to environmental law.\n2. Environmental proceedings shall be expeditious, and interim measures may be ordered where serious or irreversible harm is alleged.\n3. The costs of environmental proceedings shall not be prohibitive.\n\n## Article 13.1.5 — Monitoring and Enforcement\n\n1. The State shall operate a public environmental monitoring network and publish its data.\n2. Public authorities shall enforce environmental law against all persons equally, and enforcement failures shall be actionable under Article 1.12.3 (Standing and Access to Justice).\n\n## Article 13.1.6 — Guiding Principles\n\n1. Prevention, precaution, correction at source, and the polluter-pays principle shall guide the formulation and application of all environmental measures under this Part.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Rather than restating the right, this section builds the machinery that makes Section 1.12 enforceable: standards with teeth, assessment before authorization, open information, and broad standing. Wide standing is the load-bearing choice, since environmental harm is diffuse and individual injury is often hard to prove; the cost rule prevents standing from becoming nominal. The procedural triad of information, participation, and justice parallels the structure of the Aarhus Convention of 1998.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.12.1 (Right to a Healthy Environment); Article 1.12.3 (Standing and Access to Justice); Article 21.3.5 (Open Data and Reuse).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__forests", "path": "13-environment/forests.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.5 — Forests", "md": "# Section 13.5 — Forests\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.5.1 – 13.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section requires sustainable forest management, protects old-growth forests, imposes a reforestation duty, and recognizes community forest rights.*\n\n## Article 13.5.1 — Sustainable Forest Management\n\n1. Forests shall be managed on a sustainable basis, balancing their ecological, climatic, economic, and social functions.\n2. Forest management plans shall be based on scientific inventory and shall be reviewed periodically.\n3. Clear-cutting shall be permitted only where the management plan establishes its necessity and provides for immediate restocking.\n\n## Article 13.5.2 — Protection of Old-Growth Forests\n\n1. Old-growth and primary forests shall be protected from commercial logging.\n2. Exceptions may be provided by law only for overriding public interests, where no reasonable alternative exists and after the least damaging option has been chosen.\n\n## Article 13.5.3 — Reforestation Duty\n\n1. The State shall maintain a program of reforestation and forest restoration, giving preference to native and site-appropriate species.\n2. Harvesting shall not exceed the regenerative capacity of the forests, and degraded forest areas shall be restored.\n3. The State shall promote mixed-species stands adapted to the site and to projected climate conditions.\n\n## Article 13.5.4 — Community Forest Rights\n\n1. The law shall recognize and protect the rights of local communities to use forests sustainably for subsistence and customary purposes.\n2. The rights of Indigenous peoples concerning forests and resources on their lands and territories shall be respected under Article 28.5.2 (Lands, Territories, and Resources).\n\n## Article 13.5.5 — Forest Health\n\n1. The State shall monitor forest health and shall maintain prevention and response measures against fire, pests, and disease.\n2. Forest destruction through unlawful clearing shall be subject to restoration orders and penalties under Article 13.7.2 (Polluter Pays).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Forest law occupies the junction of climate policy, biodiversity, and rural livelihoods, which is why sustainability is defined by function rather than by timber yield alone. The old-growth rule creates a categorical protection with a narrow, court-auditable escape clause, reflecting the near-irreversibility of primary-forest loss. Community forest rights are constitutionalized because local and Indigenous stewardship has proved among the most reliable defenses against illegal clearing.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 28.5.2 (Lands, Territories, and Resources); Article 13.7.2 (Polluter Pays); Article 13.6.4 (Restoration).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__natural-resources", "path": "13-environment/natural-resources.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.8 — Natural Resources", "md": "# Section 13.8 — Natural Resources\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.8.1 – 13.8.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section vests natural resources in the Republic for the benefit of all, including future generations, and channels extraction revenue into a sovereign wealth fund.*\n\n## Article 13.8.1 — Public Ownership\n\n1. Natural resources within the territory of the Republic belong to the Republic.\n2. The State shall exercise resource ownership for the benefit of all, including future generations, and shall manage resources on a sustainable basis.\n\n## Article 13.8.2 — Transparent Extraction Licensing\n\n1. Extraction of natural resources may be authorized only under a license granted through open, competitive, and non-discriminatory procedures established by law.\n2. The criteria for granting licenses, the identities of licensees, and the material terms of licenses shall be published.\n\n## Article 13.8.3 — Sovereign Wealth Fund\n\n1. Revenue from the extraction of non-renewable natural resources shall be paid into a sovereign wealth fund established by law.\n2. The law shall govern the fund's saving and withdrawal rules, with the aim of preserving the value of resource endowments for future generations and stabilizing public finance against commodity cycles.\n3. The fund shall be managed prudently and independently of day-to-day political direction, and its accounts shall be audited under Article 8.8.2 (Mandate).\n\n## Article 13.8.4 — Transparency of Revenues\n\n1. All payments made by resource companies to the State, and all revenues received, shall be published.\n2. The beneficial ownership of licensees shall be recorded in a public register.\n\n## Article 13.8.5 — Environmental and Social Safeguards\n\n1. Extraction activities are subject to the impact-assessment and participation requirements of Article 13.1.2 (Environmental Impact Assessment).\n2. License conditions shall include financial guarantees for site reclamation, and consultation obligations toward affected communities shall apply consistently with Article 28.5.3 (Consultation and Consent).\n\n## Article 13.8.6 — Sharing of Benefits\n\n1. The law may assign a defined share of resource revenues to Provinces and Municipalities affected by extraction, without diminishing the national character of resource ownership.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Resource ownership is vested in the Republic as a whole to prevent local capture, yet benefit-sharing and consultation clauses acknowledge that extraction costs fall on specific places. The sovereign wealth fund converts an exhaustible endowment into a permanent financial asset and insulates the budget from commodity cycles; its intergenerational purpose is the fiscal expression of the sustainability principle in Article 8.7.1 (Principle of Sustainability). Full publication of payments and beneficial ownership follows the international anti-corruption consensus for extractive industries.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.7.1 (Principle of Sustainability); Article 8.8.2 (Mandate); Article 13.1.2 (Environmental Impact Assessment).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__pollution", "path": "13-environment/pollution.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.7 — Pollution", "md": "# Section 13.7 — Pollution\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.7.1 – 13.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section orders pollution control around prevention, the polluter-pays principle, strict liability for hazardous activities, and a duty of remediation.*\n\n## Article 13.7.1 — Prevention First\n\n1. Pollution shall be prevented or reduced at source, giving priority to prevention over remediation.\n2. Discharges and emissions may be authorized only within limits set by law, applying the best available techniques and without unreasonable transference of pollution between environmental media.\n\n## Article 13.7.2 — Polluter Pays\n\n1. The costs of preventing, controlling, and remediating pollution shall be borne by the polluter.\n2. Charges and levies may be imposed on emissions and discharges to internalize environmental costs.\n3. Charges shall not confer any right to pollute beyond the limits set by law.\n\n## Article 13.7.3 — Strict Liability\n\n1. Operators of hazardous activities shall be strictly liable for environmental damage caused by those activities, without need to prove fault.\n2. Operators of defined hazardous activities shall hold financial security adequate for the potential damage, as the law provides.\n\n## Article 13.7.4 — Remediation Duty\n\n1. Persons responsible for soil, water, or groundwater contamination shall remediate it to the standard required by law.\n2. Where no responsible person can be identified or is unable to act, the State shall undertake remediation and may recover the costs from any responsible party later identified.\n3. Contaminated sites shall be inventoried, and priority risks shall be addressed within time limits set by law.\n\n## Article 13.7.5 — Hazardous Substances and Waste\n\n1. Hazardous substances shall be registered, assessed, and, where unacceptable risks are found, restricted or prohibited.\n2. Waste shall be managed according to the hierarchy of prevention, reuse, recycling, recovery, and safe disposal, and transboundary movements of hazardous waste shall be controlled by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section adopts the classical prevention-first ordering because remediation is always costlier and less complete than avoidance. Strict liability for hazardous activities, complemented by mandatory financial security, allocates the risk to those who create it and spare victims the near-impossible burden of proving fault in complex industrial operations. The State's backstop remediation duty guarantees that abandoned contaminated sites are not left to future generations, with cost recovery preserving the polluter-pays principle.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 16.2.2 (Strict and Vicarious Liability); Article 13.1.2 (Environmental Impact Assessment); Article 13.4.3 (Protection of Sources).\n"}, {"id": "13-environment__water", "path": "13-environment/water.md", "part": "13-environment", "partTitle": "Environment", "title": "Section 13.4 — Water", "md": "# Section 13.4 — Water\n\n> **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources** · Articles 13.4.1 – 13.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section secures access to safe drinking water and sanitation, protects water sources, and organizes water management by river basin with priority for domestic use.*\n\n## Article 13.4.1 — Drinking Water and Sanitation\n\n1. Everyone has the right of access to sufficient, safe, acceptable, and affordable water for personal and domestic use and to adequate sanitation services.\n2. The State and the Municipalities shall progressively realize this right and shall ensure a basic minimum supply to every household.\n\n## Article 13.4.2 — Priority of Uses\n\n1. In the allocation and management of water resources, the satisfaction of essential domestic needs shall take priority over all other uses.\n2. All other uses shall be authorized according to law, taking account of ecological requirements and the public interest.\n\n## Article 13.4.3 — Protection of Sources\n\n1. Drinking-water sources shall be protected by zones in which activities that may degrade water quality are restricted or prohibited.\n2. Water bodies shall be managed to maintain or restore their ecological quality, applying the pollution rules of Article 13.7.1 (Prevention First).\n\n## Article 13.4.4 — Basin Management\n\n1. Water shall be managed according to river basins rather than administrative boundaries.\n2. Regions and Provinces shall cooperate in basin planning, and Municipalities shall deliver local water and sanitation services within their general competence under Article 7.3.2 (General Competence and Services).\n\n## Article 13.4.5 — Pricing and Affordability\n\n1. Tariffs for water services may reflect the costs of provision, subject to:\n   (a) affordability safeguards for low-income households; and\n   (b) a prohibition on disconnection from the basic minimum supply where this would endanger health or dignity.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Water is treated as both a right and a managed resource, which is why the section pairs an individual entitlement with basin-scale planning. Priority for essential domestic use gives courts and regulators a clear ranking rule for scarcity conflicts. Municipal delivery reflects the subsidiarity of the local tier, while basin planning corrects the mismatch between hydrology and administrative borders. The affordability safeguards acknowledge that cost-reflective pricing, left alone, can exclude the poorest households from an essential good.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 7.3.2 (General Competence and Services); Article 13.7.1 (Prevention First); Article 7.2.2 (Competences).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__corruption", "path": "14-criminal-law/corruption.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.6 — Corruption", "md": "# Section 14.6 — Corruption\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.6.1 – 14.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section criminalizes bribery, trading in influence, embezzlement, and illicit enrichment, with liability for officials and private parties alike and no immunity for corruption offenses.*\n\n## Article 14.6.1 — Bribery\n\n1. The Code shall punish both the giving and the receiving of an undue advantage intended to induce or reward the improper performance of a public function.\n2. The Code shall likewise punish bribery between private persons where it distorts honest business conduct.\n\n## Article 14.6.2 — Trading in Influence\n\n1. The Code shall punish promising, offering, or requesting an undue advantage by a person who claims to be able to exert an improper influence over a decision of a public authority, whether or not the influence is actually exercised.\n\n## Article 14.6.3 — Embezzlement and Illicit Enrichment\n\n1. The Code shall punish the misappropriation of funds or property entrusted to a public official or held by a public body.\n2. The Code may punish a public official whose wealth increases significantly and lawfully cannot be explained by legitimate income, subject to the procedural safeguards of Article 27.3.2 (Content and Verification).\n\n## Article 14.6.4 — Liability and Immunity\n\n1. Officials and private participants are equally liable, and the official position of the offender shall be treated as an aggravating circumstance, never as a ground of mitigation.\n2. No immunity may bar the prosecution of corruption offenses; procedural immunities may be lifted for this purpose by the authority competent under the law.\n\n## Article 14.6.5 — Enforcement\n\n1. The Anti-Corruption Commission shall investigate corruption offenses within its mandate under Article 20.3.2 (Mandate) and shall prosecute jointly with the Public Prosecution Service.\n2. The recovery of proceeds of corruption shall follow the rules on the recovery of proceeds of crime.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Criminal law completes the anti-corruption architecture built in Part 27 and Part 20: disclosure, ethics, and commission powers deter, and this section punishes. Punishing both sides of the bribe closes the defense that corruption is consented to by all participants. Illicit enrichment is included with a deliberately explicit anchor to the verification safeguards for asset declarations, acknowledging its contested character while preserving a tool against officials whose lifestyle cannot be explained. The no-immunity rule ensures that rank provides no shield for the offenses that most corrode public trust.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 20.3.2 (Mandate); Article 27.3.2 (Content and Verification); Article 14.5.4 (Recovery of Proceeds).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__criminal-code", "path": "14-criminal-law/criminal-code.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.1 — Criminal Code", "md": "# Section 14.1 — Criminal Code\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.1.1 – 14.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section mandates a single Criminal Code as the exclusive source of offenses and penalties, with special criminal laws exceptional and subordinate to the Code's general principles.*\n\n## Article 14.1.1 — Codification Mandate\n\n1. Parliament shall enact and maintain a single Criminal Code defining every criminal offense, its elements, and the penalties attached to it.\n2. No person shall be prosecuted for conduct that the Code does not define as an offense.\n\n## Article 14.1.2 — Exclusivity of the Code\n\n1. Special criminal laws may be enacted only where the Code expressly so provides, and they shall be interpreted subject to the general principles of the Code.\n2. No court may create offenses by analogy or extend an offense beyond its terms.\n\n## Article 14.1.3 — Accessibility\n\n1. The Code shall be written in plain and definite language so that persons of ordinary understanding can know what conduct is prohibited.\n2. The definitive text of the Code shall be published promptly and remain permanently available to the public free of charge.\n\n## Article 14.1.4 — Specificity of Penalties\n\n1. The Code shall prescribe the kind and range of penalties for every offense, and no penalty shall be imposed outside that range.\n2. Delegated instruments may specify technical elements, such as regulated substances or safety standards, only where the Code expressly identifies the delegation and fixes the maximum penalty; no instrument may create a new offense or aggravate an existing one, consistent with Article 4.6.3 (Delegated Legislation).\n\n## Article 14.1.5 — Maintenance and Review\n\n1. Parliament shall review the Code at regular intervals.\n2. Every proposal to create or aggravate an offense shall be accompanied by an assessment of its necessity, proportionality, and expected effects on fundamental rights.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Criminal law is where State coercion is at its maximum, and so codification is treated as a constitutional duty rather than a drafting preference: one Code, plainly written, free to read, with no offense beyond its terms. The special-laws clause preserves legislative flexibility for urgent regulation while preventing the quiet fragmentation of criminal law into inaccessible statutes. Mandatory rights impact assessments discipline the recurring political temptation to answer public anxiety with new offenses.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.6.3 (Delegated Legislation); Article 14.2.1 (Legality); Article 0.4.1 (Legality).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__cybercrime", "path": "14-criminal-law/cybercrime.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.7 — Cybercrime", "md": "# Section 14.7 — Cybercrime\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.7.1 – 14.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section defines offenses against computer systems and data and subjects every cybercrime investigative power to judicial warrant and ordinary evidence rules.*\n\n## Article 14.7.1 — Illegal Access\n\n1. The Code shall punish the intentional access to an information system without right.\n2. Access affecting critical infrastructure or producing significant harm shall constitute an aggravated form of the offense.\n\n## Article 14.7.2 — Data and System Interference\n\n1. The Code shall punish the intentional damaging, deletion, deterioration, alteration, or suppression of data without right, and the serious hindering of the functioning of an information system.\n2. The creation and distribution of tools adapted for such offenses shall be punishable only where committed with the intent that they be used for the commission of an offense, so that legitimate security research remains lawful.\n\n## Article 14.7.3 — Computer-Related Offenses\n\n1. Fraud, forgery, and extortion committed through information systems constitute offenses under the general provisions applicable to those offenses, read with the equal protection of digital property.\n\n## Article 14.7.4 — Investigative Powers\n\n1. Expedited preservation of data, production orders, and searches of information systems may be ordered only by judicial decision, in accordance with the warrant requirement of Article 15.2.1 (Warrant Requirement).\n2. Real-time collection of traffic data and interception of content shall be subject to the judicial-authorization safeguards of Article 1.5.5 (Judicial Authorization of Intrusions).\n3. There shall be no generalized monitoring of the population's communications.\n\n## Article 14.7.5 — Evidence Standards\n\n1. Digital evidence shall meet the authentication and integrity standards of Article 17.8.2 (Authentication and Integrity), and the defense shall have access to independent forensic verification.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Cybercrime provisions are kept strictly separate from cyber-investigation powers, so that the seriousness of the threat cannot be used as an argument for surveillance shortcuts. The dual-use tools clause protects security research by demanding criminal intent, acknowledging that the same code secures systems and breaks them. Routing all digital evidence through the authentication standards of the digital-law Part keeps the constitutional boundary between investigation capability and evidentiary reliability visible to courts.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 15.2.1 (Warrant Requirement); Article 17.8.2 (Authentication and Integrity); Article 1.5.5 (Judicial Authorization of Intrusions).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__financial-crimes", "path": "14-criminal-law/financial-crimes.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.5 — Financial Crimes", "md": "# Section 14.5 — Financial Crimes\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.5.1 – 14.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section defines offenses against the integrity of public funds and markets, establishes liability of legal persons, and provides for the recovery of criminal proceeds.*\n\n## Article 14.5.1 — Fraud Against the Public\n\n1. The Code shall punish the obtaining of public funds, subsidies, or benefits by deception, and the fraudulent evasion of obligations owed to the public purse.\n2. The scale of the deception and the abuse of official positions shall be treated as aggravating circumstances.\n\n## Article 14.5.2 — Market Manipulation\n\n1. The Code shall punish insider dealing and the manipulation of financial markets, protecting market integrity under Article 9.7.3 (Market Integrity).\n\n## Article 14.5.3 — Money Laundering\n\n1. The Code shall punish the conversion, transfer, concealment, or acquisition of property known to be the proceeds of crime.\n2. Money laundering shall be punishable irrespective of the type of predicate offense.\n3. The law shall require reporting of suspicious transactions by financial institutions and by designated professions.\n\n## Article 14.5.4 — Recovery of Proceeds\n\n1. Conviction of a financial or property offense shall entail the confiscation of proceeds and instrumentalities of the offense, consistent with the protection of property under Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property).\n2. Confiscated value shall be applied first to the satisfaction of victims' claims.\n\n## Article 14.5.5 — Liability of Legal Persons\n\n1. Legal persons shall be liable for offenses committed for their benefit by persons exercising control or acting in a leading position within them.\n2. Sanctions on legal persons shall include fines proportionate to the advantage gained and may include exclusion from public procurement and public funding; dissolution shall be reserved for grave and repeated offenses.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Financial crime is defined around the abuse of trust in markets and public administration rather than around archaic distinctions between forms of money. The all-offense predicate rule for laundering follows the international standard, preventing laundering law from being narrowed by treating some crimes as acceptable sources. Corporate liability is deliberately sanction-based rather than vicarious: the enterprise answers for abuse of its structures, while confiscation-with-victim-priority keeps recovery focused on restitution rather than treasury enrichment.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property); Article 14.6.5 (Enforcement); Article 14.4.5 (Restitution and Recovery).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__national-security-offenses", "path": "14-criminal-law/national-security-offenses.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.9 — National Security Offenses", "md": "# Section 14.9 — National Security Offenses\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.9.1 – 14.9.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section defines treason, espionage, and sabotage narrowly, and forbids any criminalization of journalism, dissent, or whistleblowing.*\n\n## Article 14.9.1 — Narrow Definitions\n\n1. Treason, espionage, and sabotage shall be defined by law only as concrete acts, committed with intent to prejudice the existence, security, or constitutional order of the Republic, in a manner that goes beyond the preparation of ordinary offenses.\n2. The advocacy of ideas, the criticism of constitutional institutions, and the peaceful pursuit of political aims shall not fall within these definitions.\n\n## Article 14.9.2 — Protected Activities\n\n1. No provision of the Code or of any other law shall be construed to criminalize journalism, dissent, protest, academic work, artistic expression, or whistleblowing, in conformity with Article 21.5.2 (No Punishment for Journalism) and Article 27.5.1 (Protected Disclosures).\n2. The publication of truthful information in the public interest shall not constitute espionage or a related offense merely because it is embarrassing to public authorities.\n\n## Article 14.9.3 — Conformity with Free Expression\n\n1. Offenses under this section shall be construed in conformity with freedom of expression under Article 1.3.1 (Freedom of Expression and Opinion).\n2. A restriction on the disclosure of official information is lawful only where the information is genuinely capable of causing serious prejudice to national security and the disclosure is not justified by an overriding public interest.\n\n## Article 14.9.4 — Relationship to Counter-Terrorism\n\n1. Offenses of terrorism are defined separately under Article 18.7.1 (Definition) and are subject to the same principles of this Part.\n\n## Article 14.9.5 — Review\n\n1. Parliament shall review the definitions and penalties of this section at regular intervals, with public hearings, to verify that they remain narrow, necessary, and proportionate.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*National-security offenses are the historical instrument by which constitutions have been hollowed out, so this section writes the protection of journalists, dissenters, and whistleblowers directly into criminal law rather than leaving it to interpretation. The narrow-definitions clause demands concrete acts and specific intent, excluding treason by speech. The public-interest test in Article 14.9.3 gives courts a workable standard when the State invokes secrecy, and the mandatory periodic review ensures these provisions are re-justified rather than merely renewed.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 21.5.2 (No Punishment for Journalism); Article 27.5.1 (Protected Disclosures); Article 1.3.1 (Freedom of Expression and Opinion).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__offenses-against-persons", "path": "14-criminal-law/offenses-against-persons.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.3 — Offenses Against Persons", "md": "# Section 14.3 — Offenses Against Persons\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.3.1 – 14.3.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section defines the protection of life, bodily integrity, liberty, and dignity, establishes consent-based sexual offenses, and removes limitation periods for grave offenses.*\n\n## Article 14.3.1 — Offenses Against Life\n\n1. The Code shall protect human life through offenses of intentional homicide, with graver forms for homicide committed with particular cruelty or against especially defenseless victims.\n2. The death penalty shall not exist, and no penalty may be imposed that violates the prohibition of cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment.\n\n## Article 14.3.2 — Bodily Integrity\n\n1. The Code shall punish assaults on bodily integrity, with the gravity of the offense measured by the seriousness and permanence of the harm.\n2. Domestic violence, female genital mutilation, and other forms of gender-based violence constitute offenses, and the law shall treat violence committed by a close relation as an aggravating circumstance.\n\n## Article 14.3.3 — Liberty and Dignity\n\n1. The Code shall punish kidnapping, unlawful coercion, stalking, human trafficking, and forced labor, the last two consistently with the prohibition of forced labor under Article 1.9.3 (Prohibition of Forced Labor).\n\n## Article 14.3.4 — Consent-Based Sexual Offenses\n\n1. Sexual offenses shall be defined by reference to the absence of freely given consent.\n2. Consent shall not be inferred from silence, passivity, or the absence of resistance, and no prior or existing relationship shall imply consent.\n3. Marriage or a comparable relationship shall not constitute a defense to a sexual offense.\n\n## Article 14.3.5 — Offenses Against Children\n\n1. The Code shall provide special protection for children, including offenses of sexual abuse, exploitation, grooming, and child pornography.\n2. The vulnerability of the victim by reason of age or dependency shall be an aggravating circumstance, consistent with the priority of the child's best interests under Article 22.7.2 (Protection from Violence and Exploitation).\n\n## Article 14.3.6 — No Limitation Period\n\n1. No limitation period shall apply to the prosecution of offenses against life, human trafficking, and serious sexual offenses, including offenses against child victims.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Consent, defined as free and volitional agreement rather than the absence of resistance, is made the organizing element of sexual offenses; the explicit non-inference rules respond to the persistent evidentiary myths that have historically shielded perpetrators. Abolition of the death penalty is stated here because life is the protected interest at stake, and it aligns with the mandatory refusal of surrender under Article 19.6.2 (Mandatory Refusals) where the requesting jurisdiction retains capital punishment. Removing limitation periods for the gravest offenses acknowledges that victims of childhood abuse and trafficking often come forward only decades later.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.3 (Prohibition of Forced Labor); Article 22.7.2 (Protection from Violence and Exploitation); Article 14.10.2 (Individualization).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__organized-crime", "path": "14-criminal-law/organized-crime.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.8 — Organized Crime", "md": "# Section 14.8 — Organized Crime\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.8.1 – 14.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section addresses criminal associations through conduct-based liability, judicially authorized infiltration powers, and asset forfeiture with strong safeguards.*\n\n## Article 14.8.1 — Criminal Association\n\n1. Participation in an association of three or more persons, structured over time and established for the commission of serious offenses, constitutes an offense when accompanied by an intentional act of participation.\n2. Mere membership by belief, family tie, or social association, without an act of participation, shall not be punishable.\n\n## Article 14.8.2 — Infiltration and Controlled Deliveries\n\n1. Undercover operations and controlled deliveries may be authorized only by prior judicial decision, for limited periods, and only where the evidence sought cannot reasonably be obtained by less intrusive means.\n2. The authorization shall specify the permitted acts, and agents shall not incite the commission of an offense that the person would not otherwise have committed.\n\n## Article 14.8.3 — Asset Forfeiture\n\n1. Proceeds and instrumentalities of organized crime shall be subject to forfeiture by judicial decision, consistent with the protection of property under Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property).\n2. The law may provide for extended forfeiture of assets whose lawful origin cannot be plausibly demonstrated by a person convicted within a criminal association, subject to judicial control and to the protection of bona fide third parties.\n\n## Article 14.8.4 — Collaborators of Justice\n\n1. The law shall provide for the protection of persons who cooperate with the prosecution of organized crime.\n2. Sentencing reduction for cooperation shall remain within the limits of proportionality set by Article 14.2.5 (Proportionality).\n\n## Article 14.8.5 — Review of Special Techniques\n\n1. The use of special investigative techniques under this section shall be reported annually to Parliament in a form that protects ongoing investigations.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Organized-crime law risks trading away legality and property guarantees in the name of effectiveness, so every special device here is fenced by a judicial gatekeeper and a conduct requirement. The act-of-participation rule rejects pure status offenses, keeping association liability anchored in what the accused did. Extended forfeiture is admitted as the one asymmetric tool, since criminal enterprises compartmentalize ownership, but only after conviction and under judicial control, with third-party bona fide interests protected.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property); Article 14.2.5 (Proportionality); Article 15.4.1 (Lawfulness and Exclusion).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__principles-of-criminal-law", "path": "14-criminal-law/principles-of-criminal-law.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.2 — Principles of Criminal Law", "md": "# Section 14.2 — Principles of Criminal Law\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.2.1 – 14.2.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section states the binding principles of criminal law: legality, non-retroactivity with lenity, culpability, individual guilt, proportionality, and double jeopardy.*\n\n## Article 14.2.1 — Legality\n\n1. No one shall be held guilty of an offense on account of conduct that did not constitute a criminal offense under the law at the time it was committed.\n2. Criminal law shall be construed strictly, and any ambiguity shall be resolved in favor of the accused.\n\n## Article 14.2.2 — Non-Retroactivity and Lenity\n\n1. No penalty shall be imposed that was not provided for by law at the time of the offense.\n2. Where the law in force after the commission of an offense provides for a lighter penalty, the lighter penalty shall apply.\n\n## Article 14.2.3 — Culpability\n\n1. Criminal liability requires culpability, that is, intention or negligence, as defined by law.\n2. The law may attach liability to a state of affairs only where the law so expressly provides, only for minor regulatory matters, and never for conduct punishable by imprisonment.\n\n## Article 14.2.4 — Individual Guilt\n\n1. Criminal liability is personal. No person shall be held criminally liable for the conduct of another, and no collective, family, or group liability shall exist.\n\n## Article 14.2.5 — Proportionality\n\n1. The nature and degree of punishment shall be proportionate to the gravity of the offense and the culpability of the offender.\n2. Offenses and penalties shall observe the master limitation clause of Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 14.2.6 — Double Jeopardy\n\n1. No person shall be tried or punished again for an offense for which that person has already been finally acquitted or convicted.\n2. Narrow exceptions for the retrial of finally acquitted persons may be established by law only where grave new evidence of guilt emerges, in conformity with Article 15.7.3 (Retrial and Double Jeopardy).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The six principles form the constitutional grammar of the entire Part and bind every offense defined in it. Strict construction and the lenity rule protect the citizen against retroactive or expansive interpretation, while the culpability clause prevents the quiet growth of strict liability into serious crime. The double-jeopardy exception is drafted narrowly and by cross-reference, so that the constitutional rule is never softened by judicial construction alone.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 15.7.3 (Retrial and Double Jeopardy); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 0.4.1 (Legality).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__property-offenses", "path": "14-criminal-law/property-offenses.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.4 — Property Offenses", "md": "# Section 14.4 — Property Offenses\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.4.1 – 14.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section defines the core property offenses and guarantees that intangible and digital property receive protection equal to tangible property.*\n\n## Article 14.4.1 — Offenses of Taking\n\n1. The Code shall punish the dishonest appropriation of property belonging to another, including theft, robbery, and extortion.\n2. Robbery and extortion involving violence or threats shall be punished as offenses against both property and personal liberty.\n\n## Article 14.4.2 — Fraud and Deception\n\n1. The Code shall punish obtaining property, services, or financial advantage by deception, including through false identities, forged instruments, and fraudulent representations.\n2. Fraud committed against particularly vulnerable persons or through organized means shall constitute an aggravated form of the offense.\n\n## Article 14.4.3 — Criminal Damage\n\n1. The Code shall punish the destruction of or damage to property belonging to another.\n2. Damage endangering the life or safety of persons, including arson, shall be treated as an aggravated form of the offense.\n3. Damage of minimal significance may, as the law provides, be reserved to civil remedies.\n\n## Article 14.4.4 — Intangible and Digital Property\n\n1. Intangible property, data, virtual assets, and electronic financial instruments shall receive protection equal to that of tangible property.\n2. Unlawful interference with data and systems may additionally constitute offenses under Article 14.7.2 (Data and System Interference).\n\n## Article 14.4.5 — Restitution and Recovery\n\n1. The Code shall provide for restitution to victims of property offenses as a consequence of conviction.\n2. Proceeds of property offenses shall be recoverable under the rules on the recovery of proceeds of crime.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section keeps the classical offense structure of taking, deception, and damage but modernizes the object of protection: the equal-treatment clause ensures that a stolen database or emptied digital wallet is not treated as a lesser wrong than a stolen bicycle. Linking digital interference to the dedicated cybercrime provisions prevents gaps and double definitions between the two offense groups. Restitution is constitutionalized because the injury of property crime is primarily to the victim's patrimony, and punishment alone does not repair it.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property); Article 14.7.2 (Data and System Interference); Article 14.5.4 (Recovery of Proceeds).\n"}, {"id": "14-criminal-law__sentencing", "path": "14-criminal-law/sentencing.md", "part": "14-criminal-law", "partTitle": "Criminal Law", "title": "Section 14.10 — Sentencing", "md": "# Section 14.10 — Sentencing\n\n> **Part 14 — Criminal Law** · Articles 14.10.1 – 14.10.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This section governs the purposes and individualization of sentencing, promotes alternatives to imprisonment, subjects life sentences to periodic review, and makes rehabilitation the goal of the penal system.*\n\n## Article 14.10.1 — Purposes of Sentencing\n\n1. Sentencing serves just retribution proportionate to the offense, the protection of the public, reparation toward victims and society, and the rehabilitation of the offender.\n\n## Article 14.10.2 — Individualization\n\n1. Every sentence shall be individualized according to the gravity of the offense, the culpability of the offender, and the personal circumstances relevant to the case.\n2. Sentencing courts shall state the reasons for the penalty imposed and for its position within the range provided by law.\n\n## Article 14.10.3 — Alternatives to Imprisonment\n\n1. For offenses of lesser gravity, non-custodial penalties shall be presumed, including fines scaled to income, community service, restorative justice measures, and treatment programs.\n2. Custodial sentences shall be reserved for serious offenses or where non-custodial measures have failed or are plainly inadequate.\n\n## Article 14.10.4 — Life Sentences and Review\n\n1. Life imprisonment may be imposed only for the gravest offenses.\n2. Every life sentence and every long custodial sentence shall be subject to periodic review by a court, which shall order release where continued detention is no longer justified by the purposes of sentencing under Article 14.10.1 (Purposes of Sentencing).\n\n## Article 14.10.5 — Parole and Rehabilitation\n\n1. The law shall establish a conditional-release system based on individualized assessment of the progress and risk of the person.\n2. The penal system shall provide education, work, and treatment programs supporting reintegration, consistent with the rehabilitation mandate of Article 15.8.5 (Reintegration) and the vocational pathways of Article 11.5.1 (Vocational Pathways).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The section refuses to choose between retribution and rehabilitation by naming both, but its institutional choices lean clearly toward reintegration: a presumption of non-custodial penalties, reviewable life sentences, and a parole system with real criteria. Periodic review of life sentences follows the principle that punishment must remain justifiable over time and that persons can change, a position taken by several apex courts in comparative human-rights jurisprudence. Reasoned individualization gives appeals courts and the public a basis for checking both excessive severity and unexplained leniency.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 15.8.5 (Reintegration); Article 11.5.1 (Vocational Pathways); Article 14.2.5 (Proportionality).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__appeals", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/appeals.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.7 — Appeals", "md": "# Section 15.7 — Appeals\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.7.1 – 15.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees appeal as of right in criminal cases, channels constitutional questions to the Constitutional Court, confines retrial to the accused's detriment to narrow grounds, and bars worsening on the accused's appeal.*\n\n## Article 15.7.1 — Appeal as of Right\n\n1. A party may appeal a first-instance judgment on questions of fact and of law to the next higher court.\n2. The appeal lies as of right for the prosecution and for the accused; no leave requirement may apply to an appeal against a conviction or a sentence of imprisonment.\n\n## Article 15.7.2 — Scope and Safeguards\n\n1. The appellate court shall review the assessment of evidence, the application of the law, and the sentence.\n2. A conviction may be reversed or a sentence varied only after the appellant has been heard; new evidence shall be admitted where it may affect the outcome.\n\n## Article 15.7.3 — Retrial and Double Jeopardy\n\n1. No person shall be tried again for an offense of which the person has been finally acquitted or convicted, consistent with Article 14.2.6 (Double Jeopardy).\n2. A retrial to the detriment of a finally acquitted person may be authorized by law only where grave and compelling new evidence of guilt emerges, only within a period fixed by law, and only with the approval of a superior court.\n\n## Article 15.7.4 — Constitutional Questions\n\n1. A question of constitutionality arising in criminal proceedings may be referred to the Constitutional Court as provided by Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction).\n2. A person who has exhausted the ordinary remedies may challenge the final judgment by individual complaint consistent with Article 5.3.4 (Individual Complaints).\n\n## Article 15.7.5 — No Worsening on Appeal\n\n1. On an appeal brought by the accused alone, the appellate court shall not impose a heavier sentence than the judgment under appeal.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Appeal as of right on facts and law follows the two-instance tradition of continental systems, while the prohibition of worsening on the accused's own appeal — the continental rule against reformatio in peius — keeps the right of appeal from becoming a trap. The retrial exception is drafted as a judicial valve rather than a legislative licence: grave new evidence, a fixed period, and superior-court approval together protect the finality that makes acquittals safe. The constitutional-question route ties the ordinary courts to the Constitutional Court without dismantling the single appeal path.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 14.2.6 (Double Jeopardy); Article 5.3.4 (Individual Complaints); Article 5.2.1 (Status and Jurisdiction).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__arrest", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/arrest.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.1 — Arrest and Judicial Control", "md": "# Section 15.1 — Arrest and Judicial Control\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.1.1 – 15.1.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section regulates arrest and executive deprivation of liberty: the warrant rule, information of rights, the prompt judicial appearance, habeas corpus, and remedies.*\n\n## Article 15.1.1 — Arrest on Warrant\n\n1. No person shall be arrested except under a warrant issued in advance by a judge, on reasonable suspicion, stated with particularity, that the person has committed or is about to commit an offense.\n2. A warrantless arrest is lawful only where a person is caught in the act or in immediate flight, or where delay would create an imminent danger to life; every such arrest shall be reported without delay to a judge.\n3. The law may define further narrow exceptions for serious offenses; every exception shall be construed strictly.\n\n## Article 15.1.2 — Information of Rights\n\n1. Upon arrest, the person shall be informed promptly, in a language the person understands, of the reasons for the arrest and of the person's rights, including the right to remain silent, to counsel, and to an interpreter.\n2. The person shall be enabled to notify a relative of the person's choice and to contact counsel without delay.\n\n## Article 15.1.3 — Prompt Judicial Appearance\n\n1. Every arrested person shall be brought before a judge within forty-eight hours of the arrest.\n2. The judge shall review the lawfulness and the necessity of the detention without delay and shall order immediate release unless detention is justified by law.\n\n## Article 15.1.4 — Habeas Corpus\n\n1. Every detained person may at any time challenge the lawfulness of the detention before a court, consistent with Article 1.7.4 (Habeas Corpus and Liberty).\n2. The challenge shall be simple, prompt, and free of charge, and no act of the executive may limit it; where the court finds the detention unlawful, it shall order release.\n\n## Article 15.1.5 — Treatment and Custody Record\n\n1. An arrest shall be carried out with the minimum force necessary and with respect for human dignity.\n2. Every deprivation of liberty shall be entered in a single custody record stating the time, place, and authority of the arrest and every transfer; the record shall be open to the person, to counsel, to the judge, and to oversight bodies.\n\n## Article 15.1.6 — Remedies for Unlawful Arrest\n\n1. A person unlawfully arrested or detained has the right to compensation.\n2. Unlawful conduct in connection with an arrest shall entail disciplinary responsibility and, where culpable, criminal responsibility.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The forty-eight-hour appearance converts the vague \"promptly\" of many international texts into an enforceable deadline; comparative codes commonly allow twenty-four to ninety-six hours, and the constitutional text fixes the outer limit so that ordinary legislation cannot stretch it. Habeas corpus is kept free-standing because custody is where legality fails first, a lesson as old as the writ itself. The single custody record gives the deadline an audit trail and the detainee a document instead of a denial.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.4 (Habeas Corpus and Liberty); Article 5.9.3 (Mandatory Criminal Legal Aid); Article 15.5.2 (Conditions of Release).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__bail", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/bail.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.5 — Bail and Pre-Trial Detention", "md": "# Section 15.5 — Bail and Pre-Trial Detention\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.5.1 – 15.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs liberty before judgment: the presumption of release, proportionate conditions, prompt periodic review, statutory caps on detention, and a non-punitive remand regime.*\n\n## Article 15.5.1 — Presumption of Release\n\n1. A person awaiting trial shall be presumed entitled to release.\n2. Detention pending trial may be ordered only where release would create a concrete risk of flight, of interference with evidence or witnesses, or of serious reoffending, and only where less restrictive means are inadequate to the risk.\n\n## Article 15.5.2 — Conditions of Release\n\n1. Conditions imposed on release shall be proportionate to the risk addressed and shall be the least intrusive available; financial conditions shall be set at a level the person can actually meet.\n2. Conditions shall be recorded in writing with reasons and may be varied or lifted on application or on the court's own motion.\n\n## Article 15.5.3 — Prompt Review\n\n1. An order of detention pending trial shall be reviewed by a court at intervals not exceeding thirty days.\n2. The detained person may seek earlier review where new facts emerge, and the review shall consider whether the grounds for detention persist.\n\n## Article 15.5.4 — Detention Periods\n\n1. The law shall fix maximum periods of detention pending trial according to the gravity of the offense; detention beyond the statutory maximum shall be unlawful.\n2. A person detained beyond the applicable maximum shall be released without delay, without prejudice to the continuation of the trial.\n\n## Article 15.5.5 — Remand Regime\n\n1. A person detained pending trial shall be kept apart from convicted persons and shall be treated as innocent, consistent with Article 1.7.2 (Presumption of Innocence).\n2. Remand detention shall not be punitive; access to counsel, family, and health care shall be ensured.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Bail is drafted around the risk to be managed, not the wealth of the accused; the can-meet rule closes the cash-bail trap that jails the poor while freeing the dangerous, following the just-cause structure of the Canadian Charter's pre-trial detention clause. Fixed review intervals prevent remand from quietly becoming the sentence before any conviction. The separate remand regime gives the presumption of innocence institutional form, because a person treated as guilty in custody has already lost the trial that has not yet happened.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.2 (Presumption of Innocence); Article 15.1.3 (Prompt Judicial Appearance); Article 14.10.3 (Alternatives to Imprisonment).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__evidence", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/evidence.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.4 — Evidence", "md": "# Section 15.4 — Evidence\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.4.1 – 15.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states how evidence is obtained and judged: lawful acquisition, the absolute exclusion of torture-derived material, reliability standards, the chain of custody, and disclosure.*\n\n## Article 15.4.1 — Lawfulness and Exclusion\n\n1. Evidence shall be obtained by lawful means only.\n2. Evidence obtained through a serious violation of this Constitution or of the law shall be inadmissible where the violation impaired the reliability of the evidence or the fairness of the trial.\n3. Exclusion shall not prevent the independent discovery of the same facts by lawful means.\n\n## Article 15.4.2 — Torture-Derived Evidence\n\n1. A statement or other evidence obtained by torture or by cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment shall be inadmissible for any purpose.\n2. Other evidence derived from such a statement shall be inadmissible.\n3. No consideration of reliability, gravity, or public security may qualify this Article.\n\n## Article 15.4.3 — Reliability Standards\n\n1. Evidence shall be assessed for authenticity, integrity, and reliability according to standards provided by law; electronic evidence is governed by Section 17.8 (Electronic Evidence).\n2. Expert evidence shall be given by qualified and impartial experts, and the method underlying an opinion shall be disclosed and open to challenge.\n\n## Article 15.4.4 — Chain of Custody\n\n1. Exhibits and data shall be documented from seizure to presentation in court, and every transfer shall be recorded.\n2. An unexplained gap in the chain of custody shall weigh against the admission or the weight of the exhibit.\n\n## Article 15.4.5 — Disclosure\n\n1. The Public Prosecution Service shall disclose to the defense, in due time to enable the preparation of the defense, all material evidence, including material favorable to the accused.\n2. Failure to disclose favorable material shall be remedied by the court, including by adjournment, exclusion of the affected evidence, or discharge.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The absolute exclusion of torture-derived evidence, including its derivatives, gives constitutional rank to the rule of the United Nations Convention against Torture that such statements shall not be invoked; a regime that admits torture-derived corroboration still purchases torture, so the taint is treated as incurable. Lesser violations are governed by a fairness-and-reliability test rather than automatic exclusion, which keeps the sanction proportionate to the wrong. Disclosure of favorable material stands beside the exclusion rules because a trial can be no fairer than the file the defense actually sees.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 15.3.5 (Exclusionary Effect); Article 14.2.1 (Legality); Article 17.8.2 (Authentication and Integrity).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__interrogation", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/interrogation.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.3 — Interrogation", "md": "# Section 15.3 — Interrogation\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.3.1 – 15.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section disciplines the questioning of suspects: the caution, the presence of counsel, mandatory recording, the prohibition of coercion and inducements, and the exclusion of tainted statements.*\n\n## Article 15.3.1 — The Caution\n\n1. Before questioning begins, the person shall be cautioned that the person is not obliged to answer and that anything said may be used in evidence.\n2. Questioning of a person deprived of liberty shall not begin before the person has had the opportunity to speak with counsel.\n\n## Article 15.3.2 — Counsel During Questioning\n\n1. Counsel shall be present throughout the questioning of a person deprived of liberty; the person may waive counsel only expressly, on the record, and after being cautioned.\n2. Where the person cannot afford counsel, representation shall be provided at public expense consistent with Article 5.9.3 (Mandatory Criminal Legal Aid).\n3. Where the person requests counsel, questioning shall be suspended until counsel is present.\n\n## Article 15.3.3 — Recording\n\n1. Every interrogation of a person deprived of liberty shall be recorded in full by audiovisual means, and the recording shall be preserved until the final conclusion of the case.\n2. A statement made during an unrecorded interrogation may not be used in evidence unless the failure to record was not attributable to the authorities or the person consents to its use.\n3. The absence of a recording shall weigh against the reliability of any statement offered in evidence.\n\n## Article 15.3.4 — Prohibited Methods\n\n1. Coercion, ill-treatment, threats, inducements, drugs, exhaustion, and any method capable of destroying the person's will or capacity to decide shall not be used at any stage of proceedings.\n2. The law shall regulate the duration, rest periods, and conditions of questioning and shall provide for monitoring of compliance.\n\n## Article 15.3.5 — Exclusionary Effect\n\n1. A statement obtained in breach of this Section or of Article 1.7.3 (Rights of the Accused) shall be inadmissible.\n2. Evidence derived from an inadmissible statement shall be inadmissible unless the derivation is so remote that the taint is dissipated, as provided by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Mandatory recording is the cheapest safeguard in this Part: it protects the innocent against fabrication, the honest officer against unfounded complaint, and the court against disputes about what was said. The caution-plus-counsel sequence follows the model developed in the case law of the European Court of Human Rights and in the caution doctrine of United States law, with the waiver made express and recorded because a silent waiver is what coercion prefers. Derivative exclusion is calibrated: torture taint is treated as absolute in Section 15.4, while lesser breaches admit a remoteness test.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.3 (Rights of the Accused); Article 5.9.3 (Mandatory Criminal Legal Aid); Article 15.4.2 (Torture-Derived Evidence).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__prisoner-rights", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/prisoner-rights.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.8 — Rights of Prisoners", "md": "# Section 15.8 — Rights of Prisoners\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.8.1 – 15.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects persons deprived of liberty after conviction: humane treatment, the prohibition of degrading punishment, contact with family and counsel, independent inspection, and rehabilitation.*\n\n## Article 15.8.1 — Humane Treatment\n\n1. Every person deprived of liberty shall be treated humanely and with respect for dignity, consistent with Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n2. Conditions of detention shall not aggravate the deprivation of liberty beyond what its lawful purpose requires; accommodation, food, health care, exercise, and outdoor time shall meet the minimum standards provided by law.\n\n## Article 15.8.2 — No Degrading Punishment\n\n1. Corporal punishment, restraint in fixed positions, reduction of food, and collective punishment are prohibited in places of detention.\n2. Solitary confinement may be imposed only within the strict limits fixed by law, for the shortest necessary time, and never against minors.\n3. Discipline shall be individualized, proportionate, and subject to review before an independent body.\n\n## Article 15.8.3 — Family Contact and Counsel\n\n1. A prisoner shall be entitled to regular contact with family and to confidential communication with counsel and with the courts, subject only to reasonable restrictions required by security.\n2. Restrictions shall be reasoned, recorded, and reviewable.\n\n## Article 15.8.4 — Independent Inspection\n\n1. Places of detention shall be inspected regularly by an independent body empowered to enter without notice, to speak with prisoners in private, and to report publicly.\n2. Prisoners shall have an effective internal complaints channel and access to the courts.\n\n## Article 15.8.5 — Reintegration\n\n1. The penal system shall prepare every prisoner for release through education, work, treatment, and structured contact with society, consistent with the rehabilitation mandate of Article 14.10.5 (Parole and Rehabilitation).\n2. Participation in such programs shall be voluntary and shall not be secured by coercion.\n3. A spent criminal record shall not be used to defeat reintegration, as provided by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section turns the United Nations Standard Minimum Rules for the Treatment of Prisoners — the Nelson Mandela Rules — from soft law into justiciable commands, and the inspection body borrows the unannounced-visit model of the Optional Protocol to the Convention against Torture. Rehabilitation appears as a duty of the system rather than a hope, with participation kept voluntary so that programs cannot become an instrument of pressure. The record clause answers the quiet life-long punishment that follows release when the file never closes.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 14.10.5 (Parole and Rehabilitation); Article 15.1.5 (Treatment and Custody Record); Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__search-and-seizure", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/search-and-seizure.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.2 — Search and Seizure", "md": "# Section 15.2 — Search and Seizure\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.2.1 – 15.2.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs searches and seizures: warrants with particularity, narrow exigent exceptions, heightened protection for digital data, professional privilege, and self-incrimination.*\n\n## Article 15.2.1 — Warrant Requirement\n\n1. A search or seizure shall be carried out only under a warrant issued in advance by a judge, on the basis of reasonable grounds, and subject to Article 1.5.2 (Inviolability of the Home).\n2. A warrantless search or seizure is lawful only where imminent danger to life, imminent destruction of evidence, or immediate flight of a suspect makes prior authorization impossible; the authority shall seek judicial confirmation without delay.\n\n## Article 15.2.2 — Particularity\n\n1. A warrant shall identify the place or person, the objects of the search, and the offense concerned, and shall describe the items to be seized with reasonable particularity.\n2. General exploratory warrants are prohibited.\n3. A search shall be executed within the period fixed in the warrant, shall be recorded, and the person affected shall receive a copy of the record.\n\n## Article 15.2.3 — Digital Searches\n\n1. Searches of information systems, devices, and accounts require a separate judicial authorization stating the systems concerned, the data categories, and the period covered.\n2. Data not covered by the authorization shall be secured and shall be examined only under a further warrant.\n3. Interception of communications is governed by Article 1.5.3 (Confidentiality of Communications) and by law.\n\n## Article 15.2.4 — Privilege Against Self-Incrimination\n\n1. No person shall be compelled to make statements or to produce documents or data held for personal use that would incriminate that person, consistent with Article 1.7.3 (Rights of the Accused).\n2. The privilege does not extend to existing physical evidence independent of the person's will, as provided by law.\n\n## Article 15.2.5 — Privileged Relationships\n\n1. Seizure shall not extend to materials protected by professional confidentiality between counsel and client, physician and patient, or confessor and believer, or to such other confidential relationships as the law protects.\n2. An exception may be established by law only where the material is essential to a serious charge and no less intrusive means remain.\n\n## Article 15.2.6 — Seized Property\n\n1. Seized items shall be inventoried in the presence of the person affected where practicable.\n2. Items seized without or beyond lawful authority shall be returned without delay; the law shall regulate retention of items needed as evidence.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Particularity is the working core of the warrant: a warrant that says \"search everything\" is a general warrant in disguise, which is why this Section restates the particularity discipline familiar from the Fourth Amendment of the United States Constitution and adds an express rule for digital data. The separate-authorization rule for devices reflects that a phone now holds more private material than a house. Exigent exceptions must be confirmed by a judge promptly, so necessity begins the exception and judicial review ends it.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.2 (Inviolability of the Home); Article 1.7.3 (Rights of the Accused); Article 15.4.1 (Lawfulness and Exclusion).\n"}, {"id": "15-criminal-procedure__trial-procedure", "path": "15-criminal-procedure/trial-procedure.md", "part": "15-criminal-procedure", "partTitle": "Criminal Procedure", "title": "Section 15.6 — Trial Procedure", "md": "# Section 15.6 — Trial Procedure\n\n> **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure** · Articles 15.6.1 – 15.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section orders the criminal trial: adversarial process, the public hearing, equality of arms, reasoned judgments, and a narrow expedited procedure resting on informed consent.*\n\n## Article 15.6.1 — Adversarial Process\n\n1. The trial shall be adversarial: the prosecution and the defense shall have equal opportunity to present evidence, to examine and cross-examine witnesses, and to address the court.\n2. The court shall decide only on the evidence presented and tested in the hearing.\n\n## Article 15.6.2 — Public Trial\n\n1. The trial shall be public, and judgment shall be pronounced in public, consistent with Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial).\n2. The public may be excluded only by reasoned order, only to protect minors, privacy, or the integrity of justice, and only for the duration strictly necessary.\n\n## Article 15.6.3 — Equality of Arms\n\n1. The court shall ensure that neither party enjoys a procedural advantage by reason of the resources or the official position of that party.\n2. Where the defense lacks the means to meet the prosecution's case, the court shall secure the assistance that the interests of justice require, consistent with Article 5.9.1 (Right to Legal Aid).\n\n## Article 15.6.4 — Reasoned Judgments\n\n1. Judgments shall state the evidence accepted and rejected, the reasons for the finding of guilt or acquittal, and the reasons for the sentence.\n2. Reasons shall be sufficient to permit effective review on appeal.\n\n## Article 15.6.5 — Expedited Procedure\n\n1. An expedited or simplified procedure may be used only with the informed consent of the accused, given after advice of counsel and on the record.\n2. Consent shall not be induced by the threat of a heavier penalty in the ordinary procedure, and the accused may return to the ordinary procedure at any time before judgment.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The adversarial core is stated as a duty of the court, not merely a privilege of the parties, because inequality of arms is usually structural rather than tactical. Reasoned judgment is treated as a fair-trial guarantee in its own right: it disciplines first-instance fact-finding and makes the appeal system of Section 15.7 meaningful rather than ornamental. The expedited procedure is admitted on consent, counsel, and no inducement, reflecting the comparative consensus that simplified justice is legitimate only where the accused surrenders nothing unknowingly.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial); Article 5.9.1 (Right to Legal Aid); Article 5.4.3 (Open Courts and Lay Assessors).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__civil-procedure", "path": "16-civil-law/civil-procedure.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.9 — Civil Procedure", "md": "# Section 16.9 — Civil Procedure\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.9.1 – 16.9.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section secures civil justice: fair and oral hearings within a reasonable time, a small-claims track, alternative dispute resolution that is encouraged but never compelled, and effective enforcement of judgments.*\n\n## Article 16.9.1 — Fair Hearing\n\n1. Civil proceedings shall be fair, adversarial, and decided by an independent and impartial tribunal, consistent with Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial).\n2. The decisive hearing shall be oral; decision without an oral hearing may be taken only where the parties consent or for the categories of cases provided by law.\n\n## Article 16.9.2 — Reasonable Time and Case Management\n\n1. Proceedings shall be conducted within a reasonable time, consistent with Article 5.7.1 (Timely Justice).\n2. Courts shall manage cases actively through early definition of the disputed points, proportionate exchange of evidence, and firm scheduling.\n\n## Article 16.9.3 — Small Claims\n\n1. The law shall provide a small-claims track with reduced formality, limited costs, and prompt decision, consistent with Article 5.7.2 (Simplified and Small-Claims Procedures).\n2. Appeal from the small-claims track may be limited as provided by law, save where a fundamental defect is alleged.\n\n## Article 16.9.4 — Mediation and ADR\n\n1. Courts shall inform the parties of mediation and other methods of dispute resolution and may encourage their use.\n2. No person shall be compelled to mediate; access to a court shall not be conditioned on prior settlement efforts.\n\n## Article 16.9.5 — Enforcement\n\n1. Judgments shall be enforced by the public authority without undue delay; the losing party may resist enforcement only on the narrow grounds provided by law.\n2. Enforcement shall be proportionate: minimums protecting the debtor's basic subsistence and home shall be exempt, and enforcement measures shall be reviewable by a court.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Civil procedure is drafted around proportionality: the small-claims track exists because a uniform procedure makes justice too expensive for small rights, and the ADR rule offers the modern settlement infrastructure without letting it become a condition of access, the error of compulsory-mediation experiments struck down in comparative practice. Case management duties place the fight against delay on the court rather than on the litigant. Enforcement closes the loop, because an unenforceable judgment is a recommendation; pairing effective execution with subsistence exemptions and judicial review keeps execution itself lawful.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial); Article 5.7.2 (Simplified and Small-Claims Procedures); Article 5.7.3 (Fees and Waivers).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__contracts", "path": "16-civil-law/contracts.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.1 — Contracts", "md": "# Section 16.1 — Contracts\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.1.1 – 16.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs private agreement: freedom of contract, good faith in negotiation and performance, the control of unfair standard terms, defects of consent, and consumer protection.*\n\n## Article 16.1.1 — Freedom of Contract\n\n1. Parties may conclude contracts and determine their content freely; a validly concluded contract binds the parties and may be set aside only on grounds provided by law.\n2. Freedom of contract is subject to this Constitution, to the law, and to public policy, consistent with Article 9.1.3 (Freedom of Contract).\n\n## Article 16.1.2 — Good Faith\n\n1. Parties shall negotiate, perform, and enforce contracts in accordance with good faith.\n2. Negotiations conducted in bad faith, including a break-off without reasonable grounds after creating reasonable reliance, may give rise to liability as provided by law.\n\n## Article 16.1.3 — Unfair Standard Terms\n\n1. A term that has not been individually negotiated and that causes a significant imbalance in the parties' rights and obligations to the detriment of the weaker party shall not bind that party.\n2. A term that excludes or limits liability for intentional or grossly negligent conduct shall not bind any party.\n3. Ambiguity in standard terms shall be construed against the party that supplied them; the remainder of the contract survives where it can stand without the unfair term.\n\n## Article 16.1.4 — Defects of Consent\n\n1. Consent obtained by fraud, duress, or material mistake may be set aside as provided by law.\n2. The party that induced the defect shall make good the resulting loss.\n\n## Article 16.1.5 — Consumers\n\n1. Suppliers of goods and services shall observe the guarantees of Article 1.14.3 (Fair Contract Terms) in every contract with a consumer.\n2. The law shall require plain language and pre-contractual information in consumer contracts and shall provide accessible redress consistent with Article 1.14.4 (Accessible Redress).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section keeps the classical architecture — freedom, binding force, defects of consent — and adds two modern disciplines: a general good-faith duty in the tradition of section 242 of the German Civil Code, and a rule of non-binding unfair standard terms modeled on the European Union's Unfair Contract Terms Directive. Policing operates through invalidity of the term, not of the contract, which protects the weaker party without destabilizing exchange, and the construction rule against the drafter gives the test teeth. Consumer protection is implemented by cross-reference so that the constitutional guarantee of Section 1.14 and the private law speak with one voice.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.1.3 (Freedom of Contract); Article 1.14.3 (Fair Contract Terms); Article 1.14.4 (Accessible Redress).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__divorce", "path": "16-civil-law/divorce.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.7 — Divorce", "md": "# Section 16.7 — Divorce\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.7.1 – 16.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the dissolution of marriage: no-fault divorce, mediation offered but never compelled, fair division of property, maintenance where needed, and the paramountcy of arrangements for children.*\n\n## Article 16.7.1 — Dissolution by Law\n\n1. Marriage may be dissolved only by a court or by another authority provided by law.\n2. Divorce shall be granted upon the irretrievable breakdown of the marriage; neither the grant of divorce nor its terms shall depend on the attribution of fault.\n3. Breakdown may be established by separation for a period fixed by law or by other evidence of the end of the marital relationship.\n\n## Article 16.7.2 — Mediation\n\n1. Mediation and counseling shall be offered to the parties at the outset of proceedings.\n2. Participation in mediation shall never be compelled, and refusal shall not affect the outcome of the proceedings; an agreement reached in mediation shall be confirmed only after review for fairness.\n\n## Article 16.7.3 — Division of Property\n\n1. Property acquired during the marriage and the increase in the partners' estates shall be divided according to principles of fairness fixed by law, with regard to the care of children and household, earning capacity, and the needs of each partner.\n2. Disadvantage suffered for the sake of the family shall be compensated.\n\n## Article 16.7.4 — Maintenance\n\n1. A partner who cannot maintain himself or herself adequately after divorce shall be entitled to maintenance from the other partner, according to need and capacity, as provided by law.\n\n## Article 16.7.5 — Arrangements for Children\n\n1. Arrangements for the care, residence, and contact of children shall be made according to the best interests of the child, consistent with Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child).\n2. The child shall be heard, arrangements shall be reviewed where circumstances materially change, and protection services are stated in Part 22 (Social Policy).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*No-fault divorce removes the perjury economy of blame-based systems: once the marriage has genuinely ended, the State's interest is in orderly consequences, not in adjudicated guilt, and fault survives only as evidence relevant to safety and to children. Mediation is offered because negotiated outcomes age better than imposed ones, but compulsion would convert a service into a toll gate on access to court. The division rules recognize care work as a contribution of equal dignity with earning, applying within divorce the equality command of Article 16.5.2 (Equality of Partners).*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child); Article 16.5.2 (Equality of Partners); Article 16.5.3 (Parental Responsibility).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__family-law", "path": "16-civil-law/family-law.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.5 — Family Law", "md": "# Section 16.5 — Family Law\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.5.1 – 16.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects the family as the fundamental unit of society, orders the equality of partners and joint parental responsibility, secures the voice and welfare of the child, and assigns the State its supporting duties.*\n\n## Article 16.5.1 — The Family\n\n1. The family is the fundamental unit of society and is entitled to protection.\n2. Family life may take diverse forms; the protection of this Section extends to every family without discrimination, consistent with Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n\n## Article 16.5.2 — Equality of Partners\n\n1. Partners in a family have equal rights and equal duties in its decisions, property, and care work.\n2. Household and care work shall be valued on equal terms with earning in the property and maintenance rules of this Part.\n\n## Article 16.5.3 — Parental Responsibility\n\n1. Parents hold joint responsibility for the upbringing, development, and property of their children, guided by the best interests of the child consistent with Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child).\n2. Separation of the parents does not end either parent's responsibility; contact with both parents shall be maintained where consistent with the child's best interests.\n\n## Article 16.5.4 — Voice and Welfare of the Child\n\n1. A child capable of forming views shall be heard in family proceedings affecting the child, consistent with Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children).\n2. The maintenance of children takes priority over the other claims of their parents.\n\n## Article 16.5.5 — State Support and Protection\n\n1. The State shall support families through services, benefits, and leave arrangements as provided by law.\n2. The State shall protect family members from violence within the family; protection measures shall be prompt and shall be designed so that, where possible, the person protected remains in the home and the violent person must leave.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Equality of partners is made concrete by requiring that care work count in the property and maintenance rules, which answers the classic gap where formal equality left economically dependent partners exposed upon dissolution. The family is protected in its diverse forms rather than in a single prescribed model, and cross-references import the child's paramountcy and participation rights from Section 1.15. The violence clause places the burden of departure on the violent partner rather than the victim, the design behind modern protection-order statutes.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child); Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__guardianship", "path": "16-civil-law/guardianship.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.8 — Guardianship", "md": "# Section 16.8 — Guardianship\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.8.1 – 16.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs care and representation for those who need it: the presumption of capacity, supported decision-making before any substitute decision, narrowly confined guardianship, independent review, and guardianship of children without parental care.*\n\n## Article 16.8.1 — Presumption of Capacity\n\n1. Every adult shall be presumed to have capacity to decide and to manage the person's own affairs.\n2. A finding of incapacity shall be personal, specific to the matter concerned, and based on individual assessment; generalized findings are prohibited.\n\n## Article 16.8.2 — Supported Decision-Making\n\n1. Before any substitute measure, the law shall provide for support that enables the person to decide, including the assistance of a trusted person, accessible formats, and advance directives.\n2. A supported decision is the decision of the person; the supporter shall not decide for the person.\n\n## Article 16.8.3 — Substitute Measures\n\n1. Guardianship and other substitute measures may be ordered only where supported decision-making is inadequate, only for the specific matters necessary, and only for the shortest adequate time.\n2. The guardian shall act in the person's best interests, shall respect the person's will and preferences so far as they can be ascertained, and shall account regularly to the supervising authority.\n\n## Article 16.8.4 — Independent Review\n\n1. Substitute measures shall be ordered by a court after the person has been heard with assistance, and shall be reviewed by the court at intervals fixed by law.\n2. The person may appeal and may at any time apply for termination where capacity has returned or where support suffices.\n\n## Article 16.8.5 — Guardianship of Children\n\n1. Where parents are unable or unwilling to fulfil parental responsibility, a guardian shall be appointed for the child under judicial supervision, taking account of the child's views consistent with Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children).\n2. The guardian shall be subject to the review duties of Article 16.8.4 (Independent Review), and alternative care shall follow the standards of Section 1.15 (Rights of Children).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section translates Article 12 of the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities into a decision sequence: presumption, support, and only then substitution, with substitute measures sliced to the specific matter and the shortest adequate time. For adults, the will-and-preferences standard borrows the Convention's own language rather than the best-interests test of childhood, precisely because adults are not children. Fixed review intervals guard against guardianship drifting from a protection into a confiscation of legal personality, and the mental-health regime of Article 12.7.2 (Consent and Support) applies the same philosophy in the clinical setting.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies); Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children); Article 12.7.2 (Consent and Support).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__inheritance", "path": "16-civil-law/inheritance.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.4 — Inheritance", "md": "# Section 16.4 — Inheritance\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.4.1 – 16.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs succession upon death: testamentary freedom bounded by a compulsory family share, equality among heirs without discrimination, estate administration, and intestate succession.*\n\n## Article 16.4.1 — Testamentary Freedom\n\n1. Every person may dispose of the person's estate by will within the limits of this Section.\n2. A will shall be made freely and with capacity; a will procured by fraud, duress, or material mistake may be set aside.\n\n## Article 16.4.2 — Compulsory Family Share\n\n1. The descendants, the spouse, and, as provided by law, the parents of the deceased shall be entitled to a compulsory share of the estate.\n2. The compulsory share shall be fixed by law as a fraction of the share the person would have taken on intestacy and may be displaced only on the narrow grounds provided by law, such as grave misconduct toward the deceased.\n3. Subject to the compulsory share, the deceased may dispose freely.\n\n## Article 16.4.3 — No Discrimination Among Heirs\n\n1. No person shall be excluded from succession or from the compulsory share on any ground listed in Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination), including sex, birth in or out of wedlock, or adoption.\n2. Children shall stand equal in succession; adopted children shall be placed in the same position as children born within the marriage.\n\n## Article 16.4.4 — Estate Administration\n\n1. The estate passes on death subject to the payment of its debts; an heir may accept the estate subject to an inventory and shall not thereby become liable beyond its value.\n2. Administration shall proceed under court supervision where disputes exist, and the executor or administrator shall account to the heirs.\n\n## Article 16.4.5 — Intestate Succession\n\n1. Where there is no effective will, succession follows the order of kinship fixed by law, with the spouse and the descendants taking first.\n2. Where no kin entitled to succeed can be found, the estate passes to the State, which shall apply it to public purposes.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The compulsory share reconciles the two great traditions — full testamentary freedom and the family's protected portion — by guaranteeing close kin a fraction of the statutory share while leaving the residue free, a settlement closer to the German Pflichtteil than to a fixed division of the whole estate. The nondiscrimination clause is not decorative: the exclusion of daughters, of children born outside marriage, and of adopted children was the daily business of succession law in many systems well into the twentieth century, and the clause closes that door permanently. The benefit-of-inventory rule prevents grief from becoming personal insolvency.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property); Article 16.3.1 (Ownership and Limited Rights).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__marriage", "path": "16-civil-law/marriage.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.6 — Marriage", "md": "# Section 16.6 — Marriage\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.6.1 – 16.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section regulates marriage: free and full consent, a strict minimum age of eighteen without exceptions, equal rights of the partners during and after marriage, and non-discriminatory recognition of the forms marriage may take.*\n\n## Article 16.6.1 — Freedom to Marry\n\n1. Persons of marriageable age have the right to marry and to found a family.\n2. Marriage requires the free and full consent of both partners, expressed before the competent authority; a marriage concluded without such consent is void.\n3. No marriage shall be concluded under coercion, deception, or pressure that destroys genuine consent.\n\n## Article 16.6.2 — Minimum Age\n\n1. Marriage may be concluded only by persons who have reached the age of eighteen years.\n2. No authority may grant a dispensation from the minimum age on any ground, including pregnancy, parental consent, or the celebration of a religious ceremony.\n\n## Article 16.6.3 — Equal Rights in Marriage\n\n1. Partners have equal rights and equal duties in marriage, during the marriage and upon its dissolution, in property, care, representation, and every other respect.\n2. Each partner retains full legal capacity and may choose work, profession, and property dealings freely.\n\n## Article 16.6.4 — Recognition of Forms\n\n1. Marriage may be solemnized in civil or religious form; every form recognized by law carries the same legal effects.\n2. All marriages shall be recognized without discrimination, consistent with Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); the law shall provide for registered partnerships carrying equivalent effects and shall give effect to marriages validly concluded under the law of another state.\n\n## Article 16.6.5 — Registration\n\n1. Marriages shall be registered; registration may not be refused where the requirements of consent and age are met.\n2. Failure to register shall not invalidate a marriage that was validly solemnized.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The absolute age floor with no dispensations is deliberately stricter than systems that permit judicial or parental exceptions, because child marriage is the point where family autonomy collides with the child's rights under Section 1.15, and the harm is irreversible in a way that ordinary limitations cannot repair. Consent is made the essence of validity, voiding coerced marriages outright rather than merely annulling them at discretion. Equal treatment of all recognized forms follows from the equality guarantee and leaves religious communities their rites while reserving civil effects to the civil order.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child); Article 16.7.1 (Dissolution by Law).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__property", "path": "16-civil-law/property.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.3 — Property", "md": "# Section 16.3 — Property\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.3.1 – 16.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states the private law of property: ownership with its co-operative forms, the land registry with its protective conclusiveness, good-faith acquisition of movables, and the public-law limits that keep ownership social.*\n\n## Article 16.3.1 — Ownership and Limited Rights\n\n1. Ownership entitles the holder, within the limits of this Constitution and the law, to possess, use, and dispose of the thing, consistent with Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property).\n2. Ownership may be held in co-ownership; the shares and the management of co-owned property may be regulated by agreement or by law.\n3. Land may be burdened with servitudes and other limited rights in favor of other parcels or of persons, as provided by law.\n\n## Article 16.3.2 — The Land Registry\n\n1. The State shall maintain a public land registry recording ownership and encumbrances of land.\n2. A person who acquires a right in land in good faith, for value, and in reliance on the registry shall be protected; the registry shall be conclusive in favor of such an acquirer.\n3. Entries shall be made without undue delay, and the refusal or unwarranted delay of an entry shall be reviewable before a court.\n\n## Article 16.3.3 — Good-Faith Acquisition of Movables\n\n1. A person who acquires a movable in good faith, for value, from a person without power of disposal acquires ownership, subject to the exceptions provided by law, including for stolen objects.\n2. Protection shall not extend to an acquirer who knew or, through gross negligence, could not have been unaware of the lack of power of disposal.\n\n## Article 16.3.4 — Public-Law Limits\n\n1. Expropriation and restrictions on use are governed by Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation) and Article 1.8.3 (Restrictions on Use).\n2. Planning, environmental, and heritage duties attach to ownership as provided by law, and compensation follows the standards of Article 1.8.4 (Compensation and Review).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The registry rule adopts the conclusiveness principle known from the German Grundbuch and from the Torrens systems of land registration: the register, not the chain of old deeds, is the source of certainty, and the good-faith acquirer for value is protected so that commerce in land can proceed on public faith in the record. Movables follow the opposite and older rule that no one can transfer a better title than one has, softened by the good-faith-purchase exception and the stolen-goods carve-out that most European codes retain. Public-law limits are imported by cross-reference rather than restated, keeping private and constitutional property law aligned.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation); Article 1.8.3 (Restrictions on Use); Article 1.8.4 (Compensation and Review).\n"}, {"id": "16-civil-law__torts", "path": "16-civil-law/torts.md", "part": "16-civil-law", "partTitle": "Civil Law", "title": "Section 16.2 — Torts", "md": "# Section 16.2 — Torts\n\n> **Part 16 — Civil Law** · Articles 16.2.1 – 16.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section allocates the losses that private conduct causes: fault liability, strict and vicarious liability for dangerous activities and defective products, protection of personality rights, and the principles of damages.*\n\n## Article 16.2.1 — Fault Liability\n\n1. A person who by intent or negligence causes unlawful damage to another shall make it good.\n2. Fault shall be assessed according to the care that could reasonably be required in the circumstances, and liability may be apportioned according to the contribution of each party.\n\n## Article 16.2.2 — Strict and Vicarious Liability\n\n1. A person who operates a dangerous activity, including dangerous structures, animals, emissions, and hazardous substances, shall be liable for the resulting damage even without fault, as provided by law.\n2. An employer or principal shall be liable for damage caused by a person acting in the service of, or under the direction of, that employer or principal.\n3. The law shall give persons injured by defective products an effective claim independent of contract.\n\n## Article 16.2.3 — Personality Rights\n\n1. Unlawful interference with life, health, dignity, honor, privacy, or image entitles the person affected to injunctive relief and to damages.\n2. Remedies shall be applied with regard to the freedom of expression, consistent with Article 1.3.4 (Prohibition of Prior Restraint).\n\n## Article 16.2.4 — Principles of Damages\n\n1. Damages shall restore the injured person so far as money can, covering the loss suffered and the gain prevented.\n2. Non-pecuniary loss may be compensated where its gravity warrants.\n3. Conduct contributing to the loss on the side of the injured person shall reduce the award, and reasonable mitigation of loss shall be expected.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A single fault rule keeps the general law simple, while express strict liability for dangerous activities — the modern descendant of the common-law case of Rylands v. Fletcher — carries the losses that enterprise imposes on those who never assumed them; the parallel strict liability of environmental law in Article 13.7.3 confirms the same choice in a neighboring field. Vicarious liability is stated as a general rule so victims do not depend on the solvency of the individual employee. Personality rights receive injunctive relief first, because money cannot un-publish a falsehood, while the express deference to expression safeguards keeps reputation remedies from becoming censorship.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.3.4 (Prohibition of Prior Restraint); Article 13.7.3 (Strict Liability); Article 1.14.1 (Safe Goods and Services).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__algorithms-and-transparency", "path": "17-digital-law/algorithms-and-transparency.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.5 — Algorithms and Transparency", "md": "# Section 17.5 — Algorithms and Transparency\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.5.1 – 17.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section opens the public sector's algorithmic decisions to view: a public register of algorithms, a right of explanation, bias testing, audit access, and contestability.*\n\n## Article 17.5.1 — Register of Algorithms\n\n1. Public organs shall enter in a public register every algorithmic system used in decisions or services affecting the rights or interests of persons, stating its purpose, data categories, logic in summary form, human oversight, and evaluation results.\n2. Entries shall be made before deployment and kept updated; narrow security exceptions shall be justified and shall lapse.\n\n## Article 17.5.2 — Right of Explanation\n\n1. A person affected by an algorithmic decision of a public organ may obtain an explanation of the decision's essential factors sufficient to contest it.\n2. The explanation shall be intelligible without technical training and shall be given within a period fixed by law.\n\n## Article 17.5.3 — Bias Testing\n\n1. Public organs shall test algorithmic systems for bias before deployment and periodically thereafter, and shall publish the results.\n2. A system that cannot be operated without unlawful discrimination shall not be used, consistent with Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n\n## Article 17.5.4 — Audit Access\n\n1. The Information Commission and auditors designated by it shall have access to algorithmic systems, their data and documentation, and, where necessary for the audit, their source code, under protections of confidentiality.\n2. Independent researchers may be granted supervised access as provided by law.\n\n## Article 17.5.5 — Procurement and Contestability\n\n1. Public procurement of algorithmic systems shall require compliance with this Section and shall not accept systems whose opacity defeats audit.\n2. A person affected may contest an algorithmic decision before a public organ or a court and may require that the decision be remade by a person.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Public-sector algorithms are singled out because the State does not merely use automation — it imposes it — and the audit trail must therefore be public by default rather than extracted by litigation; the register generalizes the municipal algorithm registers pioneered in cities such as Amsterdam and Helsinki. The explanation right is calibrated to contestability, not reverse engineering: enough to argue, not enough to game. Source-code access stays behind supervised audit because full public disclosure can conflict with security and with the rights of third parties.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 1.6.5 (Impartiality and Automated Decisions); Article 17.2.5 (Supervision).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__artificial-intelligence", "path": "17-digital-law/artificial-intelligence.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.4 — Artificial Intelligence", "md": "# Section 17.4 — Artificial Intelligence\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.4.1 – 17.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs artificial intelligence: risk-based duties, prohibited practices including social scoring and real-time mass biometric identification, human oversight of significant decisions, transparency of public-sector use, and accountability.*\n\n## Article 17.4.1 — Risk-Based Rules\n\n1. The development, placing on the market, and use of artificial-intelligence systems shall be regulated according to the risk they pose to rights, safety, and democratic life.\n2. High-risk systems shall be registered, tested, documented, and placed under effective human oversight before and during deployment, as provided by law.\n\n## Article 17.4.2 — Prohibited Practices\n\n1. Social scoring whereby the State or private actors evaluate the general trustworthiness of persons across unrelated contexts, with detrimental effects detached from the context of the data's origin, is prohibited.\n2. Real-time mass biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces is prohibited save where strictly necessary and authorized by law for a defined purpose, such as the targeted search for a victim or a perpetrator of a serious offense, subject to judicial authorization and independent review.\n3. Systems designed to materially distort behavior causing significant harm, and systems inferring emotions in workplaces and schools, are prohibited as provided by law.\n\n## Article 17.4.3 — Human Oversight\n\n1. A decision that significantly affects a person's rights or obligations shall not be taken solely by an artificial-intelligence system; a person shall review and decide, consistent with Article 1.6.5 (Impartiality and Automated Decisions).\n2. Oversight persons shall be competent and empowered to dissent, and shall not be subject to incentives that make oversight nominal.\n\n## Article 17.4.4 — Transparency of Public Use\n\n1. A public organ that uses artificial intelligence in a service or decision shall inform the persons affected, shall disclose the system's role, its logic in summary form, and the safeguards applied, and shall register the system consistent with Article 17.5.1 (Register of Algorithms).\n\n## Article 17.4.5 — Accountability\n\n1. Liability for harm caused by artificial-intelligence systems follows the general law of Part 16 (Civil Law); the law may ease proof where causation is technically opaque.\n2. Providers and deployers of high-risk systems shall keep logs sufficient to establish accountability after significant incidents.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Section borrows the risk-tiering of the European Union's Artificial Intelligence Act but constitutionalizes only the hard core — prohibitions, oversight, transparency — leaving technical detail to legislation that can keep pace with the technology. Social scoring is banned by its structure, cross-context evaluation with detached detriments, rather than by name, so the rule does not age with the term. Human oversight is made effective by the incentive clause, because a review button that cannot say no is not oversight.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.6.5 (Impartiality and Automated Decisions); Article 17.5.1 (Register of Algorithms); Article 16.2.2 (Strict and Vicarious Liability).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__cybersecurity", "path": "17-digital-law/cybersecurity.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.3 — Cybersecurity", "md": "# Section 17.3 — Cybersecurity\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.3.1 – 17.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes cybersecurity: security-by-design duties, incident reporting, an independent national computer emergency response team, the obligations of critical operators, and the limits that protect encryption and communications.*\n\n## Article 17.3.1 — Security by Design\n\n1. Operators of network and information systems shall take technical and organizational measures proportionate to the risk, by design and by default.\n2. The law shall set minimum duties for providers of essential services and of digital services, graded by risk.\n\n## Article 17.3.2 — Incident Reporting\n\n1. Operators shall report incidents with significant impact to the competent authority without undue delay.\n2. Reports shall be used for the security purpose for which they are given and shall not of themselves expose the operator to liability beyond the express terms of the law.\n\n## Article 17.3.3 — The National CERT\n\n1. An independent national computer emergency response team shall detect, analyze, and coordinate responses to threats and incidents.\n2. The CERT shall cooperate with operators, other authorities, and international counterparts; its powers of intervention in private systems shall be fixed by law and shall be proportionate.\n\n## Article 17.3.4 — Critical Operators\n\n1. Operators of critical infrastructure shall maintain continuity plans, resilience testing, and duties of cooperation, consistent with Section 23.6 (Critical Infrastructure).\n2. The law may combine or separate the supervision of critical infrastructure and of cybersecurity, provided that accountability for each remains identifiable.\n\n## Article 17.3.5 — Limits\n\n1. Nothing in this Section authorizes the weakening of encryption or the general monitoring of communications, consistent with Article 17.1.3 (Device Privacy and Encryption) and Article 1.5.3 (Confidentiality of Communications).\n2. Security duties shall be enforced with regard to the state of the art and to the size of the operator.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Cybersecurity is treated as an infrastructure duty rather than a police power: the CERT coordinates defense, while coercive powers over private systems stay behind statutory authorization and proportionality, in line with the investigative limits already fixed for cybercrime in Article 14.7.4 (Investigative Powers). The safe-harbor logic of incident reporting follows the practice by which regulators coax disclosure out of operators who would otherwise hide breaches. The limits clause repeats the encryption guarantee here because security mandates are where privacy rules are most quietly eroded.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.3 (Confidentiality of Communications); Article 17.1.3 (Device Privacy and Encryption); Article 14.7.4 (Investigative Powers).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__data-protection", "path": "17-digital-law/data-protection.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.2 — Data Protection", "md": "# Section 17.2 — Data Protection\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.2.1 – 17.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the statutory regime of data protection: processing principles, lawful bases and special categories, the rights of data subjects, the duties of controllers, and the dual supervision of the Information Commission.*\n\n## Article 17.2.1 — Principles of Processing\n\n1. Personal data shall be processed lawfully, fairly, and transparently, collected for specified and legitimate purposes, adequate for and limited to what is necessary, accurate, kept no longer than necessary, and secured against loss and unauthorized access.\n2. Processing incompatible with the original purpose is prohibited save where authorized by law for weighty reasons; accountability for compliance lies with the controller.\n\n## Article 17.2.2 — Lawful Bases and Special Categories\n\n1. Processing requires a basis provided by law, including the consent of the data subject, a contract, a legal obligation, or another legitimate interest that does not override the rights of the data subject.\n2. Sensitive data, including data concerning health, genetic and biometric data, and data revealing beliefs, sexuality, or ethnicity, may be processed only under a narrow legal basis carrying heightened safeguards.\n3. The data of children shall be protected specifically, consistent with Article 1.15.1 (Rights of the Child).\n\n## Article 17.2.3 — Rights of Data Subjects\n\n1. Every person has the rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction, and portability, the right to object, and the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing that significantly affects the person, consistent with Article 1.6.5 (Impartiality and Automated Decisions).\n\n## Article 17.2.4 — Duties of Controllers\n\n1. Controllers shall implement data protection by design and by default, keep records of processing, notify personal-data breaches without undue delay to the Information Commission and, where the risk so requires, to data subjects, and assess the impact of high-risk processing before it begins.\n\n## Article 17.2.5 — Supervision\n\n1. The Information Commission shall supervise the protection of personal data and the right of access to information, with the powers, orders, and sanctions provided by law, subject to appeal.\n2. Its status is stated in Part 20 (Independent Institutions); processing by public bodies is subject to the same principles save as expressly provided by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The principles are stated as processing commands rather than aspirations, following the vocabulary that the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation has made the shared grammar of data protection: minimization, purpose limitation, and accountability. Special categories receive a separate lock because their misuse cannot be undone by later correction. Consolidating supervision in the Information Commission, which also polices access to information, economizes scarce expertise but obliges the Commission to balance the two values openly, as its establishing provisions require.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination); Article 1.6.5 (Impartiality and Automated Decisions); Article 17.5.4 (Audit Access).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__digital-identity", "path": "17-digital-law/digital-identity.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.6 — Digital Identity", "md": "# Section 17.6 — Digital Identity\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.6.1 – 17.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section regulates the state digital identity: strictly voluntary use, privacy by design, equivalent channels for non-users, interoperability on open standards, and supervision by the Information Commission.*\n\n## Article 17.6.1 — Voluntary Digital Identity\n\n1. The State may provide a digital identity that enables secure authentication and selective disclosure of attributes.\n2. Use of the digital identity shall be voluntary; no person shall be required to use it in order to exercise a right, to access a service, or to fulfil an obligation.\n\n## Article 17.6.2 — Privacy by Design\n\n1. The system shall minimize data, shall support the disclosure of single attributes without revealing more, and shall not create a central record of a person's transactions; authentication shall be distinguishable from identification.\n2. Biometric templates used for identity shall be stored so as to resist extraction and reuse.\n\n## Article 17.6.3 — No Detriment for Non-Users\n\n1. Public organs shall maintain equivalent non-digital channels; a person who does not use the digital identity shall suffer no delay, cost, or disadvantage.\n2. Private persons shall not be obliged by law to rely exclusively on the state digital identity; competing identity systems may be accredited under open standards.\n\n## Article 17.6.4 — Interoperability\n\n1. The digital identity shall operate on published and open standards and shall be interoperable with accredited private identity systems and, as provided by law, with identity systems of other states.\n\n## Article 17.6.5 — Oversight\n\n1. The Information Commission shall supervise the digital-identity system; its architecture assessments, audits, and incident reports shall be published.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Voluntariness is the load-bearing wall: identity systems drift toward de facto compulsion when offline channels decay, so equivalence for non-users is a duty of the same rank as the system itself. The design clause — selective disclosure, unlinkability, no central transaction log — draws on the privacy engineering of decentralized identity models rather than on any single national design, including that of Estonia. Distinguishable authentication without identification is what keeps an identity credential from becoming a trail of breadcrumbs.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination); Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing); Article 17.1.5 (Remedies).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__digital-rights", "path": "17-digital-law/digital-rights.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.1 — Digital Rights", "md": "# Section 17.1 — Digital Rights\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.1.1 – 17.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section gives statutory effect to the digital rights of Sections 1.5 and 1.13: net neutrality, device privacy, lawful encryption, and protection from generalized surveillance, with remedies before the Information Commission and the courts.*\n\n## Article 17.1.1 — Implementation\n\n1. The Parliament shall implement the digital rights guaranteed by Section 1.13 (Digital Rights) and Section 1.5 (Privacy) by law.\n2. Those rights bind the State and, where capable of application, private persons.\n\n## Article 17.1.2 — Net Neutrality\n\n1. Providers of internet access shall treat all traffic equally, without blocking, throttling, or discrimination by content, application, service, sender, or receiver, save for transparent, proportionate, and non-discriminatory management fixed by law.\n2. Paid prioritization of traffic is prohibited.\n\n## Article 17.1.3 — Device Privacy and Encryption\n\n1. Access to a device or account shall observe the safeguards of Article 1.13.3 (Digital Privacy).\n2. Every person may use encryption; the law shall not prohibit encryption for general use, weaken generally available encryption, or mandate means of circumventing it.\n\n## Article 17.1.4 — No Generalized Surveillance\n\n1. Indiscriminate or bulk surveillance is prohibited save under the strict conditions of Article 1.13.4 (Protection from Unlawful Surveillance).\n2. Mandates to weaken the security of products or services for investigative convenience are prohibited.\n\n## Article 17.1.5 — Remedies\n\n1. A person whose digital rights are violated may complain to the Information Commission and to the courts; remedies shall include cessation, erasure, and compensation.\n2. The Information Commission exercises its mandate under Part 20 (Independent Institutions).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Net neutrality is constitutionalized as a duty of equal treatment for data packets; the layered statutory versions, such as the 2015 European Union regulation, are compressed into one enforceable command with a ban on paid prioritization, because discrimination in access replicates discrimination in speech. The encryption clauses reject mandatory backdoors as a matter of design: security weakened for one purpose is weakened for all, a trade this Part refuses to leave to administrative discretion. Complaints route through the Information Commission so that digital rights have a single, accountable supervisor.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.13.3 (Digital Privacy); Article 1.13.4 (Protection from Unlawful Surveillance); Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__electronic-evidence", "path": "17-digital-law/electronic-evidence.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.8 — Electronic Evidence", "md": "# Section 17.8 — Electronic Evidence\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.8.1 – 17.8.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section sets the standards for electronic evidence in legal proceedings: judicially authorized extraction, authentication and integrity, certified forensic methods, defense access, and preservation.*\n\n## Article 17.8.1 — Extraction Under Authorization\n\n1. Data held in information systems shall be extracted for use in proceedings only under the authorization required by Article 15.2.1 (Warrant Requirement) or under another judicial decision.\n2. Extraction shall be confined to the data categories authorized; incidental data shall be secured and dealt with under Article 15.2.3 (Digital Searches).\n\n## Article 17.8.2 — Authentication and Integrity\n\n1. Electronic evidence is admissible where its authenticity and integrity are established by the means provided by law, including cryptographic hash verification, electronic signatures, and system documentation.\n2. Any alteration, whether accidental or deliberate, shall be disclosed and shall affect the weight and the admissibility of the evidence.\n\n## Article 17.8.3 — Certified Forensics\n\n1. Forensic examination of data shall follow certified methods and shall be performed by qualified persons who record every step of the examination.\n2. Working copies shall be used for analysis; originals shall be preserved under seal.\n\n## Article 17.8.4 — Defense Access\n\n1. The defense shall receive copies of electronic evidence in a usable form and may engage an independent expert to verify the extraction and the analysis, with representation at public expense where required by Article 5.9.3 (Mandatory Criminal Legal Aid).\n\n## Article 17.8.5 — Preservation and Cross-Border Data\n\n1. Expedited preservation of data may be ordered to prevent its loss, consistent with Article 14.7.4 (Investigative Powers).\n2. Data located abroad shall be obtained through the channels provided by law and by treaty.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Digital evidence differs in volatility, not in principle: the chain-of-custody duty of Section 15.4 is met here with hashing and working-copy discipline, which make tampering detectable rather than merely forbidden. The defense-access article exists because extraction is a one-way technical operation in which the defense has no physical presence; a usable copy and an independent expert restore the equality of arms. Cross-border data is routed through treaty channels, following the design of the Budapest Convention on Cybercrime, rather than through unilateral reach.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 15.2.1 (Warrant Requirement); Article 15.2.3 (Digital Searches); Article 14.7.4 (Investigative Powers).\n"}, {"id": "17-digital-law__internet-governance", "path": "17-digital-law/internet-governance.md", "part": "17-digital-law", "partTitle": "Digital Law", "title": "Section 17.7 — Internet Governance", "md": "# Section 17.7 — Internet Governance\n\n> **Part 17 — Digital Law** · Articles 17.7.1 – 17.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section commits the Republic to the open internet: no internet shutdowns save narrowly under the emergency regime, multi-stakeholder participation in governance, content rules confined by law, and digital inclusion.*\n\n## Article 17.7.1 — The Open Internet\n\n1. The Republic shall preserve an open, free, and secure internet as an infrastructure of expression, commerce, education, and participation, consistent with Article 1.13.1 (Access to the Internet).\n2. Measures affecting the network shall be necessary, proportionate, and reviewable.\n\n## Article 17.7.2 — No Internet Shutdowns\n\n1. No organ of the State shall order the disconnection or the degradation of internet access, in whole or in part, for any region, service, or population.\n2. An exception exists only under the emergency regime of Part 26 (Emergency and Crisis), only by a measure strictly required by the exigencies, only for the shortest necessary duration, and only with immediate judicial review; a general or indefinite shutdown shall in no case be ordered.\n\n## Article 17.7.3 — Multi-Stakeholder Participation\n\n1. The State shall develop internet policy with the participation of users, industry, the technical community, civil society, and the Provinces, through open consultation as provided by law.\n\n## Article 17.7.4 — Content Governance\n\n1. Content removal or blocking may be required only by law, only by a specific decision, and only subject to review, consistent with Article 1.13.2 (Online Expression).\n2. A general duty to monitor content may not be imposed on intermediaries.\n\n## Article 17.7.5 — Digital Inclusion\n\n1. The State shall promote affordable access, digital literacy, and the participation of persons with disabilities and of rural and low-income communities in digital life.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Shutdowns are addressed by an absolute rule with a single narrow valve because they are, in practice, collective punishment of the networked public; international human-rights bodies have repeatedly found that the blanket access restrictions governments invoke rarely survive a necessity test. The valve runs through the emergency regime of Part 26 rather than through administrative discretion, and general, indefinite disconnection is excluded by definition. The multi-stakeholder clause imports the working method of internet governance bodies so that policy rests on the parties who run and use the network.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.13.1 (Access to the Internet); Article 1.13.2 (Online Expression); Article 17.1.2 (Net Neutrality).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__armed-forces", "path": "18-national-security/armed-forces.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.2 — The Armed Forces", "md": "# Section 18.2 — The Armed Forces\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.2.1 – 18.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the composition of the armed forces, the constitutional oath, the rights and duties of service members, the strict limits on internal deployment, and military discipline.*\n\n## Article 18.2.1 — Composition and Recruitment\n\n1. The armed forces consist of the land, maritime, air, and such other components as an Act of the Parliament establishes.\n2. Service is voluntary unless compulsory service has been introduced under Article 18.1.4.\n3. Recruitment shall proceed by merit and be open to all who meet uniform and objective requirements fixed by law, without discrimination.\n\n## Article 18.2.2 — Oath to the Constitution\n\n1. Every member of the armed forces shall, upon entry into service, swear or solemnly affirm an oath to respect and defend this Constitution, the laws, and the democratic order of the Republic.\n2. The text of the oath shall be prescribed under Part 30.\n3. Orders contrary to this Constitution, to the laws, or to the rules of international law applicable to armed conflict shall not be obeyed; obedience to a manifestly unlawful order does not excuse the person who obeys it.\n\n## Article 18.2.3 — Rights and Duties of Service Members\n\n1. Service members retain their fundamental rights save for limitations strictly required by military service and consistent with Article 1.1.4.\n2. Service members have the right to vote, to lawful remuneration and rest, and to petition public authorities.\n3. Service members may join professional associations recognized by law; bargaining over operational and command matters is excluded.\n\n## Article 18.2.4 — Internal Deployment\n\n1. The armed forces may be deployed within the territory of the Republic only by or under an Act of the Parliament and only where civilian forces are insufficient to counter an armed threat to public safety or a grave disaster.\n2. Internal deployment:\n   (a) shall be proportionate and temporary;\n   (b) shall remain under civilian command at all times; and\n   (c) shall lapse when the need ends.\n3. Measures affecting individual liberty during internal deployment remain subject to Article 1.7.4.\n\n## Article 18.2.5 — Discipline and Criminal Jurisdiction\n\n1. Disciplinary power shall be exercised under law, with a right to independent review of sanctions.\n2. Serious criminal offenses by service members shall be tried before the ordinary courts; military tribunals, if any are established by law, may hear only minor service-related offenses and must satisfy Article 1.7.1.\n3. The non-derogable core of fair-trial rights under Article 1.7.5 applies to every proceeding affecting a service member.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*An oath addressed to the Constitution, not to a person or party, gives every member a textual ground for refusing unlawful orders. Constitutions differ on whether the military may police at home; this Section permits internal deployment only by statute, in necessity, and under civilian command. Sending serious crimes to the ordinary courts preserves uniform fair-trial standards while minor discipline stays with the chain of command.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 18.1.4 (Conscription and Alternative Service); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 1.7.4 (Habeas Corpus and Liberty).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__border-security", "path": "18-national-security/border-security.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.6 — Border Security", "md": "# Section 18.6 — Border Security\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.6.1 – 18.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section requires lawful and humane border management, entrenches the absolute prohibition of refoulement, and confines border searches and surveillance to proportionate, legally grounded measures.*\n\n## Article 18.6.1 — Lawful and Humane Border Management\n\n1. Border control shall be exercised by designated civilian authorities on the basis of law and with respect for human dignity.\n2. Measures taken at borders shall be suitable, necessary, and proportionate to their declared purpose.\n3. The State shall ensure at the borders the same respect for rights that binds every organ of the Republic within the territory.\n\n## Article 18.6.2 — Absolute Non-Refoulement\n\n1. No person shall be refouled, at a border or elsewhere, to a territory where they face persecution, torture, or other serious irreparable harm.\n2. This prohibition is absolute; no emergency, security interest, or agreement of the Republic shall displace it.\n3. A person who expresses fear of such harm shall have access to the asylum procedure under Article 2.4.1 before any removal, consistent with Article 2.4.2.\n\n## Article 18.6.3 — Proportionate Border Searches\n\n1. Searches of persons, vehicles, goods, and electronic devices at borders require a legal basis and individualized justification; sweeping routine examination of devices is prohibited.\n2. Examination of an electronic device requires judicial authorization save in urgent cases confirmed retrospectively; only data relevant to the lawful purpose may be retained.\n3. Persons in border procedures shall be informed of their rights in a language they understand and shall have access to interpretation.\n\n## Article 18.6.4 — Border Surveillance and Data\n\n1. Border surveillance shall be targeted on lawful objectives; generalized monitoring of persons without individualized suspicion is prohibited.\n2. Data collected at borders shall be used only for lawful border management, retained no longer than necessary, and protected in accordance with Article 1.5.4.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Non-refoulement is stated here without exception because sending a person to torture or persecution destroys the very rights the border is meant to secure, a reading consistent with the absolute character of the underlying prohibitions. Border searches receive a stricter rule for electronic devices, whose contents exceed anything a traveler's bag once held. Channeling all border coercion through civilian authorities and ordinary proportionality review prevents the frontier from becoming a rights-free zone.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 2.4.2 (Non-Refoulement); Article 2.4.1 (Right to Seek and Enjoy Asylum); Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__civilian-control", "path": "18-national-security/civilian-control.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.3 — Civilian Control", "md": "# Section 18.3 — Civilian Control\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.3.1 – 18.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section secures democratic control of the armed forces through a civilian Minister of Defense, command of the President within policy set by the Parliament, and continuous parliamentary scrutiny.*\n\n## Article 18.3.1 — Civilian Minister of Defense\n\n1. The Minister of Defense shall be a civilian.\n2. The Minister of Defense is accountable to the Parliament for the armed forces and the defense administration and shall answer questions and appear before committees under the standing rules. \n3. Serving officers may not hold ministerial office or exercise ministerial authority; the chain of command remains distinct from the government of the day.\n\n## Article 18.3.2 — The President as Commander-in-Chief\n\n1. The President is commander-in-chief of the armed forces.\n2. The President exercises this command within defense policy established by the Parliament and through the Minister of Defense; the modalities of operational command shall be regulated by law.\n3. The command authority of the President does not displace the responsibility of the executive to the Parliament for defense matters.\n\n## Article 18.3.3 — Parliamentary Defense Committee\n\n1. A standing committee of the Parliament shall scrutinize defense policy, budgets, procurement, and deployments. \n2. The committee shall be informed in advance, to the fullest extent consistent with the safety of the Republic, of any intended deployment of the armed forces outside the territory of the Republic.\n3. The committee and its members may access classified information under security procedures fixed by law; secrecy shall not be used to defeat the committee's essential functions.\n\n## Article 18.3.4 — Deployment Abroad\n\n1. The armed forces shall be deployed outside the territory of the Republic only with the prior authorization of the Parliament, given by law or by resolution.\n2. In urgent cases of imminent danger to citizens abroad or of humanitarian rescue, the President may order a temporary deployment; it shall be reported to the Parliament without delay and lapses unless approved within fourteen days.\n3. International agreements governing deployments abroad require approval under Article 19.2.2.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Civilian control is implemented here at three levels: a civilian minister with personal accountability, a head of state whose command is bound by parliamentary policy, and a committee empowered to see what it must in order to judge. The urgent-deployment exception is drawn narrowly and paired with mandatory reporting and rapid sunset, a design used to reconcile rescue operations with the parliamentary war power. Conditioning foreign deployments on approval keeps the decision to use force where the Constitution places the decision to make war.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny); Article 19.2.2 (Parliamentary Approval); Article 18.1.2 (Prohibition of Wars of Aggression).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__defense", "path": "18-national-security/defense.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.1 — Defense", "md": "# Section 18.1 — Defense\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.1.1 – 18.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes defense as a sovereign duty of the Republic, gives the armed forces an exclusively defensive orientation, and places defense law, policy, and resources under constitutional and democratic control.*\n\n## Article 18.1.1 — Purpose of Defense\n\n1. The Republic shall maintain armed forces to defend its sovereignty, territorial integrity, and constitutional order against armed attack.\n2. Defense is a sovereign duty of the Republic and shall be exercised exclusively under this Constitution and law enacted by the Parliament, to which the armed forces remain subordinate at all times.\n\n## Article 18.1.2 — Prohibition of Wars of Aggression\n\n1. The Republic renounces war as an instrument of national policy and shall not wage a war of aggression.\n2. The armed forces may be employed outside the territory of the Republic only in individual or collective self-defense or in operations authorized by the Parliament.\n3. The structure, doctrine, training, and armament of the armed forces shall remain defensive in character.\n\n## Article 18.1.3 — Defense Policy and Resources\n\n1. Defense policy shall be determined by Act of the Parliament and implemented by the executive under civilian direction.\n2. The executive shall present an annual defense report to the Parliament covering readiness, deployments, and expenditure.\n3. Defense expenditure shall be appropriated through the annual budget adopted by the Parliament; no armed force shall be raised, maintained, or deployed except pursuant to appropriation and authorization by law.\n\n## Article 18.1.4 — Conscription and Alternative Service\n\n1. Compulsory military service may be imposed only by or under an Act of the Parliament of general application.\n2. A person who refuses armed service on grounds of conscience or religion shall have the right to alternative service of equivalent duration and comparable burden, of a civilian or non-combatant character.\n3. Alternative service:\n   (a) shall not be designed or administered as punishment; and\n   (b) shall carry no civil or criminal disability beyond its performance.\n\n## Article 18.1.5 — Support to Civil Authorities\n\n1. The armed forces may assist civil authorities in natural disasters and other grave crises when civilian means are insufficient.\n2. Such assistance requires the request of the competent civilian authority, remains under civilian direction at all times, and lasts only as long as the need persists.\n3. Assistance shall not include policing tasks unless a state of emergency has been declared under Part 26 and an Act so provides.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The renunciation of wars of aggression follows constitutions that entrench a defensive orientation as supreme law, while expressly preserving the inherent right of individual and collective self-defense. Making defense policy, money, and doctrine matters for the Parliament converts symbolic civilian primacy into enforceable control. The alternative-service guarantee protects conscientious objection without allowing blanket exemption from civic duty.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.1.1 (Legislative Power); Article 1.4.1 (Freedom of Conscience and Religion); Article 18.3.1 (Civilian Minister of Defense).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__intelligence-services", "path": "18-national-security/intelligence-services.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.4 — Intelligence Services", "md": "# Section 18.4 — Intelligence Services\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.4.1 – 18.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section defines the legal basis of intelligence services, subjects intrusive collection to judicial warrants, prohibits political surveillance, and guarantees remedies for victims.*\n\n## Article 18.4.1 — Mandates Defined by Law\n\n1. Intelligence services may be established and may exercise powers only on the basis of an Act of the Parliament that defines their mandate, powers, and limits.\n2. Each service shall have a single statutory purpose, such as foreign intelligence or domestic security, stated with precision.\n3. Intelligence services shall not exercise police powers of arrest, search of persons, or prosecution.\n\n## Article 18.4.2 — Judicial Warrants for Intrusive Collection\n\n1. Intrusive collection, including interception of communications and surveillance of persons, premises, or devices, requires prior judicial authorization upon a showing of necessity and proportionality, consistent with Article 1.5.5.\n2. Authorization shall specify the person, place, means, and duration and may be renewed only on a fresh showing.\n3. Collection without prior authorization is permitted only where life or grave public safety is at immediate risk; it shall be submitted without delay for retrospective judicial confirmation, and data not confirmed shall be deleted.\n\n## Article 18.4.3 — No Political Surveillance\n\n1. Intelligence services shall not collect information on persons or organizations because of their political opinions, religious beliefs, lawful association, or lawful journalism.\n2. Collection concerning a lawful organization is permissible only on individualized suspicion of an offense within the statutory mandate and shall be reported to the oversight committee established under Section 18.5.\n\n## Article 18.4.4 — Remedies for Victims\n\n1. A person who believes an intelligence service has acted unlawfully toward them may complain to the Inspector-General under Article 18.5.2 and to the courts.\n2. Courts shall afford an effective remedy, including suppression of unlawfully obtained information and just compensation.\n3. Where full disclosure of evidence would endanger the safety of the Republic, a court may use a summary procedure; the substance of the complaint shall nonetheless be independently tested.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Defining each service by a single statutory purpose counters mandate creep, the historical engine of domestic spying. Requiring judicial warrants before intrusive collection mirrors the constitutional standard for intrusions on communications and prevents oversight from becoming self-review. The prohibition of political surveillance protects the associational and expressive life that Article 1.3 and Article 1.4 guarantee. Summary procedures preserve both secrecy and fairness: the State may withhold evidence, but never the judge.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.5 (Judicial Authorization of Intrusions); Article 1.5.3 (Confidentiality of Communications); Article 18.5.2 (Inspector-General for Security and Intelligence).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__national-security-oversight", "path": "18-national-security/national-security-oversight.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.5 — National Security Oversight", "md": "# Section 18.5 — National Security Oversight\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.5.1 – 18.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section creates the machinery of oversight for security and intelligence bodies: a cleared parliamentary committee, an independent Inspector-General, annual reporting, and a protected disclosure channel.*\n\n## Article 18.5.1 — Parliamentary Oversight Committee\n\n1. A special committee of the Parliament shall oversee the intelligence services and other bodies exercising national security functions.\n2. The committee shall receive reports, examine activities, and may require the attendance of and answers from the heads of services.\n3. Members shall hold security clearance under law; the committee shall publish its reports with only narrowly confined redactions for genuinely sensitive material.\n\n## Article 18.5.2 — Inspector-General for Security and Intelligence\n\n1. An independent Inspector-General shall audit compliance of security and intelligence bodies with law, investigate complaints, and inspect operations.\n2. The Inspector-General is appointed in the manner provided by law for a single term and may be removed only for cause by a qualified majority of the Parliament.\n3. The Inspector-General may access any information, document, or premises of the services; obstruction is sanctioned by law.\n\n## Article 18.5.3 — Annual Reporting\n\n1. Each service shall submit an annual report on its activities to the committee and to the Inspector-General.\n2. A public version of each report shall be laid before the Parliament each year, with omissions limited to what the committee approves as genuinely sensitive.\n\n## Article 18.5.4 — Protected Disclosure Channel\n\n1. A secure channel shall receive disclosures from staff and contractors of security and intelligence bodies concerning serious unlawful conduct, with protection against retaliation consistent with the whistleblower regime under Part 27.\n2. Disclosures shall be examined by the Inspector-General; knowingly false or malicious disclosures receive no protection.\n3. No person shall be penalized for a disclosure made in good faith through the channel established under this Article.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Secrecy is legitimate in security work, but institutions clothed in secrecy must be answerable to institutions empowered to look. Pairing a cleared parliamentary committee with a full-time Inspector-General combines political accountability with expert, continuous audit, a combination favored in several mature oversight systems. Annual public reporting disciplines services without publishing what endangers them. The protected channel addresses the reality that the gravest abuses are most often revealed from within.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 18.5.2 (Inspector-General for Security and Intelligence); Article 18.4.4 (Remedies for Victims); Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny).\n"}, {"id": "18-national-security__terrorism-law", "path": "18-national-security/terrorism-law.md", "part": "18-national-security", "partTitle": "National Security", "title": "Section 18.7 — Terrorism Law", "md": "# Section 18.7 — Terrorism Law\n\n> **Part 18 — National Security and Defense** · Articles 18.7.1 – 18.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section disciplines counter-terrorism: narrow offense definitions, unchanged ordinary evidence and procedure, an absolute ban on coercive interrogation, reviewable administrative measures, and sunset clauses.*\n\n## Article 18.7.1 — Narrow Definitions\n\n1. Terrorism offenses shall be defined by Act with precision and limited to serious acts intended to cause death, serious bodily injury, grave destruction of property, or grave disruption of essential services, committed with the aim of intimidating the public or compelling a public authority.\n2. An offense shall not be established by the political purpose of the alleged offender alone; advocacy, protest, dissent, industrial action, and the ordinary work of journalists shall not constitute terrorism offenses, consistent with Article 14.9.2.\n\n## Article 18.7.2 — Ordinary Evidence and Procedure\n\n1. The ordinary rules of evidence and criminal procedure apply in full to the investigation and prosecution of terrorism offenses; special tribunals are prohibited.\n2. Evidence derived from torture or from cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment shall be excluded absolutely, whatever its apparent reliability.\n3. Pre-trial detention in terrorism cases shall not exceed the ordinary limits and remains subject to Article 1.7.4.\n\n## Article 18.7.3 — No Torture or Degrading Interrogation\n\n1. No person shall be subjected to torture or to cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment in the investigation of terrorism, in whatever circumstances and by whatever authority.\n2. Interrogation shall be recorded throughout; coercion, threats, and inducements are prohibited and taint every consequence derived from them.\n\n## Article 18.7.4 — Administrative Measures and Listing\n\n1. Preventive administrative measures, including designation and listing of persons or organizations, require a statutory basis, notice, and an effective opportunity to contest the measure before a court, consistent with Article 1.6.4.\n2. Secrecy shall not deprive a person of the substance of a fair hearing; a court may use a summary procedure to protect genuinely sensitive evidence.\n\n## Article 18.7.5 — Sunsets and Review\n\n1. Special measures departing from ordinary law shall be adopted for a defined period and renewed only by the Parliament after an evaluation of their necessity and proportionality.\n2. A public review of the impact of special measures on rights and on security shall be conducted at the intervals fixed by law, and its findings shall be laid before the Parliament.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Terrorism statutes fail most often through vagueness, and this Section responds by importing the narrow definitions discipline of ordinary criminal law. Keeping ordinary evidence and procedure intact rejects the recurrent claim that security justifies separate courts or lowered standards; the absolute exclusion of torture-derived evidence guards the integrity of verdicts as much as the dignity of suspects. Sunset and review clauses acknowledge that exceptional powers, once enacted, rarely repeal themselves.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 14.9.1 (Narrow Definitions); Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial); Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights).\n"}, {"id": "19-international-relations__diplomatic-relations", "path": "19-international-relations/diplomatic-relations.md", "part": "19-international-relations", "partTitle": "International Relations", "title": "Section 19.4 — Diplomatic Relations", "md": "# Section 19.4 — Diplomatic Relations\n\n> **Part 19 — International Relations** · Articles 19.4.1 – 19.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the Republic's diplomatic and consular relations, the protection of citizens abroad, and the status of international organizations to which the Republic belongs.*\n\n## Article 19.4.1 — Missions and Privileges\n\n1. The Republic shall establish and maintain diplomatic missions and consular posts in the manner provided by law.\n2. The privileges and immunities of missions, of their personnel, and of international organizations recognized by the Republic apply as provided by treaty and by law, subject to reciprocity.\n3. Missions shall be staffed and resourced so as to provide the protection and services required by Articles 19.4.2 and 19.4.3.\n\n## Article 19.4.2 — Protection of Citizens Abroad\n\n1. Every citizen has the right to the protection and assistance of the missions of the Republic while abroad.\n2. A citizen detained abroad shall be informed without delay of the right to have the detention notified to a consular officer of the Republic and to communicate with that officer.\n3. The Republic shall maintain the consular capacity necessary to make this protection effective.\n\n## Article 19.4.3 — Consular Services\n\n1. Missions shall provide consular services, including emergency assistance, documentation, and notification of next of kin.\n2. Emergency consular assistance shall be accessible to citizens regardless of means; fees, where charged, shall be reasonable and uniform.\n\n## Article 19.4.4 — International Organizations\n\n1. The Republic may join international organizations only by treaty approved under Article 19.2.2.\n2. Organizations to which the Republic belongs shall act within their mandates; the constitutional functions of the organs of the Republic are unaffected by membership.\n3. Members of staff of the Republic seconded to international organizations retain their rights as citizens and servants of the Republic as provided by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Diplomatic law is largely codified by treaty, so this Section fixes constitutional anchors rather than restating rules: reciprocity, statutory regulation of privileges, and a personal right of citizens to consular protection, which some constitutions treat as a genuine individual right. The detention-notification rule reflects the practical reality that detained citizens abroad are powerless without prompt contact. The membership clause preserves constitutional functions against erosion by institutional loyalty to international bodies.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 19.2.2 (Parliamentary Approval); Article 2.1.1 (Citizenship of the Republic); Article 19.1.4 (Good-Faith Fulfillment of Obligations).\n"}, {"id": "19-international-relations__extradition", "path": "19-international-relations/extradition.md", "part": "19-international-relations", "partTitle": "International Relations", "title": "Section 19.6 — Extradition", "md": "# Section 19.6 — Extradition\n\n> **Part 19 — International Relations** · Articles 19.6.1 – 19.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states the conditions of extradition, entrenches mandatory bars for political offenses and serious rights risks, and protects citizens and fair procedure.*\n\n## Article 19.6.1 — Requirements of Extradition\n\n1. Extradition may be granted only on the basis of a treaty or of law, for an act that is criminal under the law of the Republic and of the requesting State, and only upon the decision of a court after the person has had an opportunity to be heard.\n2. The executive shall decide on surrender only in conformity with the judicial decision.\n\n## Article 19.6.2 — Mandatory Bars\n\n1. Extradition shall be refused where the offense is a political offense, or where the request seeks to prosecute or punish a person on account of political opinion, religion, nationality, language, or membership of a group, consistent with Article 1.2.2.\n2. Extradition shall be refused where there is a real risk of the death penalty or of torture or other cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment; this bar does not depend on the receipt of assurances.\n3. Extradition shall be refused where it would be incompatible with any other fundamental right of the person under this Constitution.\n\n## Article 19.6.3 — Citizens\n\n1. A citizen of the Republic shall not be extradited save under a treaty providing for reciprocity that guarantees a fair trial before an independent court, consistent with Article 1.7.1.\n2. No citizen shall be deported, expelled, or banished from the territory of the Republic.\n\n## Article 19.6.4 — Procedure and Detention\n\n1. A person whose extradition is sought shall have access to counsel, to interpretation, and to an effective remedy against every adverse decision.\n2. Detention pending extradition shall be for the shortest time necessary, subject to periodic judicial review under Article 1.7.4, and shall not be punitive.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Extradition is surrender to a foreign system of justice, so the safeguards here are procedural and substantive at once: judicial decision, dual criminality, and the classic political-offense bar, which has narrowed in modern practice but survives for persecution dressed as prosecution. The death-penalty and torture bars are made absolute rather than assurance-based because some risks are not cured by diplomatic promises, a position reflected in leading international judgments on surrender. The citizen clause balances the traditional no-extradition rule with treaty reciprocity, always under fair-trial guarantees.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.7.1 (Right to a Fair Trial); Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 1.7.4 (Habeas Corpus and Liberty).\n"}, {"id": "19-international-relations__foreign-policy", "path": "19-international-relations/foreign-policy.md", "part": "19-international-relations", "partTitle": "International Relations", "title": "Section 19.1 — Foreign Policy", "md": "# Section 19.1 — Foreign Policy\n\n> **Part 19 — International Relations** · Articles 19.1.1 – 19.1.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section sets the purposes of the Republic's foreign policy, commits it to development cooperation, and subjects the conduct of foreign relations to democratic accountability.*\n\n## Article 19.1.1 — Purposes of Foreign Policy\n\n1. Foreign policy shall serve peace, the security of the Republic, respect for human rights, and fidelity to international law.\n2. The Republic shall settle its international disputes by peaceful means and may support collective security arrangements consistent with this Constitution.\n3. The Republic shall work for the reduction and control of armaments by international agreement.\n\n## Article 19.1.2 — Development Cooperation\n\n1. The Republic shall conduct development cooperation to reduce poverty and to strengthen human rights, good governance, and sustainability.\n2. Cooperation programs shall be transparent, conducted with respect for the priorities of partner peoples, and evaluated for effectiveness.\n3. Partner countries and programs shall be selected on published criteria and reported to the Parliament.\n\n## Article 19.1.3 — Democratic Accountability of Foreign Policy\n\n1. The executive shall keep the Parliament currently informed of the substance and development of foreign policy.\n2. Commitments of major political or financial significance shall be debated in the Parliament before they bind the Republic.\n3. Secrecy in foreign affairs is exceptional, shall be justified in the manner provided by law, and shall be limited in time.\n\n## Article 19.1.4 — Good-Faith Fulfillment of Obligations\n\n1. The Republic shall fulfill its international obligations in good faith.\n2. Every organ of the State shall act consistently with the international responsibilities of the Republic and shall support the peaceful cooperation of peoples.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Purpose clauses of this kind draw on constitutions that place peace and human rights at the head of foreign policy; they are normative enough to be invoked in interpretation without dictating individual diplomatic decisions. The accountability clauses respond to the classic problem that foreign relations are conducted by a small executive out of public view, by requiring information and debate before binding commitments. The good-faith clause makes pacta sunt servanda an internal constitutional duty of every organ, not merely an external obligation of the State.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.7.4 (Documents to Parliament); Article 18.1.2 (Prohibition of Wars of Aggression); Article 19.2.3 (Publication as Condition of Application).\n"}, {"id": "19-international-relations__international-law", "path": "19-international-relations/international-law.md", "part": "19-international-relations", "partTitle": "International Relations", "title": "Section 19.3 — International Law", "md": "# Section 19.3 — International Law\n\n> **Part 19 — International Relations** · Articles 19.3.1 – 19.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section integrates international law into the legal order of the Republic: generally recognized rules bind, peremptory norms are supreme, and all law is interpreted consistently with the Republic's obligations.*\n\n## Article 19.3.1 — Binding Force of International Law\n\n1. The generally recognized rules and principles of international law form part of the law of the Republic.\n2. They prevail over conflicting ordinary legislation, subject to this Constitution.\n\n## Article 19.3.2 — Primacy of Peremptory Norms\n\n1. The peremptory norms of general international law (jus cogens) are supreme within the legal order of the Republic.\n2. No treaty, custom, agreement, or provision of domestic law may derogate from a peremptory norm; conflicting acts are void.\n3. Such norms include, without limitation, the prohibitions of aggression, genocide, slavery, racial discrimination, apartheid, and torture, and the fundamental rules of international humanitarian law.\n\n## Article 19.3.3 — Consistent Interpretation\n\n1. Courts and every public authority shall interpret and apply the law of the Republic consistently with the international obligations of the Republic.\n2. This Article applies subject to the supremacy of this Constitution.\n3. Judgments and decisions of international courts binding upon the Republic shall be given effect by the competent organs.\n\n## Article 19.3.4 — Implementation of Obligations\n\n1. The Parliament shall enact the legislation necessary to implement binding obligations of the Republic within a reasonable time.\n2. The executive shall report periodically to the Parliament on compliance with judgments and decisions of international courts and supervisory bodies.\n3. The Republic shall support the peaceful settlement of disputes and the progressive development and codification of international law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The clause structure follows the widely adopted monist opening: customary international law enters the domestic order directly and outranks statutes, while the Constitution remains supreme. Declaring jus cogens supreme, as the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties does internationally, prevents any organ of the Republic from lawfully committing the gravest violations even by treaty. The consistent-interpretation duty lets courts harmonize statutes with obligations short of invalidation, and the implementation clause supplies the parliamentary follow-through that monism still requires for treaty law.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 19.2.3 (Publication as Condition of Application); Article 0.8.1 (Supreme Law); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts).\n"}, {"id": "19-international-relations__international-trade", "path": "19-international-relations/international-trade.md", "part": "19-international-relations", "partTitle": "International Relations", "title": "Section 19.5 — International Trade", "md": "# Section 19.5 — International Trade\n\n> **Part 19 — International Relations** · Articles 19.5.1 – 19.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section subjects trade policy to parliamentary law, requires sustainability and labor chapters in trade agreements, and preserves domestic remedies alongside the Republic's trade obligations.*\n\n## Article 19.5.1 — Trade Policy by Law\n\n1. Trade policy, including tariffs and measures affecting external trade, shall be based on an Act of the Parliament, consistent with Article 9.10.2.\n2. Trade policy shall serve prosperity while upholding the labor, environmental, and consumer standards of the Republic.\n3. Trade measures shall not discriminate among persons or goods except as the law of the Republic and its international obligations provide.\n\n## Article 19.5.2 — Sustainability and Labor Chapters\n\n1. Trade agreements of the Republic shall include chapters on environmental protection and labor rights whose commitments are no lower than the corresponding standards of the law of the Republic.\n2. No trade agreement shall require the Republic to reduce protections below the level of its law at the time of signature.\n3. The executive shall assess the social and environmental impact of a draft agreement and lay the assessment before the Parliament before approval.\n\n## Article 19.5.3 — Parliamentary Oversight of Negotiations\n\n1. The executive shall present a negotiating mandate for a significant trade agreement to the Parliament before negotiations begin.\n2. The Parliament shall receive progress reports and the text of a draft agreement sufficiently before signature and approval to permit informed deliberation.\n\n## Article 19.5.4 — Remedies and Trade Defense\n\n1. Domestic remedies, including judicial review of acts implementing trade agreements, remain available to every person, consistent with Article 4.6.5.\n2. Trade defense measures may be adopted under law and only in conformity with the obligations of the Republic, consistent with Article 9.10.5.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Trade agreements function as law for exporters, workers, and consumers, which is why this Section routes tariffs and mandates through Parliament and requires texts to arrive early enough for real scrutiny. Sustainability and labor chapters respond to the criticism that trade liberalization can outrun social protection; the no-regression clause is a floor, not a ceiling. Preserving domestic remedies ensures that international economic commitments never become a shield against review of the acts that implement them.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.10.3 (Trade Agreements); Article 9.10.2 (Tariffs by Law); Article 4.6.5 (Judicial Review of Executive Acts).\n"}, {"id": "19-international-relations__treaties", "path": "19-international-relations/treaties.md", "part": "19-international-relations", "partTitle": "International Relations", "title": "Section 19.2 — Treaties", "md": "# Section 19.2 — Treaties\n\n> **Part 19 — International Relations** · Articles 19.2.1 – 19.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section allocates treaty powers between the executive and the Parliament, conditions domestic application on publication, and involves the Parliament in withdrawal.*\n\n## Article 19.2.1 — Negotiation and Signature\n\n1. The executive negotiates and signs treaties on behalf of the Republic.\n2. Before signature of a treaty within a category stated in Article 19.2.2, the executive shall inform the foreign affairs committee of the Parliament of the text and of its intended effect.\n\n## Article 19.2.2 — Parliamentary Approval\n\n1. Treaties that regulate the political relations of the Republic, relate to matters of legislation, impose significant financial obligations, affect the territory of the Republic, concern human rights, or transfer sovereign powers require the approval of the Parliament expressed in an Act before ratification.\n2. Approval may be given subject to reservations, declarations, or conditions; ratification may proceed only in conformity with any condition of approval.\n3. Agreements excluded from this Article shall not circumvent it by fragmenting a significant commitment into smaller instruments.\n\n## Article 19.2.3 — Publication as Condition of Application\n\n1. A treaty binds the Republic internationally upon ratification in accordance with international law.\n2. A treaty shall be applied domestically only after its publication in the official gazette.\n3. No treaty of the Republic shall be secret; negotiated texts shall be made public, save for narrowly confined and time-limited annexes approved by the Parliament.\n\n## Article 19.2.4 — Withdrawal and Denunciation\n\n1. Withdrawal from, or denunciation of, a treaty within a category stated in Article 19.2.2 requires the approval of the Parliament.\n2. Where urgent necessity makes immediate withdrawal unavoidable, the executive shall report to the Parliament without delay and shall fulfill the steps required by international law.\n3. Domestic provisions implementing a treaty remain effective only so long as the Republic remains bound and shall be amended conformingly after withdrawal.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Treaty practice worldwide divides between systems requiring approval for broad categories and systems reserving it for select subjects; this Section takes the categorical approach because treaties quietly remake domestic law. The publication rule turns the maxim that secret treaties are enemies of democracy into a condition of enforceability, echoing post-war constitutional settlements. Involving the Parliament in withdrawal closes the loophole by which a state could achieve by exit what it could not achieve by entry.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 19.2.2 (Parliamentary Approval); Article 9.10.3 (Trade Agreements); Article 19.3.2 (Primacy of Peremptory Norms).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__anti-corruption-commission", "path": "20-independent-institutions/anti-corruption-commission.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.3 — The Anti-Corruption Commission", "md": "# Section 20.3 — The Anti-Corruption Commission\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.3.1 – 20.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Anti-Corruption Commission to prevent and investigate corruption offenses, to prosecute jointly with the Public Prosecution Service, and to operate under strict independence guarantees.*\n\n## Article 20.3.1 — Mandate\n\n1. The Anti-Corruption Commission shall prevent and investigate corruption offenses, including bribery, trading in influence, embezzlement, and illicit enrichment, consistent with the criminal law of the Republic.\n2. The Commission shall conduct prevention through education, risk assessment, and advice on integrity systems within public bodies.\n\n## Article 20.3.2 — Joint Prosecution\n\n1. Prosecution of corruption offenses shall be conducted jointly by the Commission and the Public Prosecution Service under the direction of the Public Prosecutor, consistent with Article 5.8.2.\n2. The decision whether to prosecute shall follow the guidelines applicable to the Public Prosecution Service; no organ of the executive may direct it.\n\n## Article 20.3.3 — Independence Guarantees\n\n1. The Commission shall be independent in the exercise of its functions; no organ may direct or impede its investigations.\n2. Its head and members serve fixed terms and may be removed only for cause by a qualified majority of the Parliament.\n3. Its funding shall be adequate and may not be reduced in a manner designed to obstruct an investigation.\n\n## Article 20.3.4 — Powers and Safeguards\n\n1. The Commission may access documents, premises, and records, including banking records under judicial authorization.\n2. Intrusive techniques require a judicial warrant on the ordinary standard of necessity and proportionality, consistent with Article 1.5.5.\n3. Persons under investigation retain every procedural right available under the general law of criminal procedure.\n4. The Commission shall publish an annual report on corruption trends and on the state of prevention within public bodies.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Anti-corruption bodies succeed or fail on independence, so this Section entrenches fixed terms, cause-only removal, and protected funding. Combining investigative capacity with prosecution conducted jointly under the Public Prosecutor preserves a single chain of prosecutorial responsibility, a design chosen over fully autonomous prosecution powers to avoid fragmenting Article 5.8. Judicial warrants for the most intrusive powers keep the Commission's arsenal within the constitutional limits that bind every investigator.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.8.5 (Relationship to Police and the Anti-Corruption Commission); Article 14.6.1 (Bribery); Article 5.8.2 (The Public Prosecutor).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__auditor-general", "path": "20-independent-institutions/auditor-general.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.5 — The Office of the Auditor-General", "md": "# Section 20.5 — The Office of the Auditor-General\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.5.1 – 20.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Office of the Auditor-General as an independent supreme audit institution, defines the Auditor-General's single term and supermajority appointment, and secures operational and budgetary independence.*\n\n## Article 20.5.1 — Status and Appointment\n\n1. The Office of the Auditor-General is the independent supreme audit institution of the Republic, established under Article 0.5.4.\n2. The Auditor-General is appointed by the Parliament by a supermajority as fixed by law, for a single term, and may not be reappointed.\n3. The Auditor-General may be removed before the end of the term only for cause, by the same supermajority, and subject to judicial review.\n\n## Article 20.5.2 — Operational and Budgetary Independence\n\n1. The Office determines its audit program, methods, and staffing independently.\n2. The Office's budget proposal shall be transmitted to the Parliament without alteration by the executive and shall not be reduced below the level necessary to perform its functions.\n3. Neither the executive nor any public body may direct or restrict an audit.\n4. Staff of the Office act independently and take instructions only from within the Office.\n\n## Article 20.5.3 — Access and Powers\n\n1. The Office shall audit all public revenue, expenditure, assets, and liabilities, including bodies in which the State holds a controlling interest.\n2. The Office has full access to the records, premises, and information of audited entities; obstruction shall be sanctioned by law.\n\n## Article 20.5.4 — Reporting and Follow-Up\n\n1. The Office shall report its findings to the Parliament and shall publish its reports, consistent with Article 4.7.4.\n2. Audited entities shall respond to findings and implement follow-up within reasonable times; the Office shall track implementation and report on it annually.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A single non-renewable term frees the Auditor-General from cultivating reappointment, the same insulation given to constitutional court judges; the supermajority appointment ensures the choice is not a government patronage prize. The budget rule removes the classic chokehold of executive control over audit funding. Publication and a tracked follow-up duty matter as much as audit power itself, because findings that are never answered rarely change how public money is spent.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions); Article 4.7.4 (Documents to Parliament); Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__election-commission", "path": "20-independent-institutions/election-commission.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.4 — The Electoral Commission", "md": "# Section 20.4 — The Electoral Commission\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.4.1 – 20.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section fixes the composition, qualifications, staggered terms, and removal rules of the Electoral Commission, the independent administrator of elections whose constitutional status is set in Part 03.*\n\n## Article 20.4.1 — Composition and Staggered Terms\n\n1. The Electoral Commission is a multi-member collegiate body; the number of members shall be fixed by law at not fewer than five.\n2. Members are appointed for a single term of fixed length, staggered so that not all seats fall vacant at once and continuity is preserved.\n3. The Commission's constitutional status and exclusive administration of national elections and referendums are governed by Article 3.5.1 and Article 3.5.3.\n\n## Article 20.4.2 — Qualifications and Incompatibilities\n\n1. Members shall be chosen for competence and integrity through an open, published process that includes an opportunity for public and parliamentary hearing.\n2. Members may not hold party office, campaign for candidates, or hold any interest in the outcome of an election they administer.\n3. Incompatibilities continue for a cooling-off period after the end of the term, as fixed by law.\n\n## Article 20.4.3 — Removal Only for Cause\n\n1. A member may be removed before the end of the term only for serious cause, namely incapacity, grave misconduct, or conviction of a serious offense, and only by a supermajority of the Parliament as fixed by law.\n2. Removal decisions are subject to review by the courts, consistent with Article 5.6.5.\n3. Suspension pending removal shall be exceptional and shall be justified in writing.\n\n## Article 20.4.4 — Accountability and Cooperation\n\n1. The Commission shall report annually to the Parliament and shall publish its regulations and its reasoned decisions on electoral disputes.\n2. Public authorities shall cooperate with the Commission; interference with its functions shall be sanctioned by law, consistent with Article 3.5.4.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Staggered single terms deny any government the chance to appoint a whole commission at once, which is the structural trick most often used to capture election administration. Cause-only removal by supermajority, open recruitment, and post-term cooling-off rules echo the good-practice standards for electoral management bodies developed by comparative election law. Preserving judicial review of removals ensures the supermajority is a shield for independence, not a weapon against it.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.5.1 (Constitutional Status); Article 3.5.4 (Accountability and Cooperation); Article 5.6.5 (No Ouster of Review).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__human-rights-commission", "path": "20-independent-institutions/human-rights-commission.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.2 — The Human Rights Commission", "md": "# Section 20.2 — The Human Rights Commission\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.2.1 – 20.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Human Rights Commission to monitor, promote, and advise on human rights, with the independence, pluralism, and resources that credible national human rights institutions require.*\n\n## Article 20.2.1 — Mandate\n\n1. The Human Rights Commission shall monitor the observance of human rights, promote awareness and education, and advise public authorities and the Parliament.\n2. Its mandate covers every right guaranteed by this Constitution and every binding human rights obligation of the Republic.\n3. The Commission shall pay particular attention to persons in detention and in other settings where rights are most at risk.\n\n## Article 20.2.2 — Independence and Pluralism\n\n1. The Commission shall be independent of the executive, pluralist in composition, adequately and predictably funded, and transparent in its work.\n2. Members shall be appointed through an open process for fixed terms and may be removed only for cause by a qualified majority of the Parliament.\n\n## Article 20.2.3 — Participation in Proceedings\n\n1. The Commission may submit observations as amicus curiae to any court, including the Constitutional Court, on questions of human rights.\n2. The Commission may assist complainants, refer matters to the competent authorities, and intervene in proceedings where the law so provides.\n\n## Article 20.2.4 — Periodic Reports\n\n1. The Commission shall publish periodic reports on the state of human rights in the Republic at the intervals fixed by law, and shall report annually to the Parliament.\n2. Public authorities shall cooperate with the Commission and shall respond to its recommendations, consistent with Article 1.1.3.\n3. The Commission may require from public bodies the information necessary for its monitoring; a refusal shall be justified in writing.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The independence, pluralist composition, adequate funding, and broad mandate required here restate the standards set for national human rights institutions by the Paris Principles, adopted by the United Nations General Assembly in 1993. An amicus role lets the Commission bring system-wide knowledge into individual cases without displacing the parties or the courts. Periodic public reporting is the institution's chief instrument: it converts scattered monitoring into a published account against which the State can be judged.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.3 (Duties of Organs of State); Article 5.3.4 (Individual Complaints); Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__information-commission", "path": "20-independent-institutions/information-commission.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.7 — The Information Commission", "md": "# Section 20.7 — The Information Commission\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.7.1 – 20.7.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Information Commission as the single supervisor of data protection and of access to information, defines its complaint-handling role, and subjects its orders and fines to judicial appeal.*\n\n## Article 20.7.1 — Mandate\n\n1. The Information Commission shall supervise compliance with data protection law and with the right of access to information held by public bodies.\n2. The Commission shall issue guidance, monitor practice, and promote awareness of both regimes.\n3. Guidance shall be published and may include codes of recommended practice for public and private bodies.\n\n## Article 20.7.2 — Complaint Handling\n\n1. Any person may complain to the Commission of an alleged violation of data protection or of the right of access to information.\n2. The Commission shall examine complaints impartially and within a reasonable time and shall inform the complainant of the outcome with reasons.\n\n## Article 20.7.3 — Orders and Fines\n\n1. The Commission may order compliance, including the correction or deletion of data, the disclosure of information, and the cessation of unlawful processing, and may impose fines as provided by law.\n2. Orders and fines are subject to appeal to a court, consistent with Article 1.6.4 and Article 5.6.1.\n3. The Commission may conduct audits and inspections of public and private bodies within its mandate.\n\n## Article 20.7.4 — Independence and Reporting\n\n1. The Commission is independent; its head serves a fixed term and may be removed only for cause by a supermajority of the Parliament.\n2. The Commission shall report annually to the Parliament, including on systemic weaknesses in transparency and data protection.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Merging data protection and access-to-information oversight in one commission recognizes that the two regimes trade in the same material and are frequently misunderstood in the same breath; several jurisdictions have converged on this model. Because the Commission both orders and enforces, appeal to a court is the indispensable check, keeping an independent regulator inside the rule of law it polices. Annual reporting on systemic weaknesses turns case-by-case supervision into a public account of how open, and how private, the Republic actually is.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts); Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__ombudsman", "path": "20-independent-institutions/ombudsman.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.1 — The Ombudsman", "md": "# Section 20.1 — The Ombudsman\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.1.1 – 20.1.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the Ombudsman as an independent investigator of maladministration, accessible to every person, empowered to investigate on complaint and on own initiative, and reporting to the Parliament.*\n\n## Article 20.1.1 — Mandate\n\n1. The Ombudsman shall investigate maladministration by public authorities, including unlawfulness, delay, error, abuse of power, discrimination, discourtesy, and failure to give reasons.\n2. The Ombudsman acts independently of the executive and answers to the Parliament.\n3. The Ombudsman may propose legislative and administrative reforms arising from investigations.\n\n## Article 20.1.2 — Access and Standing\n\n1. Every person may complain to the Ombudsman without charge and by any suitable means; complaints may be made confidentially.\n2. The Ombudsman may open an investigation on own initiative.\n3. Access to the Ombudsman shall not depend on the exhaustion of other remedies.\n4. The Ombudsman shall be accessible to persons in detention and in other closed settings, including through confidential correspondence.\n\n## Article 20.1.3 — Powers of Investigation\n\n1. The Ombudsman may require public authorities to produce documents and information and may question officials, including under oath.\n2. Classified material shall be reviewed under special procedures that preserve its confidentiality without excluding the Ombudsman.\n3. Obstruction of an investigation shall be sanctioned by law.\n\n## Article 20.1.4 — Recommendations and Reports\n\n1. The Ombudsman may recommend that an authority remedy the maladministration; the authority shall reply within a reasonable time, stating the action taken or the reasons for taking none.\n2. Where no adequate remedy follows, the Ombudsman shall report the matter to the Parliament.\n3. The Ombudsman shall publish an annual report and may publish special reports on systemic failures, consistent with Article 1.6.1.\n4. Reports shall be laid before the Parliament without alteration by the executive.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The Ombudsman descends from the Swedish model of an officer of Parliament who audits administration on behalf of the citizen. Its recommendatory power is deliberate: persuasion backed by publicity and by report to Parliament often corrects administration faster than litigation, and at no cost to the complainant. Own-initiative jurisdiction converts isolated complaints into systemic reform, which is the office's principal public value.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions); Article 1.6.1 (Fair Administrative Action); Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies).\n"}, {"id": "20-independent-institutions__statistics-authority", "path": "20-independent-institutions/statistics-authority.md", "part": "20-independent-institutions", "partTitle": "Independent Institutions", "title": "Section 20.6 — The Statistics Authority", "md": "# Section 20.6 — The Statistics Authority\n\n> **Part 20 — Independent Institutions** · Articles 20.6.1 – 20.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the professional independence of official statistics, sets quality and confidentiality standards, and assigns the Statistics Authority the coordination of state data collection.*\n\n## Article 20.6.1 — Professional Independence\n\n1. The Statistics Authority shall produce official statistics free from political or commercial interference.\n2. Decisions on statistical methods, sources, and the timing of release are made within the Authority alone; the release calendar is public and binding.\n3. The head of the Authority serves a fixed term and may be removed only for cause by a supermajority of the Parliament.\n\n## Article 20.6.2 — Quality and Standards\n\n1. Official statistics shall be relevant, accurate, timely, coherent, and comparable, applying generally accepted statistical standards and classifications.\n2. Methods and definitions shall be documented, published, and applied uniformly over time.\n\n## Article 20.6.3 — Confidentiality of Statistical Data\n\n1. Individual data collected for statistical purposes shall be used exclusively for those purposes and published only in aggregated or otherwise de-identified form.\n2. Identifiable statistical data shall not be transferred to any organ of the State or used for administrative, investigative, or enforcement purposes.\n3. Breaches of confidentiality shall be sanctioned by law, consistent with Article 1.5.4.\n\n## Article 20.6.4 — Coordination of State Data Collection\n\n1. The Authority shall coordinate statistical activities across public bodies to avoid duplication and reduce reporting burdens.\n2. Public bodies shall provide administrative data to the Authority for statistical use under safeguards consistent with Article 20.6.3.\n3. Aggregate official statistics shall be published openly by default, consistent with Article 21.3.2.\n4. The Authority shall report annually to the Parliament on the state of official statistics.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Independent statistics are an unglamorous but load-bearing institution: budgets, elections, and policy evaluation all rest on numbers the public must believe. The binding release calendar prevents the oldest abuse, the convenient delay or leak of bad figures, and reflects the principles of official statistical independence endorsed by the United Nations Statistical Commission. The strict firewall between statistical data and enforcement uses removes the fear that answering a survey becomes evidence against oneself.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.5.4 (Personal Data and Informational Self-Determination); Article 21.3.2 (Open-Data Default); Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions).\n"}, {"id": "21-media-and-information__access-to-information", "path": "21-media-and-information/access-to-information.md", "part": "21-media-and-information", "partTitle": "Media And Information", "title": "Section 21.3 — Access to Information", "md": "# Section 21.3 — Access to Information\n\n> **Part 21 — Media and Information** · Articles 21.3.1 – 21.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states the right of every person to information held by public bodies, makes open data the default posture of the State, and fixes the narrow limits of the right; procedures lie in Part 27.*\n\n## Article 21.3.1 — Right of Access\n\n1. Every person has the right of access to information held by public bodies, on request and without stating reasons, subject only to Article 21.3.3.\n2. Access shall be granted within a reasonable time fixed by law and at no or reasonable cost, consistent with Article 1.3.2.\n\n## Article 21.3.2 — Open-Data Default\n\n1. Public bodies shall publish information proactively in open, machine-readable formats that permit reuse, subject to privacy and security safeguards.\n2. Open publication is the rule; individual requests are the residual channel. Request procedures, timelines, exemptions, and appeals are governed by Part 27.\n\n## Article 21.3.3 — Limits on Access\n\n1. Access to information may be limited only where necessary to protect privacy, public security, or another legitimate aim recognized by law, and only so long as necessary, consistent with Article 1.1.4.\n2. The burden of justifying a refusal lies on the body refusing.\n3. Every refusal shall be reasoned and may be appealed to the Information Commission and thereafter to a court, consistent with Article 20.7.2.\n\n## Article 21.3.4 — Records and Documentation\n\n1. Public bodies shall create, maintain, and document records of their activities in a manner that makes this right effective.\n2. Destruction or concealment of records to defeat access is prohibited and shall be sanctioned by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The right of access turns the abstract promise of an informed citizenry into an individual, enforceable claim, and this Section deliberately pairs it with an open-data default: the best freedom-of-information law is one fewer request because the data was already published. Placing the burden of justification on the refusing authority inverts the practice of regimes where secrecy needs no reason. The records-management duty closes the quiet loophole by which the right exists on paper while the files no longer exist.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.3.2 (Freedom of Information and the Media); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 20.7.2 (Complaint Handling).\n"}, {"id": "21-media-and-information__freedom-of-press", "path": "21-media-and-information/freedom-of-press.md", "part": "21-media-and-information", "partTitle": "Media And Information", "title": "Section 21.1 — Freedom of the Press", "md": "# Section 21.1 — Freedom of the Press\n\n> **Part 21 — Media and Information** · Articles 21.1.1 – 21.1.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees the institutional freedoms of the media: no licensing or censorship, protection of sources, judicial control of newsroom searches, and protection against strategic lawsuits.*\n\n## Article 21.1.1 — No Licensing or Censorship\n\n1. The press and all media are free; censorship is prohibited, and no license or authorization shall be required as a condition of practicing journalism or operating a medium, consistent with Article 1.3.2.\n2. Editorial decisions rest solely with the editorial staff; no organ of the State may direct, suspend, or close a medium save by a final order of a court based on law, consistent with Article 1.3.4.\n\n## Article 21.1.2 — Protection of Sources\n\n1. Journalists and media staff shall not be compelled to disclose information identifying a source.\n2. Disclosure may be ordered only by a court, only where essential to prevent a serious crime or to defend a person against a grave accusation, and only after weighing the public interest in the free flow of information; the least intrusive available means shall be used.\n\n## Article 21.1.3 — Newsrooms and Materials\n\n1. Premises, devices, and materials of media shall not be searched, seized, or copied except for grave offenses, under a judicial order that specifies what may be taken and why.\n2. Material that identifies or is reasonably likely to identify a source may not be seized under any order, consistent with Article 21.1.2.\n\n## Article 21.1.4 — Protection against Strategic Lawsuits\n\n1. A civil action brought principally to silence participation in public debate may be dismissed at an early stage by a court on a showing that it lacks merit or that its purpose is suppression; costs shall normally fall on the claimant.\n2. Courts shall give such motions priority and shall consider the chilling effect of the action on public debate, consistent with Article 1.3.3.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Abolishing licensing resurrects the old constitutional settlement that journalism is a right, not a trade permitted by the State; the ban on prior restraint does the rest. Source protection and newsroom protection are the operational core of press freedom, because reporting that cannot be confidential produces no accountability, a point made forcefully in leading international case law on compelled disclosure. Anti-SLAPP protection addresses the modern replacement of censorship with litigation, where the process itself is the punishment.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.3.2 (Freedom of Information and the Media); Article 1.3.4 (Prohibition of Prior Restraint); Article 1.3.3 (Political Speech).\n"}, {"id": "21-media-and-information__journalist-protection", "path": "21-media-and-information/journalist-protection.md", "part": "21-media-and-information", "partTitle": "Media And Information", "title": "Section 21.5 — Protection of Journalists", "md": "# Section 21.5 — Protection of Journalists\n\n> **Part 21 — Media and Information** · Articles 21.5.1 – 21.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects journalists against violence and intimidation, secures source protection in every proceeding, and shields journalists' digital security against intrusion.*\n\n## Article 21.5.1 — Protection from Violence and Intimidation\n\n1. The State shall protect journalists against violence, threats, harassment, and intimidation connected with their work, consistent with Article 1.3.1.\n2. An offense committed against a journalist because of their journalism shall be treated as an aggravating circumstance.\n3. Investigations of attacks on journalists shall be expeditious, thorough, and independent, and their outcomes shall be made public.\n\n## Article 21.5.2 — Source Protection in Proceedings\n\n1. The protection of sources under Article 21.1.2 applies in every proceeding, civil, criminal, or administrative, before any authority.\n2. An order compelling disclosure may issue only on the strict judicial test of Article 21.1.2, using the least intrusive means.\n\n## Article 21.5.3 — Digital Security of Journalists\n\n1. Devices, accounts, and communications of journalists shall be protected against unlawful intrusion; access to them requires a judicial warrant under the standards of Article 1.5.5, consistent with Article 1.13.3.\n2. Spyware and comparable intrusive tools shall not be deployed against journalists save under a judicial warrant based on individualized suspicion of a grave crime, with prompt notification of the journalist and subsequent review.\n\n## Article 21.5.4 — Access and Safety Measures\n\n1. Accreditation schemes for journalists shall be content-neutral and may not be used to exclude critical voices from public events or places.\n2. Protective measures may be provided to journalists at risk, on their request, under a risk assessment published in general terms.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Journalists are the visible targets when powerful actors prefer intimidation to argument, so the State's protective duty is matched by aggravation of motive-driven offenses and mandatory public reporting of investigations. Restating source protection across all proceedings prevents the end-run of retrieving from a civil or administrative file what a criminal court would not compel. The digital rules answer modern spyware markets, under which the phone in a journalist's pocket is the new stakeout: only a warrant, only suspicion, and never silence.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.13.4 (Protection from Unlawful Surveillance); Article 1.5.5 (Judicial Authorization of Intrusions); Article 21.1.3 (Newsrooms and Materials).\n"}, {"id": "21-media-and-information__media-regulation", "path": "21-media-and-information/media-regulation.md", "part": "21-media-and-information", "partTitle": "Media And Information", "title": "Section 21.4 — Media Regulation", "md": "# Section 21.4 — Media Regulation\n\n> **Part 21 — Media and Information** · Articles 21.4.1 – 21.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section provides an independent media regulator bound by content-neutral rules, safeguards pluralism and ownership transparency, and protects minors without censoring adults.*\n\n## Article 21.4.1 — Independent Regulator\n\n1. An independent media authority shall regulate media services in the manner and within the limits of this Section, consistent with Article 0.5.4.\n2. Its members shall be appointed for fixed, staggered terms through an open process; its decisions shall be reasoned, public, and subject to appeal to a court.\n3. The authority shall cooperate with other regulators and with the Information Commission where media services process personal data.\n\n## Article 21.4.2 — Content-Neutral Rules\n\n1. Regulatory rules shall be general and content-neutral; they shall not impose licensing of journalists, prior restraint, or any penalty for lawful opinion.\n2. No rule shall authorize censorship of lawful content for adult audiences, consistent with Article 1.3.4.\n3. Rules shall apply equally to public and to private media services.\n\n## Article 21.4.3 — Pluralism and Ownership Transparency\n\n1. Ownership and control of media shall be transparent; the regulator shall maintain a public register of beneficial owners.\n2. Concentrations of media ownership that endanger pluralism may be prevented or reversed by law, subject to judicial review.\n\n## Article 21.4.4 — Protection of Minors and Media Literacy\n\n1. Measures protecting minors, such as scheduling rules, labeling, and access controls, shall be proportionate and shall not operate as general censorship, consistent with Article 1.15.4.\n2. The regulator and educational institutions shall promote media literacy among the public.\n3. The effectiveness of protection measures shall be reviewed by the regulator at intervals fixed by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The regulator is given teeth but muzzled from the worst uses of regulation: licensing journalism and prior restraint are expressly excluded because they are the instruments censorship always reaches for first. Ownership transparency and concentration rules answer the fact that private gatekeepers can suppress pluralism as effectively as any censor. Protection of minors is treated as a scheduling and labeling problem rather than a licensing problem, so that the ends are achieved without handing the State an editor's pen.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.3.4 (Prohibition of Prior Restraint); Article 1.15.4 (Protection from Violence and Exploitation); Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions).\n"}, {"id": "21-media-and-information__public-broadcasting", "path": "21-media-and-information/public-broadcasting.md", "part": "21-media-and-information", "partTitle": "Media And Information", "title": "Section 21.2 — Public Broadcasting", "md": "# Section 21.2 — Public Broadcasting\n\n> **Part 21 — Media and Information** · Articles 21.2.1 – 21.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes an independent public broadcaster with a universal service mandate, pluralist governance, editorial freedom, and funding insulated from political interference.*\n\n## Article 21.2.1 — Mandate and Universal Service\n\n1. A public broadcaster shall provide a universal service of news, education, culture, and public affairs, accessible to all persons in the territory of the Republic, including through accessible formats for persons with disabilities.\n2. The universal service shall reach the whole territory of the Republic and be provided free at the point of use.\n\n## Article 21.2.2 — Independent Governance\n\n1. The governing body of the public broadcaster shall be appointed through an open, pluralist process insulated from the executive; terms are fixed and staggered, and members may be removed only for cause by a qualified majority.\n2. Holders of political office and officials of political parties are ineligible to serve on the governing body, consistent with Article 0.5.4.\n\n## Article 21.2.3 — Pluralism and Editorial Independence\n\n1. The public broadcaster shall present news fairly and reflect the diversity of opinion in society.\n2. Neither the State nor any public authority may direct or interfere with programming decisions; election-related allocation of airtime shall follow rules fixed by law.\n3. Programming shall serve the whole public, including linguistic and cultural communities within society.\n\n## Article 21.2.4 — Insulated Funding\n\n1. Funding shall be fixed by a multi-year statutory formula or charge, adequate to the universal service mandate, and may not be altered so as to influence editorial decisions.\n2. The accounts of the public broadcaster shall be audited by the Office of the Auditor-General and published, consistent with Article 20.5.3.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Public broadcasting earns its place in a constitution only if its governance and money are armored against the governments of the day, since a state station that answers to the incumbent is propaganda with a license fee. Staggered terms, cause-only removal, and ineligibility of party officials mirror the design used for the Electoral Commission. The multi-year funding formula, audited and published, is the single most decisive protection: the power to squeeze a budget is the power to schedule the news.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions); Article 20.5.3 (Access and Powers); Article 1.3.2 (Freedom of Information and the Media).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__child-protection", "path": "22-social-policy/child-protection.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.7 — Child Protection", "md": "# Section 22.7 — Child Protection\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.7.1 – 22.7.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes child protection services implementing Sections 1.15 and 28.1, with family support first and removal only as a last resort.*\n\n## Article 22.7.1 — Best Interests and Family Support\n\n1. In all measures under this Section, the best interests of the child shall be paramount, consistently with Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child).\n2. Protection services shall be organized so that families under strain receive support first: parenting assistance, counseling, respite care, material aid, and treatment for illness or addiction.\n3. Support may not be withheld because a family has previously sought help.\n\n## Article 22.7.2 — Reporting and Response\n\n1. Professionals who work with children shall report suspected violence, exploitation, or neglect to the competent protection authority as provided by law.\n2. Reports shall be assessed promptly by qualified staff; information shall be shared among authorities only to the extent necessary for the child's protection.\n3. Where a child is in immediate danger, the authority may order urgent protective measures, which shall be submitted without delay to judicial confirmation.\n\n## Article 22.7.3 — Removal as Last Resort\n\n1. A child shall be separated from the family environment only where the child is subject to serious harm at home and no less intrusive measure can adequately protect the child.\n2. Removal shall be decided or confirmed by a court, shall state its reasons in writing, and shall be limited to the shortest time necessary.\n3. Contact with parents and siblings shall be maintained unless this would harm the child; placement shall, wherever possible, preserve the child's relations, schooling, and language.\n\n## Article 22.7.4 — Review of Placement\n\n1. Every placement outside the family shall be reviewed at defined intervals no longer than six months, with the child heard according to age and maturity under Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children).\n2. A plan for reunification with the family, or, where that is not possible, for a stable alternative placement, shall accompany every removal decision.\n3. Affected persons may contest placement decisions before an independent body.\n\n## Article 22.7.5 — Leaving Care\n\n1. Young persons leaving public care shall receive support for housing, education, and employment until they can sustain an independent life, as provided by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The family-support-first rule reflects the UN Guidelines for the Alternative Care of Children, which treat separation itself as a harm to be weighed. The six-month review ceiling responds to the drift found in many care systems, where placements persist without decisions. Comparatively, removal thresholds differ sharply across jurisdictions; this text fixes the test in general terms and leaves operational calibration to statute, keeping constitutional text out of case-by-case child welfare judgments.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child); Article 1.15.4 (Protection from Violence and Exploitation); Article 16.5.5 (State Support and Protection).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__disability", "path": "22-social-policy/disability.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.3 — Disability and Inclusion", "md": "# Section 22.3 — Disability and Inclusion\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.3.1 – 22.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees equality, reasonable accommodation, and accessibility for persons with disabilities, and supports their full participation in society.*\n\n## Article 22.3.1 — Equality and Inclusion\n\n1. Persons with disabilities are equal before the law and are entitled to inclusion in all areas of life without discrimination.\n2. The State shall remove barriers that hinder participation in employment, education, culture, sport, and public life.\n3. Affirmative measures to advance equality of persons with disabilities are permitted and shall be reviewed for effectiveness.\n\n## Article 22.3.2 — Reasonable Accommodation\n\n1. Public authorities shall provide reasonable accommodation so that persons with disabilities can exercise their rights on an equal basis with others.\n2. Private actors shall provide reasonable accommodation where this is capable of application under the general law, and denial of reasonable accommodation shall constitute discrimination.\n3. Accommodation is not required where it would impose a disproportionate burden, and the burden of demonstrating disproportionality lies with the person refusing it.\n\n## Article 22.3.3 — Accessibility and Universal Design\n\n1. The Parliament shall by law set accessibility standards for buildings, public spaces, transport, information, and communication services.\n2. Goods and services offered to the public, including digital services, shall be designed for universal use from the outset wherever possible.\n3. Public procurement shall give preference to accessible solutions at comparable cost and quality.\n\n## Article 22.3.4 — Legal Capacity and Support\n\n1. Legal capacity is presumed for every person with a disability.\n2. Supported decision-making shall be offered before any substitute decision-making arrangement is considered, in accordance with Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making).\n3. Support services, including personal assistance and communication aids, shall be available according to need and not according to wealth.\n\n## Article 22.3.5 — Participation and Monitoring\n\n1. Organizations of persons with disabilities shall be consulted on legislation and policy affecting them.\n2. An independent mechanism shall monitor the realization of the rights in this Section and report annually to the Parliament.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The architecture mirrors the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, shifting the paradigm from care to equality. Placing the burden of justifying a refusal of accommodation on the refuser follows the approach of the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990. The link to supported decision-making keeps guardianship law and social policy aligned, so that neither part of the legal system treats disability as incapacity.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.5 (Affirmative Measures); Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making); Article 17.7.5 (Digital Inclusion).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__elder-care", "path": "22-social-policy/elder-care.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.6 — Elder Care", "md": "# Section 22.6 — Elder Care\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.6.1 – 22.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees dignified aging, a statutory long-term care system, and protection of older persons from abuse and neglect.*\n\n## Article 22.6.1 — Dignity in Aging\n\n1. Older persons have the right to live in dignity, to autonomy in their daily lives, and to participate in the social, cultural, and economic life of the Republic.\n2. Support for older persons shall give priority to enabling independent living in a familiar environment for as long as the person wishes and it is compatible with safety and well-being.\n\n## Article 22.6.2 — Long-Term Care\n\n1. The Parliament shall by law establish a long-term care system covering assistance with daily activities, nursing care, and rehabilitation.\n2. Needs shall be assessed according to uniform criteria, and entitlement shall not depend on the ability to pay.\n3. Home-based and community-based services shall be developed alongside residential facilities, and the quality of both shall be subject to published standards and regular inspection.\n\n## Article 22.6.3 — Protection from Abuse\n\n1. The State shall protect older persons from violence, neglect, and financial exploitation, whether occurring in private homes, in care facilities, or elsewhere.\n2. Professionals working with older persons shall have a duty to report suspected abuse to the competent authority, and reports shall be investigated without delay.\n3. Care facilities shall be inspected at regular intervals by an authority independent of their operators, and inspection reports shall be published.\n\n## Article 22.6.4 — Autonomy and Participation\n\n1. Advance planning for care and representation, including powers of attorney and directives, shall be promoted and given legal effect before any substitute arrangement is considered.\n2. Substitute decision-making for older persons shall follow the safeguards of Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making) and shall be subject to independent review.\n3. Older persons shall be consulted on the design of care services intended for them, individually and through their representative organizations.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Constitutionalizing long-term care answers the demographic arithmetic: in an aging society, care is no longer a residual welfare question but a core function of the state. The model statute approach keeps financing choices flexible while locking in universality of needs assessment. The safeguarding duties respond to documented patterns of under-reported elder abuse, and the preference for supported decision-making keeps this Section aligned with the disability paradigm of Section 22.3 rather than reverting to paternalism.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making); Article 12.1.3 (Primary and Community Care); Article 20.1.1 (Mandate).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__housing", "path": "22-social-policy/housing.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.4 — Housing", "md": "# Section 22.4 — Housing\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.4.1 – 22.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section commits the State to adequate housing through progressive realization and protects every person from arbitrary eviction.*\n\n## Article 22.4.1 — Adequate Housing\n\n1. Every person has the right to adequate housing.\n2. Adequate housing includes security of tenure, habitability, affordability, accessibility, proximity to work and services, and respect for cultural expressions of dwelling.\n3. The State shall pursue a national housing policy directed to the progressive realization of this right by all appropriate means, including legislation, planning, and expenditure to the maximum of available resources.\n\n## Article 22.4.2 — Housing Plan and Targets\n\n1. The Government shall adopt a national housing plan with time-bound targets, and shall report on progress to the Parliament annually.\n2. The plan shall address the needs of homeless persons, persons with disabilities, large families, and persons unable to compete in the market without support.\n3. Municipalities shall integrate housing needs into their land-use plans under Section 23.5.\n\n## Article 22.4.3 — Protection from Arbitrary Eviction\n\n1. No person shall be evicted from a home except on the basis of law, by a competent authority, and in accordance with due process.\n2. Eviction measures shall be proportionate; before an eviction is carried out, the authorities shall consult the affected persons and examine alternatives, including the rehousing of households in vulnerable situations.\n3. Evictions shall not be carried out in a manner that leaves persons without shelter in conditions incompatible with human dignity.\n\n## Article 22.4.4 — Social Housing\n\n1. The State and the Municipalities shall maintain a social housing stock for households whose needs cannot be met in the market.\n2. Allocation of social housing shall follow published criteria, shall be administered without discrimination, and shall promote mixed communities rather than segregation.\n\n## Article 22.4.5 — Homelessness\n\n1. The State shall maintain a coordinated strategy to prevent and reduce homelessness, combining prevention, outreach, shelter, and pathways into permanent housing.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The elements of adequacy in Article 22.4.1 paraphrase the settled interpretation of the right to adequate housing under the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, notably General Comment No. 4 of the United Nations Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights. Progressive realization imposes an immediate duty to move deliberately rather than a license for inaction. The eviction safeguards draw on the South African Constitutional Court's jurisprudence on meaningful engagement before evictions from informal settlements.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 7.3.2 (General Competence and Services); Article 23.5.4 (Housing Integration).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__pensions", "path": "22-social-policy/pensions.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.2 — Pensions", "md": "# Section 22.2 — Pensions\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.2.1 – 22.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes old-age security as a multi-pillar pension system subject to periodic sustainability reviews and protection of accrued rights.*\n\n## Article 22.2.1 — Multi-Pillar System\n\n1. The Parliament shall by law maintain a pension system comprising:\n   (a) a basic pension, universal or residence-based, sufficient to prevent old-age poverty;\n   (b) a mandatory contributory pillar with earnings-related benefits; and\n   (c) voluntary supplementary savings under transparent prudential supervision.\n2. Each pillar shall be governed by published actuarial assumptions, and its financing shall be distinguished from the ordinary annual budget.\n\n## Article 22.2.2 — Sustainability Reviews\n\n1. An independent review of the long-term sustainability of the pension system shall take place at least every five years.\n2. The review shall examine demographic trends, contribution rates, benefit formulas, and the statutory retirement age, and shall publish its findings and recommendations in full.\n3. Where the review identifies a funding gap over its projection horizon, the Parliament shall respond within two years by law, adjusting contributions, benefits, or both, and shall state publicly the reasons for its response.\n\n## Article 22.2.3 — Protection of Accrued Rights\n\n1. Pension entitlements already accrued shall not be reduced retroactively.\n2. Adjustments to pension law may operate only prospectively and shall include transitional protection for persons within ten years of the statutory retirement age.\n3. The basic pension under Article 22.2.1 shall in no case fall below the minimum income floor established under Section 22.5.\n\n## Article 22.2.4 — Retirement and Continued Work\n\n1. The statutory retirement age shall be set by law and may be adjusted only prospectively, following a sustainability review under Article 22.2.2.\n2. The right to continue working beyond the retirement age shall be protected, and pension rules shall not operate as a prohibition on work.\n\n## Article 22.2.5 — Prevention of Old-Age Poverty\n\n1. The aggregate of basic pension, earnings-related benefits, and supplementary assistance shall secure every older person an income above the poverty line used for measurement under Section 22.5.\n2. Obligations to contribute to long-term care shall not reduce a pension below that minimum; care shall be financed under Section 22.6.\n3. Survivors' benefits shall be provided by law for dependent family members.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Multi-pillar design follows the taxonomy popularized by international financial institutions in the 1990s, while the mandatory five-year review resembles automatic-balancing mechanisms pioneered in Sweden and Germany. The Constitution fixes the architecture but leaves parameters to ordinary law, so demography can be answered without constitutional amendment. The prospective-only rule for accrued rights reflects how constitutional courts in Germany and elsewhere have mediated between pension reform and legitimate expectations.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.7.1 (Principle of Sustainability); Article 22.1.1 (Right to Social Protection).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__poverty-reduction", "path": "22-social-policy/poverty-reduction.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.5 — Poverty Reduction", "md": "# Section 22.5 — Poverty Reduction\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.5.1 – 22.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section requires a national poverty strategy built on transparent measurement and a guaranteed minimum income floor.*\n\n## Article 22.5.1 — National Poverty Strategy\n\n1. The Government shall adopt a comprehensive national strategy to reduce poverty and social exclusion, and shall renew it at least every five years.\n2. The strategy shall address income poverty and its multidimensional causes, including access to housing, education, health care, and employment.\n3. The strategy shall set measurable targets, and the Parliament shall be informed annually of progress.\n\n## Article 22.5.2 — Transparent Measurement\n\n1. The Statistics Authority shall measure poverty by officially published methods, using both an absolute poverty line and a relative threshold based on median incomes.\n2. Data shall be disaggregated by region, household type, age, disability, and other relevant characteristics so that hidden deprivation is visible.\n3. The methods of measurement may be changed only on the basis of published reasons and shall remain comparable over time.\n\n## Article 22.5.3 — Minimum Income Floor\n\n1. Every person lacking sufficient resources has the right to a minimum income floor ensuring a life in dignity.\n2. The floor shall be set by Act, shall be indexed to the cost of living, and shall at least cover adequate food, clothing, housing, and access to essential services.\n3. Conditions attached to the floor, including activation requirements, shall be reasonable; sanctions shall not reduce support below the level needed for the essentials of life.\n\n## Article 22.5.4 — Evaluation and Participation\n\n1. The strategy and the level of the minimum income floor shall be evaluated periodically against the measurements under Article 22.5.2.\n2. Persons affected by poverty and organizations representing them shall be heard in the preparation and evaluation of the strategy.\n3. The results of evaluation shall be published and shall inform the renewal of the strategy.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Guaranteeing a floor by constitution, while leaving its level to ordinary law, follows the design logic of social rights in the constitutions of Finland and Portugal: the norm is justiciable as a minimum core, but fiscal calibration remains democratic. Requiring dual absolute and relative measurement is a deliberate hedge, since each indicator alone understates or overstates deprivation. The limit on sanctions reflects the lesson of conditionality experiments that withdrawal below subsistence undermines the very employability such regimes seek to build.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 20.6.2 (Quality and Standards); Article 22.1.1 (Right to Social Protection); Article 1.2.3 (Intersectional Discrimination).\n"}, {"id": "22-social-policy__social-security", "path": "22-social-policy/social-security.md", "part": "22-social-policy", "partTitle": "Social Policy", "title": "Section 22.1 — Social Security", "md": "# Section 22.1 — Social Security\n\n> **Part 22 — Social Policy** · Articles 22.1.1 – 22.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes the right to social protection and the universal, adequate, and portable system that implements it.*\n\n## Article 22.1.1 — Right to Social Protection\n\n1. Every person within the Republic has the right to social protection against the standard risks of life, including sickness, unemployment, old age, disability, loss of a breadwinner, work injury, and the costs of raising children.\n2. Social protection shall combine contributory insurance with non-contributory assistance so that no person is left without a minimum level of protection.\n3. The Parliament shall by law define the benefits, contributions, and eligibility conditions of the system.\n\n## Article 22.1.2 — Universal Coverage\n\n1. Coverage shall extend to every worker irrespective of the form of employment, including part-time, temporary, self-employed, and platform work, and to every resident irrespective of employment status.\n2. Periods of caregiving for children or for dependent relatives shall count as periods of insurance.\n3. Gaps in coverage arising from interrupted careers shall be remedied by law and not left to administrative discretion.\n\n## Article 22.1.3 — Adequacy and Indexation\n\n1. Benefits shall be sufficient to secure a life in dignity, including adequate food, clothing, and housing.\n2. Benefit levels shall be indexed to the cost of living and reviewed at least every three years against wage developments.\n3. Minimum benefit levels shall be set by Act after consultation with representative organizations of beneficiaries and with the social partners.\n\n## Article 22.1.4 — Fair Administration\n\n1. Benefits shall be administered promptly, transparently, and accessibly; applications may be made in person and through digital channels of equal standing.\n2. Decisions shall be reasoned in writing and shall disclose the data on which they rest; automated decisions shall remain subject to human review.\n3. Every person may appeal an adverse decision to an independent body, and legal assistance shall be available as provided by law.\n\n## Article 22.1.5 — Equality and Portability\n\n1. Access to social protection shall be guaranteed without discrimination.\n2. Entitlements shall be portable throughout the Republic, irrespective of movement between Provinces, Municipalities, schemes, or jobs.\n3. Data collected for the administration of social protection may be used only for purposes compatible with the provision of benefits.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The insurance-plus-assistance architecture follows the statutory social insurance tradition of postwar Europe, while the universal floor reflects the social protection floors approach developed under the International Labour Organization. Coverage is deliberately decoupled from a lifelong standard employment biography, because working lives are now intermittent. The trade-off is cost: universality is more expensive than narrow means-testing, but it avoids the exclusion errors and stigma of purely targeted systems. Portability keeps the promise of a unitary state that social rights do not depend on where a person lives.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination); Article 12.1.1 (Universal Access).\n"}, {"id": "23-infrastructure__critical-infrastructure", "path": "23-infrastructure/critical-infrastructure.md", "part": "23-infrastructure", "partTitle": "Infrastructure", "title": "Section 23.6 — Critical Infrastructure", "md": "# Section 23.6 — Critical Infrastructure\n\n> **Part 23 — Infrastructure** · Articles 23.6.1 – 23.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects the continuity of essential services through identification, resilience standards, incident reporting, and tested continuity plans.*\n\n## Article 23.6.1 — Identification\n\n1. The Parliament shall by law define criteria for identifying critical infrastructure whose failure would endanger public safety, essential services, or the functioning of the State.\n2. Operators of designated facilities shall be listed in a register maintained under security-appropriate conditions; the register shall be as public as protection allows.\n\n## Article 23.6.2 — Resilience Standards\n\n1. Operators of critical infrastructure shall assess risks, maintain redundancy for essential functions, and adopt security measures proportionate to the potential harm.\n2. Standards shall be set with the participation of the operators concerned and shall be reviewed as threats evolve, in coordination with the cybersecurity regime of Part 17.\n3. Operators shall map their dependencies on other critical infrastructure and on providers outside the Republic where essential functions rely on them, and shall record how those dependencies are secured.\n\n## Article 23.6.3 — Incident Reporting\n\n1. Operators shall report significant incidents to the competent authority without undue delay.\n2. Reports concerning digital infrastructure shall follow the channels of Article 17.3.2 (Incident Reporting) and may be coordinated by the National CERT under Article 17.3.3 (The National CERT).\n\n## Article 23.6.4 — Continuity Plans\n\n1. Operators shall adopt continuity plans enabling the resumption of essential services after disruption, and shall test them through periodic exercises.\n2. Constitutional organs and essential public services shall maintain continuity plans for their own functioning, including the preservation of essential records.\n\n## Article 23.6.5 — Oversight\n\n1. Compliance with this Section shall be subject to independent oversight, and findings shall be reported to the Parliament in general terms without disclosing exploitable details.\n2. The oversight body shall evaluate the exercises conducted under Article 23.6.4 and shall recommend improvements to the standards of this Section.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Resilience law walks a narrow line: too public, and the rules become a target map; too secret, and operators escape accountability. The compromise here follows the approach of the European Union's NIS framework of 2016 — public duties, security-classified details, aggregate parliamentary reporting. Extending continuity planning to constitutional organs themselves addresses a gap exposed by several real crises, where the legal order survived only because officials improvised.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 17.3.4 (Critical Operators); Article 17.3.2 (Incident Reporting); Article 17.3.3 (The National CERT).\n"}, {"id": "23-infrastructure__energy", "path": "23-infrastructure/energy.md", "part": "23-infrastructure", "partTitle": "Infrastructure", "title": "Section 23.2 — Energy", "md": "# Section 23.2 — Energy\n\n> **Part 23 — Infrastructure** · Articles 23.2.1 – 23.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section secures reliable, sustainable, and affordable energy for all, with transition planning and independent regulation of energy networks.*\n\n## Article 23.2.1 — Energy Policy Objectives\n\n1. Energy policy shall secure a reliable, environmentally sound, and affordable supply of energy for the Republic.\n2. Access to energy is an essential service; disconnection of households in vulnerable situations shall be restricted by law and never endanger health or dignity.\n3. Energy policy shall respect the environmental duty of the State under Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State).\n\n## Article 23.2.2 — Transition Planning\n\n1. The Government shall adopt a national energy and climate plan defining the phased transition of energy production and consumption, and shall renew it periodically.\n2. The plan shall take into account the technical security of supply and shall support affected workers and regions through retraining and regional development measures under Part 10 and Part 7.\n\n## Article 23.2.3 — Universal Access and Consumer Protection\n\n1. Every household shall have the right to a connection to the electricity network on reasonable, published terms.\n2. Tariffs shall be transparent; vulnerable households shall benefit from targeted support rather than generalized underpricing that discourages conservation.\n\n## Article 23.2.4 — Independent Network Regulation\n\n1. Energy networks shall be regulated by an independent authority established by law.\n2. The authority shall ensure non-discriminatory third-party access to networks, transparent tariff methodologies, and investment planning adequate to maintain reliability.\n3. The independence and accountability of the authority shall meet the standards applicable to independent regulators under Section 29.3.\n\n## Article 23.2.5 — Safety and Resilience\n\n1. Technical safety standards for the production, transmission, storage, and distribution of energy shall be set and enforced by law.\n2. Critical energy facilities shall meet the resilience requirements of Section 23.6.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Energy touches every canonical tension in infrastructure law: monopoly networks, public goods, and climate obligations. The text answers with three stable commitments — universal connection, an independent network regulator, and phased transition planning — while leaving the generation mix entirely to democratic choice. The vulnerable-household rule follows the practice of several European regulators that prohibit winter disconnections, chosen here in dignity terms rather than seasonal ones.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State); Article 13.1.1 (Implementation Through Standards).\n"}, {"id": "23-infrastructure__public-infrastructure", "path": "23-infrastructure/public-infrastructure.md", "part": "23-infrastructure", "partTitle": "Infrastructure", "title": "Section 23.4 — Public Infrastructure", "md": "# Section 23.4 — Public Infrastructure\n\n> **Part 23 — Infrastructure** · Articles 23.4.1 – 23.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the planning, selection, maintenance, and integrity of public infrastructure projects over their whole life cycle.*\n\n## Article 23.4.1 — Life-Cycle Planning\n\n1. Every public infrastructure project shall be planned over its full life cycle, covering the assessment of need, comparison of alternatives, construction costs, maintenance and operation, financing, and eventual decommissioning.\n2. The method of appraisal shall be published, and the underlying assumptions of major projects shall be disclosed to the Parliament before funds are committed.\n3. The realized costs and benefits of major projects shall be evaluated after completion, compared with the original appraisal, and the comparison published.\n\n## Article 23.4.2 — Maintenance Duty\n\n1. The State shall maintain public infrastructure in a condition fit for safe use, and shall provide for maintenance expenditure in the annual budget.\n2. Authorities shall publish the condition of their infrastructure assets at defined intervals.\n3. New construction of a class of assets shall not be approved while existing assets of the same class under the responsibility of the same authority fail published safety thresholds, unless the new construction serves to remedy that failure.\n\n## Article 23.4.3 — Transparent Project Selection\n\n1. Projects shall be selected according to published criteria of public benefit and cost, applied through the annual budget under Article 8.2.4 (Budget Transparency).\n2. A pipeline of planned major projects shall be published in advance of each budget year.\n\n## Article 23.4.4 — Procurement Integrity\n\n1. Works, supplies, and services for public infrastructure shall be procured under the principles and integrity rules of Sections 8.5, including Article 8.5.3 (Integrity Rules).\n2. Contracts shall not be split to evade review thresholds, and single-source awards shall be justified in writing and recorded.\n3. Variations to contract price or duration during execution shall be documented and shall be reported to the Parliament where they materially change the appraisal under Article 23.4.1.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Deferred maintenance is the quiet fiscal illusion of infrastructure policy: it improves today's accounts at the expense of tomorrow's safety. The bar on new construction in Article 23.4.2 is deliberately strong because maintenance has no natural constituency, whereas ribbon-cutting does. Life-cycle planning and published appraisal methods follow the practice of independent infrastructure authorities such as those used in Australia and the United Kingdom to depoliticize project selection.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.5.1 (Principles); Article 8.5.3 (Integrity Rules); Article 8.2.4 (Budget Transparency).\n"}, {"id": "23-infrastructure__telecommunications", "path": "23-infrastructure/telecommunications.md", "part": "23-infrastructure", "partTitle": "Infrastructure", "title": "Section 23.3 — Telecommunications", "md": "# Section 23.3 — Telecommunications\n\n> **Part 23 — Infrastructure** · Articles 23.3.1 – 23.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section provides universal telecommunications service, independent spectrum management, and net neutrality as the operational basis of an open society.*\n\n## Article 23.3.1 — Universal Service\n\n1. Every person in the Republic shall have access to affordable basic telecommunications services, including internet access of sufficient quality for participation in public and economic life.\n2. Coverage obligations may be imposed on providers to eliminate unequal access between regions, and the costs of such obligations shall be shared fairly within the sector.\n\n## Article 23.3.2 — Spectrum and Numbering\n\n1. Radio spectrum and numbering resources are public resources administered by an independent authority established by law.\n2. Frequencies shall be allocated through open, non-discriminatory procedures published in advance; the grant of a frequency shall not confer any right to control content.\n3. The authority shall publish its decisions with reasons and shall coordinate spectrum use with neighboring states as provided by Part 19.\n\n## Article 23.3.3 — Net Neutrality\n\n1. Providers of internet access shall treat all traffic equally, without blocking, slowing, or prioritizing content, applications, or services for payment or otherwise.\n2. Reasonable traffic management is permitted only where it is necessary for network security, legal compliance, or the response to exceptional congestion, shall be non-discriminatory, and shall be disclosed publicly.\n3. This Article implements the open-internet guarantee of Article 17.7.1 (The Open Internet).\n\n## Article 23.3.4 — Privacy of Communications\n\n1. Providers shall preserve the confidentiality of communications and shall process traffic and location data only as permitted under Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing).\n\n## Article 23.3.5 — Competition\n\n1. Regulation shall promote effective competition; providers holding significant market power shall meet access and interconnection obligations so that end users benefit from choice.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Telecommunications is regulated because networks are natural monopolies and spectrum is scarce, yet the traffic itself belongs to users, not carriers — hence the strict neutrality rule in Article 23.3.3, aligned with the European Union's Open Internet Regulation of 2015. Separating spectrum management (infrastructure) from content governance (Part 21 and Part 17) prevents a licensing regime from becoming a censorship regime. Universal service is the telecommunications analogue of the universal service obligations in Section 23.1.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 17.7.1 (The Open Internet); Article 17.7.5 (Digital Inclusion); Article 9.2.4 (Abuse of Dominance).\n"}, {"id": "23-infrastructure__transport", "path": "23-infrastructure/transport.md", "part": "23-infrastructure", "partTitle": "Infrastructure", "title": "Section 23.1 — Transport", "md": "# Section 23.1 — Transport\n\n> **Part 23 — Infrastructure** · Articles 23.1.1 – 23.1.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section sets the objectives and instruments of transport policy: safety, accessibility, sustainability, integrated planning, and public service obligations.*\n\n## Article 23.1.1 — Objectives of Transport Policy\n\n1. Transport policy shall provide safe, accessible, and sustainable mobility for persons and goods throughout the Republic.\n2. Safety shall be the first objective of transport policy; the State shall pursue continuous reduction of deaths and serious injuries on all transport networks.\n3. Transport investment and regulation shall support the transition to low-emission mobility and shall be consistent with the environmental duties of Section 1.12.\n\n## Article 23.1.2 — Integrated Planning\n\n1. The Government shall adopt a national transport plan coordinating road, rail, waterborne, and air transport, and shall renew it periodically.\n2. Provinces and Municipalities shall be involved in the preparation of plans that affect their territories, and local networks shall connect to the national network.\n3. Major transport projects shall undergo environmental assessment in accordance with Article 13.1.2 (Environmental Impact Assessment).\n\n## Article 23.1.3 — Public Service Obligations\n\n1. The Parliament may by law impose public service obligations to secure basic transport services on routes that would not be commercially viable.\n2. Obligations shall be defined in advance, awarded impartially under Article 8.5.2 (Procedures and Award Criteria), and compensated fairly.\n3. Vehicles and infrastructure open to the public shall be accessible to persons with disabilities in accordance with Section 22.3.\n\n## Article 23.1.4 — Safety Regulation and Investigation\n\n1. Technical safety standards for vehicles, vessels, aircraft, and infrastructure shall be set by law and enforced by the competent authorities.\n2. Independent bodies shall investigate transport accidents with a view to prevention, and their safety recommendations shall be considered publicly by the competent authorities.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Making safety the first objective codifies the Vision Zero philosophy first adopted by the Swedish Parliament in 1997, which treats deaths on the network as a design failure rather than an accepted cost. Public service obligations are the standard instrument for reconciling network coverage with market operation, and the requirement of impartial award ties them to public procurement law. The Constitution prescribes objectives and coordination; mode-specific detail remains statutory.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 13.1.2 (Environmental Impact Assessment); Article 8.5.2 (Procedures and Award Criteria).\n"}, {"id": "23-infrastructure__urban-development", "path": "23-infrastructure/urban-development.md", "part": "23-infrastructure", "partTitle": "Infrastructure", "title": "Section 23.5 — Urban Development", "md": "# Section 23.5 — Urban Development\n\n> **Part 23 — Infrastructure** · Articles 23.5.1 – 23.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section directs sustainable urbanization through democratic land-use planning, participation, and the integration of housing policy.*\n\n## Article 23.5.1 — Sustainable Urbanization\n\n1. Urban development policy shall promote compact, mixed-use, and resource-efficient settlements, the reduction of sprawl, and adaptation to climate change.\n2. Development shall give priority to the reuse of already-developed land and existing buildings where suitable.\n\n## Article 23.5.2 — Land-Use Plans\n\n1. Land use shall be determined by binding plans adopted under the procedures of Section 24.2 and applicable planning law.\n2. Plans shall coordinate national, Provincial, and municipal interests; in case of conflict, the subsidiarity principles of Part 7 govern the allocation of competences.\n3. Plans shall state the infrastructure, services, and transit connections required by the development they permit; land may not be released for development where the essential services that development presupposes cannot be secured.\n\n## Article 23.5.3 — Participation\n\n1. Residents shall be informed of draft plans in an understandable form and heard before their adoption, in accordance with Article 7.3.5 (Community Participation).\n2. The reasoned response to objections shall be published together with the adopted plan.\n\n## Article 23.5.4 — Housing Integration\n\n1. Municipal land-use plans shall reserve land for housing adequate in quantity, location, and affordability, applying the housing policy of Section 22.4.\n2. Social housing shall be integrated into residential areas and shall not be concentrated so as to create segregated districts.\n\n## Article 23.5.5 — Public Space and Services\n\n1. Urban plans shall secure public spaces, green areas, and access to everyday services within reasonable distance of dwellings, including for persons with disabilities in accordance with Section 22.3.\n2. Planning shall reduce exposure of settlements to environmental hazards, applying the risk information produced under the environmental regime of Part 13.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Urbanization is where abstract sustainability duties become visible in daily life, so this Section binds planning to compactness, participation, and social mix rather than to aesthetics. The integration duty responds to the failure mode of social housing policy in many countries, where large mono-tenure estates concentrated disadvantage. Linking municipal plans to the land-use regime of Section 24.2 keeps one coherent chain from constitutional land guarantees to the neighborhood plan.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 7.3.5 (Community Participation); Article 24.2.1 (Planning by Law); Article 22.4.4 (Social Housing).\n"}, {"id": "24-agriculture__agriculture", "path": "24-agriculture/agriculture.md", "part": "24-agriculture", "partTitle": "Agriculture", "title": "Section 24.1 — Agriculture", "md": "# Section 24.1 — Agriculture\n\n> **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land** · Articles 24.1.1 – 24.1.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes a sustainable agriculture policy, support programs tied to public goods, research and extension, and fair supply chains.*\n\n## Article 24.1.1 — Sustainable Agriculture Policy\n\n1. The State shall pursue an agriculture policy that keeps land productive over generations, protects soil, water, and biodiversity, and strengthens the resilience of farming to climate and market shocks.\n2. Agricultural activity shall remain compatible with the environmental duty of the State under Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State).\n\n## Article 24.1.2 — Support Programs\n\n1. Public support to agriculture shall be predictable over multiple years and shall be directed to the provision of public goods and to the stability of farm incomes.\n2. Support shall not reward practices that cause avoidable environmental harm; support conditions shall be proportionate to the administrative capacity of small farms.\n3. Support programs shall be evaluated periodically and the evaluations published.\n\n## Article 24.1.3 — Research and Extension\n\n1. The State shall maintain agricultural research and an independent advisory service available to farmers, including on sustainable methods and farm management.\n2. Agricultural data collected by public bodies shall be made available to farmers and researchers in aggregated form, respecting the confidentiality rules of Section 20.6.\n\n## Article 24.1.4 — Fair Supply Chains\n\n1. Unfair trading practices that abuse bargaining power in agricultural and food supply chains shall be prohibited by law.\n2. Farmers may form producer organizations and, in accordance with Part 10, associations to negotiate the terms of sale.\n3. Competition law applies to the food sector under Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition); processing and retail markets shall be monitored for market power at the expense of producers and consumers.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Agricultural constitutions historically swung between productivism and protectionism; this text chooses a third anchor, paying for public goods rather than volume. Tying support to sustainability answers the well-documented failure of acreage and headage subsidies to arrest environmental decline. The fair-supply-chain clause reflects statutory reforms adopted in several European states after investigations showed persistent margin squeeze on primary producers, and it deliberately permits collective negotiation despite general competition law.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State); Article 9.2.1 (Protection of Competition).\n"}, {"id": "24-agriculture__animal-welfare", "path": "24-agriculture/animal-welfare.md", "part": "24-agriculture", "partTitle": "Agriculture", "title": "Section 24.5 — Animal Welfare", "md": "# Section 24.5 — Animal Welfare\n\n> **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land** · Articles 24.5.1 – 24.5.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section recognizes animals as sentient beings and requires welfare standards in their keeping, transport, and slaughter, with an enforcement authority.*\n\n## Article 24.5.1 — Sentience and Duty of Protection\n\n1. Animals are sentient beings; the State shall protect their welfare in keeping, care, transport, and slaughter.\n2. No animal shall be subjected to avoidable pain, suffering, or fear; any use of animals shall be justified by a legitimate human purpose and shall apply the least harmful practicable methods.\n3. Where human rights and interests conflict with animal welfare, restrictions shall be prescribed by law, pursue a legitimate aim, and be proportionate, in accordance with the method of Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights).\n\n## Article 24.5.2 — Welfare Standards\n\n1. The Parliament shall by law set standards for the keeping, husbandry, transport, and slaughter of animals, appropriate to each species.\n2. Standards shall be reviewed in the light of scientific and veterinary evidence and of experience under Article 13.1.5 (Monitoring and Enforcement).\n3. Education and training in animal welfare shall be provided for persons who keep or handle animals professionally.\n\n## Article 24.5.3 — Enforcement Authority\n\n1. A competent authority shall enforce animal welfare law, with powers of inspection, seizure in urgent cases, and the imposition of sanctions.\n2. Inspections shall be conducted on a risk basis and may be unannounced; inspectors shall have the qualifications prescribed by law.\n3. Persons may report suspected violations to the authority, and such reports shall be assessed without disclosing the reporter's identity where protection is required.\n\n## Article 24.5.4 — Scientific Use of Animals\n\n1. The use of animals in research and testing shall be permitted only where no alternative exists, the number of animals is reduced to the necessary minimum, and the conditions meet the standards of this Section.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The recognition of sentience follows the leading formulations in the German Basic Law of 2002 and the Lisbon Treaty, which opened ordinary legislation to animal welfare as a public interest rather than a private hobby of owners. The three-R rule for scientific use — replacement, reduction, refinement — is paraphrased in Article 24.5.4 because research use is the hardest case for proportionality. Constitutional text stays principle-level; species-by-species detail belongs to law that can track veterinary science.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 13.1.5 (Monitoring and Enforcement).\n"}, {"id": "24-agriculture__farmers-rights", "path": "24-agriculture/farmers-rights.md", "part": "24-agriculture", "partTitle": "Agriculture", "title": "Section 24.4 — Farmers' Rights", "md": "# Section 24.4 — Farmers' Rights\n\n> **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land** · Articles 24.4.1 – 24.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects fair farm income, seed autonomy subject to biosafety, participation in agricultural policy, and generational succession.*\n\n## Article 24.4.1 — Fair Income and Market Access\n\n1. The State shall promote conditions in which farmers obtain a fair standard of living from their activity, in accordance with the fair remuneration principle of Article 1.9.2 (Fair Remuneration) adapted to self-employed producers.\n2. Farmers shall have access to markets without unjustified barriers, and public quality schemes shall be open to all producers meeting the published criteria.\n\n## Article 24.4.2 — Seed Autonomy\n\n1. Farmers may save, use, exchange, and sell farm-saved seed of their own harvest, subject to biosafety requirements and to the intellectual property rules of Part 9.\n2. Prohibitions or fees shall not be designed so as to eliminate the practice of seed saving, and traditional varieties shall remain lawful.\n3. Rules protecting plant varieties shall balance the interests of breeders with the conservation of agricultural biodiversity.\n\n## Article 24.4.3 — Biosafety\n\n1. Rules on seeds, plant health, and organisms shall protect human, animal, and plant health and the environment, and shall be based on scientific evidence and risk assessment.\n2. Biosafety measures shall be applied without arbitrary discrimination between domestic and imported products of equivalent safety.\n\n## Article 24.4.4 — Participation and Succession\n\n1. Farmers and their organizations shall be consulted in the preparation of agricultural legislation and programs, consistently with Article 0.6.4 (Civil Society and Participation).\n2. The State shall support the transfer of farms between generations, including early handover, training of successors, and taxation that does not force the sale of viable farms.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Farmers' rights rarely appear in constitutions as such; they are assembled here from labor, property, and participation guarantees that self-employed producers otherwise receive unevenly. Seed autonomy is the most contested clause internationally — plant variety protection systems from the 1961 Paris convention onward restrict what farmers may reuse — so the text protects the practice as a floor while leaving the commercial framework to law. Succession support addresses the structural aging of the farming population, which no production subsidy corrects.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.9.2 (Fair Remuneration); Article 0.6.4 (Civil Society and Participation); Article 24.1.3 (Research and Extension).\n"}, {"id": "24-agriculture__fisheries", "path": "24-agriculture/fisheries.md", "part": "24-agriculture", "partTitle": "Agriculture", "title": "Section 24.6 — Fisheries", "md": "# Section 24.6 — Fisheries\n\n> **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land** · Articles 24.6.1 – 24.6.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs fishing through science-based quotas, transparent licensing, protection of small-scale fishers, and marine conservation.*\n\n## Article 24.6.1 — Science-Based Quotas\n\n1. Fishing opportunities shall be set at levels that maintain or restore fish stocks above biomass levels capable of producing the maximum sustainable yield, on the basis of the best available independent scientific advice.\n2. Total allowable catches and the allocation among fleet segments shall follow long-term management plans published in advance.\n3. Scientific advice and the decisions taken shall be published, and any departure from the advice shall state its reasons.\n\n## Article 24.6.2 — Transparent Licensing\n\n1. Fishing licenses shall be granted, renewed, and withdrawn according to published criteria, in the manner of Article 13.8.2 (Transparent Extraction Licensing).\n2. A public register shall record the holders of licenses and quotas; hidden transfers shall have no legal effect.\n3. Fees shall reflect the public value of the resource and shall not be levied so as to exclude small operators from viability.\n\n## Article 24.6.3 — Small-Scale Fishers\n\n1. The State shall secure a fair share of fishing opportunities for small-scale and coastal fishers, whose operations often carry the least environmental impact per unit landed.\n2. Support under Section 24.1 shall be accessible to small operators without disproportionate administrative burden.\n\n## Article 24.6.4 — Marine Conservation\n\n1. Marine ecosystems shall be protected through measures including closed areas and seasons, gear restrictions, and the reduction of bycatch and discards.\n2. The State shall protect the marine environment in fulfillment of Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State) and shall cooperate internationally on shared stocks, consistent with Part 19.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Fisheries collapse is the classic tragedy of the commons, and the constitutional answer here is procedural: bind the quota decision to published scientific advice and require reasons for any departure, as done in Norway and under the reform of the European Union's common fisheries policy in 2013. The register of quota holdings targets quota laundering through informal leasing. Recognizing small-scale fishers acknowledges evidence that fleet concentration delivers neither the best conservation nor the strongest coastal communities.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 13.8.2 (Transparent Extraction Licensing); Article 1.12.2 (Duty of the State).\n"}, {"id": "24-agriculture__food-security", "path": "24-agriculture/food-security.md", "part": "24-agriculture", "partTitle": "Agriculture", "title": "Section 24.3 — Food Security", "md": "# Section 24.3 — Food Security\n\n> **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land** · Articles 24.3.1 – 24.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section guarantees access to adequate food for all and prepares the Republic against supply crises through strategic reserves.*\n\n## Article 24.3.1 — Access to Adequate Food\n\n1. The State shall pursue a food security policy ensuring for every person the availability, accessibility, and nutritional adequacy of food.\n2. Food security policy shall link production under Section 24.1 with social protection under Part 22, since hunger within the Republic is in the first place a question of purchasing power and distribution.\n3. Public nutrition policy shall be based on independent scientific advice and shall promote truthful consumer information under Article 1.14.2 (Truthful Information).\n\n## Article 24.3.2 — Strategic Reserves\n\n1. The State shall maintain reserves of essential foodstuffs and agricultural inputs sufficient for foreseeable supply disruptions, financed through the budget.\n2. Reserves shall be rotated so that stocks remain usable, and their size, composition, and management shall be published annually.\n3. Reserves may be released to stabilize supply in scarcity; releases shall be non-discriminatory and shall not be used to manipulate markets for the benefit of particular traders.\n\n## Article 24.3.3 — Crisis Response\n\n1. Measures against severe food shortages, including distribution controls and priority allocations, shall follow the emergency and disaster regimes of Part 26 and shall remain temporary, proportionate, and non-discriminatory.\n2. Extraordinary burdens imposed on producers or distributors during a food crisis shall be compensated as provided by law.\n\n## Article 24.3.4 — Monitoring\n\n1. The Government shall monitor the security of food supply continuously and shall report to the Parliament annually on production, stocks, prices, and nutritional outcomes.\n2. Monitoring data shall inform the renewal of agricultural support programs under Section 24.1 and the reserves policy under Article 24.3.2.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Food security has two distinct failure modes: scarcity of supply and poverty amid plenty. The text directs constitutional attention to both — reserves for the first, the minimum income floor of Section 22.5 for the second — because stockpiles alone do not feed those who cannot pay. Publication duties for reserves respond to historical experience with opaque stock management, which invited speculation and corruption; transparency here is itself a stabilizer.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.14.2 (Truthful Information); Article 22.5.3 (Minimum Income Floor); Article 8.2.5 (Contingency Reserve).\n"}, {"id": "24-agriculture__land-use", "path": "24-agriculture/land-use.md", "part": "24-agriculture", "partTitle": "Agriculture", "title": "Section 24.2 — Land Use", "md": "# Section 24.2 — Land Use\n\n> **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land** · Articles 24.2.1 – 24.2.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section requires land-use planning by law, secures tenure for all holders, and confines expropriation to the guarantees of Section 1.8.*\n\n## Article 24.2.1 — Planning by Law\n\n1. Land use shall be determined only on the basis of plans adopted by law or under law by democratically legitimized bodies.\n2. Planning shall pursue the balanced development of urban and rural areas, the protection of agricultural land, nature, and cultural heritage, and the efficient use of land as a finite resource.\n3. Land-use planning is an exclusive public function; binding plans shall not be produced by private bodies.\n\n## Article 24.2.2 — Secure Tenure\n\n1. All forms of tenure — ownership, leasehold, customary and communal holdings, and use rights — shall be recognized and protected against arbitrary interference.\n2. The State shall maintain a public land register establishing the legal position of land, and registration shall be open to inspection.\n3. Tenure shall not be terminated except on the basis of law, for reasons made known to the holder, and with the possibility of review.\n\n## Article 24.2.3 — Expropriation\n\n1. Expropriation of land shall take place only under the conditions of Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation): for a public purpose, on the basis of law, and against fair compensation determined in advance.\n2. The intended public purpose shall be substantiated in the expropriation decision, and the land shall be used for that purpose; if it is not, the former holder shall have a right of repurchase as provided by law.\n3. Disputes over compensation and purpose shall be open to full judicial review under Article 1.8.4 (Compensation and Review).\n\n## Article 24.2.4 — Balanced Development\n\n1. Rural areas shall be served with infrastructure, services, and connectivity comparable in quality to urban areas, consistent with Section 23.1 and Section 23.5.\n2. Planning shall prefer the reuse of developed land over the conversion of farmland and open space.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The land register in Article 24.2.2 carries the weight of centuries of property practice: secure, public records are the cheapest anti-corruption device in land administration. Limiting expropriation by cross-reference rather than restatement keeps one single test — Section 1.8 — instead of parallel regimes that invite forum shopping. The repurchase sanction responds to the common abuse of expropriating for a stated purpose and then diverting the land to private development.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation); Article 1.8.4 (Compensation and Review); Article 1.8.1 (Right to Property).\n"}, {"id": "25-constitutional-process__constitutional-amendments", "path": "25-constitutional-process/constitutional-amendments.md", "part": "25-constitutional-process", "partTitle": "Constitutional Process", "title": "Section 25.1 — Constitutional Amendments", "md": "# Section 25.1 — Constitutional Amendments\n\n> **Part 25 — Constitutional Process** · Articles 25.1.1 – 25.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section fixes the tiers of constitutional amendment: ordinary amendments, entrenched provisions, schedule matters, and the restrictions that apply during emergencies.*\n\n## Article 25.1.1 — Initiative and Form\n\n1. A proposal to amend the Constitution may be introduced by one fifth of the members of either House or by the Government.\n2. A proposal to amend the Constitution may also reach the Parliament through the citizens' initiative of Section 25.2.\n3. Amendments shall be adopted as a single text; what is adopted is the whole amended Constitution as it will read.\n\n## Article 25.1.2 — Ordinary Amendments\n\n1. The provisions of this Constitution not referred to in Article 25.1.3 or in Article 25.1.4 may be amended by a two-thirds majority of each House.\n2. A constitutional amendment adopted under this Section shall be promulgated without delay and shall be published as part of this Constitution.\n\n## Article 25.1.3 — Entrenched Provisions\n\n1. The provisions listed in Section 30.5 may be amended only by a two-thirds majority of each House and by approval in a national referendum by a majority of the votes cast.\n2. The referendum shall be conducted under the rules applicable to mandatory referendums in Article 6.7.3 (Mandatory Referendum on Entrenched Amendments).\n3. No alternative to the referendum route may be used, and a referendum result may not be overridden by Parliament.\n\n## Article 25.1.4 — Schedule Matters\n\n1. Matters contained in the Schedules, insofar as they are not tied to provisions of Section 30.5, may be amended by a simple majority of each House.\n2. Schedule amendments shall be published in the same manner as ordinary amendments.\n\n## Article 25.1.5 — Restrictions During Emergencies\n\n1. During a state of emergency declared under Part 26, no constitutional amendment may be introduced or adopted.\n2. A vote on a constitutional amendment taken in violation of paragraph 1 is void.\n3. Judicial review of the validity of an amendment under this Article may not be excluded, consistently with Article 5.6.5 (No Ouster of Review).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Three amendment tiers balance stability and adaptability: a heavy ordinary route, a referendum lock for the identity of the polity, and a light route for schedules that would otherwise clog the amendment machinery. This mirrors the graded design of the German Basic Law, where a subset of principles is unamendable, and of constitutions that subject entrenched clauses to referendums, such as Denmark. The emergency moratorium answers the historical pattern that constitutions are most often rewritten under duress.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 6.7.3 (Mandatory Referendum on Entrenched Amendments); Article 5.6.5 (No Ouster of Review).\n"}, {"id": "25-constitutional-process__constitutional-continuity", "path": "25-constitutional-process/constitutional-continuity.md", "part": "25-constitutional-process", "partTitle": "Constitutional Process", "title": "Section 25.5 — Constitutional Continuity", "md": "# Section 25.5 — Constitutional Continuity\n\n> **Part 25 — Constitutional Process** · Articles 25.5.1 – 25.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section preserves the continuity of the constitutional order: succession when organs fail, the prohibition of unconstitutional abolition, and the duty of loyalty of all organs.*\n\n## Article 25.5.1 — Continuity of the Constitutional Order\n\n1. The constitutional order of the Republic is continuous; it survives changes of government, elections, crises, and the incapacity of any organ.\n2. The Constitution may be changed only through the procedures of Part 25; continuity and change are not in conflict, for amendment is the Constitution's own mode of continuing.\n\n## Article 25.5.2 — Succession of Organs\n\n1. Where a constitutional organ is permanently unable to act because its offices are unfilled or its functioning is otherwise obstructed, its essential functions shall be exercised temporarily by the organ designated by law, and only to the extent necessary to restore full capacity.\n2. Temporary exercise under paragraph 1 shall be announced publicly, shall be reported to the Parliament without delay, and shall end as soon as the organ is able to act.\n3. No organ may use the inability of another organ as a ground for assuming its ordinary powers.\n\n## Article 25.5.3 — Prohibition of Unconstitutional Abolition\n\n1. No organ, person, or group may abolish the constitutional order, dissolve the democratic institutions of the Republic, or remove the supremacy of this Constitution by any means other than amendment under Part 25.\n2. Acts attempting such abolition are void from the outset and create no obligations.\n3. Organs and officials shall not comply with such acts; the validity of measures taken in reliance on them may be tested under Section 5.6.\n\n## Article 25.5.4 — Duty of Loyalty\n\n1. All organs of the Republic, all officials, and all service members owe loyalty to the constitutional order and shall actively preserve it.\n2. Loyalty includes the duty to lawful dissent and to the loyal opposition; it does not license obedience to unconstitutional commands.\n\n## Article 25.5.5 — Continuity of the State\n\n1. The Republic continues as a subject of law notwithstanding changes of government or organs; its treaties, obligations, and property remain in force, subject to this Constitution.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Every constitution is ultimately a bet that institutions will outlast their occupants; this Section supplies the fallback rules for the day the bet is tested. The void-from-the-outset rule in Article 25.5.3 echoes the German Basic Law's defense of the democratic order and the constitutional identity doctrine of several European courts. Succession clauses are deliberately narrow — temporary, announced, and reported — because substituting organs is itself a danger if left open-ended.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.8.1 (Supreme Law); Article 5.6.5 (No Ouster of Review); Article 25.1.1 (Initiative and Form).\n"}, {"id": "25-constitutional-process__constitutional-convention", "path": "25-constitutional-process/constitutional-convention.md", "part": "25-constitutional-process", "partTitle": "Constitutional Process", "title": "Section 25.2 — Constitutional Convention", "md": "# Section 25.2 — Constitutional Convention\n\n> **Part 25 — Constitutional Process** · Articles 25.2.1 – 25.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section enables the citizens and the Parliament to convene a Constitutional Convention and submits its outcome to the amendment procedures.*\n\n## Article 25.2.1 — Citizens' Initiative\n\n1. At least five per cent of the citizens entitled to vote may submit to the Parliament a reasoned proposal to convene a Constitutional Convention.\n2. Within six months of receiving a valid proposal, the Parliament shall either resolve to convene the Convention or submit the question of convocation to a national referendum.\n3. The question shall be approved in a referendum by a majority of the votes cast.\n\n## Article 25.2.2 — Parliamentary Convocation\n\n1. The Parliament may convene a Constitutional Convention on its own initiative by resolution of each House.\n2. The resolution shall define the subject matters within the mandate of the Convention and the timetable for its work.\n\n## Article 25.2.3 — Composition and Mandate\n\n1. The Convention shall be composed so as to reflect the diversity of the Republic; its composition and procedure shall be determined by law adopted before its convocation.\n2. The Convention may deliberate only within the mandate assigned to it; it may not itself amend the Constitution.\n3. The Convention shall receive the administrative support it requires and shall publish its deliberations.\n\n## Article 25.2.4 — Limits\n\n1. The Convention shall observe the principles of Section 25.5 and shall not propose the abolition of the democratic constitutional order.\n2. Proposals concerning entrenched provisions under Section 30.5 shall be identified as such in the Convention's report.\n\n## Article 25.2.5 — Referendum on the Outcome\n\n1. The Convention shall transmit its proposals to the Parliament with its final report.\n2. Within twelve months, the Parliament shall introduce implementing amendment bills under Section 25.1 or shall publish its reasoned decision not to do so.\n3. Proposals affecting entrenched provisions shall, if adopted by the Parliament, be put to a national referendum as required by Article 6.7.3 (Mandatory Referendum on Entrenched Amendments).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A convention route inserts a deliberative reset valve that ordinary amendment votes lack: elected delegates with a mandate can rethink structures that parliamentarians are too invested in to review. The Icelandic assembly of 2011 and Ireland's Citizens' Assembly of 2016 show both the promise and the peril of such bodies, which is why this text hard-wires the parliamentary follow-up duty. Requiring the outcome to travel through Section 25.1 preserves the amendment tiers; the Convention advises the Constitution, it does not replace it.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.6.5 (Direct Democracy); Article 6.7.1 (Routes to a National Referendum).\n"}, {"id": "25-constitutional-process__constitutional-interpretation", "path": "25-constitutional-process/constitutional-interpretation.md", "part": "25-constitutional-process", "partTitle": "Constitutional Process", "title": "Section 25.3 — Constitutional Interpretation", "md": "# Section 25.3 — Constitutional Interpretation\n\n> **Part 25 — Constitutional Process** · Articles 25.3.1 – 25.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs how the Constitution is read: methods of interpretation, respect for precedent, evolution consistent with dignity, and the duty to give reasons.*\n\n## Article 25.3.1 — Methods of Interpretation\n\n1. All organs of the Republic shall interpret this Constitution in accordance with its text, structure, and purpose, and in harmony with the Constitution as a whole.\n2. Interpretation shall begin with the ordinary meaning of the words in their context; purpose and consequences may refine, but not contradict, the text.\n3. Interpretation consistent with the Republic's obligations under international law shall be preferred, in accordance with Article 19.3.3 (Consistent Interpretation).\n\n## Article 25.3.2 — Precedent\n\n1. Decisions of the Constitutional Court on constitutional questions bind all organs and courts.\n2. A departure from a settled interpretation shall state expressly the grounds of the departure and the reasons why the earlier decision should no longer stand.\n\n## Article 25.3.3 — Evolving Understanding\n\n1. Constitutional provisions may be interpreted in the light of present conditions and experience, within the limits of their text and purpose.\n2. Evolving interpretation shall remain consistent with human dignity and with the nature of this Constitution as the charter of a democratic republic.\n3. Interpretation shall not effect an amendment that the procedures of Section 25.1 have not accomplished.\n\n## Article 25.3.4 — Duty to Give Reasons\n\n1. Every organ applying or interpreting this Constitution shall state the reasons for its understanding in decisions of public effect.\n2. Reasons shall be intelligible to the public and shall identify the provision interpreted and the method relied upon.\n\n## Article 25.3.5 — Conforming Interpretation\n\n1. Statutes and subordinate law shall, wherever possible, be read so as to conform to this Constitution, following Article 0.8.4 (Conforming Interpretation).\n2. Where no conforming reading exists, the law shall be applied as written and its validity may be tested under Section 5.6.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Interpretation clauses are the quiet engine of constitutional longevity: they tell successors how to apply a text written decades earlier. The hierarchy of text, structure, purpose, and precedent condenses the methodology developed by courts across many systems, while the dignity anchor reflects the interpretive practice of the Federal Constitutional Court of Germany. Article 25.3.3 guards the boundary that comparative practice has found essential — courts may update meaning, but live-origin limitations and referendums, not judges, change the text.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 19.3.3 (Consistent Interpretation); Article 0.8.4 (Conforming Interpretation); Article 5.3.6 (Effect of Decisions).\n"}, {"id": "25-constitutional-process__constitutional-review", "path": "25-constitutional-process/constitutional-review.md", "part": "25-constitutional-process", "partTitle": "Constitutional Process", "title": "Section 25.4 — Constitutional Review Commission", "md": "# Section 25.4 — Constitutional Review Commission\n\n> **Part 25 — Constitutional Process** · Articles 25.4.1 – 25.4.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section establishes a periodic Constitutional Review Commission that examines the Constitution in public and feeds proposals into the amendment process; it is distinct from judicial review under Section 5.6.*\n\n## Article 25.4.1 — Establishment and Cycle\n\n1. A Constitutional Review Commission shall be convened every fifteen years.\n2. The Commission shall be established by law in good time before its convocation and shall be composed so as to include the parliamentary groups and independent members with expertise in law and public affairs.\n3. Its members shall serve in their personal capacity and shall declare any interests in matters before it.\n\n## Article 25.4.2 — Mandate\n\n1. The Commission shall examine the functioning of this Constitution, including the working of constitutional institutions, the realization of rights in practice, the coherence of the text, and the implementation gaps identified by constitutional bodies.\n2. The Commission may consider any provision, including entrenched provisions, and shall report whether and how it should be amended.\n\n## Article 25.4.3 — Public Participation\n\n1. The Commission shall invite submissions from the public, hold hearings accessible throughout the Republic, and publish an interim report before finalizing its recommendations.\n2. Hearings shall include the perspective of educators and civic organizations, so that the Constitution's operation is explained, not only evaluated.\n\n## Article 25.4.4 — Report and Follow-Up\n\n1. The Commission shall deliver a final report with reasoned recommendations.\n2. Within twelve months, the Parliament shall process recommendations under Section 25.1 or shall publish its reasoned decision not to do so.\n3. The follow-up given to each recommendation shall be recorded in a public register.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This mechanism answers a real defect of constitutions without any of the usual risks: most texts are reviewed piecemeal or only after crisis. Periodic review follows the model of the Austrian and Dutch constitutional survey bodies and the standing review practice of some Nordic parliaments, scaled to a fixed fifteen-year cycle. The Section deliberately does not touch judicial review under Section 5.6 — courts invalidate unconstitutional acts case by case; the Commission evaluates the whole document in public, and its proposals carry only the ordinary force of proposals.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.6.4 (Civil Society and Participation); Article 25.1.2 (Ordinary Amendments).\n"}, {"id": "26-emergency-and-crisis__emergency-safeguards", "path": "26-emergency-and-crisis/emergency-safeguards.md", "part": "26-emergency-and-crisis", "partTitle": "Emergency And Crisis", "title": "Section 26.5 — Emergency Safeguards", "md": "# Section 26.5 — Emergency Safeguards\n\n> **Part 26 — Emergency and Crisis** · Articles 26.5.1 – 26.5.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section entrenches the limits of every crisis regime: non-derogable rights, continuing oversight, expiry of measures, the amendment freeze, and accountability after termination.*\n\n## Article 26.5.1 — Non-Derogable Rights\n\n1. The rights listed in Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights) may not be derogated from in any circumstance, in war as in peace.\n2. Beyond that list, a right may be limited in an emergency only under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights), to the extent strictly required by the exigencies.\n3. A provision purporting to suspend a non-derogable right is void from adoption, and no organ of the State may act upon it.\n\n## Article 26.5.2 — Habeas Corpus and Judicial Access\n\n1. The right to bring habeas corpus proceedings remains available in full at all times.\n2. Every person detained under emergency powers shall be brought promptly before a judge and informed of the reasons in a language the person understands.\n3. Secret places of detention are prohibited; every place of detention remains subject to judicial access and to inspection by the Ombudsman and the Human Rights Commission.\n\n## Article 26.5.3 — Continuing Parliamentary and Institutional Oversight\n\n1. The Parliament shall review the continuation of the emergency at every renewal and may attach conditions to approval.\n2. The Ombudsman and the Human Rights Commission retain their full mandates during an emergency and shall report publicly on the observance of rights, under Article 20.2.4 (Periodic Reports).\n3. Obstruction of oversight institutions shall be sanctioned by law.\n\n## Article 26.5.4 — Expiry of Emergency Measures\n\n1. Every emergency measure lapses automatically when the emergency ends, without repeal.\n2. An emergency measure shall not acquire permanence by repetition; its provisions may be re-enacted as ordinary law only through the full legislative process, and emergency legislation shall remain formally distinct from ordinary law.\n\n## Article 26.5.5 — Amendment Freeze\n\n1. During a state of emergency or war, no amendment of this Part, of Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights), or of the entrenched provisions of Section 30.5 may be introduced, adopted, or proclaimed.\n2. The restriction in clause 1 subsists until six months after termination of the emergency.\n3. A purported amendment in breach of this Article is void.\n\n## Article 26.5.6 — Accountability After Termination\n\n1. Within six months after termination of an emergency, an independent review of the measures taken shall be laid before the Parliament and published.\n2. A person subjected to a measure unlawful under this Constitution is entitled to compensation without prejudice to any other remedy.\n3. Officials remain personally responsible for unlawful acts committed under emergency powers, under Article 4.8.4 (Accountability and Review).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section makes the emergency powers of the preceding Sections acceptable: everything not expressly non-derogable is derogable, and everything derogable is temporary, reported, and reviewable. The amendment freeze and its six-month tail prevent a government from manufacturing an emergency in order to rewrite the constitution, while post-termination review and compensation follow the truth-seeking model of modern commissions of inquiry.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 1.7.4 (Habeas Corpus and Liberty); Article 4.8.4 (Accountability and Review).\n"}, {"id": "26-emergency-and-crisis__natural-disasters", "path": "26-emergency-and-crisis/natural-disasters.md", "part": "26-emergency-and-crisis", "partTitle": "Emergency And Crisis", "title": "Section 26.2 — Natural Disasters", "md": "# Section 26.2 — Natural Disasters\n\n> **Part 26 — Emergency and Crisis** · Articles 26.2.1 – 26.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section organizes disaster response through a standing management authority, a solidarity fund, limited evacuation powers, and binding reconstruction duties.*\n\n## Article 26.2.1 — Disaster Management Authority\n\n1. An Act shall establish a national disaster-management authority for prevention, preparedness, response, and recovery.\n2. The authority shall operate continuously, and not only during a state of emergency under Section 26.1.\n3. The authority shall coordinate the services of the State, the Provinces, and the Municipalities under Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle).\n4. The authority may exercise coercive powers only where this Constitution or an Act confers them.\n\n## Article 26.2.2 — The Solidarity Fund\n\n1. An Act shall establish a national solidarity fund for relief and reconstruction.\n2. The fund shall be financed from appropriations, lawful donations, and other sources fixed by law.\n3. Allocations shall follow published criteria, shall be reported publicly, and shall prioritize the persons and communities most severely affected.\n4. The accounts of the fund shall be audited under Article 8.8.1 (Scope of Audit).\n\n## Article 26.2.3 — Evacuation and Urgent Measures\n\n1. An evacuation may be ordered only where necessary to protect life or health, only for the shortest necessary duration, and only by the authority or minister designated by law.\n2. An evacuation order shall be reasoned in writing and shall be subject to prompt review by a court.\n3. Public authorities shall protect the property of evacuated persons and shall prevent unlawful entry into evacuated areas.\n4. Nothing in this Article authorizes expropriation except under Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation).\n\n## Article 26.2.4 — Reconstruction\n\n1. Public authorities shall restore essential services, housing, and infrastructure within the shortest reasonable time.\n2. Reconstruction shall prioritize the persons and areas most severely affected and shall apply resilience standards that reduce future vulnerability.\n3. Contracts for reconstruction shall be awarded under Article 8.5.1 (Principles) and published.\n\n## Article 26.2.5 — Preparedness and Early Warning\n\n1. The State shall maintain:\n   (a) risk assessments for foreseeable disasters;\n   (b) early-warning systems; and\n   (c) public information duties.\n2. Periodic exercises shall test the emergency plans of every level of government.\n3. Operators of critical infrastructure shall cooperate with the authority under the resilience regime of Part 23 (Infrastructure).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Disaster response is separated from the state of emergency because catastrophes demand logistics rather than rights restrictions, and only the narrow evacuation power touches liberty. The solidarity fund constitutionalizes shared-cost relief, while routing its accounts through the supreme audit institution keeps crisis spending from becoming patronage. The reconstruction duty ensures the regime does not end at the evacuation, and subsidiarity keeps local first responders in the lead.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 8.5.1 (Principles); Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation); Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle).\n"}, {"id": "26-emergency-and-crisis__pandemics", "path": "26-emergency-and-crisis/pandemics.md", "part": "26-emergency-and-crisis", "partTitle": "Emergency And Crisis", "title": "Section 26.3 — Pandemics", "md": "# Section 26.3 — Pandemics\n\n> **Part 26 — Emergency and Crisis** · Articles 26.3.1 – 26.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs the public-health emergency regime: graduated escalation, limits on coercive measures, scientific advice, compensation, and continuity of essential services.*\n\n## Article 26.3.1 — Graduated Response\n\n1. The ordinary public-health powers of Section 12.2 (Public Health) apply first and shall be used wherever they suffice.\n2. A public-health emergency may be declared only where those powers are demonstrably inadequate, and only under Article 26.1.1 (Grounds and Declaration).\n3. The declaration shall state the nature of the risk, the measures contemplated, the territorial scope, and the anticipated duration.\n4. The declaration lapses, and is approved and renewed, under Article 26.1.2 (Approval and Duration).\n\n## Article 26.3.2 — Limits on Coercive Measures\n\n1. The following measures may be imposed only by law, on the best available evidence, and only where necessary and proportionate to the risk:\n   (a) isolation;\n   (b) quarantine;\n   (c) mandatory examination or treatment; and\n   (d) restrictions on movement or assembly.\n2. Such measures shall be the least restrictive available, shall be time-limited, and shall be subject to prompt review by a court or an independent body.\n3. Coercive measures shall not discriminate and shall serve no purpose other than the protection of health.\n4. The special protection of health data under Article 12.6.1 (Specially Protected Data) applies without exception during a public-health emergency.\n\n## Article 26.3.3 — Independent Scientific Advice\n\n1. Measures with major public-health impact shall rest on the advice of the scientific body established under Article 12.2.4 (Scientific Advice and Transparency).\n2. The advice received, the evidence relied upon, and the reasons for departing from it shall be published.\n3. Dissenting scientific opinions shall be recorded and published with the advice.\n\n## Article 26.3.4 — Compensation for Extraordinary Burdens\n\n1. A person bearing an extraordinary and disproportionate burden imposed for the protection of public health is entitled to compensation as the law provides.\n2. Extraordinary burdens include compulsory exclusion from work and the closure of a lawful business.\n3. Claims shall be decided within a reasonable time and are subject to judicial review.\n\n## Article 26.3.5 — Continuity of Essential Services\n\n1. During a public-health emergency, public authorities shall secure the continuity of healthcare, care in institutions, education, and the supply of food and essential goods.\n2. Restrictions within care facilities shall respect the dignity of the persons confined and their contact with the outside world.\n3. In-person education shall be interrupted only for the shortest necessary period, and distance learning shall be provided meanwhile.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The graduated structure makes escalation the exception: ordinary communicable-disease powers come first, and the emergency regime is reserved for the rare situation they cannot manage. The science provisions make transparency the price of coercion, since published advice and recorded dissent let the public judge whether measures track the evidence. Compensation borrows the old quarantine-compensation tradition and converts it into a general principle of public-health solidarity.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 12.2.6 (Emergencies and Compensation); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 26.1.2 (Approval and Duration).\n"}, {"id": "26-emergency-and-crisis__state-of-emergency", "path": "26-emergency-and-crisis/state-of-emergency.md", "part": "26-emergency-and-crisis", "partTitle": "Emergency And Crisis", "title": "Section 26.1 — State of Emergency", "md": "# Section 26.1 — State of Emergency\n\n> **Part 26 — Emergency and Crisis** · Articles 26.1.1 – 26.1.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs declaration, approval, duration, and review of the state of emergency.*\n\n## Article 26.1.1 — Grounds and Declaration\n\n1. A state of emergency may be declared only where war, armed conflict, widespread public danger, or grave catastrophe threatens the life of the nation and ordinary measures are demonstrably inadequate.\n2. The President shall declare it on the proposal of the Government and with the countersignature of the Prime Minister.\n3. The declaration shall state its grounds, territorial scope, intended measures, and the provisions of ordinary law affected.\n4. The declaration shall be published immediately and communicated to the Parliament, which shall convene without delay.\n\n## Article 26.1.2 — Approval and Duration\n\n1. The declaration lapses thirty days after publication unless the Parliament approves it by a majority of the members of each House.\n2. The Parliament shall vote on the declaration within seven days.\n3. Approval and each renewal may be given for up to thirty days.\n4. The total period shall not exceed six months in any twelve-month period unless war is authorized under Article 26.4.1 (Authorization of War).\n\n## Article 26.1.3 — Proportionate and Reasoned Measures\n\n1. Emergency measures shall be adopted only by a law or a ministerial order founded on a law, confirmed by the Parliament within seven days; the reasons for every measure shall be published upon adoption.\n2. Every measure shall satisfy Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) as the least restrictive means strictly required by the exigencies.\n3. A measure applies only within its stated scope and duration and ceases with its grounds.\n4. Internet access may be restricted only within the exception of Article 17.7.2 (No Internet Shutdowns).\n\n## Article 26.1.4 — Immediate Judicial Review\n\n1. Every declaration and emergency measure is subject to immediate judicial review; no law may exclude or delay it.\n2. Any affected person may apply without charge; the Constitutional Court decides challenges to the declaration with priority.\n3. The courts shall remain open, and their ordinary jurisdiction shall not be curtailed.\n4. A court shall set aside any measure exceeding the grounds or outlasting the danger.\n\n## Article 26.1.5 — Termination\n\n1. The President shall terminate the state of emergency without delay once the grounds have ceased; the Parliament may terminate it at any time.\n2. Upon termination, every emergency measure lapses automatically and ordinary law resumes in full.\n3. Termination shall be published immediately.\n\n## Article 26.1.6 — Reporting and Transparency\n\n1. The Government shall report to the Parliament at intervals not exceeding fourteen days, stating which measures remain in force and why.\n2. A public register of emergency measures, their legal bases, and duration shall be maintained.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The declaration, exigency, and notification requirements restate the derogation standard of Article 4 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights. The six-month ceiling, the open courts, and countersignature treat emergency powers as a loan from ordinary law for which the requesting Government must answer.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 4.8.3 (Compliance with Part 26); Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts).\n"}, {"id": "26-emergency-and-crisis__war-powers", "path": "26-emergency-and-crisis/war-powers.md", "part": "26-emergency-and-crisis", "partTitle": "Emergency And Crisis", "title": "Section 26.4 — War Powers", "md": "# Section 26.4 — War Powers\n\n> **Part 26 — Emergency and Crisis** · Articles 26.4.1 – 26.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section allocates the war powers: the Parliament authorizes war, the President directs operations, and the law of armed conflict protects persons in the Republic's power.*\n\n## Article 26.4.1 — Authorization of War\n\n1. A state of war shall not be declared and war shall not be waged except with the authorization of the Parliament by a majority of the members of each House, consistent with Article 18.1.2 (Prohibition of Wars of Aggression).\n2. The authorization shall state its purpose, legal basis, geographic scope, and the maximum duration of hostilities.\n3. The President directs operations as commander-in-chief within the defense policy established by the Parliament, under Article 18.3.2 (The President as Commander-in-Chief).\n\n## Article 26.4.2 — Major Deployments\n\n1. The armed forces shall be deployed outside the Republic only with the prior authorization of the Parliament under Article 18.3.4 (Deployment Abroad).\n2. A deployment ordered in urgent necessity shall be reported to the Parliament without delay and lapses unless approved within fourteen days.\n3. Deployment within the Republic follows Article 18.2.4 (Internal Deployment).\n\n## Article 26.4.3 — Duration and Termination\n\n1. A war authorization shall be given for a limited period and renewed only by the Parliament.\n2. The Parliament may terminate the state of war at any time by majority vote of the members present in each House.\n3. Upon cessation of hostilities, the Government shall report to the Parliament and the state of war shall end by proclamation.\n4. Derogations from ordinary law during war are admissible only under Section 26.1 and within the limits of Section 26.5.\n\n## Article 26.4.4 — Reporting and Oversight\n\n1. The Government shall report on the conduct of hostilities at regular intervals and immediately upon the Parliament's request.\n2. The standing parliamentary defense committee shall be informed to the fullest extent consistent with the safety of the Republic, under Article 18.3.3 (Parliamentary Defense Committee).\n3. Every deployment decision shall be documented and available to the committee.\n\n## Article 26.4.5 — Protection of Civilians and Prisoners\n\n1. The rules of international law applicable to armed conflict bind the Republic and every member of its armed forces.\n2. The Republic shall protect and treat humanely: civilians; persons hors de combat and prisoners of war; and the wounded and shipwrecked, who shall be collected and cared for.\n3. Grave breaches of the law of armed conflict shall be investigated and prosecuted under the Republic's criminal law.\n4. Independent humanitarian organizations shall be permitted to perform their functions in areas under the Republic's control, under conditions fixed by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Concurrent parliamentary majorities keep the gravest decision a republic takes with its widest mandate, while the commander-in-chief directs operations rather than choosing their purposes. The fourteen-day sunset on urgent deployments ensures necessity never ripens into a standing war power. The humanitarian clauses entrench the Geneva Conventions because the treatment of prisoners and civilians is where the temptation to except oneself from law is strongest.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 18.3.2 (The President as Commander-in-Chief); Article 18.3.4 (Deployment Abroad); Article 19.3.1 (Binding Force of International Law).\n"}, {"id": "27-transparency-and-accountability__asset-disclosures", "path": "27-transparency-and-accountability/asset-disclosures.md", "part": "27-transparency-and-accountability", "partTitle": "Transparency And Accountability", "title": "Section 27.3 — Asset Disclosures", "md": "# Section 27.3 — Asset Disclosures\n\n> **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability** · Articles 27.3.1 – 27.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section requires senior public officials to declare assets, income, and liabilities, makes declarations public with privacy safeguards, and attaches sanctions to false declarations.*\n\n## Article 27.3.1 — Persons and Occasions of Disclosure\n\n1. The following shall declare their assets, income, and liabilities: the President, the Prime Minister, ministers, members of the Parliament, judges, the Public Prosecutor, the Auditor-General, the heads and members of the Part 20 institutions, and such further senior officials as the law designates.\n2. Persons who formally announce candidacy for an office listed in clause 1 shall declare before the election.\n3. A declaration shall be filed on taking office, annually, and on leaving office.\n4. Persons acting in such an office shall declare before acting, under Article 30.3.3 (Vacancies and Acting Capacity).\n\n## Article 27.3.2 — Content and Verification\n\n1. The declaration shall state the declarant's assets, income, and liabilities, and those of spouse and dependent children where the office may affect them, as fixed by law.\n2. The declaration shall record gifts received in connection with the office.\n3. The Anti-Corruption Commission shall verify declarations, cross-check them against tax, land, corporate, and financial records, and audit a proportion each year under Article 20.3.1 (Mandate).\n4. Members of the Parliament declare under the registry of their House, under Article 3.10.2 (Asset Disclosure).\n\n## Article 27.3.3 — Publication and Privacy Safeguards\n\n1. Declarations shall be published in a public electronic registry, searchable free of charge.\n2. Data whose publication would risk the safety or core privacy of the declarant or family, such as residence addresses and security arrangements, shall be withheld from publication but remain available to the verifying authorities.\n3. The Information Commission shall supervise the balance between publication and privacy under Article 17.2.4 (Duties of Controllers).\n\n## Article 27.3.4 — Sanctions\n\n1. A false, incomplete, or late declaration shall attract administrative sanctions fixed by law.\n2. A declaration shown to conceal illicit enrichment shall be reported to the Public Prosecution Service.\n3. A person who fails to file a required declaration shall not take up the office; repeated failure may result in ineligibility for such offices for a period fixed by law.\n\n## Article 27.3.5 — Registry and Retention\n\n1. The Anti-Corruption Commission shall maintain the registry of declarations.\n2. Declarations shall be retained for the period fixed by law after the office ends, to permit post-term verification.\n3. Registry data shall be processed only for verification, publication, and integrity purposes.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Asset disclosure is the cheapest anticorruption instrument available to a republic: it costs little, deters much, and converts unexplained wealth into a provable offense, which is why codes from the United Nations Convention against Corruption onward treat it as core infrastructure. The verification duty separates a real system from a filing ritual, and privacy safeguards are constitutional because family addresses in hostile hands have chilled candid disclosure. Ineligibility attaches to non-filing so that the sanction lands on the career, not merely the purse.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 20.3.1 (Mandate); Article 3.10.2 (Asset Disclosure); Article 17.2.4 (Duties of Controllers).\n"}, {"id": "27-transparency-and-accountability__conflicts-of-interest", "path": "27-transparency-and-accountability/conflicts-of-interest.md", "part": "27-transparency-and-accountability", "partTitle": "Transparency And Accountability", "title": "Section 27.4 — Conflicts of Interest", "md": "# Section 27.4 — Conflicts of Interest\n\n> **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability** · Articles 27.4.1 – 27.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section requires identification and registration of conflicts of interest, recusal from affected decisions, gift discipline, cooling-off periods for the revolving door, and their enforcement.*\n\n## Article 27.4.1 — Identification and Declaration\n\n1. A person exercising public functions shall avoid situations in which a personal interest could improperly influence, or reasonably appear to influence, the exercise of those functions.\n2. A conflict of interest shall be declared without delay to the competent register under Article 27.1.4 (Registers of Interests).\n3. The declaration shall describe the interest with the particularity needed to assess it.\n\n## Article 27.4.2 — Recusal\n\n1. A decision-maker with a declared or known personal interest in a matter shall not participate in its deliberation or decision.\n2. The recusal and its reasons shall be recorded in the decision file, consistent with Article 3.10.3 (Recusal).\n3. A decision taken in breach of recusal may be set aside by a court or by the competent review body.\n\n## Article 27.4.3 — Gifts and Hospitality\n\n1. A person exercising public functions shall not solicit or accept any gift, benefit, or promise connected with those functions beyond a token value fixed by law.\n2. Gifts within the permitted value shall be entered in the register.\n3. A benefit offered by a person seeking a decision from the public authority shall be refused and reported.\n\n## Article 27.4.4 — The Revolving Door\n\n1. A former Prime Minister, minister, or senior official shall not, for eighteen months after leaving office, accept employment, lobby, or act for consideration in matters within the person's former responsibility.\n2. The law may extend the period for particular offices and impose longer incompatibilities on former members of independent institutions, under Article 20.4.2 (Qualifications and Incompatibilities).\n3. During the cooling-off period, the person shall notify the competent authority of any employment offer within the sectors formerly supervised.\n\n## Article 27.4.5 — Enforcement\n\n1. The Anti-Corruption Commission shall monitor compliance and investigate suspected breaches, and the ethics commission under Section 27.6 shall issue advisory opinions on doubtful cases.\n2. Breaches of this Section attract sanctions fixed by law, including fines, recovery of benefits, and exclusion from public functions for a period fixed by law.\n3. Procurement and subsidy decisions affected by an undisclosed conflict shall be reviewed under Article 8.5.3 (Integrity Rules).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The recusal rule protects the decision rather than punishing the person: an interested decision-maker may be honest, but the public cannot verify honesty from outside, so appearance is part of the harm, following the OECD standards for managing conflict of interest. The revolving-door clause is drafted narrowly, a fixed eighteen-month period with a subject-matter limit, because rules that are too broad strip retiring officials of their livelihoods and chill public service. The notification duty lets the authority police the boundary without prohibiting it, and reviewable procurement decisions close the loop between integrity rules and spent money.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 3.10.3 (Recusal); Article 8.5.3 (Integrity Rules); Article 20.4.2 (Qualifications and Incompatibilities).\n"}, {"id": "27-transparency-and-accountability__freedom-of-information", "path": "27-transparency-and-accountability/freedom-of-information.md", "part": "27-transparency-and-accountability", "partTitle": "Transparency And Accountability", "title": "Section 27.2 — Freedom of Information", "md": "# Section 27.2 — Freedom of Information\n\n> **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability** · Articles 27.2.1 – 27.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section fixes the right-of-access procedure: requests, timelines, narrow exhaustive exemptions, and Information Commission review.*\n\n## Article 27.2.1 — Requests and Timelines\n\n1. Any person may request information held by a public authority by any suitable means, without stating reasons, under Article 21.3.1 (Right of Access).\n2. The authority shall acknowledge the request promptly and decide it within twenty days.\n3. A single reasoned extension of up to ten days is permissible for requests of exceptional volume or complexity.\n4. A request concerning an imminent threat to life or liberty shall be decided within forty-eight hours.\n\n## Article 27.2.2 — Exhaustive Exemptions\n\n1. Access may be refused only for the exemptions enumerated by law, and the list is exhaustive: protection of personal data; public security and defense; international relations; the prevention, investigation, and prosecution of offenses; third-party commercial confidentiality; and the internal deliberative space of the Cabinet.\n2. Exemptions shall be construed narrowly, require demonstrated risk of concrete harm, and lapse when the harm is no longer probable.\n3. Information shall be disclosed notwithstanding an exemption where the public interest clearly outweighs the harm, save where an Act precludes disclosure of that class of information.\n\n## Article 27.2.3 — Partial Disclosure and Transfer\n\n1. Where part of a record falls within an exemption, the remainder shall be disclosed.\n2. A request within the competence of another authority shall be transferred without delay, and the applicant informed within seven days.\n\n## Article 27.2.4 — Review by the Information Commission\n\n1. A refusal, partial refusal, or failure to decide in time may be brought before the Information Commission by the requester.\n2. The Commission shall examine the matter under Article 20.7.2 (Complaint Handling) and may order disclosure under Article 20.7.3 (Orders and Fines).\n3. An order of the Commission, and a refusal to order disclosure, may be appealed to a court.\n4. The Commission shall publish anonymized decisions clarifying the application of the exemptions.\n\n## Article 27.2.5 — Assistance and Fees\n\n1. Public authorities shall assist requesters, in particular persons with disabilities, persons who are illiterate, and persons lacking command of the official languages, under Article 5.7.5 (Interpretation and Accessibility).\n2. Fees shall not exceed the reasonable cost of reproduction; the decision itself is free.\n3. Requests shall not be refused because of their number alone; abusive volumes are managed by reasonable rules fixed by law.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*A short, exhaustive exemption list with a narrow construction most reliably distinguishes genuine secrecy regimes from convenient ones; open-ended exceptions are where freedom-of-information laws go to die. The public-interest override follows the strongest international formulations of the right of access, while the save for statutory secrecy classes respects genuine vetting and investigation confidentiality. Delegating review to the Information Commission, which also supervises data protection, balances the two regimes trading in the same material, and fixed timelines with an expedited life-and-liberty track convert the right into working procedure.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 20.7.2 (Complaint Handling); Article 20.7.3 (Orders and Fines); Article 21.3.1 (Right of Access).\n"}, {"id": "27-transparency-and-accountability__government-ethics", "path": "27-transparency-and-accountability/government-ethics.md", "part": "27-transparency-and-accountability", "partTitle": "Transparency And Accountability", "title": "Section 27.6 — Government Ethics", "md": "# Section 27.6 — Government Ethics\n\n> **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability** · Articles 27.6.1 – 27.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section completes the integrity framework with codes of conduct, ethics training, an independent ethics commission, and a public register of lobbyists.*\n\n## Article 27.6.1 — Codes of Conduct\n\n1. The Parliament shall adopt by law codes of conduct for persons exercising public functions, including ministers, civil servants, and local government members.\n2. Each House of the Parliament enforces the code for its members under Article 3.10.1 (Duty of Integrity).\n3. The codes shall give practical expression to the general duties of public authorities under Article 29.2.1 (General Duties of Public Authorities) and to this Part's integrity rules.\n\n## Article 27.6.2 — Ethics Training\n\n1. A person taking up a public function to which a code applies shall complete training on it within the period fixed by law.\n2. Training shall be repeated periodically and shall include the handling of real conflict-of-interest cases.\n3. Public authorities shall advise their staff on applying the codes in individual situations.\n\n## Article 27.6.3 — The Independent Ethics Commission\n\n1. An Act shall establish an independent ethics commission to advise, monitor, and enforce the codes of conduct.\n2. Commission members shall be appointed for fixed terms through an open process and removed only for cause by a qualified majority of the Parliament.\n3. The commission shall issue advisory opinions on doubtful cases, publish them in anonymized form, and refer suspected offenses to the Anti-Corruption Commission under Article 20.3.1 (Mandate).\n4. The commission shall report annually to the Parliament on the state of public ethics.\n\n## Article 27.6.4 — Register of Lobbyists\n\n1. An Act shall establish a public register of persons who lobby decision-makers of the Republic professionally.\n2. Registered lobbyists shall disclose their clients, the matters lobbied, and the organs approached at intervals fixed by law.\n3. Decision-makers shall not meet an unregistered professional lobbyist on matters of legislation or public expenditure.\n4. The register and disclosures shall be published in open data format under Article 27.1.2 (Proactive Publication).\n\n## Article 27.6.5 — Advisory Opinions and Sanctions\n\n1. Any person bound by a code may seek an advisory opinion from the ethics commission; a person acting in reliance on an opinion acts in good faith.\n2. Breaches of a code attract graduated sanctions fixed by law, from reprimand to exclusion from public functions.\n3. Sanctions are subject to judicial review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Codes, training, and an advisory organ convert integrity from a private virtue into a working system that a new official can follow, an approach refined in the public-service ethics regimes of the OECD states. The ethics commission is deliberately advisory in the first instance: cheap, fast, publishable advice prevents most breaches, while prosecution remains with the bodies of Part 20. The lobbyist register completes the picture, because influence flows through intermediaries as much as through bribes, and the meeting bar gives the register teeth without banning the profession. Anonymized opinions let the rules grow case law that every office can consult.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 29.2.1 (General Duties of Public Authorities); Article 4.5.3 (Integrity Codes); Article 3.10.1 (Duty of Integrity).\n"}, {"id": "27-transparency-and-accountability__transparency", "path": "27-transparency-and-accountability/transparency.md", "part": "27-transparency-and-accountability", "partTitle": "Transparency And Accountability", "title": "Section 27.1 — Transparency", "md": "# Section 27.1 — Transparency\n\n> **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability** · Articles 27.1.1 – 27.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section makes openness the default posture of the State: proactive publication, public registers, and narrow limits on disclosure justified by the authority that withholds.*\n\n## Article 27.1.1 — Openness by Default\n\n1. Information held by a public authority is open to the public unless a limitation is justified under Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) or under the specific exemptions of Section 27.2.\n2. The authority that withholds information bears the burden of justifying the withholding.\n3. Where an exemption applies only to part of a document, the remainder shall be disclosed.\n\n## Article 27.1.2 — Proactive Publication\n\n1. Public authorities shall publish, without awaiting a request: laws and regulations; budgets and execution data; contracts and their beneficiaries; the reasons for administrative decisions; and the registers maintained under this Part.\n2. Publication shall be in open, machine-readable formats that permit reuse, consistent with Article 21.3.2 (Open-Data Default).\n3. The categories of information subject to proactive publication shall be specified by law and reviewed periodically for adequacy.\n\n## Article 27.1.3 — Limits on Openness\n\n1. Openness may be limited only where necessary to protect privacy, public security, or another legitimate aim recognized by law, and only so long as necessary.\n2. A limitation shall be reasoned, shall be the least restrictive available, and shall be reviewed whenever its grounds change.\n3. The identity of officials and of decision-makers shall not be withheld as a matter of course.\n\n## Article 27.1.4 — Registers of Interests\n\n1. Public authorities shall maintain public registers of the declared interests of their office holders and decision-makers.\n2. The registers shall record declared interests, recusals, and the treatment of gifts under Section 27.4.\n3. A person who fails to declare a material interest shall be liable to the sanctions of Section 27.3 and Section 27.4.\n\n## Article 27.1.5 — Timeliness, Formats, and Records\n\n1. Proactively published information shall be current, and a delayed publication shall be reasoned.\n2. Information shall be provided free of charge or at no more than the reasonable cost of reproduction.\n3. Public authorities shall maintain records of their activities sufficient to make transparency effective, consistent with Article 21.3.4 (Records and Documentation).\n4. Personal data published or released shall be processed consistently with Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Openness by default reverses the historical default of administration, which was secrecy until someone demonstrated a right to know; the burden of justification placed on the withholding authority is the operational heart of the reversal. The proactive publication list is drafted to include contracts and their beneficiaries because procurement is where public money most often meets private interests quietly, a lesson emphasized by open-government practice since the adoption of the first freedom-of-information statute in Sweden in 1766. Registers of interests tie this Section to the integrity regimes of Sections 27.3 and 27.4 so that disclosure is not merely available but located. The records duty closes the loophole by which a right of access survives while the files quietly disappear.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 21.3.1 (Right of Access); Article 21.3.2 (Open-Data Default); Article 8.6.4 (Publication of Execution Data).\n"}, {"id": "27-transparency-and-accountability__whistleblower-protection", "path": "27-transparency-and-accountability/whistleblower-protection.md", "part": "27-transparency-and-accountability", "partTitle": "Transparency And Accountability", "title": "Section 27.5 — Whistleblower Protection", "md": "# Section 27.5 — Whistleblower Protection\n\n> **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability** · Articles 27.5.1 – 27.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects persons who report serious wrongdoing: protected disclosures, safe channels, prohibition of retaliation, confidentiality, and oversight of the channels.*\n\n## Article 27.5.1 — Protected Disclosures\n\n1. Any person may report, to a competent authority or to the public, serious wrongdoing known to the person in a work-related or official context: corruption and illicit enrichment; abuse of public power; violations of rights guaranteed by this Constitution; dangers to public health, safety, or the environment; obstruction of justice; and the concealment of any of these.\n2. A disclosure in good faith and on reasonable grounds is protected under this Section.\n3. A disclosure the person knows to be false receives no protection.\n\n## Article 27.5.2 — Channels\n\n1. Public and private employers shall maintain internal reporting channels, and an Act shall establish an independent external channel for reports internal channels cannot safely receive.\n2. Reports concerning bodies exercising national security functions shall be made through the secure channel of Article 18.5.4 (Protected Disclosure Channel).\n3. A reporter may choose the safest channel; internal reporting shall not be a condition of external reporting.\n\n## Article 27.5.3 — Protection from Retaliation\n\n1. Dismissal, demotion, harassment, exclusion, blacklisting, or any other detriment because of a protected disclosure is prohibited and void.\n2. A person subjected to retaliation is entitled to reinstatement and full compensation for the harm.\n3. In proceedings following a protected disclosure, the employer or authority bears the burden of proving that a measure was taken for reasons unrelated to the disclosure.\n\n## Article 27.5.4 — Confidentiality and Anonymity\n\n1. The identity of a reporter remains confidential unless the reporter consents to disclosure.\n2. Channels shall accept anonymous reports where the law so provides and assess them on their content.\n3. Identity may be revealed only by court order in proceedings where disclosure is strictly necessary for a fair trial, after the reporter has been heard.\n\n## Article 27.5.5 — Oversight of Channel Integrity\n\n1. The Ombudsman shall monitor the functioning and safety of the channels and report publicly on their integrity, under Article 20.1.1 (Mandate).\n2. Obstruction of a channel or an attempt to identify a reporter is sanctioned by law.\n3. The Ombudsman may refer channel shortcomings to the competent authority and shall publish the response.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Whistleblowing is the reporting line of last resort: it surfaces what registers, audits, and inspections miss, which is why standards from the Council of Europe to the European Union treat reporter protection as an integrity system. Protection turns on good faith and reasonable grounds rather than being right, because demanding accuracy would silence the uncertain insiders who see most. The choice-of-channel rule and the burden reversal answer the two classic failures: forcing reporters back into the hierarchy they accuse, and formally neutral dismissals that are retaliation in fact. Placing oversight with the Ombudsman keeps the guardian independent of every body it watches, and the channel of Section 18.5 keeps the security sector inside the protection system.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 18.5.4 (Protected Disclosure Channel); Article 20.1.1 (Mandate); Article 20.3.1 (Mandate).\n"}, {"id": "28-special-populations__children", "path": "28-special-populations/children.md", "part": "28-special-populations", "partTitle": "Special Populations", "title": "Section 28.1 — Children", "md": "# Section 28.1 — Children\n\n> **Part 28 — Special Populations** · Articles 28.1.1 – 28.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states cross-cutting duties of all institutions toward children: best interests, identity and family care, participation, and protection in institutional and digital settings.*\n\n## Article 28.1.1 — Priority of the Child's Best Interests\n\n1. Every organ of the State and every institution exercising public functions shall treat the child's best interests as a primary consideration in every decision affecting a child, in legislation, budgeting, administration, and adjudication alike, under Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child).\n2. The assessment of the child's best interests shall be documented in decisions of significant effect.\n\n## Article 28.1.2 — Identity, Family, and Care\n\n1. Public authorities shall secure birth registration, a name, and a nationality for every child born within the Republic's jurisdiction.\n2. Every child has the right to family care; separation shall occur only as a last resort, under the protection services of Section 22.7 (Child Protection).\n3. Where necessary, alternative care shall be consistent with the child's dignity and development, under Article 1.15.5 (Non-Derogable Essentials).\n\n## Article 28.1.3 — Participation\n\n1. A child capable of forming views shall be heard, directly or through a representative, in every judicial and administrative proceeding affecting the child, under Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children).\n2. Information relevant to a decision affecting a child shall be provided in a form the child understands.\n3. Public authorities shall give due weight to children's views in planning services for children, including schools and care facilities.\n\n## Article 28.1.4 — The Duty of All Institutions\n\n1. Every public institution shall prioritize children's rights in its planning, budgeting, staffing, and service design.\n2. Laws and major policies affecting children shall be assessed for their impact before adoption.\n3. Officials working with children shall be trained in children's rights and screened for suitability.\n4. Article 16.5.4 (Voice and Welfare of the Child) is respected in every proceeding concerning a child.\n\n## Article 28.1.5 — Protection in Institutional and Digital Settings\n\n1. Children in schools, care facilities, detention, and health institutions enjoy special protection against violence, exploitation, and degrading treatment, under Article 1.15.4 (Protection from Violence and Exploitation).\n2. Digital environments and services offered to children shall apply safety-by-design standards consistent with Part 17 (Digital Law).\n3. Children's institutions shall undergo regular independent inspection, and the Ombudsman shall be accessible to every child without charge.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section translates the rights of Section 1.15 into duties of every institution, because a child meets the State through schools, clinics, courts, and agencies, each controlling one small part of the child's world. The documentation duty in the best-interests assessment follows the Convention on the Rights of the Child practice of making the standard auditable rather than rhetorical. Impact assessment before adoption borrows the method used for environmental and fiscal policy and applies it to the population least able to lobby, and the Ombudsman's accessibility reflects the finding that children rarely complain unless the mechanism reaches out to them.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.15.2 (Best Interests of the Child); Article 1.15.3 (Participation of Children); Article 16.5.4 (Voice and Welfare of the Child).\n"}, {"id": "28-special-populations__elderly", "path": "28-special-populations/elderly.md", "part": "28-special-populations", "partTitle": "Special Populations", "title": "Section 28.2 — The Elderly", "md": "# Section 28.2 — The Elderly\n\n> **Part 28 — Special Populations** · Articles 28.2.1 – 28.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states the duties of all institutions toward older persons: dignity and autonomy in aging, participation, protection from abuse and neglect, adequate income and services, and respect for advance directives.*\n\n## Article 28.2.1 — Dignity in Aging\n\n1. Every older person has the right to age in dignity, with the fullest possible autonomy and independence.\n2. Age shall not be a ground of unequal treatment inconsistent with Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n3. Public services shall be designed so that older persons can use them without dependence on third parties, unless the person chooses assistance.\n\n## Article 28.2.2 — Participation\n\n1. Older persons shall be enabled to participate in the social, cultural, economic, and political life of the Republic.\n2. Public authorities shall consult the organizations of older persons on policies and services that affect them, including long-term care and pension design.\n3. Segregation of older persons by age alone shall not be imposed by public bodies.\n\n## Article 28.2.3 — Protection from Abuse and Neglect\n\n1. The State shall protect older persons from physical, psychological, and financial abuse, and from neglect, whether in their homes or in institutions.\n2. Persons and bodies in contact with older persons shall report suspected abuse to the competent authority as the law provides.\n3. Support to manage the affairs of an older person shall follow the supported decision-making model of Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making) before any substitute measure is considered.\n\n## Article 28.2.4 — Income and Services\n\n1. The pension system shall prevent old-age poverty, consistent with Article 22.2.5 (Prevention of Old-Age Poverty).\n2. Long-term care of adequate quality shall be available under Section 22.6 (Elder Care), with standards of staffing, dignity, and inspection fixed by law.\n3. Care in institutions shall respect the private life, correspondence, and personal choices of the persons living there.\n\n## Article 28.2.5 — Autonomy and Advance Directives\n\n1. The free and informed consent of the person is required for medical treatment, consistent with Article 12.7.2 (Consent and Support).\n2. An advance directive expressing an older person's wishes regarding future care shall be respected within the limits fixed by law.\n3. A person appointed to assist under an advance directive shall act in the person's interest and shall be subject to review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The constitutional position of older persons is often left to pensions law alone; this Section instead frames aging as a matter of dignity, autonomy, and participation, following the direction of the international human-rights work on the rights of older persons. The financial-abuse clause addresses the most common and least prosecuted form of elder abuse, typically committed by persons closest to the victim, which is why the reporting duty extends to private bodies in regular contact. Supported decision-making is placed ahead of guardianship to keep the default of Section 16.8 intact even where capacity fluctuates. The advance-directive clause gives practical effect to autonomy at the stage of life when autonomy is most easily lost.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 22.2.5 (Prevention of Old-Age Poverty); Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making); Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n"}, {"id": "28-special-populations__indigenous-peoples", "path": "28-special-populations/indigenous-peoples.md", "part": "28-special-populations", "partTitle": "Special Populations", "title": "Section 28.5 — Indigenous Peoples", "md": "# Section 28.5 — Indigenous Peoples\n\n> **Part 28 — Special Populations** · Articles 28.5.1 – 28.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section recognizes indigenous peoples and their rights: identity and institutions, lands and resources, free, prior, and informed consent, cultural heritage, and participation.*\n\n## Article 28.5.1 — Recognition\n\n1. The Republic recognizes the indigenous peoples within its territory, their distinct identities, cultures, languages, and traditional institutions.\n2. Forced assimilation and the destruction of indigenous culture are prohibited.\n3. Indigenous peoples shall not be displaced from their territories except under Article 28.5.2 and with full respect for their rights.\n\n## Article 28.5.2 — Lands, Territories, and Resources\n\n1. The lands, territories, and resources that indigenous peoples have traditionally owned, occupied, or used shall be recognized and protected, with secure tenure as the law provides.\n2. The State shall share fairly the benefits arising from the use of natural resources on indigenous lands, and shall respect Article 0.2.5 (Intergenerational Responsibility) in their management.\n3. Relocation of indigenous peoples requires their free, prior, and informed consent; where exceptionally given, it entitles them to compensation and return when the grounds for relocation cease.\n\n## Article 28.5.3 — Consultation and Consent\n\n1. The State shall consult indigenous peoples in good faith, through their representative institutions, on legislative and administrative measures that may affect them, before adoption.\n2. Measures or projects substantially affecting indigenous lands, territories, or resources may proceed only with the free, prior, and informed consent of the peoples concerned, given without coercion and before authorization.\n3. Consultation procedures shall be fixed by law, shall be accessible, and shall record how the outcome shaped the decision.\n\n## Article 28.5.4 — Cultural Heritage\n\n1. The languages, oral traditions, sacred sites, ceremonies, and traditional knowledge of indigenous peoples shall be protected by law.\n2. Traditional knowledge shall not be used or commercialized without the consent of the people concerned; benefit-sharing follows Article 9.9.5 (The Public Domain) where protection expires.\n3. Sacred sites shall be accessible to the communities concerned for their ceremonies.\n\n## Article 28.5.5 — Participation and Institutions\n\n1. Indigenous peoples may maintain and develop their own representative institutions and may participate in public decision-making through them.\n2. The law may reserve representation for indigenous peoples in advisory bodies and provide for their participation in managing protected areas and natural resources.\n3. Public authorities shall consult indigenous peoples on education and health policies affecting them.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The free, prior, and informed consent standard is drawn from the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples and ILO Convention No. 169, and this Section adopts its strong form: consent, not mere consultation, for measures touching lands and resources, the bases of indigenous self-perpetuation. Making consultation precede authorization prevents decisions being announced and then defended. The benefit-sharing and relocation clauses answer the two deepest historical grievances, resource extraction without return and removal without recourse, with structural rather than remedial rules, while recognition of traditional institutions respects the plurality of the Republic.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.8.2 (Expropriation); Article 19.3.1 (Binding Force of International Law); Article 0.2.5 (Intergenerational Responsibility).\n"}, {"id": "28-special-populations__minorities", "path": "28-special-populations/minorities.md", "part": "28-special-populations", "partTitle": "Special Populations", "title": "Section 28.4 — Minorities", "md": "# Section 28.4 — Minorities\n\n> **Part 28 — Special Populations** · Articles 28.4.1 – 28.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section protects cultural, religious, and linguistic minorities: identity, equality with special measures, effective participation, minority-language services, and conditions for identity to flourish.*\n\n## Article 28.4.1 — Protection of Identity\n\n1. Persons belonging to a cultural, religious, or linguistic minority shall not be denied the right, individually or in community with others, to enjoy their own culture, to profess and practice their own religion, and to use their own language.\n2. Forced assimilation is prohibited; no organ of the State shall adopt measures aimed at destroying or suppressing a minority's identity.\n3. Minorities may establish and maintain their own institutions, associations, and media as the law provides.\n\n## Article 28.4.2 — Equality and Special Measures\n\n1. Persons belonging to minorities enjoy full equality under Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n2. Special measures to secure effective equality are admissible under Article 1.2.5 (Affirmative Measures) and are not discrimination.\n3. The allocation of public support to minority cultural activities shall follow transparent, non-discriminatory criteria.\n\n## Article 28.4.3 — Effective Participation\n\n1. Public authorities shall create conditions for persons belonging to minorities to participate effectively in decisions concerning them, in cultural, religious, economic, and public life.\n2. Consultation procedures for measures substantially affecting a minority shall be fixed by law and conducted in good faith.\n3. The law may provide for representation of minorities in advisory and public bodies.\n\n## Article 28.4.4 — Minority-Language Services\n\n1. In areas where a minority language is used by a significant number of inhabitants, public authorities shall provide, as fixed by law: services in that language, official documents on request, and use of the language before courts and administrative organs.\n2. Education in minority languages shall be provided under Article 11.1.4 (Instruction in Minority Languages).\n3. No person shall be disadvantaged for using their language with public authorities.\n\n## Article 28.4.5 — Conditions for Identity\n\n1. Public authorities shall promote the conditions for persons belonging to minorities to maintain and develop their culture, language, and traditions.\n2. Research and data on minorities shall be collected with the safeguards of Section 28.6 and respect the right to self-identification.\n3. The protection of this Section extends to religious and linguistic communities that are not territorially concentrated.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*The core formulation restates Article 27 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, whose individual-and-community formula allows members of minorities to enjoy culture in community without creating corporate privileges, and adds the negative guarantee that European minority instruments made explicit: no forced assimilation. The language-services clause is a demand-driven duty, fixed by law and scaled to significant use, because rigid territorial language rules can harden boundaries that effective participation is meant to soften. Consultation in good faith borrows the standard of Section 28.5, graduated to the lighter weight due to measures that affect minorities without dispossessing them, and self-identification in data collection follows the census practice that identity is reported, not assigned.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.2.4 (Protection of Minorities); Article 11.1.4 (Instruction in Minority Languages); Article 1.2.5 (Affirmative Measures).\n"}, {"id": "28-special-populations__persons-with-disabilities", "path": "28-special-populations/persons-with-disabilities.md", "part": "28-special-populations", "partTitle": "Special Populations", "title": "Section 28.3 — Persons with Disabilities", "md": "# Section 28.3 — Persons with Disabilities\n\n> **Part 28 — Special Populations** · Articles 28.3.1 – 28.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section states the duties of all institutions toward persons with disabilities: full equality, legal capacity, accessibility as a precondition, the reasonable-accommodation duty, and liberty and integrity.*\n\n## Article 28.3.1 — Full Equality and Inclusion\n\n1. Persons with disabilities enjoy every right of this Constitution without discrimination and have the right to inclusion in the life of the community.\n2. Special measures to accelerate equality are admissible under Article 1.2.5 (Affirmative Measures) and are not discrimination.\n3. Public authorities shall involve persons with disabilities and their representative organizations in the development of policies that affect them.\n\n## Article 28.3.2 — Legal Capacity\n\n1. Persons with disabilities enjoy legal capacity on an equal basis with others in all aspects of life.\n2. Where a person requires assistance to exercise capacity, supported decision-making under Article 16.8.2 (Supported Decision-Making) shall be provided before any substitute measure is considered.\n3. A substitute measure shall be proportionate, time-limited, and subject to periodic review by a court or independent body.\n\n## Article 28.3.3 — Accessibility\n\n1. The built environment, transport, information, communication, and public and private services open to the public shall be accessible to persons with disabilities, applying universal-design standards fixed by law.\n2. Accessibility is a precondition of equality; a service inaccessible without justification is a service denied.\n3. New public buildings, infrastructure, and digital services shall comply with the accessibility standards from their entry into service.\n\n## Article 28.3.4 — Reasonable Accommodation\n\n1. Public authorities and providers of goods and services available to the public shall provide reasonable accommodation to persons with disabilities, unless the accommodation would impose a disproportionate or undue burden.\n2. The denial of reasonable accommodation constitutes discrimination.\n3. The burden of demonstrating that an accommodation is disproportionate lies on the person or body refusing it.\n\n## Article 28.3.5 — Liberty and Integrity\n\n1. No person shall be deprived of liberty on the ground of disability alone.\n2. Medical treatment requires the free and informed consent of the person; where consent cannot be given, supported consent shall be sought before a substitute decision, consistent with Article 12.7.3 (Least-Restrictive Care).\n3. Any measure restricting the liberty or integrity of a person with disabilities shall be the least restrictive available, time-limited, and subject to prompt review.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*This Section follows the paradigm shift of the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, which relocated disability from an object of charity to a question of equality and access, and it borrows that treaty's strongest tools. Making accessibility a precondition of equality and denial of accommodation a form of discrimination gives the two duties teeth: one applies before the fact through design standards, the other after the fact through courts. The legal-capacity clause ends the era in which a diagnosis could erase legal personhood, aligning this Section with the guardianship reform of Section 16.8. The liberty guarantee responds to the historical confinement of persons with disabilities on grounds of status rather than conduct, which modern review treats as among the most serious rights violations.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 22.3.2 (Reasonable Accommodation); Article 22.3.3 (Accessibility and Universal Design); Article 16.8.1 (Presumption of Capacity).\n"}, {"id": "28-special-populations__vulnerable-groups", "path": "28-special-populations/vulnerable-groups.md", "part": "28-special-populations", "partTitle": "Special Populations", "title": "Section 28.6 — Vulnerable Groups", "md": "# Section 28.6 — Vulnerable Groups\n\n> **Part 28 — Special Populations** · Articles 28.6.1 – 28.6.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section addresses intersectional disadvantage: recognition of compound and multiple discrimination, tailored measures, disaggregated data for policy, and impact assessment with consultation.*\n\n## Article 28.6.1 — Intersectional Disadvantage\n\n1. The State shall recognize that disadvantage may arise from the combination of grounds, such as sex, disability, age, ethnic origin, migration status, or poverty, and shall address that compound disadvantage in its policies.\n2. The recognition of intersectional disadvantage under Article 1.2.3 (Intersectional Discrimination) applies to the design and evaluation of all public services.\n3. Measures for vulnerable groups shall be designed with the participation of the groups concerned.\n\n## Article 28.6.2 — Multiple Discrimination\n\n1. A claim of discrimination may be based on the combination of two or more grounds.\n2. Remedies shall reflect the cumulative harm of multiple discrimination rather than averaging its components.\n3. Courts and equality bodies shall develop the doctrine of combined grounds consistently with Article 1.2.2 (Prohibition of Discrimination).\n\n## Article 28.6.3 — Tailored Measures\n\n1. Public authorities shall adopt targeted programs for groups facing intersectional disadvantage, including outreach, support services, and priority of access where justified.\n2. Special measures shall be proportionate to the disadvantage addressed and shall be reviewed for necessity.\n3. The minimum income strategy under Article 22.5.1 (National Poverty Strategy) shall take account of groups facing compound disadvantage.\n\n## Article 28.6.4 — Disaggregated Data\n\n1. Official statistics relevant to equality shall be disaggregated by the grounds of disadvantage, within the safeguards for statistical confidentiality of Article 20.6.3 (Confidentiality of Statistical Data).\n2. The Statistics Authority shall coordinate the disaggregation standards across public bodies under Article 20.6.4 (Coordination of State Data Collection).\n3. Individual data shall not be disaggregated in a form that permits identification, and respondents' self-identification shall govern.\n\n## Article 28.6.5 — Assessment and Consultation\n\n1. Laws and major policies with likely effects on vulnerable groups shall be assessed for those effects before adoption and evaluated after implementation.\n2. Consultation shall reach the groups affected through their organizations and in accessible formats.\n3. The results of assessments shall be published, and the reasoning for departing from their findings shall be stated.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Intersectionality names a real phenomenon that single-ground equality law misses: the person disadvantaged as a woman, as a member of a minority, and as a person with disabilities is not thrice but differently disadvantaged. Allowing combined-ground claims follows the development of equality doctrine in several constitutional courts that abandoned additive models of discrimination. The data provisions are the quiet engine of the Section, since policy for compound disadvantage can only be as good as the statistics that reveal it, provided the firewall of Section 20.6 holds. Requiring assessment before adoption and evaluation after implementation converts good intentions into a reviewable record, the same discipline this Constitution applies to children in Section 28.1.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.2.3 (Intersectional Discrimination); Article 20.6.4 (Coordination of State Data Collection); Article 22.5.1 (National Poverty Strategy).\n"}, {"id": "29-institutions__constitutional-bodies", "path": "29-institutions/constitutional-bodies.md", "part": "29-institutions", "partTitle": "Institutions", "title": "Section 29.1 — Constitutional Bodies", "md": "# Section 29.1 — Constitutional Bodies\n\n> **Part 29 — Public Institutions** · Articles 29.1.1 – 29.1.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section defines the constitutional bodies of the Republic, reserves their creation and abolition to constitutional amendment, and protects their independence and funding.*\n\n## Article 29.1.1 — Definition and Enumeration\n\n1. Constitutional bodies are the organs established directly by this Constitution: the Parliament and its Houses; the President; the Government and the Cabinet; the courts; the Public Prosecution Service; the independent institutions of Part 20; the Provincial Councils and municipal organs of Part 07; and such other bodies as this Constitution establishes.\n2. A body listed in clause 1 may be created, abolished, merged, or reconstituted only by amendment of this Constitution under Part 25 (Constitutional Process).\n\n## Article 29.1.2 — Protection of Status\n\n1. No Act may abolish a constitutional body, transfer its essential functions to a non-constitutional body, or dissolve it into an ordinary agency.\n2. A change in the name, seat, or internal organization of a constitutional body shall not affect its status or independence.\n\n## Article 29.1.3 — Funding Floors\n\n1. The appropriations of constitutional bodies shall not be reduced below the level necessary for the independent performance of their functions.\n2. A proposal to reduce the appropriation of an independent institution shall be reasoned in writing and shall state the effects on its mandate.\n3. Where a constitutional body considers its funding inadequate for independence, it shall state so publicly in its annual report.\n\n## Article 29.1.4 — Preservation of Independence Guarantees\n\n1. Statutory reorganization may not dilute the guarantees of independence that this Constitution attaches to a constitutional body.\n2. A court reviewing the legality of a reorganization shall give determinative weight to the purpose of the guarantees, consistent with Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions).\n\n## Article 29.1.5 — Analogy for Newly Established Bodies\n\n1. A body established by amendment acquires the status, funding protection, and independence guarantees appropriate to its function, as fixed by the amending text.\n2. Until the amending text provides otherwise, the funding floor of Article 29.1.3 applies to the new body.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Constitutional bodies are the load-bearing walls of the constitutional order, and this Section does for institutions what entrenchment clauses do for rights: it removes them from the reach of transient majorities. The funding floor addresses the modern, quiet form of abolition, starving an institution through the budget rather than repealing it, a risk the Auditor-General's budget independence in Article 20.5.2 was the first to answer. Allowing name and internal organization to change by statute keeps the rule from fossilizing administration, while the essential-functions test keeps the change from doing by surgery what abolition would do by axe. The analogy clause for newly established bodies lets future amendments create new constitutional bodies without drafting each funding guarantee from scratch.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.5.4 (Independent Institutions); Article 20.5.2 (Operational and Budgetary Independence); Article 5.1.4 (Financial Autonomy).\n"}, {"id": "29-institutions__independent-agencies", "path": "29-institutions/independent-agencies.md", "part": "29-institutions", "partTitle": "Institutions", "title": "Section 29.5 — Independent Agencies", "md": "# Section 29.5 — Independent Agencies\n\n> **Part 29 — Public Institutions** · Articles 29.5.1 – 29.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section sets the standards for independent agencies of the administration: establishment by law with a clear mandate, sunset and evaluation, accountability to the Parliament, merit staffing, and limits on delegation.*\n\n## Article 29.5.1 — Establishment by Law\n\n1. An independent agency may be created only by an Act that states its mandate, its functions, its funding, and the accountability attaching to it.\n2. An agency shall be established only where a demonstrable public purpose is not better served by an existing authority, consistent with Article 29.3.1 (Creation by Law).\n3. The mandate of an agency shall be interpreted strictly; powers beyond it may not be presumed.\n\n## Article 29.5.2 — Sunset and Evaluation\n\n1. Every agency shall be evaluated by the Parliament at intervals not exceeding ten years, on the basis of a published evaluation of its performance.\n2. The continuation of an agency requires an affirmative decision of the Parliament; an agency whose evaluation shows that its purpose has lapsed shall be wound up.\n3. The evaluation shall consider necessity, effectiveness, cost, and the effects on citizens and businesses.\n\n## Article 29.5.3 — Accountability to the Parliament\n\n1. An agency shall report annually to the Parliament on its activities and shall make its reports public.\n2. The responsible committee may require reports, documents, and the attendance of the agency's head, consistent with Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny).\n3. An agency shall answer questions and requests for information without delay.\n\n## Article 29.5.4 — Merit Staffing\n\n1. The staff of an agency shall be recruited by open competition on merit, consistent with Article 4.5.1 (Merit Recruitment).\n2. Staff shall observe the political neutrality and integrity rules applicable to the civil service.\n3. Recruitment shall include screening for conflicts of interest under Section 27.4.\n\n## Article 29.5.5 — Limits on Delegation\n\n1. An agency may delegate functions only as provided by law, within defined limits and under written conditions.\n2. Essential functions, including final decision-making on individual cases, shall not be delegated outside the agency.\n3. The responsible minister remains accountable to the Parliament for the agency's compliance with law, consistent with Article 0.5.5 (Limits of Delegation).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Agencies are created to be technically insulated, which is exactly why they need the strongest accountability design: the sunset evaluation forces the Parliament to re-purchase each agency's existence with evidence rather than inherit it by inertia, a discipline borrowed from sunset statutes and periodic agency reviews in several administrative systems. The strict-mandate rule protects the citizen from the slow accretion of unrequested power, the standard pathology of expert bodies. Merit staffing ties agencies to the civil service's integrity culture while the conflict screening connects them to the integrity regime of Part 27. The delegation limits complete the design: insulation from politics must not become insulation from law, and the minister's residual accountability keeps a named person answerable for every agency's legality.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.5.5 (Limits of Delegation); Article 4.5.1 (Merit Recruitment); Article 4.7.2 (Committee Scrutiny).\n"}, {"id": "29-institutions__public-authorities", "path": "29-institutions/public-authorities.md", "part": "29-institutions", "partTitle": "Institutions", "title": "Section 29.2 — Public Authorities", "md": "# Section 29.2 — Public Authorities\n\n> **Part 29 — Public Institutions** · Articles 29.2.1 – 29.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section imposes the general duties binding every public authority: legality, impartiality, proportionality, a service mandate, remedies for the individual, an administrative procedure act, and State liability.*\n\n## Article 29.2.1 — General Duties of Public Authorities\n\n1. A public authority shall act only on a legal basis and within the limits of its competence, consistent with Article 0.4.1 (Legality).\n2. A public authority shall treat every person equally and impartially, decide matters objectively, and not pursue improper purposes.\n3. A public authority shall observe proportionality in the means it employs and give reasons for its decisions, under Article 1.6.2 (Notice and Reasons).\n\n## Article 29.2.2 — The Service Mandate\n\n1. Public authorities shall provide services that are accessible, timely, courteous, and free of unnecessary formality.\n2. The law shall fix service standards, including maximum processing times, for the principal public services.\n3. Digital channels shall be provided under Article 17.6.1 (Voluntary Digital Identity), and no person shall suffer detriment for using a non-digital channel.\n\n## Article 29.2.3 — Remedies and Complaints\n\n1. Every person affected by a decision of a public authority has the right to its review, under Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies).\n2. Every public authority shall operate a complaints procedure, and the Ombudsman's access shall not depend on its exhaustion, under Article 20.1.2 (Access and Standing).\n3. A complaint shall be answered within a reasonable time fixed by law.\n\n## Article 29.2.4 — The Administrative Procedure Act\n\n1. The Parliament shall adopt by law an administrative procedure act codifying at a minimum: the conduct of proceedings, notice and hearing of affected persons, reasons, timelines, access to files, and the form and publication of decisions.\n2. The administrative procedure act shall apply to all public authorities save where this Constitution provides otherwise.\n\n## Article 29.2.5 — Liability of the State\n\n1. The State is liable for the unlawful acts of its public authorities and officials to the same extent as a private person for fault, under Article 16.2.1 (Fault Liability).\n2. The State may exercise recourse against an official who acted intentionally or with gross negligence.\n3. Immunities of officials shall be confined to what is necessary for the functioning of the office.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Every public body in the Republic is governed by one short grammar of legality, impartiality, and proportionality, so that a citizen need not learn a different legal culture for each counter, window, and portal. The service mandate is constitutionalized because administrative quality is not a luxury: processing times and courteous treatment determine whether rights are real for the person before the office. Mandating an administrative procedure act follows the codification tradition of the continental administrative-law codes, while leaving its details to legislation keeps the Constitution free of procedural minutiae. State liability on the private-law fault standard, with recourse limited to intent and gross negligence, balances compensation against the willingness of officials to decide at all.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 1.6.1 (Fair Administrative Action); Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies); Article 17.6.3 (No Detriment for Non-Users).\n"}, {"id": "29-institutions__regulators", "path": "29-institutions/regulators.md", "part": "29-institutions", "partTitle": "Institutions", "title": "Section 29.3 — Regulators", "md": "# Section 29.3 — Regulators\n\n> **Part 29 — Public Institutions** · Articles 29.3.1 – 29.3.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section sets the constitutional standards for independent regulators: creation by law, independence and expertise, transparency, appeal, and coordination.*\n\n## Article 29.3.1 — Creation by Law\n\n1. An independent regulator shall be created by an Act that defines its mandate, its objectives, its powers, and the sectors or matters within its competence.\n2. A regulator shall not be established by decree, and its core mandate shall not be altered except by law.\n3. The independence and accountability of a regulator shall meet the standards of this Section.\n\n## Article 29.3.2 — Independence and Expertise\n\n1. The members of a regulator shall be appointed on merit through an open, published process, for fixed staggered terms, and may be removed only for cause by a qualified majority of the Parliament.\n2. Members shall possess the expertise the mandate requires and shall observe the incompatibilities fixed by law.\n3. The Government may adopt framework policy for a sector but shall not direct the decisions of a regulator in individual cases.\n4. The funding of a regulator shall be adequate and shall not be reduced in a manner designed to obstruct its mandate.\n\n## Article 29.3.3 — Transparency and Accountability\n\n1. A regulator shall publish its rules, its reasoned decisions, and its guidance, and shall conduct consultations before rules of general application.\n2. A regulator shall report annually to the Parliament on the exercise of its mandate.\n3. Registers of licensed or supervised undertakings shall be public, subject to the limits of Section 27.1.\n\n## Article 29.3.4 — Appeal\n\n1. An individual decision of a regulator shall be subject to appeal to a court, consistent with Article 1.6.4 (Review and Remedies).\n2. The law may provide for an internal reconsideration preceding the appeal, which shall not unreasonably delay access to the court.\n\n## Article 29.3.5 — Coordination\n\n1. Regulators whose mandates intersect shall coordinate to avoid duplication and conflicting requirements, and shall cooperate with the corresponding authorities of other States as the law provides.\n2. The law may authorize joint proceedings and the mutual use of information within the limits of data protection.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Independent regulators hold concentrated power over markets and speech, so this Section writes their constitution in miniature: created by law, staffed on merit, removable only by cause, and appealable like any other power, meeting the standard that the independent media regulator of Section 21.4 and the banking supervisor of Section 9.6 already satisfy. The framework-versus-cases distinction lets governments set policy without capturing decisions, which is the precise boundary most regulatory scandals test. Coordination duties respond to the modern reality that a single firm may face five regulators, and that conflicting demands are themselves a form of arbitrary power. Annual reporting to the Parliament keeps a technical mandate inside democratic visibility without subjecting it to daily politics.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 21.4.1 (Independent Regulator); Article 9.6.3 (Independence of Supervision); Article 9.7.4 (The Securities Regulator).\n"}, {"id": "29-institutions__state-owned-enterprises", "path": "29-institutions/state-owned-enterprises.md", "part": "29-institutions", "partTitle": "Institutions", "title": "Section 29.4 — State-Owned Enterprises", "md": "# Section 29.4 — State-Owned Enterprises\n\n> **Part 29 — Public Institutions** · Articles 29.4.1 – 29.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Section governs state-owned enterprises: commercial objectivity, competitive neutrality, ownership transparency, governance and parliamentary reporting, and the law-bound creation and divestment of state holdings.*\n\n## Article 29.4.1 — Commercial Objectivity\n\n1. A state-owned enterprise shall be operated according to commercial principles unless an Act expressly assigns to it a public-service obligation.\n2. A public-service obligation shall be defined as precisely as possible and shall be compensated on the terms fixed by law.\n3. A state-owned enterprise shall not exercise public authority save where an Act confers it.\n\n## Article 29.4.2 — Competitive Neutrality\n\n1. The State shall ensure that a state-owned enterprise competes on equal terms with private undertakings, applying the competition rules of Article 9.2.5 (Concentrations and Public Undertakings) without exception.\n2. A state-owned enterprise shall not receive privileged access to public finance, guarantees, land, information, or regulatory treatment.\n3. Taxation and regulatory supervision shall apply to a state-owned enterprise on the same terms as to comparable private undertakings.\n\n## Article 29.4.3 — Ownership Transparency\n\n1. The State shall maintain a public register of its shareholdings and of the enterprises in which it holds a controlling interest, consistent with Article 9.4.4 (Transparency of Ownership).\n2. The State shall publish annually a consolidated report on the performance, finances, and public-service obligations of state-owned enterprises.\n3. The accounts of every state-owned enterprise shall be audited under Article 8.8.1 (Scope of Audit).\n\n## Article 29.4.4 — Governance\n\n1. The boards of state-owned enterprises shall be appointed on merit through open processes fixed by law and shall include independent members.\n2. No organ of the State shall direct the individual transactions of a state-owned enterprise otherwise than through the board.\n3. Ownership policy shall be set in writing and shall be reported to the Parliament.\n\n## Article 29.4.5 — Creation and Divestment\n\n1. A state-owned enterprise shall be created or wound up only by law.\n2. A holding of the State shall be divested only by law, through a transparent and competitive process at a fair valuation, and the proceeds shall accrue to the public budget.\n3. The conditions of employment of the staff of a divested enterprise shall be protected as the law provides.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*State-owned enterprises fail their publics in two opposite ways: as politicized employers that squander capital, or as private monopolies that quietly carry public privilege, and this Section aims at both failures at once. Competitive neutrality, developed in OECD practice, is the operative idea: the State competes where it chooses, but never referees with a thumb on the scale for its own team. The register and consolidated report make the state's commercial footprint visible to the Parliament, and the audit link brings the enterprises inside the scope of the supreme audit institution, which is where their money ultimately lives. Requiring divestment by law prevents the quiet privatization of public assets to well-placed buyers, and the employment clause keeps the process from visiting its costs on those least able to bear them.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 9.4.4 (Transparency of Ownership); Article 8.5.1 (Principles); Article 20.5.3 (Access and Powers).\n"}, {"id": "30-schedules__constitutional-offices", "path": "30-schedules/constitutional-offices.md", "part": "30-schedules", "partTitle": "Schedules", "title": "Section 30.3 — Constitutional Offices", "md": "# Section 30.3 — Constitutional Offices\n\n> **Part 30 — Schedules** · Articles 30.3.1 – 30.3.4 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Schedule summarizes in one table the constitutional offices with appointment, term, and removal; governing articles prevail over the summary.*\n\n## Article 30.3.1 — Scope and Effect\n\n1. The table in Article 30.3.2 lists the Republic's constitutional offices.\n2. The table is declaratory; a divergent governing article prevails.\n3. A holder of a listed office is a senior public official for Section 27.3 (Asset Disclosures).\n\n## Article 30.3.2 — Table of Constitutional Offices\n\n1. The constitutional offices are the following:\n\n| Office | Appointment | Term | Removal |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| President | Joint sitting, both Houses | Six years, single | Article 4.1.6 (Impeachment) |\n| Prime Minister | Election by the Lower House | While confidence holds | Article 4.2.4 (Constructive Vote of No Confidence) |\n| Ministers | President, on the Prime Minister's proposal | Cabinet mandate | Dismissal on that proposal, Article 4.3.1 (Appointment of Ministers) |\n| Judges, Constitutional Court | President, on binding recommendation | Twelve years, single | Article 5.1.5 (Removal for Cause) |\n| Judges, other courts | President, on binding recommendation | To statutory retirement age | Article 5.1.5 (Removal for Cause) |\n| Public Prosecutor | As provided by law | Seven years, single | For cause, by law |\n| Auditor-General | Parliament, by supermajority | Single term | For cause, by the same supermajority |\n| Heads of Part 20 institutions | Open process, fixed terms | Fixed, staggered where collegiate | Cause only, qualified majority |\n\n2. Judicial appointments rest on the Judicial Service Commission's binding recommendation under Article 5.5.4 (Binding Recommendations).\n3. Staggered terms of collegiate institutions follow Article 20.4.1 (Composition and Staggered Terms).\n\n## Article 30.3.3 — Vacancies and Acting Capacity\n\n1. Succession to, and vacancy in, a listed office follow the governing article or, where silent, the law.\n2. An acting holder takes the oath or affirmation under Section 30.1 before acting and bears the office's duties while so acting.\n3. Acting capacity shall not exceed the period fixed by law or, in default, ninety days.\n\n## Article 30.3.4 — Amendment of This Schedule\n\n1. This Schedule is amended by simple majority under the schedule tier of Part 25 (Constitutional Process).\n2. An amendment altering the substance of a constitutional office follows the tier applicable to the establishing provision.\n3. A certified consolidated text of this Schedule is published after each amendment.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Gathering the offices into one table serves reader, voter, and drafter: appointment, term, and removal are the three levers of every independence arrangement, and seeing them side by side exposes any lever left loose. The table is expressly declaratory because duplicating rules invites divergence. Classifying the holders as senior public officials links the schedule to Part 27 integrity regimes without re-listing anyone, and the two-tier amendment rule keeps a schedule edit from moving an office into or out of constitutional protection.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 5.5.4 (Binding Recommendations); Article 20.4.1 (Composition and Staggered Terms); Article 4.1.6 (Impeachment).\n"}, {"id": "30-schedules__electoral-districts", "path": "30-schedules/electoral-districts.md", "part": "30-schedules", "partTitle": "Schedules", "title": "Section 30.2 — Electoral Districts", "md": "# Section 30.2 — Electoral Districts\n\n> **Part 30 — Schedules** · Articles 30.2.1 – 30.2.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Schedule governs delimitation of constituencies and districts: the Boundary Commission's mandate, the principles of delimitation, procedure, and review.*\n\n## Article 30.2.1 — Scope\n\n1. This Schedule governs the delimitation of constituencies and districts for elections other than that of the Lower House, whose constituencies correspond to the Provinces under Article 6.1.4 (Constituency Boundaries), and any revision of the provincial constituency structure that an Act provides to preserve equality of the vote.\n2. Its principles apply to districts for local elections and referendums, under Article 7.4.1 (Administration by the Electoral Commission).\n\n## Article 30.2.2 — The Boundary Commission\n\n1. An independent Boundary Commission shall delimit constituencies and districts under this Schedule.\n2. Its members are appointed for fixed terms through an open, published process, may be removed only for cause, and may not hold party office.\n3. The Commission is supported by professional staff and the data services of the Statistics Authority.\n4. The Commission's budget shall be adequate and shall not be reduced so as to obstruct its work.\n\n## Article 30.2.3 — Principles of Delimitation\n\n1. The Commission shall observe, in order of priority:\n   (a) the equality of the vote, so that electors per seat are comparable;\n   (b) geographic contiguity and the accessibility of the district;\n   (c) the coherence of communities of interest and of administrative and natural boundaries.\n2. The Commission shall use the most recent census and electoral register data.\n3. The Commission shall not take account of voting patterns, the prospects of parties or candidates, or the residence of incumbents.\n4. A deviation from population equality shall not exceed the statutory tolerance and shall be reasoned.\n\n## Article 30.2.4 — Procedure\n\n1. The Commission shall publish draft delimitations with their data and reasons.\n2. Any person may object within the period fixed by law, and the Commission shall hold public hearings where substantial objections are made.\n3. The final determination is published with reasons and takes effect for the next election; the Parliament may correct only procedural defects.\n\n## Article 30.2.5 — Review Cycle and Review by Courts\n\n1. The Commission shall review all delimitations after each census, at intervals not exceeding ten years, and whenever population movements impair the equality of the vote.\n2. A determination of the Commission is subject to review by the courts for breach of this Schedule, under Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts).\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Boundary rules are stated in the Constitution because districting is the classic instrument by which a majority entrenches itself, and the ban on using voting patterns or incumbents' residences is the rule's teeth. The priority ordering is deliberate: arithmetic equality first, geography and community second, so contiguity cannot be a pretext for salamander shapes. The independent commission model follows parliamentary democracies whose redistributions are accepted precisely because the commission takes no electoral advantage into account. Public drafts, objections, and reasoned determinations make the process accountable, and the ten-year cycle keeps maps honest as populations move.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 6.1.4 (Constituency Boundaries); Article 20.4.1 (Composition and Staggered Terms); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts).\n"}, {"id": "30-schedules__oath-of-office", "path": "30-schedules/oath-of-office.md", "part": "30-schedules", "partTitle": "Schedules", "title": "Section 30.1 — Oath of Office", "md": "# Section 30.1 — Oath of Office\n\n> **Part 30 — Schedules** · Articles 30.1.1 – 30.1.6 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Schedule prescribes the oaths and solemn affirmations of the principal offices; the affirmation has the same effect as the oath.*\n\n## Article 30.1.1 — Form and Effect\n\n1. The oaths of this Schedule are taken publicly upon taking office before the organ designated by law, the oath of the President before the Constitutional Court.\n2. Every oath may be taken as a solemn affirmation with the same effect, omitting \"swear\" and \"So help me God\".\n3. A person who declines the oath or affirmation does not enter upon office until taking it.\n4. The oath for further offices is fixed by law in the form of this Schedule.\n\n## Article 30.1.2 — Oath of the President\n\nThe President takes this oath:\n\n\"I, (name), do swear that I will faithfully discharge the office of President of the Republic; uphold and defend the Constitution and the laws; respect, protect, and fulfill the rights of every person; and devote my powers to the well-being and democracy of the Republic. So help me God.\"\n\n## Article 30.1.3 — Oath of the Prime Minister and Ministers\n\nThe Prime Minister and ministers take this oath:\n\n\"I, (name), do swear that I will faithfully discharge the duties of my office; uphold and defend the Constitution and the laws; act in the interest of the Republic and all its people, without fear or favour, affection or ill-will; and preserve the confidentiality my office requires. So help me God.\"\n\n## Article 30.1.4 — Oath of Judges\n\nEvery judge takes this oath:\n\n\"I, (name), do swear that I will administer justice impartially and conscientiously, according to this Constitution and the laws; decide without fear, favour, affection, or ill-will; and preserve the independence and dignity of my office. So help me God.\"\n\n## Article 30.1.5 — Oath of Members of Parliament\n\nA member of the Parliament takes this oath before taking the seat:\n\n\"I, (name), do swear that I will bear true allegiance to the Constitution and the people of the Republic; faithfully discharge the duties of a member; and conduct myself in the public interest. So help me God.\"\n\n## Article 30.1.6 — Oath of Members of the Armed Forces\n\nMembers of the armed forces take this oath upon entry into service, under Article 18.2.2 (Oath to the Constitution):\n\n\"I, (name), do swear that I will respect and defend the Constitution, the laws, and the democratic order of the Republic; obey only lawful orders; and faithfully serve the Republic at all times. So help me God.\"\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Oaths bind the office holder to the Constitution and the people rather than to a person or party, which is why the service-member's text forbids obeying unlawful orders, mirroring Section 18.2. The affirmation alternative keeps public office open to those whose conscience refuses an oath. The presidential oath is taken before the Constitutional Court, the institution that guards the order being sworn, and judges swear to preserve the independence of their office itself.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 18.2.2 (Oath to the Constitution); Article 4.1.2 (Term of Office); Article 3.4.2 (Term and Mandate).\n"}, {"id": "30-schedules__protected-provisions", "path": "30-schedules/protected-provisions.md", "part": "30-schedules", "partTitle": "Schedules", "title": "Section 30.5 — Protected Provisions", "md": "# Section 30.5 — Protected Provisions\n\n> **Part 30 — Schedules** · Articles 30.5.1 – 30.5.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Schedule lists the entrenched provisions that, in addition to the ordinary amendment majorities, require a national referendum majority under Part 25 (Constitutional Process).*\n\n## Article 30.5.1 — Entrenchment\n\n1. An amendment of a provision listed in Article 30.5.2 requires, in addition to a two-thirds majority of the members of each House, approval by a majority of the valid votes cast in a national referendum.\n2. The referendum is conducted under Article 6.7.3 (Mandatory Referendum on Entrenched Amendments).\n3. A purported amendment adopted without the required referendum is void.\n\n## Article 30.5.2 — Protected Content\n\n1. The entrenched provisions are:\n   (a) the republican and democratic form of the State, including the parliamentary order and the principle of free, periodic elections under Article 0.6.2 (Free and Periodic Elections);\n   (b) human dignity under Article 0.7.1 (Inviolability of Human Dignity);\n   (c) equality and the prohibition of discrimination under Section 1.2;\n   (d) judicial independence under Section 5.1;\n   (e) the non-derogable rights under Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights);\n   (f) the amendment rules of Part 25 (Constitutional Process);\n   (g) the amendment freeze of Article 26.5.5 (Amendment Freeze);\n   (h) this Schedule and the entrenchment rule of Article 30.5.1.\n2. The protection of clause 1 extends to repeal and to any alteration diminishing a protected provision's substance.\n\n## Article 30.5.3 — Scope of the Protection\n\n1. An amendment touching protected content in any respect falls under this Schedule, irrespective of its direction or of the proportion affected.\n2. An amendment that only extends protection, without altering existing substance, follows the ordinary tier.\n3. The addition of further provisions to this Schedule follows the entrenched tier.\n\n## Article 30.5.4 — Referendum Requirements\n\n1. The question submitted to the Electorate shall reproduce the substance of the amendment faithfully and comprehensibly.\n2. The referendum is administered by the Electoral Commission, under Article 3.5.3 (Exclusive Administration).\n3. The amendment enters into force only after publication of the certified referendum result.\n\n## Article 30.5.5 — Verification\n\n1. The Constitutional Court shall verify, before proclamation, that the requirements of this Schedule and of Part 25 (Constitutional Process) have been observed.\n2. The Court shall decide a verification proceeding with priority.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Entrenchment protects not against change but against unreflective change: the referendum tier ensures that the provisions constituting the Republic, its form, dignity, equality, courts, and the hard core of rights, can be altered only when the whole electorate, not merely a supermajority of its representatives, bears the responsibility. The content list parallels the eternity-clause tradition of the German Basic Law while keeping every protected item concrete rather than open-ended, which is why equality is protected through Section 1.2 and judicial independence through Section 5.1. Including the amendment rules of Part 25 and the emergency amendment freeze prevents the tiers themselves from being lowered, and the verification role of the Constitutional Court gives the tier an enforcer, making the referendum requirement a legal fact rather than a convention.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 6.7.3 (Mandatory Referendum on Entrenched Amendments); Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 5.1.1 (Independence of Courts).\n"}, {"id": "30-schedules__transition-rules", "path": "30-schedules/transition-rules.md", "part": "30-schedules", "partTitle": "Schedules", "title": "Section 30.4 — Transition Rules", "md": "# Section 30.4 — Transition Rules\n\n> **Part 30 — Schedules** · Articles 30.4.1 – 30.4.5 · Adopted — v1.0.0\n\n*This Schedule orders entry into force of the Constitution: continuity of consistent law, first appointments, continuity of institutions and obligations, and repeal of inconsistent provisions.*\n\n## Article 30.4.1 — Entry into Force\n\n1. This Constitution enters into force upon its proclamation in accordance with the procedure that brought it into being.\n2. Upon entry into force, the organs established by this Constitution are constituted and take up their functions under this Schedule.\n3. The oath or affirmation under Section 30.1 shall be taken before the functions of the listed offices are exercised.\n\n## Article 30.4.2 — Continuity of Existing Law\n\n1. Existing law continues in force insofar as it is consistent with this Constitution.\n2. To the extent of inconsistency, existing law ceases to apply, without need of express repeal.\n3. Existing law shall be interpreted in conformity with this Constitution, consistent with Article 0.8.4 (Conforming Interpretation).\n\n## Article 30.4.3 — First Appointments\n\n1. The Parliament in office at the entry into force continues for its term and exercises the powers of this Constitution.\n2. The President, the Prime Minister, and the ministers in office continue and are deemed appointed under this Constitution, subject to taking the oath or affirmation without delay.\n3. The first judges of the Constitutional Court shall be designated by law; their terms are staggered under Article 5.3.2 (Terms of Judges).\n4. The first holders of the offices in Section 30.3 shall be appointed without delay under the governing articles.\n\n## Article 30.4.4 — Continuity of Institutions and Obligations\n\n1. The courts, the Public Prosecution Service, and the public administration continue to function; pending proceedings are unaffected.\n2. The property, contracts, and public debt of the State devolve upon the competent organs of the Republic.\n3. Rights of individuals arising under existing law are preserved insofar as consistent with this Constitution.\n\n## Article 30.4.5 — Repeal and Conforming Amendments\n\n1. The Government shall lay before the Parliament, and the Parliament shall process within the periods fixed by the transition legislation, the measures required to repeal inconsistent provisions and conform existing law to this Constitution.\n2. Until such measures are taken, the rule of Article 30.4.2 governs.\n3. No provision of existing law shall be revived after ceasing to apply under this Schedule except by fresh enactment.\n\n## Design Note\n\n*Transition rules are the bridge every constitution must build from the old order to the new, and the continuity principle follows the maxim that the State outlives its constitutions: courts stay open, judges keep sitting, and debts are honored. The deemed-appointment clause avoids the first-hour paradox in which the offices that must install the new order are the very ones awaiting installation. The inconsistency rule, under which inconsistent law ceases rather than being repealed piecemeal, borrows the supremacy mechanics of Section 0.8, and the repeal program with fixed periods makes the cleanup a duty rather than an option.*\n\n**Cross-references:** Article 0.8.4 (Conforming Interpretation); Article 5.3.2 (Terms of Judges); Article 30.1.1 (Form and Effect).\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.1-general-principle", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.1-general-principle.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.1 — General Principle of Eligibility", "md": "# Section 31.1 — General Principle of Eligibility\n\nNo person may hold governing office — legislative, executive, or judicial, at any level from a village council to the World Assembly — on the strength of birth, wealth, popularity, or force alone. Every officeholder must clear the same three gates: **education**, **psychological fitness**, and **physical fitness**, defined in 31.2–31.4 and re-applied throughout a career under 31.5.\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.1.1 (Scope).** This Part applies to every elected, appointed, or hereditary person who exercises binding governmental power, including ministers, judges, legislators, regional governors, and — for any governing role beyond ceremonial duties — members of the Royal Family.\n\n**Article 31.1.2 (No Exemptions by Birth).** Being born into the Royal Family, a political dynasty, or a wealthy family confers no exemption from Articles 31.2–31.4. A monarch's child is a candidate like any other before they may hold a governing office; see 31.6.\n\n**Article 31.1.3 (No Exemption by Election).** Winning a popular election qualifies a candidate to stand, but does not itself satisfy the eligibility gates. A candidate must clear 31.2–31.4 *before* their name may appear on a ballot or appointment list.\n\n**Article 31.1.4 (Independent Administration).** The gates are administered by the Integrity Branch's Governance Eligibility Board (see Part 37), never by the government the candidate hopes to join, to prevent incumbents from writing their own exam.\n\n*Design Note: The core idea this Part protects is simple — power should never be easier to get than a driving license. The three-gate model borrows the \"competence before authority\" logic used for pilots, surgeons, and judges in most existing legal systems, and extends it, for the first time, to political power itself.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 31.2 (Education), 31.3 (Psychological Fitness), 31.4 (Physical Fitness), 31.6 (Royal Heirs), 37 (Integrity Branch), 04-executive/head-of-state.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.1.5 (Timeline).** Candidacy applications open no later than 180 days before an election; the eligibility gates (31.2–31.4) must be completed and certified no later than 60 days before the vote, giving candidates a fixed 120-day certification window.\n\n**Article 31.1.6 (Cost Coverage).** All three gates are administered free of charge to the candidate, funded by the Integrity Branch's budget, so that cost can never be a barrier to standing for office.\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.2-education-requirement", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.2-education-requirement.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.2 — Education Requirement", "md": "# Section 31.2 — Education Requirement\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.2.1 (Minimum Credential).** No candidate may be certified eligible for governing office without a recognized qualification in public administration, constitutional law, public policy, economics, or an equivalent field of governance studies, awarded by an accredited institution.\n\n**Article 31.2.2 (Governance Studies Curriculum).** The curriculum must cover, at minimum: constitutional structure, budgeting and public finance, human rights law, environmental and animal-welfare law, criminal justice principles, and the history of governmental failure (corruption, atrocity, and collapse case studies), so that officeholders understand not only how to govern but how governments go wrong.\n\n**Article 31.2.3 (Equivalency Pathway).** A candidate without a formal degree may qualify through an equivalency examination of the same rigor, so that eligibility depends on demonstrated knowledge, not on wealth or access to universities.\n\n**Article 31.2.4 (Continuing Education).** Sitting officeholders must complete refresher governance education at least once every five years as a condition of Article 31.5 re-certification.\n\n*Design Note: The requirement is deliberately about governance competence, not general academic prestige — a brilliant surgeon or engineer with no training in public administration is not, by that fact alone, qualified to run a ministry.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 31.1, 31.5, 11-education/higher-education.md, 07-local-government (for local-tier equivalents)\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.2.5 (Recognized Institutions).** The Governance Eligibility Board maintains a public list of accredited institutions and equivalency-exam centers in every region, reviewed every three years, with a mandatory minimum of one free public option per 2 million residents.\n\n**Article 31.2.6 (Equivalency Exam Structure).** The equivalency exam (31.2.3) consists of a written constitutional-law and public-finance section (60%) and an applied case-study section (40%), scored by at least two independent examiners with a documented disagreement-resolution procedure.\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.3-psychological-fitness-test", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.3-psychological-fitness-test.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.3 — Psychological Fitness Test", "md": "# Section 31.3 — Psychological Fitness Test\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.3.1 (Purpose).** The test exists to screen for traits with a demonstrated record of causing governmental harm — unchecked impulsivity, pathological dishonesty, disregard for the welfare of others, and susceptibility to corruption — never to screen for ordinary mental illness, neurodivergence, or personal struggle, which are not disqualifying in themselves.\n\n**Article 31.3.2 (Administering Body).** The test is designed and scored by an independent panel of clinical psychologists and ethicists under the Integrity Branch, insulated from the government of the day, using validated, peer-reviewed instruments rather than ad hoc questioning.\n\n**Article 31.3.3 (Repeatability & Appeal).** A failed test may be retaken after a minimum interval, and every result may be appealed to the Integrity Courts (Part 37/38). No candidate may be permanently barred without an appeal opportunity.\n\n**Article 31.3.4 (Confidentiality).** Results are confidential; only the pass/fail certification, not the underlying clinical detail, is made public, to protect candidates' dignity while preserving public accountability for the outcome.\n\n**Article 31.3.5 (Non-Discrimination).** This test shall never be used to exclude candidates on the basis of disability, neurodivergence, or a treated and stable mental health history unrelated to governance risk; doing so is itself a violation of Article 1.2 (Equality) and is subject to challenge before the Constitutional Court.\n\n*Design Note: This is the most sensitive Article in the whole Constitution and the one most easily abused by a future government wanting to disqualify opponents. Article 31.3.5 and the independence guarantee in 31.3.2 exist specifically to prevent that misuse; any amendment weakening either is subject to the entrenchment rule in 0.5.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 31.1, 31.5, 1.2 (Equality), 41-disability rights sections, 05-judiciary/constitutional-court.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.3.6 (Retest Interval).** A failed test may be retaken no sooner than 90 days later, with a maximum of three attempts per election cycle.\n\n**Article 31.3.7 (Panel Composition).** Each candidate is assessed by a panel of at least three clinicians, none of whom may share a political party affiliation with the candidate, with panel membership rotated randomly from a certified national roster to prevent targeting.\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.4-physical-fitness-standard", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.4-physical-fitness-standard.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.4 — Physical & Health Fitness Standard", "md": "# Section 31.4 — Physical & Health Fitness Standard\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.4.1 (Baseline Standard).** Officeholders must meet a baseline standard of capacity to perform the duties of the office, assessed functionally — can the person fulfil the responsibilities, with reasonable accommodation — never by a checklist of diagnoses.\n\n**Article 31.4.2 (Reasonable Accommodation).** Any physical or sensory disability must first be assessed for reasonable accommodation before any question of ineligibility arises; a wheelchair user, a blind candidate, or a candidate with a chronic condition is not, by that fact, unfit.\n\n**Article 31.4.3 (Emergency Capacity Review).** For the very highest offices (Head of Government, Head of State's governing deputies), a periodic review confirms the office-holder retains the capacity to respond to genuine emergencies, with the same appeal rights as 31.3.3.\n\n**Article 31.4.4 (Prohibition on Abuse).** As with 31.3.5, this standard may never be used as a pretext for political removal; any disputed finding goes to the Integrity Courts, not to the officeholder's political rivals.\n\n*Design Note: History offers as many examples of physically vigorous leaders causing catastrophic harm as of frail ones governing wisely. This Article is deliberately narrow — functional capacity for the specific duties, nothing more — to avoid becoming a tool of ableism or political convenience.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 31.1, 31.3, 41-disability rights, 26-emergency-and-crisis\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.4.5 (Review Frequency).** The baseline functional-capacity review occurs at candidacy, then every five years in office, or immediately upon a documented emergency-relevant incapacitating event, whichever comes first.\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.5-continuous-recertification", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.5-continuous-recertification.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.5 — Continuous Re-Certification", "md": "# Section 31.5 — Continuous Re-Certification\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.5.1 (Term-Linked Review).** Every officeholder undergoes the education refresher (31.2.4), a repeat psychological screening (31.3), and a repeat fitness review (31.4) at least once per elected term, and more frequently for offices with emergency powers.\n\n**Article 31.5.2 (No Automatic Removal).** A single flagged review triggers a structured improvement or leave process, not automatic removal; only a sustained, appeal-exhausted failure triggers Article 31.7 disqualification.\n\n**Article 31.5.3 (Transparency of Process, Not Content).** The public is told that a review occurred and its pass/fail outcome; clinical and personal detail remains protected under 31.3.4.\n\n*Design Note: Power tends to change people, sometimes for the worse; a single entry exam is not enough. Re-certification treats fitness to govern as a standard to be maintained, not a badge earned once and kept forever.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 31.2, 31.3, 31.4, 31.7\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.5.4 (Improvement Process Length).** A structured improvement or leave process under Article 31.5.2 runs for a fixed period of 6 months, after which a second review determines certification or referral to Article 31.7 disqualification proceedings.\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.6-royal-succession-and-testing", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.6-royal-succession-and-testing.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.6 — Royal Succession and the Testing of Heirs", "md": "# Section 31.6 — Royal Succession and the Testing of Heirs\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.6.1 (The Crown Is Ceremonial).** The Crown, held by hereditary succession under 04-executive/head-of-state.md, is a ceremonial and unifying office with defined reserve powers (dissolution deadlock-breaking, mercy, and constitutional guardianship) but does **not**, by itself, direct ministries, courts, or legislation.\n\n**Article 31.6.2 (Heirs Are Candidates for Governing Office).** Should any member of the Royal Family wish to hold an actual governing office — a ministry, a governorship, a judgeship, a seat in the Assembly — they must clear Articles 31.2–31.4 exactly as any other citizen, with no expedited track and no private examiners.\n\n**Article 31.6.3 (Separation of Crown and Cabinet).** No serving Head of Government or Minister may simultaneously be the Sovereign Monarch, preserving the separation of powers of 0.4 and preventing the concentration your ancestors' worst monarchies suffered from.\n\n**Article 31.6.4 (Education of Heirs).** Heirs to the Crown receive the same governance-studies curriculum (31.2.2) as any candidate, from childhood, not as a courtesy but as a constitutional duty — an uneducated monarch is as much a design flaw as an uneducated minister.\n\n*Design Note: This Article is the hinge between \"king\" and \"constitutional government run by tested professionals\" — it keeps the unifying, continuity-giving symbol of a Crown while making sure no one governs by blood alone. If a purely elected head of state is preferred instead of a hereditary Crown, this section and 04-executive/head-of-state.md would need to be revised together.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 04-executive/head-of-state.md, 31.1, 0.3 (Founding: The Crown), 0.4 (Separation of Powers)\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.6.5 (Age of First Testing).** Heirs first sit the age-appropriate governance-studies curriculum assessment at 16, and may not be certified for any governing office (as opposed to ceremonial Crown succession) before age 21 and full completion of Articles 31.2–31.4.\n"}, {"id": "31-government-eligibility__31.7-disqualification-and-removal", "path": "31-government-eligibility/31.7-disqualification-and-removal.md", "part": "31-government-eligibility", "partTitle": "Government Eligibility", "title": "Section 31.7 — Disqualification and Removal", "md": "# Section 31.7 — Disqualification and Removal\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 31.7.1 (Grounds).** An officeholder is disqualified for: a sustained, appeal-exhausted failure of Articles 31.2–31.4; a criminal conviction under Part 37 (corruption) or Part 38 (serious crimes); or a finding of gross violation of the rights in Part 01.\n\n**Article 31.7.2 (Process).** Disqualification is decided by the Integrity Courts (37.9), never by a simple vote of political rivals, to prevent the process itself from becoming a tool of persecution.\n\n**Article 31.7.3 (Right of Reply and Appeal).** Every disqualification proceeding guarantees the officeholder a public hearing, legal representation, and one appeal to the Constitutional Court.\n\n**Article 31.7.4 (Interim Succession).** On disqualification, an interim officeholder — never a family member of the disqualified person, to avoid dynastic capture of the vacancy — serves until a fresh, fully-vetted successor is certified.\n\n*Design Note: A removal power strong enough to remove the corrupt must be built so it cannot be turned against the honest. Independent adjudication (37.9) and guaranteed appeal are the two safeguards doing that work here.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 31.1–31.5, 37 (Integrity Branch), 38 (Penal Code Extended), 05-judiciary/constitutional-court.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 31.7.5 (Hearing Timeline).** A disqualification hearing must be scheduled within 30 days of referral and concluded within 90 days, to prevent indefinite suspension being used as an informal removal tool.\n\n**Article 31.7.6 (Interim Successor Term).** An interim officeholder under Article 31.7.4 serves no longer than 180 days before a fully certified successor must take office.\n"}, {"id": "32-animal-and-plant-rights__32.1-animal-welfare-baseline", "path": "32-animal-and-plant-rights/32.1-animal-welfare-baseline.md", "part": "32-animal-and-plant-rights", "partTitle": "Animal And Plant Rights", "title": "Section 32.1 — Animal Welfare Baseline", "md": "# Section 32.1 — Animal Welfare Baseline\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 32.1.1 (Sentience Recognized).** Animals are recognized as sentient beings capable of suffering, and this Constitution imposes a duty on the state, and on every person, to avoid causing them unnecessary pain, fear, or distress.\n\n**Article 32.1.2 (Universal Minimum Floor).** Every region sets its own detailed animal-welfare rules, but no region may fall below this floor: (a) prohibition of gratuitous cruelty; (b) a duty to provide adequate food, water, shelter, and veterinary care to animals in human custody; (c) prohibition of abandonment; (d) humane methods required for any lawful killing, including for food (see 32.4).\n\n**Article 32.1.3 (Wildlife).** Wild animals and their habitats receive protection under Part 32.5 (Protected Species) and Part 13 (Environment); this Article governs animals in human custody or affected by human activity generally.\n\n**Article 32.1.4 (Enforcement).** Each region maintains an Animal Welfare Authority with inspection and prosecution powers; egregious or repeated cruelty is a crime under 32.2.\n\n*Design Note: The floor is deliberately a floor, not a ceiling — consistent with the \"rights are floors\" principle used throughout this Constitution — so that regions with stronger cultural or religious commitments to animal protection may always go further.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.4 (Animal Rights, Part 01), 32.2 (Cruelty Crimes), 32.4 (Meat and Dietary Law), 24-agriculture/animal-welfare.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 32.1.5 (Inspection Frequency).** Commercial animal-custody operations (farms, transport, research facilities) are inspected at minimum annually, with unannounced inspections permitted at any time on credible complaint.\n\n**Article 32.1.6 (Minimum Space and Care Standards).** Each region publishes species-specific minimum space, light, and veterinary-access standards within two years of this Constitution's adoption, reviewed every five years by independent veterinary science panels.\n"}, {"id": "32-animal-and-plant-rights__32.2-animal-cruelty-crimes", "path": "32-animal-and-plant-rights/32.2-animal-cruelty-crimes.md", "part": "32-animal-and-plant-rights", "partTitle": "Animal And Plant Rights", "title": "Section 32.2 — Animal Cruelty: Crimes and Punishments", "md": "# Section 32.2 — Animal Cruelty: Crimes and Punishments\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 32.2.1 (Classification).** Animal cruelty is classified by severity: (I) neglect through ignorance — mandatory education order and civil fine; (II) reckless neglect causing suffering — fine and custody-license revocation; (III) intentional cruelty or torture — criminal conviction, custodial sentence proportionate to harm, and lifetime ban on animal ownership; (IV) organized cruelty for entertainment or profit (illegal fighting rings, poaching syndicates) — treated as organized crime under 38 (Penal Code Extended), with asset confiscation.\n\n**Article 32.2.2 (Aggravating Factors).** Cruelty committed in the presence of a child, for profit, or as part of organized activity is an aggravating factor increasing the sentence within its class.\n\n**Article 32.2.3 (Rehabilitation Consistency).** Consistent with the Constitution's rehabilitation-first sentencing philosophy (38-penal-code-extended/restorative-justice.md), Class I and II offenders are directed first toward education and supervised correction; Class III and IV offenders face custodial punishment because the harm and intent are severe.\n\n*Design Note: The tiered structure mirrors how this Constitution treats human-harm crimes — proportionate to intent and harm, rehabilitative where possible, firm where the conduct is deliberate or organized — so that \"low violence\" as a constitutional value extends to violence against animals, not only between humans.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 32.1, 38-penal-code-extended, 14-criminal-law/sentencing.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 32.2.4 (Sentencing Guide).** Class I: mandatory education order plus fine up to one month's median regional income. Class II: fine up to six months' median income and licence revocation of up to five years. Class III: custodial sentence of six months to five years plus lifetime ownership ban. Class IV: treated under Part 38 organized-crime sentencing, minimum three years, with full asset confiscation under Part 37.\n"}, {"id": "32-animal-and-plant-rights__32.3-plant-and-ecosystem-protection", "path": "32-animal-and-plant-rights/32.3-plant-and-ecosystem-protection.md", "part": "32-animal-and-plant-rights", "partTitle": "Animal And Plant Rights", "title": "Section 32.3 — Plant and Ecosystem Protection", "md": "# Section 32.3 — Plant and Ecosystem Protection\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 32.3.1 (Ecological Standing).** Significant ecosystems — old-growth forests, wetlands, coral reefs, and other officially designated systems — are recognized as holding protected legal status, and may be represented in court by a designated Guardian body when threatened, even absent a human plaintiff.\n\n**Article 32.3.2 (Duty Not to Destroy Without Process).** No forest, wetland, or other designated ecosystem may be cleared, drained, or fundamentally altered without an independent environmental impact review and a public objection period.\n\n**Article 32.3.3 (Restoration Duty).** Where destruction is unavoidable for a permitted public purpose, the responsible party bears a restoration or equivalent-offset duty, verified by the Environmental Authority.\n\n**Article 32.3.4 (Traditional and Indigenous Stewardship).** Where indigenous or traditional communities have long-standing stewardship practices for a given ecosystem, those practices are given weight in the impact review and, where consistent with the welfare floor, preserved.\n\n*Design Note: This Article gives plants and ecosystems a form of legal voice without pretending a tree can sue on its own — a designated Guardian body does that job, similar to how a guardian ad litem represents a child's interests in court.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.5 (Plant & Ecosystem Rights, Part 01), 13-environment (Part 13), 24-agriculture/land-use.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 32.3.5 (Review Period).** The independent environmental impact review (32.3.2) must be completed within 120 days of filing, with a public objection period of no fewer than 30 days before any final decision.\n\n**Article 32.3.6 (Restoration Ratio).** Where destruction is authorized, the restoration or offset duty (32.3.3) is set at a minimum 3:1 ratio of restored-or-protected equivalent habitat to habitat destroyed.\n"}, {"id": "32-animal-and-plant-rights__32.4-meat-consumption-and-dietary-customary-law", "path": "32-animal-and-plant-rights/32.4-meat-consumption-and-dietary-customary-law.md", "part": "32-animal-and-plant-rights", "partTitle": "Animal And Plant Rights", "title": "Section 32.4 — Meat Consumption and Dietary Customary Law", "md": "# Section 32.4 — Meat Consumption and Dietary Customary Law\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 32.4.1 (World Floor, Local Custom).** This Constitution does not impose a single world-wide diet. Every region retains authority to regulate meat consumption, slaughter methods, and dietary custom according to its own culture, religion, and ecology, subject only to the universal welfare floor in Article 32.1.2(d) — that any killing for food must be humane.\n\n**Article 32.4.2 (Religious and Cultural Methods).** Slaughter methods tied to religious or cultural practice are lawful where they meet independently verified humane standards; where a traditional method and the humane-standard requirement are in tension, the region's relevant religious and veterinary authorities jointly certify a compliant protocol rather than the practice being banned outright.\n\n**Article 32.4.3 (Sustainable Limits).** Regions may set sustainability-based limits on meat production (e.g., land, water, and emissions ceilings) under Part 13 and Part 24, separate from and in addition to welfare regulation.\n\n**Article 32.4.4 (No Discrimination Against Diet).** No person may be denied employment, education, or public service on the basis of their dietary choice, whether vegetarian, vegan, or a diet including meat, consistent with Article 1.2 (Equality).\n\n**Article 32.4.5 (Labelling and Transparency).** All meat and animal-product packaging must disclose welfare-standard compliance (e.g., \"humane-certified\"), giving consumers informed choice rather than the state dictating what they eat.\n\n*Design Note: This is the clause that resolves the tension in a genuinely worldwide constitution between a universal animal-welfare floor and enormous cultural and religious diversity around food. The answer chosen here is subsidiarity: one floor (no cruelty), many ceilings (local custom decides everything above it).*\n\n**Cross-references:** 32.1, 24-agriculture/farmers-rights.md, 1.4, 0.2 (Founding Principle: local custom respected above the floor)\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 32.4.6 (Certification Renewal).** Humane-slaughter protocol certification under Article 32.4.2 is reviewed and renewed every three years by the joint religious-and-veterinary authority named in that Article.\n\n**Article 32.4.7 (Labelling Standard).** Humane-certified labelling (32.4.5) must appear on packaging in a font no smaller than the product name, verified by random audit at a minimum rate of 5% of certified producers per year.\n"}, {"id": "32-animal-and-plant-rights__32.5-protected-species-list", "path": "32-animal-and-plant-rights/32.5-protected-species-list.md", "part": "32-animal-and-plant-rights", "partTitle": "Animal And Plant Rights", "title": "Section 32.5 — World-Level Protected Species List", "md": "# Section 32.5 — World-Level Protected Species List\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 32.5.1 (World Register).** A World Register of Protected Species is maintained by the Environmental Authority, listing species at risk of extinction across any region, updated at least every two years by independent scientific review.\n\n**Article 32.5.2 (Baseline Prohibitions).** Hunting, trading, or trafficking a listed species is a crime under this Constitution regardless of regional law, enforced alongside regional wildlife authorities.\n\n**Article 32.5.3 (Habitat Linkage).** Listing a species automatically triggers heightened protection, under Article 32.3, for the ecosystems that sustain it.\n\n**Article 32.5.4 (Community Compensation).** Where protection restricts a traditional livelihood (e.g., subsistence hunting), the state provides a transition and compensation program rather than leaving affected communities unsupported.\n\n*Design Note: A worldwide register only works if it is scientific and updated, not political and frozen — hence independent review rather than legislative vote on the list itself.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 32.1, 32.3, 13-environment/biodiversity.md, 24-agriculture/fisheries.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 32.5.5 (Review Cycle).** The World Register is reviewed at minimum every two years, with emergency additions possible within 30 days on a documented, scientifically supported extinction-risk finding.\n"}, {"id": "32-animal-and-plant-rights__32.6-deforestation-and-land-clearing-law", "path": "32-animal-and-plant-rights/32.6-deforestation-and-land-clearing-law.md", "part": "32-animal-and-plant-rights", "partTitle": "Animal And Plant Rights", "title": "Section 32.6 — Deforestation and Land-Clearing Law", "md": "# Section 32.6 — Deforestation and Land-Clearing Law\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 32.6.1 (Permit Requirement).** Clearing of forest above a defined threshold size requires a permit following the impact review of Article 32.3.2; unpermitted clearing is a crime under 38-penal-code-extended/environmental-crime.md.\n\n**Article 32.6.2 (Net-Zero Clearing Target).** Each region sets a binding schedule toward net-zero permitted deforestation, reported annually to the World Environmental Authority.\n\n**Article 32.6.3 (Illegal Logging as Organized Crime).** Large-scale illegal logging, where linked to organized trafficking, is prosecuted as organized crime under Part 38, with confiscation of equipment, land-use rights, and proceeds.\n\n*Design Note: Placing this alongside animal and plant rights, rather than burying it purely in environmental policy, reflects the Constitution's view that a forest's destruction is a rights violation against the ecosystem itself (32.3.1), not merely a regulatory infraction.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 32.3, 38-penal-code-extended/environmental-crime.md, 13-environment/forests.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 32.6.4 (Threshold Definition).** The permit threshold in Article 32.6.1 is set at 2 hectares of contiguous forest cover, reviewed every five years against updated ecological data.\n\n**Article 32.6.5 (Net-Zero Milestones).** Each region's net-zero schedule (32.6.2) must show measurable reduction milestones at years 3, 6, and 10 from adoption, reported to the World Environmental Authority annually.\n"}, {"id": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography__33.1-mountain-and-hill-conservation", "path": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography/33.1-mountain-and-hill-conservation.md", "part": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography", "partTitle": "Hills Mountains And Geography", "title": "Section 33.1 — Mountain and Hill Conservation", "md": "# Section 33.1 — Mountain and Hill Conservation\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 33.1.1 (Special Terrain Status).** Mountains, hills, and highland ecosystems are recognized as ecologically sensitive terrain requiring rules distinct from lowland land-use law, given their role in water catchment, biodiversity, and erosion control.\n\n**Article 33.1.2 (Slope-Based Building Limits).** Regions must set slope-based construction limits (the steeper the land, the stricter the limit) to prevent landslide risk and habitat loss, reviewed by independent geological survey.\n\n**Article 33.1.3 (Watershed Protection).** Any hill or mountain range that forms a river's watershed receives heightened protection under Part 13 (Water Governance), since damage upstream harms every community downstream.\n\n**Article 33.1.4 (Sacred and Cultural Sites).** Mountains and hills of religious or cultural significance to a community receive additional protected status at that community's request, safeguarded from commercial development without consent.\n\n*Design Note: Highland terrain is disproportionately fragile and disproportionately under-regulated in most existing legal systems, precisely because damage there is invisible until a landslide or a dried-up river makes it visible downstream. This section exists to regulate the cause, not just respond to the consequence.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 13-environment/water.md, 32.3, 23-infrastructure/urban-development.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 33.1.5 (Slope Bands).** Construction is unrestricted below a 10% grade, permit-only with mitigation between 10–25%, and prohibited above 25% except for essential public infrastructure with independent geological sign-off.\n"}, {"id": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography__33.2-highland-settlement-law", "path": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography/33.2-highland-settlement-law.md", "part": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography", "partTitle": "Hills Mountains And Geography", "title": "Section 33.2 — Highland Settlement Law", "md": "# Section 33.2 — Highland Settlement Law\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 33.2.1 (Right to Remain).** Existing highland communities have a right to remain on ancestral land and are not to be displaced for commercial development without free, prior, and informed consent and fair compensation.\n\n**Article 33.2.2 (Infrastructure Standards).** Highland settlements are entitled to the same baseline infrastructure standards (Part 23) as lowland settlements — roads, water, electricity, healthcare, and schools — adapted to terrain, so that \"hard to reach\" is never used as an excuse for permanent neglect.\n\n**Article 33.2.3 (Disaster Preparedness).** Highland regions maintain landslide and flash-flood early-warning systems as part of Part 26 (Emergency and Crisis) planning.\n\n*Design Note: The instinct in many existing states is to under-invest in mountainous or hilly regions because they are costly to service; this Article makes that under-investment a constitutional breach, not merely an unfortunate budget outcome.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 23-infrastructure, 26-emergency-and-crisis/natural-disasters.md, 33.1\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 33.2.4 (Infrastructure Parity Deadline).** Highland settlements must reach baseline infrastructure parity with lowland settlements of comparable population within 10 years of this Constitution's adoption, tracked in the annual Auditor-General report.\n"}, {"id": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography__33.3-geographic-zoning-and-land-classification", "path": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography/33.3-geographic-zoning-and-land-classification.md", "part": "33-hills-mountains-and-geography", "partTitle": "Hills Mountains And Geography", "title": "Section 33.3 — Geographic Zoning and Land Classification", "md": "# Section 33.3 — Geographic Zoning and Land Classification\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 33.3.1 (National Land Classification System).** All territory is classified into zones (urban, agricultural, forested, highland, coastal, protected) with zone-specific rules that govern together with, not in place of, local government authority under Part 07.\n\n**Article 33.3.2 (Rezoning Procedure).** Rezoning requires public notice, an environmental and social impact assessment, and a vote of the affected local council, preventing quiet reclassification for private benefit.\n\n**Article 33.3.3 (Cross-Reference to Clean Cities).** Urban zones are additionally governed by the cleanliness and sanitation standards of Part 35.\n\n*Design Note: A single classification system, applied consistently from hilltop to coastline, is what lets the rest of this Constitution's terrain-specific rules (13, 24, 32, 33, 35) actually interlock instead of contradicting each other.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 07-local-government, 13-environment, 35-clean-cities-and-sanitation\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 33.3.4 (Rezoning Timeline).** A rezoning application requires a minimum 60-day public notice period and a local council vote no sooner than 90 days after filing.\n"}, {"id": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety__34.1-low-violence-policy", "path": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety/34.1-low-violence-policy.md", "part": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety", "partTitle": "Low Violence And Public Safety", "title": "Section 34.1 — The Low-Violence Constitutional Policy", "md": "# Section 34.1 — The Low-Violence Constitutional Policy\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 34.1.1 (Constitutional Commitment).** The State commits, as a matter of constitutional principle rather than ordinary policy, to the sustained reduction of violence — interpersonal, domestic, communal, and state — as measured by publicly reported metrics reviewed annually.\n\n**Article 34.1.2 (Prevention Before Punishment).** Government budgets must demonstrate investment in violence-prevention (education, mental health access, poverty reduction, community mediation) proportionate to, and not merely as an afterthought to, spending on policing and punishment.\n\n**Article 34.1.3 (State Violence Constrained).** The State itself is bound by the strictest proportionality standard when using force — police and military use of force is governed by the standards in Part 39 (policing) and Part 05/38 (use-of-force liability) — because a constitution that asks citizens to be less violent must first bind its own institutions.\n\n**Article 34.1.4 (Annual Violence Report).** An independent Violence Reduction Commission publishes an annual report on trends, causes, and government performance against this Article, submitted to the Legislature and made public.\n\n*Design Note: \"Low violence\" is deliberately framed as a metric-driven, budget-linked commitment rather than a slogan — the annual report in 34.1.4 is what keeps it enforceable rather than aspirational.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 34.2, 34.3, 34.4, 14-criminal-law, 39-security-and-international-extended/policing-standards.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 34.1.5 (Reporting Metrics).** The annual Violence Reduction Commission report (34.1.4) tracks, at minimum: homicide rate, domestic-violence incidence, youth-violence incidence, incarceration rate, and reoffending rate, each benchmarked against the prior five-year rolling average.\n\n**Article 34.1.6 (Budget Ratio Target).** Government budgets must show prevention-oriented spending (education, mental health, poverty reduction, mediation) reaching parity with enforcement spending within 10 years, on a published annual glide path.\n"}, {"id": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety__34.2-weapons-control", "path": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety/34.2-weapons-control.md", "part": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety", "partTitle": "Low Violence And Public Safety", "title": "Section 34.2 — Weapons Control", "md": "# Section 34.2 — Weapons Control\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 34.2.1 (Licensing Principle).** Ownership of weapons capable of lethal force is subject to licensing that includes the psychological-fitness principles of Article 31.3, adapted for civilian applicants, plus safe-storage requirements.\n\n**Article 34.2.2 (Prohibited Categories).** Weapons whose primary design purpose is mass casualty have no lawful civilian use anywhere in the territory; regional variation is permitted only for traditional, sporting, and defensive categories, not for this prohibited category.\n\n**Article 34.2.3 (Registry and Tracing).** A world-linked weapons registry allows tracing of any weapon used in a crime back to its licensed owner, supporting both prevention and prosecution.\n\n*Design Note: This Article sets a floor (no mass-casualty weapons, mandatory licensing with fitness screening) while leaving the harder question of ordinary civilian firearm policy to regional law, since cultures vary enormously and reasonably on this point.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 34.1, 31.3, 38-penal-code-extended\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 34.2.4 (Licence Renewal).** Civilian weapons licences are reviewed every three years, incorporating the psychological-fitness screening standard of Article 31.3 adapted for civilian risk assessment.\n"}, {"id": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety__34.3-violence-prevention-and-rehabilitation", "path": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety/34.3-violence-prevention-and-rehabilitation.md", "part": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety", "partTitle": "Low Violence And Public Safety", "title": "Section 34.3 — Violence Prevention and Rehabilitation", "md": "# Section 34.3 — Violence Prevention and Rehabilitation\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 34.3.1 (Early Intervention).** Schools (Part 11) and health systems (Part 12) must maintain early-identification and support programs for children and adults showing risk factors for future violence, framed as care rather than surveillance.\n\n**Article 34.3.2 (Restorative Options).** Wherever consistent with victim safety, restorative-justice processes (38-penal-code-extended/restorative-justice.md) are offered as an alternative or supplement to incarceration for non-severe violent offenses.\n\n**Article 34.3.3 (Reentry Support).** Formerly incarcerated persons are guaranteed housing, employment, and health-access support on release, consistent with 38-penal-code-extended/parole-and-reentry.md, because failed reentry is one of the strongest predictors of repeat violence.\n\n*Design Note: Prevention and rehabilitation are treated here as safety infrastructure, on the same constitutional footing as roads or hospitals — not as leniency, but as the most effective known lever for reducing future harm.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 34.1, 38-penal-code-extended, 11-education, 12-health\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 34.3.4 (Coverage Target).** Early-identification and support programs under Article 34.3.1 must be available in 100% of public schools within 8 years of adoption, with interim coverage targets of 50% by year 4.\n"}, {"id": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety__34.4-community-policing-standards", "path": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety/34.4-community-policing-standards.md", "part": "34-low-violence-and-public-safety", "partTitle": "Low Violence And Public Safety", "title": "Section 34.4 — Community Policing Standards", "md": "# Section 34.4 — Community Policing Standards\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 34.4.1 (De-Escalation First).** Policing standards require de-escalation training and require it as the default response, with force used only as a last resort and always proportionate to the threat.\n\n**Article 34.4.2 (Community Oversight).** Every policing jurisdiction maintains a civilian oversight board with power to review use-of-force incidents and recommend discipline, independent of police command.\n\n**Article 34.4.3 (Transparency).** Use-of-force incidents are logged and reported publicly in aggregate, consistent with the transparency principles of Part 27.\n\n*Design Note: A \"low violence\" society requires policing that itself models low violence; this section applies the same restraint the Constitution asks of citizens to the institutions asking citizens to comply.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 34.1, 39-security-and-international-extended/policing-standards.md, 27-transparency-and-accountability\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 34.4.4 (Reporting Interval).** Use-of-force aggregate data (34.4.3) is published quarterly, with any incident resulting in serious injury or death reported to the civilian oversight board within 72 hours.\n"}, {"id": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation__35.1-public-cleanliness-standard", "path": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation/35.1-public-cleanliness-standard.md", "part": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation", "partTitle": "Clean Cities And Sanitation", "title": "Section 35.1 — Public Cleanliness Standard", "md": "# Section 35.1 — Public Cleanliness Standard\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 35.1.1 (Constitutional Standard).** Every municipality must maintain public spaces — streets, parks, transit, waterways — to a defined cleanliness standard, measured by litter density, waste-collection frequency, and public sanitation surveys conducted at least annually.\n\n**Article 35.1.2 (Shared Duty).** Cleanliness is a shared civic duty as well as a government responsibility: littering, illegal dumping, and public sanitation violations are enforceable civil offenses with graduated fines, paired with accessible public bins and collection infrastructure so that the duty is realistic to meet.\n\n**Article 35.1.3 (No Cleanliness Without Infrastructure First).** No cleanliness enforcement action may be taken in an area where the municipality has failed to provide adequate waste infrastructure — the duty runs on the state first, the citizen second.\n\n*Design Note: This models the widely admired public-cleanliness culture of cities such as Japan's, but deliberately sequences the duty — infrastructure obligation first, citizen obligation second — so it functions as genuine civic culture rather than as a pretext for punishing poverty.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 35.2, 13-environment/pollution.md, 23-infrastructure\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 35.1.4 (Survey Frequency).** Public sanitation and litter-density surveys are conducted at minimum annually in every municipality above 10,000 residents, published in the Clean Cities Certification report (Article 35.4.1).\n"}, {"id": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation__35.2-waste-management-and-recycling", "path": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation/35.2-waste-management-and-recycling.md", "part": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation", "partTitle": "Clean Cities And Sanitation", "title": "Section 35.2 — Waste Management and Recycling", "md": "# Section 35.2 — Waste Management and Recycling\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 35.2.1 (Universal Collection).** Every settlement, urban or highland (see Part 33), is entitled to regular waste collection as baseline infrastructure.\n\n**Article 35.2.2 (Sorting and Recycling Mandate).** Municipalities must provide sorted collection (organic, recyclable, hazardous, general) and meet minimum recycling-rate targets set and reviewed by the Environmental Authority.\n\n**Article 35.2.3 (Producer Responsibility).** Manufacturers bear extended responsibility for the end-of-life disposal of packaging and durable goods, funding collection and recycling systems rather than leaving the cost to municipalities alone.\n\n*Design Note: Article 35.2.3 shifts a meaningful share of the cost of cleanliness away from municipal budgets and onto the producers whose packaging creates the waste, which is what makes the ambitious standard in 35.1 fiscally realistic.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 35.1, 13-environment, 09-economy\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 35.2.4 (Recycling Rate Targets).** Municipalities must reach a minimum 40% recycling rate within 5 years and 65% within 12 years of adoption, with the Environmental Authority publishing a public compliance league table.\n"}, {"id": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation__35.3-urban-cleanliness-culture-and-education", "path": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation/35.3-urban-cleanliness-culture-and-education.md", "part": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation", "partTitle": "Clean Cities And Sanitation", "title": "Section 35.3 — Urban Cleanliness Culture and Education", "md": "# Section 35.3 — Urban Cleanliness Culture and Education\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 35.3.1 (Curriculum Requirement).** Civic cleanliness and environmental stewardship are taught as part of the compulsory civic-education curriculum under Part 11, from early childhood, on the premise that a clean society is a taught culture, not only an enforced rule.\n\n**Article 35.3.2 (Community Participation Programs).** Municipalities are encouraged, and may be required by regional law, to run community clean-up and shared-space stewardship programs, particularly involving schools.\n\n*Design Note: The most cited feature of famously clean cities is not the fine schedule but decades of civic education normalising the behavior — this section constitutionalizes that education, not just the enforcement.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 35.1, 11-education/compulsory-education.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 35.3.3 (Curriculum Hours).** Civic cleanliness and stewardship education must comprise at minimum 20 instructional hours per school year at primary level and 10 hours at secondary level.\n"}, {"id": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation__35.4-clean-cities-certification-system", "path": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation/35.4-clean-cities-certification-system.md", "part": "35-clean-cities-and-sanitation", "partTitle": "Clean Cities And Sanitation", "title": "Section 35.4 — Clean Cities Certification System", "md": "# Section 35.4 — Clean Cities Certification System\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 35.4.1 (Certification Tiers).** A World Clean Cities Certification (Bronze/Silver/Gold/Platinum) is awarded annually to municipalities meeting the cleanliness, recycling, and air/water-quality standards of Parts 13 and 35, published as a public league table.\n\n**Article 35.4.2 (Funding Incentive).** Certified municipalities receive priority access to infrastructure grants under Part 08/23, creating a fiscal incentive for continued performance rather than relying on enforcement alone.\n\n*Design Note: Public league tables have repeatedly proven more effective at sustaining civic pride and continued investment than penalties alone; this Article turns that observation into standing law.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 35.1, 35.2, 08-public-finance, 23-infrastructure\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 35.4.3 (Tier Thresholds).** Bronze requires meeting the universal floor of Article 35.1; Silver requires a 50% recycling rate; Gold requires 65% plus verified air- and water-quality compliance under Part 13; Platinum requires all Gold criteria plus a demonstrated 5-year improving trend.\n"}, {"id": "36-media-integrity__36.1-press-freedom-baseline", "path": "36-media-integrity/36.1-press-freedom-baseline.md", "part": "36-media-integrity", "partTitle": "Media Integrity", "title": "Section 36.1 — Press Freedom Baseline", "md": "# Section 36.1 — Press Freedom Baseline\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 36.1.1 (Guarantee).** Freedom of the press is guaranteed and may not be restricted by prior censorship; restrictions after publication must meet the strict proportionality test of Article 1.1.4.\n\n**Article 36.1.2 (Source Protection).** Journalists may not be compelled to reveal confidential sources except by court order in the narrowest, most serious circumstances (e.g., preventing imminent loss of life), consistent with 27-transparency-and-accountability/whistleblower-protection.md.\n\n**Article 36.1.3 (No State Monopoly).** The State may fund public broadcasting (21-media-and-information/public-broadcasting.md) but may never hold a monopoly over news media or criminalize criticism of the government as such.\n\n*Design Note: This baseline is placed here, alongside the anti-corruption Articles of 36.2–36.4, deliberately: a free press and an incorruptible press are two halves of the same guarantee against unaccountable power.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.2 (Freedom of Expression), 21-media-and-information, 36.2\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 36.1.4 (Source Protection Order Threshold).** A court order compelling source disclosure under Article 36.1.2 requires a documented, imminent, and otherwise unpreventable risk to life, reviewable on an expedited appeal within 14 days.\n"}, {"id": "36-media-integrity__36.2-media-ownership-transparency", "path": "36-media-integrity/36.2-media-ownership-transparency.md", "part": "36-media-integrity", "partTitle": "Media Integrity", "title": "Section 36.2 — Media Ownership Transparency", "md": "# Section 36.2 — Media Ownership Transparency\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 36.2.1 (Public Beneficial-Ownership Register).** All media outlets above a defined audience threshold must disclose their true beneficial owners in a public register, updated within 30 days of any change of control.\n\n**Article 36.2.2 (Anti-Concentration Rule).** No single owner or affiliated group may control more than a defined share of any regional media market, to prevent both government and private capture of the public information space.\n\n**Article 36.2.3 (Political Ownership Conflict).** A sitting officeholder or their immediate family may not hold a controlling stake in a media outlet operating in their own jurisdiction, to prevent officeholders from owning the outlets meant to hold them accountable.\n\n*Design Note: Media corruption most often begins with hidden ownership, not with individual bad reporting — transparency of ownership is therefore the highest-leverage single rule in this Part.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 36.1, 04-integrity-branch equivalents in Part 37, 9-economy/monopolies.md\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 36.2.4 (Concentration Cap).** No single owner or affiliated group may control more than 30% of any regional news-media market by audience share, reviewed annually by the Integrity Branch.\n"}, {"id": "36-media-integrity__36.3-anti-disinformation-and-manipulation-offenses", "path": "36-media-integrity/36.3-anti-disinformation-and-manipulation-offenses.md", "part": "36-media-integrity", "partTitle": "Media Integrity", "title": "Section 36.3 — Anti-Disinformation and Manipulation Offenses", "md": "# Section 36.3 — Anti-Disinformation and Manipulation Offenses\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 36.3.1 (Narrow Definition).** \"Disinformation offense\" means the knowing, deliberate fabrication or distortion of material facts, spread at scale, for the purpose of manipulating an election, financial market, or public-health decision — never mere error, satire, opinion, or good-faith reporting later found incorrect.\n\n**Article 36.3.2 (No General Speech Crime).** This Article creates no general offense of \"false speech\"; it is narrowly limited to the deliberate, large-scale manipulation defined in 36.3.1, to prevent it from being misused to suppress dissent or criticism.\n\n**Article 36.3.3 (Platform Transparency Duty).** Large digital platforms must disclose paid political advertising and algorithmic amplification of political content, enforced under Part 17 (Digital Law) and Part 40 (Technology Extended).\n\n*Design Note: The line drawn in 36.3.1–36.3.2 is the most important one in this section — nearly every historical abuse of \"anti-disinformation\" law has come from blurring the line between genuine manipulation and unwelcome truth or opinion; this Article is written to make that blurring textually difficult.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.2 (Freedom of Expression), 17-digital-law, 40-technology-and-future-extended\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 36.3.4 (Adjudication Standard).** A disinformation-offense finding under Article 36.3.1 requires proof, to the criminal standard, of both the factual falsity and the deliberate intent to manipulate; a civil regulator may never make this finding — only a court, with full appeal rights.\n"}, {"id": "36-media-integrity__36.4-media-corruption-crimes-and-punishments", "path": "36-media-integrity/36.4-media-corruption-crimes-and-punishments.md", "part": "36-media-integrity", "partTitle": "Media Integrity", "title": "Section 36.4 — Media Corruption: Crimes and Punishments", "md": "# Section 36.4 — Media Corruption: Crimes and Punishments\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 36.4.1 (Bribery of Journalists or Editors).** Paying or accepting payment to suppress, distort, or fabricate news coverage is a corruption offense under Part 37, carrying licence revocation for the outlet and personal criminal liability for the individuals involved.\n\n**Article 36.4.2 (Government Bribery of Media).** A government official who channels public funds or advertising to a media outlet in exchange for favorable coverage commits an aggravated corruption offense under Part 37, with mandatory referral to the Integrity Courts.\n\n**Article 36.4.3 (Undisclosed State Propaganda).** State-funded content presented to the public as independent journalism, without disclosure of its government funding, is a disinformation offense under Article 36.3.\n\n*Design Note: This Article is where Parts 36 and 37 (Integrity Branch) meet directly — media corruption is prosecuted exactly like any other corruption offense, with the same courts, penalties, and asset-recovery tools, rather than being treated as a separate or lesser category.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 36.1, 36.2, 37-integrity-branch, 38-penal-code-extended\n\n\n## Implementation Detail\n\n**Article 36.4.4 (Penalty Schedule).** Bribery of journalists or editors (36.4.1): licence suspension of 1–5 years and personal fine up to twice the bribe value. Government bribery of media (36.4.2): mandatory referral to the Integrity Courts, minimum 2-year custodial sentence for the officials involved, and permanent disqualification under Article 31.7.\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__083-integrity-branch", "path": "37-integrity-branch/083-integrity-branch.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-083 · The Integrity Branch", "md": "# WC-083 · The Integrity Branch\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **083 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The fourth power: mission, structure and constitutional armour\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document establishes the Integrity Branch as a co-equal branch with the mission of zero tolerance of corruption, and arms it against every known form of political retaliation.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Mission\n\n1.1 The mission of the Integrity Branch is to achieve and sustain a state of zero tolerance of corruption: prevention by design, detection by data, investigation without fear, prosecution without delay, and recovery without compromise.\n\n1.2 Corruption means the abuse of entrusted power for private gain, in the public or private sector, as defined in document 108.\n\n1.3 The Branch serves the people, not the government of the day.\n\n### Article 2 — Structure\n\n2.1 The Branch comprises: the Integrity Commission, with prevention, education, investigation and prosecution divisions; the Integrity Courts; the regional integrity offices; and the Citizens' Oversight Assembly.\n\n2.2 The Auditor-General cooperates under the gateway of document 075.\n\n2.3 The Branch's jurisdiction covers all branches, all levels, all public money, and the private sector where it touches public functions.\n\n### Article 3 — Constitutional armour\n\n3.1 The Branch is a branch of the state; its existence, mandate and budget protection cannot be reduced by ordinary statute, only by the full amendment procedure with the unamendable-core guarantee of document 008.\n\n3.2 No emergency, war, or caretaker period suspends the Branch; its powers expand to emergency procurement automatically under document 080.\n\n3.3 Attacks on the Branch - defunding, staffing freezes, hostile reorganisation, criminalising its lawful methods - are high constitutional offences.\n\n### Article 4 — Duty to act\n\n4.1 The Branch shall register and assess every corruption complaint within fifteen days and communicate its decision to the complainant.\n\n4.2 Declining to investigate a credible complaint requires written reasons, appealable to the Integrity Courts.\n\n4.3 The Branch may not practise deliberate slowness: statutory clocks apply to every procedural stage.\n\n### Article 5 — Self-integrity\n\n5.1 The Branch is itself under the strongest regime: its officials disclose assets annually and at every promotion, its files are audited by the Auditor-General, and its closed cases are reviewed by the Citizens' Oversight Assembly.\n\n5.2 Commission investigators are rotated across sectors and regions on statutory cycles.\n\n5.3 Corruption inside the Branch carries aggravated penalties under document 108.\n\n## Commentary\n\nEvery known anti-corruption agency has faced the same two threats: capture by the executive it is meant to police, and erosion by budget and statute. This document answers both with branch rank, amendment-level protection, and automatic expansion of jurisdiction in emergencies, where corruption historically peaks. The self-integrity regime - asset disclosure, external audit, citizen review of closed cases - is deliberately stricter than for any other institution, because the Branch's only asset is credibility. The duty to act with clocks and appealable refusals prevents the quiet pathology of agencies that bury complaints in procedural amber.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Hong Kong | ICAC ordinance and three-pronged mandate | Prevention, education and prosecution model |\n| 2 | Singapore | Prevention of Corruption Act; CPIB direct reporting | Fearsome independence with prime-minister reporting |\n| 3 | Botswana | DCEC establishment doctrine | Multi-tool anti-graft body in a developing state |\n| 4 | Ukraine | NABU establishment lessons 2015-2024 | Political-resistance hardening |\n| 5 | Kenya | 2010 Constitution Ethics and Anti-Corruption Commission | Constitutional anchoring of the agency |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-007 · The Four Branches of the State](../01-founding-charter/007-the-four-branches.md)\n- [WC-084 · The Integrity Commission](084-integrity-commission.md)\n- [WC-107 · The Citizens' Oversight Assembly](107-citizens-oversight.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__084-integrity-commission", "path": "37-integrity-branch/084-integrity-commission.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-084 · The Integrity Commission", "md": "# WC-084 · The Integrity Commission\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **084 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The operating agency: divisions, powers and duties\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document charters the Integrity Commission's four divisions and equips them with the powers that make corruption a high-risk, low-reward business.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Divisions\n\n1.1 The Commission operates through: a Prevention Division that audits systems and designs controls; an Education Division that trains officials and informs citizens; an Investigations Division with full police powers; and a Prosecution Division that prosecutes corruption offences directly.\n\n1.2 A Data Office fuses procurement, asset, company, customs and banking signals into risk models, governed by document 086 safeguards.\n\n1.3 A Witness and Whistleblower Office administers rewards and protections under document 091.\n\n### Article 2 — Core powers\n\n2.1 The Commission may: require production of any document including bank, tax and company records under judicial confirmation within seventy-two hours; enter and search premises; arrest suspects; freeze accounts and assets; and operate covert surveillance under the warrant regime of document 027.\n\n2.2 It may summon any person, including sitting officials, under the fast-track immunities procedure of document 102.\n\n2.3 It may seize passports of flight-risk suspects for up to thirty days, renewable by the Integrity Courts.\n\n### Article 3 — Direct prosecution\n\n3.1 The Prosecution Division files and conducts corruption cases without the consent of any prosecutorial monopoly, ending the classic veto point of political prosecutors.\n\n3.2 The general prosecutor retains parallel jurisdiction for non-corruption offences flowing from the same facts; conflicts are resolved by the Integrity Courts.\n\n3.3 Plea cooperation and immunity-from-prosecution of minor participants require judicial approval and publication of reasons in aggregate.\n\n### Article 4 — Obligations\n\n4.1 The Commission answers every complaint in writing within fifteen days and publishes monthly statistics of intake, closure and duration.\n\n4.2 Its annual report to Parliament details systemic weaknesses and names the institutions that failed to implement its prevention recommendations.\n\n4.3 Failure to implement prevention recommendations within a statutory deadline is an integrity offence of the responsible official.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe decisive design choice is direct prosecution: comparative experience shows that where the anti-graft agency must hand cases to a politically-controlled prosecution service, the cases die in transfer. The fusion Data Office follows the success of pattern-detection in tax and customs administrations, where fraud is found by anomaly rather than complaint. Mandatory published statistics on case duration defeat the last refuge of corrupt systems, the investigation that formally exists but never ends.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Singapore | CPIB powers of arrest, search and investigation | Full investigative toolkit |\n| 2 | Hong Kong | ICAC operational divisions | Divisional architecture |\n| 3 | Lithuania | STT prevention department model | Systemic prevention audits |\n| 4 | Georgia | 2004-2012 patrol and data-led enforcement lessons | Data-led risk targeting |\n| 5 | Rwanda | OMB investigation and prosecution integration | Integrated case pipeline |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-088 · Prosecution of Corruption](088-corruption-prosecution.md)\n- [WC-087 · Investigative Powers and Safeguards](087-investigative-powers.md)\n- [WC-086 · The Preventive Mandate](086-preventive-mandate.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__085-commission-appointment-and-tenure", "path": "37-integrity-branch/085-commission-appointment-and-tenure.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-085 · Commission Appointment and Tenure", "md": "# WC-085 · Commission Appointment and Tenure\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **085 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** How commissioners are chosen, protected and removed\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines the selection, protection and removal of the Integrity Commissioners so that no government can install or intimidate them.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Selection\n\n1.1 The Commission has five members: the Chief Commissioner, two members with judicial or prosecutorial background, one forensic accountant or auditor, and one member from civil society.\n\n1.2 Members are selected by an independent panel of retired appellate judges and integrity-council delegates after a public open call; finalists are heard in public parliamentary session; appointment is by the President without discretion.\n\n1.3 One-time-only presidential refusal with reasons returns the file once.\n\n### Article 2 — Terms\n\n2.1 Members serve staggered, single, non-renewable eight-year terms, so no single government appoints a majority.\n\n2.2 Salaries are set at the level of apex-court judges and never reduced.\n\n2.3 Full-time incompatibility rules apply: no party membership, no private practice, no directorships.\n\n### Article 3 — Removal\n\n3.1 Removal only by the Constitutional Court for proven incapacity, grave misconduct or corruption, on referral by two thirds of Parliament or the Citizens' Oversight Assembly.\n\n3.2 Suspension pending removal may be granted only by the Integrity Courts, for cause, thirty days at a time.\n\n3.3 A removed member never serves in public office again.\n\n### Article 4 — Vacancies and interim\n\n4.1 Vacancies are filled within ninety days by the same panel procedure; the senior deputy acts in the interim with full powers but no policy changes.\n\n4.2 Quorum rules keep the Commission functional at four members; below that, the Integrity Courts assume urgent caseload.\n\n4.3 Staggering is preserved by appointing successors for the remainder of terms.\n\n## Commentary\n\nEvery serious regression story begins with the appointment capture of the integrity agency: packed panels, loyalists, revolving chairmen. The design answers with an independent panel, staggered single terms, salary floors, and judicial-only removal - the exact formula that kept supreme audit institutions alive under hostile governments. The public hearing of finalists borrows from judicial-appointment practice and turns candidate quality into a visible public fact. The interim rule denies the sabotage technique of leaving an agency headless and therefore immobile.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Ireland / UK | open-competition judicial-style panels | Public-call selection of guardians |\n| 2 | United States | FBI director single ten-year term | Long single term, stagger logic |\n| 3 | Mexico | IFE/INE councillor appointment saga | Panel-of-notables selection for electoral organs |\n| 4 | European Union | Court of Auditors member tenure | Protected term and salary floor |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-083 · The Integrity Branch](083-integrity-branch.md)\n- [WC-071 · Judicial Appointments](../03-governance/071-judicial-appointments.md)\n- [WC-107 · The Citizens' Oversight Assembly](107-citizens-oversight.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__086-preventive-mandate", "path": "37-integrity-branch/086-preventive-mandate.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-086 · The Preventive Mandate", "md": "# WC-086 · The Preventive Mandate\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **086 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Corruption-proofing systems before money moves\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document gives the Commission the mandate to redesign systems so that corruption becomes structurally difficult, not merely punishable.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — System audits\n\n1.1 The Prevention Division audits high-risk systems - procurement, permits, licensing, subsidies, taxes, customs, land administration - on a published rolling plan and on Integrity-Court order after any systemic finding.\n\n1.2 Audit standards are international and published; conclusions are public with management responses.\n\n1.3 Re-audit within eighteen months is mandatory for every critical finding.\n\n### Article 2 — Corruption proofing of bills\n\n2.1 Every bill affecting money, permits or discretion carries a corruption-risk assessment prepared by the Commission, annexed before the second reading.\n\n2.2 The assessment identifies discretion points, single-officer chokepoints, information asymmetries and conflict risks, and proposes controls.\n\n2.3 Parliament departing from the assessment states reasons in the committee report.\n\n### Article 3 — Process redesign\n\n3.1 Where a system produces repeated findings, the Commission may impose design duties: dual control, random assignment, service charters with deadlines, digital queues and audit trails.\n\n3.2 Agencies implement within statutory deadlines or face the follow-up regime of document 077.\n\n3.3 Redesigns are validated by red-team exercises that attempt simulated corruption.\n\n### Article 4 — Education and culture\n\n4.1 The Education Division delivers mandatory annual training to all officials, school curricula modules, and public campaigns with measurable outcome indicators.\n\n4.2 Integrity training completion is a condition of delegation of financial authority under document 066.\n\n4.3 The Commission publishes an annual Integrity Index of institutions under document 290.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPrevention is where the zero in zero-corruption is actually earned: prosecution alone yields scandals, systems yield integrity. Corruption-risk assessment of bills follows the practice pioneered in Korea and adopted in several Eastern European states, and its cost is trivial against the damage of a single captured law. The red-team validation of redesigns borrows from security practice, because control systems that are never attacked cannot be said to resist attack. The Integrity Index creates public competitive pressure that moves institutions more reliably than sanctions do.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | South Korea | Anti-corruption and Impact Assessment on bills | Corruption-risk analysis of legislation |\n| 2 | Hong Kong | ICAC Prevention Department studies | Systems-process audits |\n| 3 | Georgia | service-delivery redesign, one-stop public service halls | Discretion-reduction redesign |\n| 4 | Estonia | X-Road and digital queue design | Audit-trail by architecture |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-084 · The Integrity Commission](084-integrity-commission.md)\n- [WC-066 · Public Service Codes and Duties](../03-governance/066-public-service-codes.md)\n- [WC-099 · Public Procurement](099-procurement.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__087-investigative-powers", "path": "37-integrity-branch/087-investigative-powers.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-087 · Investigative Powers and Safeguards", "md": "# WC-087 · Investigative Powers and Safeguards\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **087 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The full toolkit with judges, clocks and remedies for abuse\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document grants the Commission the complete investigative toolkit of a specialised police force, under judicial control and strict time limits.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The toolkit\n\n1.1 Investigators hold the powers of judicial police: identification, summons, interrogation with counsel, search, seizure, arrest, technical and financial surveillance, controlled deliveries and covert operations for corruption offences.\n\n1.2 Bank, tax, customs, telecom and company data are produced on Commission order confirmed by a judge within seventy-two hours.\n\n1.3 Cross-border evidence follows the fast MLA gateways of document 105.\n\n### Article 2 — Judicial control\n\n2.1 Covert measures require the specialised warrant of document 027; seizures and freezes are confirmed by the Integrity Courts within forty-eight hours.\n\n2.2 Interrogations are recorded; minors, and witnesses in fear, receive special protections.\n\n2.3 The duty of candour applies to investigators: exculpatory material is preserved and disclosed.\n\n### Article 3 — Clocks\n\n3.1 Preliminary inquiry concludes within ninety days; investigation within two years, extendable once by the Integrity Courts for complex financial cases.\n\n3.2 Charging decisions issue within sixty days of file closure.\n\n3.3 Delays are reported monthly and breaches sanctionable.\n\n### Article 4 — Remedies for abuse\n\n4.1 Targets may complain to the Integrity Courts; unlawful evidence is excluded under document 031; malicious investigation yields compensation and officer discipline.\n\n4.2 The Citizens' Oversight Assembly reviews closed files where no charge followed for patterns of targeting.\n\n4.3 Publishing targets' names before charge is prohibited; leaks are offences.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPowerful investigators without safeguards become the corruption they hunt, so every power sits inside a judicial confirmation and a statutory clock. The ninety-day inquiry limit exists because the 'preliminary investigation' is the classic freezer where inconvenient politicians are parked forever. Conversely, the rights of targets are real: pre-charge anonymity with leak offences prevents the weaponisation of investigation as media punishment, a technique documented in multiple jurisdictions.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Hong Kong | POCO powers and judicial oversight | Strong powers with court confirmation |\n| 2 | Germany | StPO investigative judges and exclusion rules | Judicial control and evidence exclusion |\n| 3 | European Union | EPPO direct investigation powers | Supranational direct investigation model |\n| 4 | Latvia | KNAB combined powers experience | Combined security-and-corruption toolkit calibration |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-084 · The Integrity Commission](084-integrity-commission.md)\n- [WC-088 · Prosecution of Corruption](088-corruption-prosecution.md)\n- [WC-026 · Privacy](../02-rights-and-liberties/026-privacy.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__088-corruption-prosecution", "path": "37-integrity-branch/088-corruption-prosecution.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-088 · Prosecution of Corruption", "md": "# WC-088 · Prosecution of Corruption\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **088 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** From file to verdict: charging, cooperation and appeal\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs the prosecution of corruption cases: charging criteria, cooperation deals, transparency and appeal discipline.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Charging\n\n1.1 The Prosecution Division charges where the evidence would justify conviction, applying published charging criteria; declinations are reasoned and reviewable by the Integrity Courts on complaint.\n\n1.2 Every charging decision is logged with reasons; selective non-prosecution is an offence.\n\n1.3 Statutes of limitation for corruption run from discovery, never from the act, and toll during any obstruction.\n\n### Article 2 — Cooperation and settlement\n\n2.1 Cooperation agreements with minor participants require full disclosure, asset surrender and judicial approval with published reasons in anonymised form.\n\n2.2 Deferred prosecutions of corporations are available with independent monitors, published terms and victim restitution before shareholder distributions.\n\n2.3 Immunity of a principal offender requires the Integrity Courts' consent, a written justification and publication in aggregate annually.\n\n### Article 3 — No negotiation on the essential\n\n3.1 Charge bargaining away corruption offences or asset forfeiture in full is prohibited.\n\n3.2 Sentences below statutory minimums for corruption require written judicial reasons published with the judgment.\n\n3.3 Political or diplomatic pressure on prosecutors is the obstruction offence of document 108.\n\n### Article 4 — Appeals\n\n4.1 Both sides may appeal on law and fact to the Integrity Appeals Chamber.\n\n4.2 Acquittals are appealable where new evidence emerges; no double jeopardy without strict novelty, under document 031.\n\n4.3 Retrials after appellate reversal follow the milder-law rule.\n\n## Commentary\n\nProsecution is where anti-corruption systems are usually strangled politely: declinations without reasons, endless cooperation deals, and settlements without monitors. Each is addressed: reasoned declinations, judicially-approved cooperation with published logic, and the prohibition of bargaining away forfeiture, which was the standard hole in plea practice. Limitation running from discovery follows modern anti-corruption conventions, because complex schemes are designed to surface after limitation would otherwise have run.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United States | DOJ deferred prosecution and monitor practice | Corporate settlement discipline |\n| 2 | United Kingdom | DPP code for prosecutors; deferred prosecution guidance | Published charging criteria |\n| 3 | UNCAC | Arts. 30, 37 immunity and cooperation | Cooperation and immunity standards |\n| 4 | Romania | DNA prosecutorial success and reversal lessons | Strong specialised prosecution with safeguards |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-089 · The Integrity Courts](089-integrity-courts.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n- [WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery](092-confiscation-and-recovery.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__089-integrity-courts", "path": "37-integrity-branch/089-integrity-courts.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-089 · The Integrity Courts", "md": "# WC-089 · The Integrity Courts\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **089 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Specialised, fast, expert judicial track for corruption cases\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document establishes specialised courts for corruption and connected financial crime with expert judges, fast clocks and full transparency.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Establishment\n\n1.1 Integrity Courts of first instance and an Integrity Appeals Chamber are part of the judiciary, staffed by judges selected for financial-crime expertise through the appointments path of document 069.\n\n1.2 Judges serve staggered seven-year assignments in the track, renewable once, with the full independence guarantees of the judiciary.\n\n1.3 Court staff include forensic accountants and data analysts under judicial direction.\n\n### Article 2 — Jurisdiction\n\n2.1 Jurisdiction covers corruption offences, unexplained-wealth cases, procurement fraud, recovery actions, electoral corruption offences, and obstruction of integrity institutions.\n\n2.2 Confiscation and recovery proceedings may run in parallel with criminal cases.\n\n2.3 Conflicts with ordinary jurisdiction are resolved by the joint senate of the apex courts.\n\n### Article 3 — Speed and transparency\n\n3.1 Indictment to trial within twelve months; judgment within six months of closing arguments; breaches are reported to the judicial council.\n\n3.2 Proceedings are public with witness protection exceptions; all judgments are published with reasons, including asset tables.\n\n3.3 Reasons state why the court departed, if it did, from sentencing guidelines.\n\n### Article 4 — Supervision powers\n\n4.1 Integrity Courts confirm seizures, freezes and passport orders, and review investigation legitimacy within forty-eight hours of complaint.\n\n4.2 They supervise corporate compliance monitors and can void contracts procured by corruption under document 148.\n\n4.3 Their structural orders - deadlines on institutions - carry contempt escalation under document 067.\n\n## Commentary\n\nSpecialised anti-corruption chambers have shown the speed and expertise advantage in Slovakia, Kenya's anti-corruption divisions and the UK's economic-crime courts; the corpus adopts the model while keeping judges inside the ordinary judicial career to protect quality. Financial expertise of staff matters as much as judges, because forensic accounting is where these trials are won. Publication of asset tables with judgments builds the public record that makes recovery socially credible.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Slovakia | Specialised Criminal Court | Dedicated track for corruption and organised crime |\n| 2 | United Kingdom | economic crime courts and CPL confiscation practice | Specialised financial judges |\n| 3 | Kenya | anti-corruption divisions practice | Fast-track divisions |\n| 4 | Bangladesh | specialised speedy tribunal lessons | Speed calibrated with rights |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-088 · Prosecution of Corruption](088-corruption-prosecution.md)\n- [WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery](092-confiscation-and-recovery.md)\n- [WC-067 · Structure of the Judiciary](../03-governance/067-judiciary-structure.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__090-asset-disclosure", "path": "37-integrity-branch/090-asset-disclosure.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-090 · Asset and Interest Disclosure", "md": "# WC-090 · Asset and Interest Disclosure\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **090 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Public registers of wealth for everyone who exercises public power\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document imposes universal, public, verified asset and interest disclosure on every person who exercises public power, and their close circles.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Who discloses\n\n1.1 Disclosure is mandatory for: elected officials at every level; ministers and political appointees; judges, prosecutors and senior investigators; generals and police commanders; chief executives and boards of state enterprises and agencies; procurement officials above thresholds; tax, customs and land officials; and candidates for any of these offices before nomination is valid.\n\n1.2 Disclosure covers spouse, partner and dependent children, and beneficial interests held by intermediaries or relatives.\n\n1.3 Failure to file blocks entry into office; false filing voids appointment.\n\n### Article 2 — What is disclosed\n\n2.1 Assets: real property, vehicles, watercraft, aircraft, securities, bank and cash holdings above one year's salary, business ownership, beneficial stakes, debts, and liabilities; and income sources by category.\n\n2.2 Interests: board positions, outside employment, gifts above nominal value from any source, memberships of bodies, and relevant contracts.\n\n2.3 Cryptographic wallets and digital assets are included and traceable by address.\n\n### Article 3 — Public and verified\n\n3.1 Registers are public online in open data, searchable by name and office, with privacy-safe redaction of home addresses.\n\n3.2 The Commission verifies a risk-based sample of at least twenty percent annually, plus every declaration flagged by analytics, complaints or press.\n\n3.3 Every verification result is published: consistent, inconsistent-under-explanation, or referred for investigation.\n\n### Article 4 — Lifecycle\n\n4.1 Declarations are filed on entry, annually by a fixed calendar date, on promotion, and on exit; exit declarations freeze monitoring for five years of subsequent change.\n\n4.2 Unexplained variation between declarations triggers the wealth procedure of document 089.\n\n4.3 Retention for thirty years in the public archive.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPublic, verified and universal disclosure is the strongest single transparency device, and the design choices are deliberately aggressive: pre-nomination validity blocks the run-for-immunity trick, family coverage blocks the proxy-holding trick, and exit declarations with five-year monitoring block the cash-out-on-retirement trick. The twenty percent risk-based verification with publication of every result turns the register from a filing exercise into a live detection instrument, and the analytics of the Data Office feed it continuously.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Ukraine | e-declaration system 2016 | Public online officials' asset register |\n| 2 | Romania | asset declaration verification agency practice | Verification with publication |\n| 3 | Taiwan | Act on Property-Declaration by Public Servants | Controlled publication with judicial follow-up |\n| 4 | France | HATVP high-authority transparency | Elected officials' interest publication |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-091 · Unexplained Wealth Jurisdiction](091-unexplained-wealth.md)\n- [WC-105 · Public Ethics Codes](105-public-ethics-codes.md)\n- [WC-100 · Conflict of Interest](100-conflict-of-interest.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__091-unexplained-wealth", "path": "37-integrity-branch/091-unexplained-wealth.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-091 · Unexplained Wealth Jurisdiction", "md": "# WC-091 · Unexplained Wealth Jurisdiction\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **091 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The reversal of the burden for unjustifiable enrichment of officials\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document creates civil unexplained-wealth orders and their criminal counterpart, shifting the burden to officials whose wealth outruns their lawful income.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The order\n\n1.1 Where an official subject to disclosure holds assets disproportionate to lawful income, the Integrity Commission may apply for an Unexplained Wealth Order before the Integrity Courts.\n\n1.2 The order requires the respondent to explain the acquisition with evidence; the standard is the balance of probabilities.\n\n1.3 The claim is against the asset, not only the person: death or flight does not extinguish it.\n\n### Article 2 — Triggers\n\n2.1 Triggers are analytical (declaration variance), transactional (cash patterns, luxury purchases), investigative (connected persons), or external (credible reports).\n\n2.2 Third parties holding assets for officials are joined to the procedure with the same burden.\n\n2.3 Fictional loans, gambling or gift narratives require documentary proof, not testimony alone.\n\n### Article 3 — Effect and sanction\n\n3.1 Unexplained assets are confiscated to the treasury; deliberate false explanation adds the criminal offence of obstruction with aggravated corruption grading.\n\n3.2 Confiscation is without prejudice to criminal prosecution, which may follow on the same facts.\n\n3.3 Wrongly-issued orders are compensated on proof of lawful origin with costs.\n\n### Article 4 — Constitutional calibration\n\n4.1 The reversed burden applies only to disclosure-subject officials, only to assets acquired during office or one year before, and only before an independent court - not an agency.\n\n4.2 Proceedings are public; judgments publish the full asset table.\n\n4.3 The guarantee against self-incrimination is preserved: silence in the criminal file cannot alone ground conviction, but the civil confiscation may proceed on the unexplained balance.\n\n## Commentary\n\nUnexplained wealth provisions are the most litigated and most valuable instruments in modern anti-corruption law; the corpus adopts them with the calibration that survives constitutional review: officials only, assets from the office period, independent courts, and a genuine opportunity to explain. The in-rem character defeats the classic escape of dying or fleeing with the loot. Pairing civil confiscation with criminal exposure keeps the incentive structure honest: explanation is possible, but fabricated explanations are a new offence.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United Kingdom | UWO regime 2017; CPL recovery | Civil recovery with reversed burden |\n| 2 | Singapore | explanation duties in corruption law | Officials' explanation obligations |\n| 3 | Hong Kong | POCO s.10 unexplained property offence | Criminal unexplained property |\n| 4 | Italy | legge Spazzacorrotti seizure practice | Preventive seizure for officials |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-090 · Asset and Interest Disclosure](090-asset-disclosure.md)\n- [WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery](092-confiscation-and-recovery.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__092-confiscation-and-recovery", "path": "37-integrity-branch/092-confiscation-and-recovery.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery", "md": "# WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **092 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Taking the profit out of corruption, fully and by default\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document makes confiscation automatic, comprehensive and victim-restoring, so that corruption is never a rational investment.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Automatic confiscation\n\n1.1 Conviction of a corruption offence carries mandatory confiscation of the proceeds, benefits and instrumentalities, without judicial discretion to waive.\n\n1.2 Equivalent-value confiscation applies where proceeds are dissipated, transformed or hidden.\n\n1.3 Third-party transfers in bad faith or without value are set aside.\n\n### Article 2 — Civil and non-conviction based\n\n2.1 Where prosecution is impossible by death, flight or immunity, civil recovery proceeds against assets on the balance of probabilities.\n\n2.2 Preventive freezing within forty-eight hours of charge application preserves the fund.\n\n2.3 Management of frozen assets is professional, costed and reported; depreciation is a recoverable fault of the managing agency.\n\n### Article 3 — Distribution\n\n3.1 Recovery priority: victims of the corrupt act; the public treasury; restitution to affected institutions.\n\n3.2 A published recovery report names amounts, cases and destinations annually.\n\n3.3 No recovery fund may be spent without budget-line transparency.\n\n### Article 4 — Corporate assets\n\n4.1 Confiscation extends to corporate proceeds and to profits of shareholders who knew, constructive knowledge included for control persons.\n\n4.2 Compliance programmes reduce individual culpability, never the confiscation of proceeds.\n\n4.3 Dissolution of repeat-offender firms follows the corporate-crime rules of document 138.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe economics of deterrence live in recovery, not in prison: as long as expected profit exceeds expected sanction times probability, corruption pays. Mandatory confiscation with equivalent-value powers removes the profit line of the ledger entirely. Priority to victims converts recovery from a treasury windfall into a justice instrument, which sustains public cooperation; published destination reports prevent the recycling of recovered loot into discretionary funds, an abuse documented in several systems.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | confiscation directives non-conviction based recovery | Extended and civil confiscation |\n| 2 | United States | forfeiture practice with limits | In rem forfeiture mechanics |\n| 3 | United Kingdom | POCA recovery and administrators | Professional asset management |\n| 4 | Switzerland | foreign illicit assets restitution practice | Victim-priority restitution |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-106 · International Cooperation and Asset Recovery](106-international-recovery.md)\n- [WC-135 · Corporate and White-Collar Crime](../05-justice-and-penal-law/135-corporate-crime.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__093-whistleblower-protection", "path": "37-integrity-branch/093-whistleblower-protection.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-093 · Whistleblower Protection and Rewards", "md": "# WC-093 · Whistleblower Protection and Rewards\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **093 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The sensor network of integrity: protection, compensation and rewards\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document protects whistleblowers absolutely and pays them for value delivered, turning every official into a potential integrity sensor.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Protected disclosures\n\n1.1 Any person who, in good faith and on reasonable belief, reports corruption, illegality, danger to public health or safety, fraud or cover-up, is protected against dismissal, demotion, harassment, blacklisting and any retaliation, in public or private employment.\n\n1.2 Protection attaches at disclosure, survives leaving the job, and extends to witnesses and helpers.\n\n1.3 Anonymous channels preserve protection; identity is revealed only with consent or sealed court order.\n\n### Article 2 — Reverse burden\n\n2.1 In retaliation cases, the employer bears the burden of proving that adverse action was unrelated to the disclosure.\n\n2.2 Interim reinstatement is available within fourteen days pending the case.\n\n2.3 Remedies: reinstatement, full compensation including career-loss, aggravated damages for malicious retaliation, and criminal sanction for retaliators in office.\n\n### Article 3 — Rewards\n\n3.1 Where a disclosure leads to recovery or verified savings, the whistleblower receives a statutory share of ten to thirty percent by decision of the Integrity Courts, capped and taxed as ordinary income.\n\n3.2 Rewards are paid before treasury transfer, within ninety days of realisation.\n\n3.3 Bad-faith or fabricated reporting forfeits protection and constitutes the offence of false accusation.\n\n### Article 4 — Channels\n\n4.1 Protected channels exist inside every employer above fifty staff, with the Integrity Commission as universal external channel, accepting anonymous reports in all languages.\n\n4.2 Feedback on report status is owed within fifteen days; investigation updates follow at statutory intervals.\n\n4.3 Gagging clauses, NDAs and settlement terms that suppress crime reporting are void.\n\n## Commentary\n\nWhistleblowers are the single most productive source of corruption discovery, ahead of audits and media; the design protects them with the reverse burden and interim reinstatement that made US and French systems functional, and pays them on the US False Claims Act model, which turned qui tam reporting into an industry of integrity. Voiding suppressive NDAs closes the private exit through which organisations buy silence. Feedback duties within fifteen days are the cheap fix for the trust problem: channels without answers train people not to report.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United States | False Claims Act qui tam; SEC and CFTC reward programmes | Percentage rewards for whistleblowers |\n| 2 | European Union | Directive 2019/1937 | Channel and reverse-burden standards |\n| 3 | South Africa | Protected Disclosures Act | Broad protection coverage |\n| 4 | France | Sapin II regime | Internal-external channel sequencing |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-094 · Journalists' Source Protection](094-source-protection.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n- [WC-105 · Public Ethics Codes](105-public-ethics-codes.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__094-source-protection", "path": "37-integrity-branch/094-source-protection.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-094 · Journalists' Source Protection", "md": "# WC-094 · Journalists' Source Protection\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **094 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The press as integrity institution: sources, seizures and surveillance\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document protects journalism's information supply chain so that exposure of corruption remains possible when institutions fail.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The core\n\n1.1 No journalist shall be compelled to reveal a source or hand over material gathered for journalism, save by the strict three-part court test of document 019.\n\n1.2 Seizures in newsrooms require judicial authorisation, executed with independent counsel present; material unrelated to the warrant is sealed.\n\n1.3 Surveillance of journalists, their metadata or their families by any authority is the criminal offence of obstruction of press freedom.\n\n### Article 2 — Data protection for sources\n\n2.1 Newsroom data enjoys heightened protection: no bulk seizure, no cloud grabs without the same warrant, and encryption tools for journalists are lawful and protected.\n\n2.2 Platform data requests touching journalists go through the journalistic-material gateway with prior notice and challenge opportunity.\n\n2.3 Device searches at borders are limited to copy-and-seal under judicial control, never to rummage.\n\n### Article 3 — Anti-SLAPP\n\n3.1 Vexatious lawsuits against journalism on public-interest matters are dismissed early with costs against the plaintiff; repeat abusive claimants pay treble costs.\n\n3.2 Judges certify claims as abusive on motion of the defendant, media councils or the Ombudsman.\n\n3.3 Judgments report abusive-filing patterns to the Integrity Commission.\n\n### Article 4 — Cooperation\n\n4.1 The Integrity Commission operates a journalism liaison: leaked material is received with source-protection guarantees, evaluated and, if corroborated, converted into investigations.\n\n4.2 Journalists are never compelled to become witnesses in cases arising from their reporting, save the general three-part test.\n\n4.3 Killings, threats and harassment of journalists are priority offences under document 128.\n\n## Commentary\n\nInvestigative journalism is the integrity system of last resort, and its infrastructure fails quietly through surveillance, seizures and lawsuits rather than censorship. Anti-SLAPP rules follow the EU directive and the strongest provincial practice, because litigation is the modern instrument to bury exposés. The liaison channel acknowledges reality: most major corruption scandals began with a reporter, and the state's duty is to protect the channel, not to capture it.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Convention | Goodwin v UK; Sanoma | Source protection standard |\n| 2 | European Union | anti-SLAPP directive 2024 | Early dismissal of abusive suits |\n| 3 | Sweden | constitutional source protection, criminal liability for leaks of source identity | Strongest statutory protection |\n| 4 | Belgium | source protection act | Legislated newsroom search limits |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-021 · Press and Media Freedom](../02-rights-and-liberties/021-press-and-media-freedom.md)\n- [WC-093 · Whistleblower Protection and Rewards](093-whistleblower-protection.md)\n- [WC-095 · Open Government and Freedom of Information](095-open-government.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__095-open-government", "path": "37-integrity-branch/095-open-government.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-095 · Open Government and Freedom of Information", "md": "# WC-095 · Open Government and Freedom of Information\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **095 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Public information by default with narrow, logged exceptions\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document establishes the radical-openness regime: every public document releasable unless law says no, with the presumption resolving for release.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The principle\n\n1.1 All information held by public bodies is public, unless exempted by a specific statute naming a protectable interest.\n\n1.2 Exemptions are narrow, harm-tested, time-limited and never class-wide: national security narrowly, personal privacy, ongoing investigations, and cabinet deliberation for a maximum of ten years.\n\n1.3 Commercial confidentiality may never shield public contracts, prices or performance data.\n\n### Article 2 — The right\n\n2.1 Any person requests any document without stating reasons, identity or nationality; answers issue within fifteen working days, extensions reasoned and appealable.\n\n2.2 Partial release is the rule; blackening is documented with exemption grounds per redaction.\n\n2.3 The public-interest override forces release where the public benefit outweighs the exemption, always for corruption and abuse information.\n\n### Article 3 — Proactive publication\n\n3.1 Registers, budgets, contracts, subsidies, permits, statistics, laws and their drafts are published proactively in open, machine-readable formats, free of charge.\n\n3.2 The open-data catalogue follows the common formats of document 165 with per-dataset owners and update duties.\n\n3.3 FOI performance - volumes, response times, refusal grounds - is reported quarterly per body.\n\n### Article 4 — Records law\n\n4.1 Every significant public act is recorded and archived; private communication channels for public business are prohibited, and auto-deletion of public records is a criminal offence.\n\n4.2 Archives transfer to openness on statutory schedules of thirty years, ten for decision files.\n\n4.3 Destruction of records during investigations is the aggravated offence of document 108.\n\n## Commentary\n\nSunlight is the classic disinfectant, and the corpus writes the strongest version of it: no reason for asking, harm-tested exemptions, class-wide secrecy forbidden, and the public-interest override with teeth for corruption information. The records clauses matter as much as release: corruption migrates to private messaging apps precisely where archives do not reach, so off-channel public business is banned, and auto-deletion becomes a crime rather than an oops.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Sweden | Freedom of the Press Act 1766 principle of public access | Oldest and strongest openness regime |\n| 2 | Mexico | 2002 transparency law and IFAI/INAI model | Autonomous transparency body |\n| 3 | India | RTI Act practice | No-reason requests and PI override |\n| 4 | Ukraine | open data and ProZorro publication | Proactive registers by default |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-166 · Right to Information: Operations](../07-public-administration/166-right-to-information-operations.md)\n- [WC-096 · Beneficial Ownership Transparency](096-beneficial-ownership.md)\n- [WC-026 · Privacy](../02-rights-and-liberties/026-privacy.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__096-beneficial-ownership", "path": "37-integrity-branch/096-beneficial-ownership.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-096 · Beneficial Ownership Transparency", "md": "# WC-096 · Beneficial Ownership Transparency\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **096 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Ending anonymous companies: who owns what, publicly\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document abolishes anonymous corporate ownership and makes every owner of every company publicly traceable.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The register\n\n1.1 Every legal person registered or operating in the state files a verified beneficial-ownership declaration identifying natural persons holding or controlling above ten percent, voting control, or management-control positions.\n\n1.2 The register is public, machine-readable, free, and searchable by person and company; banks, notaries, procurement bodies and land registries use it under law.\n\n1.3 Filing is a condition of incorporation, property registration, contracting with the state, and opening bank accounts.\n\n### Article 2 — Verification and accuracy\n\n2.1 Registrars verify declarations against identity documents, cross-check with company, tax, land and customs data, and publish confidence flags.\n\n2.2 Discrepancies between the register and data held by regulated professionals are reported duties for banks and notaries.\n\n2.3 Stale declarations older than one year block new filings and state contracts.\n\n### Article 3 — Foreign and complex structures\n\n3.1 Foreign entities doing business or holding assets must disclose their ownership chains to natural persons, up to the top; trusts and foundations file equivalent declarations.\n\n3.2 Nominee arrangements are lawful only with registered, published declarations of the beneficial owner.\n\n3.3 Non-compliant foreign structures may not buy land, bid for contracts, or sue in the courts.\n\n### Article 4 — Sanctions\n\n4.1 False declaration is a corruption-grade offence for the declarer and the nominee; the company is struck and its assets frozen pending true ownership.\n\n4.2 Professional enablers - notaries, lawyers, accountants - who knowingly construct anonymity are charged as participants.\n\n4.3 Privacy of natural persons is protected by address redaction only, never by name shielding.\n\n## Commentary\n\nAnonymous companies are the freight system of corruption: bribes, looted assets and sanctions evasion all travel in bearer-structure vehicles. The register design follows the strongest practice - free, public, verified, machine-readable - and closes the foreign-entity hole through which domestic registries are bypassed. Striking non-compliant companies from access to land, courts and contracts converts transparency from a filing form into a licence to operate.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United Kingdom | PSC register 2016 | Public persons-with-significant-control register |\n| 2 | Estonia | e-business registry accuracy | Verified digital company data |\n| 3 | Denmark | beneficial-ownership register practice | Cross-check verification |\n| 4 | Ukraine | open company register | Free public registry |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-095 · Open Government and Freedom of Information](095-open-government.md)\n- [WC-150 · Corporate Governance and Directors' Duties](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/150-corporate-governance.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__097-campaign-finance", "path": "37-integrity-branch/097-campaign-finance.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-097 · Campaign Finance", "md": "# WC-097 · Campaign Finance\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **097 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Clean money for clean politics: caps, public funds, real-time disclosure\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document takes big and dark money out of elections through caps, public funding, disclosure and enforcement that bites.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The ceiling\n\n1.1 Campaign spending is capped per party and candidate at statutory amounts indexed to inflation, with separate caps for the official campaign period.\n\n1.2 Contributions from natural persons are capped per donor per cycle; contributions from legal persons, state contractors and foreign sources are prohibited.\n\n1.3 Loans below market terms, in-kind services and price-discounted media count at full value within the cap.\n\n### Article 2 — Public funding\n\n2.1 Public campaign funds support parties by a formula of votes and seats, with additional support for new entrants qualifying signatures.\n\n2.2 Public funds are paid early and on compliance with disclosure; misuse is recoverable with penalty.\n\n2.3 Broadcast free time of document 057 substitutes for a share of the fund.\n\n### Article 3 — Real-time disclosure\n\n3.1 Donations above the statutory threshold are reported to the Electoral Commission within forty-eight hours and published online within seventy-two.\n\n3.2 Full accounts, audited by chartered auditors, are filed within sixty days after elections with every expense itemised above threshold.\n\n3.3 Sponsorship of events, third-party campaigns and issue advertising within ninety days of elections are within the regime and disclosed.\n\n### Article 4 — Enforcement\n\n4.1 Overspending voids public funding and attracts fines up to the overspend; knowing violations forfeit seats and annul elections under document 052.\n\n4.2 Circumvention through proxies and associations is a corruption offence of document 108.\n\n4.3 The Electoral Commission audits a random thirty percent of accounts and all accounts of winners.\n\n## Commentary\n\nMoney is the oxygen of political capture, and the regime cuts both its supply - contribution caps, corporate prohibition - and its shadow - real-time, itemised, audited disclosure. Prohibiting state contractors from donating severs the direct quid pro quo channel documented in every serious procurement-fraud study. The seat-forfeiture sanction is the one that history shows changes behaviour: fines are budgeted as risk, lost seats are not.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Canada | Canada Elections Act contribution caps and audits | Strict caps with serious audit |\n| 2 | Germany | party finance transparency rulings | Disclosure and public funding balance |\n| 3 | France | campaign account commissions and reimbursement | Audited accounts gating reimbursement |\n| 4 | United Kingdom | PPERA; real-time large donations reporting | 48-72 hour large-donation reports |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-053 · Electoral Integrity and Dispute Resolution](../03-governance/053-electoral-integrity.md)\n- [WC-098 · Lobbying Transparency](098-lobbying-transparency.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__098-lobbying-transparency", "path": "37-integrity-branch/098-lobbying-transparency.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-098 · Lobbying Transparency", "md": "# WC-098 · Lobbying Transparency\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **098 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Influence in the light: registers, footprints and cooling-off\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document regulates lobbying: registration, spending disclosure, a legislative footprint, and the ban on buying access.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The register\n\n1.1 Professional lobbyists - in-house and agency - register before any advocacy, naming clients, subjects, bills and budgets.\n\n1.2 Meetings with officeholders covered by the register are logged by the officeholder: date, participants, subject, position.\n\n1.3 The register is public, free and machine-readable; acting while unregistered is an offence.\n\n### Article 2 — The legislative footprint\n\n2.1 Every bill publishes its footprint: which meetings were held with whom, and which submissions were received, at each stage.\n\n2.2 Non-public drafts circulating in consultations are logged and published on introduction.\n\n2.3 Ministers' and senior officials' diaries are published monthly under document 063.\n\n### Article 3 — Access and gifts\n\n3.1 Selling access is prohibited: paid meetings, sponsored travel, honoraria and think-tank funding in exchange for contact are offences for payer and receiver.\n\n3.2 Gifts and hospitality to officeholders follow the zero threshold of document 099.\n\n3.3 Charities and academia follow the register when advocacy is a paid activity.\n\n### Article 4 — Cooling-off\n\n4.1 Officeholders and senior officials may not lobby their former institution for five years; ministers for seven; procurement officials for five on their files.\n\n4.2 Breaches void the lobbying privilege and are offences for both sides.\n\n4.3 The register records cooling-off status to prevent accidental hiring into the ban.\n\n## Commentary\n\nLobbying is legitimate and inevitable; invisible lobbying is where corruption incubates. The footprint device follows the EU transparency register's evolution and the US LARA-style state laws, and it answers the question every scandal raises: who was in the room when this clause was drafted. The five-to-seven-year cooling-off follows the Canadian regime, the longest well-tested, and voiding the privilege plus two-sided liability makes it enforceable in practice.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | Transparency Register and足迹 obligations | Register with footprint logic |\n| 2 | Canada | Lobbying Act five-year ban | Longest-tested cooling-off |\n| 3 | United States | LARA and FARA disclosure logic | Foreign-agent and client disclosure |\n| 4 | Slovenia / Chile | lobbying statutes with diary logs | Officeholder logging duty |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-097 · Campaign Finance](097-campaign-finance.md)\n- [WC-101 · Gifts, Hospitality and the Revolving Door](101-gifts-and-revolving-door.md)\n- [WC-054 · The Legislative Process](../03-governance/054-legislative-process.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__099-procurement", "path": "37-integrity-branch/099-procurement.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-099 · Public Procurement", "md": "# WC-099 · Public Procurement\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **099 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Open contracting: the single largest corruption channel, closed\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs the purchase of everything by the state: e-procurement, open data, integrity filters and debarment.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Open by construction\n\n1.1 All procurement above thresholds is run on a single national e-procurement system: one portal, one machine-readable pipeline, public at every stage.\n\n1.2 Tenders, specifications, questions, answers, bids, evaluation scores, contracts, amendments, payments and performance are published in the standard open-contracting data structure.\n\n1.3 Only genuinely narrow exceptions - narrow security grounds confirmed by a judge - allow unpublished awards, and the exception itself is published.\n\n### Article 2 — Design rules\n\n2.1 Splitting to avoid thresholds is void; framework and central-purchasing bodies aggregate for scale.\n\n2.2 Specifications are outcome-based, neutral to brands, and designed by officers without supplier contact; tailored specs that fit one bidder trigger automatic review.\n\n2.3 Evaluation criteria and weights are fixed and published before offers; scoring sheets are individual, signed and published.\n\n### Article 3 — Integrity filters\n\n3.1 Bidders declare beneficial ownership, conflicts, prior debarments and subcontracts; declarations are verified through the register of document 093.\n\n3.2 Conflict-screening blocks officials with ties to bidders from the process; assignment is logged.\n\n3.3 The Commission runs a market-watch analytics unit: single-bidder rates, price dispersion, amendment and cost-overrun patterns, publication lag, and cartel-signature tests, publishing quarterly risk reports by agency.\n\n### Article 4 — Debarment and remedies\n\n4.1 Corruption, cartel conduct and fraud convictions debar the firm and control persons for ten years; systemic breaches for five.\n\n4.2 Debarment lists are public with cross-recognition under international agreements.\n\n4.3 Losing bidders challenge awards before the administrative courts in a standstill window of fifteen days with fast decision in thirty.\n\n## Commentary\n\nProcurement is where between ten and thirty percent of public investment is typically lost to corruption, so the regime is designed as an open pipeline rather than a set of prohibitions: publication by architecture, as in Ukraine's ProZorro, where visibility is the enforcement. Analytics-based market watch follows the most productive modern supervision, because cartels and tailored specifications are found in data patterns, not complaints. The standstill challenge window is the small procedural detail that makes the whole system justiciable in time to matter.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Ukraine | ProZorro system 2016 | Single public e-procurement pipeline |\n| 2 | Chile | ChileCompra transparency | Published procurement data |\n| 3 | Georgia | procurement reform results | Simple rules, published awards |\n| 4 | European Union | procurement directives remedies | Standstill and review procedures |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-175 · Procurement Operations](../07-public-administration/175-procurement-operations.md)\n- [WC-096 · Beneficial Ownership Transparency](096-beneficial-ownership.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__100-conflict-of-interest", "path": "37-integrity-branch/100-conflict-of-interest.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-100 · Conflict of Interest", "md": "# WC-100 · Conflict of Interest\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **100 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Public duty first: disclosure, recusal and management\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines conflicts of interest for every officeholder and imposes the machinery of disclosure, recusal and sanction.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The rule\n\n1.1 A conflict exists where a personal, family, financial or political interest could improperly influence, or appear to influence, the exercise of public duty.\n\n1.2 The appearance standard applies: what a reasonable citizen would think matters.\n\n1.3 Conflicts are managed, never hidden; disclosure is owed at the moment of knowledge, before acting.\n\n### Article 2 — Management\n\n2.1 Disclose to the superior, ethics officer or commission; recuse from decisions, files and negotiations; screens and divestments where recusal is impossible.\n\n2.2 Recused officials do not attend, advise or receive information on the matter.\n\n2.3 Blind trusts are the standard instrument for portfolios of ongoing financial interest, with certified entry and exit.\n\n### Article 3 — Family and post-employment\n\n3.1 Hiring of relatives in one's office or supervision chain is prohibited; relatives may not be contracted by the office.\n\n3.2 Post-employment conflicts follow the cooling-off regime of document 099.\n\n3.3 Campaign staff and donors are conflicted from procurement and appointments in the winner's administration for its first year.\n\n### Article 4 — Sanctions\n\n4.1 Non-disclosure and conflicted action are integrity offences; acts taken in conflict are voidable at the instance of the state.\n\n4.2 Personal gain obtained through conflict is confiscated under document 092.\n\n4.3 Repeated conflicts are presumptive corruption under document 108.\n\n## Commentary\n\nConflict rules are the constitutional hygiene of public power, and their failure mode is always the same: the small exception, the family hire, the helpful meeting. The article makes the appearance standard explicit, which is the doctrinal core that keeps the rule usable, and it makes conflicted acts voidable - a remedy with deterrent structure, since the deal dies with the conflict. Campaign-donor conflicts in the first year attack the spoils economy that follows every election.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United States | 18 USC 208; government ethics rules | Recusal and appearance standards |\n| 2 | European Union | ethics framework and revolving-door rules | Post-office conflict management |\n| 3 | Canada | Conflict of Interest Act | Blind trust and family rules |\n| 4 | Chile | probidad statutes | Latin-American probity regime |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-090 · Asset and Interest Disclosure](090-asset-disclosure.md)\n- [WC-101 · Gifts, Hospitality and the Revolving Door](101-gifts-and-revolving-door.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__101-gifts-and-revolving-door", "path": "37-integrity-branch/101-gifts-and-revolving-door.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-101 · Gifts, Hospitality and the Revolving Door", "md": "# WC-101 · Gifts, Hospitality and the Revolving Door\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **101 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Zero gifts, and a five-year lock on cashing office into private jobs\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document enforces the zero-gift rule and the revolving-door cooling-off regime with void contracts and two-sided liability.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Zero threshold\n\n1.1 Officeholders accept no gifts, hospitality, travel, discounts or advantages connected to their function, above nominal courtesy value fixed at one month's minimum wage.\n\n1.2 Nominal courtesies are logged and published quarterly; everything else is refused or surrendered to the state.\n\n1.3 Family members and staff are covered by the same rule through the officeholder.\n\n### Article 2 — Sponsored travel\n\n2.1 Official travel is publicly funded; junkets, conference sponsorships and study tours by private interests are prohibited.\n\n2.2 Academic invitations with public academic programmes follow the rules of the host institution, logged and published.\n\n2.3 Secret diplomatic or corporate travel is an offence for both sides.\n\n### Article 3 — The revolving door\n\n3.1 Senior officials and ministers may not take employment, mandates or consultancies with entities they regulated, contracted or supervised for five years; procurement officials for five years on their files; for seven years where they granted licences of major value.\n\n3.2 Approval of exceptions is by the Integrity Commission with publication.\n\n3.3 Golden handshakes and bonuses conditioned on public decisions are bribes.\n\n### Article 4 — Enforcement\n\n4.1 Violations are offences for both the giver and the receiver; the private party is barred from public contracts for ten years.\n\n4.2 Contracts closed through door-traffic within the ban are voidable.\n\n4.3 The register of departures and destinations is public for all senior officials.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe zero-gift rule with a low nominal threshold follows the Singaporean clean-civil-service logic: the gift economy starts small, and its first instalment is the social hook of later capture. Two-sided liability for the private party matters because firms calculate the price of capture as a business cost; making it a ten-year procurement ban changes the arithmetic. The published departure register lets journalists and the Commission watch the door in real time, which is where enforcement actually happens.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Singapore | gift regime and CPIB enforcement culture | Zero-tolerance gift rules |\n| 2 | Canada | five-year post-employment ban | Tested cooling-off duration |\n| 3 | France | revolving door control and the HATVP review | Post-office approval and publication |\n| 4 | European Union | staff regulations cooling-off | Institutional revolving-door limits |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-100 · Conflict of Interest](100-conflict-of-interest.md)\n- [WC-098 · Lobbying Transparency](098-lobbying-transparency.md)\n- [WC-103 · Anti-Bribery and Anti-Extortion](103-anti-bribery.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__102-immunities-restricted", "path": "37-integrity-branch/102-immunities-restricted.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-102 · Immunity Restricted for Corruption", "md": "# WC-102 · Immunity Restricted for Corruption\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **102 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** No shield of office against integrity investigation\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document strips immunity for corruption offences at every level while preserving functional protections for votes and opinions.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The principle\n\n1.1 No immunity of any office - political, diplomatic, parliamentary, judicial - blocks investigation, search, arrest or prosecution of corruption offences.\n\n1.2 Functional immunity for votes and opinions follows document 082 unchanged.\n\n1.3 The fast-track lifting procedure of document 082 applies, with the thirty-day Supreme Court opinion and the sixty-day chamber decision.\n\n### Article 2 — Sitting officials\n\n2.1 The President, ministers and parliamentarians are chargeable without the ordinary preliminary motions where the Integrity Courts certify probable cause on the evidence.\n\n2.2 Pre-trial measures for sitting officials are decided by the Integrity Courts directly, preserving the separation from political bodies.\n\n2.3 Court certification requires documented acts, not political accusation, and is appealable immediately.\n\n### Article 3 — Diplomatic and international\n\n3.1 Diplomatic immunity does not cover active or passive bribery, laundering or recovery actions; the sending state's waiver is presumed requested by treaty.\n\n3.2 Head-of-state immunity abroad excludes international crimes including grand corruption under document 139.\n\n3.3 Official acts defence is unavailable for corruption: corruption is never an official function.\n\n### Article 4 — After office\n\n4.1 Protection ends with the office; prosecution for acts in office is time-barred only by the long limitation of document 085, running from discovery.\n\n4.2 Amnesties and pardons for corruption are void under document 064.\n\n4.3 Successor governments may not extinguish pending corruption cases of predecessors.\n\n## Commentary\n\nFunctional immunity protects democracy; criminal immunity protects criminals, and the corpus separates the two cleanly. Certification by Integrity Courts rather than political bodies answers both the abuse risk of political prosecutions and the abuse risk of political shields, keeping the gate judicial and evidence-based. Voiding amnesties and pardons for corruption closes the documented pipelines of self-protection that leaders carved into their exit strategies in multiple systems.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | France | functional-only immunity doctrine | Split of functional and criminal immunity |\n| 2 | UNCAC | Art. 30 and 31 immunities considerations | International standard on immunity limits |\n| 3 | France / Korea | ex-presidents prosecuted practice | No ex-officio protection |\n| 4 | Italy | rejection of corruption amnesty practice | Amnesty limits for corruption |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-082 · Immunities and Their Limits](../03-governance/082-legislative-immunities.md)\n- [WC-088 · Prosecution of Corruption](088-corruption-prosecution.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](../05-justice-and-penal-law/136-international-crimes.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__103-anti-bribery", "path": "37-integrity-branch/103-anti-bribery.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-103 · Anti-Bribery and Anti-Extortion", "md": "# WC-103 · Anti-Bribery and Anti-Extortion\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **103 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The classic offences, modernised for facilitation payments and chains\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines the bribery and extortion offences completely, abolishing the loopholes that survive in older codes.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Bribery defined\n\n1.1 Active bribery: promising, offering or giving any undue advantage to a public official or candidate, directly or through intermediaries, for acting or refraining.\n\n1.2 Passive bribery: requesting, agreeing to receive or accepting such advantage, including through family members or related entities.\n\n1.3 The advantage is defined by function: money, goods, services, employment, contracts, forgiveness, discretion exercised in one's favour, quiet charity donations and sponsorship equivalents.\n\n### Article 2 — Facilitation payments abolished\n\n2.1 Small or routine facilitation payments are bribes; no de minimis carve-out exists.\n\n2.2 Cultural or customary practice is no defence; the defence of duress exists only for genuine extortion, immediately reported.\n\n2.3 Hospitality, gifts and travel connected to any official decision follow the zero rule of document 099.\n\n### Article 3 — Extortion and demand-side\n\n3.1 Extortion by officials - demanding advantages for lawful duties - is an aggravated corruption offence.\n\n3.2 The coerced payer who reports promptly is protected and not charged, and may recover the payment.\n\n3.3 Private-to-private bribery in commercial dealings is a distinct offence where damage flows to non-consenting owners.\n\n### Article 4 — Chains and intermediaries\n\n4.1 Using intermediaries, consultants, charitable foundations, agents or relatives is the same offence with the intermediary liable.\n\n4.2 Ignorance of an intermediary's corruption is unavailable to principals with grossly negligent supervision.\n\n4.3 Beneficial owners of bribing entities share criminal liability with the entity.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe abolition of facilitation payments follows the strictest modern statutes and the FCPA-Sapin convergence: every carve-out becomes the channel through which systems rot, because the small bribe trains the big one. Protecting the coerced payer who reports converts extortion victims into witnesses, which is the only practical detection route for demand-side corruption. Intermediary liability with gross-negligence exposure for principals closes the consultant structure through which most grand bribery is laundered.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United Kingdom | Bribery Act 2010 s.1-2, 7 | No facilitation carve-out; corporate offence |\n| 2 | France | Sapin II influence peddling and corporate duties | Influence-trading and compliance duty |\n| 3 | United States | FCPA and books-records provisions | Payment concealment as offence |\n| 4 | OECD Convention | 1997 convention, and 2022 updated recommendation | Common definitions of the bribery offences |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n- [WC-104 · Anti-Nepotism and Cronyism](104-anti-nepotism.md)\n- [WC-135 · Corporate and White-Collar Crime](../05-justice-and-penal-law/135-corporate-crime.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__104-anti-nepotism", "path": "37-integrity-branch/104-anti-nepotism.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-104 · Anti-Nepotism and Cronyism", "md": "# WC-104 · Anti-Nepotism and Cronyism\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **104 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Merit in public jobs and contracts, family and friendship aside\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document abolishes the private staffing of the state: hiring, appointments and contracts by merit, with family and political ties excluded.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Public employment\n\n1.1 Public jobs are filled by the competitive rules of document 065; hiring relatives, partners or household members into one's office, supervision or budget line is prohibited.\n\n1.2 Career acceleration, postings and perks for relatives of officials are void acts with disciplinary consequence.\n\n1.3 Political appointments are confined to the capped private offices of document 063 with public job descriptions.\n\n### Article 2 — Public contracts\n\n2.1 Contracts to family members or their companies are void under the procurement rules; the single-source exception may never cover them.\n\n2.2 Friends, party donors and campaign staff follow the first-year conflict block of document 097.\n\n2.3 Beneficial-ownership checks reveal hidden family ties automatically.\n\n### Article 3 — Boards and honours\n\n3.1 Appointments to state boards, agencies and honours are made by open competition with published criteria; the spoils system is unconstitutional.\n\n3.2 Ambassadorships, regulatory posts and SOE chairs are merit posts, never rewards.\n\n3.3 Appointment files with scores are public for senior posts.\n\n### Article 4 — Enforcement\n\n4.1 Nepotism is a corruption-grade integrity offence for the patron and a void act for the beneficiary's position.\n\n4.2 Salaries paid under void hires are recoverable from both.\n\n4.3 The Commission audits hiring patterns by kinship analytics across agencies annually.\n\n## Commentary\n\nNepotism is the entry-level corruption through which citizens experience the state as someone else's family business, and it is the recruiting ground for grander capture. Making void the hire and recoverable the salary converts it from a free option into a costly exposure. Kinship analytics sounds intrusive but runs on declaration cross-matches that are already public, and it detects the patterns that individual complaints never see.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | France | 2016 loi Sapin II and parliamentary staff rules | Nepotism limits in public staffing |\n| 2 | Italy | civil-service competition tradition | Merit examination culture |\n| 3 | United States | federal anti-nepotism statutes | Statutory family-hire prohibition |\n| 4 | Mexico | constitutional career-service amendments | Professional civil service lock |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-065 · The Civil Service](../03-governance/065-civil-service.md)\n- [WC-100 · Conflict of Interest](100-conflict-of-interest.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__105-public-ethics-codes", "path": "37-integrity-branch/105-public-ethics-codes.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-105 · Public Ethics Codes", "md": "# WC-105 · Public Ethics Codes\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **105 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** One code for all officeholders, statutory and sanctioned\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document creates the single statutory ethics code for every officeholder in the state, with common sanctions and one referee.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — One code\n\n1.1 A single Public Ethics Code applies to elected officials, ministers, advisers, civil servants, judges, military, police, SOE officers and contractors performing public functions.\n\n1.2 The code sets minimum standards; institutions may add stricter, never weaker rules.\n\n1.3 The code is law: it is enacted by organic statute and may not be amended by internal circular.\n\n### Article 2 — Contents\n\n2.1 The code fixes: the duties of documents 066; the disclosure duties of document 090; conflict management of document 097; the gift and door rules of document 099; political neutrality in service delivery; and courtesy standards toward citizens.\n\n2.2 Plain-language guides, case examples and advisory opinions accompany the code.\n\n2.3 Advisory opinions protect officials who act in line with them.\n\n### Article 3 — Referee\n\n3.1 The Integrity Commission administers the code: advisory opinions, investigations, and referrals to the disciplinary councils or the Integrity Courts.\n\n3.2 Disciplinary councils sit with independent chairs under document 066.\n\n3.3 Sanctions are published quarterly in anonymised aggregate.\n\n### Article 4 — Compliance systems\n\n4.1 Employers above fifty staff run internal channels, training and risk registers under the whistleblower regime.\n\n4.2 Compliance failures are attributed to leadership, not only to juniors.\n\n4.3 Certified compliance is a condition for public contracts of major value.\n\n## Commentary\n\nFragmented codes are how standards get gamed: one ministry strict, a state company lax. A single statutory code with one referee and common sanctions ends forum shopping between regimes. The advisory-opinion mechanism is the undervalued gem of modern ethics administration: it converts the code from a trap into guidance, protects good-faith officials, and generates the case law that makes standards concrete.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | New Zealand | Public Service Act standards model | Unified standards instrument |\n| 2 | Canada | Values and Ethics Code family | Multi-institution code architecture |\n| 3 | Hong Kong | ICAC advisory service | Advisory opinions practice |\n| 4 | OECD | ethics recommendation 2017 | Framework standards |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-066 · Public Service Codes and Duties](../03-governance/066-public-service-codes.md)\n- [WC-086 · The Preventive Mandate](086-preventive-mandate.md)\n- [WC-093 · Whistleblower Protection and Rewards](093-whistleblower-protection.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__106-international-recovery", "path": "37-integrity-branch/106-international-recovery.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-106 · International Cooperation and Asset Recovery", "md": "# WC-106 · International Cooperation and Asset Recovery\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **106 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Chasing stolen wealth across borders with speed and standing\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document builds the international machinery: fast MLA, non-conviction-based return, and standing for victim states.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Fast mutual legal assistance\n\n1.1 MLA requests for corruption, fraud and laundering are executed within ninety days through a central authority staffed by the Integrity Commission and the Justice Ministry jointly.\n\n1.2 Bank secrecy, tax confidentiality and corporate secrecy do not block identification, freezing and evidence transfer for corruption offences.\n\n1.3 Urgent freezes execute within forty-eight hours through the Egmont-group financial intelligence channel.\n\n### Article 2 — Non-conviction-based return\n\n2.1 Assets are returned to victim states and victims on civil standards where conviction is impossible by death, flight or immunity, following document 092.\n\n2.2 Comity and human-rights safeguards protect bona fide third parties.\n\n2.3 Return agreements are published; deducted costs are itemised.\n\n### Article 3 — Victim-state standing\n\n3.1 Foreign states may sue in domestic courts to recover looted assets, with equal standing and no national-security carve-out.\n\n3.2 Limitation runs from knowledge of the theft abroad.\n\n3.3 Settlements require publication and victim consultation.\n\n### Article 4 — Foreign bribes and laundering\n\n4.1 Bribery of foreign officials by nationals or residents is prosecutable as domestic bribery, with no double-criminality barrier.\n\n4.2 Laundering definitions cover proceeds of foreign corruption and self-laundering.\n\n4.3 The state recognises foreign confiscation orders and enforces them after rights review.\n\n## Commentary\n\nStolen public money travels; recovery regimes historically did not, and the corpus adopts the strongest instruments - civil standards, no bank-secrecy barrier, standing for victim states - because kleptocracy is transnational by design. Prosecuting foreign official bribery without double-criminality follows the OECD convention's logic to its end point. Publishing return agreements with itemised costs answers the documented abuse where recovery became a fee business for intermediaries.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Switzerland | foreign illicit assets return practice | Return and settlement discipline |\n| 2 | United Kingdom | MLA and POCA international provisions | Freeze-first cooperation |\n| 3 | Jersey / Singapore | asset-recovery cooperation practice | FIU and confiscation cooperation |\n| 4 | UNCAC | Chapter V asset recovery | International recovery framework |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery](092-confiscation-and-recovery.md)\n- [WC-013 · Territory and Jurisdiction](../01-founding-charter/013-territory-and-jurisdiction.md)\n- [WC-139 · Extradition and Mutual Legal Assistance](../05-justice-and-penal-law/139-extradition-and-mla.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__107-citizens-oversight", "path": "37-integrity-branch/107-citizens-oversight.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-107 · The Citizens' Oversight Assembly", "md": "# WC-107 · The Citizens' Oversight Assembly\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **107 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The people's own integrity watchdog, selected by lot\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document creates a standing assembly of ordinary citizens with real powers to audit the integrity system itself.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Composition\n\n1.1 The Citizens' Oversight Assembly comprises fifty members drawn annually by civic lottery, stratified by age, gender, region, education and income, from the volunteers and duty register.\n\n1.2 Members are paid, trained in integrity practice, and barred from party or lobbying office for their term and three years after.\n\n1.3 Members' names are public; their independence is protected by offence.\n\n### Article 2 — Powers\n\n2.1 The Assembly reviews: closed Integrity Commission case files where no charge followed, on a sampled and petition basis; the Commission's annual plan and budget proposal; systemic audit findings; and the integrity performance of institutions.\n\n2.2 It hears complainants, whistleblowers and officials under confidentiality rules, with the power to require answers.\n\n2.3 It issues public recommendations to the Commission, the Integrity Courts and Parliament, which must be answered in writing within sixty days.\n\n### Article 3 — Checks on the watchdog\n\n3.1 The Assembly may refer matters to the Integrity Courts and trigger the removal procedure for commissioners.\n\n3.2 It cannot direct investigations or reveal sealed material; leaks are offences.\n\n3.3 Its own operations are audited by the Auditor-General.\n\n### Article 4 — Local assemblies\n\n4.1 Regional and municipal oversight panels follow the same lottery model with local jurisdiction.\n\n4.2 They review local procurement sampling, asset-disclosure anomalies and service quality.\n\n4.3 Findings feed the national Integrity Index of document 290.\n\n## Commentary\n\nWho audits the auditor is the honest question about any integrity monopoly, and the answer here is the same random technology that legitimises juries: ordinary citizens with training, access and voice. The powers are calibrated - review, hearing, referral, trigger - without prosecutorial direction, preserving professional independence while ending self-certification. Local panels extend the same logic to the level where citizens personally experience corruption, which is where legitimacy is actually built.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Ireland | Citizens' Assembly model | Sortition legitimacy |\n| 2 | Philippines | participatory audit practice with COA | Citizen audit pilots |\n| 3 | South Korea | participatory budget and audit inputs | Citizen-referred audits |\n| 4 | Belgium | permanent citizens' councils | Institutionalised mini-publics |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-058 · Citizens' Assemblies](../03-governance/058-citizens-assemblies.md)\n- [WC-083 · The Integrity Branch](083-integrity-branch.md)\n- [WC-078 · The Auditor-General](../03-governance/078-auditor-general.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "37-integrity-branch__108-corruption-offenses", "path": "37-integrity-branch/108-corruption-offenses.md", "part": "37-integrity-branch", "partTitle": "Integrity Branch", "title": "WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading", "md": "# WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading\n\n> **Part 04 — Integrity Branch - Zero Corruption** · Document **108 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The complete catalogue of corruption crimes with penalty classes\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines every corruption offence with grading into the penalty classes of Part 05, from petty bribery to grand corruption.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Catalogue\n\n1.1 The corruption offences are: active and passive bribery; foreign official bribery; trading in influence; bribery of candidates and office-seekers; extortion by officials; embezzlement and misappropriation of public property; fraud against the treasury; abuse of office or discretion for gain; illegal enrichment under document 089; document falsification in official records; obstruction of integrity institutions; witness and source retaliation; procurement manipulation; conflict-of-interest profiteering; and laundering of corruption proceeds.\n\n1.2 Each offence is defined in the penal document of Part 05 indicated by class; this document fixes names and grading.\n\n1.3 Attempt and conspiracy are punished as completed offences.\n\n### Article 2 — Grading\n\n2.1 Petty corruption below three months' median wage: Class M with mandatory restitution and integrity training; public employment consequences follow regardless of amount.\n\n2.2 Ordinary corruption: Class III to II felony by value and office.\n\n2.3 Grand corruption - by officials above director level, amounts above fifty median annual wages, systemic schemes or harm to life, health or elections: Class I felony with the recovery and disqualification regimes.\n\n### Article 3 — Aggravating and mitigating\n\n3.1 Aggravated: emergency and disaster contexts, judicial or integrity-office abuse, abuse of aid or development funds, and recidivism.\n\n3.2 Mitigating: full voluntary disclosure before discovery, complete restitution, and substantial cooperation with conviction of others.\n\n3.3 Mitigation may never remove mandatory confiscation or disqualification.\n\n### Article 4 — Consequences beyond prison\n\n4.1 Mandatory disqualification from public office and public contracts for a term proportionate to class, from five years to lifetime.\n\n4.2 Loss of honours, pensions forfeited above the contribution-funded part, and voidance of acts procured by the offence.\n\n4.3 Forfeited proceeds follow the recovery priority of document 092.\n\n## Commentary\n\nA corruption catalogue with explicit grading does three jobs at once: it unifies the terminology that fragmented statutes scatter, it ties every offence to proportionate penalty classes, and it imports the doctrine of grand corruption into operational law. The consequence architecture beyond prison - disqualification, pension forfeiture, voidance - is where corruption is actually punished for incumbents, because careers and contracts matter more to them than months.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | UNCAC | Arts. 15-25 criminalisation catalogue | Common offence taxonomy |\n| 2 | Singapore | PCA penalties and disqualification practice | Severity and career consequences |\n| 3 | Hong Kong | POCO offence list | Complete offence coverage |\n| 4 | European Union | corruption directive grading logic | Value-based aggravation |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-103 · Anti-Bribery and Anti-Extortion](103-anti-bribery.md)\n- [WC-114 · Proportionality in Sentencing](../05-justice-and-penal-law/114-proportionality-sentencing.md)\n- [WC-092 · Confiscation and Domestic Recovery](092-confiscation-and-recovery.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- This Part is the zero-corruption architecture; it may be adopted alone as an organic statute where full constitutional reform is not yet possible.\n- Build the registers before the courts: disclosure, beneficial ownership and open contracting are the foundation the prosecutions stand on.\n- Expect resistance precisely where the old system was most profitable - that resistance is the diagnostic.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__116-restorative-justice", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/116-restorative-justice.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-116 · Restorative Justice", "md": "# WC-116 · Restorative Justice\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **116 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Repair as justice: conferencing, agreements and safeguards\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document institutionalises restorative processes as a mainstream track, with safeguards, not as an alternative experiment.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Availability\n\n1.1 Restorative process is available at every stage: diversion before charge, during trial, before sentencing, and post-sentence, for any offence where victim, offender and prosecutor consent.\n\n1.2 Participation is voluntary for all parties, revocable at any time without penalty.\n\n1.3 Facilitators are trained, certified and independent of the prosecution.\n\n### Article 2 — The process\n\n2.1 Conferencing includes victim, offender, supporters and community delegates; it addresses harm, responsibility and reparation.\n\n2.2 Agreements may include apology, restoration, compensation, service to the victim's choice of community, and treatment.\n\n2.3 Agreements are filed with the court, monitored, and completion cancels or reduces the charge or sentence as agreed.\n\n### Article 3 — Safeguards\n\n3.1 No restorative process without full information, legal advice, and separate preparation of the parties.\n\n3.2 Children participate with guardians and specialists, following document 122 rules.\n\n3.3 Failure or breach returns the case to the ordinary track without the process being used as evidence of guilt or as punishment.\n\n### Article 4 — Serious cases\n\n4.1 In grave cases, restorative process supplements but never replaces the criminal proceeding.\n\n4.2 Victim-led models for organised or sexual violence require specialist accreditation and court approval.\n\n4.3 Community panels may take regulatory and minor Class M matters as a formal diversion, with published outcomes.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe evidence from New Zealand family-group conferencing, Rwanda's post-genocide processes and theelfast experiments is consistent: restorative tracks reduce recidivism, raise victim satisfaction and cost a fraction of custody. The design keeps them mainstream but safe: consent, preparation, advice, and the right to return to the ordinary track. Supplement-not-substitute for grave cases answers the legitimate fear that repair language could swallow justice for violence.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | New Zealand | family group conferencing 1989 | Statutory conferencing model |\n| 2 | Rwanda | Gacaca post-genocide practice | Mass-scale community justice lessons |\n| 3 | Belgium | restorative provisions and practice | Mainstream court-annexed track |\n| 4 | Norway | mediation service national model | National mediation infrastructure |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-137 · Rights of Victims](137-victim-rights.md)\n- [WC-115 · Sentencing Guidelines and Procedure](115-sentencing-guidelines.md)\n- [WC-121 · Juvenile Justice](121-juvenile-justice.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__117-prisoners-rights", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/117-prisoners-rights.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-117 · Prisoners' Rights and Prison Regime", "md": "# WC-117 · Prisoners' Rights and Prison Regime\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **117 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Prisons as rehabilitation institutions with enforceable rights\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document makes the prison a rights-bearing rehabilitation institution with inspection, minimum standards and sanctions for keepers.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Residual rights\n\n1.1 Prisoners retain all rights not necessarily removed by the sentence: dignity, safety, health, family contact, expression, religion, education, complaint and vote.\n\n1.2 The aim of the regime is preparation for release from day one.\n\n1.3 Cell space, light, sanitation, food and outdoor time meet minimum standards set by statute and treaty body guidance.\n\n### Article 2 — Regime\n\n2.1 A full day of activity: work paid at public rates with savings, education, vocational training, treatment and exercise.\n\n2.2 No forced labour, no private leasing of convict labour, no profit from prisoner communication pricing.\n\n2.3 Solitary confinement: maximum 15 consecutive days, 22 minimum hours out of cell, never for minors, pregnant women, or the mentally ill, never as punishment, only as documented last resort for safety.\n\n### Article 3 — Oversight\n\n3.1 Independent prison boards of judges and citizens visit unannounced, with private prisoner interviews and published reports.\n\n3.2 The Ombudsman handles complaints with access to everything; retaliation is an offence.\n\n3.3 Prison statistics - population, turnover, incidents, solitary days - publish quarterly.\n\n### Article 4 — Keepers' liability\n\n4.1 Torture, ill-treatment and failure of care in custody are offences of document 121 with command responsibility.\n\n4.2 Every death in custody triggers an independent investigation with a public inquest verdict.\n\n4.3 Cover-ups, camera gaps and record losses are aggravated offences, presumed against the institution.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPrisons are the state's most total power over persons, and their corruption is physical rather than financial: contraband economies, brutality and neglect. The article ends the three worst practices by explicit text: private convict leasing, solitary-as-punishment, and unexplained camera gaps, each documented as the signature of abuse. Paying work at public rates with savings at release is the Nordic device that converts sentence time into reintegration capital.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Norway | Correctional Services Act principles | Rehabilitation-as-regime model |\n| 2 | Nelson Mandela Rules | UN Standard Minimum Rules 2015 | Solitary limits and standards |\n| 3 | Germany | BVerfG life imprisonment doctrine; prisoners' rights | Constitutional prisoners' rights |\n| 4 | United Kingdom | prison ombudsman and death-in-custody investigations | Independent investigation practice |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-120 · Prohibition of Torture and Ill-Treatment](120-prohibition-of-torture.md)\n- [WC-118 · Parole, Release and Reentry](118-parole-and-reentry.md)\n- [WC-057 · Anti-Entrenchment and Political Competition](../03-governance/057-oversight-tools.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__118-parole-and-reentry", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/118-parole-and-reentry.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-118 · Parole, Release and Reentry", "md": "# WC-118 · Parole, Release and Reentry\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **118 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Release as a supervised right with support, not a lottery\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs release preparation, parole decision and reentry support so that punishment ends at the gate.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Release preparation\n\n1.1 From one third of the term, every prisoner has a statutory reintegration plan: housing, work, treatment, documents and a named probation officer.\n\n1.2 Temporary release on licence is used to test reentry, with revocation rules and no new punishment for the original offence.\n\n1.3 Parole hearings are adversarial, with counsel, evidence, victim input and reasons.\n\n### Article 2 — Parole decision\n\n2.1 Parole is granted at two thirds of term as the rule, unless proven current dangerousness on structured, validated criteria.\n\n2.2 Refusal requires written reasons and a re-listing within one year; lifetime parole as a structure is prohibited.\n\n2.3 Parole boards are mixed professional-citizen bodies under the judicial appointments logic.\n\n### Article 3 — Reentry support\n\n3.1 The state provides identity documents, a bridge allowance, health coverage continuity and work-placement priority under statutory right.\n\n3.2 Housing for released persons is a duty of the municipality; homelessness at the gate is a system failure, reportable.\n\n3.3 Voting, work rights and licences restore automatically at release; only court-ordered specific restrictions survive.\n\n### Article 4 — Probation\n\n4.1 Probation is social work with authority: caseload caps, training and dual mandate of support and enforcement.\n\n4.2 Technical violations answer with graduated responses; custody only for repeated or serious breach.\n\n4.3 Records of supervision are sealed on completion under document 133.\n\n## Commentary\n\nRecidivism is manufactured at the gate: no documents, no housing, no work and no coverage produce the first new offence within months, and the corpus treats each as a statutory entitlement rather than charity. Parole at two-thirds as the rule with proven dangerousness as the exception reverses the discretionary parole lottery documented in punitive systems. Automatic restoration of civil rights ends the civil death that some systems impose for life after sentence completion.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Norway / Sweden | reintegration and parole practice | Release-preparation model |\n| 2 | Germany | resocialisation as constitutional aim | Reentry as rights framework |\n| 3 | Portugal | drug and reentry policy results | Support-first outcomes |\n| 4 | Canada | parole board decision rules | Reasoned, structured parole |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-138 · Criminal Records and Rehabilitation of Records](138-criminal-records.md)\n- [WC-117 · Prisoners' Rights and Prison Regime](117-prisoners-rights.md)\n- [WC-132 · Drug Policy](132-drug-policy.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__119-abolition-of-death-penalty", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/119-abolition-of-death-penalty.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-119 · Abolition of the Death Penalty", "md": "# WC-119 · Abolition of the Death Penalty\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **119 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The absolute, unamendable end of state killing\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document implements the abolition of the death penalty in all cases, and defines the substitute maximum penalty.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Abolition\n\n1.1 No court, tribunal, military commission or any other organ may impose or execute the death penalty for any offence, in war or peace.\n\n1.2 Existing death sentences commute to the maximum Class I term with the review rules of document 116.\n\n1.3 This article belongs to the unamendable core and no referendum may restore it.\n\n### Article 2 — Extradition and transfer\n\n2.1 No extradition, expulsion, surrender or transfer to a death-penalty jurisdiction without binding assurances in writing that the penalty will neither be sought nor imposed, verified at diplomatic level.\n\n2.2 Assurance breaches are treaty violations triggering return of the person.\n\n2.3 Life means life with review: the substitute sanction follows document 116, never any pseudo-executive substitute.\n\n### Article 3 — No functional equivalents\n\n3.1 Death penalty by other names - deliberate medical neglect to death, lethal prison conditions, unofficial disappearances - is murder under the homicide article with official aggravation.\n\n3.2 War or emergency powers do not revive capital jurisdiction; military codes conform within one year.\n\n3.3 Public officials cooperating with foreign executions face Class I liability.\n\n### Article 4 — Memory and remedy\n\n4.1 Historic wrongful executions are acknowledged, files opened to families, and compensation paid to descendants on statutory criteria.\n\n4.2 Commemoration and data collection on historic use are a research duty of the Justice Ministry.\n\n4.3 No statute of limitation applies to the associated tortures and frame-ups.\n\n## Commentary\n\nAbolition is total, unamendable, and extended to functional equivalents because states that lose the formal penalty have sometimes kept the substance through neglect and disappearances. The extradition-assurance regime follows European practice with the added teeth of return-on-breach. Acknowledging historic executions is included deliberately: credible justice systems audit their worst history rather than bury it, and families of the wrongfully hanged hold real claims.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | Charter Art. 2; Protocols 6 and 13 ECHR | Abolition in all circumstances |\n| 2 | South Africa | S v Makwanyane | Dignity-based judicial abolition |\n| 3 | Phillippines (2018) / Turkey precedents | no-return clauses | Constitutional locks against reintroduction |\n| 4 | Mexico | 2005 constitutional abolition | Full constitutional text abolition |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-017 · Right to Life and Personal Security](../02-rights-and-liberties/017-right-to-life.md)\n- [WC-120 · Prohibition of Torture and Ill-Treatment](120-prohibition-of-torture.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](136-international-crimes.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__120-prohibition-of-torture", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/120-prohibition-of-torture.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-120 · Prohibition of Torture and Ill-Treatment", "md": "# WC-120 · Prohibition of Torture and Ill-Treatment\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **120 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The absolute ban with exclusion, investigation and command liability\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document implements the absolute prohibition of torture with the full enforcement apparatus: exclusion, investigation, redress and command responsibility.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The ban\n\n1.1 No torture, cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment, by any person, at any time, for any reason, including emergency, war, orders and necessity of state.\n\n1.2 The definition includes mental suffering and pharmacological, environmental and systemic techniques; consent is no defence.\n\n1.3 No derogation, no exception, no balancing.\n\n### Article 2 — Exclusion\n\n2.1 Statements and evidence obtained by torture or coercion are inadmissible in any proceeding, against anyone, including co-accused.\n\n2.2 Derivative evidence from torture is inadmissible; from lawful covert sources is assessed independently.\n\n2.3 The state bears the burden of proving lawful origin where the defence raises a real risk of coercion.\n\n### Article 3 — Investigation and redress\n\n3.1 Every credible allegation triggers a prompt, independent investigation by the Integrity Commission and ordinary prosecutors jointly.\n\n3.2 Victims receive reparation: rehabilitation, compensation, satisfaction and guarantees of non-repetition.\n\n3.3 Command responsibility extends to superior officers and ministers who knew or should have known and failed to act.\n\n### Article 4 — Custody safeguards\n\n4.1 Custody records, medical examination on entry and exit, lawyer access within the first hour, full recording of interrogations, and CCTV coverage with tamper liability are mandatory.\n\n4.2 Secret detention sites and ghost detainees are high constitutional crimes with international notification duty.\n\n4.3 Diplomatic assurances against torture do not permit transfer where risk is real.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe absolute ban is completed by the machinery that makes it real, because torture survives precisely in the procedural dark: unrecorded interviews, camera gaps, medical exams that never happen. Full recording and entry-exit medical exams are the cheapest and most effective documented prevention. Command liability and the prohibition of secret detention answer the two institutional structures through which modern torture systems were built.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | CAT | Arts. 1-16 | Absolute prohibition and investigation duties |\n| 2 | CAT jurisprudence | Agiza and diplomatic assurances line | No transfer on real risk |\n| 3 | Israel | Public Committee against Torture 1999 | No necessity defence for torture |\n| 4 | European Union | CPT visit mechanism | Preventive visiting mechanism |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-117 · Prisoners' Rights and Prison Regime](117-prisoners-rights.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](136-international-crimes.md)\n- [WC-087 · Investigative Powers and Safeguards](../04-integrity-branch/087-investigative-powers.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__121-juvenile-justice", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/121-juvenile-justice.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-121 · Juvenile Justice", "md": "# WC-121 · Juvenile Justice\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **121 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** A separate, welfare-first system for children in conflict with the law\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document builds the juvenile system: age of criminal responsibility, special procedure, custody as last resort and sealing.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Age and scope\n\n1.1 Criminal responsibility begins at fourteen; below it, no prosecution, only child-welfare measures.\n\n1.2 Fourteen to eighteen falls within juvenile jurisdiction in all matters, whatever the offence gravity.\n\n1.3 Juvenile status is by age at act, and measures continue until twenty-one at most.\n\n### Article 2 — Procedure\n\n2.1 Specialised judges, prosecutors and defenders trained in child development; parents present; closed hearings; child-friendly language and timing.\n\n2.2 Diversion is the presumption for all but gravest violence: restorative conferencing, education, treatment.\n\n2.3 No child is tried in adult court, ever; grave offences add resources, not a change of forum.\n\n### Article 3 — Sanctions and custody\n\n3.1 Juvenile custody is a last resort for proven grave violence, in small educational facilities near family, for the shortest period, reviewed every six months.\n\n3.2 Maximum juvenile measure: eight years, with reentry support to twenty-one.\n\n3.3 No juvenile record enters general police databases; sealing is automatic at majority plus two years.\n\n### Article 4 — Welfare safety-net\n\n4.1 Status offences - truancy, curfew, running away, alcohol - are not crimes.\n\n4.2 Children in the justice system retain education, health and family rights fully.\n\n4.3 Detained children receive schooling, and detention never interrupts their curriculum year.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe corpus fixes responsibility at fourteen following the median of advanced systems and CRC guidance, because lower ages criminalise poverty and school failure rather than culpability. Diversion presumption plus sealed records answers the documented criminogenic effect of early justice contact, which is among the strongest findings in the field. Keeping all juveniles in juvenile court with added resources for grave cases ends the transfer practice that produced the worst of both worlds.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | UN CRC | Arts. 37, 40 | Juvenile justice standards |\n| 2 | Germany | JGG youth courts law | Educational principle statute |\n| 3 | Finland | age of responsibility 15 and diversion practice | Diversion-first results |\n| 4 | South Africa | Child Justice Act | Assessment and diversion architecture |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-038 · Rights of the Child](../02-rights-and-liberties/038-childrens-rights.md)\n- [WC-138 · Criminal Records and Rehabilitation of Records](138-criminal-records.md)\n- [WC-116 · Restorative Justice](116-restorative-justice.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__122-homicide", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/122-homicide.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-122 · Homicide and Offences Against Life", "md": "# WC-122 · Homicide and Offences Against Life\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **122 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Murder, manslaughter, infanticide and assistance at peril\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines the offences against life with clear gradations and the duty to rescue.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Murder\n\n1.1 Murder is intentional killing, or intentional infliction of grievous harm foreseeably causing death, with Class I penalty.\n\n1.2 Premeditation, cruelty, killing for concealment of another offence, killing an on-duty official, and domestic-context killings are aggravating circumstances.\n\n1.3 Mass killing and killing with terror purpose carry the terrorism aggravation of document 129.\n\n### Article 2 — Manslaughter\n\n2.1 Manslaughter is unintentional killing by unlawful or grossly negligent act, Class III to II by circumstances.\n\n2.2 Reckless killing in traffic, construction and industry follows the corporate-crime rules where systemic failure is present.\n\n2.3 Provocation reduces class only under the strict limits of document 113.\n\n### Article 3 — Infanticide and end of life\n\n3.1 Killing a newborn by a mother in the disturbance of childbirth or lactation is a distinct reduced offence with treatment priority.\n\n3.2 Assisted dying is regulated, not criminalised, under the health law of Part 09 with safeguards; killing outside those safeguards is homicide.\n\n3.3 Withdrawal of futile treatment with consent is lawful medical practice, never homicide.\n\n### Article 4 — Duty to rescue\n\n4.1 Failure to help a person in evident peril, where assistance is possible without serious risk, is a misdemeanour.\n\n4.2 Professionals on duty bear a heightened duty.\n\n4.3 Good Samaritan clauses protect honest helpers from liability for imperfect rescue.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe homicide article keeps the murder-manslaughter line on intent and gross negligence rather than result alone, which is where prosecutorial manipulation of tragedy historically lives. The infanticide recognition follows English and Indian precedent, treating the disturbance of childbirth as a real medical category rather than a sentimental excuse. Duty-to-rescue follows the French-Belgian tradition: cheap to comply, powerful for social trust, with protection for honest attempts.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | England & Wales | Infanticide Acts; homicide structure | Infanticide category and grading |\n| 2 | France | non-assistance a personne en danger | Duty to rescue |\n| 3 | Germany | StGB 211-222 structure | Intent-based grading |\n| 4 | Canada | assisted dying framework 2016 | Regulated end-of-life path |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-123 · Violence Offenses](123-violence-offenses.md)\n- [WC-125 · Domestic and Gender-Based Violence](125-domestic-violence.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](136-international-crimes.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__123-violence-offenses", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/123-violence-offenses.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-123 · Violence Offenses", "md": "# WC-123 · Violence Offenses\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **123 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Assault, coercion, endangerment and hate-crime enhancement\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines non-fatal violence with bodily-integrity grading and the hate-crime enhancement.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Assault and battery\n\n1.1 Battery is unlawful physical force or harmful contact; aggravated by weapon, group, ambush, vulnerability of the victim or on-duty status of the target.\n\n1.2 Grading by harm: Class M for transient injury; Class III for serious injury; Class II for grievous or permanent injury; Class I where death results under document 127's rules.\n\n1.3 Consent validates contact in sport, medicine and ordinary life; consent never legitimises grievous harm.\n\n### Article 2 — Coercion and threats\n\n2.1 Threats of crime, coercion of will, stalking and intimidation are offences from Class M up, with protection orders available to victims.\n\n2.2 Threats against officials, journalists and witnesses are aggravated and prosecuted by the Integrity Commission.\n\n2.3 Swatting, doxxing and organised harassment campaigns are aggravated forms.\n\n### Article 3 — Endangerment\n\n3.1 Endangering life by reckless conduct: dangerous driving, fire-setting, leaving hazards, Class III to II.\n\n3.2 Failure of custodial duty toward children and dependents is aggravated endangerment.\n\n3.3 Arson and building violations with risk to occupants follow the environmental-crime stacking where land or habitat is harmed.\n\n### Article 4 — Hate-crime enhancement\n\n4.1 Where any offence is selected by prejudice against protected characteristics under document 016, penalty class rises by one step.\n\n4.2 Bias motive is proved as a selection factor, not as a separate ideology trial.\n\n4.3 Organised hate violence is treated as organised crime under document 128.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe hate-crime enhancement follows the model that works in practice: selection motive as an aggravator on the base offence, not a separate speech crime, which protects expression while punishing targeted violence. Threats against journalists and witnesses are deliberately routed to the Integrity Commission, because they are the standard instruments of corruption's self-defence. Grading violence by harm keeps proportionality anchored to the victim's body, not to the offender's biography.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | England & Wales | Offences Against the Person structure | Harm-graded violence |\n| 2 | OSCE / ODIHR | hate crime guidance | Bias-selection model |\n| 3 | Germany | StGB 224 aggravated battery | Aggravation catalogue |\n| 4 | United States | swatting prosecutions | Digital-threat forms |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-122 · Homicide and Offences Against Life](122-homicide.md)\n- [WC-125 · Domestic and Gender-Based Violence](125-domestic-violence.md)\n- [WC-124 · Sexual Offenses](124-sexual-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__124-sexual-offenses", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/124-sexual-offenses.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-124 · Sexual Offenses", "md": "# WC-124 · Sexual Offenses\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **124 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Consent as the core, protection of complainants, expert procedure\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines sexual offences around consent, with protective procedure and modern evidence rules.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Rape and assault\n\n1.1 Rape is non-consensual penetration, by any body part or object, of vagina, anus or mouth; consent is free, specific, revocable and present in mind throughout.\n\n1.2 Consent is absent where obtained by force, threat, coercion, abuse of authority, incapacitation, fraud as to the act, or where the person cannot consent by age, capacity or status.\n\n1.3 No corroboration requirement, no resistance requirement, no marital exemption, no recent-complaint requirement; the claimant's testimony is evidence like any other.\n\n### Article 2 — Other offences\n\n2.1 Sexual assault on non-penetrative contact, sexual coercion, exploitation of dependents, patients and prisoners by caregivers and officials, and sexual offences against children with full protection rules.\n\n2.2 Age of protection: sexual acts with persons below sixteen are offences per se, with close-in-age tolerance of two years for consensual adolescent contact.\n\n2.3 Offences against children carry Class I grading with treatment orders.\n\n### Article 3 — Procedure\n\n3.1 Specialised prosecutors, victim advocates, recorded evidence-in-chief, protective cross-examination through the judge, closed session at the claimant's choice.\n\n3.2 Complainant's sexual history, style and prior relationship with third parties is inadmissible except on relevance to consent decided by the judge in camera.\n\n3.3 Rape myths are excluded as reasoning; judgments citing them are appellate error.\n\n### Article 4 — False allegations\n\n4.1 Knowingly false reporting is an offence, prosecuted at the Class M level with the strict proof required of all offences.\n\n4.2 The risk of discouraging genuine complaints is managed by trial transparency rules and non-disclosure of complainant identity.\n\n4.3 Lawyers filing pressure complaints in custody matters are disciplined.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe consent-centred definitions follow the 2010s reform wave in England, Wales, Germany and the Nordic countries, replacing coercion-centred codes that silenced most victims. Procedure is where these cases historically failed, so the protections are structural: judge-filtered cross, advocate support, rape-myth exclusion. False allegations remain criminal but at modest gravity and strict proof, reflecting the evidence that they are rare and that their politicisation chills genuine complaints.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | England & Wales | Sexual Offences Act 2003; consent jurisprudence | Consent-centred code |\n| 2 | Germany | 2016 Nein-Heisst-Nein reform | No-means-no to consent standard |\n| 3 | Istanbul Convention | Arts. 33-39 | Definition and procedure standards |\n| 4 | Sweden | 2018 consent law | Consent as the core element |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-123 · Violence Offenses](123-violence-offenses.md)\n- [WC-125 · Domestic and Gender-Based Violence](125-domestic-violence.md)\n- [WC-137 · Rights of Victims](137-victim-rights.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__125-domestic-violence", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/125-domestic-violence.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-125 · Domestic and Gender-Based Violence", "md": "# WC-125 · Domestic and Gender-Based Violence\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **125 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** A specialist regime: protective orders, risk assessment, integrated support\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document creates the specialist regime for domestic and gender-based violence, from first call to long-term safety.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Offences and grading\n\n1.1 Domestic violence is a distinct offence and an aggravating context for all offences in this Part, covering physical, sexual, psychological, economic violence and coercive control between partners, family members, cohabitants and exes.\n\n1.2 Coercive control - patterns of isolation, surveillance, micro-regulation of life and threats - is an offence without a single assault.\n\n1.3 Repeat patterns escalate class; strangulation is grave injury.\n\n### Article 2 — Protective orders\n\n2.1 Ex parte emergency orders within twelve hours of request, confirmed within seventy-two hours by the court with a hearing; duration up to two years renewable.\n\n2.2 The aggressor is removed from the home; firearms and licences are suspended immediately.\n\n2.3 Breach is an offence with GPS monitoring as an enforcement condition.\n\n### Article 3 — Risk and response\n\n3.1 Standardised risk assessment at every police contact; repeat visits logged on the national risk map.\n\n3.2 One-stop support centres: police, medical, forensic, legal, shelter and financial services co-located with a single case manager.\n\n3.3 Victims never bear protection costs.\n\n### Article 4 — Children and workplace\n\n4.1 Children who witness violence are recognised as victims with support and placement rules under document 023.\n\n4.2 Employers provide paid safety leave and job protection; relocation and address confidentiality programmes operate by statute.\n\n4.3 Stalking follows document 129's harassment rules with the same protective apparatus.\n\n## Commentary\n\nDomestic violence is where violence hides in plain sight for years, and the regime concentrates on the two moments that matter: the first call, with risk assessment and same-day removal of the aggressor, and the long tail, with integrated support and renewal of orders. Removing the aggressor rather than the victim reverses the historical design that punished victims with homelessness. Coercive control as a standalone offence follows England and Wales 2015 and several European codes, which finally reached violence that never bruises.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Istanbul Convention | Whole-instrument framework | Integrated architecture |\n| 2 | England & Wales | Serious Crime Act 2015 coercive control | Coercive control offence |\n| 3 | Spain | specialised courts and risk assessment | Specialist DV courts |\n| 4 | Nordic practice | one-stop centres | Coordinated support centres |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-039 · Gender Equality](../02-rights-and-liberties/039-gender-equality.md)\n- [WC-124 · Sexual Offenses](124-sexual-offenses.md)\n- [WC-037 · Protection of Family Life](../02-rights-and-liberties/037-family-protection.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__133-trafficking-and-slavery", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/133-trafficking-and-slavery.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-133 · Trafficking and Modern Slavery", "md": "# WC-133 · Trafficking and Modern Slavery\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **133 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Exploitation as an offence with victim status, not immigration status\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines trafficking, slavery and forced labour with victim protection as the first design goal.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Offences\n\n1.1 Trafficking in persons - recruitment, transport, harbouring or receipt by force, fraud, coercion, deception or abuse of vulnerability for exploitation - is Class I.\n\n1.2 Slavery, servitude, forced labour, debt bondage, forced marriage, organ trafficking and sale of persons are specific Class I offences.\n\n1.3 Exploitation of a victim's prostitution or of forced criminality is included; consent of the victim is no defence.\n\n### Article 2 — Victim status\n\n2.1 Identified victims receive residence status for the duration of proceedings and a path to permanent status for cooperating victims and unaccompanied children.\n\n2.2 No prosecution of victims for unlawful acts committed under coercion, including immigration offences.\n\n2.3 Protection includes safe housing, medical and psychological care, legal aid, translation and, where desired, family reunification.\n\n### Article 3 — Detection and duty\n\n3.1 Mandatory vigilance duties for supply chains above statutory turnover: risk mapping, audits, disclosure and remediation under Part 08 labour rules.\n\n3.2 First responders - police, labour inspection, health, coast guard, border officers - follow national identification standards with presumption of victimhood for minors.\n\n3.3 Victim compensation from the offender and a state fund of last resort, payable within one year of claim.\n\n### Article 4 — Structure\n\n4.1 Organised trafficking follows document 128's aggravated regime with full asset recovery.\n\n4.2 Labour exploitation in agriculture, construction, domestic and care work is inspected under the labour inspectorate's powers of entry.\n\n4.3 Public procurement excludes suppliers with unresolved slavery findings.\n\n## Commentary\n\nTrafficking law's historical failure was treating victims as immigration offenders, which delivered them back to their exploiters and destroyed prosecutions; the reflection-period residence rule and non-punishment principle fix that at the root. Supply-chain vigilance follows the strongest modern transparency statutes, because demand-side liability is where the economics turn. The state compensation fund with a one-year clock answers victims' years-long wait that functioned as denial.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Palermo Protocol | 2000 trafficking definition | Definition and protection duties |\n| 2 | European Convention | 2005 anti-trafficking convention | Reflection period and non-punishment |\n| 3 | United Kingdom | Modern Slavery Act statements | Supply-chain transparency |\n| 4 | EU 2024 directive | Mandatory diligence | Corporate due-diligence duties |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-030 · Right to Work and Fair Labour](../02-rights-and-liberties/030-right-to-work.md)\n- [WC-130 · Organized Crime](130-organized-crime.md)\n- [WC-042 · Asylum and Refugee Rights](../02-rights-and-liberties/042-refugees-and-asylum.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__134-environmental-crime", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/134-environmental-crime.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-134 · Environmental Crime and Ecocide", "md": "# WC-134 · Environmental Crime and Ecocide\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **134 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Crimes against nature: pollution, wildlife, water, and ecocide\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document criminalises serious environmental harm with personal liability for decision-makers and ecocide at the top of the scale.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Offences\n\n1.1 Unlawful pollution of air, water, soil or sea causing serious harm: Class II; with death or widespread damage: Class I.\n\n1.2 Unlawful waste trafficking, dangerous-substance dumping, ozone and climate-system sabotage, and destruction of protected habitats or species are specific offences.\n\n1.3 Ecocide - unlawful or wanton acts committed with knowledge of substantial likelihood of severe, widespread or long-term environmental damage - is a Class I international crime under document 139.\n\n### Article 2 — Corporate and personal\n\n2.1 Corporate environmental crime follows the liability rules of document 138 with restoration orders, receiver remedies for repeat offenders, and site clean-up funds.\n\n2.2 Directors and officers who directed, tolerated or concealed are personally liable, Class III to I by gravity.\n\n2.3 False monitoring data and bribery of inspectors follow the corruption catalogue with aggravation.\n\n### Article 3 — Enforcement tools\n\n3.1 Environmental inspectors hold entry, sampling, seizure and closure powers with judicial confirmation within forty-eight hours.\n\n3.2 Restoration-first remedies: courts order clean-up, habitat rebuilding and species recovery with bonds and deadlines.\n\n3.3 Standing follows document 022 with NGOs and affected communities; class mechanisms apply.\n\n### Article 4 — Scale and repetition\n\n4.1 Cumulative and repeated violations aggregate to systemic offence grading.\n\n4.2 Transboundary harm triggers international cooperation under document 105.\n\n4.3 Sanctions publish per-company records feeding procurement debarment.\n\n## Commentary\n\nEnvironmental crime is typically a business model with a fine line item, so the article attaches restoration-first orders, bonds, receivers and debarment - the instruments that convert pollution into a bad investment. Personal director liability is the check on the corporate shield that made executives indifferent to compliance. Ecocide, defined with the Stop Ecocide drafting discipline, completes the ladder for the gravest organised destruction of nature.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | EU environmental-crime directive 2008/2024 | Directive framework | Offence catalogue |\n| 2 | France | 2016 environment chapter and ecocide debates | Named offences in penal code |\n| 3 | Brazil | environmental crimes law 9605 | Restoration-first sanctions |\n| 4 | Rome Statute discussions | ecocide definition proposals | Ecocide drafting standard |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-035 · Right to a Healthy Environment](../02-rights-and-liberties/035-right-to-environment.md)\n- [WC-226 · Climate Law](../09-society-and-environment/226-climate-law.md)\n- [WC-135 · Corporate and White-Collar Crime](135-corporate-crime.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__135-corporate-crime", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/135-corporate-crime.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-135 · Corporate and White-Collar Crime", "md": "# WC-135 · Corporate and White-Collar Crime\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **135 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Legal persons on trial: liability, sanctions and compliance\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document completes corporate criminal liability with sanctions that bite assets, licences and existence.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Liability\n\n1.1 Legal persons are liable for offences by organs and agents acting in their interest within their powers, and for supervision failures that enabled the offence.\n\n1.2 Public-interest entities, SOEs, NGOs and public authorities can be liable as offenders.\n\n1.3 Liability does not replace individuals: parallel prosecution of decision-makers is mandatory where evidence supports it.\n\n### Article 2 — Sanctions\n\n2.1 Fines calibrated to turnover and gain, minimum multiples of benefit; confiscation in full; debarment from public contracts and licences; publication of judgment and remediation plans.\n\n2.2 Compliance monitors and judicial supervision for systemic offenders; dissolution for predators whose business model is crime.\n\n2.3 Victim restitution precedes every state claim.\n\n### Article 3 — Compliance and leniency\n\n3.1 Effective, pre-existing compliance programmes mitigate individuals' exposure and sanctions, but never remove confiscation or restitution.\n\n3.2 Leniency for first confessors of cartels and bribery follows the competition model with published criteria.\n\n3.3 Audited self-reporting with full remediation earns deferred prosecution under the conditions of document 085.\n\n### Article 4 — Professional enablers\n\n4.1 Lawyers, accountants, auditors, notaries and company agents who enable crimes are individually liable with professional consequences.\n\n4.2 Professional privilege does not cover crime-fraud participation.\n\n4.3 Bars and institutes operate fitness rules mirroring the code under oversight of the Ombudsman.\n\n## Commentary\n\nCorporate crime is where corruption and ordinary crime meet the balance sheet, and fines-as-cost-of-business is the documented failure mode; turnover-based minima, monitors and dissolution change that arithmetic. The parallel-prosecution mandate for individuals answers the all-too-familiar corporate conviction with zero humans charged. Enabler liability with the crime-fraud exception ends the professional assist that laundered everything from tax schemes to trafficking.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United Kingdom | Bribery Act s.7; failure-to-prevent doctrine | Failure-to-prevent liability |\n| 2 | France | Sapin II compliance duty | Mandatory programmes with monitors |\n| 3 | Italy | Legislative Decree 231/2001 | Entity liability with compliance defence |\n| 4 | United States | sentencing guidelines for organisations | Turnover-based fine logic |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-127 · Fraud and Financial Crime](127-fraud-and-financial-crime.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](../04-integrity-branch/108-corruption-offenses.md)\n- [WC-150 · Corporate Governance and Directors' Duties](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/150-corporate-governance.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "38-penal-code-extended__137-victim-rights", "path": "38-penal-code-extended/137-victim-rights.md", "part": "38-penal-code-extended", "partTitle": "Penal Code Extended", "title": "WC-137 · Rights of Victims", "md": "# WC-137 · Rights of Victims\n\n> **Part 05 — Justice and Penal Law** · Document **137 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Recognition, information, participation, protection, compensation\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document makes victims rights-holders in the penal process, not bystanders to it.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Recognition and standards\n\n1.1 Victims of crime are recognised as rights-holders, including indirect victims and the dependents of the deceased.\n\n1.2 Minimum standards: respect, recognition, information, support, protection, participation and compensation, in every case.\n\n1.3 Repeat and vulnerable victims receive priority treatment without filing heroics.\n\n### Article 2 — Information and participation\n\n2.1 Victims are informed of rights at first contact, of case status at every stage, and of release or escape of the offender in time to act.\n\n2.2 Victim statements are part of the process: impact statements at sentencing, opinions on conditional release, and the right to review non-prosecution decisions under the criteria of document 085.\n\n2.3 Interpretation, translation of key decisions and dedicated contact persons are statutory.\n\n### Article 3 — Protection\n\n3.1 Risk assessment at every contact; protective orders under documents 126 and 131; separate waiting spaces, shielded testimony and pseudonymity where fear is real.\n\n3.2 Intimidation and retaliation are offences; contact bans are enforced with location monitoring.\n\n3.3 Children, elders, disabled and trafficked persons have specialist pathways with guardians.\n\n### Article 4 — Compensation and restorative access\n\n4.1 Restitution by the offender has first claim on assets before the state; the state compensation fund pays within one year where the offender cannot.\n\n4.2 Restorative processes are offered under document 117's consent rules.\n\n4.3 Support services - shelters, crisis lines, trauma care - are funded as statutory services with national coverage.\n\n## Commentary\n\nSystems that forget victims lose public cooperation and with it their evidence base; the corpus treats victims as the process's load-bearing participants, with information, voice and protection rather than ceremony. The state compensation fund with a one-year clock answers the documented decade-long waits that functioned as second victimisation. Prioritising restitution over state claims aligns the recovery machinery of Part 04 with justice rather than treasury optics.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | Victims' Rights Directive 2012 | Standards catalogue |\n| 2 | UN Declaration | 1985 Basic Principles of Justice for Victims | Foundational principles |\n| 3 | Germany | victim reform laws; psychosocial support in court | In-court support practice |\n| 4 | New Zealand | victims code and notification duties | Notification practice |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-116 · Restorative Justice](116-restorative-justice.md)\n- [WC-125 · Domestic and Gender-Based Violence](125-domestic-violence.md)\n- [WC-124 · Sexual Offenses](124-sexual-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Penal reform requires the sentencing council and the training of document 294 before new codes bite.\n- Commutation of existing death sentences and records-sealing schedules are part of adoption, not afterthoughts.\n- Decriminalisation lists take effect on day one; exoneration of prior convictions follows document 134's automatic procedure.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__235-security-architecture", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/235-security-architecture.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-235 · The Security Architecture", "md": "# WC-235 · The Security Architecture\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **235 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Map of the security sector and its civilian constitution\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document lays out the whole security sector - military, police, intelligence, emergency - under civilian constitutional control.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The map\n\n1.1 The security sector comprises the defence forces under document 236, the police under documents 238-239, the intelligence services under document 237, the civil protection under document 241, and the cyber defence under document 242.\n\n1.2 All security bodies are established by statute, mission-bounded, and subject to parliamentary oversight, judicial control and integrity jurisdiction.\n\n1.3 No armed body exists outside this map; militias and private armies are criminal organisations under document 128.\n\n### Article 2 — Civilian control\n\n2.1 The executive directs, Parliament authorises and funds, courts review, and the Integrity Branch polices the sector.\n\n2.2 Security appointments above command threshold require parliamentary confirmation with public hearings.\n\n2.3 Uniformed personnel are barred from party politics; the armed forces vote but do not campaign.\n\n### Article 3 — Rights in uniform\n\n3.1 Personnel of the sector hold the rights of Part 02 with the service-law adaptations of statute.\n\n3.2 Conscientious objection, complaint channels and union-adjacent representation exist in all services.\n\n3.3 Commands violating the law of armed conflict are refused and reported - the duty of document 007.\n\n### Article 4 — The sector budget\n\n4.1 Security budgets follow full transparency under document 175 with classified annexes controlled by the audit court.\n\n4.2 Procurement follows document 098 with security-graded publication - never total opacity.\n\n4.3 Off-budget security spending is prohibited absolutely.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe security sector concentrates the state's coercive power, so the chapter draws its constitutional cage: statutory existence, mission bounds, quadripartite control by parliament, courts, executive and integrity. Classifying procurement annexes to the audit court rather than to ministers is the compromise that protects secrets from enemies without protecting them from auditors - the single most important clause in security integrity.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Germany | Innere Fuehrung and parliamentary control tradition | Citizen-in-uniform model |\n| 2 | United States | Goldwater-Nols and oversight committees | Committee oversight |\n| 3 | South Africa | 1996 security services chapter | Constitutional security chapter |\n| 4 | Switzerland | militia tradition | Citizen-army accountability |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-236 · The Armed Forces](236-armed-forces.md)\n- [WC-238 · Intelligence Services and Oversight](238-intelligence-oversight.md)\n- [WC-237 · War Powers and Use of Force](237-war-powers.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__237-war-powers", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/237-war-powers.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-237 · War Powers and Use of Force", "md": "# WC-237 · War Powers and Use of Force\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **237 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The decision to fight: authorisation, limits, reporting\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs the decision to use armed force abroad and its limits.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Authorisation\n\n1.1 Armed force abroad requires prior parliamentary authorisation by majority, specifying mission, scope, duration and rules of engagement.\n\n1.2 Urgent action in collective self-defence under the UN Charter is permitted with immediate parliamentary convocation within seventy-two hours and ratification within thirty days.\n\n1.3 Secret warfare is prohibited: no unauthorised covert kinetic operations.\n\n### Article 2 — Limits\n\n2.1 Operations follow international humanitarian law absolutely; torture, targeted killings outside combat law, and indiscriminate weapons are prohibited.\n\n2.2 Mission creep requires new authorisation; extensions beyond one year require fresh votes.\n\n2.3 War in violation of the Charter is the crime of aggression with command liability under document 139.\n\n### Article 3 — Reporting\n\n3.1 Monthly classified reporting to the oversight committee with quarterly public summaries.\n\n3.2 Civilian casualty counts are kept, reported to Parliament and published in aggregate - no secret ledgers.\n\n3.3 The audit court reviews operational costs.\n\n### Article 4 — After\n\n4.1 Deployments end by parliamentary decision or mission completion; withdrawal is as lawful as deployment.\n\n4.2 Veterans receive the care and recognition guarantees of document 017 analogues in Part 09.\n\n4.3 Lessons-learned reviews are published within two years.\n\n## Commentary\n\nWar powers are where executives everywhere maximised and parliaments atrophied, so the chapter restores the authorisation machinery with clocks and mission definitions that resist drift. Mandating casualty counting follows the practice of the most accountable militaries - what is counted is restrained. The prohibition of secret warfare answers the documented decade of unauthorised covert campaigns that made the authorisation power decorative.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United States | War Powers Resolution lessons | Authorization clocks |\n| 2 | Germany | BVerfG deployment judgments | Parliamentary army doctrine |\n| 3 | Denmark | war powers commission practice | Mission-definition discipline |\n| 4 | UN Charter | Art. 2(4), 51 | The legal frame |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-236 · The Armed Forces](236-armed-forces.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](../05-justice-and-penal-law/136-international-crimes.md)\n- [WC-238 · Intelligence Services and Oversight](238-intelligence-oversight.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__239-police-organization", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/239-police-organization.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-239 · The Police", "md": "# WC-239 · The Police\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **239 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Organisation, independence and community accountability\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document establishes policing as civilian, local and accountable.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Organisation\n\n1.1 Policing is organised nationally for serious and organised crime and regionally-municipally for community policing, under a single professional statute.\n\n1.2 Police independence in individual investigations from political direction is guaranteed; priorities are set by law and community plans.\n\n1.3 Municipal police follow national standards under document 072.\n\n### Article 2 — Recruitment and training\n\n2.1 Selection includes psychological screening, integrity vetting and community representation targets.\n\n2.2 Training emphasises de-escalation, law, human rights and first aid; annual in-service requalification includes these.\n\n2.3 Higher education pathways and civilian specialists professionalise investigations.\n\n### Article 3 — Powers\n\n3.1 Stop, search, arrest and detention follow documents 017 and 025 with proportionality and recording.\n\n3.2 Equipment: body-worn cameras for uniformed interaction with strict retention; lethal weapons rules under document 016; military-grade equipment requires statutory authorisation and public disclosure.\n\n3.3 Facial recognition and predictive policing follow document 026's prohibitions and human-in-the-loop rules.\n\n### Article 4 — Accountability\n\n4.1 Independent police complaint bodies investigate serious incidents with full powers and publish outcomes.\n\n4.2 Every use of force is reported; statistics publish quarterly by unit and demographics.\n\n4.3 The Integrity Commission polices police corruption with priority; internal affairs exists but never closes corruption files alone.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPolicing succeeds by consent, and consent is built by independence in cases, visibility in force statistics and real complaint machinery. The chapter routes corruption in policing directly to the Integrity Commission because internal affairs historically buried it. Camera retention rules and demographic force statistics are the transparency couple that changed behaviour wherever mandated.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | United Kingdom | policing by consent; IOPC model | Consent and complaint machinery |\n| 2 | Nordic countries | police education university model | Professional training |\n| 3 | Germany | federal-state police split | Layered organisation |\n| 4 | Northern Ireland | Patten reform lessons | Community accountability design |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-240 · Policing Standards and Use of Force](240-policing-standards.md)\n- [WC-239 · The Police](239-police-organization.md)\n- [WC-107 · The Citizens' Oversight Assembly](../04-integrity-branch/107-citizens-oversight.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__240-policing-standards", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/240-policing-standards.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-240 · Policing Standards and Use of Force", "md": "# WC-240 · Policing Standards and Use of Force\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **240 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The rules of engagement at home: force, protest, custody\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document sets the operational standards of policing.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Use of force\n\n1.1 Force is lawful only where necessary, proportionate and preceded by de-escalation; the ladder - presence, dialogue, control, restraint, intermediate, lethal - is mandatory.\n\n1.2 Chokeholds and neck restraints are prohibited; shooting at vehicles and warning shots are prohibited.\n\n1.3 Every lethal-force event triggers the independent investigation of document 016.\n\n### Article 2 — Protest policing\n\n2.1 Assemblies follow document 020's facilitation duty; communication with organisers is documented and command decisions logged.\n\n2.2 Kettling and indiscriminate dispersal are prohibited; tear gas and water cannon need commander-level authorisation, medical presence and are banned against children and elders.\n\n2.3 Plain-clothes officers identify themselves at arrest; snatch squads are prohibited.\n\n### Article 3 — Custody\n\n3.1 Detention follows document 025's clocks; custody records, medical screening, CCTV and lawyer access are mandatory.\n\n3.2 Interrogation recording is complete; torture and coerced statements are excluded under document 121.\n\n3.3 Vulnerable detainees - minors, disabled, intoxicated, mental-health crises - receive specialist handling with health co-response.\n\n### Article 4 — Data and surveillance\n\n4.1 Camera footage is retained by rule with access logs; delete-on-schedule is the default.\n\n4.2 Watchlists require criteria, review and judicial confirmation; secret watchlists are void.\n\n4.3 Ethnic profiling is prohibited; stop-data publishes for antidiscrimination audit under document 030.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe standards chapter writes the hard operational lessons of the last decades into rules rather than manuals: force ladders, protest facilitation, custody cameras, watchlist criteria. Banning neck restraints and warning shots follows the reforms that cut deaths in custody. Health co-response for crisis calls answers the documented reality that police became the default mental-health service without training for it.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Court | use-of-force and protest jurisprudence | Necessity doctrine |\n| 2 | United States reforms | state-level use-of-force acts | Restriction lists |\n| 3 | Germany / Sweden | police university training models | De-escalation training |\n| 4 | England & Wales | PACE custody safeguards | Custody regimes |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-239 · The Police](239-police-organization.md)\n- [WC-022 · Freedom of Assembly](../02-rights-and-liberties/022-freedom-of-assembly.md)\n- [WC-117 · Prisoners' Rights and Prison Regime](../05-justice-and-penal-law/117-prisoners-rights.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__241-arms-control", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/241-arms-control.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-241 · Arms Control and Disarmament", "md": "# WC-241 · Arms Control and Disarmament\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **241 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Weapons of mass destruction banned, conventional trade controlled\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document implements the disarmament commitments of the state.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Weapons of mass destruction\n\n1.1 Nuclear, biological and chemical weapons and their means of delivery are prohibited; their acquisition, development, stockpiling and transfer are crimes under document 139.\n\n1.2 The state joins and implements all relevant treaties - NPT, CWC, BWC, TPNW.\n\n1.3 Any treaty-compatible civilian nuclear programme is under international safeguards with domestic transparency.\n\n### Article 2 — Conventional arms\n\n2.1 Arms exports follow humanitarian criteria: no exports where they would violate humanitarian law or human rights, or to conflict parties; embargo violations are crimes.\n\n2.2 End-user certificates, brokering controls and diversion risk assessments are statutory.\n\n2.3 Arms export decisions above thresholds require parliamentary committee notification with annual reports.\n\n### Article 3 — Domestic\n\n3.1 Civilian firearms licensing is strict: background checks, training, registration and safe storage; automatic weapons are prohibited for civilians.\n\n3.2 Ammunition marking enables tracing; 3D-printed ghost guns are treated as firearms.\n\n3.3 Buyback and amnesty programmes operate periodically with public safety evaluation.\n\n### Article 4 — Disarmament diplomacy\n\n4.1 The state supports disarmament negotiations, verification science and transparency measures.\n\n4.2 Defence conversion programmes assist industry transition under document 205's machinery.\n\n4.3 Arms-trade corruption follows document 108 with priority - a documented epicentre of grand corruption.\n\n## Commentary\n\nArms corruption is grand corruption's veteran form: commissions on weapon deals funded party systems across the democratic world, so the chapter inserts the integrity machinery directly into export control. The domestic licensing follows the systems with the lowest gun death rates - registration, training, storage. Defence conversion funding keeps the disarmament commitment honest for workers who built the old arsenal.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | TPNW / NPT | nuclear ban and non-proliferation | Treaty framework |\n| 2 | ATT | Arms Trade Treaty criteria | Export criteria |\n| 3 | Switzerland / Nordic practice | strict licensing models | Domestic licensing |\n| 4 | CWC verification | OPCW practice | Verification science |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-236 · The Armed Forces](236-armed-forces.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](../05-justice-and-penal-law/136-international-crimes.md)\n- [WC-108 · Corruption Offenses: Definitions and Grading](../04-integrity-branch/108-corruption-offenses.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__243-civil-protection", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/243-civil-protection.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-243 · Civil Protection and Disaster Management", "md": "# WC-243 · Civil Protection and Disaster Management\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **243 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Preparedness, response and honest recovery\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs protection against disasters natural and man-made.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Preparedness\n\n1.1 A national civil-protection agency maintains hazard and risk maps, scenario plans and stockpiles.\n\n1.2 Critical infrastructure operators follow resilience standards, stress tests and outage reporting under document 261.\n\n1.3 Regular public exercises include communities and schools.\n\n### Article 2 — Response\n\n2.1 Response is coordinated by the agency with clear command under document 080's emergency declarations.\n\n2.2 Volunteers and NGOs integrate through pre-agreed frameworks with equipment and insurance.\n\n2.3 Public communication is single-voiced, honest about uncertainty and multilingual.\n\n### Article 3 — Recovery\n\n3.1 Relief aid distribution follows transparency rules: beneficiary lists anonymised, procurements under document 098's emergency caps.\n\n3.2 Insurance pools and solidarity funds are pre-financed by statute with triggers.\n\n3.3 Reconstruction builds back to higher resilience standards.\n\n### Article 4 — Learning\n\n4.1 Every major event triggers an independent review with a public report.\n\n4.2 Recommendations are tracked in the follow-up regime of document 077.\n\n4.3 Compensation for state failure in duty of care follows ordinary liability.\n\n## Commentary\n\nDisaster corruption is a documented genre - relief diverted, reconstruction bid-rigged - so the chapter applies the full integrity machinery to the emergency window rather than exempting it. Pre-financed solidarity funds with automatic triggers replace the discretionary begging that characterised catastrophic recovery. Honest-uncertainty communication is mandated because false reassurance is the deadliest product of disaster politics.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Japan | disaster management system | Preparedness architecture |\n| 2 | Netherlands | multi-layer water safety | Risk-based planning |\n| 3 | Turkey / NZ quake lessons | reconstruction integrity cases | Anti-corruption in recovery |\n| 4 | EU rescEU | solidarity mechanism | Cross-border assistance |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-080 · Emergency Powers](../03-governance/080-emergency-powers.md)\n- [WC-226 · Climate Law](../09-society-and-environment/226-climate-law.md)\n- [WC-215 · Public Health](../09-society-and-environment/215-public-health.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__244-cyber-defense", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/244-cyber-defense.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-244 · Cyber Defence", "md": "# WC-244 · Cyber Defence\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **244 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Defending the digital state without becoming a surveillance state\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs military and security cyber capabilities.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Mission\n\n1.1 Cyber defence protects critical infrastructure, state systems and elections under national command.\n\n1.2 Offensive cyber capabilities exist by statute under document 236's war-powers regime - never for domestic intelligence.\n\n1.3 The military cyber command is separate from the domestic cybersecurity agency of document 261.\n\n### Article 2 — Rules\n\n2.1 Operations follow international humanitarian law: distinction, proportionality and precaution apply in cyberspace.\n\n2.2 Critical civilian services - hospitals, water, power - are protected objects; attacking them is a war crime.\n\n2.3 Hack-back by private actors is prohibited; attribution standards are evidentiary and published in aggregate.\n\n### Article 3 — Coordination\n\n3.1 A national cyber centre fuses threat intelligence across defence, intelligence, police and the CSIRT network.\n\n3.2 Mandatory reporting of significant incidents follows document 261 with voluntary-sharing incentives.\n\n3.3 Election systems follow the integrity regime of documents 052-053 with defended-in-depth audits.\n\n### Article 4 — Limits\n\n4.1 Domestic surveillance by military cyber tools is prohibited absolutely.\n\n4.2 Stockpiled vulnerabilities follow an equities process: disclosure by default, retention only for defined necessity, reviewed by the surveillance court.\n\n4.3 The oversight committee of document 237 reviews operations quarterly.\n\n## Commentary\n\nCyber defence must protect the digital state without swallowing the private digital sphere, and the chapter draws that line in doctrine: military tools abroad, agency tools at home, one court above both. The equities process for vulnerabilities follows the reform consensus after the Shadow Brokers era - hoarded exploits leak, and citizens pay. Protecting hospitals and water systems as protected objects states the humanitarian law rule the sector needed in writing.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Tallinn Manual process | IHL in cyberspace | Application doctrine |\n| 2 | Netherlands / Estonia | national cyber security strategies | Centre architecture |\n| 3 | United States | Vulnerabilities Equities Process | Equities review |\n| 4 | EU NIS2 | incident reporting regimes | Reporting duties |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-262 · Cybersecurity Law](../11-technology-and-future/262-cybersecurity-law.md)\n- [WC-238 · Intelligence Services and Oversight](238-intelligence-oversight.md)\n- [WC-237 · War Powers and Use of Force](237-war-powers.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__247-labor-migration", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/247-labor-migration.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-247 · Labour Migration", "md": "# WC-247 · Labour Migration\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **247 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Economic channels: demand-driven, rights-protected\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs labour migration channels and migrant worker rights.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Channels\n\n1.1 Labour migration follows published shortage lists with points-based selection and employer sponsorship duties.\n\n1.2 Seasonal and circular schemes are statutory with wage-parity and housing standards.\n\n1.3 Regional mobility agreements facilitate movement with rights portability.\n\n### Article 2 — Employer duties\n\n2.1 Sponsors guarantee contracts, wages and conditions; violations bar future sponsorship with publication.\n\n2.2 Recruitment fees charged to workers are prohibited - employer pays; recruitment agencies are licensed.\n\n2.3 Gangmaster and exploitative intermediaries follow document 131's trafficking regime.\n\n### Article 3 — Worker rights\n\n3.1 Migrant workers hold all labour rights of document 017 with complaint channels regardless of status.\n\n3.2 Visa portability after a period ends dependence on single employers.\n\n3.3 Social security is portable by treaty and accrues from day one.\n\n### Article 4 — Regularisation and controls\n\n4.1 Regularisation programmes follow statutory criteria where exploitation or long residence equities exist.\n\n4.2 Employer sanctions follow document 131 for knowing exploitation; workers are protected from prosecution for status in labour claims.\n\n4.3 Migration statistics publish annually with labour-market impact assessments.\n\n## Commentary\n\nLabour migration's documented abuses run through one dependency: the visa tied to a single employer, so portability after a period and employer-pays recruitment are the two structural fixes. Worker complaints surviving status is the enforcement key - undocumented silence is the exploiter's business model. These rules also serve domestic workers: wage parity stops migration being a wage-suppression instrument.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Canada | points and provincial nominations | Selection model |\n| 2 | ILO conventions | C97, C143; fair recruitment | Recruitment standards |\n| 3 | UAE reforms / kafala critiques | sponsorship lessons | Portability fixes |\n| 4 | New Zealand | RSE scheme standards | Seasonal schemes |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-030 · Right to Work and Fair Labour](../02-rights-and-liberties/030-right-to-work.md)\n- [WC-133 · Trafficking and Modern Slavery](../05-justice-and-penal-law/133-trafficking-and-slavery.md)\n- [WC-245 · Borders and Migration Management](245-border-management.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__251-human-rights-foreign-policy", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/251-human-rights-foreign-policy.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-251 · Human Rights in Foreign Policy and Sanctions", "md": "# WC-251 · Human Rights in Foreign Policy and Sanctions\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **251 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Global Magnitsky machinery, arms criteria, honest trade-offs\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document implements human-rights and anti-corruption sanctions and coherence.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Sanctions\n\n1.1 Targeted sanctions - asset freezes and entry bans - are imposed on individuals responsible for gross violations and significant corruption by statutory process.\n\n1.2 Designations are evidence-based, with due-process notice, challenge and delisting before the sanctions court.\n\n1.3 Listings coordinate with allies and publish reasoned summaries.\n\n### Article 2 — Coherence\n\n2.1 Arms exports apply document 239's humanitarian criteria; human-rights dialogues carry deliverables and consequences.\n\n2.2 Development and trade preferences are tied to rights and anti-corruption performance with published scorecards.\n\n2.3 No torture-rendition cooperation: intelligence-sharing excludes torture-tainted information.\n\n### Article 3 — Protection\n\n3.1 Human-rights defenders abroad receive visa fast-tracks, emergency shelter and asylum priority under document 028.\n\n3.2 Journalists at risk receive protective visas and relocation support.\n\n3.3 Election observation is offered and received as good practice under document 052.\n\n### Article 4 — Honesty\n\n4.1 The government reports annually to Parliament on rights dialogues, sanctions imposed and evaded, and coherence failures.\n\n4.2 Commercial interests never silently override the criteria; overrides are stated and justified publicly.\n\n4.3 Victims participate in sanctions design through consultation.\n\n## Commentary\n\nGlobal human-rights machinery works only when it is lawful, targeted and honest, which is why the chapter adds due process and delisting to the Magnitsky instruments - legitimacy is the sanction's fuel. The coherence clauses prevent the documented pattern of values speeches followed by arms deliveries. Emergency visas for defenders and journalists are the cheapest and most honourable foreign policy there is.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Global Magnitsky acts | US, EU, UK, Canada regimes | Targeted sanctions |\n| 2 | EU human-rights dialogue practice | benchmarks | Dialogue machinery |\n| 3 | EU guidelines | human rights defenders | Defender protection |\n| 4 | Shelter City practice | NL and EU networks | Relocation support |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-248 · Foreign Policy](248-foreign-policy.md)\n- [WC-106 · International Cooperation and Asset Recovery](../04-integrity-branch/106-international-recovery.md)\n- [WC-241 · Arms Control and Disarmament](241-arms-control.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__252-peacekeeping", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/252-peacekeeping.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-252 · Peacekeeping and Peacebuilding", "md": "# WC-252 · Peacekeeping and Peacebuilding\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **252 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Blue helmets and the long peace: consent, protection, accountability\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs participation in international peace operations.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Participation\n\n1.1 Contributions to UN, regional and mandated operations follow document 236's authorisation with mission definitions.\n\n1.2 Troop-contributing decisions state protection-of-civilians mandates explicitly.\n\n1.3 No participation in operations without adequate legal protection frameworks.\n\n### Article 2 — Conduct\n\n2.1 National jurisdiction over personnel for crimes applies extraterritorially; immunity agreements never cover crimes.\n\n2.2 Sexual exploitation and abuse are prosecuted with victim-first procedure; zero tolerance means zero exceptions in law.\n\n2.3 Mission-wide conduct data publish with incident statistics.\n\n### Article 3 — Peacebuilding\n\n3.1 Post-conflict programmes follow rule-of-law first: courts, integrity institutions and records before elections.\n\n3.2 Disarmament, demobilisation and reintegration are funded as decade programmes, not photo-ops.\n\n3.3 Local ownership: national actors lead with international support under documented compacts.\n\n### Article 4 — Learning\n\n4.1 Operation lessons publish within two years of completion.\n\n4.2 Casualties of local staff and civilians are counted and reported - no invisible victims.\n\n4.3 Peace-operation expenditure follows document 098 with mission-level audits.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPeace operations carry the UN's finest purposes and its worst scandals, and both trace to accountability: the chapter keeps national criminal jurisdiction extraterritorial so immunity never shields abuse. Rule-of-law-first peacebuilding follows the learned sequence of every successful transition - honest courts before competitive elections. Counting local victims and staff honours those the statistics historically erased.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | UN peacekeeping practice | protection mandates | Mission standards |\n| 2 | Zeid report | SEA reforms | Conduct rules |\n| 3 | Rwanda / Balkans lessons | sequencing | Peacebuilding order |\n| 4 | Colombia / Liberia DDR | reintegration programmes | DDR practice |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-237 · War Powers and Use of Force](237-war-powers.md)\n- [WC-136 · International Crimes](../05-justice-and-penal-law/136-international-crimes.md)\n- [WC-248 · Foreign Policy](248-foreign-policy.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__253-neutrality", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/253-neutrality.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-253 · Neutrality and Non-Aggression", "md": "# WC-253 · Neutrality and Non-Aggression\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **253 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The state's posture toward wars it does not join\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs neutrality policy for states choosing that posture.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Status\n\n1.1 A state may adopt permanent neutrality by organic law: no participation in wars between others, no military alliances.\n\n1.2 Neutrality never means indifference to international law: document 139's crimes remain everyone's business.\n\n1.3 Armed neutrality - defence capacity without alliance - is the compatible model.\n\n### Article 2 — Duties\n\n2.1 Impartial treatment of belligerents under the law of neutrality; humanitarian duties stand above.\n\n2.2 Sanctions compliance under UN decisions overrides strict neutrality - the Charter's primacy.\n\n2.3 Neutrality law binds private actors: mercenarism and war profiteering from neutral territory are crimes.\n\n### Article 3 — Services\n\n3.1 Good offices, mediation and hosting negotiations are the neutral's classical role, funded and staffed professionally.\n\n3.2 Geneva-convention duties - wounded, prisoners, civilians - apply fully.\n\n3.3 Neutrality never shields war criminals: document 139's jurisdiction applies.\n\n### Article 4 — Change of posture\n\n4.1 Abandoning neutrality follows the amendment-grade procedure with referendum.\n\n4.2 Collective-security participation under UN command is compatible with neutrality.\n\n4.3 Armed attack suspends neutrality by the Charter's inherent right of self-defence.\n\n## Commentary\n\nNeutrality is an optional posture, not a value in itself, and the chapter renders it honest: no mercenary profiteering behind the shield, no war-criminal shelter, full Charter compliance. The good-offices role converts neutrality from passivity into service. Making the posture changeable only by referendum protects it from quiet erosion by executive practice.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Switzerland | neutrality law and practice | The classical model |\n| 2 | Austria | constitutional neutrality | Legalised neutrality |\n| 3 | Turkmenistan / others | recognised neutrality cases | UN-recognised status |\n| 4 | Ireland / Malta | military neutrality with EU membership | Compatible architectures |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-236 · The Armed Forces](236-armed-forces.md)\n- [WC-254 · Global Cooperation and International Organisations](254-global-cooperation.md)\n- [WC-237 · War Powers and Use of Force](237-war-powers.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "39-security-and-international-extended__254-global-cooperation", "path": "39-security-and-international-extended/254-global-cooperation.md", "part": "39-security-and-international-extended", "partTitle": "Security And International Extended", "title": "WC-254 · Global Cooperation and International Organisations", "md": "# WC-254 · Global Cooperation and International Organisations\n\n> **Part 10 — Security and International Order** · Document **254 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The state as a good citizen of the world\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs membership and conduct in international organisations.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Membership\n\n1.1 The state participates in the UN system and regional organisations as a duty of cooperative statehood.\n\n1.2 Membership transfers follow document 243's referendum rule; contributions are budgeted transparently.\n\n1.3 Candidacies for international bodies follow merit processes with published nomination files.\n\n### Article 2 — Standards\n\n2.1 The state implements international standards through domestication with gap reporting to Parliament.\n\n2.2 International organisation inspections and reviews are accepted with follow-up regimes.\n\n2.3 Peer-review obligations are honoured on schedule - good citizenship is measurable.\n\n### Article 3 — Reform\n\n3.1 The state advocates transparency, budget integrity and accountability reform in the bodies it joins.\n\n3.2 International organisation immunity is functional, never personal for crimes.\n\n3.3 Whistleblower protection applies to seconded staff under document 091's channels.\n\n### Article 4 — People\n\n4.1 National participation in international civil service is supported with career paths.\n\n4.2 Youth delegate and civil-society delegations accompany the state to major conferences.\n\n4.3 International organisation failures affecting citizens are litigated with the state's diplomatic support.\n\n## Commentary\n\nSmall and medium states live by the rules of institutions, so the chapter makes good citizenship deliberate: timely implementation, accepted reviews, honoured peer obligations, and reform advocacy inside the clubs. Treating organisation immunity as functional-only protects the corpus's accountability principle from the international layer. Career support builds the national presence through which influence actually flows.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Nordic states | multilateralist practice | Good citizenship model |\n| 2 | Switzerland | IO seat policy | Hosting and participation |\n| 3 | UN conventions | reporting and peer review | Standards machinery |\n| 4 | Estonia / Singapore | digital cooperation leadership |  niche diplomacy |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-250 · International Law in Domestic Order](250-international-law.md)\n- [WC-248 · Foreign Policy](248-foreign-policy.md)\n- [WC-249 · Treaties and Ratification](249-treaties.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Security-sector reform starts with parliamentary oversight and budget transparency before any new capabilities.\n- Asylum procedure and border management are adoptable as statute packages with immediate effect.\n- War powers and intelligence warrants require the surveillance court; build it first.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__255-technology-charter", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/255-technology-charter.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-255 · The Technology Charter", "md": "# WC-255 · The Technology Charter\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **255 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** First principles for the digital age: human primacy, openness, security\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document states the constitutional principles governing all technology policy.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Primacy\n\n1.1 Technology serves human beings; humans remain responsible for decisions that affect rights.\n\n1.2 Human dignity and rights apply in full to digital life - the offline constitution is the online constitution.\n\n1.3 No technology adoption may create unaccountable power over persons.\n\n### Article 2 — Design duties\n\n2.1 Public digital systems are accessible, multilingual, privacy-by-design and security-by-design.\n\n2.2 Open standards and interoperability are defaults; source code of public systems is open subject to narrow security exceptions.\n\n2.3 Data minimisation and purpose limitation are engineering requirements, not policy annexes.\n\n### Article 3 — Oversight\n\n3.1 An AI and Technology Authority registers consequential systems, audits them, and enforces the rules of this Part.\n\n3.2 Parliament's technology committee reviews the register annually with public hearings.\n\n3.3 Serious digital incidents are investigated with reports published.\n\n### Article 4 — Innovation balance\n\n4.1 Regulation is proportionate and innovation-friendly for low-risk uses; strict for high-risk uses with rights at stake.\n\n4.2 Sandboxes with safeguards let startups test under supervision.\n\n4.3 Public investment in sovereign capability - compute, models, skills - follows the utility rules of document 260.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe charter is the meta-rule of the Part: technology receives no exemption from the constitution and no vague deference either - it is registered, audited and classified by risk. The human-responsibility clause anchors every later document: automation may advise, but someone answerable decides. Sovereign capability in compute and models is treated as infrastructure because dependency on foreign black boxes became a strategic risk the corpus refuses to ignore.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | digital principles declaration and AI act | Risk-based architecture |\n| 2 | Estonia | digital state principles | Once-only and openness |\n| 3 | OECD AI principles | 2019 updated | Human-centred AI |\n| 4 | UNESCO | AI ethics recommendation | Global ethics baseline |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-256 · AI Governance](256-ai-governance.md)\n- [WC-258 · The Public Data Framework](258-data-framework.md)\n- [WC-257 · Algorithmic Transparency and Automated Decisions](257-algorithmic-transparency.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__260-digital-utility", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/260-digital-utility.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-260 · Digital Infrastructure as Public Utility", "md": "# WC-260 · Digital Infrastructure as Public Utility\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **260 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Connectivity, compute and platforms as essential infrastructure\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs digital infrastructure with utility discipline.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Connectivity\n\n1.1 Universal high-speed connectivity is a statutory obligation with geographic and social tariffs under document 026's access right.\n\n1.2 Backbone and passive infrastructure are open-access wholesale; retail competition rides on it.\n\n1.3 Network resilience and outage compensation follow licence conditions.\n\n### Article 2 — Compute and clouds\n\n2.1 Strategic compute capacity is public or mixed-ownership with open access for research and SMEs.\n\n2.2 Public workloads avoid single-vendor lock-in under document 170; data portability is contractual in every public cloud.\n\n2.3 Sovereign hosting for critical public systems follows document 242's defence requirements.\n\n### Article 3 — Platforms\n\n3.1 Gatekeeper platforms follow document 188's duties: interoperability, portability, fair terms.\n\n3.2 Essential private services - payments, app stores, messaging - carry continuity obligations like utilities.\n\n3.3 Marketplace dominance is regulated, not sanctified.\n\n### Article 4 — Funding\n\n4.1 Universal service funds finance unprofitable areas transparently with open-data coverage maps.\n\n4.2 Infrastructure sharing rules cut civil-works costs; permit clocks under document 166 accelerate builds.\n\n4.3 Public digital investment follows the appraisal gates of document 200.\n\n## Commentary\n\nConnectivity became the condition of citizenship - taxes, school, health - so the chapter treats it with utility law's old virtues: universal obligation, open wholesale, continuity duties. Public compute answers the market's structural concentration in the AI era with access rather than prohibition. Open-data coverage maps keep subsidy honesty visible to the towns the maps forgot.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Finland | broadband universal service | Legal right |\n| 2 | European Union | connectivity infrastructure package | Funding and sharing |\n| 3 | Australia NBN / Singapore | open-access wholesale models | Wholesale architecture |\n| 4 | EU AI compute initiatives | public compute proposals | Public compute |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-028 · Digital Rights and Internet Access](../02-rights-and-liberties/028-digital-rights.md)\n- [WC-188 · Monopolies and Essential Facilities](../08-economic-order/188-monopolies.md)\n- [WC-204 · Infrastructure and Public Investment](../08-economic-order/204-infrastructure.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__263-digital-assets", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/263-digital-assets.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-263 · Crypto-Assets and Digital Money", "md": "# WC-263 · Crypto-Assets and Digital Money\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **263 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Tokens, stablecoins and blockchain under honest finance rules\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs crypto-assets and distributed-ledger finance.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Classification\n\n1.1 Assets classify as payment tokens, utility tokens, asset-referenced or e-money tokens, and security tokens - each to its regime.\n\n1.2 Fully-backed stablecoins are e-money: reserve custody, redemption at par and daily disclosure are licence conditions.\n\n1.3 Algorithmic unbacked stablecoins are prohibited as inherently deceptive.\n\n### Article 2 — Market integrity\n\n2.1 Exchanges and custodians are licensed with segregation of client assets, proof-of-reserves audits and capital.\n\n2.2 Market abuse rules apply fully - insider dealing and manipulation including on-chain forms are document 132 offences.\n\n2.3 AML rules follow document 105 with travel-rule compliance; privacy is balanced by lawful-investigation access with warrants.\n\n### Article 3 — Consumer protection\n\n3.1 Risk warnings, suitability checks and complaint channels are mandatory; aggressive influencer marketing is regulated.\n\n3.2 Failure regimes assign orderly wind-down with client-asset priority.\n\n3.3 No bailout: rescue never extends to token issuers' insolvency.\n\n### Article 4 — Public ledger uses\n\n4.1 Public registries may use DLT where it improves integrity - notarisation, procurement, voting audits - with open verification.\n\n4.2 State e-currency follows document 189's cash-protective rules.\n\n4.3 Energy-intensive consensus for new public uses is restricted by climate criteria under document 223.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe crypto chapter applies the corpus's oldest lesson to its newest assets: finance is honest when issuance is transparent, custody is segregated and abuse is crime - the same rules as banks, applied without fear or favour. Prohibiting algorithmic stablecoins follows their documented collapses; protecting client assets in failure follows every exchange bankruptcy. Public-ledger uses are welcomed exactly where they serve the transparency spine - procurement and registries.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | MiCA regulation | The first comprehensive regime |\n| 2 | Singapore / Switzerland | licensed-market practice | Mature licensing |\n| 3 | FATF travel rule | Recommendation 16 | AML application |\n| 4 | Estonia / Georgia | blockchain registries | State ledger uses |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-158 · Financial Services and Banking](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/158-financial-services.md)\n- [WC-189 · Money and Currency](../08-economic-order/189-currency.md)\n- [WC-129 · Cybercrime](../05-justice-and-penal-law/129-cybercrime.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__264-biotechnology", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/264-biotechnology.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-264 · Biotechnology", "md": "# WC-264 · Biotechnology\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **264 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Gene editing, synthetic biology and biosecurity\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs biotechnology within rights and safety limits.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Red lines\n\n1.1 Germline editing for reproduction is prohibited; somatic therapies proceed under clinical-trial and ethics law.\n\n1.2 Human cloning for reproduction and human-animal chimeras for organ harvesting are prohibited.\n\n1.3 Non-consensual bio-interventions are crimes against bodily integrity under document 016.\n\n### Article 2 — Governance\n\n2.1 A bioethics council with pluralist membership reviews protocols and advises statutory rules.\n\n2.2 Clinical applications follow the medicines regime of document 196 with gene-therapy vigilance.\n\n2.3 Agricultural biotech follows the risk assessment and labelling of documents 214 and 217 with coexistence rules.\n\n### Article 3 — Biosecurity\n\n3.1 Dual-use research oversight with registry, risk tiers and international notification follows the pandemic-era lessons.\n\n3.2 Gain-of-function experiments on dangerous pathogens require national ethics review and public register entries.\n\n3.3 Biolabs are licensed, inspected and their inventories secured.\n\n### Article 4 — Innovation access\n\n4.1 Publicly funded biotech publishes data with patient-consent governance under document 258's commons.\n\n4.2 Patent rules follow document 153 - no patents on natural genes; licensing for public-health needs.\n\n4.3 Global access commitments accompany publicly-funded discoveries.\n\n## Commentary\n\nBiotech policy must be honest about both promises and dangers: cures and bioweapons ride the same science, so the chapter pairs research freedom with registries and tiered oversight. The germline red line follows the global consensus after the 2018 embryo-editing scandal. Commons publication and public-health licensing keep publicly-funded biology serving the many rather than becoming a toll booth.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Oviedo Convention | human rights and biomedicine | The baseline |\n| 2 | WHO | genome-editing governance framework | Post-He Jiankui standards |\n| 3 | European Union | GMO and clinical-trials regimes | Risk governance |\n| 4 | NASEM guidance | dual-use biology | Biosecurity tiers |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-265 · Genetic and Reproductive Rights](265-genetic-rights.md)\n- [WC-215 · Public Health](../09-society-and-environment/215-public-health.md)\n- [WC-045 · Freedom of Science and Access to Knowledge](../02-rights-and-liberties/045-science-and-knowledge.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__265-genetic-rights", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/265-genetic-rights.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-265 · Genetic and Reproductive Rights", "md": "# WC-265 · Genetic and Reproductive Rights\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **265 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Your genes are yours: privacy, non-discrimination, choice\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document protects persons in their genetic and reproductive dimensions.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Genetic privacy\n\n1.1 Genetic data are special-category data under document 026: explicit consent for each purpose, no surreptitious testing.\n\n1.2 Genetic test results belong to the person; relatives' access follows consent or narrow judicial order.\n\n1.3 Genetic biobanks operate under the commons rules of document 258 with withdrawal rights.\n\n### Article 2 — Non-discrimination\n\n2.1 Genetic features are a prohibited discrimination ground under document 016 for insurance, employment, credit and education.\n\n2.2 Insurers may not require or use predictive genetic results - a bright-line rule.\n\n2.3 Employers may test only for demonstrated safety-relevant conditions with consent.\n\n### Article 3 — Reproductive choice\n\n3.1 Reproductive decisions belong to the person: contraception, fertility treatment including IVF and safe abortion services are health rights under document 018.\n\n3.2 Pre-implantation testing is restricted to serious conditions with counselling; sex selection for non-medical reasons is prohibited.\n\n3.3 Surrogacy is regulated for altruistic forms with the child's and surrogate's rights protected; commercial exploitation is prohibited.\n\n### Article 4 — Parentage and identity\n\n4.1 Persons have the right to know their genetic origins with donor-identity rules balancing donors' privacy.\n\n4.2 Genetic parentage tests follow court order or consent - secret tests are inadmissible and sanctionable.\n\n4.3 Adoptees and donor-conceived persons access origin information at majority.\n\n## Commentary\n\nGenetic information is the most intimate and the most familial data, so the chapter fixes bright lines: no insurer demands, no employer fishing, no secret testing. Reproductive rights are treated as health rights within document 018's guarantees - the frame that survived constitutional review in the strongest systems. Origin-information rights answer the adult wounds that secrecy regimes produced.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | GDPR genetic-data provisions | Special-category protection |\n| 2 | Iceland practice | deCODE debates lessons | Population-genomics safeguards |\n| 3 | Council of Europe | genetic testing protocols | Testing standards |\n| 4 | Portugal / Spain laws | assisted reproduction statutes | Reproductive governance |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-264 · Biotechnology](264-biotechnology.md)\n- [WC-027 · Personal Data Protection](../02-rights-and-liberties/027-data-protection.md)\n- [WC-037 · Protection of Family Life](../02-rights-and-liberties/037-family-protection.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__266-neurorights", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/266-neurorights.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-266 · NeuroRights", "md": "# WC-266 · NeuroRights\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **266 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Mental privacy, cognitive liberty and personality protection\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document protects the mind against emerging neurotechnology.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Mental privacy\n\n1.1 Neural data are the most protected category of personal data; collection requires explicit consent for narrow purposes.\n\n1.2 Real-time decoding of thought or affect without consent is prohibited.\n\n1.3 Consumer neurotech follows medical-grade privacy rules regardless of marketing category.\n\n### Article 2 — Cognitive liberty\n\n2.1 No person shall be subjected to neuro-interventions without free informed consent - non-consensual alteration of brain function is a crime against bodily and mental integrity.\n\n2.2 Coerced consent in custody, employment and education is void; neuro-interrogation is torture under document 121.\n\n2.3 Military and security neuro-applications follow the prohibition - no exception.\n\n### Article 3 — Personality continuity\n\n3.1 Interventions materially affecting personality, memory or agency require ethics review, medical indication and reversibility assessment.\n\n3.2 Enhancement by free choice is lawful with safety regulation; workplace coercion of enhancement is prohibited.\n\n3.3 The integrity of the person is not negotiable by contract.\n\n### Article 4 — Institutional\n\n4.1 The Technology Authority's register includes neuro systems; the bioethics council reviews protocols.\n\n4.2 Neurorights are included in the unamendable core review list of document 008's dignity clause.\n\n4.3 Research follows the 3Rs adapted to sentience -Replacement, Reduction, Refinement plus welfare.\n\n## Commentary\n\nNeurights are constitutionalised before the technology matures because the mind is the last inviolable sphere - waiting for the market to mature first is how every privacy battle was lost. The consent absolutism covers the coercion channels - custody, work, school - where neuro-tools would otherwise arrive as conditions. Placing mental integrity under the dignity clause means future devices meet the corpus's oldest protection on day one.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Chile | 2021 constitutional neurorights amendment | The first neurorights law |\n| 2 | UNESCO | neurotechnology ethics recommendation | International guidance |\n| 3 | Colorado / California statutes | neural-data privacy acts | State legislation |\n| 4 | Ienca-Andorno scholarship | mental privacy doctrine | Framework doctrine |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-264 · Biotechnology](264-biotechnology.md)\n- [WC-027 · Personal Data Protection](../02-rights-and-liberties/027-data-protection.md)\n- [WC-015 · Human Dignity](../02-rights-and-liberties/015-dignity.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__267-space-governance", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/267-space-governance.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-267 · Space Governance", "md": "# WC-267 · Space Governance\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **267 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The province of all: orbits, spectrum and celestial bodies\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs the state's space activities.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Principles\n\n1.1 Space activities follow the Outer Space Treaty: exploration for all humankind, no national appropriation of celestial bodies, peaceful uses.\n\n1.2 The state opposes weaponisation of space and supports debris-mitigation rules.\n\n1.3 Spectrum and orbital slots are administered as scarce commons with transparent coordination.\n\n### Article 2 — National activity\n\n2.1 A space agency operates civilian programmes - earth observation, science, connectivity - with open data as default.\n\n2.2 The state registers space objects, bears responsibility for national activities including private ones, and requires licence, insurance and debris plans.\n\n2.3 Launch liability follows the liability convention with domestic recourse.\n\n### Article 3 — Commercial\n\n3.1 Commercial licences require safety, debris, spectrum and national-security review with published criteria.\n\n3.2 Mega-constellation licensing assesses astronomy, debris and deorbit end-of-life duties.\n\n3.3 Satellite earth-observation data sales respect international law and privacy limits on imaging.\n\n### Article 4 — Benefit sharing\n\n4.1 Earth-observation public-good data - disaster response, climate monitoring - are shared internationally.\n\n4.2 Resource utilisation follows future international rules with benefit-sharing commitments; unilateral celestial property claims are void.\n\n4.3 Space research results publish under document 031's open-access rules.\n\n## Commentary\n\nOrbit is the commons where tragedy-of-commons is measured in collision cascades, so the chapter internalises debris duty in every licence and deorbit in every end-of-life plan. Opposing weaponisation follows the corpus's abolitionist spine upward. Benefit-sharing clauses position the state on the right side of the resource-rush treaties now being written.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Outer Space Treaty 1967 | core principles | The constitution of space |\n| 2 | UN COPUOS | debris and long-term sustainability guidelines | Sustainability rules |\n| 3 | Artemis Accords debate | resource utilisation positions | Resource governance |\n| 4 | New Zealand / Luxembourg practice | space activity laws | Domestic licensing |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-045 · Freedom of Science and Access to Knowledge](../02-rights-and-liberties/045-science-and-knowledge.md)\n- [WC-254 · Global Cooperation and International Organisations](../10-security-and-international/254-global-cooperation.md)\n- [WC-226 · Climate Law](../09-society-and-environment/226-climate-law.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__268-autonomous-systems", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/268-autonomous-systems.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-268 · Autonomous Systems", "md": "# WC-268 · Autonomous Systems\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **268 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Robots, drones and vehicles: responsibility without gaps\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs autonomous physical systems and their liability.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Safety and approval\n\n1.1 High-risk autonomous systems - vehicles, drones, industrial robots - require conformity approval with safety cases and scenario testing.\n\n1.2 Autonomous weapons that select and engage targets without human control are prohibited absolutely.\n\n1.3 Public-space operation requires registration, identification and insurance.\n\n### Article 2 — Human oversight\n\n2.1 Safety-critical autonomous operations retain human supervisory control with takeover capacity proportionate to risk.\n\n2.2 High-consequence civilian uses - policing, care, transport of persons - keep meaningful human control under document 256.\n\n2.3 Autonomy levels are disclosed to users and passengers.\n\n### Article 3 — Liability\n\n3.1 Liability for harm by autonomous systems is strict for operators and manufacturers with reversed burden on defect for victims.\n\n3.2 Black-box logging is mandatory for accident reconstruction with privacy-preserving access rules.\n\n3.3 No liability gaps: contracts cannot shift responsibility away from accountable parties.\n\n### Article 4 — Labour and ethics\n\n4.1 Workplace automation follows consultation duties and transition guarantees of documents 256 and 205.\n\n4.2 Care robots assist, never replace, human care relationships in elder and child care.\n\n4.3 Research ethics reviews cover human-robot interaction studies.\n\n## Commentary\n\nAutonomous systems arrive with a responsibility vacuum - the vendor blames the user, the user the vendor - so the chapter closes it: strict liability, reversed burden, black boxes for truth. The autonomous-weapons prohibition follows the majority international position and the corpus's human-responsibility spine. The care-robot clause keeps technology in service of the care relationship rather than as its cheap replacement.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | AI act and machinery regulation | Conformity frameworks |\n| 2 | Germany | autonomous driving law 2017/2021 | Vehicle autonomy law |\n| 3 | UN GGE debates | lethal autonomous weapons | LAWS positions |\n| 4 | ICRC | autonomy in weapons positions | Humanitarian limits |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-256 · AI Governance](256-ai-governance.md)\n- [WC-026 · Privacy](../02-rights-and-liberties/026-privacy.md)\n- [WC-202 · Labour Market and Full Employment](../08-economic-order/202-labor-market.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__269-digital-literacy", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/269-digital-literacy.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-269 · Digital Education and Literacy", "md": "# WC-269 · Digital Education and Literacy\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **269 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The capability floor for digital citizenship\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document guarantees digital literacy as educational infrastructure.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Curriculum\n\n1.1 Digital literacy is a compulsory curriculum strand from primary school: critical use, safety, rights, code basics and algorithmic awareness.\n\n1.2 Media literacy - recognising manipulation, deepfakes and targeted influence - is embedded in civics under document 017.\n\n1.3 Teacher training includes digital pedagogy with continuing development.\n\n### Article 2 — Adult provision\n\n2.1 Public adult digital training is free through libraries, municipalities and the learning accounts of document 197.\n\n2.2 Elders receive targeted programmes with patient formats.\n\n2.3 Employer co-funding of upskilling follows the levy rules of document 197.\n\n### Article 3 — Tool access\n\n3.1 Device and connectivity support for low-income students is a statutory programme.\n\n3.2 Public computing points in libraries and municipal halls maintain assisted access.\n\n3.3 Assistive technologies for disabled users are provided as of right.\n\n### Article 4 — Public competence\n\n4.1 The statistics office measures digital skills with published indicators by region and age.\n\n4.2 Public-service design assumes and supports the least skilled user - no service may require skills beyond the literacy floor.\n\n4.3 Digital-inclusion strategy reports annually to Parliament.\n\n## Commentary\n\nDigital rights without digital literacy are privileges for the connected, so the chapter builds the capability floor: curriculum, adult provision, devices and measurement. Designing public services to the floor - not the median - is the clause that keeps e-government from becoming a wall for the elderly and poor. Media literacy arrives as civics, because manipulation resistance is now a democratic survival skill.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Finland | media literacy tradition | The benchmark |\n| 2 | Estonia | tiger leap and coding curricula | Curriculum integration |\n| 3 | EU digital skills framework | DigComp | Competence framework |\n| 4 | Singapore | digital skills for all programmes | Adult provision |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-031 · Right to Education](../02-rights-and-liberties/031-right-to-education.md)\n- [WC-028 · Digital Rights and Internet Access](../02-rights-and-liberties/028-digital-rights.md)\n- [WC-174 · E-Government and Digital Administration](../07-public-administration/174-e-government.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "40-technology-and-future-extended__270-future-generations-council", "path": "40-technology-and-future-extended/270-future-generations-council.md", "part": "40-technology-and-future-extended", "partTitle": "Technology And Future Extended", "title": "WC-270 · The Future Generations Council", "md": "# WC-270 · The Future Generations Council\n\n> **Part 11 — Technology and the Future** · Document **270 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** A standing voice for those not yet born\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document establishes and arms the Future Generations Council.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Composition\n\n1.1 The Council has eleven members: four by civic lottery among citizens under thirty, four by open competition for expertise in ecology, economics, health and technology, and three nominated by civil society.\n\n1.2 Members serve staggered five-year terms with the protections of constitutional organs under document 075.\n\n1.3 Youth members rotate every two years.\n\n### Article 2 — Powers\n\n2.1 The Council reviews all bills, budgets and national strategies for generational impact and issues published opinions.\n\n2.2 Opinions attach to parliamentary documents; the responsible committee must answer in writing before final vote - the binding answer duty.\n\n2.3 The Council may petition the Constitutional Court on breaches of the sustainability principles of documents 003 and 046.\n\n### Article 3 — Instruments\n\n3.1 The Council maintains the generational accounts, the long-term risk register and the twenty-five-year state of the future report.\n\n3.2 It runs annual youth assemblies feeding its agenda.\n\n3.3 Its secretariat is professional and its data follows document 258's commons.\n\n### Article 4 — Guarantees\n\n4.1 The Council's budget is protected like other constitutional organs.\n\n4.2 Abolition or weakening follows the amendment procedure with the entrenched protection of document 009.\n\n4.3 Government departments must respond to Council information requests within thirty days.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe Council is the enforcement organ of document 046: representation with procedure - published opinions, binding answers, generational accounts and standing before the constitutional court. Lottery seats for the young make the institution demographically honest about whose future it defends. The Welsh pioneer proved the model works; the corpus arms it with justiciability the Welsh commissioner lacked.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Wales | 2015 Future Generations Act | The pioneer |\n| 2 | Hungary's early green ombudsman | institutional lessons | Cautionary model |\n| 3 | New Zealand | wellbeing budgeting | Long-term budgeting |\n| 4 | UN declaration 2024 | future generations pact | Global momentum |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-046 · Rights of Future Generations](../02-rights-and-liberties/046-future-generations.md)\n- [WC-191 · The Public Debt Brake](../08-economic-order/191-debt-brake.md)\n- [WC-226 · Climate Law](../09-society-and-environment/226-climate-law.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- The technology chapters presume the data-protection framework; sequence document 026's statute before AI and platform rules.\n- The algorithmic register can start small - every public body lists its five most consequential systems in year one.\n- Digital-identity rollout must include the offline fallbacks from day one or the excluded will litigate rightly.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__189-currency", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/189-currency.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-189 · Money and Currency", "md": "# WC-189 · Money and Currency\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **189 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Legal tender, cash rights and the payments commons\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs money: issue, legal tender, cash access and payments.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Issue and tender\n\n1.1 The monetary unit is defined by statute; the central bank has the sole right of issue under document 078.\n\n1.2 Banknotes and coins are legal tender for all debts public and private without limit for cash.\n\n1.3 Digital central-bank currency, if issued, is a public payment option, never a substitute for cash or an instrument of surveillance.\n\n### Article 2 — Cash rights\n\n2.1 Cash acceptance is mandatory at physical points of sale below a statutory cap, with narrow exemptions filed and published.\n\n2.2 Cash access: withdrawal and deposit points within reasonable distance maintained by geographic service duty on banks.\n\n2.3 No person is compelled to hold a bank account to receive public payments - cash payout options remain.\n\n### Article 3 — Payments\n\n3.1 Payment systems are regulated infrastructure with cost-based interchange, instant payments and open access for providers.\n\n3.2 Fees are transparent; surcharging is banned for standard instruments.\n\n3.3 Operational resilience and outage compensation are licence conditions.\n\n### Article 4 — Integrity of money\n\n4.1 Counterfeiting is a Class II offence; export controls cover currency equipment.\n\n4.2 Cash transport and processing are licensed, audited activities.\n\n4.3 Large-cash payment limits apply above statutory thresholds with reporting, not prohibition, for private transactions.\n\n## Commentary\n\nCash is the anonymous payment technology of the poor and the free, and its quiet abolition would create a surveillance chokepoint the corpus refuses; hence the cash-rights clauses with geographic access duties. Instant payments with cost-based interchange follow the strongest modern reforms, cutting the private tax on commerce. CBDC language keeps the technology public without ever making it the eye of the state.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Eurozone / Nordic practice | instant payment systems | Payment modernisation |\n| 2 | Sweden debate | cash access legislation 2024 | Cash access duties |\n| 3 | European Union | interchange regulation | Cost-based fees |\n| 4 | BIS / central bank practice | CBDC design principles | Public-option design |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-077 · The Central Bank](../03-governance/077-central-bank.md)\n- [WC-158 · Financial Services and Banking](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/158-financial-services.md)\n- [WC-263 · Crypto-Assets and Digital Money](../11-technology-and-future/263-digital-assets.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__191-debt-brake", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/191-debt-brake.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-191 · The Public Debt Brake", "md": "# WC-191 · The Public Debt Brake\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **191 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Structural balance by rule with escape clauses and transparency\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document implements the constitutional debt brake over all levels of government.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The rule\n\n1.1 Structural net borrowing of the state may not exceed zero point three five percent of GDP in ordinary years; regions and municipalities balance current budgets annually.\n\n1.2 Cyclical effects are smoothed by the economic-cycle adjustment; one-off effects are excluded transparently.\n\n1.3 The rule binds in law with automatic correction mechanism when exceeded.\n\n### Article 2 — Escape clauses\n\n2.1 Exceptions: natural disasters, emergencies under document 080, severe recession beyond the cycle adjustment - each declared, quantified and time-limited by parliamentary supermajority.\n\n2.2 Repayment plans accompany every escape with fixed schedules.\n\n2.3 War financing follows its own regime under document 235 with post-conflict consolidation plans.\n\n### Article 3 — Transparency and control\n\n3.1 The independent fiscal council validates assumptions and publishes compliance assessments with each budget.\n\n3.2 Generational accounts and debt sustainability analyses are mandatory annexes.\n\n3.3 Hidden liabilities - PPP, guarantees, pensions - are fully booked.\n\n### Article 4 — Local discipline\n\n4.1 Local capital budgets may borrow for investment within debt-service ratios under document 074.\n\n4.2 Bailouts are statutory, conditional and penalise the borrowing decision-makers politically.\n\n4.3 Consolidation support is available for hardship with reform conditions.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe debt brake is the German-Swiss instrument with the EU's failed experiences as calibration: strict structural ceiling, automatic correction, escape only for genuine catastrophes with repayment plans. Booking hidden liabilities kills the PPP-and-guarantee games through which deficits hid for decades. The fiscal council's independent validation is the difference between a rule and a suggestion.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Germany | Art. 109-115 Basic Law Schuldenbremse | Structural brake |\n| 2 | Switzerland | debt brake at cantonal level | Subnational rules |\n| 3 | Chile | structural balance rule | Cycle adjustment |\n| 4 | European Union | fiscal compact lessons | Escape-clause design |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-192 · Budget Rules and Fiscal Discipline](192-budget-rules.md)\n- [WC-046 · Rights of Future Generations](../02-rights-and-liberties/046-future-generations.md)\n- [WC-179 · Public Financial Management](../07-public-administration/179-public-financial-management.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__194-income-tax", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/194-income-tax.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-194 · Personal Income Tax", "md": "# WC-194 · Personal Income Tax\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **194 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** One tax, one base, fair progressivity\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document designs the personal income tax as the system's progressive core.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Base\n\n1.1 Global income taxation: employment, business, capital, pensions above thresholds, and rental income in one base.\n\n1.2 Capital gains above a lifetime allowance join the base; the family home is exempt within limits.\n\n1.3 Losses offset across sources with anti-avoidance rules for artificial losses.\n\n### Article 2 — Rate structure\n\n2.1 A progressive schedule with a top marginal rate around half, refundable credits at the bottom, and no cliff effects.\n\n2.2 Indexation of brackets to prices; periodic real-bracket review.\n\n2.3 Joint filing options with splitting benefits capped to avoid marriage distortions.\n\n### Article 3 — Withholding and filing\n\n3.1 Employers, banks and platforms withhold at source; pre-filled returns confirm in minutes under document 173.\n\n3.2 Self-employed follow cash-basis accounting below thresholds with flat-rate options.\n\n3.3 Filing is annual, digital, assisted and free.\n\n### Article 4 — Credits\n\n4.1 Refundable credits: child credit, care credit, earned-income credit, education credit.\n\n4.2 Credits pay out monthly on request - poverty does not wait for April.\n\n4.3 No credits for investment schemes without economic substance.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe income tax design follows the Nordic-global-income model with the compliance ease of the pre-filled systems, because progressivity that citizens can actually file is progressivity that survives politics. Monthly refundable credits convert the tax system into the social policy delivery layer, which is cheaper and more universal than parallel agencies. The lifetime capital-gains allowance protects honest savers while closing the realiser's escape.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Nordic countries | global income taxation | Comprehensive base |\n| 2 | United States / UK | EITC-style credits | Refundable credits |\n| 3 | Germany | withholding tradition | Source collection |\n| 4 | France | withholding-at-source reform 2019 | Modern collection |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-193 · Principles of Taxation](193-taxation-principles.md)\n- [WC-195 · Wealth, Inheritance and Capital Taxes](195-wealth-tax.md)\n- [WC-198 · Social Security](198-social-security.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__195-wealth-tax", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/195-wealth-tax.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-195 · Wealth, Inheritance and Capital Taxes", "md": "# WC-195 · Wealth, Inheritance and Capital Taxes\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **195 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Taxing the stock, not just the flow\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document designs wealth, inheritance and property taxes with anti-avoidance architecture.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Net wealth tax\n\n1.1 A moderate annual net wealth tax above a high exemption - one percent above one thousand median household incomes, two percent above ten thousand.\n\n1.2 Base: worldwide assets of residents, domestic assets of non-residents, with treaty relief.\n\n1.3 Valuations: market-based with mass appraisal, appealed through the property tribunals.\n\n### Article 2 — Inheritance tax\n\n2.1 Inheritance tax with high family allowances - one hundred median incomes to children, fifty to others - and progressive rates above.\n\n2.2 Business and farm relief with jobs-and-continuity conditions and clawback on break-up.\n\n2.3 Lifetime gift aggregation closes the living-transfer escape.\n\n### Article 3 — Property tax\n\n3.1 Annual land value taxation on site value, split-rate discouraging speculation, with exemptions for subsistence farms.\n\n3.2 Vacancy surcharges on empty housing beyond twelve months.\n\n3.3 Registers make ownership and value public under document 155.\n\n### Article 4 — Anti-avoidance\n\n4.1 Exit taxes on expatriation of unrealised gains; anti-avoidance on trusts under document 159.\n\n4.2 General anti-avoidance rule with economic substance doctrine.\n\n4.3 Automatic exchange of financial information closes offshore opacity.\n\n## Commentary\n\nTaxing stocks as well as flows is the structural answer to the dynastic concentration documented across economies, and the design keeps the burden on the truly wealthy with high thresholds rather than middle-class property. Land value taxation is included as the least distortive and most transparent of all taxes, resting on registers the corpus already makes public. Exit taxes and gift aggregation close the two doors through which wealth-tax systems historically leaked.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Switzerland | cantonal wealth taxes | Administered wealth tax |\n| 2 | Norway / Spain | wealth tax practice | Modern calibration |\n| 3 | Denmark / Singapore | land value and site taxation | Land value tax |\n| 4 | OECD CRS | automatic exchange framework | Transparency backbone |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-148 · Succession and Inheritance](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/148-inheritance.md)\n- [WC-193 · Principles of Taxation](193-taxation-principles.md)\n- [WC-145 · Property and Things](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/145-property.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__196-environmental-tax", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/196-environmental-tax.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-196 · Environmental and Consumption Taxes", "md": "# WC-196 · Environmental and Consumption Taxes\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **196 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Pricing pollution, protecting essentials: VAT, carbon, sin taxes\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document designs consumption and environmental taxes with social compensation.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — VAT\n\n1.1 One value-added tax with a standard rate and a short reduced list: food, medicine, books, children's goods.\n\n1.2 Small-business simplification with annual schemes; refunds within statutory periods.\n\n1.3 Cross-border follows destination rules with trust-and-check controls.\n\n### Article 2 — Carbon and pollution\n\n2.1 Carbon pricing rises on a published trajectory to the social cost, covering fuels, industry and aviation with border adjustment for imports from unpriced jurisdictions.\n\n2.2 Revenues recycle as per-capita dividends protecting low-income households.\n\n2.3 Pollution taxes on NOx, waste, plastics and chemicals follow the damage-cost logic.\n\n### Article 3 — Excise and sin\n\n3.1 Excises on alcohol, tobacco, gambling and sugar-sweetened drinks are set with health objectives, minimum floors and smuggling-risk management.\n\n3.2 Vaping products are taxed as tobacco-adjacent.\n\n3.3 Gambling taxation licenses honest operators while contribution caps prevent state dependence.\n\n### Article 4 — Fees and earmarking\n\n4.1 User fees follow cost-recovery principles under document 175; no fee funds general budgets without statute.\n\n4.2 Earmarking of environmental revenues is limited and transparent to the green transition accounts.\n\n4.3 All rates publish with their evidence base and sunset under document 177.\n\n## Commentary\n\nPricing pollution is the cheapest environmental policy ever tested, and per-capita dividend recycling solves its fairness problem in one stroke, which is why the carbon-plus-dividend architecture is adopted whole. Short reduced-VAT lists follow the evidence that long lists leak revenue to consultants while barely helping the poor. Border adjustment prevents honest domestic industry from being undercut by unpriced carbon imports.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | New Zealand | GST single rate | Clean VAT design |\n| 2 | Canada | carbon fee and dividend provinces | Dividend recycling |\n| 3 | EU CBAM | carbon border adjustment | Border carbon mechanism |\n| 4 | UK / Nordic | sugar and alcohol duty design | Health excises |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-226 · Climate Law](../09-society-and-environment/226-climate-law.md)\n- [WC-193 · Principles of Taxation](193-taxation-principles.md)\n- [WC-034 · Right to Food and Water](../02-rights-and-liberties/034-right-to-food-and-water.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__197-tax-evasion", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/197-tax-evasion.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-197 · Fighting Tax Evasion and Avoidance", "md": "# WC-197 · Fighting Tax Evasion and Avoidance\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **197 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Compliance strategy: data, treaties, penalties and promotion\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document sets the strategy against tax evasion and aggressive avoidance.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Detection\n\n1.1 Third-party reporting is universal: employers, banks, platforms, notaries, casinos and crypto-asset providers report automatically.\n\n1.2 International exchange of information, beneficial ownership and land registers are fused in the risk analytics of document 086.\n\n1.3 Industry-specialist audit teams match sector patterns to returns.\n\n### Article 2 — Legal tools\n\n2.1 General anti-avoidance rule with economic substance; exit taxes; transfer-pricing with country-by-country reporting publication.\n\n2.2 Promoter penalties for scheme design; privilege does not shield scheme marketing.\n\n2.3 Offshore structures without substance are disregarded for tax.\n\n### Article 3 — Penalties and prosecution\n\n3.1 Graduated penalties: error, negligence, avoidance, evasion - with fraud prosecuted as the crime of document 132.\n\n3.2 Voluntary disclosure programmes with full payment, interest and reduced penalties, one-time windows.\n\n3.3 Public enforcement data by sector and taxpayer class publishes annually.\n\n### Article 4 — Service-first compliance\n\n4.1 The promotion ladder: make it easy, make it visible, make it hurt - in that order.\n\n4.2 Advance rulings and cooperative compliance for complex taxpayers under document 173.\n\n4.3 Compliance-gap targets for the top percentile are public policy, not tactics.\n\n## Commentary\n\nThe evidence on tax compliance is unambiguous: service plus third-party data collects more than fear, so the strategy leads with promotion and reserves punishment for dishonesty. Publishing the top-percentile gap changes the politics of enforcement by making double standards visible. Promoter liability attacks the supply side of avoidance - the industry that industrialised it.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | OECD BEPS | country-by-country reporting | Multinational transparency |\n| 2 | Denmark / Sweden | third-party data culture | Data-matched compliance |\n| 3 | United Kingdom | DOTAS promoter regime | Promoter penalties |\n| 4 | Norway | public tax return transparency debate | Publishing practice |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-177 · Tax Administration](../07-public-administration/177-tax-administration.md)\n- [WC-127 · Fraud and Financial Crime](../05-justice-and-penal-law/127-fraud-and-financial-crime.md)\n- [WC-096 · Beneficial Ownership Transparency](../04-integrity-branch/096-beneficial-ownership.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__199-universal-basic-services", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/199-universal-basic-services.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-199 · Universal Basic Services", "md": "# WC-199 · Universal Basic Services\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **199 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The social guarantee: what everyone gets as of right\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document defines the universal services floor guaranteed to every resident.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The guarantee\n\n1.1 Every resident has an enforceable claim to the essentials of participation: basic banking, digital identity and connectivity, a postal address, healthcare, education, emergency shelter, and legal aid.\n\n1.2 Services are delivered as rights under the public services charter, not as charity with conditions.\n\n1.3 Eligibility is residence, full stop - no status tests beyond registration.\n\n### Article 2 — Standard and access\n\n2.1 National service standards define minimum quality, with equalisation funding so geography does not decide quality.\n\n2.2 Assisted channels under document 171 guarantee offline access.\n\n2.3 Waiting-time maxima and remedies apply across the floor.\n\n### Article 3 — Funding\n\n3.1 Services are funded from general taxation with transparent cost publication per service.\n\n3.2 User charges are capped at ability-to-pay with automatic waivers.\n\n3.3 Outsourcing never transfers the rights - providers contract under the charter.\n\n### Article 4 — Beyond the floor\n\n4.1 Universal basic income pilots are a policy option for Parliament; the constitutional floor is services, not cash.\n\n4.2 Food and transport vouchers in crisis follow humanitarian standards with dignity design.\n\n4.3 The floor indexes to living-cost data published independently.\n\n## Commentary\n\nUniversal services are the anti-corruption answer to welfare: no discretionary gatekeeping, no brokers, no favours - everyone gets the service by residence, so there is nothing to bribe for. Banking, address and connectivity are added to the classic list because exclusion from them now excludes from everything else. Keeping cash UBI as policy option rather than constitutional right preserves fiscal flexibility while the services floor is locked.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Nordic universalism | universal services model | Universality principle |\n| 2 | Finland | basic income experiment | Evidence on cash pilots |\n| 3 | UK Labour-institution tradition | universal credit lessons | Delivery design |\n| 4 | Estonia | digital identity as service | Digital floor |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-033 · Right to Adequate Housing](../02-rights-and-liberties/033-right-to-housing.md)\n- [WC-032 · Right to Health](../02-rights-and-liberties/032-right-to-health.md)\n- [WC-198 · Social Security](198-social-security.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__200-healthcare-funding", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/200-healthcare-funding.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-200 · Healthcare System and Financing", "md": "# WC-200 · Healthcare System and Financing\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **200 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Universal coverage, primary-care-first, honest pricing\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document designs the financing and organisation of universal healthcare.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Coverage\n\n1.1 Single-payer coverage for the defined benefit basket: primary, specialist, hospital, pharmaceuticals, mental health, dental core, rehabilitation and palliative care.\n\n1.2 No upfront payment at point of care; cost-sharing is limited to solidarity-calibrated co-payments with caps and exemptions.\n\n1.3 Private complementary insurance is free but never buys queue-jumping in public services.\n\n### Article 2 — Organisation\n\n2.1 Primary care is the system's front door: registered family practices with gatekeeping-light, multidisciplinary teams and capitation-plus-performance payment.\n\n2.2 Regional hospital networks with referral protocols; academic centres anchor quality.\n\n2.3 Public health and prevention are funded as statutory programmes under document 217.\n\n### Article 3 — Pricing and procurement\n\n3.1 Reference pricing and health-technology assessment govern pharmaceuticals; negotiation is transparent with published outcomes.\n\n3.2 Medical devices and services follow DRG-style case payment with quality adjustments.\n\n3.3 Procurement follows document 098 with medical-specific integrity screens - a classic corruption hotspot.\n\n### Article 4 — Workforce and quality\n\n4.1 Workforce planning is statutory with training commissions and minimum staffing standards.\n\n4.2 Clinical quality registries, outcomes publication and incident-reporting systems are mandatory and no-fault.\n\n4.3 Patient rights under the charter include choice of practice, second opinion, records access and complaint redress.\n\n## Commentary\n\nHealthcare financing is where universality meets the hardest fiscal choices, and the chapter follows the strongest-performing systems: single payer for the basket, primary care first, and pricing power via assessment and reference pricing. Publishing negotiated pharma outcomes ends the opacity where the biggest health-corruption money historically moved. No-fault incident reporting is the cultural switch that turns error into learning instead of concealment.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Nordic / NHS tradition | single-payer universality | Coverage model |\n| 2 | Germany | sickness funds and quality regimes | Social insurance variant |\n| 3 | Australia | PBS pricing and assessment | Pharmaceutical price discipline |\n| 4 | Netherlands | primary care gatekeeping evidence | Primary-first evidence |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-032 · Right to Health](../02-rights-and-liberties/032-right-to-health.md)\n- [WC-215 · Public Health](../09-society-and-environment/215-public-health.md)\n- [WC-099 · Public Procurement](../04-integrity-branch/099-procurement.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__201-education-funding", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/201-education-funding.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-201 · Education Funding", "md": "# WC-201 · Education Funding\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **201 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Fair funding formulas, teachers and lifelong learning accounts\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document designs the financing of education from early childhood to lifelong learning.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Formula funding\n\n1.1 Schools are funded by transparent weighted-student formulas: base plus deprivation, language, disability and rural factors.\n\n1.2 The formula and its parameters are public law; discretionary grants are minimal and logged under document 175.\n\n1.3 Equalisation guarantees comparable resources per similar student across regions.\n\n### Article 2 — Teachers\n\n2.1 Teacher pay sits at graduate professional bands with recruitment incentives for hard-to-staff areas.\n\n2.2 Professional development entitlements and career ladders without leaving the classroom.\n\n2.3 Teacher supply planning is statutory with bursary pipelines.\n\n### Article 3 — Higher education\n\n3.1 Tuition-free public higher education for first degrees within normal duration; maintenance grants means-tested.\n\n3.2 Income-contingent deferred fees for private or extended study.\n\n3.3 Research funding follows the dual-support model with peer review under document 031.\n\n### Article 4 — Lifelong\n\n4.1 Individual learning accounts fund adult reskilling with portability between providers.\n\n4.2 Employer training levies with sectoral governance.\n\n4.3 Recognition of prior learning under document 017.\n\n## Commentary\n\nEducation funding is where equality is manufactured or forfeited, and weighted formulas in public law remove the discretionary grants that fed both favouritism and neglect. Tuition-free first degrees with means-tested maintenance follow the successful European models that kept social mobility alive. Learning accounts make the system adult-friendly, which the automation transition documented as a necessity.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Netherlands | weighted student funding | Lump-sum with weights |\n| 2 | Finland | equity of school funding | Equal outcomes tradition |\n| 3 | Germany | dual vocational funding | VET financing |\n| 4 | France / Singapore | individual training accounts | Lifelong accounts |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-031 · Right to Education](../02-rights-and-liberties/031-right-to-education.md)\n- [WC-214 · Vocational Training and Apprenticeships](../09-society-and-environment/214-vocational-training.md)\n- [WC-208 · Just Transition and Industrial Policy](208-just-transition.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__202-labor-market", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/202-labor-market.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-202 · Labour Market and Full Employment", "md": "# WC-202 · Labour Market and Full Employment\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **202 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** The employment mandate: activation, flexicurity and shared prosperity\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document sets the labour-market policy framework of the corpus.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — The mandate\n\n1.1 The state pursues full employment as a policy objective with instruments: macro-stabilisation, activation, training and public investment.\n\n1.2 Employment policy is coordinated by a tripartite council under document 149.\n\n1.3 Statistics under the independent office measure performance honestly.\n\n### Article 2 — Flexicurity\n\n2.1 Flexible hiring under fair dismissal rules of document 148; generous income security under document 195; strong activation and training guarantees.\n\n2.2 Transitions between jobs, sectors and regions are supported with mobility allowances and portability of entitlements.\n\n2.3 Platform and gig work is regularised by the presumption of employment under document 017.\n\n### Article 3 — Working time and sharing\n\n3.1 The forty-hour default with collectively bargained variation; overtime premium; part-time equality of hourly terms.\n\n3.2 Right to request flexibility with business-responsibility answers; fathers' quotas in parental leave.\n\n3.3 Work-sharing in downturns is supported with short-time work schemes.\n\n### Article 4 — Wages and productivity\n\n4.1 Minimum wage commissions set floors annually with evidence from the productivity data.\n\n4.2 Sectoral bargaining under document 149 coordinates wages with competitiveness.\n\n4.3 Productivity policy: investment allowances, diffusion of technology to SMEs and workplace codetermination.\n\n## Commentary\n\nFlexicurity is the package that reconciles dynamic economies with security, and the chapter adopts its full logic rather than its slogans: flexible rules, real income insurance, hard activation. The employment mandate with honest statistics keeps unemployment a policy failure rather than an act of god. Work-sharing instruments exist because the alternative to short-time schemes was always layoffs first and rehiring never.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Denmark | flexicurity model | The synthesis |\n| 2 | Germany | Kurzarbeit short-time work | Downturn instrument |\n| 3 | Nordic tradition | active labour market programmes | Activation evidence |\n| 4 | OECD | job strategy revisions | Policy framework |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-030 · Right to Work and Fair Labour](../02-rights-and-liberties/030-right-to-work.md)\n- [WC-154 · Collective Labor and Unions](../06-civil-and-commercial-law/154-collective-labor.md)\n- [WC-208 · Just Transition and Industrial Policy](208-just-transition.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__207-sovereign-wealth-fund", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/207-sovereign-wealth-fund.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-207 · The Sovereign Wealth and Stabilisation Fund", "md": "# WC-207 · The Sovereign Wealth and Stabilisation Fund\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **207 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Saving windfalls for the many and the future\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document establishes the sovereign fund with iron governance.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Purpose\n\n1.1 The Fund saves non-renewable revenues and windfalls, stabilises the budget against shocks, and invests for future generations.\n\n1.2 The Fund is a constitutional organ under document 075's protections.\n\n1.3 Fiscal rule: annual draw equals expected real return - around three percent - never principal.\n\n### Article 2 — Governance\n\n2.1 Board with independent majority, ethical mandates and published criteria; management is professional and indexed.\n\n2.2 All holdings, returns, costs and votes publish quarterly; transparency beats secrecy by design.\n\n2.3 No political instructions, no home-country rescues, no connected-party investments.\n\n### Article 3 — Ethics and integrity\n\n3.1 Exclusion frameworks for corruption, arms, tobacco and severe rights violators with active ownership.\n\n3.2 The Integrity Commission audits the Fund with the full powers of document 077.\n\n3.3 Managers and board are under the ethics code with cooling-off under document 099.\n\n### Article 4 — Uses\n\n4.1 Drawdowns beyond the rule require supermajority and a stated emergency under document 080.\n\n4.2 Regional and generational equity reporting accompanies annual accounts.\n\n4.3 Dissolution of the Fund is an unamendable-core protection for its principal.\n\n## Commentary\n\nSovereign funds fail exactly where the corpus is strongest: opaque politics raiding patient capital, so the chapter gives the fund branch-level protection, a spending rule of expected return, and total publication of votes and holdings - the Norwegian standard with harder edges. Prohibiting connected-party investment and political rescues closes the documented abuse channels. Protecting the principal in the unamendable core is the strongest promise a constitution can make to its unborn.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Norway | GPFG governance and transparency | The model |\n| 2 | Chile | copper stabilisation rules | Stabilisation architecture |\n| 3 | Santiago Principles | IWG framework | SWP standards |\n| 4 | Alaska model | citizen dividend | Direct distribution option |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-046 · Rights of Future Generations](../02-rights-and-liberties/046-future-generations.md)\n- [WC-206 · Natural Resource Governance](206-natural-resources.md)\n- [WC-076 · Independent Constitutional Organs](../03-governance/076-independent-bodies.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "41-economic-order-extended__208-just-transition", "path": "41-economic-order-extended/208-just-transition.md", "part": "41-economic-order-extended", "partTitle": "Economic Order Extended", "title": "WC-208 · Just Transition and Industrial Policy", "md": "# WC-208 · Just Transition and Industrial Policy\n\n> **Part 08 — Economic Order** · Document **208 / 300** · World Constitution (WC) v1.0\n> [Index](../INDEX.md) · [README](../README.md) · [Sources](../SOURCES.md)\n\n**Subject.** Structural change with workers and regions carried, not sacrificed\n\n## Preamble\n\nThis document governs industrial and transition policy with integrity conditions.\n\n## Articles\n\n### Article 1 — Mandate\n\n1.1 Structural transitions - decarbonisation, digitalisation, deindustrialisation - are managed with statutory just-transition plans.\n\n1.2 Plans cover employment, retraining, regional investment and social protection with funded budgets.\n\n1.3 The Future Generations Council reviews plans under document 270.\n\n### Article 2 — Industrial policy discipline\n\n2.1 Horizontal instruments first: R&D, skills, infrastructure, energy; selective aid only for proven market failures.\n\n2.2 Aid is transparent, performance-based, capped and sunset under document 177; the subsidy register publishes all beneficiaries.\n\n2.3 Failed aid is recovered by clawback conditions.\n\n### Article 3 — Regional\n\n3.1 Transition regions receive priority investment funds with local governance and published milestones.\n\n3.2 EU-style cohesion logic: convergence targets with monitoring by the fiscal council.\n\n3.3 One-company towns get diversification plans before closure decisions.\n\n### Article 4 — Workers\n\n4.1 Guaranteed reemployment or income bridge for displaced workers over fifty; pension bridging where reemployment fails.\n\n4.2 Early-warning duty on employers planning structural change.\n\n4.3 Works councils participate in transition design under document 149.\n\n## Commentary\n\nIndustrial policy is returning everywhere, and the corpus's condition is integrity: registers, performance, sunsets, clawbacks - otherwise subsidy becomes the polite name for patronage. Just-transition plans answer the moral charge that climate and digital transitions sacrifice the few for the many. Early-warning and works-council participation convert closures from ambushes into managed change.\n\n## Comparative origins\n\n| # | Source | Provision / practice | What the WC adopts |\n|---|--------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | European Union | Just Transition Fund | Transition funding |\n| 2 | Germany | coal commission model | Negotiated transition |\n| 3 | Korea | industrial policy history lessons | Selective-aid discipline |\n| 4 | Denmark | flexicurity conversions | Worker bridges |\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [WC-226 · Climate Law](../09-society-and-environment/226-climate-law.md)\n- [WC-202 · Labour Market and Full Employment](202-labor-market.md)\n- [WC-185 · The Economic Constitution](185-economic-constitution.md)\n\n## Notes for adopting states\n\n- Fiscal rules require the independent fiscal council to be credible; appoint it before the first budget under the new regime.\n- Tax reform moves as a package: base-broadening, pre-filling and compliance-gap publication together.\n- The debt brake and the sovereign fund are intergenerational instruments; adopt them early, govern them forever.\n\n---\n*World Constitution (WC) v1.0 · Model Edition 2026 · This file is model law offered for national adoption under CC BY 4.0. It is not legal advice.*\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.1-social-compact", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.1-social-compact.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.1 — The Social Compact", "md": "# Section 42.1 — The Social Compact\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.1.1 (Statement of Compact).** The State and its people owe each other reciprocal duties: the State guarantees the rights and services set out across this Constitution; the citizen owes civic participation, honest taxation, lawful conduct, and stewardship of shared spaces and resources (see Part 35, Part 32).\n\n**Article 42.1.2 (Solidarity Principle).** Public policy must weigh the impact on the most vulnerable — children, the elderly, the disabled, the poor — as a matter of constitutional duty, not discretionary generosity.\n\n*Design Note: This section exists to state, in one place, the reciprocal logic that runs through every other Part — rights and duties as a two-way compact, not a one-way grant from ruler to subject.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 0.2, 22-social-policy, 34.1\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.10-oceans", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.10-oceans.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.10 — Oceans and Marine Governance", "md": "# Section 42.10 — Oceans and Marine Governance\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.10.1 (Marine Protected Areas).** A world network of Marine Protected Areas is established and expanded on independent scientific review, paralleling the terrestrial protections of Part 32.3 and Part 13.\n\n**Article 42.10.2 (Sustainable Fisheries).** Fishing quotas are set by independent scientific assessment of stock health, not by short-term economic pressure, coordinated with 24-agriculture/fisheries.md.\n\n**Article 42.10.3 (Marine Pollution).** Dumping of plastic, chemical, or industrial waste into any waterway or ocean is a crime under 38-penal-code-extended/environmental-crime.md.\n\n*Design Note: Oceans cross every regional boundary the rest of this Constitution otherwise respects, which is why they are governed at the world level rather than left to regional customary law, unlike diet (32.4) or religion (42.7).*\n\n**Cross-references:** 32.3, 32.5, 13-environment/water.md, 24-agriculture/fisheries.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.11-circular-economy", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.11-circular-economy.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.11 — Circular Economy", "md": "# Section 42.11 — Circular Economy\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.11.1 (Design for Reuse).** Manufacturers of durable goods must meet minimum repairability and recyclability standards, enforced jointly with the producer-responsibility rules of Part 35.2.\n\n**Article 42.11.2 (Right to Repair).** Consumers and independent repair providers have a legal right to repair manuals, spare parts, and diagnostic tools at fair cost, overriding contrary manufacturer restrictions.\n\n*Design Note: This is the economic engine behind the Clean Cities standard in Part 35 — a truly clean, low-waste society requires goods designed to last and be repaired, not only better bins.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 35-clean-cities-and-sanitation, 9-economy/competition.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.12-civil-society", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.12-civil-society.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.12 — Civil Society", "md": "# Section 42.12 — Civil Society\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.12.1 (Freedom of Association Reaffirmed).** Non-governmental organizations, unions, and civic associations may form, operate, and receive funding (domestic or foreign, subject to the transparency disclosure of Part 27) without government pre-approval of their positions or activities.\n\n**Article 42.12.2 (Protection from Reprisal).** Civil society actors are protected from state reprisal — deregistration, tax harassment, or surveillance — for lawful criticism of government, enforceable before the Constitutional Court.\n\n*Design Note: Concentrating power is easier when independent civic organizations are weak; this section protects the \"third sector\" as a structural counterweight, alongside the free press of Part 36.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.2 (Freedom of Association), 27-transparency-and-accountability, 36-media-integrity\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.13-youth-policy", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.13-youth-policy.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.13 — Youth Policy", "md": "# Section 42.13 — Youth Policy\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.13.1 (Youth Voice).** Regions must maintain a youth advisory body with a formal channel to submit views into the legislative process, particularly on education, environment, and long-term fiscal policy that will bear most heavily on their generation.\n\n**Article 42.13.2 (Transition to Adulthood Support).** Young people leaving state care, education, or the justice system are guaranteed a transition-support program (housing, employment guidance) consistent with 38-penal-code-extended/parole-and-reentry.md and Part 22.\n\n*Design Note: This section connects to the Future Generations Council of Part 40, giving young people both a long-term institutional voice (there) and a direct, present-day legislative channel (here).*\n\n**Cross-references:** 40-technology-and-future-extended/future-generations-council.md, 22-social-policy, 11-education\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.14-demographics", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.14-demographics.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.14 — Demographics", "md": "# Section 42.14 — Demographics\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.14.1 (Demographic Monitoring).** The State maintains independent demographic monitoring (aging, birth rates, migration flows) through its Statistics Authority (20-independent-institutions/statistics-authority.md), published transparently to inform, not dictate, policy.\n\n**Article 42.14.2 (No Coercive Population Policy).** The State may never adopt coercive measures — forced sterilization, birth quotas, or punitive childlessness taxes — to influence population size; family-size decisions remain a private matter under Article 1.5 (Privacy) and Article 16 (Family Law).\n\n*Design Note: 42.14.2 is written as an explicit prohibition, not a silence, because demographic anxiety has historically been the justification for some of the worst rights violations in real constitutional history; naming the prohibition directly closes that door.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.5, 16-civil-law/family-law.md, 20-independent-institutions/statistics-authority.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.2-family-policy", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.2-family-policy.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.2 — Family Policy", "md": "# Section 42.2 — Family Policy\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.2.1 (Family Support).** The State supports families through parental leave, childcare access, and family counselling services, without prescribing any single family structure as the only legitimate one.\n\n**Article 42.2.2 (Non-Discrimination Among Family Forms).** Married couples, unmarried partners, single parents, and extended-family households receive equal legal recognition and support, consistent with Article 1.2 (Equality).\n\n*Design Note: Family policy is placed here, separate from the Bill of Rights, because it is about affirmative support programs, not the underlying right — the right to found a family is already protected in Part 01/16.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.2, 16-civil-law/family-law.md, 22-social-policy\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.3-early-childhood", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.3-early-childhood.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.3 — Early Childhood", "md": "# Section 42.3 — Early Childhood\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.3.1 (Universal Early-Childhood Access).** Every child has guaranteed access to early-childhood care and pre-primary education from age three, funded as part of the compulsory education system (Part 11).\n\n**Article 42.3.2 (Nutrition and Health Screening).** Early-childhood programs include mandatory nutrition and basic health screening, linked to the healthcare-funding provisions of Part 41.\n\n*Design Note: Investment here is treated as the highest-return point in the whole education system, consistent with the evidence base most existing school systems already act on.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 11-education, 41-economic-order-extended/healthcare-funding.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.4-culture-and-arts", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.4-culture-and-arts.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.4 — Culture and the Arts", "md": "# Section 42.4 — Culture and the Arts\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.4.1 (Cultural Rights Affirmed).** Every community's language, art, music, and cultural practice is protected and may not be suppressed by the State, consistent with Part 01 (Cultural Rights) and Part 43's protection of minorities and indigenous peoples.\n\n**Article 42.4.2 (Public Funding, No Content Control).** The State may fund cultural institutions (museums, theatres, heritage sites) but may not condition that funding on ideological content, to prevent state capture of artistic expression.\n\n**Article 42.4.3 (Heritage Preservation).** Sites and practices of significant cultural heritage are registered and protected from destruction or unauthorized commercial exploitation.\n\n*Design Note: 42.4.2 mirrors the press-freedom logic of Part 36 — public funding must never become a leash on expression.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1-rights (cultural rights), 36-media-integrity, 28-special-populations\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.5-media-pluralism", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.5-media-pluralism.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.5 — Media Pluralism", "md": "# Section 42.5 — Media Pluralism\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.5.1 (Pluralism Duty).** Beyond the ownership-transparency rules of Part 36, the State has an affirmative duty to support a plurality of independent voices — including minority-language and regional outlets — through non-discretionary funding formulas.\n\n**Article 42.5.2 (Public Broadcaster Independence).** The public broadcaster (21-media-and-information/public-broadcasting.md) is governed by an independent board insulated from the government of the day, funded by a formula set in statute rather than annual political negotiation.\n\n*Design Note: Ownership transparency (Part 36) stops bad actors from capturing media; pluralism funding (this section) proactively ensures enough independent actors exist in the first place.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 36-media-integrity, 21-media-and-information/public-broadcasting.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.6-sports", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.6-sports.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.6 — Sport and Physical Culture", "md": "# Section 42.6 — Sport and Physical Culture\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.6.1 (Right to Participate).** Every person has a right of access to public sporting and recreational facilities, regardless of income, consistent with the education and health duties in Parts 11 and 12.\n\n**Article 42.6.2 (Anti-Doping and Integrity).** National and world sporting bodies are bound by anti-doping and match-fixing integrity standards, enforced through the Integrity Branch's sporting-integrity unit (Part 37).\n\n**Article 42.6.3 (Youth Sport Safety).** Youth sport programs must meet child-safeguarding standards under Part 38 (Juvenile Justice) and Part 01 (Children's Rights).\n\n*Design Note: Sport is included not as decoration but because physical culture is a genuine, low-cost lever for the low-violence and public-health goals of Parts 34 and 12.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 34-low-violence, 12-health, 37-integrity-branch, 38-penal-code-extended/juvenile-justice.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.7-religion-and-state", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.7-religion-and-state.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.7 — Religion and the State", "md": "# Section 42.7 — Religion and the State\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.7.1 (No State Religion, Full Freedom).** The State establishes no official religion and treats all faiths, and the absence of faith, with equal standing before the law, consistent with Article 1.4 (Freedom of Religion and Conscience).\n\n**Article 42.7.2 (Accommodation of Religious Custom).** Regional and local law may accommodate religious custom — holidays, dietary practice (Part 32.4), dress, and family law variations — provided it does not fall below the universal rights floor of Part 01.\n\n**Article 42.7.3 (No Compulsion).** No person may be compelled to participate in a religious observance, nor penalized in employment, education, or public service for their religion or lack of it.\n\n*Design Note: This is the same subsidiarity logic used for meat consumption in 32.4 — one rights floor everywhere, local custom governing everything above it, so a world constitution does not have to flatten every culture's religious life to be workable.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 1.4, 32.4, 22-social-policy, 28-special-populations/minorities.md\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.8-integration-policy", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.8-integration-policy.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.8 — Integration Policy", "md": "# Section 42.8 — Integration Policy\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.8.1 (Two-Way Integration).** Integration of migrants and newcomers is treated as a two-way process: the State provides language and civic-orientation programs, and receiving communities are supported in accommodating newcomers, rather than integration being framed as an obligation on migrants alone.\n\n**Article 42.8.2 (Non-Discrimination in Access).** Public services (education, health, employment support) are available to lawful residents regardless of length of residence, subject to the citizenship distinctions set out in Part 02.\n\n*Design Note: Framing integration as reciprocal (42.8.1) is deliberate — one-way integration mandates have a documented record of breeding resentment rather than cohesion.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 02-citizenship, 22-social-policy, 1-rights (equality)\n"}, {"id": "42-society-and-culture-extended__42.9-rural-development", "path": "42-society-and-culture-extended/42.9-rural-development.md", "part": "42-society-and-culture-extended", "partTitle": "Society And Culture Extended", "title": "Section 42.9 — Rural Development", "md": "# Section 42.9 — Rural Development\n\n## Articles\n\n**Article 42.9.1 (Rural Infrastructure Parity).** Rural and highland communities (see Part 33) are entitled to the same infrastructure investment ratio, per capita, as urban communities, reviewed by the Auditor-General (20-independent-institutions/auditor-general.md).\n\n**Article 42.9.2 (Agricultural Livelihood Protection).** Land-use and trade policy (Part 09, Part 24) must account for the livelihood impact on smallholder and rural communities before implementation.\n\n*Design Note: This section is the rural counterpart to the highland-specific protections in Part 33, extending the same \"no permanent neglect\" principle to all rural, not only mountainous, communities.*\n\n**Cross-references:** 33-hills-mountains-and-geography, 24-agriculture, 23-infrastructure\n"}, {"id": "CHANGELOG", "path": "CHANGELOG.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "Changelog", "md": "# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to the World Constitution repository are documented here. The format follows the project's governance rules: Major.Minor.Patch, with substantive changes cross-referenced to `annexes/amendment-history.md`.\n\n## [1.0.0] — 2026-08-31 — Initial release\n\n### Added\n\n- **Root documents (7).** `README.md` with the master index; `LICENSE.md` dedicating the work to the public domain under CC0 1.0; `CONTRIBUTING.md` with drafting standards and the two-track amendment process; `GOVERNANCE.md` describing maintainership, decision rules, and versioning; this `CHANGELOG.md`; `GLOSSARY.md` with plain-language definitions of constitutional vocabulary; `DEFINITIONS.md` with the operative defined terms used normatively throughout the text.\n- **Part 00 — Foundation (8 sections).** The Preamble; constitutional principles; sovereignty; rule of law; separation of powers; democracy; human dignity; constitutional supremacy.\n- **Part 01 — Rights and Freedoms (15 sections).** The Bill of Rights with the master limitation clause at Article 1.1.4, plus equality, expression, religion, privacy, due process, fair trial, property, labor, education, healthcare, environmental, digital, consumer, and children's rights.\n- **Part 02 — Citizenship and Migration (7 sections).** Citizenship, nationality, immigration, asylum, refugees, naturalization, loss of citizenship.\n- **Part 03 — The Legislature (10 sections).** The bicameral Parliament, its houses, elections, the Electoral Commission's parliamentary role, parties, campaign finance, the legislative process, privileges, and member integrity.\n- **Part 04 — The Executive (8 sections).** The President, the Prime Minister and Cabinet, ministries, the Public Service, executive powers and accountability, and the executive's role in emergencies.\n- **Part 05 — The Judiciary (9 sections).** Judicial independence, the Supreme Court, the Constitutional Court, lower courts, appointments through the Judicial Service Commission, judicial review, access to justice, prosecutors, legal aid.\n- **Part 06 — Elections and Referendums (7 sections).** Proportional representation with open lists, registration, voting, election security, observers, disputes, referendums.\n- **Part 07 — Local Government (6 sections).** Provinces, regions, municipalities, local elections, local finance, decentralization.\n- **Part 08 — Public Finance (8 sections).** Taxation, budget, debt, the Central Bank, procurement, spending, fiscal responsibility, audit.\n- **Part 09 — The Economy (10 sections).** Economic freedom, competition, monopolies, corporations, small business, banking, financial markets, bankruptcy, intellectual property, trade.\n- **Part 10 — Labor (8 sections).** Employment, minimum standards, wages, working hours, safety, unions, collective bargaining, unemployment.\n- **Part 11 — Education (7 sections).** The education system, compulsory education, higher education, universities, vocational training, academic freedom, finance.\n- **Part 12 — Health (7 sections).** The healthcare system, public health, hospitals, the medical profession, medicines, health data, mental health.\n- **Part 13 — Environment and Natural Resources (8 sections).** Environmental rights implementation, climate policy, air, water, forests, biodiversity, pollution, natural resources.\n- **Part 14 — Criminal Law (10 sections).** The Criminal Code, principles, offenses against persons, property offenses, financial crimes, corruption, cybercrime, organized crime, national-security offenses, sentencing.\n- **Part 15 — Criminal Procedure (8 sections).** Arrest, search and seizure, interrogation, evidence, bail, trial, appeals, prisoner rights.\n- **Part 16 — Civil Law (9 sections).** Contracts, torts, property, inheritance, family law, marriage, divorce, guardianship, civil procedure.\n- **Part 17 — Digital Law (8 sections).** Digital rights implementation, data protection, cybersecurity, artificial intelligence, algorithmic transparency, digital identity, internet governance, electronic evidence.\n- **Part 18 — National Security and Defense (7 sections).** Defense, armed forces, civilian control, intelligence services, oversight, border security, terrorism law.\n- **Part 19 — International Relations (6 sections).** Foreign policy, treaties, international law, diplomatic relations, international trade, extradition.\n- **Part 20 — Independent Institutions (7 sections).** The Ombudsman, Human Rights Commission, Anti-Corruption Commission, Electoral Commission, Auditor-General, Statistics Authority, Information Commission.\n- **Part 21 — Media and Information (5 sections).** Press freedom, public broadcasting, access to information, media regulation, journalist protection.\n- **Part 22 — Social Policy (7 sections).** Social security, pensions, disability, housing, poverty reduction, elder care, child protection.\n- **Part 23 — Infrastructure (6 sections).** Transport, energy, telecommunications, public infrastructure, urban development, critical infrastructure.\n- **Part 24 — Agriculture and Land (6 sections).** Agriculture, land use, food security, farmers' rights, animal welfare, fisheries.\n- **Part 25 — Constitutional Process (5 sections).** Amendments, constitutional convention, interpretation, periodic review, continuity.\n- **Part 26 — Emergency and Crisis (5 sections).** The state of emergency, natural disasters, pandemics, war powers, emergency safeguards.\n- **Part 27 — Transparency and Accountability (6 sections).** Transparency, freedom of information, asset disclosures, conflicts of interest, whistleblower protection, government ethics.\n- **Part 28 — Special Populations (6 sections).** Children, the elderly, persons with disabilities, minorities, indigenous peoples, vulnerable groups.\n- **Part 29 — Public Institutions (5 sections).** Constitutional bodies, public authorities, regulators, state-owned enterprises, independent agencies.\n- **Part 30 — Schedules (5 sections).** Oaths of office, electoral-district principles, constitutional offices, transition rules, protected provisions.\n- **Annexes (6).** Consolidated legal definitions; the index of rights; the index of institutions; the cross-reference guide; the amendment history with its recording template; the implementation roadmap.\n- **Research (6).** Comparative notes on constitutions, rights design, courts, electoral systems, economic systems, and the project's own design principles.\n\n### Notes\n\n- Total: 248 Markdown files. Every section file conforms to the binding specification: numbered Articles, a master limitation clause at Article 1.1.4, unique Design Notes, and verified cross-references.\n- The text describes a fictional polity (\"the Republic\") for educational purposes and prescribes nothing for any real jurisdiction.\n"}, {"id": "CONTRIBUTING", "path": "CONTRIBUTING.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "Contributing to the World Constitution", "md": "# Contributing to the World Constitution\n\nThank you for your interest in improving this model constitution. This repository is a public-domain educational resource, and every contribution — from a typo fix to a new institutional design — makes it more useful to educators, students, and drafters worldwide.\n\n## Ways to contribute\n\n1. **Editorial fixes.** Spelling, grammar, cross-reference accuracy, and formatting corrections are always welcome and are reviewed quickly. These changes do not alter meaning and require only one maintainer's approval.\n2. **Substantive drafting.** Proposals that change, add, or remove norms (Articles, clauses, Design Notes) are reviewed under the amendment process below. Open an issue first so the community can discuss the design before a pull request is written.\n3. **Comparative research.** The `research/` directory grows through contributions that add real-world comparative material. Cite primary sources (constitutions, treaties, standards) and summarize neutrally.\n4. **Annexes and indexes.** The `annexes/` directory must stay synchronized with the text. If your change moves or renames an Article, update `rights-index.md`, `institutional-index.md`, and `cross-references.md` in the same pull request.\n5. **Translations.** Translations of the Preamble and Part 00 are especially valuable. Place them in a `translations/` folder and note that they are non-normative.\n\n## Drafting standards\n\nAll section files follow the binding specification in `constitution-spec.md` (development copy) and the conventions below:\n\n- **Numbering.** Parts map to top-level directories; Sections to files; Articles to `{Part}.{Section}.{n}`. Never renumber existing Articles — supersede them through the amendment process instead.\n- **Language.** Plain, precise legal English. \"shall\" imposes a duty; \"may\" confers discretion; \"shall not\" prohibits. Define a term once, capitalize it thereafter, and add it to `GLOSSARY.md` and `DEFINITIONS.md` if it is new.\n- **Length.** Section bodies run 350–500 words. Concision is a feature: a constitution that cannot be read cannot be understood.\n- **Design Notes.** Every section ends with a 2–4 sentence Design Note explaining the rationale and trade-offs. Real constitutions may be cited there, but never in normative text — the Republic is deliberately fictional.\n- **Cross-references.** Always cite the full article number plus its short title. Broken references are treated as editorial bugs.\n- **Neutrality.** No real country may be endorsed or criticized in normative text, and no provision may depend on a particular legal system, religion, or language community.\n\n## The amendment process for the text\n\nBecause the repository models a constitution, changes to normative content follow a two-track process that mirrors Part 25:\n\n1. **Ordinary amendment (most provisions).** An issue gathers feedback for at least 14 days; a pull request then requires approval from two maintainers, one of whom must review the cross-reference and annex impact.\n2. **Entrenched provisions (Section 30.5).** Provisions listed there — the republican and democratic form, human dignity, equality, judicial independence, non-derogable rights, and the amendment rules themselves — additionally require a documented community comment period of at least 30 days and approval by three maintainers, with the rationale recorded in `annexes/amendment-history.md`.\n\nEvery merged substantive change increments the version in `CHANGELOG.md` and appends a row to the amendment history.\n\n## Pull request checklist\n\nBefore opening a pull request, please confirm that:\n\n- [ ] Every changed section file passes the word-count and template checks (350–500 words; H1, blockquote, italic scope line, 4–8 Articles, Design Note, Cross-references).\n- [ ] New terms are added to `GLOSSARY.md` and, where operative, `DEFINITIONS.md`.\n- [ ] Cross-references in both directions have been checked.\n- [ ] The relevant annex tables have been updated.\n- [ ] A `CHANGELOG.md` entry is included for substantive changes.\n- [ ] The Design Note is unique and actually explains the design choice.\n\n## Code of conduct\n\nContributors agree to keep discussion focused on designs and texts, to critique positions rather than persons, and to assume good faith across cultures and legal traditions. Maintainers may moderate off-topic or abusive threads.\n\n## Licensing\n\nBy contributing, you dedicate your contribution to the public domain under CC0 1.0, consistent with `LICENSE.md`. You confirm that you have the right to do so and that your contribution does not copy proprietary text.\n"}, {"id": "DEFINITIONS", "path": "DEFINITIONS.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "Definitions", "md": "# Definitions\n\nOperative defined terms used normatively throughout this Constitution. Wherever these terms appear capitalized in a section file, they carry the meaning stated here. Each entry states the meaning and the section in which the term is established or developed. Interpretive rules for the Constitution itself are found in Section 25.3.\n\n## The State and its Territory\n\n1. **\"the Republic\"** means the sovereign, unitary, democratic state constituted by this Constitution, acting through its organs and on the whole of its territory (Sections 0.1–0.3).\n2. **\"the State\"** means the Republic as a legal person, including all organs of state at every level (Section 0.3).\n3. **\"the territory of the Republic\"** means the land, waters, and airspace over which the Republic exercises sovereignty, as defined by law (Section 18.6).\n4. **\"organ of state\"** means any department, institution, court, tribunal, body, or functionary exercising public power or performing a public function under the Constitution or law (Section 29.2).\n5. **\"public authority\"** means any organ of state and any private person exercising public power or performing a public function under law (Section 29.2).\n\n## The People\n\n6. **\"the people of the Republic\"** means the community of citizens and all persons subject to the Republic's laws, in whom sovereignty resides (Sections 0.3, 2.1).\n7. **\"citizen\"** means a person holding the citizenship of the Republic by birth, descent, registration, or naturalization (Section 2.1).\n8. **\"non-citizen\"** means any person present in the territory of the Republic who is not a citizen, whose lawful residence confers rights that may not be arbitrarily withheld (Sections 2.3–2.5).\n9. **\"refugee\"** means a person recognized under Section 2.5 as owing to a well-founded fear of persecution and unable or unwilling to avail themselves of the protection of their State (Section 2.5).\n\n## Constitutional Fundamentals\n\n10. **\"human dignity\"** means the inviolable intrinsic worth of every person, the supreme value from which all rights derive (Section 0.7).\n11. **\"the rule of law\"** means the requirement that all public power derive from, be exercised under, and conform to law that is public, clear, general, prospective, and equally enforced (Section 0.4).\n12. **\"constitutional supremacy\"** means the obligation of every organ and person to accord with this Constitution, which prevails over inconsistent law and conduct (Section 0.8).\n13. **\"entrenched provision\"** means a provision listed in Section 30.5, amendable only under Section 25.1 by two-thirds of each House plus a national referendum (Sections 25.1, 30.5).\n14. **\"non-derogable right\"** means a right listed in Section 1.1 and Section 26.5 that may not be limited by derogation during a state of emergency (Sections 1.1, 26.5).\n\n## Rights Doctrine\n\n15. **\"right\"** means an entitlement conferred or recognized by Part 01, enforceable by any person to whom it applies (Section 1.1).\n16. **\"limitation of a right\"** means any measure that restricts, burdens, or otherwise interferes with the enjoyment of a right, lawful only under Article 1.1.4 (Section 1.1).\n17. **\"proportionality\"** means the analytical sequence of legitimacy of aim, suitability, necessity, and balance stricto sensu, applied to every limitation (Article 1.1.4).\n18. **\"derogation\"** means a temporary, published, parliamentary-approved suspension of a derogable right during a state of emergency (Section 26.5).\n\n## The Legislature\n\n19. **\"the Parliament\"** means the bicameral legislature of the Republic, consisting of the Lower House and the Upper House (Section 3.1).\n20. **\"the Lower House\"** means the chamber of four hundred members elected by proportional representation for four-year terms (Section 3.2).\n21. **\"the Upper House\"** means the chamber of Provincial representation, three members per Province elected by the Provincial Councils for staggered six-year terms (Section 3.3).\n22. **\"Act\"** means a law duly adopted by Parliament and assented to under Section 3.8 (Section 3.8).\n23. **\"money bill\"** means a bill dealing only with taxation, appropriation, public debt, or the budget, subject to the special procedure of Section 3.3 (Sections 3.3, 8.2).\n\n## The Executive\n\n24. **\"the President\"** means the head of state, elected by a joint sitting of Parliament for a single six-year term (Section 4.1).\n25. **\"the Prime Minister\"** means the head of government, a member of the Lower House who commands its confidence (Section 4.2).\n26. **\"the Cabinet\"** means the Prime Minister and the ministers, acting under collective responsibility (Section 4.3).\n27. **\"the Public Service\"** means the career body of officials serving the executive under merit and neutrality principles (Section 4.5).\n28. **\"countersignature\"** means the ministerial signature required for the validity of specified presidential acts (Sections 4.1, 26.1).\n\n## The Judiciary and Prosecution\n\n29. **\"the Constitutional Court\"** means the apex court for constitutional questions, of eleven judges serving single non-renewable twelve-year terms (Section 5.3).\n30. **\"the Supreme Court\"** means the apex court of the general court system, deciding final appeals and unifying case law (Section 5.2).\n31. **\"the Judicial Service Commission\"** means the independent body whose merit-based recommendations bind the President in judicial appointments (Section 5.5).\n32. **\"the Public Prosecution Service\"** means the independent institution responsible for criminal prosecutions, headed by the Public Prosecutor (Section 5.8).\n33. **\"judicial review\"** means the power described in Section 5.6 to refuse application to, or invalidate, unconstitutional acts (Section 5.6).\n\n## Subnational Government\n\n34. **\"Province\"** means a primary subnational unit of the Republic governed by an elected Provincial Council (Section 7.1).\n35. **\"Region\"** means a cooperative association of Provinces formed for shared services, without separate sovereignty (Section 7.2).\n36. **\"Municipality\"** means a local-government unit governed by an elected Municipal Council (Section 7.3).\n37. **\"subsidiarity\"** means the allocation of functions to the lowest level of government capable of discharging them effectively (Section 7.6).\n\n## Public Money\n\n38. **\"the Central Bank\"** means the independent monetary authority with price stability as its primary mandate (Section 8.4).\n39. **\"the Office of the Auditor-General\"** means the independent institution auditing all public funds and reporting to Parliament (Sections 8.8, 20.5).\n40. **\"the national currency\"** means the unit of account of the Republic, whose name and denominations are fixed by law (Section 8.4).\n41. **\"public funds\"** means all revenues, borrowings, and other resources received or controlled by any organ of state (Section 8.6).\n\n## Elections and Emergency\n\n42. **\"the Electoral Commission\"** means the independent institution administering all national elections and referendums (Sections 3.5, 20.4).\n43. **\"state of emergency\"** means the crisis regime declared under Section 26.1, subject to parliamentary approval, time limits, and judicial review (Section 26.1).\n44. **\"entrenchment\"** means the heightened amendment protection attaching to provisions listed in Section 30.5 (Section 30.5).\n\n## Independent Institutions\n\n45. **\"independent institution\"** means a constitutional organ listed in Part 20 — the Ombudsman, the Human Rights Commission, the Anti-Corruption Commission, the Electoral Commission, the Office of the Auditor-General, the Statistics Authority, and the Information Commission — characterized by security of tenure, protected budgets, and removal only for cause (Part 20).\n46. **\"the Information Commission\"** means the independent institution supervising both data protection and access to information (Sections 17.2, 20.7, 27.2).\n"}, {"id": "GLOSSARY", "path": "GLOSSARY.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "Glossary", "md": "# Glossary\n\nPlain-language definitions of the constitutional vocabulary used throughout this repository. Operative terms that carry precise legal meaning in the text are separately defined in `DEFINITIONS.md`. Terms are alphabetical; cross-references point to the section where the concept is developed.\n\n**Act** — a law formally adopted by Parliament and in force; also called a statute. See Section 3.8.\n\n**Amendment (constitutional)** — a formal change to the Constitution itself, made under the tiered procedure of Part 25.\n\n**Appropriation** — an authorization by Parliament allowing the government to spend public money for a stated purpose. See Section 8.2.\n\n**Bicameral** — consisting of two chambers; the Parliament of the Republic comprises the Lower House and the Upper House. See Section 3.1.\n\n**Bill** — a draft law under discussion in Parliament; it becomes an Act upon adoption and assent. See Section 3.8.\n\n**Boundary Commission** — the independent body that draws and reviews electoral district boundaries under equality principles. See Section 30.2.\n\n**By-election** — an election held to fill a vacancy arising between general elections. See Section 3.4.\n\n**Cabinet** — the body of ministers headed by the Prime Minister that directs government policy under collective responsibility. See Section 4.3.\n\n**Checks and balances** — the arrangement by which each branch of government limits the others. See Section 0.5.\n\n**Citizen** — a member of the political community of the Republic with full civic rights and duties. See Section 2.1.\n\n**Civic duty** — an obligation of citizenship, such as jury-free civic participation norms or voting, which the text encourages without penalizing abstention. See Section 6.3.\n\n**Collective bargaining** — negotiation between unions and employers over working conditions, resulting in binding collective agreements. See Section 10.7.\n\n**Collective responsibility** — the rule that the Cabinet stands or falls together on government policy. See Section 4.3.\n\n**Competence** — the legal power of an organ or level of government to act on a subject. See Section 4.6 and Section 7.1.\n\n**Confidence** — the support of the Lower House that a Prime Minister must hold and can lose through a constructive vote of no confidence. See Section 4.2.\n\n**Conflict of interest** — a situation in which a public official's private interests could improperly influence official duties. See Section 27.4.\n\n**Constitutional convention** — a specially convened body empowered to propose fundamental revision of the Constitution. See Section 25.2.\n\n**Constitutional Court** — the apex court for constitutional questions, composed of eleven judges serving single non-renewable twelve-year terms. See Section 5.3.\n\n**Constitutionalism** — the principle that all public power is created, limited, and disciplined by the Constitution. See Section 0.2.\n\n**Constitutional review (periodic)** — the scheduled evaluation of the Constitution's performance by a Constitutional Review Commission every fifteen years. See Section 25.4.\n\n**Derogation** — a temporary suspension of a right during a declared emergency, permitted only for derogable rights under strict conditions. See Section 26.5.\n\n**Devolution** — the transfer of meaningful decision-making power to Provinces and Municipalities within a unitary state. See Section 7.6.\n\n**Dissolution** — the early end of the Lower House's term followed by general elections. See Section 3.2.\n\n**Due process** — the requirement that public decisions affecting rights follow fair procedures: notice, reasons, hearing, review. See Section 1.6.\n\n**Electoral Commission** — the independent constitutional institution that administers all national elections and referendums. See Sections 3.5 and 20.4.\n\n**Entrenched provision** — a provision that can be amended only by supermajority plus a national referendum. See Section 30.5.\n\n**Equalization** — fiscal transfers that reduce disparities in revenue and service capacity between subnational governments. See Section 7.5.\n\n**Expropriation** — the compelled transfer of private property to the State, lawful only for a public purpose, under law, and against just and prompt compensation. See Section 1.8.\n\n**Fiscal council** — the independent body that assesses whether budgets and debt strategy comply with fiscal-responsibility rules. See Section 8.7.\n\n**Fiscal year** — the twelve-month period for which the budget is adopted and accounts are audited. See Section 8.2.\n\n**Freedom of information** — the right to request and receive information held by public bodies, subject to narrow, exhaustive exemptions. See Sections 21.3 and 27.2.\n\n**Habeas corpus** — the remedy compelling a authority to justify a detention before a judge without delay. See Section 15.1.\n\n**Head of government** — the Prime Minister, who directs the government and holds the confidence of the Lower House. See Section 4.2.\n\n**Head of state** — the President, elected by a joint sitting of Parliament for a single six-year term. See Section 4.1.\n\n**Horizontal effect** — the application of rights in disputes between private persons, where the right is capable of such application. See Section 1.1.\n\n**Independent institution** — a constitutional organ outside the three branches — such as the Ombudsman or the Auditor-General — designed to guard integrity. See Part 20.\n\n**Judicial independence** — the guarantee that judges decide according to law alone, protected by tenure, salary security, and removal only for cause. See Section 5.1.\n\n**Judicial review** — the power of courts to refuse application to unconstitutional acts, and of the Constitutional Court to invalidate them. See Section 5.6.\n\n**Jus cogens** — peremptory norms of international law from which no derogation is permitted; they bind the Republic absolutely. See Section 19.3.\n\n**Limitation clause** — the provision governing when and how rights may be limited; the Republic's single master clause is Article 1.1.4. See Section 1.1.\n\n**Lower House** — the 400-member chamber of Parliament elected for four-year terms by proportional representation. See Section 3.2.\n\n**Maladministration** — faulty, unlawful, or unreasonable exercise of public power short of illegality, remedied by the Ombudsman. See Section 20.1.\n\n**Master limitation clause** — see Limitation clause; the single clause through which all rights in Part 01 are limited.\n\n**Money bill** — a bill dealing only with taxation, appropriation, or debt, which the Upper House can delay but not amend. See Sections 3.3 and 8.2.\n\n**Non-derogable right** — a right that survives every emergency: life, freedom from torture and slavery, conscience and religion, fair-trial core, and legality in criminal law. See Sections 1.1 and 26.5.\n\n**Non-refoulement** — the absolute prohibition on returning a person to a territory where they face persecution, torture, or serious harm. See Section 2.4.\n\n**Ombudsman** — the independent institution that investigates maladministration and recommends correction. See Section 20.1.\n\n**Open lists** — electoral lists on which voters may express preferences for individual candidates, affecting the order of election. See Section 6.1.\n\n**Opposition** — the parties not supporting the government; their rights in Parliament are protected. See Section 3.9.\n\n**Parliament** — the bicameral legislature of the Republic, the supreme legislative authority. See Section 3.1.\n\n**Parliamentary privilege** — the protections that let members speak and act freely in parliamentary proceedings, limited to protect the institution's function. See Section 3.9.\n\n**Precedent** — a prior judicial decision followed in later cases to secure consistency; the Constitutional Court's interpretations bind all organs. See Section 25.3.\n\n**Presiding officer** — the Speaker of the Lower House or the President of the Upper House, who chairs proceedings impartially. See Section 3.1.\n\n**Prime Minister** — the head of government; a member of the Lower House who must hold its confidence. See Section 4.2.\n\n**Proclamation** — a formal public act by which the President declares matters such as an emergency, subject to countersignature and parliamentary approval. See Section 26.1.\n\n**Proportional representation** — an electoral family that allocates seats in proportion to votes; the Republic uses open lists with a 3% national threshold. See Section 6.1.\n\n**Proportionality** — the test asking whether a rights limitation is suitable, necessary, and balanced; proportionality stricto sensu weighs benefits against harm. See Article 1.1.4.\n\n**Province** — the primary subnational unit of the Republic, governed by an elected Provincial Council. See Section 7.1.\n\n**Public authority** — any organ or person exercising public power under the Constitution, however created. See Section 29.2.\n\n**Public Prosecution Service** — the independent institution responsible for criminal prosecutions, headed by the Public Prosecutor. See Section 5.8.\n\n**Quorum** — the minimum members who must be present for a chamber to validly decide. See Section 3.1.\n\n**Reasonable accommodation** — the adjustments required, without disproportionate burden, to enable persons with disabilities equal participation. See Section 22.3.\n\n**Referendum** — a direct national vote on a measure; mandatory for entrenched amendments, and available by parliamentary decision or citizens' initiative. See Section 6.7.\n\n**Region** — a cooperative grouping of Provinces for shared services; not a sovereign tier. See Section 7.2.\n\n**Reserve powers** — the President's narrow discretionary powers, exercisable only within constitutional limits, such as the single referral of a bill. See Section 4.1.\n\n**Rule of law** — the principle that all power is exercised under, and constrained by, law that is clear, general, prospective, and equally applied. See Section 0.4.\n\n**Section** — a file of this Constitution, numbered by Part and position (e.g., Section 5.3).\n\n**Separation of powers** — the assignment of legislative, executive, and judicial functions to distinct organs. See Section 0.5.\n\n**State of emergency** — a declared, time-limited crisis regime permitting derogations from derogable rights under parliamentary and judicial control. See Section 26.1.\n\n**Subsidiarity** — the rule that functions are performed at the lowest level of government capable of performing them effectively. See Section 7.6.\n\n**Sunset clause** — an expiry date on a power or measure, forcing renewal or lapse. See Section 18.7 and Section 26.5.\n\n**Supremacy (constitutional)** — the rule that the Constitution prevails over every other law and act. See Section 0.8.\n\n**Suspensive (delaying) veto** — the Upper House's power to delay, but not defeat, ordinary legislation. See Section 3.3.\n\n**Statute** — see Act.\n\n**Treaty** — an international agreement binding the Republic once approved and published. See Section 19.2.\n\n**Ultra vires** — an act beyond legal powers; ultra vires acts are invalid and reviewable. See Section 5.6.\n\n**Vertical effect** — the application of rights against organs of the State. See Section 1.1.\n\n**Voter registration** — the continuous, accurate roll of persons entitled to vote, maintained automatically. See Section 6.2.\n\n**Whistleblower** — a person who reports wrongdoing within a public or private organization and enjoys protection against retaliation. See Section 27.5.\n"}, {"id": "GOVERNANCE", "path": "GOVERNANCE.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "Governance of the World Constitution Project", "md": "# Governance of the World Constitution Project\n\nThis document describes how the repository itself is governed: who maintains it, how decisions are made, how versions are released, and how disagreements are resolved. It concerns the *project*, not the fictional Republic — the Republic's own governance is defined by the constitution text in Parts 00–30.\n\n## Principles\n\n1. **Public good.** The repository exists for education and civic study. Decisions optimize for clarity, neutrality, and pedagogical value, not for any political program.\n2. **Open participation.** Anyone may open issues, comment on proposals, and submit pull requests under `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n3. **Neutrality.** The project takes no position on real-world constitutional controversies. Comparative material in `research/` describes; it does not advocate.\n4. **Transparency.** All decisions, including rejected proposals, remain publicly visible in issues and in `annexes/amendment-history.md`.\n\n## Roles\n\n- **Maintainers** (target: three to seven individuals) merge pull requests, manage releases, and enforce the code of conduct. Maintainers are added by consensus of existing maintainers after sustained, high-quality contributions.\n- **The Editorial Committee** (rotating subset of maintainers, minimum two) reviews substantive amendment proposals, checks cross-reference integrity, and guards consistency with the canonical design decisions in the specification.\n- **Contributors** are everyone else — and the project's most important resource. Any contributor may become a maintainer.\n\n## Decision-making\n\n| Decision type | Process | Approval |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Editorial (typos, formatting, links) | Direct pull request | 1 maintainer |\n| New comparative research or annex content | Pull request | 1 maintainer + Editorial Committee review |\n| Ordinary amendment to normative text | Issue (≥14 days) → pull request | 2 maintainers |\n| Entrenched provision change (Section 30.5 list) | Issue (≥30 days) → pull request | 3 maintainers, rationale recorded |\n| Governance changes (this document) | Issue (≥14 days) → pull request | 2 maintainers |\n\nIf consensus fails, maintainers vote; ties are broken by the Editorial Committee's senior member. All votes are recorded in the issue.\n\n## Versioning and releases\n\nThe project uses semantic versioning adapted for a text corpus:\n\n- **Major** (v1 → v2): structural change — Parts added, removed, or renumbered; canonical design decisions altered.\n- **Minor** (v1.0 → v1.1): new Sections or substantive normative changes within existing Parts.\n- **Patch** (v1.0.0 → v1.0.1): editorial corrections, reference fixes, annex synchronization.\n\nEvery release: the `CHANGELOG.md` is updated, `annexes/amendment-history.md` gains rows for substantive changes, and a tagged snapshot is published. Releases occur at least annually if there are changes, and may be skipped in quiet years.\n\n## Quality gates\n\nNo release ships with: broken cross-references; section files outside the word-count band; missing Design Notes; annex indexes out of sync with the text; or terms used normatively before definition. A verification script and manual spot-checks by the Editorial Committee are run before each tagged release.\n\n## Succession and continuity\n\nIf the maintainer group becomes inactive for twelve consecutive months, any contributor may call for new maintainers through an open issue; the most active contributors from the preceding year form the candidate slate, and the community selects maintainers by ranked-choice vote among candidates. This mirrors, in miniature, the constitutional-continuity principles the project teaches.\n\n## Relationship to the constitution text\n\nThe project's governance intentionally parallels the constitution it maintains: layered amendment rules (ordinary versus entrenched), recorded reasons, indexed history, and periodic review. Contributors who experience the process will understand Part 25 and Part 27 better than any lecture could convey.\n"}, {"id": "LICENSE", "path": "LICENSE.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "License", "md": "# License\n\n**World Constitution** — a model constitution for education, drafting, and civic study.\nCopyright © 2026 the contributors to the World Constitution project.\n\n## CC0 1.0 Universal — Public Domain Dedication\n\nThe person or persons who have associated work with this document (the \"Dedicator\" or \"Certifier\") hereby solely and deliberately dedicate the work to the public domain by waiving all of their rights to the work worldwide under copyright law, including all related and neighboring rights, to the extent allowed by law.\n\nYou can copy, modify, distribute and perform the work, even for commercial purposes, all without asking permission. See the full legal text below.\n\n### Statement of Purpose\n\nThe works of world legislation worldwide should be freely available to educators, students, drafters, and citizens. By offering this model constitution under CC0, its authors relinquish all claim of exclusivity, so that anyone may study, adapt, translate, adopt, or build upon it without restriction or attribution requirement — though attribution is appreciated and never harmful.\n\n### Creative Commons Legal Code — CC0 1.0 Universal\n\nCREATIVE COMMONS CORPORATION IS NOT A LAW FIRM AND DOES NOT PROVIDE LEGAL SERVICES. DISTRIBUTION OF THIS DOCUMENT DOES NOT CREATE AN ATTORNEY-CLIENT RELATIONSHIP. CREATIVE COMMONS PROVIDES THIS INFORMATION ON AN \"AS-IS\" BASIS. CREATIVE COMMONS MAKES NO WARRANTIES REGARDING THE USE OF THIS DOCUMENT OR THE INFORMATION OR WORKS PROVIDED HEREUNDER, AND DISCLAIMS LIABILITY FOR DAMAGES RESULTING FROM THE USE OF THIS DOCUMENT OR THE INFORMATION OR WORKS PROVIDED HEREUNDER.\n\n*Statement of Purpose:* The laws of most jurisdictions throughout the world automatically confer exclusive Copyright and Related Rights (defined below) upon the creator and subsequent owner(s) (each and all, an \"owner\") of an original work of authorship and/or a database (each, a \"Work\").\n\nCertain owners wish to permanently relinquish those rights to a Work for the purpose of contributing to a commons of creative, cultural and scientific works (\"Commons\") that the public can reliably and without fear of later claims of infringement build upon, modify, incorporate in other works, reuse and redistribute as freely as possible in any form whatsoever and for any purposes, including without limitation commercial purposes. These owners may contribute to the Commons to promote the ideal of a free culture and the further production of creative, cultural and scientific works, or to gain reputation or greater distribution for their Work in part through the use and efforts of others.\n\nFor these and/or other purposes and motivations, and without any expectation of additional consideration or compensation, the person associating CC0 with a Work (the \"Dedicator\"), to the extent that he or she is an owner of Copyright and Related Rights in the Work, voluntarily elects to apply CC0 to the Work and publicly distribute the Work under its terms, with knowledge of his or her Copyright and Related Rights in the Work and the meaning and intended legal effect of CC0 on those rights.\n\n1. **Copyright and Related Rights.** A Work made available under CC0 may be protected by copyright and related or neighboring rights (\"Copyright and Related Rights\"). Copyright and Related Rights include, without limitation, the following: (i) the right to reproduce, adapt, distribute, perform, display, communicate, and translate a Work; (ii) moral rights retained by the original author(s) and/or performer(s); (iii) publicity and privacy rights pertaining to a person's image or likeness depicted in a Work; (iv) rights protecting against unfair competition in regards to a Work, subject to the limitations in paragraph 4(a), below; (v) rights protecting the extraction, dissemination, use and reuse of data in a Work; (vi) database rights (such as those arising under Directive 96/9/EC of the European Parliament and of the Council of 11 March 1996 on the legal protection of databases, and under any national implementation thereof, including any amended or successor version of such directive); and (vii) other similar, equivalent or corresponding rights throughout the world based on applicable law or treaty, and any national implementations thereof.\n\n2. **Waiver.** To the greatest extent permitted by, but not in contravention of, applicable law, Affirmer hereby overtly, fully, permanently, irrevocably and unconditionally waives, abandons, and surrenders all of Affirmer's Copyright and Related Rights and associated claims and causes of action, whether now known or unknown (including existing as well as future claims and causes of action), in the Work (i) in all territories worldwide, (ii) for the maximum duration provided by applicable law or treaty (including future time extensions), (iii) in any current or future medium and for any number of copies, and (iv) for any purpose whatsoever, including without limitation commercial, advertising or promotional purposes (the \"Waiver\"). Affirmer makes the Waiver for the benefit of each member of the public at large and to the detriment of Affirmer's heirs and successors, fully intending that such Waiver shall not be subject to revocation, rescission, cancellation, termination, or any other legal or equitable action to disrupt the quiet enjoyment of the Work by the public as contemplated by Affirmer's express Statement of Purpose.\n\n3. **Public License Fallback.** If any part of the Waiver is found to be invalid or unenforceable under applicable law, then the Waiver shall be preserved to the maximum extent permitted, and in the event the Waiver is held fully invalid or unenforceable, the Copyright and Related Rights are, to the extent they are so held, licensed to the public at large on the following terms: (a) anyone is free to reproduce, adapt, distribute, perform, display, communicate and translate the Work for any purpose, including commercial purposes; (b) anyone may make use of the Work subject to any applicable moral rights of the original authors; and (c) no warranty of any kind is given with respect to the Work.\n\n4. **Limitations and Disclaimers.** (a) No trademark or patent rights held by Affirmer are waived, abandoned, surrendered, licensed or otherwise affected by this document. (b) Affirmer offers the Work as-is and makes no representations or warranties of any kind concerning the Work, express, implied, statutory or otherwise, including without limitation warranties of title, merchantability, fitness for any particular purpose, non-infringement, or the absence of latent or other defects, accuracy, or the present or absence of errors, whether or not discoverable. (c) To the fullest extent permitted by law, Affirmer disclaims all liability for damages, including direct, special, incidental, consequential, punitive and exemplary damages, arising from or related to the use of the Work or this document. (d) Affirmer is under no obligation to provide support, service, updates or enhancements of the Work.\n\n*The full text of CC0 1.0 Universal is available from Creative Commons: creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode*\n"}, {"id": "README", "path": "README.md", "part": "_root", "partTitle": "Root Documents", "title": "The World Constitution — Royal Edition", "md": "# The World Constitution — Royal Edition\n### A unified constitution for one global state, drafted for \"Lion\" and Claude\n\n> 364 files · merged from two prior model constitutions · new Parts on government eligibility testing, animal & plant rights, low-violence policy, clean cities, hill/mountain law, media integrity, and society & culture.\n\n## Authorship\n\nThis edition was co-authored by **Lion** (concept, philosophy, and the founding requirements: low violence, education-gated government with psychological and physical testing, universal animal and plant protection with country-specific customary law, clean-cities standards, media anti-corruption law) and **Claude** (structuring, legal drafting, and merging the source material below into one coherent text).\n\n## What this is built from\n\n1. **Structure and drafting style** — inherited from the original *World Constitution* teaching edition (three-level Article numbering, Design Notes explaining the reasoning behind every section, cross-references, DEFINITIONS.md/GLOSSARY.md conventions).\n2. **Legal substance for corruption and the penal code** — the Integrity Branch (Part 37) and the extended penal code (Part 38) are drawn from the *World Constitution v1.0* corpus, which was offered under CC BY 4.0 for exactly this kind of reuse and adaptation.\n3. **Six entirely new Parts (31–36)**, written fresh for this edition, carrying Lion's specific requirements:\n   - **Part 31 — Government Eligibility**: no one governs — minister, judge, or royal heir — without clearing an education requirement, an independent psychological fitness test, and a physical fitness standard, re-applied throughout their career.\n   - **Part 32 — Animal and Plant Rights**: a universal welfare floor for animals and ecosystems, with country-specific customary law preserved for meat consumption and diet above that floor.\n   - **Part 33 — Hills, Mountains and Geography**: dedicated terrain-specific law for highland regions, often neglected in real-world constitutions.\n   - **Part 34 — Low Violence and Public Safety**: a constitutional, metric-driven commitment to reducing violence, prevention-first, binding on the state's own use of force as much as on citizens.\n   - **Part 35 — Clean Cities and Sanitation**: a \"Japan model\" cleanliness standard, sequenced so government infrastructure duties come before citizen enforcement.\n   - **Part 36 — Media Integrity**: press freedom paired with ownership transparency and corruption penalties, narrowly targeted at deliberate manipulation rather than general speech.\n   - **Part 42 — Society and Culture Extended**: 14 further topics not covered by either source project — family policy, culture and the arts, media pluralism, sport, religion and the state, integration, rural development, oceans, circular economy, civil society, youth policy, and demographics.\n\n## The Crown\n\nThis edition keeps a hereditary **Crown** as a ceremonial, unifying Head of State (Part 04, Part 31.6) — but no member of the Royal Family may hold an actual governing office without passing the same eligibility gates as any other citizen (Part 31). This was the central design tension in Lion's brief — a king, but no unearned power — and it is the throughline of the whole edition.\n\n## Honest limits\n\n- This is **model law for a fictional unified state**, not a real legal instrument, legal advice, or a claim that world government is achievable or desirable — that is a separate political question this document does not resolve.\n- Parts 37–41 were copied and lightly adapted from the source v1.0 corpus rather than rewritten from scratch; they are flagged in each folder as extended/inherited material.\n- Parts 00–30 are inherited from the teaching-edition source and describe a single fictional \"Republic\" recast here as the governed state under the Crown.\n- Six Parts (31–36) are original to this edition.\n\n## Repository map\n\n- `00`–`30` — Foundation, rights, government structure, law (inherited structure)\n- `31` — Government Eligibility *(new)*\n- `32` — Animal and Plant Rights *(new)*\n- `33` — Hills, Mountains and Geography *(new)*\n- `34` — Low Violence and Public Safety *(new)*\n- `35` — Clean Cities and Sanitation *(new)*\n- `36` — Media Integrity *(new)*\n- `37` — Integrity Branch (anti-corruption, adapted from v1.0)\n- `38` — Penal Code Extended (adapted from v1.0)\n- `39` — Security and International Extended (adapted from v1.0)\n- `40` — Technology and Future Extended (adapted from v1.0)\n- `41` — Economic Order Extended (adapted from v1.0)\n- `42` — Society and Culture Extended *(new)*\n- `annexes/`, `research/` — indexes and comparative notes (inherited)\n\n## Assumptions made where Lion had not yet specified an answer\n\nThese can be revised on request:\n- The Crown is ceremonial with limited reserve powers, not an executive monarchy (Part 04, 31.6).\n- The death penalty is abolished (consistent with the low-violence policy in Part 34); see `38-penal-code-extended/abolition-of-death-penalty.md`.\n- Regions keep local courts for family and minor matters under one shared core criminal code (Part 15/16), consistent with the customary-law approach taken for diet in Part 32.4.\n"}, {"id": "annexes__amendment-history", "path": "annexes/amendment-history.md", "part": "annexes", "partTitle": "Annexes", "title": "Amendment History", "md": "# Amendment History\n\n*About this annex: this annex records how the Constitution itself has changed and provides the template for recording every future change. It is part of the living governance of the text: an amendment that is not recorded here is not part of the authorized corpus.*\n\n## The adoption record\n\nVersion 1.0.0 is the initial publication of the Constitution. It was adopted on **2026-08-31** and published as a complete corpus of **248 files**: 229 section files across the 31 numbered Parts, 7 root documents, 6 annexes, and 6 research notes. Within the Constitution's own narrative, the text is adopted by the people in a national referendum, having been deliberated upon and enacted by a freely elected constituent assembly, and it enters into force upon the proclamation of its ratification (Section 0.1). The transition from any prior legal order to this Constitution is governed by Section 30.4 (Transition Rules), which preserves existing law only insofar as it is consistent with the new text.\n\n## How the Constitution may be changed\n\nThe amendment rules are deliberately tiered, and any future row in the history table below must identify which tier was used. Ordinary amendments require a two-thirds majority of each House (Section 25.1). Entrenched provisions — the republican and democratic form of the state, human dignity, equality, judicial independence, the non-derogable rights, and the amendment rules themselves, as listed in Section 30.5 — require the same two-thirds majorities plus a national referendum majority, and that referendum is mandatory by force of Article 6.7.3. Matters in the Schedules may be adjusted by simple majority, because they carry operational rather than constitutional weight. No amendment may be adopted during a state of emergency, and the emergency Part cannot be amended while the emergency lasts (Section 26.5).\n\n## History table\n\nThe table is the authoritative record of the text's versions. It contains a single completed row; every future amendment appends one row, completed at the moment the amendment is adopted.\n\n| Version | Date | Sections affected | Summary | Adopted by |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| v1.0.0 | 2026-08-31 | All 31 Parts, annexes, and research notes (248 files) | Initial adoption and publication of the Constitution in full | The people of the Republic, by national referendum, upon enactment by a freely elected constituent assembly |\n\n## Rules for completing the table\n\nFuture contributors should follow five conventions. First, version numbers increase in whole steps for constitutional amendments and in minor steps for editorial corrections that change no norm; the version column records what was actually published. Second, the \"Sections affected\" column lists the section numbers touched, using the Part.Section scheme (for example, \"Sections 3.2, 25.1\"), so that the table doubles as an index of change. Third, the summary states the legal effect of the amendment in one sentence, not its political history. Fourth, the \"Adopted by\" column records the organ and majority — for example, a two-thirds majority of each House, or that majority plus a referendum majority for entrenched provisions. Fifth, a row is appended only after the adopting procedure has fully concluded and the amending text has been published as part of the corpus; an amendment is not \"adopted\" because it has been proposed, passed one House, or been announced.\n\nBecause the amendment rules are themselves entrenched, this annex cannot be used to smuggle change: any proposal to alter the tiers, the entrenched list, or the recording duties must clear the highest gate the Constitution provides. The table's plainness is the point. A reader who wishes to know whether a provision is original or amended compares the current text with the row that last touched its Section — nothing more is required.\n\n**Cross-references:** Section 0.1 (Preamble); Section 25.1 (Constitutional Amendments); Section 25.4 (Constitutional Review); Section 26.5 (Emergency Safeguards); Section 30.5 (Protected Provisions).\n"}, {"id": "annexes__cross-references", "path": "annexes/cross-references.md", "part": "annexes", "partTitle": "Annexes", "title": "Guide to Cross-References", "md": "# Guide to Cross-References\n\n*About this annex: this annex explains the numbering system used throughout the Constitution and traces the main thematic threads that run across its Parts. It is a navigation aid for readers and a style guide for future contributors who cite the text.*\n\n## The numbering system\n\nThe Constitution is organized in three layers. The corpus is divided into **31 numbered Parts**; each Part lives in a directory whose name encodes its number, such as `01-rights` for Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms). Each Part contains **Sections** numbered by position: the third file of Part 05 is Section 5.3. Each Section contains **Articles** numbered by Section: the second article of Section 5.3 is Article 5.3.2. Articles carry short titles, and clauses beneath them are numbered 1., 2., 3., with sub-clauses lettered (a), (b), (c) and further numbered (i), (ii), (iii).\n\nEvery cross-reference in the normative text therefore takes the full form \"Article 5.6.3 (Abstract Review)\" — number plus short title — so that a reader can verify the citation without opening a search window. References to a whole topic use the Section number, as in \"Section 26.1 (State of Emergency)\", and references to a whole field use the Part, as in \"Part 26 (Emergency and Crisis)\". The Preamble (Section 0.1) is unnumbered prose and contains no Articles. This scheme is deliberately arithmetic: the address of a norm never changes because an unrelated Part grew or shrank, and any citation can be checked mechanically against the directory tree.\n\n## The main thematic threads\n\nThe Parts are thematic, but the Constitution is a web: rights imply remedies, money implies audit, emergencies imply safeguards. The table below follows the principal threads. Each row names where a thread starts, the key stops along the way, and where it ends.\n\n| Thread | Start | Key stops | End |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Rights and remedies | Article 1.1.1 (Nature and Purpose of Rights) | Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights); Articles 1.2.1–1.15.5; Article 5.3.4 (Individual Complaints); Article 5.6.2 (Concrete Review) | Section 20.2 (Human Rights Commission monitoring) and Section 25.4 (periodic constitutional review) |\n| Democratic legitimacy | Article 0.3.1 (Inalienability of Popular Sovereignty) | Article 3.2.2 (Electoral Principle); Article 6.1.2 (The Electoral System for the Lower House); Article 6.6.2 (Appeal to the Constitutional Court); Section 6.7 (Referendums) | Section 25.1 (Constitutional Amendments) |\n| Lawful exercise of power | Article 0.4.1 (Legality) | Article 1.6.1 (Fair Administrative Action); Article 4.6.3 (Delegated Legislation); Article 5.6.1 (Power of All Courts) | Article 0.8.2 (Invalidation of Conflicting Law) |\n| Public money | Article 8.1.1 (Legality of Taxation) | Article 8.2.3 (Parliamentary Adoption); Article 8.3.4 (Prohibition of Monetary Financing); Article 8.6.2 (Accounting Officers) | Article 8.8.5 (Follow-Up Obligations), under the Office of the Auditor-General (Section 20.5) |\n| Emergency and safeguards | Article 4.8.1 (Declaration and Countersignature) | Section 26.1 (State of Emergency regime); Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights); Article 5.3.3 (Exclusive Jurisdiction) for immediate review | Section 26.5 (Emergency Safeguards) |\n| Integrity and accountability | Article 0.5.2 (Checks and Balances) | Article 3.10.2 (Asset Disclosure); Section 27.3 (Asset Disclosures); Section 27.4 (Conflicts of Interest) | Article 14.6.1 (Bribery), enforced through Section 20.3 (Anti-Corruption Commission) |\n| Subsidiarity and devolution | Article 7.6.1 (The Subsidiarity Principle) | Articles 7.1.1–7.3.5 (Provinces, Regions, Municipalities); Article 7.5.2 (Shared Taxes and Equalization); Article 5.3.5 (Inter-Organ and Province Disputes) | Article 7.6.4 (Protection of Local Self-Government) |\n| Digital order | Article 1.13.1 (Access to the Internet) | Article 17.1.4 (No Generalized Surveillance); Article 17.2.1 (Principles of Processing); Article 15.2.3 (Digital Searches) | Section 20.7 (the Information Commission as dual supervisor) |\n| Open information | Article 1.3.2 (Freedom of Information and the Media) | Article 21.3.2 (Open-Data Default); Section 27.2 (Freedom of Information procedures); Article 20.7.2 (Complaint Handling) | Article 21.5.1 (Protection from Violence and Intimidation) |\n| International law | Article 19.3.1 (Binding Force of International Law) | Article 19.2.2 (Parliamentary Approval); Article 19.3.2 (Primacy of Peremptory Norms); Article 9.10.3 (Trade Agreements) | Article 19.1.4 (Good-Faith Fulfillment of Obligations) |\n\n## How to read and write citations\n\nThree habits keep citations reliable. First, always cite the fullest address the point requires: a clause, an article, a section, or a part — never a bare paragraph number that could migrate between editions. Second, pair every article number with its short title, matching the title in the target file exactly; titles are part of the citation's checksum. Third, when a topic appears both as a right in Part 01 and as a regime in a later Part, cite the right for its core guarantees and the later Part for its implementation, as this table does for the digital and open-information threads. Contributors amending the text should re-verify every affected citation at the same time as the amendment, because a numbering scheme is only as trustworthy as its weakest cross-reference.\n\n**Cross-references:** Part 00 (Foundation), Section 0.8; Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms), Section 1.1; Section 5.6 (Judicial Review); Section 25.1 (Constitutional Amendments); Section 30.5 (Protected Provisions).\n"}, {"id": "annexes__implementation-roadmap", "path": "annexes/implementation-roadmap.md", "part": "annexes", "partTitle": "Annexes", "title": "Implementation Roadmap", "md": "# Implementation Roadmap\n\n*About this annex: this annex sets out a phased roadmap for bringing the Constitution into full operation, from entry into force to the first scheduled constitutional review. Milestones are relative to entry into force rather than tied to calendar dates, because the Constitution deliberately uses generic expressions of time.*\n\nThe roadmap serves two audiences. For the educator, it shows how a constitution's norms become a working state: institutions must exist before mandates can be exercised, and rights are justiciable from the first day even while implementing statutes lag behind. For the contributor, it identifies which Sections carry sequencing weight — the transition rules of Section 30.4, the establishment of courts and independent institutions, and the review mechanism of Section 25.4 — so that citations and classroom exercises can follow the same order.\n\n| Phase | Focus | Key actions | Anchor sections |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Phase 0 | Entry into force | Proclaim ratification and bring the Constitution into force; preserve existing law only insofar as consistent; adopt interim measures; begin first appointments; confirm that all existing statutes and acts are subject to the supremacy clause and judicial review from day one. | Section 0.1; Section 0.8; Section 30.4 |\n| Phase 1 | Institution building | Constitute the Constitutional Court and the Supreme Court; establish the Judicial Service Commission with binding recommendations; appoint the Public Prosecutor; establish the independent institutions of Part 20 (Ombudsman, Human Rights Commission, Anti-Corruption Commission, Electoral Commission, Auditor-General, Statistics Authority, Information Commission); establish Central Bank governance, the independent fiscal council, and the competition authority; appoint the Boundary Commission. | Sections 5.2–5.5; Section 5.8; Sections 20.1–20.7; Section 8.4; Section 8.7; Section 9.2; Section 30.2 |\n| Phase 2 | Legislation backlog | Enact the priority statutes that the Constitution mandates but leaves to ordinary law: the consolidated Criminal Code; data-protection and artificial-intelligence law; freedom-of-information procedures; social-protection frameworks; education and health financing laws; assess and conform the inherited statute book through the consistency rule of the transition provisions. | Section 14.1; Sections 17.2 and 17.4; Section 27.2; Section 22.1; Sections 11.2 and 12.1; Section 30.4 |\n| Phase 3 | Devolution of functions | Elect Provincial Councils and Municipal councils under the Electoral Commission; transfer devolved competences under the subsidiarity principle; constitute Regions as cooperative groupings for shared services; establish equalization transfers and the no-unfunded-mandates rule in local finance; hold the first common local election day. | Sections 7.1–7.4; Section 7.5; Section 7.6 |\n| Phase 4 | Fiscal frameworks | Institutionalize the annual budget adopted before the fiscal year; adopt the debt strategy and statutory sustainability limits; operate open, competitive procurement with its independent review body; publish monthly execution data; capitalize the sovereign wealth fund from resource revenues; align local borrowing limits with the national framework. | Sections 8.2–8.6; Section 13.8; Section 7.5 |\n| Phase 5 | First constitutional review | Convene the Constitutional Review Commission at the constitutionally fixed interval; conduct public participation; process proposals through the ordinary and entrenched amendment tiers; consider the constitutional-convention route where an initiative matures; record any adopted change in the amendment history. | Section 25.4; Section 25.1; Section 25.2 |\n\nThree sequencing principles explain the order. First, **institutions before mandates**: the courts, the Electoral Commission, and the Part 20 institutions must exist before the functions that depend on them — election administration, audit, data supervision — can lawfully be exercised. Second, **rights first**: nothing in the roadmap delays the justiciability of Part 01; the master limitation clause (Article 1.1.4) and the individual complaint (Article 5.3.4) operate immediately, even while Phase 2 legislation is outstanding, and courts apply existing law through the constitutional lens in the interim. Third, **review is scheduled, not improvised**: the fifteen-year review cycle of Section 25.4 is fixed in advance so that amendment pressure accumulates toward a legitimate, participatory moment instead of fragmenting into ad hoc change.\n\nThe phases overlap in practice, and the table records emphasis, not exclusivity. Local elections in Phase 3, for example, cannot wait for every Phase 2 statute; the Electoral Commission administers them under the constitutional integrity standards as soon as Provinces and Municipalities are constituted. What the roadmap forbids is reversal of dependencies: no fiscal framework without an Auditor-General, no devolution without an Electoral Commission, no review without the amendment tiers of Section 25.1.\n\n**Cross-references:** Section 30.4 (Transition Rules); Section 25.1 (Constitutional Amendments); Section 25.4 (Constitutional Review); Section 20.4 (The Electoral Commission); Section 8.8 (Audit).\n"}, {"id": "annexes__institutional-index", "path": "annexes/institutional-index.md", "part": "annexes", "partTitle": "Annexes", "title": "Index of Institutions", "md": "# Index of Institutions\n\n*About this annex: this annex indexes the institutions of the Republic, the Section that establishes each, the powers that define it, and the guarantees that keep it independent. It is a finding aid for readers, not a source of law: the binding text is the cited provision itself.*\n\nThe Constitution assigns institutional independence a constitutional rank. The classic organs — Parliament, the executive, the courts — are separated and checked under Part 00, while Part 20 establishes a family of independent institutions that stand outside the daily chain of political instruction. Several further bodies — the Central Bank, the Boundary Commission, the independent fiscal council, and the competition authority — are not part of Part 20 but receive their own independence guarantees in the Parts that govern their fields. Every institution listed here is accountable in the end to Parliament or to the courts; independence means freedom from political direction, never freedom from law.\n\n| Institution | Establishing section | Key powers | Independence guarantees |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| the Parliament | Section 3.1 | Supreme legislative authority; joint sittings for presidential election; quorum and decision rules (Article 3.1.4) | Direct mandate from the electorate; freedom of speech in proceedings (Article 3.9.1) |\n| the Lower House | Section 3.2 | Legislation; confidence in the Prime Minister; dissolution and early elections (Article 3.2.5) | Fixed four-year term; election by proportional representation with a 3% national threshold (Article 3.2.2) |\n| the Upper House | Section 3.3 | Delay of ordinary bills up to 90 days (Article 3.3.4) and of money bills up to 21 days (Article 3.3.5); no absolute veto | Equal representation of each Province; staggered six-year terms (Article 3.3.3) |\n| the Constitutional Court | Section 5.3 | Exclusive jurisdiction over constitutional questions (Article 5.3.3); individual complaints (Article 5.3.4); inter-organ and Province disputes (Article 5.3.5) | Eleven judges on single non-renewable twelve-year terms (Article 5.3.2); binding Judicial Service Commission recommendations (Article 5.5.4); financial autonomy (Article 5.1.4) |\n| the Supreme Court | Section 5.2 | Final appeals; unification of case law (Article 5.2.4); practice directions (Article 5.2.5) | Independence of courts and judges (Section 5.1); removal only for cause through the judicial-council procedure (Article 5.1.5) |\n| the High Courts and District Courts | Section 5.4 | General jurisdiction; specialized chambers (Article 5.4.5); open courts with optional lay assessors (Article 5.4.3) | Objective rules for case allocation (Article 5.4.4); same guarantees as all courts (Section 5.1) |\n| the Judicial Service Commission | Section 5.5 | Merit assessment of candidates; transparent hearings (Article 5.5.3); binding recommendations to the President (Article 5.5.4) | Composition fixed by the Constitution (Article 5.5.1); tenure and promotion rules insulating judgeships from patronage (Article 5.5.5) |\n| the Public Prosecution Service | Section 5.8 | Prosecution of offenses; transparent instructions in individual cases (Article 5.8.3); prosecution guidelines (Article 5.8.4) | Independence from the executive (Article 5.8.1); a single seven-year term for the Public Prosecutor (Article 5.8.2) |\n| the Electoral Commission | Sections 3.5 and 20.4 | Exclusive administration of all national and local elections and referendums (Articles 3.5.3 and 7.4.1) | Constitutional independence (Article 3.5.2); multi-member staggered terms (Article 20.4.1); removal only for cause by supermajority (Article 20.4.3) |\n| the Central Bank | Section 8.4 | Primary mandate of price stability (Article 8.4.1); exclusive note issue (Article 8.4.3); instruments and financial stability (Article 8.4.4) | Governance insulated from government instruction (Article 8.4.2); prohibition of monetary financing (Article 8.3.4); accountability by public report (Article 8.4.5) |\n| the Ombudsman | Section 20.1 | Investigation of maladministration; own-initiative investigations (Article 20.1.3); recommendations and annual report (Article 20.1.4) | Open standing for every person (Article 20.1.2); constitutional status under Part 20 |\n| the Human Rights Commission | Section 20.2 | Monitoring, education, and advice (Article 20.2.1); participation in proceedings as amicus (Article 20.2.3); periodic rights reports (Article 20.2.4) | Independence and pluralism of membership (Article 20.2.2), on the model of the Paris Principles |\n| the Anti-Corruption Commission | Section 20.3 | Prevention and investigation of corruption offenses; joint prosecution with the Public Prosecution Service (Article 20.3.2) | Express independence guarantees (Article 20.3.3); defined powers with safeguards (Article 20.3.4) |\n| the Office of the Auditor-General | Section 20.5 | Audit of all public funds (Article 8.8.1); access and powers (Article 20.5.3); reports to Parliament and follow-up (Articles 20.5.4 and 8.8.5) | Appointment by supermajority for a single term (Article 20.5.1); operational and budgetary independence (Article 20.5.2) |\n| the Statistics Authority | Section 20.6 | Production of official statistics; coordination of all state data collection (Article 20.6.4) | Professional independence (Article 20.6.1); confidentiality of statistical data (Article 20.6.3) |\n| the Information Commission | Section 20.7 | Supervision of data protection and of access to information (Article 20.7.1); complaint handling (Article 20.7.2); orders and fines (Article 20.7.3) | Independence and reporting duties (Article 20.7.4); orders appealable to the courts |\n| the Boundary Commission | Section 30.2 | Delimitation of constituency boundaries under principles of equality of the vote, contiguity, and community (Article 6.1.4) | Independent, non-partisan composition; a fixed review cycle (Section 30.2) |\n| the independent fiscal council | Section 8.7 | Assessment of fiscal policy and of compliance with statutory reference targets (Article 8.7.2); fiscal risk reporting (Article 8.7.4) | Independence from the executive (Article 8.7.3) |\n| the competition authority | Section 9.2 | Enforcement against prohibited agreements (Article 9.2.3) and abuse of dominance (Article 9.2.4); control of concentrations (Article 9.2.5) | Constitutional status as an independent authority (Article 9.2.2) |\n\nReaders should note three patterns. First, independence is engineered, not asserted: staggered or single terms, supermajority appointments, cause-only removal, and budgetary insulation recur across the Part 20 institutions. Second, independence is always paired with accountability — annual reports, parliamentary oversight, or appealable decisions — so that no institution is beyond scrutiny. Third, overlap is avoided by design: where an institution appears in two Parts, one Section carries its status and the other its function, and the two cross-reference each other, as with the Electoral Commission in Sections 3.5 and 20.4 and the Auditor-General in Sections 20.5 and 8.8.\n\n**Cross-references:** Part 05 (The Judiciary), Sections 5.1–5.5; Part 20 (Independent Institutions), Sections 20.1–20.7; Section 8.4 (The Central Bank); Section 8.7 (Fiscal Responsibility); Section 9.2 (Competition); Section 30.2 (Electoral Districts).\n"}, {"id": "annexes__legal-definitions", "path": "annexes/legal-definitions.md", "part": "annexes", "partTitle": "Annexes", "title": "Consolidated Legal Definitions", "md": "# Consolidated Legal Definitions\n\n*About this annex: this annex collects the working vocabulary of the Constitution in one place so that educators and students can resolve every capitalized term without searching the corpus. It is a reading aid, not a source of law: the binding meaning of each term is fixed by the provision cited in the table, which would govern over any divergence here.*\n\nThe Constitution uses a deliberately small, load-bearing vocabulary. Institutions are capitalized exactly as defined — \"the Lower House\", never \"the lower house\" — while generic words carry their ordinary meaning unless a provision gives them a specific one. Each definition lives in the Section where its term does most of its work, and where one institution appears in two places — the Electoral Commission by function in Section 3.5 and by composition in Section 20.4 — the provisions cross-reference each other rather than duplicate definitions.\n\n| Term | Meaning as used in this Constitution | Primary location |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| the Republic | The unitary, democratic state whose Constitution this is; a neutral name for no real country. | Section 0.1; Article 2.1.1 |\n| organ of the State | Any body or person exercising a public power or performing a public function under the Constitution or a law. | Article 1.1.3 |\n| the Parliament | The bicameral supreme legislative authority, comprising the Lower House and the Upper House. | Article 3.1.1 |\n| the Lower House | The confidence chamber: 400 members, four-year terms, proportional representation with open lists and a 3% national threshold. | Article 3.2.1 |\n| the Upper House | The territorial chamber: three members per Province, elected by the Provincial Councils for staggered six-year terms, with delaying powers only. | Article 3.3.1 |\n| the President | The head of state, elected by a joint sitting for a single six-year term, with ceremonial duties and narrow reserve powers. | Section 4.1 |\n| the Prime Minister | The head of government: a member of the Lower House who must hold its confidence. | Article 4.2.1 |\n| the Cabinet | The college of ministers appointed by the President on the Prime Minister's advice, bound by collective responsibility. | Article 4.3.1 |\n| constructive vote of no confidence | A motion that displaces the Prime Minister only when the Lower House simultaneously elects a successor. | Article 4.2.4 |\n| money bill | A bill whose sole purpose is taxation, appropriation, borrowing, or debt management, certified by the Lower House presiding officer subject to Constitutional Court determination. | Article 3.3.5 |\n| the Constitutional Court | The highest organ for the protection of the Constitution: eleven judges on single non-renewable twelve-year terms, holding exclusive jurisdiction over constitutional questions. | Article 5.3.1 |\n| the Supreme Court | The apex court of the general court system: final appeals and unification of case law. | Article 5.2.1 |\n| the Judicial Service Commission | The independent commission that nominates judges through transparent, merit-based hearings; its recommendations bind the President. | Section 5.5 |\n| the Public Prosecution Service | The prosecution authority, independent of the executive, headed by the Public Prosecutor serving a single seven-year term. | Article 5.8.1 |\n| the Electoral Commission | The independent commission administering all national and local elections and referendums; its status is fixed in Part 03 and its composition in Part 20. | Section 3.5; Section 20.4 |\n| the Central Bank | The independent monetary authority with the primary mandate of price stability and exclusive note issue. | Article 8.4.1 |\n| the national currency | The money of the Republic; its name is left to statute, and its issue is monopolized by the Central Bank. | Article 8.4.3 |\n| the Office of the Auditor-General | The independent supreme audit institution, auditing all public funds and reporting to Parliament. | Section 20.5; Article 8.8.2 |\n| the Ombudsman | The independent institution investigating maladministration, on complaint from any person and on its own initiative. | Section 20.1 |\n| the Human Rights Commission | The independent national human-rights institution for monitoring, education, and advice, on the Paris Principles model. | Section 20.2 |\n| the Anti-Corruption Commission | The independent institution preventing and investigating corruption offenses, prosecuting jointly with the Public Prosecution Service. | Section 20.3 |\n| the Information Commission | The single supervisor of both data protection and access to information, with order-making and fining powers subject to appeal. | Section 20.7 |\n| the Statistics Authority | The independent authority guaranteeing the professional independence, quality, and confidentiality of official statistics. | Section 20.6 |\n| Province | The primary subnational unit of the unitary state, governed by a directly elected Provincial Council. | Article 7.1.1 |\n| Region | A cooperative grouping of Provinces for shared services, with voluntary composition and no separate sovereignty. | Article 7.2.1 |\n| Municipality | The local tier of government, holding a constitutional right of self-government over local matters. | Article 7.3.1 |\n| constitutional body | An institution created by the Constitution itself, creatable or abolishable only by constitutional amendment. | Section 29.1 |\n| law of general application | A published legislative act of general scope; the only instrument by which rights may be limited under the master clause. | Article 1.1.4 |\n| non-derogable right | A right listed in Article 1.1.5 that may never be suspended, even in a declared emergency; a purporting suspension is void from adoption. | Article 1.1.5 |\n| state of emergency | The crisis regime declared by the President with countersignature, lapsing after 30 days unless Parliament approves, capped at six months per twelve-month period absent war. | Section 26.1 |\n| entrenched provision | A provision listed in Section 30.5, amendable only by a two-thirds majority of each House plus a national referendum majority. | Section 30.5; Section 25.1 |\n| referendum | A binding nationwide vote, triggered by Parliament, by citizens' initiative, or mandatorily for entrenched amendments. | Section 6.7 |\n| independent fiscal council | The independent assessor of fiscal policy and of compliance with the statutory reference targets. | Article 8.7.3 |\n| sovereign wealth fund | The constitutionally mandated fund receiving revenues from publicly owned natural resources for present and future generations. | Article 13.8.3 |\n| peremptory norm (jus cogens) | A norm of international law recognized by the international community as a whole as non-derogable and supreme within the Republic's legal order. | Article 19.3.2 |\n\nThree drafting habits explain the table's shape. First, definitions operate as interpretive rules: where a term is defined, the definition governs even against loose ordinary usage. Second, no definition is repeated; provisions that need a term cite it instead. Third, operational detail is left to statute — the fiscal year, the currency's name, and the number of Provinces — so this annex stays complete without inventing facts the Constitution declines to fix.\n\n**Cross-references:** Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms), Section 1.1; Part 03 (The Legislature), Sections 3.1–3.3; Part 05 (The Judiciary), Sections 5.2–5.8; Part 20 (Independent Institutions), Sections 20.1–20.7; Section 25.1 (Constitutional Amendments); Section 30.5 (Protected Provisions).\n"}, {"id": "annexes__rights-index", "path": "annexes/rights-index.md", "part": "annexes", "partTitle": "Annexes", "title": "Index of Rights", "md": "# Index of Rights\n\n*About this annex: this annex maps every right of Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms) to its primary articles, its limitation rule, and its derogation status. It is a navigation tool for readers, not a source of law: the binding text is the cited article itself.*\n\nPart 01 is built around two structural rules. First, **Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights)** is the single master limitation clause: every right in the Part may be limited only by a law of general application, and only if the limitation passes a five-element test — legality, legitimate aim, suitability, necessity, and proportionality stricto sensu — with the burden of justification on the State. No right carries its own private limitation formula. Second, **Article 1.1.5 (Non-Derogable Rights)** lists the rights that may never be suspended, even during a declared emergency; a provision purporting to suspend them is void from its adoption.\n\n| Right | Primary article | Limitation | Derogation status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Application and limitation of rights (framework) | Articles 1.1.1–1.1.5 | Master clause in Article 1.1.4; horizontal application in Article 1.1.2 | Core list in Article 1.1.5 never derogable |\n| Equality and non-discrimination | Articles 1.2.1–1.2.5 | Article 1.1.4; affirmative measures expressly permitted under Article 1.2.5 | Limitable |\n| Expression, information, media, assembly, association | Articles 1.3.1–1.3.5 | Article 1.1.4; prior restraint prohibited (Article 1.3.4); political speech at the core (Article 1.3.3) | Limitable |\n| Conscience and religion | Articles 1.4.1–1.4.5 | Article 1.1.4 within statutory bounds; state neutrality and non-establishment (Article 1.4.2) | Non-derogable (Articles 1.4.5 and 1.1.5(d)) |\n| Privacy and personal data | Articles 1.5.1–1.5.5 | Article 1.1.4; intrusions require judicial authorization (Article 1.5.5) | Limitable |\n| Fair administrative action | Articles 1.6.1–1.6.5 | Article 1.1.4; notice, reasons, hearing, and review (Articles 1.6.2–1.6.4) | Limitable |\n| Fair trial | Articles 1.7.1–1.7.5 | Article 1.1.4 within statutory bounds; core protected by Article 1.7.5, including habeas corpus (Article 1.7.4) | Non-derogable core (Article 1.1.5(e)) |\n| Property | Articles 1.8.1–1.8.4 | Expropriation only for public purpose, under law, against just and prompt compensation (Article 1.8.2) | Limitable |\n| Labor rights | Articles 1.9.1–1.9.5 | Article 1.1.4; strike regime in Part 10 (Article 10.7.4) | Forced labor non-derogable (Article 1.1.5(c)); the remainder limitable |\n| Education | Articles 1.10.1–1.10.4 | Article 1.1.4; free and compulsory basic education protected (Article 1.10.2) | Limitable |\n| Healthcare | Articles 1.11.1–1.11.4 | Article 1.1.4; progressive realization (Article 1.11.3) and minimum core obligations (Article 1.11.4); non-discriminatory emergency care (Article 1.11.2) | Limitable; emergency care strongly protected |\n| Healthy environment | Articles 1.12.1–1.12.4 | Article 1.1.4; standing and remedies secured by Articles 1.12.3–1.12.4 | Limitable |\n| Digital rights | Articles 1.13.1–1.13.4 | Article 1.1.4; no unlawful surveillance (Article 1.13.4) | Limitable |\n| Consumer protection | Articles 1.14.1–1.14.4 | Article 1.1.4 | Limitable |\n| Rights of the child | Articles 1.15.1–1.15.5 | Article 1.1.4; best interests of the child paramount (Article 1.15.2) | Non-derogable essentials (Article 1.15.5) |\n| Right to life | Article 1.1.5(a) | Absolute protection against derogation; enforced through the criminal law of Part 14, notably Article 14.3.1 | Non-derogable |\n\nThree reading rules help when using this table. First, the \"primary article\" column names the range of articles in which the right is stated; the core guarantee normally sits in the first article of each range (for example, Article 1.12.1 states the right to a healthy environment, and Article 1.12.3 secures standing for environmental claims). Second, every row marked \"limitable\" points back to the same master clause, Article 1.1.4; the difference between rows lies in the specific statutory conditions attached in the right's own articles, not in a different limitation test. Third, rights marked non-derogable in part carry a protective core that survives even a valid state of emergency, while the rest of the right remains limitable under ordinary rules.\n\nWhere a right is implemented by a later Part, the implementation follows the right: environmental policy is elaborated in Part 13, digital regulation in Part 17, labor standards in Part 10, education in Part 11, and health in Part 12. Enforcement runs through the courts: any court may refuse to apply an unconstitutional act (Article 5.6.1), and a person may lodge a constitutional complaint with the Constitutional Court after exhausting other remedies (Article 5.3.4). The Human Rights Commission supports this machinery through monitoring and advice (Section 20.2).\n\n**Cross-references:** Part 01 (Rights and Freedoms), Sections 1.1–1.15; Section 5.3 (The Constitutional Court); Section 5.6 (Judicial Review); Section 20.2 (The Human Rights Commission); Section 26.5 (Emergency Safeguards).\n"}, {"id": "research__comparative-constitutions", "path": "research/comparative-constitutions.md", "part": "research", "partTitle": "Research", "title": "Comparative Constitutions", "md": "# Comparative Constitutions\n\n*About this research note: this note compares the structure and rigidity of eight real constitutions and relates them to the design of this project's Constitution. It is written for educational use; the comparison is descriptive, and no real constitutional arrangement is endorsed as a model to be transplanted.*\n\n## Eight constitutions, briefly\n\n| Constitution | Form of government | Structure | Amendment rigidity | A lesson for this project |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| United States (1787) | Presidential republic | Federal; seven brief articles plus 27 amendments | Article V: two-thirds of both chambers plus three-fourths of the states; never comprehensively rewritten | Short texts need strong courts; this project chose more detail with a written limitation clause instead |\n| Germany, Basic Law (1949) | Parliamentary republic | Federal; eternity clause protects dignity, democracy, and federalism (Article 79(3)) | Two-thirds of both chambers; core unamendable | Source of the constructive vote of no confidence used in Article 4.2.4 |\n| India (1950) | Parliamentary republic | Federal in form with strong central features; the longest national constitution | Article 368 procedures; courts police an unwritten \"basic structure\" | Shows how a detailed constitution is amended often; this project instead fixes operational detail in Schedules |\n| South Africa (1996) | Parliamentary republic | Unitary with provinces; justiciable bill of rights | Tiered under Section 74: higher majorities for foundational Section 1 | Direct ancestor of this project's single limitation clause and tiered amendment design |\n| Canada (1867/1982) | Parliamentary democracy under a constitutional monarch | Federal; Charter of Rights added in 1982 | Section 38 general formula (seven provinces with half the population); Section 33 override | The notwithstanding clause warns against override mechanisms; this project has none |\n| Japan (1947) | Parliamentary constitutional monarchy | Unitary; symbolic emperor; pacifist Article 9 | Article 96: two-thirds of both chambers plus a majority referendum; never amended | Confirms that a referendum-anchored amendment route can stabilize a text for decades |\n| Brazil (1988) | Presidential republic | Federal; detailed social and economic rights | Three-fifths of each chamber in two rounds; some matters unamendable | Illustrates the cost of exhaustive detail: frequent amendment of ordinary matters |\n| Kenya (2010) | Presidential republic | Unitary with devolved counties; extensive bill of rights | Parliamentary route and a popular-initiative route, both ending in a referendum | Model for this project's citizens'-initiative referendum route (Section 6.7) |\n\n## What the comparison shows\n\nThree axes organize the eight texts. The first is **form of government**: the United States and Brazil elect a president who is both head of state and head of government, while Germany, India, South Africa, Canada, and Japan locate executive power in a cabinet responsible to the legislature. This project follows the parliamentary family, but with a distinction borrowed from practice rather than from any single text: a President elected by Parliament for a single six-year term as head of state, and a Prime Minister who must hold the confidence of the Lower House. The single non-renewable term avoids the re-election incentives that have strained presidential systems, while the constructive vote of no confidence, taken from the German model, protects against cabinet instability.\n\nThe second axis is **territorial structure**: five of the eight are federal in law or in practice, while Japan is unitary and South Africa and Kenya are unitary with substantial devolution. This project adopts the last pattern — a unitary state whose Provinces, Regions, and Municipalities are protected by a subsidiarity principle rather than by divided sovereignty — because it lets the text model devolution without importing the complex disputes that federal allocation of competences produces.\n\nThe third axis is **rigidity**. The United States text is nearly immovable in practice; Japan's has never been amended; India's is formally flexible but judicially bounded; South Africa and Germany entrench only their cores. This project's tiers (Section 25.1) sit deliberately in the South African position: ordinary amendments by two-thirds of each House, entrenched provisions (Section 30.5) also requiring a referendum majority, and Schedules adjustable by simple majority. The design keeps the constitutional core where real constitutions keep theirs — dignity, democracy, judicial independence — while admitting that operational detail must remain cheap to adjust.\n\nFinally, the eight texts differ in **detail**: the American Constitution fits in a pamphlet, the Indian fills a volume. This project occupies a middle course — full enough that a student can trace money, emergencies, and digital rights from right to institution to procedure, yet spare enough that the thematic Parts remain readable from beginning to end.\n\n## Sources consulted\n\n- Constitution of the United States of America (1787), with the Bill of Rights (1791) and the twenty-seven amendments.\n- Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (1949).\n- Constitution of India (1950).\n- Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996).\n- Constitution Act, 1867, and Constitution Act, 1982 (Canada), including the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.\n- Constitution of Japan (1947).\n- Constitution of the Federative Republic of Brazil (1988).\n- Constitution of Kenya (2010).\n"}, {"id": "research__comparative-courts", "path": "research/comparative-courts.md", "part": "research", "partTitle": "Research", "title": "Comparative Courts", "md": "# Comparative Courts\n\n*About this research note: this note compares how real systems organize judicial review of legislation and appoint their highest judges, and then explains this project's choice of a distinct Constitutional Court with binding commission recommendations. It is descriptive and educational; the binding text is Part 05.*\n\n## Centralized versus diffuse review\n\nThe organizing question is: who may refuse to apply an unconstitutional law? The **diffuse** model, born with Marbury v. Madison (1803) in the United States, lets every judge in the ordinary hierarchy disregard statutes contrary to the constitution; review is incidental to ordinary litigation. The **centralized** model, invented by Hans Kelsen for the Austrian Constitution of 1920, concentrates the power in a dedicated court, sometimes with exclusive jurisdiction, so that constitutional questions are decided uniformly and, often, in the abstract. Germany, Italy, Spain, and France follow the centralized design with constitutional courts or councils; Japan (Article 81) and Canada follow the American pattern, with review available across the court system.\n\nBoth models generate hybrids. India gives the Supreme Court and the High Courts concurrent constitutional jurisdiction (Articles 32 and 226), producing diffuse review with a strong apex court; Brazil combines concrete review by all judges with abstract review concentrated in its Supreme Federal Tribunal. The practical trade-offs concern consistency, access, and speed: diffuse review constitutionalizes all litigation but risks divergence among courts; centralized review is uniform but can bottleneck, which is why Germany and South Africa filter access to their constitutional courts while ordinary courts send preliminary questions upward.\n\n| System | Review model | Apex constitutional organ | Access design |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| United States (1787) | Diffuse; all courts | Supreme Court of the United States | Cases and controversies only |\n| Austria (1920) | Centralized; origin of the model | Constitutional Court | Referral by courts; party and minority access |\n| Germany (1949) | Centralized | Federal Constitutional Court | Filtered individual complaints; preliminary referrals |\n| Italy (1947) | Centralized | Constitutional Court | Incidental referral by ordinary courts in genuine cases |\n| France (1958) | Centralized; ex ante review, later reinforced by a preliminary-ruling mechanism (2008 reform) | Constitutional Council | Referral by litigants through the priority question of constitutionality |\n| Canada (1982) | Diffuse; all courts | Supreme Court of Canada | Any proceeding raising the Charter |\n| Japan (1947) | Diffuse; all courts (Article 81) | Supreme Court of Japan | Incidental in ordinary litigation |\n| India (1950) | Hybrid; diffuse with strong apex | Supreme Court of India | Direct petitions (Article 32) and High Court petitions (Article 226) |\n| South Africa (1996) | Hybrid; appeals concentrated at the apex | Constitutional Court | Constitutional matters; leave to appeal filters |\n| This Constitution | Hybrid | the Constitutional Court (Section 5.3) | Concrete review by any court (Article 5.6.2); abstract review and individual complaints after exhaustion of remedies centralized (Articles 5.6.3 and 5.3.4) |\n\nThis project's design is deliberately hybrid. Every court may refuse to apply an unconstitutional act, so constitutional defense never waits on a single docket (Articles 5.6.1 and 5.6.2); but abstract review, inter-organ and Province disputes, election validity, and individual complaints after ordinary remedies are exhausted belong exclusively to the Constitutional Court (Article 5.3.3). The separation of the Constitutional Court from the Supreme Court (Section 5.2) mirrors the German and South African practice of giving constitutional questions a specialist bench, while the American insight is preserved in the diffuse floor of concrete review.\n\n## Appointment models\n\nWho appoints judges decides whose constitution it is. The United States combines presidential nomination with Senate confirmation and life tenure. Germany elects its Federal Constitutional Court judges in halves by the two legislative chambers, each by a two-thirds majority, for single non-renewable twelve-year terms. Italy splits appointments three ways among the head of state, Parliament, and the supreme courts. France uses nine-year non-renewable terms with appointments shared between the President and the chambers. South Africa interviews candidates publicly through a Judicial Service Commission before the President appoints; Kenya's commission recruits with public participation; India's collegium of senior judges selects, a practice unique in the democratic world.\n\n| Jurisdiction | Appointment method | Term |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| United States | President nominates; Senate confirms | Life tenure |\n| Germany | Bundestag and Bundesrat each elect half, two-thirds majority | Twelve years, non-renewable |\n| Italy | One third President, one third Parliament, one third supreme courts | Nine years, non-renewable |\n| South Africa | Judicial Service Commission interviews; President appoints | Twelve years or retirement age |\n| Kenya | Judicial Service Commission recruits with public participation; President appoints | Tenure protected by constitution |\n| This Constitution | Judicial Service Commission recommends by binding recommendation (Section 5.5); President appoints | Eleven judges; single non-renewable twelve-year terms, staggered (Section 5.3) |\n\nThe binding recommendation is this project's distinctive choice. Where a commission merely advises, the appointing organ retains a final political filter; where a collegium monopolizes selection, accountability to other branches thins. Making the Judicial Service Commission's recommendation binding (Article 5.5.4) — after transparent, merit-based hearings (Article 5.5.3) — removes the last discretion from the appointing President while leaving the formal act of appointment in constitutional hands, a compromise between the South African and German models that keeps appointments professional, public, and predictable. The single non-renewable term, with one-third of seats expiring every four years, then protects sitting judges: no one rules on cases knowing that a future reappointment depends on pleasing today's politicians.\n\n## Sources consulted\n\n- Austrian Federal Constitutional Law (1920).\n- Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (1949), Articles 92–104.\n- Constitution of Italy (1947), Title VI.\n- Constitution of the French Republic (1958), as amended in 2008.\n- Constitution of the United States of America (1787), Article III.\n- Constitution of India (1950), Articles 32, 141, and 226.\n- Constitution of Japan (1947), Article 81.\n- Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996), Sections 167 and 174–178.\n- Constitution of Kenya (2010), Chapter Ten.\n- European Commission for Democracy through Law (Venice Commission), opinions and reports on judicial appointments and judicial independence.\n- Marbury v. Madison, Supreme Court of the United States (1803).\n"}, {"id": "research__comparative-economic-systems", "path": "research/comparative-economic-systems.md", "part": "research", "partTitle": "Research", "title": "Comparative Economic Systems", "md": "# Comparative Economic Systems\n\n*About this research note: this note surveys how real systems protect central bank independence, discipline public finance with fiscal rules and fiscal councils, and govern public ownership, before explaining this project's design in Sections 8.4 and 8.7. It is descriptive and educational; the binding text is Part 08.*\n\n## Central bank independence\n\nModern monetary constitutions separate the goal from the politician. The German Bundesbank, established by its 1957 act, became the template: legal independence, a price-stability orientation, and a prohibition on financing the state. The European Union generalized the model — the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union gives the European Central Bank a primary mandate of price stability (Article 127), forbids member-state instruction (Article 130), and prohibits central banks from extending overdraft facilities or buying sovereign debt directly from their governments (Article 123). The United States chose a dual mandate of maximum employment and stable prices; New Zealand's Reserve Bank Act 1989 pioneered statutory inflation targeting, later imitated worldwide. Comparative experience draws two distinctions: **goal independence** (who sets the objective) is rare, while **instrument independence** (how the objective is pursued) is the global norm; and independence is always purchased with accountability — published minutes, reports to the legislature, and appearances before parliamentary committees.\n\nThis Constitution follows the mainstream. Article 8.4.1 gives the Central Bank independence with a primary mandate of price stability supporting the general economic policy; Article 8.4.3 reserves note issue to it; Article 8.4.4 equips it with instruments and financial-stability duties; and Article 8.4.5 requires public accountability reporting. The prohibition of monetary financing appears in Article 8.3.4, with the narrow exception allowing ordinary monetary-policy operations on market terms — the same carve-out that the European prohibition contains, because a central bank that may never hold sovereign paper could not conduct open-market operations at all.\n\n## Fiscal rules and fiscal councils\n\nRules that bind budgets attempt to solve a structural bias: governments spend other people's future money. The German debt brake, written into the Basic Law in 2009, caps the federal structural deficit at 0.35% of gross domestic product; the Swiss debt brake (Article 126 of its 1999 Constitution) requires expenditure to track estimated revenue over the cycle; the European Union's reference values of 3% deficit and 60% debt (Maastricht, 1992) anchor its excessive-deficit procedure. A second family is transparency-led: New Zealand's Fiscal Responsibility Act 1994 forces governments to publish honest projections rather than capping numbers directly; Chile's structural-balance rule since 2001 ties spending to medium-term revenue estimated by independent panels.\n\nBecause rules invite creative accounting, a third generation of institutions audits them: the United Kingdom's Office for Budget Responsibility (created by statute in 2011) and Ireland's Fiscal Advisory Council produce or scrutinize independent forecasts, while the long-established Netherlands bureau for economic policy analysis shows how decades of independent projection discipline debate. This Constitution's Article 8.7 states the principle of intergenerational equity (Article 8.7.1), requires deficit and debt reference targets to be set by law (Article 8.7.2) — leaving numbers to ordinary legislation, which can adapt — and establishes an independent fiscal council (Article 8.7.3) with risk-reporting duties (Article 8.7.4). Budgetary process remains parliamentary: the annual budget must be adopted before the fiscal year begins (Article 8.2.3), and no expenditure is lawful without authorization (Article 8.6.1), with every public fund audited by the Office of the Auditor-General (Articles 8.8.1 and 20.5.1).\n\n## Ownership models\n\nConstitutions also decide who owns what. State ownership of enterprises is a fact in nearly every economy; the comparative question is governance. The OECD Guidelines on Corporate Governance of State-Owned Enterprises (2015) codify the emerging standard — competitive neutrality, commercially objective ownership, transparent reporting — so that public firms neither distort markets nor become hidden treasuries. Natural-resource revenues receive constitutional treatment where they dominate the budget: Norway's Government Pension Fund Global converts petroleum wealth into a diversified, rule-governed fund for future generations, and Chile's stabilization funds smooth copper-driven cycles.\n\n| Design choice | Selected practice | Rationale | This Constitution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Central bank independence | Bundesbank; European Central Bank; Reserve Bank of New Zealand | Insulate money from electoral cycles | Article 8.4, with price stability primary and reporting duties |\n| Prohibition of monetary financing | European Union (TFEU Article 123) | Prevent debt monetization and inflationary finance | Article 8.3.4, with a narrow market-terms exception |\n| Numerical fiscal rules | German and Swiss debt brakes; EU reference values | Bind governments against deficit bias | Reference targets set by law (Article 8.7.2) |\n| Independent fiscal councils | Office for Budget Responsibility (United Kingdom); Irish Fiscal Advisory Council | Honest forecasts; rule compliance | Article 8.7.3 |\n| SOE governance | OECD Guidelines (2015) | Competitive neutrality; transparency | Section 29.4 |\n| Sovereign wealth funds | Norway's Government Pension Fund Global; Chilean stabilization funds | Intergenerational sharing of resource wealth | Article 13.8.3 |\n\nThe pattern behind the table is consistent: constitutional texts fix principles, institutions, and prohibitions, while statutes fix numbers, rates, and procedures. This Constitution keeps that division. It entrenches independence, prohibits monetization, and requires sustainability, but it does not entrench a deficit percentage, an interest rate, or an ownership percentage — the lessons of the comparative record suggest that rigid numbers age badly, while independent institutions with clear mandates age well.\n\n## Sources consulted\n\n- Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union (2007), Articles 123, 126–130.\n- Act Establishing the Deutsche Bundesbank (1957).\n- Reserve Bank of New Zealand Act 1989.\n- Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (1949), Articles 109–115 (debt brake).\n- Federal Constitution of the Swiss Confederation (1999), Article 126.\n- Fiscal Responsibility Act 1994 (New Zealand).\n- Budget Responsibility and National Audit Act 2011 (United Kingdom).\n- Treaty on European Union (Maastricht, 1992), deficit and debt reference values.\n- OECD Guidelines on Corporate Governance of State-Owned Enterprises (2015).\n- International Monetary Fund, Code of Good Practices on Fiscal Transparency (2007).\n- Government Pension Fund Global (Norway), statutory mandate.\n"}, {"id": "research__comparative-electoral-systems", "path": "research/comparative-electoral-systems.md", "part": "research", "partTitle": "Research", "title": "Comparative Electoral Systems", "md": "# Comparative Electoral Systems\n\n*About this research note: this note surveys the three great families of electoral systems, the working of thresholds and list types, and the indirect election of second chambers, before explaining this project's choice of proportional representation with open lists and a 3% national threshold. It is descriptive and educational; the binding text is Part 06.*\n\n## The three families\n\nElectoral systems translate votes into seats, and every formula buys something and sells something. **Majoritarian** systems — first past the post in the United Kingdom, the United States, Canada, and India; the two-round system in France; preferential ballots in Australia's lower house — concentrate power and anchor members in districts, but systematically overreward pluralities and can manufacture majorities. **Proportional** systems — list PR across most of continental Europe, the single transferable vote in Ireland and Malta — mirror the electorate's diversity, at the cost of coalition government. **Mixed** systems blend the two: mixed-member proportional (Germany, New Zealand) tops up district winners from party lists so the total result stays proportional, while parallel or non-compensated systems (Japan) seat district winners and list members side by side, leaving the list half only weakly corrective.\n\n| System | Example jurisdictions | Key features | Typical effects |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| First past the post | United Kingdom; United States; Canada; India | One member per district; plurality wins | Strong two-party or two-bloc pressure; disproportional outcomes |\n| Two-round majority | France | Runoff between leading candidates when no majority | Encourages alliances; high districting sensitivity |\n| Single transferable vote | Ireland; Malta; Australian Senate | Ranked ballots in multimember districts; surplus transfers | Proportional with personal choice; complex counting |\n| Closed-list proportional | Spain; Portugal | Parties fix list order; seats filled top-down | Strong proportionality; high party control over candidates |\n| Open-list proportional | Finland; Brazil | Voters choose candidates within lists | Proportionality with personal accountability; strong intraparty competition |\n| Mixed-member proportional | Germany; New Zealand | District seats plus compensatory list seats | Proportional totals with local representation |\n| Parallel mixed (non-compensated) | Japan | District and list seats counted separately | Partial proportionality; large-party advantage persists |\n\n## Thresholds and list types\n\nThresholds are the counterweight that keeps parliaments governable. Germany and New Zealand exclude parties below 5% (with a seat-holding escape valve in New Zealand); Turkey long excluded parties below 10%, recently relaxed; Israel lowers its bar to 3.25%; the Netherlands' pure PR carries only the arithmetic threshold of one seat in a 150-member chamber, about 0.67%. The trade-off is exact: a 1% threshold lets small ideological families win representation but fragments coalitions; a 10% threshold stabilizes cabinets but silences whole electorates. Open versus closed lists shift power inside parties: open lists (Finland, Brazil) make candidates answer to voters and weaken party machines; closed lists (Spain, Portugal) let parties present balanced teams, including through zipper lists that alternate by gender, at the price of candidate selection controlled from the top.\n\nThis project sits deliberately in the middle of each axis. Article 6.1.2 gives the Lower House proportional representation with **open candidate lists** and a **3% national threshold**, allocated by the **Droop quota** over province-wide lists (Article 6.1.3). The 3% line excludes micro-parties without extinguishing new movements; open lists preserve the personal accountability that pure closed lists surrender; the Droop quota, shared with the single transferable vote, allocates seats with the least wasted vote of the standard formulas. Article 6.1.1 states the underlying principle — formal equality of the vote — and Article 6.1.4 ties district boundaries to an independent commission under the principles of Section 30.2, because thresholds and formulas matter little if boundary lines are drawn by the incumbents they entrench. Article 6.1.5 then stabilizes electoral law for a fixed period, echoing the comparative lesson that rules must not be rewritten by their expected beneficiaries between elections.\n\n## Second chambers and election administration\n\nThe Upper House is elected indirectly: three members per Province, chosen by the Provincial Councils for staggered six-year terms (Section 3.3). Indirect election has real precedents — India's Council of States is elected by state legislative assemblies; Ireland's Senate mixes vocational panels with university seats — while Canada's appointed chamber and Germany's Bundesrat of state governments show the appointed and executive alternatives. The indirect route fits this project's unitary design: the chamber represents Provinces as territorial units without duplicating a direct popular mandate, and its powers are confined to delay (Articles 3.3.4 and 3.3.5).\n\nAdministration is constitutionalized as well. Universal suffrage begins at 18; registration is automatic (Article 6.2.1); the ballot is secret and accessible (Articles 6.3.1 and 6.3.2); and paper ballots or a verified paper trail are mandatory everywhere, with post-election audits (Article 6.4.1 and 6.4.4) — a response to the recurring comparative finding that trust in outcomes depends less on the formula than on visible, verifiable administration by an independent commission (Sections 3.5 and 20.4). Disputes run to the Constitutional Court on expedited timelines (Article 6.6.2), closing the loop between electoral design and constitutional adjudication.\n\n## Sources consulted\n\n- International IDEA, Electoral System Design: The New International IDEA Handbook (2005).\n- Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (1949), with the Federal Electoral Act.\n- Electoral Act 1993 (New Zealand).\n- Constitution of Ireland (1937) and Irish electoral law (single transferable vote).\n- Public Offices Election Act (Japan, 1950).\n- Electoral Code of France (two-round legislative elections).\n- Electoral law of the Netherlands (Kieswet).\n- Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996), Chapter 9 institutions.\n- Constitution of Kenya (2010), Chapter Seven.\n- Lijphart, Patterns of Democracy (2nd edition, 2012).\n"}, {"id": "research__comparative-rights", "path": "research/comparative-rights.md", "part": "research", "partTitle": "Research", "title": "Comparative Rights", "md": "# Comparative Rights\n\n*About this research note: this note compares how real constitutional systems limit and suspend rights, and how they make rights bind private parties, before explaining the choices made in this project's Part 01. It is descriptive and educational; the binding text of this Constitution is Part 01 itself.*\n\n## Limitation clauses: three designs\n\nConstitutional rights are rarely absolute, and drafters have chosen three ways to say so. The **European Convention on Human Rights** writes a qualified clause into each high-level right: interference must be \"in accordance with the law\", pursue a listed \"legitimate aim\", and be \"necessary in a democratic society\". The approach is precise but repetitive, and courts must harmonize differently worded clauses. **Canada's Charter** (Section 1) and **South Africa's Constitution** (Section 36) invert the design with a single master clause covering all rights; the Canadian Supreme Court's Oakes test (1986) requires a pressing and substantial objective, rational connection, minimal impairment, and proportionality of effects. The **United States Bill of Rights** contains no general limitation clause at all; judges supply the filter through tiers of scrutiny, from rational-basis review to strict scrutiny.\n\nThis project adopts the single master clause. Article 1.1.4 (Limitation of Rights) requires a law of general application and a five-element test — legality, legitimate aim, suitability, necessity, and proportionality stricto sensu — and places the burden of justification on the State. Compared with the Convention model, one clause is easier to teach and applies identically to every right; compared with the American model, the test is written down, so its elements are contestable in text rather than discoverable only in doctrine.\n\n| Instrument | Limitation formula | Derogation mechanism | Horizontal effect |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| European Convention on Human Rights (1950) | Per-right qualified clauses (Articles 8–11): law, aim, necessity in a democratic society | Article 15, in war or public emergency; Articles 2, 3, 4(1), and 7 non-derogable | Indirect, through Convention obligations on states |\n| International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (1966) | Per-right clauses | Article 4, with formal notification; strictly required by the exigencies of the situation | Limited; states parties bind themselves |\n| United States Bill of Rights (1791) | No general clause; judicial scrutiny fills the gap | None expressed; habeas corpus suspension clause in the main text | Weak: the state-action doctrine restricts application to private parties |\n| Canadian Charter (1982) | Section 1: reasonable limits, demonstrably justified in a free and democratic society | Sections 32–33: no formal derogation, but a legislative override with a five-year sunset | Section 32: binds governments and legislatures |\n| German Basic Law (1949) | Per-right limits by statute; essence of the right inviolable (Article 19(2)); proportionality doctrine developed judicially | None in peacetime; emergency articles removed in 1968 reform | Indirect horizontal effect (Drittwirkung) through general clauses, from the Lüth case (1958) |\n| South African Constitution (1996) | Section 36: law of general application; reasonableness and justifiability; less restrictive means considered | Section 37, with parliamentary approval and a fixed non-derogable core | Section 8(2): binds natural and juristic persons where capable |\n| This Constitution | Single master clause, Article 1.1.4, with five elements and the burden on the State | No suspension permitted of the Article 1.1.5 list; suspension is void from adoption | Article 1.1.2: binds private persons where the right is capable of such application |\n\n## Derogation in emergencies\n\nThe Convention and the Covenant permit formal derogation in public emergencies, subject to non-derogable cores: the right to life, freedom from torture, freedom from slavery, and legality in criminal law. South Africa's Section 37 adds procedural muscle — parliamentary approval and a defined list that survives the emergency. Canada took the opposite path, preferring a legislative override (Section 33) that operates even in ordinary times but expires after five years. This project accepts derogation machinery nowhere: Article 1.1.5 lists the non-derogable rights — life; freedom from torture and cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment; freedom from slavery and forced labor; conscience and religion; the fair-trial core including habeas corpus; and the principle of legality in criminal law — and declares any purporting suspension void from adoption. The emergency regime of Part 26 therefore regulates measures rather than suspending rights.\n\n## Horizontal effect\n\nWhether rights bind private parties decides a constitution's reach into workplaces, contracts, and platforms. The American state-action doctrine keeps the Bill of Rights aimed at government; the German courts achieved indirect horizontal effect by reading private-law general clauses through fundamental rights, beginning with Lüth (1958); South Africa wrote the point into Section 8(2). This project follows South Africa: Article 1.1.2 binds all organs of the State and also natural and juristic persons in their relations with one another to the extent the right is capable of such application — a formula that lets privacy and expression reach digital platforms without pretending that every private preference is a constitutional violation.\n\n## Sources consulted\n\n- European Convention on Human Rights (1950), Articles 8–11 and 15.\n- International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (1966), Article 4.\n- Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (1949), Articles 1–19.\n- Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms (1982), Sections 1, 32, and 33.\n- Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996), Sections 8, 36, and 37.\n- Constitution of the United States of America: Bill of Rights (1791) and Fourteenth Amendment (1868).\n- R v Oakes, Supreme Court of Canada (1986).\n- Lüth, Federal Constitutional Court of Germany (1958).\n- Government of the Republic of South Africa v Grootboom, Constitutional Court of South Africa (2000).\n"}, {"id": "research__design-principles", "path": "research/design-principles.md", "part": "research", "partTitle": "Research", "title": "Design Principles", "md": "# Design Principles\n\n*About this research note: this note documents the drafting principles actually used in this project — clarity, enforceability, a single limitation clause, tiered amendment, schedules for operational detail, and design notes for pedagogy — and sets each against real drafting practice. It explains the text rather than adding to it; the binding text is the Constitution itself.*\n\n## Clarity\n\nThe Constitution is written in short sentences, with one normative idea per clause and the operative verb carrying the rule: \"shall\" for duties, \"may\" for powers, \"shall not\" for prohibitions. The principle has a real drafting pedigree. The United Kingdom's Renton Committee on the Preparation of Legislation (1975) attacked over-complex statutory English; the plain-language movement that followed reshaped public drafting in many jurisdictions, culminating in instruments such as the United States Plain Writing Act 2010. Constitutions written late in the twentieth century — South Africa (1996), Kenya (2010) — read visibly more plainly than their nineteenth-century relatives. This project applies the same discipline structurally as well as verbally: article short titles (\"Limitation of Rights\", \"Prohibition of Monetary Financing\") act as headers for retrieval, and every cross-reference carries the article number plus its short title, so a citation can be checked without leaving the page.\n\n## Enforceability\n\nEvery norm is drafted so that a court could apply it. Where the text creates a right, it names the organ, the standard, and the remedy: the right to a fair trial (Article 1.7.1) is secured by habeas corpus (Article 1.7.4) and individual complaint after exhaustion of remedies (Article 5.3.4); the audit function names who audits what and who reports to whom (Articles 8.8.1 and 8.8.4). The contrast is with directive language — India's directive principles of state policy and Ireland's directive social principles are famous and deliberately non-justiciable. This project instead follows the South African choice of justiciable social rights, softened by internal qualification: progressive realization and minimum core obligations in healthcare (Articles 1.11.3 and 1.11.4) make the right enforceable against arbitrary retreat while honest about capacity. A norm this Constitution cannot imagine anyone violating — \"the State shall be prosperous\" — would not belong in it.\n\n## The single limitation clause\n\nAll limitation runs through one clause. Article 1.1.4 requires a law of general application and a five-element test — legality, legitimate aim, suitability, necessity, proportionality stricto sensu — with the burden of justification on the State. The design follows Canada's Section 1 and South Africa's Section 36 rather than the per-right qualified clauses of the European Convention on Human Rights, because one transparent test is easier to teach, apply, and audit than a dozen differently worded ones. The counterpart is the closed non-derogable list of Article 1.1.5: a limitation clause this strong is tolerable only if the core — life, physical integrity, slavery, conscience and religion, fair trial, criminal legality — is declared beyond suspension, with any purporting suspension void from adoption.\n\n## Tiered amendment\n\nThe Constitution prices change by importance. Ordinary amendments need two-thirds of each House; entrenched provisions (Section 30.5) need that majority plus a national referendum; schedule matters adjust by simple majority (Section 25.1). The tiers echo real practice: the German eternity clause entrenches identity absolutely; South Africa's Section 74 sets higher bars for foundational provisions; Japan's Article 96 requires a referendum; Switzerland submits constitutional amendments to the people routinely. The project's addition is explicit coupling of the tiers to a history record: every adopted change must be entered in the amendment history annex, so the reader can always tell original text from amendment.\n\n## Schedules for operational detail\n\nOaths, boundary principles, a table of constitutional offices, transition rules, and the protected-provisions list live in Part 30 rather than in the normative Parts. The division copies the function of the Indian and South African schedules: keep the durable rules in the body and the adjustable machinery at the edge, where amendment is cheaper and failure less visible. A text that needed a referendum to change an oath form would be mispriced; a text that kept rights in a schedule would be misbuilt.\n\n## Design notes for pedagogy\n\nEvery section ends with a Design Note of two to four sentences that states the rationale and the trade-off struck, and comparative references to real constitutions appear only there — never in the normative text, which speaks for a fictional Republic. The practice serves the project's audience of educators and students: the note converts a drafting choice into a teachable one, and confines real-world citation to the place where it informs rather than distracts. Combined with the annexes (definitions, indexes, cross-reference guide) and these research notes, the corpus is designed to be read linearly by a learner and randomly by a drafter, with the numbering scheme — Part.Section.Article — reliable under both approaches.\n\n| Principle | Where it lives in this Constitution | Comparative echo |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Clarity | Style rules; short titles; full-form cross-references | Renton Report (1975); South Africa 1996; Kenya 2010 |\n| Enforceability | Remedies in Parts 01, 05, and 06 | Justiciable rights under South Africa 1996 |\n| Single limitation clause | Article 1.1.4 with Article 1.1.5 | Canada Section 1; South Africa Section 36 |\n| Tiered amendment | Sections 25.1 and 30.5 | Germany Article 79(3); Japan Article 96 |\n| Schedules | Part 30 | Indian and South African schedules |\n| Design notes | End of every section | Constitution-building primers and handbooks |\n\n## Sources consulted\n\n- Renton Committee, The Preparation of Legislation (United Kingdom, 1975).\n- Plain Writing Act 2010 (United States).\n- Constitution of the Republic of South Africa (1996).\n- Constitution of Kenya (2010).\n- Constitution of India (1950).\n- Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (1949).\n- Constitution of Japan (1947).\n- Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms (1982), Section 1.\n- Constitution of the United States of America (1787), Article V.\n- European Convention on Human Rights (1950).\n- International IDEA, constitution-building primers and handbook series.\n"}];
